window.ASK_KB={"meta":{"topics":{"hashing":"Arrays & Hashing","two-pointers":"Two Pointers","sliding-window":"Sliding Window","stack":"Stack","binary-search":"Binary Search","linked-list":"Linked List","recursion":"Recursion & Backtracking","heap":"Heap","trie":"Trie","graphs":"Graphs","dp":"Dynamic Programming","greedy-intervals":"Greedy & Intervals","bits":"Bit Manipulation","sd-start":"Start Here: Design Thinking","sd-java":"Java & OOP","sd-solid":"SOLID","sd-creational":"Creational Patterns","sd-structural":"Structural Patterns","sd-behavioral":"Behavioral Patterns","sd-lld":"LLD Problems","sd-basics":"System Design Basics","sd-databases":"Databases","sd-distributed":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-hld":"HLD Problems"},"n":1305,"q":5451},"kb":[{"i":"t-hashing","k":"topic","s":"hashing","t":"Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Arrays & Hashing","Arrays & Hashing overview","When to use Arrays & Hashing","Arrays & Hashing template","Arrays & Hashing common mistakes","explain Arrays & Hashing","Arrays & Hashing cheat sheet","Arrays & Hashing notes"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing hashing topic pattern","a":"<p>The topic every interview starts with. Trade memory for time, remember what you&#x27;ve seen, and turn O(n²) scans into one pass.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Trade memory for time: remember what you've seen in a dict/set so the inner loop becomes an O(1) lookup.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Seen before?<\/b> → <code>set<\/code>: check, then add, in one pass.<\/li><li><b>Pair to a target<\/b> → dict <code>value → index<\/code>; look up <code>target − x<\/code> <i>before<\/i> storing x.<\/li><li><b>Group things that are 'the same'<\/b> → a canonical key: sorted string or a 26-count tuple.<\/li><li><b>Count things<\/b> → <code>Counter<\/code>; top-k by frequency → bucket by count (O(n)) or a heap.<\/li><li><b>Range sums<\/b> → prefix sums: <code>sum(l..r) = pre[r+1] − pre[l]<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Subarray sums to k<\/b> (negatives allowed) → prefix sum + dict of how often each prefix appeared; start with <code>{0: 1}<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Values in 1..n<\/b> → the array is its own hash: mark index <code>x − 1<\/code> (negate / swap into place).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Lists can't be dict keys; use a tuple.<\/li><li>Store the index <i>after<\/i> the lookup, or x pairs with itself.<\/li><li>Dict/set ops are O(1) <i>average<\/i>; say so.<\/li><li>Matrix in place: record which rows/cols to clear first, then clear.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Have I seen this before?: The question behind most array problems, and the data structure that answers it in O(1).<\/li><li>Prefix sums: Precompute running totals once, and any range sum becomes one subtraction. Then combine with a dict for the famous “subarray sums to k”.<\/li><li>Matrix moves &amp; the full set: In-place matrix tricks interviewers love, then every problem in this topic worth practicing.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Why a dict turns O(n²) into O(n), and what it costs in memory.<\/li><li>The decoder: Read the problem, spot the phrase, reach for the tool.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"seen = {}                          # remember what you've passed\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    if target - x in seen:         # ask before you store\n        return [seen[target - x], i]\n    seen[x] = i"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-hashing"],["🎬 Two Sum in one pass","#film-hashing"]]},{"i":"cx-hashing","k":"cx","s":"hashing","t":"Arrays & Hashing: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Arrays & Hashing","Arrays & Hashing big o","space complexity Arrays & Hashing","Arrays & Hashing complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technique<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Set / dict lookup pass<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code> average<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Group by canonical key<\/td><td><code>O(n · k)<\/code> with count tuples<\/td><td><code>O(n · k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Bucket sort by frequency<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Prefix sums, then queries<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code> build, <code>O(1)<\/code> query<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Index-as-hash marking<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> extra<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Rotate / spiral / set zeroes<\/td><td><code>O(R · C)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> extra<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-hashing","k":"list","s":"hashing","t":"All Arrays & Hashing problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Arrays & Hashing problems","Arrays & Hashing questions","which Arrays & Hashing problems should I do","Arrays & Hashing leetcode list","practice problems for Arrays & Hashing"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-217\"><b>Contains Duplicate<\/b> <small>LeetCode 217 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-242\"><b>Valid Anagram<\/b> <small>LeetCode 242 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-1\"><b>Two Sum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-49\"><b>Group Anagrams<\/b> <small>LeetCode 49 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-347\"><b>Top K Frequent Elements<\/b> <small>LeetCode 347 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-238\"><b>Product of Array Except Self<\/b> <small>LeetCode 238 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-36\"><b>Valid Sudoku<\/b> <small>LeetCode 36 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-128\"><b>Longest Consecutive Sequence<\/b> <small>LeetCode 128 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-560\"><b>Subarray Sum Equals K<\/b> <small>LeetCode 560 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-303\"><b>Range Sum Query – Immutable<\/b> <small>LeetCode 303 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-169\"><b>Majority Element<\/b> <small>LeetCode 169 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-271\"><b>Encode and Decode Strings<\/b> <small>LeetCode 271 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-41\"><b>First Missing Positive<\/b> <small>LeetCode 41 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-48\"><b>Rotate Image<\/b> <small>LeetCode 48 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-54\"><b>Spiral Matrix<\/b> <small>LeetCode 54 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-hashing-73\"><b>Set Matrix Zeroes<\/b> <small>LeetCode 73 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html"]]},{"i":"t-two-pointers","k":"topic","s":"two-pointers","t":"Two Pointers","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Two Pointers","Two Pointers overview","When to use Two Pointers","Two Pointers template","Two Pointers common mistakes","explain Two Pointers","Two Pointers cheat sheet","Two Pointers notes"],"w":"Two Pointers two pointers topic pattern","a":"<p>Two indexes, one pass, no extra memory. Learn when to walk them toward each other and when to send one ahead.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Two indexes move toward each other (sorted input) or in the same direction (reader/writer) so you never need a nested loop.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sorted + pair/triple to a target<\/b> → L and R at the ends: sum too small → L++, too big → R−−.<\/li><li><b>3Sum<\/b> → sort, fix i, two-pointer the rest; skip equal neighbours to avoid duplicates.<\/li><li><b>Container / trapping water<\/b> → move the shorter side; it's the bottleneck.<\/li><li><b>Remove / compact in place<\/b> → writer <code>w<\/code> and reader <code>r<\/code>; copy kept values to <code>w<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Three-way partition<\/b> (sort colors) → low / mid / high pointers (Dutch flag).<\/li><li><b>Merge two sorted arrays in place<\/b> → fill from the back.<\/li><li><b>Palindrome checks<\/b> → compare from both ends; 'delete one char' → try skipping either side once.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Two pointers needs sorted input (or a monotonic reason to move).<\/li><li>Dedup in 3Sum: skip after a match, not before.<\/li><li>Sorting costs O(n log n); say it.<\/li><li>Off-by-one: <code>while l &lt; r<\/code>, not <code>&lt;=<\/code>, for pairs.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Walk toward each other: Sorted input gives every comparison a direction. Two pointers from the ends squeeze out the answer in one pass.<\/li><li>Read fast, write slow: Both pointers move the same way. One reads every element, the other only advances when something is worth keeping.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Count pointer moves, not loops.<\/li><li>The decoder: Spot the phrase, pick the pointer setup.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"l, r = 0, len(nums) - 1\nwhile l < r:\n    s = nums[l] + nums[r]\n    if s == target:\n        return [l, r]\n    if s < target:\n        l += 1        # need bigger\n    else:\n        r -= 1        # need smaller"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-two-pointers"],["🎬 Two pointers, closing in","#film-two-pointers"]]},{"i":"cx-two-pointers","k":"cx","s":"two-pointers","t":"Two Pointers: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Two Pointers","Two Pointers big o","space complexity Two Pointers","Two Pointers complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Two Pointers complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Converging pair on sorted input<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code> (+ <code>O(n log n)<\/code> to sort)<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>3Sum / 4Sum<\/td><td><code>O(n²)<\/code> / <code>O(n³)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> extra<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Read / write in place<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Dutch national flag<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>, one pass<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Trapping rain water<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-two-pointers","k":"list","s":"two-pointers","t":"All Two Pointers problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Two Pointers problems","Two Pointers questions","which Two Pointers problems should I do","Two Pointers leetcode list","practice problems for Two Pointers"],"w":"Two Pointers problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-125\"><b>Valid Palindrome<\/b> <small>LeetCode 125 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-167\"><b>Two Sum II – Input Array Is Sorted<\/b> <small>LeetCode 167 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-15\"><b>3Sum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 15 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-16\"><b>3Sum Closest<\/b> <small>LeetCode 16 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-18\"><b>4Sum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 18 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-11\"><b>Container With Most Water<\/b> <small>LeetCode 11 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-42\"><b>Trapping Rain Water<\/b> <small>LeetCode 42 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-26\"><b>Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array<\/b> <small>LeetCode 26 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-283\"><b>Move Zeroes<\/b> <small>LeetCode 283 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-75\"><b>Sort Colors<\/b> <small>LeetCode 75 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-88\"><b>Merge Sorted Array<\/b> <small>LeetCode 88 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-977\"><b>Squares of a Sorted Array<\/b> <small>LeetCode 977 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-680\"><b>Valid Palindrome II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 680 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-392\"><b>Is Subsequence<\/b> <small>LeetCode 392 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-two-pointers-881\"><b>Boats to Save People<\/b> <small>LeetCode 881 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html"]]},{"i":"t-sliding-window","k":"topic","s":"sliding-window","t":"Sliding Window","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Sliding Window","Sliding Window overview","When to use Sliding Window","Sliding Window template","Sliding Window common mistakes","explain Sliding Window","Sliding Window cheat sheet","Sliding Window notes"],"w":"Sliding Window sliding window topic pattern","a":"<p>Longest, shortest, at most k: contiguous subarray questions collapse into one expand-and-shrink loop.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> A contiguous range that grows on the right and shrinks on the left, updating a running summary instead of recomputing.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Fixed size k<\/b> → add the new item, drop the item k back.<\/li><li><b>Longest valid<\/b> → expand R every step; while invalid, shrink L; record after fixing.<\/li><li><b>Shortest valid<\/b> → expand R; while valid, record and shrink L.<\/li><li><b>Window state<\/b>: a Counter, a set, a 'need/have' pair, or a running sum.<\/li><li><b>Replace up to k chars<\/b> → window is valid while <code>len − maxCount ≤ k<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Exactly k distinct<\/b> → <code>atMost(k) − atMost(k − 1)<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Max of every window<\/b> → monotonic deque of indexes (decreasing values).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Negative numbers break the 'shrink while sum too big' logic; use prefix sums + dict instead.<\/li><li>Update the answer at the right moment (after shrinking for 'longest').<\/li><li>Each index enters and leaves once → O(n), even with the inner while.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Expand right, shrink left: Variable-size windows for “longest” and “shortest” contiguous answers. One loop, two pointers, each index touched twice at most.<\/li><li>Fixed windows &amp; the deque: When the size is given, add one and drop one. When you need the max of every window, keep a monotonic deque.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: A while inside a for that is still O(n): amortised counting.<\/li><li>The decoder: Is it a window? Which kind?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"l = 0\nfor r, x in enumerate(nums):\n    add(x)                          # expand right\n    while not valid():\n        remove(nums[l])             # shrink left\n        l += 1\n    best = max(best, r - l + 1)     # window [l, r] is valid"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sliding-window"],["🎬 The window that breathes","#film-sliding-window"]]},{"i":"cx-sliding-window","k":"cx","s":"sliding-window","t":"Sliding Window: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Sliding Window","Sliding Window big o","space complexity Sliding Window","Sliding Window complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Sliding Window complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Variable window<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>: each index enters and leaves once<\/td><td><code>O(k)<\/code> or <code>O(alphabet)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Fixed window, running sum<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Fixed window, count compare<\/td><td><code>O(n · 26)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(26)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Sliding max with deque<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Exactly K via atMost<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>, two passes<\/td><td><code>O(k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-sliding-window","k":"list","s":"sliding-window","t":"All Sliding Window problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Sliding Window problems","Sliding Window questions","which Sliding Window problems should I do","Sliding Window leetcode list","practice problems for Sliding Window"],"w":"Sliding Window problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-121\"><b>Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock<\/b> <small>LeetCode 121 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-3\"><b>Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters<\/b> <small>LeetCode 3 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-424\"><b>Longest Repeating Character Replacement<\/b> <small>LeetCode 424 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-567\"><b>Permutation in String<\/b> <small>LeetCode 567 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-438\"><b>Find All Anagrams in a String<\/b> <small>LeetCode 438 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-76\"><b>Minimum Window Substring<\/b> <small>LeetCode 76 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-239\"><b>Sliding Window Maximum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 239 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-209\"><b>Minimum Size Subarray Sum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 209 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-643\"><b>Maximum Average Subarray I<\/b> <small>LeetCode 643 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-904\"><b>Fruit Into Baskets<\/b> <small>LeetCode 904 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-992\"><b>Subarrays with K Different Integers<\/b> <small>LeetCode 992 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-1004\"><b>Max Consecutive Ones III<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1004 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sliding-window-1423\"><b>Maximum Points You Can Obtain from Cards<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1423 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html"]]},{"i":"t-stack","k":"topic","s":"stack","t":"Stack","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Stack","Stack overview","When to use Stack","Stack template","Stack common mistakes","explain Stack","Stack cheat sheet","Stack notes"],"w":"Stack stack topic pattern","a":"<p>Last in, first out. Matching brackets, evaluating expressions, and the monotonic stack that answers “next greater” for every element in one pass.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> A stack holds the most recent unfinished thing; a monotonic stack holds items still waiting for their answer.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Matching pairs / nesting<\/b> → push openers, pop on a closer and check it matches.<\/li><li><b>Evaluate / decode<\/b> → push operands or (string, count) frames; pop when the scope closes.<\/li><li><b>Next greater / warmer<\/b> → monotonic decreasing stack of indexes; a bigger value pops and answers them.<\/li><li><b>Previous smaller + next smaller<\/b> → largest rectangle: each bar's width is between them.<\/li><li><b>Min stack<\/b> → store (value, min so far) pairs.<\/li><li><b>Remove k digits / smallest result<\/b> → monotonic increasing stack, pop while bigger and k &gt; 0.<\/li><li><b>Circular array<\/b> → loop 2n times with <code>i % n<\/code>.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Store indexes, not values, when you need distances or widths.<\/li><li>Flush the stack at the end (sentinel 0 for histograms).<\/li><li>Empty-stack checks before pop / peek.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Last in, first out: A stack remembers the most recent unfinished thing. Brackets, expressions, undo, and nested structures all need exactly that.<\/li><li>The monotonic stack: “Next greater element” for every position in one pass. The stack holds everyone still waiting for their answer.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Each element is pushed once and popped once, however deep the while loop looks.<\/li><li>The decoder: Plain stack or monotonic? Increasing or decreasing?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"stack = []                     # indexes still waiting\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    while stack and nums[stack[-1]] < x:\n        j = stack.pop()\n        answer[j] = x          # x is j's next greater\n    stack.append(i)"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-stack"],["🎬 Last in, first out","#film-stack"]]},{"i":"cx-stack","k":"cx","s":"stack","t":"Stack: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Stack","Stack big o","space complexity Stack","Stack complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Stack complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Bracket matching, RPN, decode<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Min stack operations<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> each<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Monotonic stack (next greater, spans)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>: each index pushed and popped once<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Largest rectangle in histogram<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Maximal rectangle in a matrix<\/td><td><code>O(R · C)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(C)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Stack","stack.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-stack","k":"list","s":"stack","t":"All Stack problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Stack problems","Stack questions","which Stack problems should I do","Stack leetcode list","practice problems for Stack"],"w":"Stack problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-stack-20\"><b>Valid Parentheses<\/b> <small>LeetCode 20 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-155\"><b>Min Stack<\/b> <small>LeetCode 155 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-150\"><b>Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation<\/b> <small>LeetCode 150 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-394\"><b>Decode String<\/b> <small>LeetCode 394 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-71\"><b>Simplify Path<\/b> <small>LeetCode 71 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-844\"><b>Backspace String Compare<\/b> <small>LeetCode 844 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-739\"><b>Daily Temperatures<\/b> <small>LeetCode 739 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-496\"><b>Next Greater Element I<\/b> <small>LeetCode 496 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-503\"><b>Next Greater Element II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 503 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-901\"><b>Online Stock Span<\/b> <small>LeetCode 901 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-853\"><b>Car Fleet<\/b> <small>LeetCode 853 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-84\"><b>Largest Rectangle in Histogram<\/b> <small>LeetCode 84 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-85\"><b>Maximal Rectangle<\/b> <small>LeetCode 85 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-402\"><b>Remove K Digits<\/b> <small>LeetCode 402 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-stack-735\"><b>Asteroid Collision<\/b> <small>LeetCode 735 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html"]]},{"i":"t-binary-search","k":"topic","s":"binary-search","t":"Binary Search","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Binary Search","Binary Search overview","When to use Binary Search","Binary Search template","Binary Search common mistakes","explain Binary Search","Binary Search cheat sheet","Binary Search notes"],"w":"Binary Search binary search topic pattern","a":"<p>Not just “find x in a sorted array”. Any yes/no question that flips once can be binary searched, including the answer itself.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Throw away half the search space each step. Works on any monotonic yes/no question, not just sorted arrays.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Template<\/b>: find the first index where <code>ok(mid)<\/code> is true; <code>lo, hi = 0, n<\/code>; <code>while lo &lt; hi<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>First / last position<\/b> → two lower-bound searches (x and x + 1).<\/li><li><b>Rotated array<\/b> → one half is always sorted; check if the target lies in it.<\/li><li><b>Minimum in rotated<\/b> → compare <code>mid<\/code> with <code>hi<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Binary search on the answer<\/b> (Koko, ship packages, split array) → search the value; <code>ok(v)<\/code> is a greedy check.<\/li><li><b>2D sorted matrix<\/b> → treat as one array: <code>r, c = divmod(mid, cols)<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Peak element<\/b> → move toward the bigger neighbour.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Infinite loop: with <code>lo = mid<\/code> you need <code>mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2<\/code>.<\/li><li>Decide inclusive vs exclusive <code>hi<\/code> once and stick to it.<\/li><li>Search range for 'answer' problems: from the smallest possible to the largest possible value.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Find the boundary: Binary search doesn&#x27;t really find values. It finds the spot where a yes/no answer flips. Get that one idea right and the off-by-one bugs disappear.<\/li><li>Search the answer: When checking a candidate answer is easy but finding it is hard, binary search over the answers themselves.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Halving is the cheapest thing in computer science: a billion items in 30 steps.<\/li><li>The decoder: What&#x27;s the predicate, and where does it flip?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"lo, hi = 0, len(nums)            # answer is in [lo, hi]\nwhile lo < hi:\n    mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n    if condition(mid):           # True for mid and everything after\n        hi = mid                 # mid might be the answer\n    else:\n        lo = mid + 1             # mid is definitely not\nreturn lo                        # first index where condition is True"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-binary-search"],["🎬 Halve it, again and again","#film-binary-search"]]},{"i":"cx-binary-search","k":"cx","s":"binary-search","t":"Binary Search: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Binary Search","Binary Search big o","space complexity Binary Search","Binary Search complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Binary Search complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Search a sorted array<\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Search a sorted R × C matrix as one list<\/td><td><code>O(log(R · C))<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Binary search on the answer<\/td><td><code>O(log(range) × cost of feasible)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Median of two sorted arrays<\/td><td><code>O(log(min(m, n)))<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Kth smallest in sorted matrix<\/td><td><code>O(n · log(max − min))<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Binary Search","binary-search.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-binary-search","k":"list","s":"binary-search","t":"All Binary Search problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Binary Search problems","Binary Search questions","which Binary Search problems should I do","Binary Search leetcode list","practice problems for Binary Search"],"w":"Binary Search problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-704\"><b>Binary Search<\/b> <small>LeetCode 704 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-35\"><b>Search Insert Position<\/b> <small>LeetCode 35 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-34\"><b>First and Last Position<\/b> <small>LeetCode 34 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-74\"><b>Search a 2D Matrix<\/b> <small>LeetCode 74 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-278\"><b>First Bad Version<\/b> <small>LeetCode 278 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-153\"><b>Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array<\/b> <small>LeetCode 153 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-33\"><b>Search in Rotated Sorted Array<\/b> <small>LeetCode 33 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-162\"><b>Find Peak Element<\/b> <small>LeetCode 162 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-540\"><b>Single Element in a Sorted Array<\/b> <small>LeetCode 540 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-875\"><b>Koko Eating Bananas<\/b> <small>LeetCode 875 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-1011\"><b>Capacity To Ship Packages Within D Days<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1011 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-410\"><b>Split Array Largest Sum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 410 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-69\"><b>Sqrt(x)<\/b> <small>LeetCode 69 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-378\"><b>Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix<\/b> <small>LeetCode 378 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-4\"><b>Median of Two Sorted Arrays<\/b> <small>LeetCode 4 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-binary-search-981\"><b>Time Based Key-Value Store<\/b> <small>LeetCode 981 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html"]]},{"i":"t-linked-list","k":"topic","s":"linked-list","t":"Linked List","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Linked List","Linked List overview","When to use Linked List","Linked List template","Linked List common mistakes","explain Linked List","Linked List cheat sheet","Linked List notes"],"w":"Linked List linked list topic pattern","a":"<p>Pointer surgery without losing the patient. Dummy nodes, reversal, fast and slow pointers: three tools that solve nearly every list problem.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Pointer surgery: save next, then cut. A dummy head removes edge cases; fast/slow pointers find middles and cycles.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Reverse<\/b> → <code>prev, cur<\/code>: save <code>nxt<\/code>, point <code>cur.next = prev<\/code>, advance both.<\/li><li><b>Dummy node<\/b> whenever the head might change (merge, remove, partition).<\/li><li><b>Middle<\/b> → slow 1 step, fast 2 steps.<\/li><li><b>Cycle<\/b> → fast meets slow; cycle start: reset one to head and move both 1 step.<\/li><li><b>Nth from end<\/b> → move fast n ahead, then move both.<\/li><li><b>Reorder / palindrome<\/b> → find middle, reverse the second half, merge or compare.<\/li><li><b>LRU cache<\/b> → dict + doubly linked list (move to front on use, evict from back).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Lose the rest of the list by overwriting next before saving it.<\/li><li>Check <code>fast and fast.next<\/code> before jumping 2.<\/li><li>Merge k lists: heap of (value, i, node), the i breaks ties.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Pointer surgery: Rewire nodes without losing any. Save before you cut, use a dummy head, and draw the arrows before you code them.<\/li><li>Fast &amp; slow: Two pointers at different speeds find the middle, detect cycles and locate where the cycle starts, all in O(1) memory.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Count hops along the list, and watch out for recursion’s hidden stack.<\/li><li>The decoder: Which of the three tools does this need?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"dummy = ListNode(0, head)       # head may change? use a dummy\nprev, curr = None, head\nwhile curr:\n    nxt = curr.next             # 1. save\n    curr.next = prev            # 2. rewire\n    prev, curr = curr, nxt      # 3. step"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-linked-list"],["🎬 Reverse a linked list","#film-linked-list"]]},{"i":"cx-linked-list","k":"cx","s":"linked-list","t":"Linked List: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Linked List","Linked List big o","space complexity Linked List","Linked List complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Linked List complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technique<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Reverse (iterative)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Merge two sorted<\/td><td><code>O(m + n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Fast / slow (middle, cycle)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Merge k sorted with a heap<\/td><td><code>O(N log k)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Sort list (merge sort)<\/td><td><code>O(n log n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code> stack<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>LRU get / put<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(capacity)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Linked List","linked-list.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-linked-list","k":"list","s":"linked-list","t":"All Linked List problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Linked List problems","Linked List questions","which Linked List problems should I do","Linked List leetcode list","practice problems for Linked List"],"w":"Linked List problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-206\"><b>Reverse Linked List<\/b> <small>LeetCode 206 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-21\"><b>Merge Two Sorted Lists<\/b> <small>LeetCode 21 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-141\"><b>Linked List Cycle<\/b> <small>LeetCode 141 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-142\"><b>Linked List Cycle II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 142 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-876\"><b>Middle of the Linked List<\/b> <small>LeetCode 876 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-19\"><b>Remove Nth Node From End<\/b> <small>LeetCode 19 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-143\"><b>Reorder List<\/b> <small>LeetCode 143 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-234\"><b>Palindrome Linked List<\/b> <small>LeetCode 234 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-2\"><b>Add Two Numbers<\/b> <small>LeetCode 2 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-138\"><b>Copy List with Random Pointer<\/b> <small>LeetCode 138 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-160\"><b>Intersection of Two Linked Lists<\/b> <small>LeetCode 160 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-61\"><b>Rotate List<\/b> <small>LeetCode 61 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-328\"><b>Odd Even Linked List<\/b> <small>LeetCode 328 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-148\"><b>Sort List<\/b> <small>LeetCode 148 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-23\"><b>Merge k Sorted Lists<\/b> <small>LeetCode 23 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-25\"><b>Reverse Nodes in k-Group<\/b> <small>LeetCode 25 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-linked-list-146\"><b>LRU Cache<\/b> <small>LeetCode 146 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html"]]},{"i":"t-recursion","k":"topic","s":"recursion","t":"Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Recursion & Backtracking","Recursion & Backtracking overview","When to use Recursion & Backtracking","Recursion & Backtracking template","Recursion & Backtracking common mistakes","explain Recursion & Backtracking","Recursion & Backtracking cheat sheet","Recursion & Backtracking notes"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking recursion topic pattern","a":"<p>Recursion, binary trees and backtracking, one call at a time. Every idea has Python you can read and a visual you can step through.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Trust the function: solve for one node / one choice, assume the recursive call handles the smaller problem.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Three questions<\/b>: base case? what do I ask my children? what do I return?<\/li><li><b>Info flows down<\/b> (pass params: depth, range, path) or <b>up<\/b> (return values: height, gain, found).<\/li><li><b>'Best path through a node'<\/b> → return the best one-sided value, update a global with both sides.<\/li><li><b>BST<\/b> → inorder is sorted; validate with a (low, high) range.<\/li><li><b>Backtracking<\/b>: choose → explore → un-choose; record a <i>copy<\/i> of the path.<\/li><li><b>Order doesn't matter<\/b> → start index; <b>order matters<\/b> → used[]; reuse allowed → recurse with i, else i + 1.<\/li><li><b>Duplicates<\/b> → sort; skip <code>i &gt; start and a[i] == a[i−1]<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Cost<\/b>: time ≈ nodes in the recursion tree × work per node; space = max depth.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Forgetting to undo the choice.<\/li><li>Appending <code>path<\/code> instead of <code>path[:]<\/code>.<\/li><li>Calling the same subproblem twice (<code>pow(n//2) * pow(n//2)<\/code>) → exponential.<\/li><li>Python recursion limit ~1000: deep lists/trees may need iteration.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Trust the function: What recursion actually is, how the call stack holds it, and the one habit that makes it easy.<\/li><li>Trees are recursion you can see: Every node is the root of a smaller tree. Learn the three visits, the two directions, then the problems interviewers love.<\/li><li>Choose, explore, un-choose: Backtracking is a depth-first walk over a tree of decisions. One template, four questions, two tricks, and every backtracking problem starts to look the same.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Time and space for any recursive function, by drawing the tree and adding up its levels. No formulas to memorise.<\/li><li>The decoder: Read the problem, spot the phrase, reach for the template. Then prove it with the final round.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"def backtrack(state):\n    if is_goal(state):\n        res.append(copy(state))      # snapshot, never the live list\n        return\n    for choice in choices(state):\n        if not valid(choice):\n            continue                 # prune\n        make(choice)                 # choose\n        backtrack(state)             # explore\n        undo(choice)                 # un-choose"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-recursion"],["🎬 The decision tree","#film-recursion"]]},{"i":"cx-recursion","k":"cx","s":"recursion","t":"Recursion & Backtracking: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Recursion & Backtracking","Recursion & Backtracking big o","space complexity Recursion & Backtracking","Recursion & Backtracking complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table> <thead><tr><th>Problem<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Why<\/th><\/tr><\/thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Tree traversal / most tree DP<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>, space <code>O(h)<\/code><\/td><td>Each node once; stack as deep as the tree<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Subsets<\/td><td><code>O(n · 2ⁿ)<\/code><\/td><td>2ⁿ subsets, copying each costs up to n<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Permutations<\/td><td><code>O(n · n!)<\/code><\/td><td>n! leaves, copy of length n each<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Combinations (n choose k)<\/td><td><code>O(k · C(n, k))<\/code><\/td><td>One copy per combination<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Generate parentheses<\/td><td><code>O(4ⁿ / √n)<\/code><\/td><td>Catalan number of valid strings<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>N-Queens<\/td><td><code>O(n!)<\/code><\/td><td>Each row has fewer safe columns than the last<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Word search<\/td><td><code>O(R · C · 3ᴸ)<\/code><\/td><td>Every start, then 3 new directions per letter<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody> <\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-recursion","k":"list","s":"recursion","t":"All Recursion & Backtracking problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Recursion & Backtracking problems","Recursion & Backtracking questions","which Recursion & Backtracking problems should I do","Recursion & Backtracking leetcode list","practice problems for Recursion & Backtracking"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-50\"><b>Pow(x, n)<\/b> <small>LeetCode 50 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-206\"><b>Reverse Linked List (recursive)<\/b> <small>LeetCode 206 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-21\"><b>Merge Two Sorted Lists<\/b> <small>LeetCode 21 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-24\"><b>Swap Nodes in Pairs<\/b> <small>LeetCode 24 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-classic\"><b>Tower of Hanoi<\/b> <small>Classic · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-104\"><b>Maximum Depth of Binary Tree<\/b> <small>LeetCode 104 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-226\"><b>Invert Binary Tree<\/b> <small>LeetCode 226 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-101\"><b>Symmetric Tree<\/b> <small>LeetCode 101 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-543\"><b>Diameter of Binary Tree<\/b> <small>LeetCode 543 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-113\"><b>Path Sum II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 113 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-236\"><b>Lowest Common Ancestor<\/b> <small>LeetCode 236 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-98\"><b>Validate Binary Search Tree<\/b> <small>LeetCode 98 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-105\"><b>Construct Tree from Preorder and Inorder<\/b> <small>LeetCode 105 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-124\"><b>Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 124 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-114\"><b>Flatten Binary Tree to Linked List<\/b> <small>LeetCode 114 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-199\"><b>Binary Tree Right Side View<\/b> <small>LeetCode 199 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-103\"><b>Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order<\/b> <small>LeetCode 103 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-230\"><b>Kth Smallest Element in a BST<\/b> <small>LeetCode 230 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-235\"><b>Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST<\/b> <small>LeetCode 235 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-572\"><b>Subtree of Another Tree<\/b> <small>LeetCode 572 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-297\"><b>Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree<\/b> <small>LeetCode 297 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-437\"><b>Path Sum III<\/b> <small>LeetCode 437 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-863\"><b>All Nodes Distance K in Binary Tree<\/b> <small>LeetCode 863 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-108\"><b>Convert Sorted Array to BST<\/b> <small>LeetCode 108 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-450\"><b>Delete Node in a BST<\/b> <small>LeetCode 450 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-337\"><b>House Robber III<\/b> <small>LeetCode 337 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-129\"><b>Sum Root to Leaf Numbers<\/b> <small>LeetCode 129 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-968\"><b>Binary Tree Cameras<\/b> <small>LeetCode 968 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-77\"><b>Combinations<\/b> <small>LeetCode 77 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-40\"><b>Combination Sum II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 40 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-47\"><b>Permutations II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 47 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-17\"><b>Letter Combinations of a Phone Number<\/b> <small>LeetCode 17 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-131\"><b>Palindrome Partitioning<\/b> <small>LeetCode 131 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-37\"><b>Sudoku Solver<\/b> <small>LeetCode 37 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-784\"><b>Letter Case Permutation<\/b> <small>LeetCode 784 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-494\"><b>Target Sum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 494 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-52\"><b>N-Queens II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 52 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-698\"><b>Partition to K Equal Sum Subsets<\/b> <small>LeetCode 698 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-1219\"><b>Path with Maximum Gold<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1219 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-980\"><b>Unique Paths III<\/b> <small>LeetCode 980 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-212\"><b>Word Search II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 212 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-recursion-282\"><b>Expression Add Operators<\/b> <small>LeetCode 282 · Hard<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html"]]},{"i":"t-heap","k":"topic","s":"heap","t":"Heap","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Heap","Heap overview","When to use Heap","Heap template","Heap common mistakes","explain Heap","Heap cheat sheet","Heap notes"],"w":"Heap heap topic pattern","a":"<p>Always grab the best item next, in O(log n). Top K, merging sorted streams, running medians and scheduling all live here.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> A heap gives you the smallest (or largest) item in O(log n), perfect for 'top k', 'next best' and merging sorted streams.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Python <code>heapq<\/code> is a min-heap<\/b>; push negatives for a max-heap.<\/li><li><b>Top k largest<\/b> → min-heap of size k; pop when it grows past k. O(n log k).<\/li><li><b>Kth largest in a stream<\/b> → the same size-k min-heap; its top is the answer.<\/li><li><b>Merge k sorted<\/b> → heap of the current head of each list.<\/li><li><b>Running median<\/b> → max-heap for the low half, min-heap for the high half, sizes differ by ≤ 1.<\/li><li><b>Scheduling<\/b> (tasks, rooms) → heap of end times / counts; greedily take the best available.<\/li><li><b>Heapify<\/b> a list is O(n).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Tuples compare element by element: add an index to break ties between uncomparable items.<\/li><li>Size-k heap for 'k largest' is a <i>min<\/i>-heap.<\/li><li>Quickselect is average O(n) but worst O(n²).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>How a heap works: A complete binary tree stored in a plain list, where every parent beats its children. That&#x27;s enough to give you the best item in O(1) and update in O(log n).<\/li><li>Top K, merging, medians: Keep only what matters, always pull the best available, and balance two halves.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Everything costs the height of the tree, and the height is log n.<\/li><li>The decoder: Which heap, holding what, capped at what size?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n \nheap = []\nfor x in nums:\n    heapq.heappush(heap, x)\n    if len(heap) > k:\n        heapq.heappop(heap)     # evict the weakest\nreturn heap[0]                  # kth largest"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-heap"],["🎬 The heap keeps its promise","#film-heap"]]},{"i":"cx-heap","k":"cx","s":"heap","t":"Heap: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Heap","Heap big o","space complexity Heap","Heap complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Heap complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation / pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>push / pop<\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>peek (heap[0])<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>heapify a list<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Top k with a size-k heap<\/td><td><code>O(n log k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Merge k sorted, N items total<\/td><td><code>O(N log k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Running median<\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code> add, <code>O(1)<\/code> query<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Heap","heap.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-heap","k":"list","s":"heap","t":"All Heap problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Heap problems","Heap questions","which Heap problems should I do","Heap leetcode list","practice problems for Heap"],"w":"Heap problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-heap-703\"><b>Kth Largest Element in a Stream<\/b> <small>LeetCode 703 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-1046\"><b>Last Stone Weight<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1046 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-973\"><b>K Closest Points to Origin<\/b> <small>LeetCode 973 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-215\"><b>Kth Largest Element in an Array<\/b> <small>LeetCode 215 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-347\"><b>Top K Frequent Elements<\/b> <small>LeetCode 347 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-621\"><b>Task Scheduler<\/b> <small>LeetCode 621 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-767\"><b>Reorganize String<\/b> <small>LeetCode 767 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-355\"><b>Design Twitter<\/b> <small>LeetCode 355 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-295\"><b>Find Median from Data Stream<\/b> <small>LeetCode 295 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-23\"><b>Merge k Sorted Lists<\/b> <small>LeetCode 23 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-502\"><b>IPO<\/b> <small>LeetCode 502 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-1642\"><b>Furthest Building You Can Reach<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1642 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-heap-253\"><b>Meeting Rooms II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 253 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html"]]},{"i":"t-trie","k":"topic","s":"trie","t":"Trie","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Trie","Trie overview","When to use Trie","Trie template","Trie common mistakes","explain Trie","Trie cheat sheet","Trie notes"],"w":"Trie trie topic pattern","a":"<p>A tree of characters where every path is a prefix. Autocomplete, word dictionaries and many-words-on-one-board problems get fast here.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> A tree of characters: every path from the root spells a prefix, so prefix questions cost O(length of word).<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Node<\/b> = <code>children<\/code> dict + <code>end<\/code> flag (or the word itself).<\/li><li><b>Insert / search / startsWith<\/b> → walk one char at a time.<\/li><li><b>Wildcard '.'<\/b> → DFS over all children at that position.<\/li><li><b>Many words on a board<\/b> → build a trie of words, one DFS over the board, prune dead prefixes.<\/li><li><b>Autocomplete<\/b> → walk to the prefix node, then collect (or keep the top few at each node).<\/li><li><b>Max XOR pair<\/b> → bitwise trie; at each bit try to go the opposite way.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Mark word ends; a prefix is not a word.<\/li><li>Remove found words in Word Search II to avoid duplicates.<\/li><li>Space can be large: O(total characters).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>A tree of prefixes: Every path from the root spells a prefix. Words that share a beginning share the path, so prefix questions cost O(length of the prefix), no matter how many words you store.<\/li><li>Searching beyond exact words: Wildcards, autocomplete, many words on one board, and bits instead of letters.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Cost depends on the word’s length, not on how many words are stored.<\/li><li>The decoder: Is a trie really needed, and what does each node carry?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"class TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.children = {}      # char -> TrieNode\n        self.end = False        # a word ends here\n \nnode = root\nfor ch in word:\n    node = node.children.setdefault(ch, TrieNode())\nnode.end = True"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-trie"],["🎬 Words that share a path","#film-trie"]]},{"i":"cx-trie","k":"cx","s":"trie","t":"Trie: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Trie","Trie big o","space complexity Trie","Trie complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Trie complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Insert / search / startsWith<\/td><td><code>O(L)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(total characters)<\/code> overall<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Wildcard search<\/td><td><code>O(26ᵈ · L)<\/code> worst, d = number of dots<\/td><td><code>O(L)<\/code> stack<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Autocomplete under a prefix<\/td><td><code>O(L + size of the subtree)<\/code><\/td><td><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Max XOR pair (binary trie)<\/td><td><code>O(n · 32)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n · 32)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Word Search II<\/td><td><code>O(R · C · 4 · 3ᴸ⁻¹)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(total characters)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Trie","trie.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-trie","k":"list","s":"trie","t":"All Trie problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Trie problems","Trie questions","which Trie problems should I do","Trie leetcode list","practice problems for Trie"],"w":"Trie problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-trie-208\"><b>Implement Trie (Prefix Tree)<\/b> <small>LeetCode 208 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-trie-211\"><b>Design Add and Search Words<\/b> <small>LeetCode 211 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-trie-212\"><b>Word Search II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 212 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-trie-14\"><b>Longest Common Prefix<\/b> <small>LeetCode 14 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-trie-648\"><b>Replace Words<\/b> <small>LeetCode 648 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-trie-1268\"><b>Search Suggestions System<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1268 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-trie-421\"><b>Maximum XOR of Two Numbers<\/b> <small>LeetCode 421 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-trie-720\"><b>Longest Word in Dictionary<\/b> <small>LeetCode 720 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-trie-677\"><b>Map Sum Pairs<\/b> <small>LeetCode 677 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html"]]},{"i":"t-graphs","k":"topic","s":"graphs","t":"Graphs","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Graphs","Graphs overview","When to use Graphs","Graphs template","Graphs common mistakes","explain Graphs","Graphs cheat sheet","Graphs notes"],"w":"Graphs graphs topic pattern","a":"<p>Grids, dependencies, networks. BFS, DFS, topological sort, union-find and Dijkstra, with a reflex for when to reach for each.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Nodes + edges. Pick the traversal by the question: BFS for fewest steps, DFS for 'explore everything', topological sort for order, union-find for groups, Dijkstra for weighted shortest paths.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Grid<\/b> = graph: neighbours are the 4 directions; mark visited when you push.<\/li><li><b>Count islands / regions<\/b> → flood fill from every unvisited cell.<\/li><li><b>Fewest steps, unweighted<\/b> → BFS; many starts → multi-source BFS (all in the queue at time 0).<\/li><li><b>Prerequisites / order<\/b> → topological sort (Kahn: in-degree 0 queue). Fewer than n popped → cycle.<\/li><li><b>Connected groups / redundant edge<\/b> → union-find with path compression.<\/li><li><b>Weighted shortest path<\/b> → Dijkstra (heap of (dist, node), skip stale entries). ≤ k edges → Bellman-Ford k+1 rounds.<\/li><li><b>Minimum spanning tree<\/b> → Prim (heap) or Kruskal (sorted edges + union-find).<\/li><li><b>Reverse thinking<\/b>: start from the border / the goal (Pacific-Atlantic, surrounded regions).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Mark visited on push, not on pop, or BFS revisits nodes.<\/li><li>Dijkstra fails with negative edges.<\/li><li>Directed vs undirected: add both directions for undirected.<\/li><li>Trees need parent pointers to go 'up' (distance k).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Traverse anything: Grids, networks and dependency lists are all graphs. Learn to see the nodes and edges, then BFS or DFS does the rest.<\/li><li>Order &amp; groups: Topological sort orders tasks with dependencies. Union-find tracks which nodes are connected as edges arrive.<\/li><li>Shortest paths &amp; spanning trees: Weighted edges need more than BFS. Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford with a stop limit, and Prim&#x27;s minimum spanning tree.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Every vertex once, every edge once: O(V + E).<\/li><li>The decoder: What are the nodes, what are the edges, and which algorithm fits?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n \nseen = {start}\nq = deque([start])\nwhile q:\n    node = q.popleft()\n    for nb in graph[node]:\n        if nb not in seen:\n            seen.add(nb)          # mark when enqueued\n            q.append(nb)"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-graphs"],["🎬 The BFS wave","#film-graphs"]]},{"i":"cx-graphs","k":"cx","s":"graphs","t":"Graphs: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Graphs","Graphs big o","space complexity Graphs","Graphs complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Graphs complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Use when<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>BFS / DFS<\/td><td><code>O(V + E)<\/code><\/td><td>Reachability, components, unweighted shortest path<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Kahn’s topological sort<\/td><td><code>O(V + E)<\/code><\/td><td>Ordering with dependencies, directed cycle check<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Union-find (path compression + rank)<\/td><td><code>~O(α(n))<\/code> per op<\/td><td>Dynamic connectivity, redundant edges<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Dijkstra with a heap<\/td><td><code>O(E log V)<\/code><\/td><td>Non-negative weights<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Bellman-Ford (k rounds)<\/td><td><code>O(k · E)<\/code><\/td><td>Negative weights or a stop limit<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Prim / Kruskal MST<\/td><td><code>O(E log V)<\/code><\/td><td>Cheapest way to connect everything<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Graphs","graphs.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-graphs","k":"list","s":"graphs","t":"All Graphs problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Graphs problems","Graphs questions","which Graphs problems should I do","Graphs leetcode list","practice problems for Graphs"],"w":"Graphs problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-200\"><b>Number of Islands<\/b> <small>LeetCode 200 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-695\"><b>Max Area of Island<\/b> <small>LeetCode 695 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-994\"><b>Rotting Oranges<\/b> <small>LeetCode 994 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-286\"><b>Walls and Gates<\/b> <small>LeetCode 286 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-417\"><b>Pacific Atlantic Water Flow<\/b> <small>LeetCode 417 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-130\"><b>Surrounded Regions<\/b> <small>LeetCode 130 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-133\"><b>Clone Graph<\/b> <small>LeetCode 133 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-785\"><b>Is Graph Bipartite?<\/b> <small>LeetCode 785 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-127\"><b>Word Ladder<\/b> <small>LeetCode 127 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-207\"><b>Course Schedule<\/b> <small>LeetCode 207 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-210\"><b>Course Schedule II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 210 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-269\"><b>Alien Dictionary<\/b> <small>LeetCode 269 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-323\"><b>Number of Connected Components<\/b> <small>LeetCode 323 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-684\"><b>Redundant Connection<\/b> <small>LeetCode 684 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-261\"><b>Graph Valid Tree<\/b> <small>LeetCode 261 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-743\"><b>Network Delay Time<\/b> <small>LeetCode 743 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-787\"><b>Cheapest Flights Within K Stops<\/b> <small>LeetCode 787 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-1584\"><b>Min Cost to Connect All Points<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1584 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-graphs-778\"><b>Swim in Rising Water<\/b> <small>LeetCode 778 · Hard<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html"]]},{"i":"t-dp","k":"topic","s":"dp","t":"Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Dynamic Programming","Dynamic Programming overview","When to use Dynamic Programming","Dynamic Programming template","Dynamic Programming common mistakes","explain Dynamic Programming","Dynamic Programming cheat sheet","Dynamic Programming notes"],"w":"Dynamic Programming dp topic pattern","a":"<p>Recursion that remembers. Find the state, write the recurrence, fill the table. The same four steps crack every DP problem.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Recursion that remembers. Define a state, write how it depends on smaller states, then fill a table (or memoize).<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Recipe<\/b>: state → recurrence (what was the last decision?) → base case → order → answer.<\/li><li><b>1-D<\/b>: climbing stairs, house robber, decode ways: <code>dp[i]<\/code> from <code>dp[i−1], dp[i−2]<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Unbounded knapsack<\/b> (coin change): loop amounts, reuse coins. Combinations count → coins outer loop.<\/li><li><b>0/1 knapsack<\/b> (partition, target sum): loop sums <i>backwards<\/i> so each item is used once.<\/li><li><b>Two strings<\/b> (LCS, edit distance): <code>dp[i][j]<\/code> on prefixes; match → diagonal.<\/li><li><b>Grid paths<\/b>: <code>dp[r][c] = top + left<\/code>; one row of memory is enough.<\/li><li><b>Intervals<\/b> (burst balloons, palindromes): fill by increasing length; think of the last action.<\/li><li><b>State machines<\/b> (stock with cooldown): one variable per state per day.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Greedy looks right for coin change but fails.<\/li><li>Off-by-one: use size n + 1 tables with an empty-prefix row.<\/li><li>Say time = states × work per state.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Recursion that remembers: DP isn&#x27;t a new algorithm. It&#x27;s the recursion you already know, plus a notebook so you never solve the same subproblem twice.<\/li><li>1-D patterns: A handful of shapes cover most one-dimensional DP: take or skip, best way to reach an amount, best run ending here.<\/li><li>2-D tables: Two strings, a grid, or items × capacity. The table is a map of subproblems, and each cell reads its neighbours.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: States × work per state. That’s the whole formula.<\/li><li>The decoder: Is it DP? What&#x27;s the state? Which shape?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"# 1. state:      dp[i] = best using the first i houses\n# 2. recurrence: last decision was skip or take\n# 3. base:       dp[0] = 0, dp[1] = nums[0]\n# 4. order:      small i first, so inputs are ready\nfor i in range(2, n + 1):\n    dp[i] = max(dp[i - 1], dp[i - 2] + nums[i - 1])"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-dp"],["🎬 When greedy lies, DP remembers","#film-dp"]]},{"i":"cx-dp","k":"cx","s":"dp","t":"Dynamic Programming: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Dynamic Programming","Dynamic Programming big o","space complexity Dynamic Programming","Dynamic Programming complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Dynamic Programming complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Shape<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>1-D, constant work per state<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> with rolling variables<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Coin change / unbounded knapsack<\/td><td><code>O(amount · coins)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(amount)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>LIS<\/td><td><code>O(n²)<\/code>, or <code>O(n log n)<\/code> with tails + binary search<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Two strings / grid<\/td><td><code>O(m · n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code> with one rolling row<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>0/1 knapsack, subset sum<\/td><td><code>O(n · capacity)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(capacity)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Interval DP (burst balloons)<\/td><td><code>O(n³)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n²)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Dynamic Programming","dp.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-dp","k":"list","s":"dp","t":"All Dynamic Programming problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Dynamic Programming problems","Dynamic Programming questions","which Dynamic Programming problems should I do","Dynamic Programming leetcode list","practice problems for Dynamic Programming"],"w":"Dynamic Programming problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-dp-70\"><b>Climbing Stairs<\/b> <small>LeetCode 70 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-746\"><b>Min Cost Climbing Stairs<\/b> <small>LeetCode 746 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-198\"><b>House Robber<\/b> <small>LeetCode 198 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-213\"><b>House Robber II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 213 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-91\"><b>Decode Ways<\/b> <small>LeetCode 91 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-152\"><b>Maximum Product Subarray<\/b> <small>LeetCode 152 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-300\"><b>Longest Increasing Subsequence<\/b> <small>LeetCode 300 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-5\"><b>Longest Palindromic Substring<\/b> <small>LeetCode 5 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-647\"><b>Palindromic Substrings<\/b> <small>LeetCode 647 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-139\"><b>Word Break<\/b> <small>LeetCode 139 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-322\"><b>Coin Change<\/b> <small>LeetCode 322 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-518\"><b>Coin Change II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 518 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-416\"><b>Partition Equal Subset Sum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 416 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-494\"><b>Target Sum<\/b> <small>LeetCode 494 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-62\"><b>Unique Paths<\/b> <small>LeetCode 62 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-1143\"><b>Longest Common Subsequence<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1143 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-72\"><b>Edit Distance<\/b> <small>LeetCode 72 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-309\"><b>Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown<\/b> <small>LeetCode 309 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-dp-312\"><b>Burst Balloons<\/b> <small>LeetCode 312 · Hard<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html"]]},{"i":"t-greedy-intervals","k":"topic","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Greedy & Intervals","Greedy & Intervals overview","When to use Greedy & Intervals","Greedy & Intervals template","Greedy & Intervals common mistakes","explain Greedy & Intervals","Greedy & Intervals cheat sheet","Greedy & Intervals notes"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals greedy intervals topic pattern","a":"<p>Take the locally best move and never look back, when you can prove it&#x27;s safe. Plus the sort-then-sweep tricks behind every interval problem.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Make the locally best choice when you can argue it never hurts. For intervals, sorting is the whole trick.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Max subarray<\/b> → Kadane: <code>cur = max(x, cur + x)<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Reachability<\/b> (jump game) → track the farthest reachable index.<\/li><li><b>Gas station<\/b> → if total ≥ 0 an answer exists; restart after the tank goes negative.<\/li><li><b>Merge intervals<\/b> → sort by start; extend the last or append.<\/li><li><b>Max non-overlapping / min removals / arrows<\/b> → sort by end; keep the earliest-ending.<\/li><li><b>Meeting rooms<\/b> → sort starts and ends (or a heap of end times).<\/li><li><b>Two-sided constraints<\/b> (candy) → one pass left-to-right, one right-to-left.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Greedy needs a reason: say why the choice is safe (exchange argument).<\/li><li>Touching intervals: does [1,2] overlap [2,3]? Ask.<\/li><li>Sort by start to merge, by end to schedule.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Take the best move, never look back: Greedy algorithms commit to the locally best choice. They&#x27;re short and fast, but only correct when you can argue the choice never hurts later.<\/li><li>Intervals: Sort, then sweep. The only real decision is whether to sort by start or by end.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: The sort costs more than the greedy itself.<\/li><li>The decoder: Greedy or DP? Sort by what?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"intervals.sort()                       # merge: sort by START\nres = [intervals[0]]\nfor s, e in intervals[1:]:\n    if s <= res[-1][1]:\n        res[-1][1] = max(res[-1][1], e)\n    else:\n        res.append([s, e])\n# keep the most / remove the fewest: sort by END"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-greedy-intervals"],["🎬 Sweep and merge","#film-greedy-intervals"]]},{"i":"cx-greedy-intervals","k":"cx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Greedy & Intervals: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Greedy & Intervals","Greedy & Intervals big o","space complexity Greedy & Intervals","Greedy & Intervals complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>One-pass greedy (reach, Kadane, gas station)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>, <code>O(1)<\/code> space<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Sort + sweep (merge, non-overlap, arrows)<\/td><td><code>O(n log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Meeting rooms (sweep or heap)<\/td><td><code>O(n log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Insert interval (already sorted)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Offline queries with a heap<\/td><td><code>O((n + q) log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-greedy-intervals","k":"list","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"All Greedy & Intervals problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Greedy & Intervals problems","Greedy & Intervals questions","which Greedy & Intervals problems should I do","Greedy & Intervals leetcode list","practice problems for Greedy & Intervals"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-53\"><b>Maximum Subarray<\/b> <small>LeetCode 53 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-55\"><b>Jump Game<\/b> <small>LeetCode 55 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-45\"><b>Jump Game II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 45 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-134\"><b>Gas Station<\/b> <small>LeetCode 134 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-846\"><b>Hand of Straights<\/b> <small>LeetCode 846 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-1899\"><b>Merge Triplets to Form Target<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1899 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-763\"><b>Partition Labels<\/b> <small>LeetCode 763 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-678\"><b>Valid Parenthesis String<\/b> <small>LeetCode 678 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-135\"><b>Candy<\/b> <small>LeetCode 135 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-1029\"><b>Two City Scheduling<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1029 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-56\"><b>Merge Intervals<\/b> <small>LeetCode 56 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-57\"><b>Insert Interval<\/b> <small>LeetCode 57 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-435\"><b>Non-overlapping Intervals<\/b> <small>LeetCode 435 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-452\"><b>Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons<\/b> <small>LeetCode 452 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-253\"><b>Meeting Rooms II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 253 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-greedy-intervals-1851\"><b>Minimum Interval to Include Each Query<\/b> <small>LeetCode 1851 · Hard<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html"]]},{"i":"t-bits","k":"topic","s":"bits","t":"Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA topics","q":["What is Bit Manipulation","Bit Manipulation overview","When to use Bit Manipulation","Bit Manipulation template","Bit Manipulation common mistakes","explain Bit Manipulation","Bit Manipulation cheat sheet","Bit Manipulation notes"],"w":"Bit Manipulation bits topic pattern","a":"<p>XOR cancels pairs, n &amp; (n − 1) erases a bit, masks list every subset. Small tricks that turn into one-line answers.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Numbers are columns of bits. XOR cancels pairs, AND masks, shifts move bits, and n & (n − 1) clears the lowest set bit.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>x ^ x = 0, x ^ 0 = x<\/b> → find the single number; missing number.<\/li><li><b>n & (n − 1)<\/b> → drop the lowest 1 bit; power of two if result is 0.<\/li><li><b>n & −n<\/b> → isolate the lowest 1 bit (split into two groups).<\/li><li><b>Test / set / clear bit i<\/b>: <code>n &gt;&gt; i & 1<\/code>, <code>n | 1 &lt;&lt; i<\/code>, <code>n & ~(1 &lt;&lt; i)<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Subsets<\/b> → masks 0..2ⁿ − 1.<\/li><li><b>Counting bits DP<\/b>: <code>bits[i] = bits[i &gt;&gt; 1] + (i & 1)<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Add without +<\/b>: XOR = sum without carry, AND &lt;&lt; 1 = carry.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Python ints are unbounded: mask with <code>0xFFFFFFFF<\/code> for 32-bit behaviour.<\/li><li>Operator precedence: wrap bit expressions in parentheses.<\/li><li>Negative numbers in Python shift arithmetically.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Five tricks that do everything: XOR cancels pairs, n &amp; (n − 1) erases a bit, x &amp; −x isolates one, shifts move bits around, and masks test them.<\/li><li>Masks: a number is a subset: n bits can stand for n yes/no choices. Count from 0 to 2ⁿ − 1 and you&#x27;ve listed every subset.<\/li><li>Counting the cost: Fixed-width integers make most bit tricks O(1), and loops over bits O(log n).<\/li><li>The decoder: Which trick does this one need?<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"py":"acc = 0\nfor x in nums:\n    acc ^= x              # pairs cancel: a ^ a = 0\nn &= n - 1                # erase the lowest set bit\nlow = x & -x              # keep only the lowest set bit\nfor mask in range(1 << k):     # every subset of k items\n    ..."},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-bits"],["🎬 XOR makes pairs vanish","#film-bits"]]},{"i":"cx-bits","k":"cx","s":"bits","t":"Bit Manipulation: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Bit Manipulation","Bit Manipulation big o","space complexity Bit Manipulation","Bit Manipulation complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Bit Manipulation complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Expression<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td><code>x &amp; 1<\/code><\/td><td>Is x odd? (lowest bit)<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x &gt;&gt; i &amp; 1<\/code><\/td><td>Bit i of x<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x | (1 &lt;&lt; i)<\/code> / <code>x &amp; ~(1 &lt;&lt; i)<\/code><\/td><td>Set / clear bit i<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x ^ (1 &lt;&lt; i)<\/code><\/td><td>Toggle bit i<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x &amp; (x − 1)<\/code><\/td><td>Clear the lowest set bit<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x &amp; −x<\/code><\/td><td>Keep only the lowest set bit<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x &gt; 0 and x &amp; (x − 1) == 0<\/code><\/td><td>Power of two<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>a ^ b ^ b<\/code><\/td><td>a (pairs cancel)<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Bit Manipulation","bits.html#chcost"]]},{"i":"pl-bits","k":"list","s":"bits","t":"All Bit Manipulation problems","cat":"DSA topics","q":["list of Bit Manipulation problems","Bit Manipulation questions","which Bit Manipulation problems should I do","Bit Manipulation leetcode list","practice problems for Bit Manipulation"],"w":"Bit Manipulation problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-bits-136\"><b>Single Number<\/b> <small>LeetCode 136 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-191\"><b>Number of 1 Bits<\/b> <small>LeetCode 191 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-338\"><b>Counting Bits<\/b> <small>LeetCode 338 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-190\"><b>Reverse Bits<\/b> <small>LeetCode 190 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-268\"><b>Missing Number<\/b> <small>LeetCode 268 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-371\"><b>Sum of Two Integers<\/b> <small>LeetCode 371 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-231\"><b>Power of Two<\/b> <small>LeetCode 231 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-461\"><b>Hamming Distance<\/b> <small>LeetCode 461 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-137\"><b>Single Number II<\/b> <small>LeetCode 137 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-260\"><b>Single Number III<\/b> <small>LeetCode 260 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-78\"><b>Subsets (bitmask)<\/b> <small>LeetCode 78 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-201\"><b>Bitwise AND of Numbers Range<\/b> <small>LeetCode 201 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-bits-89\"><b>Gray Code<\/b> <small>LeetCode 89 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-start","k":"topic","s":"sd-start","t":"Start Here: Design Thinking","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is Start Here: Design Thinking","Start Here: Design Thinking overview","When to use Start Here: Design Thinking","Start Here: Design Thinking template","Start Here: Design Thinking common mistakes","explain Start Here: Design Thinking","Start Here: Design Thinking cheat sheet","Start Here: Design Thinking notes"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking sd start topic pattern","a":"<p>Before patterns and boxes: the one idea every design decision comes from, how to read a UML class diagram in five minutes, and a map of how the whole track links together.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Every design answers one question: what will change? Put the thing that changes behind an interface.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>A box is a class<\/b>: name, fields (−private), methods (+public).<\/li><li><b>Six arrows<\/b>: inheritance ▷ (is-a), implementation ▷ dashed, composition ◆ (owns, dies with), aggregation ◇ (has, lives on), association →, dependency ⇢ (uses).<\/li><li><b>LLD round<\/b>: classes, relationships, patterns, working code, concurrency.<\/li><li><b>HLD round<\/b>: requirements, numbers, API, data model, architecture, deep dive.<\/li><li><b>The ladder<\/b>: OOP → SOLID → patterns → LLD → HLD; each layer reuses the one below.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Jumping into classes before listing use cases.<\/li><li>Using inheritance where composition fits.<\/li><li>Not saying the trade-off of each choice.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Design is about change: One idea explains SOLID, every design pattern, and most of system design. Learn it first.<\/li><li>Reading UML in 5 minutes: Every pattern and LLD answer in this track is drawn as a class diagram. Learn the six arrows once, using Java you already know.<\/li><li>The map: How the whole track links together, so each new topic lands on something you already know.<\/li><li>The two interviews: What each round wants, and the script to follow.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// The whole track in one move:\n// 1. find what changes      -> the notification channel\n// 2. hide it behind a stable interface\ninterface Channel { void send(User u, String msg); }\n// 3. pass it in from outside\nclass NotificationService {\n    private final List<Channel> channels;\n    NotificationService(List<Channel> channels) { this.channels = channels; }\n}"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Start Here: Design Thinking","sd-start.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-start"]]},{"i":"cx-sd-start","k":"cx","s":"sd-start","t":"Start Here: Design Thinking: complexities to know","cat":"Complexity","q":["time complexity of Start Here: Design Thinking","Start Here: Design Thinking big o","space complexity Start Here: Design Thinking","Start Here: Design Thinking complexity cheat sheet"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking complexity time space big o","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Arrow<\/th><th>Means<\/th><th>Java<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>solid line, hollow triangle ▷<\/td><td>IS-A (inheritance)<\/td><td><code>class Dog extends Animal<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>dashed line, hollow triangle ▷<\/td><td>implements a contract<\/td><td><code>class Card implements Payment<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>filled diamond ◆ at the owner<\/td><td>owns a part (composition): part dies with owner<\/td><td><code>private final Engine engine = new Engine();<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>hollow diamond ◇ at the owner<\/td><td>has a reference (aggregation): part lives on<\/td><td><code>List&lt;Player&gt; players<\/code> passed in<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>solid arrow →<\/td><td>knows about (association)<\/td><td>a field pointing to another object<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>dashed arrow ⇢<\/td><td>uses briefly (dependency)<\/td><td>a parameter or local variable<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Counting the cost: Start Here: Design Thinking","sd-start.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-java","k":"topic","s":"sd-java","t":"Java & OOP","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is Java & OOP","Java & OOP overview","When to use Java & OOP","Java & OOP template","Java & OOP common mistakes","explain Java & OOP","Java & OOP cheat sheet","Java & OOP notes"],"w":"Java & OOP sd java topic pattern","a":"<p>Every design pattern is written in this vocabulary. Objects vs references, interfaces vs abstract classes, polymorphism and composition: get these sharp and patterns become readable.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Java variables hold references (arrows), interfaces are contracts, and the object's real class decides which override runs.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Four pillars<\/b>: encapsulation (hide state), abstraction (expose a contract), inheritance (reuse is-a), polymorphism (one call, many behaviours).<\/li><li><b>Pass-by-value of references<\/b>: a method can mutate the object, not reassign the caller's variable.<\/li><li><b>== vs equals<\/b>: identity vs value. Override <code>equals<\/code> → override <code>hashCode<\/code> too.<\/li><li><b>Interface vs abstract class<\/b>: capability contract vs shared half-built base (state + constructor).<\/li><li><b>Overloading<\/b> picked at compile time; <b>overriding<\/b> picked at runtime by the object's class.<\/li><li><b>Generics<\/b>: type safety at compile time; erased at runtime. <code>? extends<\/code> read, <code>? super<\/code> write (PECS).<\/li><li><b>Prefer HAS-A<\/b> (composition) over IS-A unless it's truly a subtype.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Mutable keys in a HashMap.<\/li><li>String comparison with ==.<\/li><li>static methods are not overridden (hidden).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Objects &amp; references: The four pillars, the one memory idea that makes patterns readable, and the keywords every pattern is full of.<\/li><li>Contracts &amp; polymorphism: Interfaces, abstract classes, dynamic dispatch, generics and enums: the machinery patterns are built on.<\/li><li>Design sense: Composition over inheritance, and the coupling and cohesion vocabulary that makes design answers sound senior.<\/li><li>The decoder: Interview phrasing in, the right concept out.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// Program to an interface, compose behaviour\ninterface PaymentMethod { boolean pay(long paise); }\n \nclass Checkout {\n    private final PaymentMethod method;      // HAS-A, swappable\n    Checkout(PaymentMethod method) { this.method = method; }\n    boolean complete(long amount) { return method.pay(amount); }\n}"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-java"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-java","k":"list","s":"sd-java","t":"All Java & OOP problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of Java & OOP problems","Java & OOP questions","which Java & OOP problems should I do","Java & OOP leetcode list","practice problems for Java & OOP"],"w":"Java & OOP problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-pillars\"><b>The Four Pillars<\/b> <small>Foundation 01 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-refs\"><b>Objects vs References<\/b> <small>Foundation 02 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-equals\"><b>== vs equals &amp; hashCode<\/b> <small>Foundation · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-access\"><b>Access Modifiers<\/b> <small>Foundation 03 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-staticfinal\"><b>static · final · this<\/b> <small>Foundation 06 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-ifaceabs\"><b>Interface vs Abstract Class<\/b> <small>Foundation 04 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-poly\"><b>Polymorphism &amp; Dispatch<\/b> <small>Foundation 05 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-generics\"><b>Generics<\/b> <small>Foundation 07 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-enums\"><b>Enums<\/b> <small>Foundation 08 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-hasa\"><b>IS-A vs HAS-A<\/b> <small>Foundation 09 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-java-coupling\"><b>Coupling &amp; Cohesion<\/b> <small>Foundation 10 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-solid","k":"topic","s":"sd-solid","t":"SOLID","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is SOLID","SOLID overview","When to use SOLID","SOLID template","SOLID common mistakes","explain SOLID","SOLID cheat sheet","SOLID notes"],"w":"SOLID sd solid topic pattern","a":"<p>Five principles that explain why every design pattern looks the way it does. Learn the smell each one cures and you can spot them in any codebase.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> SOLID is a list of smells and their cures; every design pattern is one of these cures applied to a specific change.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>S<\/b>ingle responsibility: one reason to change → split by who asks for changes.<\/li><li><b>O<\/b>pen/closed: add a new class instead of editing an if-else chain (Strategy).<\/li><li><b>L<\/b>iskov: a subclass must keep the parent's promises (Square is not a Rectangle).<\/li><li><b>I<\/b>nterface segregation: small role interfaces, nobody implements dummy methods.<\/li><li><b>D<\/b>ependency inversion: high-level code depends on interfaces; inject the implementation.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Over-splitting into one-method classes for no change reason.<\/li><li>Throwing UnsupportedOperationException is an LSP/ISP smell.<\/li><li>DIP ≠ a DI framework; constructor injection is enough.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>S, O &amp; L: One reason to change, add-don&#x27;t-edit, and subclasses that keep their promises. Each one: the smell, the question, the move, as a growing class diagram.<\/li><li>I &amp; D: Small interfaces, and arrows that point at abstractions.<\/li><li>The decoder: Interview phrasing in, the right principle out.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// D: depend on the interface, receive the implementation\ninterface Notifier { void send(String to, String msg); }\n \nclass OrderService {\n    private final Notifier notifier;                 // abstraction\n    OrderService(Notifier n) { this.notifier = n; }  // injected\n    void place(Order o) { /* ... */ notifier.send(o.email(), \"Placed\"); }\n}\n// new OrderService(new EmailNotifier())  or  new OrderService(new SmsNotifier())"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-solid"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-solid","k":"list","s":"sd-solid","t":"All SOLID problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of SOLID problems","SOLID questions","which SOLID problems should I do","SOLID leetcode list","practice problems for SOLID"],"w":"SOLID problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-solid-srp\"><b>Single Responsibility<\/b> <small>SOLID · S · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-solid-ocp\"><b>Open / Closed<\/b> <small>SOLID · O · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-solid-lsp\"><b>Liskov Substitution<\/b> <small>SOLID · L · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-solid-isp\"><b>Interface Segregation<\/b> <small>SOLID · I · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-solid-dip\"><b>Dependency Inversion<\/b> <small>SOLID · D · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-solid-solidreview\"><b>Refactor a Notification Service<\/b> <small>Applied · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-creational","k":"topic","s":"sd-creational","t":"Creational Patterns","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is Creational Patterns","Creational Patterns overview","When to use Creational Patterns","Creational Patterns template","Creational Patterns common mistakes","explain Creational Patterns","Creational Patterns cheat sheet","Creational Patterns notes"],"w":"Creational Patterns sd creational topic pattern","a":"<p>Who creates objects, and how. Singleton, Factory in its three flavours, Builder and Prototype: the patterns that keep new out of your business logic.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Creational patterns decide who creates objects, so callers don't depend on concrete classes or messy constructors.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Singleton<\/b>: one instance. Thread-safe via eager init, holder idiom, enum, or double-checked locking with <code>volatile<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Simple factory<\/b>: one method picks the class from input.<\/li><li><b>Factory method<\/b>: subclasses decide which product to create.<\/li><li><b>Abstract factory<\/b>: a family of related products that must match (Mac button + Mac checkbox).<\/li><li><b>Builder<\/b>: many optional params, readable and immutable result; validate in <code>build()<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Prototype<\/b>: copy a configured object (deep vs shallow copy).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>DCL without volatile can publish a half-built object.<\/li><li>Singletons hide dependencies and hurt testing: inject them.<\/li><li>Shallow copies share mutable fields.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>One &amp; many: Singleton for exactly one, Factory for choosing which one. Each pattern: the pain, the question, the move, as a growing class diagram.<\/li><li>Build &amp; copy: Builder for objects with many optional parts, Prototype for cloning a configured one.<\/li><li>The decoder: Requirement phrasing in, the right creational pattern out.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// Builder: named, validated, immutable\nPizza p = Pizza.builder()\n        .size(Size.LARGE)\n        .cheese(true)\n        .topping(\"olives\")\n        .build();          // validates once, returns an immutable Pizza"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-creational"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-creational","k":"list","s":"sd-creational","t":"All Creational Patterns problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of Creational Patterns problems","Creational Patterns questions","which Creational Patterns problems should I do","Creational Patterns leetcode list","practice problems for Creational Patterns"],"w":"Creational Patterns problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-creational-singleton\"><b>Singleton<\/b> <small>Creational · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-creational-dcl\"><b>Double-Checked Locking<\/b> <small>Creational · concurrency · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-creational-factory\"><b>Simple Factory<\/b> <small>Creational · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-creational-factorymethod\"><b>Factory Method<\/b> <small>Creational · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-creational-abstractfactory\"><b>Abstract Factory<\/b> <small>Creational · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-creational-builder\"><b>Builder<\/b> <small>Creational · GoF · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-creational-prototype\"><b>Prototype<\/b> <small>Creational · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-structural","k":"topic","s":"sd-structural","t":"Structural Patterns","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is Structural Patterns","Structural Patterns overview","When to use Structural Patterns","Structural Patterns template","Structural Patterns common mistakes","explain Structural Patterns","Structural Patterns cheat sheet","Structural Patterns notes"],"w":"Structural Patterns sd structural topic pattern","a":"<p>How objects are wrapped and combined. Decorator, Adapter, Facade, Composite, Bridge, Proxy and Flyweight, each with a one-line tell.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Structural patterns wrap or compose objects to add features, translate interfaces or hide complexity, without changing the original classes.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Decorator<\/b>: same interface, wraps another, adds behaviour; stack in any combination.<\/li><li><b>Proxy<\/b>: same interface, controls access (lazy load, cache, auth, remote).<\/li><li><b>Adapter<\/b>: converts one interface into the one the client expects.<\/li><li><b>Facade<\/b>: one simple entry point over a complex subsystem.<\/li><li><b>Composite<\/b>: tree where leaf and group share an interface (files and folders).<\/li><li><b>Bridge<\/b>: split two independent dimensions (shape × renderer) to avoid class explosion.<\/li><li><b>Flyweight<\/b>: share immutable intrinsic state across many objects.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Decorator vs Proxy: same shape, different intent (add features vs control access).<\/li><li>Adapter changes the interface; Decorator keeps it.<\/li><li>Inheritance for every combination → class explosion.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Wrappers: Decorator, Proxy, Adapter and Facade all wrap something. Build each diagram step by step, then learn to tell them apart by intent.<\/li><li>Trees &amp; splits: Composite for part-whole trees, Bridge for two independent dimensions, Flyweight for millions of tiny objects.<\/li><li>The decoder: Requirement phrasing in, the right structural pattern out.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// Decorator: wrap, add, delegate\nBeverage order = new Whip(new Milk(new Espresso()));\norder.cost();          // 20 + 10 + 100 = 130\norder.description();   // \"Espresso, milk, whip\""},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-structural"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-structural","k":"list","s":"sd-structural","t":"All Structural Patterns problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of Structural Patterns problems","Structural Patterns questions","which Structural Patterns problems should I do","Structural Patterns leetcode list","practice problems for Structural Patterns"],"w":"Structural Patterns problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-structural-decorator\"><b>Decorator<\/b> <small>Structural · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-structural-proxy\"><b>Proxy<\/b> <small>Structural · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-structural-adapter\"><b>Adapter<\/b> <small>Structural · GoF · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-structural-facade\"><b>Facade<\/b> <small>Structural · GoF · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-structural-composite\"><b>Composite<\/b> <small>Structural · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-structural-bridge\"><b>Bridge<\/b> <small>Structural · GoF · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-structural-flyweight\"><b>Flyweight<\/b> <small>Structural · GoF · Hard<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-behavioral","k":"topic","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is Behavioral Patterns","Behavioral Patterns overview","When to use Behavioral Patterns","Behavioral Patterns template","Behavioral Patterns common mistakes","explain Behavioral Patterns","Behavioral Patterns cheat sheet","Behavioral Patterns notes"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns sd behavioral topic pattern","a":"<p>How objects talk and share work. Strategy, Observer, Command, Chain of Responsibility, State, Iterator, Template Method, Mediator and Memento.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Behavioural patterns turn 'what varies at runtime' (the algorithm, the listeners, the action, the mode) into objects.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Strategy<\/b>: swappable algorithm behind an interface.<\/li><li><b>Observer<\/b>: subject keeps a list of listeners and notifies all on change.<\/li><li><b>Command<\/b>: an action as an object → undo, queue, log, retry.<\/li><li><b>State<\/b>: each mode is a class; the context delegates and switches state objects.<\/li><li><b>Chain of Responsibility<\/b>: handlers in a line; each handles or passes on.<\/li><li><b>Template Method<\/b>: fixed skeleton in the base class, steps overridden.<\/li><li><b>Iterator / Mediator / Memento<\/b>: walk a collection / central coordinator / snapshot to restore.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Strategy vs State: who switches? Client picks a strategy; states switch themselves.<\/li><li>Observer memory leaks: unsubscribe.<\/li><li>Huge switch on mode → State.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Swap &amp; notify: Strategy, Observer and Command. Build each diagram step by step; notice they share one skeleton.<\/li><li>Flow &amp; state: State, Chain of Responsibility, Template Method, Iterator and Mediator.<\/li><li>The decoder: Requirement phrasing in, the right behavioural pattern out.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// Strategy: the algorithm is an object you pass in\ninterface FareStrategy { Money fare(Trip t); }\n \nclass RideService {\n    private final FareStrategy pricing;\n    RideService(FareStrategy pricing) { this.pricing = pricing; }\n    Money quote(Trip t) { return pricing.fare(t); }\n}\nnew RideService(new SurgeFare(1.8));\nnew RideService(t -> Money.of(t.km() * 12));   // a lambda is a strategy too"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-behavioral"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-behavioral","k":"list","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"All Behavioral Patterns problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of Behavioral Patterns problems","Behavioral Patterns questions","which Behavioral Patterns problems should I do","Behavioral Patterns leetcode list","practice problems for Behavioral Patterns"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-behavioral-strategy\"><b>Strategy<\/b> <small>Behavioral · GoF · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-behavioral-observer\"><b>Observer<\/b> <small>Behavioral · GoF · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-behavioral-command\"><b>Command<\/b> <small>Behavioral · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-behavioral-iterator\"><b>Iterator<\/b> <small>Behavioral · GoF · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-behavioral-state\"><b>State<\/b> <small>Behavioral · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-behavioral-cor\"><b>Chain of Responsibility<\/b> <small>Behavioral · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-behavioral-template\"><b>Template Method<\/b> <small>Behavioral · GoF · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-behavioral-mediator\"><b>Mediator<\/b> <small>Behavioral · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-behavioral-memento\"><b>Memento<\/b> <small>Behavioral · GoF · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-lld","k":"topic","s":"sd-lld","t":"LLD Problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is LLD Problems","LLD Problems overview","When to use LLD Problems","LLD Problems template","LLD Problems common mistakes","explain LLD Problems","LLD Problems cheat sheet","LLD Problems notes"],"w":"LLD Problems sd lld topic pattern","a":"<p>Machine-coding rounds: Parking Lot, ATM, Elevator and BookMyShow. One repeatable method from requirements to classes to Java, plus the concurrency bugs they all hide.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> One method for every machine-coding round: requirements → nouns → class diagram → patterns where things change → code → concurrency.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Clarify<\/b> 3–5 use cases and what's out of scope.<\/li><li><b>Nouns → classes, verbs → methods<\/b> on the class owning the data.<\/li><li><b>Enums<\/b> for fixed categories; <b>Strategy<\/b> for rules that change (pricing, spot finding).<\/li><li><b>State<\/b> for mode-driven machines (ATM, vending); <b>Observer<\/b> for displays/notifications.<\/li><li><b>Elevator<\/b>: SCAN scheduling; requests as commands.<\/li><li><b>Booking<\/b>: seat lock with expiry; avoid double booking by a conditional update or row lock.<\/li><li><b>Concurrency<\/b>: check-then-act isn't atomic → synchronized / CAS / <code>UPDATE … WHERE status='FREE'<\/code> / optimistic version column.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>God class with all the logic.<\/li><li>Forgetting two gates/users can race for one resource.<\/li><li>Not saying which pattern and why.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>The method: One repeatable recipe for every machine-coding round. Every problem below is built with it, one step at a time, as a growing class diagram.<\/li><li>Parking Lot: The most-asked LLD question, built from the requirements up.<\/li><li>ATM &amp; Elevator: Two machines whose behaviour depends on their mode.<\/li><li>Booking &amp; races: BookMyShow&#x27;s model, and the concurrency bug hiding in every booking problem.<\/li><li>Pick a pattern: Requirement phrasing in, pattern out. This is the decoder for every LLD round.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// The LLD recipe, as code shape\nenum VehicleType { BIKE, CAR, TRUCK }                 // fixed categories -> enum\nclass ParkingLot {                                     // nouns -> classes\n    private final List<Level> levels;\n    private final SpotFinder finder;                   // variation -> Strategy\n    private final FeePolicy fees;                      // variation -> Strategy\n    Ticket park(Vehicle v) { ... }                     // verbs -> methods\n    Money exit(Ticket t) { ... }\n}"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-lld"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-lld","k":"list","s":"sd-lld","t":"All LLD Problems problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of LLD Problems problems","LLD Problems questions","which LLD Problems problems should I do","LLD Problems leetcode list","practice problems for LLD Problems"],"w":"LLD Problems problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-lld-parkinglot\"><b>Parking Lot<\/b> <small>LLD classic · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-lld-atm\"><b>ATM<\/b> <small>LLD classic · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-lld-elevator\"><b>Elevator<\/b> <small>LLD classic · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-lld-bookmyshow\"><b>BookMyShow<\/b> <small>LLD + concurrency · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-lld-race\"><b>Race Conditions<\/b> <small>Concurrency · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-lld-seatsql\"><b>Conditional Update &amp; Row Locks<\/b> <small>Concurrency · SQL · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-lld-optimistic\"><b>Optimistic Locking<\/b> <small>Concurrency · SQL · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-basics","k":"topic","s":"sd-basics","t":"System Design Basics","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is System Design Basics","System Design Basics overview","When to use System Design Basics","System Design Basics template","System Design Basics common mistakes","explain System Design Basics","System Design Basics cheat sheet","System Design Basics notes"],"w":"System Design Basics sd basics topic pattern","a":"<p>How to run the interview, then the building blocks every design uses: scaling, load balancers, API gateways, API design and real-time communication.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Every HLD answer follows the same framework, and a few numbers let you size the system before drawing boxes.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Framework<\/b>: requirements (functional + non-functional) → estimates → API → data model → high-level design → deep dives → trade-offs.<\/li><li><b>Numbers<\/b>: 1 day ≈ 10⁵ s; 1M req/day ≈ 12 req/s; peak ≈ 2–3× average.<\/li><li><b>Scale out<\/b>: stateless app servers behind a load balancer; state in DB/cache.<\/li><li><b>L4 vs L7<\/b> load balancing: by connection vs by request content.<\/li><li><b>API gateway<\/b>: auth, rate limiting, routing in one place.<\/li><li><b>API design<\/b>: resources, idempotency keys, pagination (cursor), versioning.<\/li><li><b>Real-time<\/b>: short polling → long polling → SSE (server → client) → WebSockets (both ways).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Designing before agreeing on requirements and scale.<\/li><li>Single points of failure.<\/li><li>Forgetting read vs write ratio.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>The interview: A 45-minute HLD round has a shape. Follow it and you&#x27;ll never freeze at a blank whiteboard.<\/li><li>Scale: From one server to millions of users, one bottleneck at a time.<\/li><li>Talking to clients: Designing APIs people can&#x27;t misuse, and pushing updates in real time.<\/li><li>The decoder: Requirement phrasing in, building block out.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// Estimation in five lines\nlong dau = 100_000_000L, reqPerUser = 10;\nlong perDay = dau * reqPerUser;             // 1e9 requests/day\nlong avgQps = perDay / 100_000;             // 1 day ~ 1e5 s  -> ~10k QPS\nlong peakQps = avgQps * 3;                  // plan for peak  -> ~30k QPS\nlong storagePerYear = 5_000_000L * 1_000 * 365;  // 5M writes/day x 1 KB -> ~1.8 TB/yr"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-basics"],["🎬 From one server to millions","#film-sd-scale"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-basics","k":"list","s":"sd-basics","t":"All System Design Basics problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of System Design Basics problems","System Design Basics questions","which System Design Basics problems should I do","System Design Basics leetcode list","practice problems for System Design Basics"],"w":"System Design Basics problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-basics-framework\"><b>Interview Framework<\/b> <small>SD Basics 01 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-basics-estimation\"><b>Back-of-the-envelope<\/b> <small>SD Basics · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-basics-scaling\"><b>Scaling 1 → 1M<\/b> <small>SD Basics 02 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-basics-loadbalancer\"><b>Load Balancers<\/b> <small>SD Basics 03 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-basics-apigateway\"><b>API Gateway<\/b> <small>SD Basics 04 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-basics-apidesign\"><b>API Design<\/b> <small>SD Basics 05 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-basics-realtime\"><b>Polling, SSE, WebSockets<\/b> <small>SD Basics 06 · Medium<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-databases","k":"topic","s":"sd-databases","t":"Databases","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is Databases","Databases overview","When to use Databases","Databases template","Databases common mistakes","explain Databases","Databases cheat sheet","Databases notes"],"w":"Databases sd databases topic pattern","a":"<p>Pick the right store, make reads fast with indexes, keep money safe with transactions, then survive growth with replication and sharding. Plus the database questions interviewers actually ask.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Choose the store by access pattern, index for your queries, and know what transactions, replication and sharding cost you.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>SQL<\/b>: relations, joins, transactions. <b>NoSQL<\/b>: KV, document, wide-column, graph for scale/shape.<\/li><li><b>Index<\/b> = sorted copy (B-tree) you can binary search; composite index follows the leftmost-prefix rule.<\/li><li><b>ACID<\/b>; isolation levels: read committed, repeatable read, serializable. Lost update → row lock or version check.<\/li><li><b>Replication<\/b>: leader-follower for reads; replication lag → read-your-writes issues.<\/li><li><b>Sharding<\/b>: split by key; pick a key that spreads load and keeps queries on one shard.<\/li><li><b>Consistent hashing<\/b>: adding a node moves only ~1/n of keys; virtual nodes even out load.<\/li><li><b>N+1 queries<\/b> → join or batch fetch.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Sharding on a hot key (celebrity, today's date).<\/li><li>Index on every column slows writes.<\/li><li>Cross-shard joins and transactions are expensive.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Choosing: Pick the store from the access pattern, not from fashion.<\/li><li>Indexes &amp; ACID: Make reads fast without scanning; make writes safe when several happen at once.<\/li><li>Scaling data: Copies for reads and safety, splits for writes and size.<\/li><li>The decoder: Requirement phrasing in, data decision out.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"-- The three moves that fix most database problems\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_time ON orders (user_id, created_at);   -- fast reads\n \nBEGIN;                                                                -- safe writes\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 500 WHERE id = 1 AND balance >= 500;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 500 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;\n \n-- shard by user_id when one primary can't keep up                      -- scale writes"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-databases"],["🎬 Consistent hashing","#film-sd-ring"],["🎬 Replicas and shards","#film-sd-shard"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-databases","k":"list","s":"sd-databases","t":"All Databases problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of Databases problems","Databases questions","which Databases problems should I do","Databases leetcode list","practice problems for Databases"],"w":"Databases problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-sqlnosql\"><b>SQL vs NoSQL<\/b> <small>Data 02 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-normalize\"><b>Normalisation vs Denormalisation<\/b> <small>Data · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-indexes\"><b>Indexes<\/b> <small>Data 03 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-compositeindex\"><b>Composite Index<\/b> <small>Data 03 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-nplus1\"><b>N+1 Queries<\/b> <small>Data · ORM · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-acid\"><b>ACID Transactions<\/b> <small>Data 03 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-isolation\"><b>Isolation Levels &amp; Lost Update<\/b> <small>Data 03 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-replication\"><b>Replication<\/b> <small>Data 04 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-sharding\"><b>Sharding<\/b> <small>Data 05 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-consistenthashing\"><b>Consistent Hashing<\/b> <small>Data 05 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-databases-secondsalary\"><b>Second Highest Salary<\/b> <small>SQL classic · Easy<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-distributed","k":"topic","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is Caching, Queues & Resilience","Caching, Queues & Resilience overview","When to use Caching, Queues & Resilience","Caching, Queues & Resilience template","Caching, Queues & Resilience common mistakes","explain Caching, Queues & Resilience","Caching, Queues & Resilience cheat sheet","Caching, Queues & Resilience notes"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience sd distributed topic pattern","a":"<p>The parts inside the boxes: caches and CDNs, Kafka and async work, sagas and the outbox, circuit breakers, the clever algorithms, uptime math and security.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Caches and queues buy speed and decoupling; sagas, outbox and breakers keep things correct and alive when parts fail.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Caching<\/b>: cache-aside (most common), write-through, write-back; TTL + eviction (LRU).<\/li><li><b>CDN<\/b> for static and media; object storage (S3) for files, DB for metadata.<\/li><li><b>Kafka<\/b>: partitioned log; order within a partition; consumer groups; at-least-once → idempotent consumers.<\/li><li><b>Saga<\/b>: a chain of local transactions with compensations instead of a distributed transaction.<\/li><li><b>Outbox<\/b>: write the event in the same DB transaction, publish later.<\/li><li><b>Resilience<\/b>: timeouts, retries with backoff + jitter, circuit breaker, bulkheads.<\/li><li><b>Toolbox<\/b>: Bloom filter (maybe/no), geohash (nearby), HyperLogLog (approx count), JWT/OAuth.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>Cache stampede on a hot key expiring → lock or early refresh.<\/li><li>Retries without idempotency → double charges.<\/li><li>CAP: under a partition choose consistency or availability.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Speed: Caches, CDNs and blob storage: serve bytes from as close and as cheap as possible.<\/li><li>Async &amp; consistency: Queues, Kafka, and keeping data correct across services without distributed transactions.<\/li><li>Staying up: Failure handling, clever data structures, uptime maths, observability and security.<\/li><li>The decoder: Requirement phrasing in, building block out.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// Cache-aside in four lines\nProduct get(long id) {\n    Product p = cache.get(\"product:\" + id);\n    if (p == null) { p = db.find(id); cache.set(\"product:\" + id, p, Duration.ofMinutes(10)); }\n    return p;\n}"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-distributed"],["🎬 Cache-aside, hit and miss","#film-sd-cache"],["🎬 Queues absorb the spike","#film-sd-queue"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-distributed","k":"list","s":"sd-distributed","t":"All Caching, Queues & Resilience problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of Caching, Queues & Resilience problems","Caching, Queues & Resilience questions","which Caching, Queues & Resilience problems should I do","Caching, Queues & Resilience leetcode list","practice problems for Caching, Queues & Resilience"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-caching\"><b>Caching Strategies<\/b> <small>Data 06 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-cdn\"><b>CDN &amp; Object Storage<\/b> <small>Data 07 · Easy<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-kafka\"><b>Message Queues &amp; Kafka<\/b> <small>Systems 01 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-saga\"><b>Sagas<\/b> <small>Systems 02 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-outbox\"><b>Transactional Outbox<\/b> <small>Systems 02 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-circuitbreaker\"><b>Circuit Breaker &amp; Retries<\/b> <small>Systems 03 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-bloom\"><b>Bloom Filter<\/b> <small>Systems 04 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-geohash\"><b>Geohash &amp; Proximity<\/b> <small>Systems 04 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-hyperloglog\"><b>HyperLogLog<\/b> <small>Systems 04 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-auth\"><b>Auth: JWT &amp; OAuth<\/b> <small>Systems 06 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-distributed-payments\"><b>Reliable Payments<\/b> <small>Applied · Hard<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html"]]},{"i":"t-sd-hld","k":"topic","s":"sd-hld","t":"HLD Problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["What is HLD Problems","HLD Problems overview","When to use HLD Problems","HLD Problems template","HLD Problems common mistakes","explain HLD Problems","HLD Problems cheat sheet","HLD Problems notes"],"w":"HLD Problems sd hld topic pattern","a":"<p>Eleven classic designs, each run through the same framework: requirements, estimates, API, data model, architecture, then the one deep dive that matters.<\/p><p><b>The idea:<\/b> Every classic is the same six moves; what differs is the one deep dive the interviewer cares about.<\/p><p><b>Key moves<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>URL shortener<\/b>: unique IDs (counter blocks + Base62), cache hot redirects, 302.<\/li><li><b>Rate limiter<\/b>: token bucket / sliding window in Redis, atomic with Lua.<\/li><li><b>Feed<\/b>: fan-out on write for normal users, on read for celebrities (hybrid).<\/li><li><b>Chat (WhatsApp)<\/b>: WebSocket gateways, message IDs, per-chat ordering, offline store + push.<\/li><li><b>Uber / Zomato<\/b>: location updates into geohash cells, matching, state machine for orders.<\/li><li><b>YouTube / Dropbox<\/b>: chunked upload to object storage, transcoding pipeline, CDN; dedup by chunk hash.<\/li><li><b>Airbnb / booking<\/b>: availability search + strong consistency on reserve (conditional update).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Traps<\/b><\/p><ul><li>No numbers → no justification for caches or shards.<\/li><li>Forgetting failure modes and idempotency.<\/li><li>Over-designing before the interviewer picks a deep dive.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Chapters<\/b><\/p><ol><li>The framework: One answer shape for every HLD question. Every problem on this page is built with it, box by box.<\/li><li>Read-heavy systems: When reads dwarf writes, do the work at write time so reads are a lookup.<\/li><li>Real-time &amp; maps: Persistent connections, and things that move.<\/li><li>Correctness first: Systems where doing something twice, or not at all, is the bug.<\/li><li>Files &amp; media: Big bytes never go through your application servers.<\/li><li>The decoder: Problem in, the deep dive they&#x27;re waiting for out.<\/li><\/ol>","c":{"java":"// The deep dive of the URL shortener: unique 7-char codes\n// Each app server leases a block of 1,000 ids from a counter, then encodes locally\nString encode(long id) {\n    final String A = \"0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\";\n    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n    do { sb.append(A.charAt((int) (id % 62))); id /= 62; } while (id > 0);\n    return sb.reverse().toString();          // 62^7 = 3.5 trillion codes\n}"},"ct":"Template","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html"],["Revision notes","notes.html#n-sd-hld"],["🎬 Token bucket rate limiter","#film-sd-rate"]]},{"i":"pl-sd-hld","k":"list","s":"sd-hld","t":"All HLD Problems problems","cat":"System design topics","q":["list of HLD Problems problems","HLD Problems questions","which HLD Problems problems should I do","HLD Problems leetcode list","practice problems for HLD Problems"],"w":"HLD Problems problems list questions leetcode","a":"<ul><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-urlshortener\"><b>URL Shortener<\/b> <small>HLD 01 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-ratelimiter\"><b>Rate Limiter<\/b> <small>HLD 02 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-notification\"><b>Notification System<\/b> <small>HLD 03 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-autocomplete\"><b>Autocomplete<\/b> <small>HLD 04 · Medium<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-whatsapp\"><b>WhatsApp<\/b> <small>HLD 05 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-twitterfeed\"><b>Twitter Feed<\/b> <small>HLD 06 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-uber\"><b>Uber<\/b> <small>HLD 07 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-zomato\"><b>Zomato / Food Delivery<\/b> <small>HLD 08 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-youtube\"><b>YouTube<\/b> <small>HLD 09 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-airbnb\"><b>Airbnb<\/b> <small>HLD 10 · Hard<\/small><\/li><li data-ask=\"p-sd-hld-dropbox\"><b>Dropbox<\/b> <small>HLD 11 · Hard<\/small><\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html"]]},{"i":"p-hashing-217","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Contains Duplicate","sub":"LeetCode 217 · Easy · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":217,"q":["Contains Duplicate","how to solve Contains Duplicate","Contains Duplicate approach","Contains Duplicate solution","leetcode 217","lc 217","Return True if any value appears at least twice.","Is any number in the list repeated?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing “Seen before?” → set. Complexity O(n) time, O(n) space","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> “Seen before?” → set.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A set remembers what you&#x27;ve already seen; checking it is O(1).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Is any number in the list repeated?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Compare every pair: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Walk through the numbers.<\/li><li>If the number is already in the set, return True.<\/li><li>Otherwise add it and continue. Return False at the end.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> “Seen before?” question.<\/li><li><b>Tool:<\/b> A set.<\/li><li><b>Pass:<\/b> Check, then add.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(n) space<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> len(set(nums)) != len(nums) works but can’t stop early.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def contains_duplicate(nums):\n    seen = set()\n    for x in nums:\n        if x in seen:\n            return True\n        seen.add(x)\n    return False","java":"public boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n    Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (seen.contains(x)) return true;\n        seen.add(x);\n    }\n    return false;\n}","cpp":"bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {\n    unordered_set<int> seen;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (seen.count(x)) return true;\n        seen.insert(x);\n    }\n    return false;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-217"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/contains-duplicate/"]],"ask":"Return True if any value appears at least twice."},{"i":"p-hashing-242","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Valid Anagram","sub":"LeetCode 242 · Easy · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":242,"q":["Valid Anagram","how to solve Valid Anagram","Valid Anagram approach","Valid Anagram solution","leetcode 242","lc 242","Are s and t anagrams of each other?","Do the two strings use exactly the same letters, the same number of times?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Frequencies → Counter. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Frequencies → Counter.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Count letters in s, subtract counts for t; every count must end at 0.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Do the two strings use exactly the same letters, the same number of times?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort both and compare: O(n log n). Fine, but counting is faster.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If lengths differ, return False.<\/li><li>Count each letter of s (+1) and of t (−1).<\/li><li>Return True only if every count is 0.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Same letters, same counts.<\/li><li><b>Tool:<\/b> Counter (or a 26-slot array).<\/li><li><b>Compare:<\/b> Counts must match exactly.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Check lengths first for a quick exit.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import Counter\n\ndef is_anagram(s, t):\n    return Counter(s) == Counter(t)","java":"public boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {\n    if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;\n    int[] count = new int[26];\n    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) count[ch - 'a']++;\n    for (char ch : t.toCharArray()) count[ch - 'a']--;\n    for (int c : count) if (c != 0) return false;\n    return true;\n}","cpp":"bool isAnagram(string s, string t) {\n    if (s.size() != t.size()) return false;\n    int count[26] = {};\n    for (char ch : s) count[ch - 'a']++;\n    for (char ch : t) count[ch - 'a']--;\n    for (int c : count) if (c != 0) return false;\n    return true;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-242"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-anagram/"]],"ask":"Are s and t anagrams of each other?"},{"i":"p-hashing-1","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Two Sum","sub":"LeetCode 1 · Easy · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":1,"q":["Two Sum","how to solve Two Sum","Two Sum approach","Two Sum solution","leetcode 1","lc 1","Return indexes of two numbers that add up to target.","Find two different positions whose values add up to target."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Pair with a target → complement lookup. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Pair with a target → complement lookup.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> For each x you need target − x. Store values you&#x27;ve passed in a dict value → index.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Find two different positions whose values add up to target.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every pair: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Loop i, x over the array.<\/li><li>If target − x is in the dict, return [dict[target − x], i].<\/li><li>Otherwise store dict[x] = i.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Each x needs a partner target − x.<\/li><li><b>Tool:<\/b> Dict value → index.<\/li><li><b>Order:<\/b> Look up first, then store.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Sorting + two pointers loses the original indexes unless you carry them along.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def two_sum(nums, target):\n    seen = {}                        # value -> index\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        need = target - x\n        if need in seen:\n            return [seen[need], i]\n        seen[x] = i                  # store AFTER checking","java":"public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {\n    Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();   // value -> index\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        int need = target - nums[i];\n        if (seen.containsKey(need))\n            return new int[]{seen.get(need), i};\n        seen.put(nums[i], i);                        // store AFTER checking\n    }\n    return new int[0];\n}","cpp":"vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> seen;              // value -> index\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); i++) {\n        int need = target - nums[i];\n        if (seen.count(need))\n            return {seen[need], i};\n        seen[nums[i]] = i;                     // store AFTER checking\n    }\n    return {};\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-1"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum/"]],"ask":"Return indexes of two numbers that add up to target."},{"i":"p-hashing-49","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Group Anagrams","sub":"LeetCode 49 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":49,"q":["Group Anagrams","how to solve Group Anagrams","Group Anagrams approach","Group Anagrams solution","leetcode 49","lc 49","Group words that are anagrams of each other.","Put words with the same letters (in any order) into the same group."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Group by sameness → canonical key. Complexity O(n · k)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Group by sameness → canonical key.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Anagrams share a signature: their sorted letters (or 26 letter counts). Use it as a dict key.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Put words with the same letters (in any order) into the same group.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Compare every pair of words letter by letter: O(n²·k).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each word compute its key (sorted string or count tuple).<\/li><li>Append the word to groups[key].<\/li><li>Return all the groups.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Group by “same after rearranging”.<\/li><li><b>Key:<\/b> Tuple of 26 counts (or sorted word).<\/li><li><b>Pass:<\/b> Append each word to its key’s list.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · k)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Lists can’t be dict keys; convert to a tuple.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import defaultdict\n\ndef group_anagrams(strs):\n    groups = defaultdict(list)\n    for w in strs:\n        key = [0] * 26\n        for ch in w:\n            key[ord(ch) - ord(\"a\")] += 1\n        groups[tuple(key)].append(w)     # same counts -> same group\n    return list(groups.values())","java":"public List<List<String>> groupAnagrams(String[] strs) {\n    Map<String, List<String>> groups = new HashMap<>();\n    for (String w : strs) {\n        int[] key = new int[26];\n        for (char ch : w.toCharArray()) key[ch - 'a']++;\n        groups.computeIfAbsent(Arrays.toString(key), k -> new ArrayList<>())\n              .add(w);                       // same counts -> same group\n    }\n    return new ArrayList<>(groups.values());\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<string>> groupAnagrams(vector<string>& strs) {\n    map<array<int, 26>, vector<string>> groups;\n    for (auto& w : strs) {\n        array<int, 26> key{};\n        for (char ch : w) key[ch - 'a']++;\n        groups[key].push_back(w);            // same counts -> same group\n    }\n    vector<vector<string>> res;\n    for (auto& [k, g] : groups) res.push_back(g);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-49"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/group-anagrams/"]],"ask":"Group words that are anagrams of each other."},{"i":"p-hashing-347","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Top K Frequent Elements","sub":"LeetCode 347 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":347,"q":["Top K Frequent Elements","how to solve Top K Frequent Elements","Top K Frequent Elements approach","Top K Frequent Elements solution","leetcode 347","lc 347","Return the k most frequent elements.","Which k numbers appear most often?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Top k by frequency → bucket sort. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Top k by frequency → bucket sort.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Count with a dict, then bucket numbers by frequency (index = count); read buckets from high to low.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Which k numbers appear most often?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Count, then sort by count: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Count every number.<\/li><li>buckets[c] gets all numbers seen c times.<\/li><li>Walk buckets from n down to 1, collecting until you have k.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Count:<\/b> Counter.<\/li><li><b>Order:<\/b> Buckets indexed by frequency.<\/li><li><b>Collect:<\/b> High to low until k.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Bucket list size is len(nums) + 1, because a count can equal n.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import Counter\n\ndef top_k_frequent(nums, k):\n    count = Counter(nums)\n    buckets = [[] for _ in range(len(nums) + 1)]   # index = frequency\n    for x, c in count.items():\n        buckets[c].append(x)\n    res = []\n    for c in range(len(buckets) - 1, 0, -1):         # most frequent first\n        for x in buckets[c]:\n            res.append(x)\n            if len(res) == k:\n                return res","java":"public int[] topKFrequent(int[] nums, int k) {\n    Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();\n    for (int x : nums) count.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);\n    List<List<Integer>> buckets = new ArrayList<>();       // index = frequency\n    for (int i = 0; i <= nums.length; i++) buckets.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    for (var e : count.entrySet()) buckets.get(e.getValue()).add(e.getKey());\n    int[] res = new int[k];\n    int w = 0;\n    for (int c = nums.length; c > 0; c--)                    // most frequent first\n        for (int x : buckets.get(c)) {\n            res[w++] = x;\n            if (w == k) return res;\n        }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> topKFrequent(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> count;\n    for (int x : nums) count[x]++;\n    vector<vector<int>> buckets(nums.size() + 1);           // index = frequency\n    for (auto& [x, c] : count) buckets[c].push_back(x);\n    vector<int> res;\n    for (int c = nums.size(); c > 0; c--)                    // most frequent first\n        for (int x : buckets[c]) {\n            res.push_back(x);\n            if ((int)res.size() == k) return res;\n        }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-347"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/top-k-frequent-elements/"]],"ask":"Return the k most frequent elements."},{"i":"p-hashing-238","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Product of Array Except Self","sub":"LeetCode 238 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":238,"q":["Product of Array Except Self","how to solve Product of Array Except Self","Product of Array Except Self approach","Product of Array Except Self solution","leetcode 238","lc 238","ans[i] = product of all other elements, no division, O(n).","For each position, multiply every other number, without using division."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Left and right info → two passes. Complexity O(n), O(1) extra","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Left and right info → two passes.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Answer = (product of everything left of i) × (product of everything right of i).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For each position, multiply every other number, without using division.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> For each i multiply all others: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Pass left to right: ans[i] = running product of the left side.<\/li><li>Pass right to left with a running product of the right side.<\/li><li>Multiply it into ans[i].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Needs everything left and right of i.<\/li><li><b>Pass 1:<\/b> Left products.<\/li><li><b>Pass 2:<\/b> Multiply in right products.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1) extra<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Division fails with zeros, and it’s banned anyway.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def product_except_self(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    ans = [1] * n\n    left = 1\n    for i in range(n):               # ans[i] = product of everything left of i\n        ans[i] = left\n        left *= nums[i]\n    right = 1\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):   # times everything right of i\n        ans[i] *= right\n        right *= nums[i]\n    return ans","java":"public int[] productExceptSelf(int[] nums) {\n    int n = nums.length;\n    int[] ans = new int[n];\n    int left = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {        // ans[i] = product of everything left of i\n        ans[i] = left;\n        left *= nums[i];\n    }\n    int right = 1;\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {   // times everything right of i\n        ans[i] *= right;\n        right *= nums[i];\n    }\n    return ans;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> productExceptSelf(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<int> ans(n);\n    int left = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {        // ans[i] = product of everything left of i\n        ans[i] = left;\n        left *= nums[i];\n    }\n    int right = 1;\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {   // times everything right of i\n        ans[i] *= right;\n        right *= nums[i];\n    }\n    return ans;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-238"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/product-of-array-except-self/"]],"ask":"ans[i] = product of all other elements, no division, O(n)."},{"i":"p-hashing-36","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Valid Sudoku","sub":"LeetCode 36 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":36,"q":["Valid Sudoku","how to solve Valid Sudoku","Valid Sudoku approach","Valid Sudoku solution","leetcode 36","lc 36","Check that no row, column or 3×3 box repeats a digit (filled cells only).","Check the filled cells: no digit may repeat in a row, a column or a 3×3 box."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing “Seen before?” → set, with tagged keys. Complexity O(81)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> “Seen before?” → set, with tagged keys.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> One pass with sets per row, per column and per box; box index = (r // 3, c // 3).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Check the filled cells: no digit may repeat in a row, a column or a 3×3 box.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> For each cell rescan its row, column and box: lots of repeated work.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Skip empty cells.<\/li><li>If the digit is already in that row&#x27;s, column&#x27;s or box&#x27;s set, return False.<\/li><li>Add it to all three sets. Return True at the end.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Three “seen before?” checks per cell.<\/li><li><b>Key:<\/b> (“row”, r, v), (“col”, c, v), (“box”, r//3, c//3, v).<\/li><li><b>Pass:<\/b> One set holds all three kinds.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(81)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Box index is (r // 3, c // 3), not r // 3 + c // 3 alone without care.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def is_valid_sudoku(board):\n    seen = set()\n    for r in range(9):\n        for c in range(9):\n            v = board[r][c]\n            if v == \".\":\n                continue\n            keys = ((\"row\", r, v), (\"col\", c, v), (\"box\", r // 3, c // 3, v))\n            if any(k in seen for k in keys):\n                return False\n            seen.update(keys)\n    return True","java":"public boolean isValidSudoku(char[][] board) {\n    Set<String> seen = new HashSet<>();\n    for (int r = 0; r < 9; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < 9; c++) {\n            char v = board[r][c];\n            if (v == '.') continue;\n            // add() returns false if the key was already there\n            if (!seen.add(\"row\" + r + v) || !seen.add(\"col\" + c + v)\n                    || !seen.add(\"box\" + r / 3 + c / 3 + v))\n                return false;\n        }\n    return true;\n}","cpp":"bool isValidSudoku(vector<vector<char>>& board) {\n    unordered_set<string> seen;\n    for (int r = 0; r < 9; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < 9; c++) {\n            char v = board[r][c];\n            if (v == '.') continue;\n            string keys[3] = {\"row\" + to_string(r) + v, \"col\" + to_string(c) + v,\n                              \"box\" + to_string(r / 3) + to_string(c / 3) + v};\n            for (auto& k : keys)\n                if (!seen.insert(k).second) return false;   // already seen\n        }\n    return true;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-36"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-sudoku/"]],"ask":"Check that no row, column or 3×3 box repeats a digit (filled cells only)."},{"i":"p-hashing-128","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Longest Consecutive Sequence","sub":"LeetCode 128 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":128,"q":["Longest Consecutive Sequence","how to solve Longest Consecutive Sequence","Longest Consecutive Sequence approach","Longest Consecutive Sequence solution","leetcode 128","lc 128","Length of the longest run of consecutive integers, in O(n).","How long is the longest run like 4, 5, 6, 7 using numbers from the list (any order)?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Count runs only from their start. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Count runs only from their start.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Put everything in a set. Only start counting at x if x − 1 is NOT in the set (x starts a run).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How long is the longest run like 4, 5, 6, 7 using numbers from the list (any order)?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort and scan: O(n log n). The problem asks for O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Build a set of all numbers.<\/li><li>For each x where x − 1 is missing, count x, x+1, x+2… while present.<\/li><li>Keep the longest count.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Tool:<\/b> Set of all numbers.<\/li><li><b>Start:<\/b> Only where x − 1 is missing.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> Count up while x + 1 exists.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Iterate over the set, not the list, so duplicates don’t restart walks.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def longest_consecutive(nums):\n    s = set(nums)\n    best = 0\n    for x in s:\n        if x - 1 in s:\n            continue                 # not a start: its run is counted elsewhere\n        y = x\n        while y + 1 in s:\n            y += 1\n        best = max(best, y - x + 1)\n    return best","java":"public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {\n    Set<Integer> s = new HashSet<>();\n    for (int x : nums) s.add(x);\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int x : s) {\n        if (s.contains(x - 1)) continue;   // not a start: its run is counted elsewhere\n        int y = x;\n        while (s.contains(y + 1)) y++;\n        best = Math.max(best, y - x + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {\n    unordered_set<int> s(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int x : s) {\n        if (s.count(x - 1)) continue;      // not a start: its run is counted elsewhere\n        int y = x;\n        while (s.count(y + 1)) y++;\n        best = max(best, y - x + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-128"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-consecutive-sequence/"]],"ask":"Length of the longest run of consecutive integers, in O(n)."},{"i":"p-hashing-560","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Subarray Sum Equals K","sub":"LeetCode 560 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":560,"q":["Subarray Sum Equals K","how to solve Subarray Sum Equals K","Subarray Sum Equals K approach","Subarray Sum Equals K solution","leetcode 560","lc 560","Count contiguous subarrays summing to k (negatives allowed).","How many contiguous pieces of the array add up exactly to k?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Subarray sum = k → prefix + dict. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Subarray sum = k → prefix + dict.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> sum(i..j) = prefix[j] − prefix[i−1]. So for each prefix p, count earlier prefixes equal to p − k.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How many contiguous pieces of the array add up exactly to k?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check every start and end: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Keep a dict of prefix sums seen, starting {0: 1}.<\/li><li>Add each number to the running prefix p.<\/li><li>Add count[p − k] to the answer, then count[p] += 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Subarray sum = difference of prefixes.<\/li><li><b>Tool:<\/b> Running sum + dict of prefix counts.<\/li><li><b>Seed:<\/b> {0: 1}.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Sliding window fails with negatives.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def subarray_sum(nums, k):\n    count, running = 0, 0\n    seen = {0: 1}                    # prefix sum -> times seen\n    for x in nums:\n        running += x\n        count += seen.get(running - k, 0)\n        seen[running] = seen.get(running, 0) + 1\n    return count","java":"public int subarraySum(int[] nums, int k) {\n    int count = 0, running = 0;\n    Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();   // prefix sum -> times seen\n    seen.put(0, 1);\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        running += x;\n        count += seen.getOrDefault(running - k, 0);\n        seen.merge(running, 1, Integer::sum);\n    }\n    return count;\n}","cpp":"int subarraySum(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    int count = 0, running = 0;\n    unordered_map<int, int> seen{{0, 1}};    // prefix sum -> times seen\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        running += x;\n        if (seen.count(running - k)) count += seen[running - k];\n        seen[running]++;\n    }\n    return count;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-560"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/subarray-sum-equals-k/"]],"ask":"Count contiguous subarrays summing to k (negatives allowed)."},{"i":"p-hashing-303","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Range Sum Query – Immutable","sub":"LeetCode 303 · Easy · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":303,"q":["Range Sum Query – Immutable","how to solve Range Sum Query – Immutable","Range Sum Query – Immutable approach","Range Sum Query – Immutable solution","leetcode 303","lc 303","Answer many sumRange(l, r) calls on a fixed array.","Answer many 'sum from index l to r' questions on an array that never changes."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Many range sums → prefix array. Complexity O(n) build, O(1) query","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Many range sums → prefix array.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Precompute prefix sums once; any range is a subtraction.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Answer many &#x27;sum from index l to r&#x27; questions on an array that never changes.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Add up the range each time: O(n) per query.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>pre[i+1] = pre[i] + nums[i].<\/li><li>sumRange(l, r) = pre[r+1] − pre[l].<\/li><li>Each query is O(1).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Build:<\/b> pre with a leading 0.<\/li><li><b>Query:<\/b> pre[r + 1] − pre[l].<\/li><li><b>Cost:<\/b> O(n) once, O(1) each.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) build, O(1) query<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The leading 0 removes every edge case at l = 0.<\/p>","c":{"py":"class NumArray:\n    def __init__(self, nums):\n        self.pre = [0]\n        for x in nums:\n            self.pre.append(self.pre[-1] + x)    # pre[i] = sum of first i\n\n    def sumRange(self, left, right):\n        return self.pre[right + 1] - self.pre[left]","java":"class NumArray {\n    private final int[] pre;                 // pre[i] = sum of first i\n\n    public NumArray(int[] nums) {\n        pre = new int[nums.length + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + nums[i];\n    }\n\n    public int sumRange(int left, int right) {\n        return pre[right + 1] - pre[left];\n    }\n}","cpp":"class NumArray {\n    vector<int> pre;                         // pre[i] = sum of first i\npublic:\n    NumArray(vector<int>& nums) : pre(nums.size() + 1, 0) {\n        for (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + nums[i];\n    }\n    int sumRange(int left, int right) {\n        return pre[right + 1] - pre[left];\n    }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-303"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/range-sum-query-immutable/"]],"ask":"Answer many sumRange(l, r) calls on a fixed array."},{"i":"p-hashing-169","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Majority Element","sub":"LeetCode 169 · Easy · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":169,"q":["Majority Element","how to solve Majority Element","Majority Element approach","Majority Element solution","leetcode 169","lc 169","Return the element appearing more than n/2 times.","One value appears more than half the time. Which one?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Frequency with O(1) space → voting. Complexity O(n), O(1) space","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Frequency with O(1) space → voting.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Boyer–Moore voting: pair off different values; the majority survives.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> One value appears more than half the time. Which one?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Count everything in a dict: O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Keep a candidate and a count.<\/li><li>If count is 0, take the current number as candidate.<\/li><li>Same as candidate → count+1, different → count−1. Return the candidate.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Easy way:<\/b> Counter.<\/li><li><b>O(1) way:<\/b> Boyer–Moore voting: a candidate and a count.<\/li><li><b>Why:<\/b> The majority outvotes everyone else combined.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1) space<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Only valid when a true majority is guaranteed.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def majority_element(nums):\n    cand, count = None, 0\n    for x in nums:\n        if count == 0:\n            cand = x                  # fresh candidate\n        count += 1 if x == cand else -1\n    return cand","java":"public int majorityElement(int[] nums) {\n    int cand = 0, count = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (count == 0) cand = x;          // fresh candidate\n        count += (x == cand) ? 1 : -1;\n    }\n    return cand;\n}","cpp":"int majorityElement(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int cand = 0, count = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (count == 0) cand = x;          // fresh candidate\n        count += (x == cand) ? 1 : -1;\n    }\n    return cand;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-169"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/majority-element/"]],"ask":"Return the element appearing more than n/2 times."},{"i":"p-hashing-271","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Encode and Decode Strings","sub":"LeetCode 271 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":271,"q":["Encode and Decode Strings","how to solve Encode and Decode Strings","Encode and Decode Strings approach","Encode and Decode Strings solution","leetcode 271","lc 271","Serialize a list of strings into one string and back. Strings may contain any character.","Turn a list of strings into one string and back, even if the strings contain any character."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Arbitrary content → length-prefix, never a bare delimiter. Complexity O(total length)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Arbitrary content → length-prefix, never a bare delimiter.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Prefix every string with its length and a marker, e.g. &#x27;4#lint&#x27;. Lengths tell you exactly where each string ends.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Turn a list of strings into one string and back, even if the strings contain any character.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Join with a separator: breaks when a string contains the separator.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Encode: for each s write len(s) + &#x27;#&#x27; + s.<\/li><li>Decode: read digits up to &#x27;#&#x27;, that&#x27;s the length L.<\/li><li>Take the next L characters as one string; repeat.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Any delimiter could appear inside a string.<\/li><li><b>Trick:<\/b> Length-prefix: “5#hello”.<\/li><li><b>Decode:<\/b> Read digits to “#”, then take exactly n characters.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(total length)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Splitting on a delimiter breaks the moment a string contains it.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def encode(strs):\n    return \"\".join(f\"{len(s)}#{s}\" for s in strs)   # length-prefix each string\n\ndef decode(s):\n    res, i = [], 0\n    while i < len(s):\n        j = s.index(\"#\", i)\n        n = int(s[i:j])\n        res.append(s[j + 1 : j + 1 + n])\n        i = j + 1 + n\n    return res","java":"public String encode(List<String> strs) {\n    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n    for (String s : strs) sb.append(s.length()).append('#').append(s);   // length-prefix\n    return sb.toString();\n}\n\npublic List<String> decode(String s) {\n    List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < s.length()) {\n        int j = s.indexOf('#', i);\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(s.substring(i, j));\n        res.add(s.substring(j + 1, j + 1 + n));\n        i = j + 1 + n;\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"string encode(vector<string>& strs) {\n    string out;\n    for (auto& s : strs) out += to_string(s.size()) + \"#\" + s;   // length-prefix\n    return out;\n}\n\nvector<string> decode(string s) {\n    vector<string> res;\n    size_t i = 0;\n    while (i < s.size()) {\n        size_t j = s.find('#', i);\n        int n = stoi(s.substr(i, j - i));\n        res.push_back(s.substr(j + 1, n));\n        i = j + 1 + n;\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-271"],["LeetCode (Premium)","https://leetcode.com/problems/encode-and-decode-strings/"]],"ask":"Serialize a list of strings into one string and back. Strings may contain any character."},{"i":"p-hashing-41","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"First Missing Positive","sub":"LeetCode 41 · Hard · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":41,"q":["First Missing Positive","how to solve First Missing Positive","First Missing Positive approach","First Missing Positive solution","leetcode 41","lc 41","Smallest missing positive integer, O(n) time, O(1) extra space.","What's the smallest positive integer not in the list? Use O(1) extra space."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Values 1…n with O(1) space → index-as-hash. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Values 1…n with O(1) space → index-as-hash.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> The answer is in 1..n+1. Use the array itself as the set: put value v at index v − 1.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> What&#x27;s the smallest positive integer not in the list? Use O(1) extra space.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort, or use a set: breaks the time or space limit.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each index, while nums[i] is in 1..n and not already in its home, swap it home.<\/li><li>Scan: the first i where nums[i] ≠ i + 1 → answer i + 1.<\/li><li>If all fit, the answer is n + 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Answer is in 1…n + 1.<\/li><li><b>Clean:<\/b> Replace out-of-range values with n + 1.<\/li><li><b>Mark:<\/b> Negate index v − 1 for each v; first positive index + 1 is the answer.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Use abs() when reading, since earlier marks may have flipped signs.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def first_missing_positive(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    for i in range(n):\n        if nums[i] <= 0 or nums[i] > n:\n            nums[i] = n + 1                  # values that can't matter\n    for x in nums:\n        v = abs(x)\n        if v <= n:\n            nums[v - 1] = -abs(nums[v - 1])  # mark \"v is present\"\n    for i in range(n):\n        if nums[i] > 0:\n            return i + 1\n    return n + 1","java":"public int firstMissingPositive(int[] nums) {\n    int n = nums.length;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n        if (nums[i] <= 0 || nums[i] > n) nums[i] = n + 1;   // values that can't matter\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        int v = Math.abs(x);\n        if (v <= n) nums[v - 1] = -Math.abs(nums[v - 1]);   // mark \"v is present\"\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n        if (nums[i] > 0) return i + 1;\n    return n + 1;\n}","cpp":"int firstMissingPositive(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n        if (nums[i] <= 0 || nums[i] > n) nums[i] = n + 1;   // values that can't matter\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        int v = abs(x);\n        if (v <= n) nums[v - 1] = -abs(nums[v - 1]);        // mark \"v is present\"\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n        if (nums[i] > 0) return i + 1;\n    return n + 1;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-41"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/first-missing-positive/"]],"ask":"Smallest missing positive integer, O(n) time, O(1) extra space."},{"i":"p-hashing-48","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Rotate Image","sub":"LeetCode 48 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":48,"q":["Rotate Image","how to solve Rotate Image","Rotate Image approach","Rotate Image solution","leetcode 48","lc 48","Rotate an n×n matrix 90° clockwise in place.","Rotate a square matrix 90° clockwise without making a copy."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Rotate → transpose + flip. Complexity O(n²), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Rotate → transpose + flip.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Clockwise rotation = transpose (swap across the diagonal) + reverse each row.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Rotate a square matrix 90° clockwise without making a copy.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Build a new matrix: O(n²) extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Swap matrix[i][j] with matrix[j][i] for j &gt; i.<\/li><li>Reverse every row.<\/li><li>Done, in place.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Map:<\/b> (r, c) → (c, n − 1 − r).<\/li><li><b>Step 1:<\/b> Transpose.<\/li><li><b>Step 2:<\/b> Reverse each row.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n²), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Transpose only the upper triangle (c &gt; r), or you swap everything back.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def rotate(matrix):\n    n = len(matrix)\n    for r in range(n):\n        for c in range(r + 1, n):\n            matrix[r][c], matrix[c][r] = matrix[c][r], matrix[r][c]   # transpose\n    for row in matrix:\n        row.reverse()                                               # mirror","java":"public void rotate(int[][] matrix) {\n    int n = matrix.length;\n    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++)\n        for (int c = r + 1; c < n; c++) {        // transpose\n            int t = matrix[r][c]; matrix[r][c] = matrix[c][r]; matrix[c][r] = t;\n        }\n    for (int[] row : matrix)                       // mirror each row\n        for (int l = 0, r = n - 1; l < r; l++, r--) {\n            int t = row[l]; row[l] = row[r]; row[r] = t;\n        }\n}","cpp":"void rotate(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {\n    int n = matrix.size();\n    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++)\n        for (int c = r + 1; c < n; c++)\n            swap(matrix[r][c], matrix[c][r]);      // transpose\n    for (auto& row : matrix)\n        reverse(row.begin(), row.end());           // mirror\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-48"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/rotate-image/"]],"ask":"Rotate an n×n matrix 90° clockwise in place."},{"i":"p-hashing-54","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Spiral Matrix","sub":"LeetCode 54 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":54,"q":["Spiral Matrix","how to solve Spiral Matrix","Spiral Matrix approach","Spiral Matrix solution","leetcode 54","lc 54","Return all elements in spiral order.","Read the matrix in a clockwise spiral from the outside in."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Spiral → four shrinking walls. Complexity O(R · C)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Spiral → four shrinking walls.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Keep four walls (top, bottom, left, right) and shrink them after each side.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Read the matrix in a clockwise spiral from the outside in.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Track visited cells and turn when blocked: works, extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Read the top row left→right, then top += 1.<\/li><li>Read the right column top→bottom, then right −= 1.<\/li><li>Bottom row right→left and left column bottom→top if walls haven&#x27;t crossed; repeat.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> Four walls.<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> Top row, right column, bottom row, left column.<\/li><li><b>Guard:<\/b> Re-check walls before the last two edges.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Single-row or single-column leftovers get double-counted without the guards.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def spiral_order(matrix):\n    res = []\n    top, bottom, left, right = 0, len(matrix) - 1, 0, len(matrix[0]) - 1\n    while top <= bottom and left <= right:\n        for c in range(left, right + 1):\n            res.append(matrix[top][c])\n        top += 1\n        for r in range(top, bottom + 1):\n            res.append(matrix[r][right])\n        right -= 1\n        if top <= bottom:\n            for c in range(right, left - 1, -1):\n                res.append(matrix[bottom][c])\n            bottom -= 1\n        if left <= right:\n            for r in range(bottom, top - 1, -1):\n                res.append(matrix[r][left])\n            left += 1\n    return res","java":"public List<Integer> spiralOrder(int[][] matrix) {\n    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    int top = 0, bottom = matrix.length - 1, left = 0, right = matrix[0].length - 1;\n    while (top <= bottom && left <= right) {\n        for (int c = left; c <= right; c++) res.add(matrix[top][c]);\n        top++;\n        for (int r = top; r <= bottom; r++) res.add(matrix[r][right]);\n        right--;\n        if (top <= bottom) {\n            for (int c = right; c >= left; c--) res.add(matrix[bottom][c]);\n            bottom--;\n        }\n        if (left <= right) {\n            for (int r = bottom; r >= top; r--) res.add(matrix[r][left]);\n            left++;\n        }\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> spiralOrder(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {\n    vector<int> res;\n    int top = 0, bottom = matrix.size() - 1, left = 0, right = matrix[0].size() - 1;\n    while (top <= bottom && left <= right) {\n        for (int c = left; c <= right; c++) res.push_back(matrix[top][c]);\n        top++;\n        for (int r = top; r <= bottom; r++) res.push_back(matrix[r][right]);\n        right--;\n        if (top <= bottom) {\n            for (int c = right; c >= left; c--) res.push_back(matrix[bottom][c]);\n            bottom--;\n        }\n        if (left <= right) {\n            for (int r = bottom; r >= top; r--) res.push_back(matrix[r][left]);\n            left++;\n        }\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-54"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/spiral-matrix/"]],"ask":"Return all elements in spiral order."},{"i":"p-hashing-73","k":"prob","s":"hashing","t":"Set Matrix Zeroes","sub":"LeetCode 73 · Medium · Arrays & Hashing","cat":"DSA problems","n":73,"q":["Set Matrix Zeroes","how to solve Set Matrix Zeroes","Set Matrix Zeroes approach","Set Matrix Zeroes solution","leetcode 73","lc 73","If a cell is 0, zero its row and column, in place.","Wherever there's a 0, wipe its whole row and column to 0, in place."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing O(1) marking in a matrix → first row/col as flags. Complexity O(R · C), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> O(1) marking in a matrix → first row/col as flags.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Use the first row and first column as markers (plus one flag for the first column itself).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Wherever there&#x27;s a 0, wipe its whole row and column to 0, in place.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Copy the matrix, or store all zero positions: extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Mark: for each 0 at (r, c) set matrix[r][0] = matrix[0][c] = 0.<\/li><li>Zero cells whose row or column marker is 0 (skip row 0 / col 0 first).<\/li><li>Finally zero row 0 and column 0 if they needed it.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Flags:<\/b> Row 0 and column 0.<\/li><li><b>Remember:<\/b> Whether row 0 / col 0 had zeros originally.<\/li><li><b>Apply:<\/b> Inner cells first, then row 0 / col 0.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Zeroing row 0 too early erases your flags.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def set_zeroes(matrix):\n    R, C = len(matrix), len(matrix[0])\n    row0 = any(matrix[0][c] == 0 for c in range(C))\n    col0 = any(matrix[r][0] == 0 for r in range(R))\n    for r in range(1, R):\n        for c in range(1, C):\n            if matrix[r][c] == 0:\n                matrix[r][0] = matrix[0][c] = 0      # flags live in row 0 / col 0\n    for r in range(1, R):\n        for c in range(1, C):\n            if matrix[r][0] == 0 or matrix[0][c] == 0:\n                matrix[r][c] = 0\n    if row0:\n        for c in range(C):\n            matrix[0][c] = 0\n    if col0:\n        for r in range(R):\n            matrix[r][0] = 0","java":"public void setZeroes(int[][] matrix) {\n    int R = matrix.length, C = matrix[0].length;\n    boolean row0 = false, col0 = false;\n    for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) if (matrix[0][c] == 0) row0 = true;\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) if (matrix[r][0] == 0) col0 = true;\n    for (int r = 1; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 1; c < C; c++)\n            if (matrix[r][c] == 0) matrix[r][0] = matrix[0][c] = 0;   // flags in row 0 / col 0\n    for (int r = 1; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 1; c < C; c++)\n            if (matrix[r][0] == 0 || matrix[0][c] == 0) matrix[r][c] = 0;\n    if (row0) for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) matrix[0][c] = 0;\n    if (col0) for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) matrix[r][0] = 0;\n}","cpp":"void setZeroes(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {\n    int R = matrix.size(), C = matrix[0].size();\n    bool row0 = false, col0 = false;\n    for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) if (matrix[0][c] == 0) row0 = true;\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) if (matrix[r][0] == 0) col0 = true;\n    for (int r = 1; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 1; c < C; c++)\n            if (matrix[r][c] == 0) matrix[r][0] = matrix[0][c] = 0;   // flags in row 0 / col 0\n    for (int r = 1; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 1; c < C; c++)\n            if (matrix[r][0] == 0 || matrix[0][c] == 0) matrix[r][c] = 0;\n    if (row0) for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) matrix[0][c] = 0;\n    if (col0) for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) matrix[r][0] = 0;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","hashing.html#p-73"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/set-matrix-zeroes/"]],"ask":"If a cell is 0, zero its row and column, in place."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-125","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Valid Palindrome","sub":"LeetCode 125 · Easy · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":125,"q":["Valid Palindrome","how to solve Valid Palindrome","Valid Palindrome approach","Valid Palindrome solution","leetcode 125","lc 125","Is s a palindrome, considering only letters and digits, ignoring case?","Ignoring punctuation, spaces and case, does it read the same both ways?"],"w":"Two Pointers Palindrome → converge from both ends. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Palindrome → converge from both ends.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Two pointers from both ends, skipping non-alphanumerics.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Ignoring punctuation, spaces and case, does it read the same both ways?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Build a cleaned, reversed copy and compare: O(n) extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>l = 0, r = end.<\/li><li>Move l right / r left past non-letters and non-digits.<\/li><li>Compare lowercase letters; mismatch → False. Move both inward.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> Both ends.<\/li><li><b>Skip:<\/b> Non-alphanumerics on each side.<\/li><li><b>Compare:<\/b> Lower-cased characters.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Building a cleaned string is fine but costs O(n) space.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def is_palindrome(s):\n    l, r = 0, len(s) - 1\n    while l < r:\n        while l < r and not s[l].isalnum():\n            l += 1\n        while l < r and not s[r].isalnum():\n            r -= 1\n        if s[l].lower() != s[r].lower():\n            return False\n        l, r = l + 1, r - 1\n    return True","java":"public boolean isPalindrome(String s) {\n    int l = 0, r = s.length() - 1;\n    while (l < r) {\n        while (l < r && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(l))) l++;\n        while (l < r && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(r))) r--;\n        if (Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(l)) != Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(r)))\n            return false;\n        l++; r--;\n    }\n    return true;\n}","cpp":"bool isPalindrome(string s) {\n    int l = 0, r = s.size() - 1;\n    while (l < r) {\n        while (l < r && !isalnum(s[l])) l++;\n        while (l < r && !isalnum(s[r])) r--;\n        if (tolower(s[l]) != tolower(s[r])) return false;\n        l++; r--;\n    }\n    return true;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-125"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-palindrome/"]],"ask":"Is s a palindrome, considering only letters and digits, ignoring case?"},{"i":"p-two-pointers-167","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Two Sum II – Input Array Is Sorted","sub":"LeetCode 167 · Medium · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":167,"q":["Two Sum II – Input Array Is Sorted","how to solve Two Sum II – Input Array Is Sorted","Two Sum II – Input Array Is Sorted approach","Two Sum II – Input Array Is Sorted solution","leetcode 167","lc 167","Return 1-indexed positions of two numbers summing to target, O(1) space.","The array is sorted. Find the two positions (1-indexed) that sum to target."],"w":"Two Pointers Sorted pair sum → converge. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Sorted pair sum → converge.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sorted means: too small → move left pointer up; too big → move right pointer down.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The array is sorted. Find the two positions (1-indexed) that sum to target.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Every pair: O(n²). A dict works but uses O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>l = 0, r = n − 1.<\/li><li>sum &lt; target → l += 1; sum &gt; target → r −= 1.<\/li><li>Equal → return [l + 1, r + 1].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> Both ends.<\/li><li><b>Move:<\/b> Too small → l, too big → r.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> Exact match.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The answer is 1-indexed.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def two_sum_sorted(nums, target):\n    l, r = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while l < r:\n        s = nums[l] + nums[r]\n        if s == target:\n            return [l + 1, r + 1]       # problem wants 1-indexed\n        if s < target:\n            l += 1                      # need bigger: only l can help\n        else:\n            r -= 1                      # need smaller: only r can help","java":"public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int l = 0, r = nums.length - 1;\n    while (l < r) {\n        int s = nums[l] + nums[r];\n        if (s == target) return new int[]{l + 1, r + 1};   // problem wants 1-indexed\n        if (s < target) l++;          // need bigger: only l can help\n        else r--;                     // need smaller: only r can help\n    }\n    return new int[0];\n}","cpp":"vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    int l = 0, r = nums.size() - 1;\n    while (l < r) {\n        int s = nums[l] + nums[r];\n        if (s == target) return {l + 1, r + 1};   // problem wants 1-indexed\n        if (s < target) l++;          // need bigger: only l can help\n        else r--;                     // need smaller: only r can help\n    }\n    return {};\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-167"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum-ii-input-array-is-sorted/"]],"ask":"Return 1-indexed positions of two numbers summing to target, O(1) space."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-15","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"3Sum","sub":"LeetCode 15 · Medium · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":15,"q":["3Sum","how to solve 3Sum","3Sum approach","3Sum solution","leetcode 15","lc 15","All unique triplets summing to 0.","Find every distinct triple of numbers that adds up to 0."],"w":"Two Pointers K-sum → sort + fix + two pointers. Complexity O(n²)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> K-sum → sort + fix + two pointers.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort, fix one number, then two-pointer the rest for −fixed.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Find every distinct triple of numbers that adds up to 0.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Three nested loops: O(n³), plus deduplication.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort the array.<\/li><li>For each i (skip duplicates of nums[i]), run two pointers on i+1..end.<\/li><li>On a hit, record it and skip duplicate values on both sides.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> First.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> nums[i], skip repeats.<\/li><li><b>Search:<\/b> Two pointers on the rest; skip repeated seconds.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n²)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Dedupe by skipping neighbours, not with a set of tuples.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def three_sum(nums):\n    nums.sort()\n    res = []\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 2):\n        if i > 0 and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]:\n            continue                          # same first number: skip\n        l, r = i + 1, len(nums) - 1\n        while l < r:\n            s = nums[i] + nums[l] + nums[r]\n            if s < 0:\n                l += 1\n            elif s > 0:\n                r -= 1\n            else:\n                res.append([nums[i], nums[l], nums[r]])\n                l += 1\n                while l < r and nums[l] == nums[l - 1]:\n                    l += 1                    # skip duplicate second numbers\n    return res","java":"public List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] nums) {\n    Arrays.sort(nums);\n    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 2; i++) {\n        if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;   // same first number: skip\n        int l = i + 1, r = nums.length - 1;\n        while (l < r) {\n            int s = nums[i] + nums[l] + nums[r];\n            if (s < 0) l++;\n            else if (s > 0) r--;\n            else {\n                res.add(List.of(nums[i], nums[l], nums[r]));\n                l++;\n                while (l < r && nums[l] == nums[l - 1]) l++;   // skip duplicate second numbers\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> threeSum(vector<int>& nums) {\n    sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    int n = nums.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n - 2; i++) {\n        if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;   // same first number: skip\n        int l = i + 1, r = n - 1;\n        while (l < r) {\n            int s = nums[i] + nums[l] + nums[r];\n            if (s < 0) l++;\n            else if (s > 0) r--;\n            else {\n                res.push_back({nums[i], nums[l], nums[r]});\n                l++;\n                while (l < r && nums[l] == nums[l - 1]) l++;   // skip duplicate second numbers\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-15"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/3sum/"]],"ask":"All unique triplets summing to 0."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-16","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"3Sum Closest","sub":"LeetCode 16 · Medium · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":16,"q":["3Sum Closest","how to solve 3Sum Closest","3Sum Closest approach","3Sum Closest solution","leetcode 16","lc 16","Sum of three numbers closest to target.","Which sum of three numbers is closest to target?"],"w":"Two Pointers K-sum → sort + fix + two pointers. Complexity O(n²)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> K-sum → sort + fix + two pointers.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Same as 3Sum: sort, fix one, two pointers; keep the best distance.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Which sum of three numbers is closest to target?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> All triples: O(n³).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort.<\/li><li>For each i, l = i+1, r = end; compute s.<\/li><li>Update the closest; s &lt; target → l += 1, else r −= 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> First.<\/li><li><b>Track:<\/b> Best by |sum − target|.<\/li><li><b>Move:<\/b> Same rule as 3Sum.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n²)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Return early on an exact match.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def three_sum_closest(nums, target):\n    nums.sort()\n    best = nums[0] + nums[1] + nums[2]\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 2):\n        l, r = i + 1, len(nums) - 1\n        while l < r:\n            s = nums[i] + nums[l] + nums[r]\n            if abs(s - target) < abs(best - target):\n                best = s\n            if s < target:\n                l += 1\n            elif s > target:\n                r -= 1\n            else:\n                return s\n    return best","java":"public int threeSumClosest(int[] nums, int target) {\n    Arrays.sort(nums);\n    int best = nums[0] + nums[1] + nums[2];\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 2; i++) {\n        int l = i + 1, r = nums.length - 1;\n        while (l < r) {\n            int s = nums[i] + nums[l] + nums[r];\n            if (Math.abs(s - target) < Math.abs(best - target)) best = s;\n            if (s < target) l++;\n            else if (s > target) r--;\n            else return s;\n        }\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int threeSumClosest(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    int best = nums[0] + nums[1] + nums[2], n = nums.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n - 2; i++) {\n        int l = i + 1, r = n - 1;\n        while (l < r) {\n            int s = nums[i] + nums[l] + nums[r];\n            if (abs(s - target) < abs(best - target)) best = s;\n            if (s < target) l++;\n            else if (s > target) r--;\n            else return s;\n        }\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-16"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/3sum-closest/"]],"ask":"Sum of three numbers closest to target."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-18","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"4Sum","sub":"LeetCode 18 · Medium · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":18,"q":["4Sum","how to solve 4Sum","4Sum approach","4Sum solution","leetcode 18","lc 18","All unique quadruplets summing to target.","Find every distinct group of four numbers that adds up to target."],"w":"Two Pointers K-sum → one more fixed loop per extra number. Complexity O(n³)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> K-sum → one more fixed loop per extra number.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort; two outer loops fix two numbers, two pointers find the other two.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Find every distinct group of four numbers that adds up to target.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Four loops: O(n⁴).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort.<\/li><li>Loop i, then j &gt; i (skip duplicates each time).<\/li><li>Two pointers on the rest for target − nums[i] − nums[j].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> First.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> Two numbers with nested loops, each skipping repeats.<\/li><li><b>Search:<\/b> Two pointers for the last two.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n³)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Skip check for the second loop is b &gt; a + 1, not b &gt; 0.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def four_sum(nums, target):\n    nums.sort()\n    n, res = len(nums), []\n    for a in range(n - 3):\n        if a > 0 and nums[a] == nums[a - 1]:\n            continue\n        for b in range(a + 1, n - 2):\n            if b > a + 1 and nums[b] == nums[b - 1]:\n                continue\n            l, r = b + 1, n - 1\n            while l < r:\n                s = nums[a] + nums[b] + nums[l] + nums[r]\n                if s < target:\n                    l += 1\n                elif s > target:\n                    r -= 1\n                else:\n                    res.append([nums[a], nums[b], nums[l], nums[r]])\n                    l += 1\n                    while l < r and nums[l] == nums[l - 1]:\n                        l += 1\n    return res","java":"public List<List<Integer>> fourSum(int[] nums, int target) {\n    Arrays.sort(nums);\n    int n = nums.length;\n    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int a = 0; a < n - 3; a++) {\n        if (a > 0 && nums[a] == nums[a - 1]) continue;\n        for (int b = a + 1; b < n - 2; b++) {\n            if (b > a + 1 && nums[b] == nums[b - 1]) continue;\n            int l = b + 1, r = n - 1;\n            while (l < r) {\n                long s = (long) nums[a] + nums[b] + nums[l] + nums[r];   // avoid overflow\n                if (s < target) l++;\n                else if (s > target) r--;\n                else {\n                    res.add(List.of(nums[a], nums[b], nums[l], nums[r]));\n                    l++;\n                    while (l < r && nums[l] == nums[l - 1]) l++;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> fourSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    for (int a = 0; a < n - 3; a++) {\n        if (a > 0 && nums[a] == nums[a - 1]) continue;\n        for (int b = a + 1; b < n - 2; b++) {\n            if (b > a + 1 && nums[b] == nums[b - 1]) continue;\n            int l = b + 1, r = n - 1;\n            while (l < r) {\n                long long s = (long long)nums[a] + nums[b] + nums[l] + nums[r];   // avoid overflow\n                if (s < target) l++;\n                else if (s > target) r--;\n                else {\n                    res.push_back({nums[a], nums[b], nums[l], nums[r]});\n                    l++;\n                    while (l < r && nums[l] == nums[l - 1]) l++;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-18"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/4sum/"]],"ask":"All unique quadruplets summing to target."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-11","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Container With Most Water","sub":"LeetCode 11 · Medium · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":11,"q":["Container With Most Water","how to solve Container With Most Water","Container With Most Water approach","Container With Most Water solution","leetcode 11","lc 11","Two lines that hold the most water.","Pick two vertical lines that, with the x-axis, hold the most water."],"w":"Two Pointers Container → move the shorter side. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Container → move the shorter side.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Start widest. The shorter line limits the water, so only moving it can help.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Pick two vertical lines that, with the x-axis, hold the most water.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> All pairs: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>l = 0, r = end.<\/li><li>area = (r − l) × min(h[l], h[r]); keep the max.<\/li><li>Move whichever pointer has the shorter line.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Start:<\/b> Widest.<\/li><li><b>Area:<\/b> min height × width.<\/li><li><b>Move:<\/b> The shorter wall.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Moving the taller wall can skip the optimum.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_area(height):\n    l, r = 0, len(height) - 1\n    best = 0\n    while l < r:\n        area = min(height[l], height[r]) * (r - l)\n        best = max(best, area)\n        if height[l] < height[r]:\n            l += 1          # shorter wall can never do better\n        else:\n            r -= 1\n    return best","java":"public int maxArea(int[] height) {\n    int l = 0, r = height.length - 1, best = 0;\n    while (l < r) {\n        int area = Math.min(height[l], height[r]) * (r - l);\n        best = Math.max(best, area);\n        if (height[l] < height[r]) l++;   // shorter wall can never do better\n        else r--;\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int maxArea(vector<int>& height) {\n    int l = 0, r = height.size() - 1, best = 0;\n    while (l < r) {\n        int area = min(height[l], height[r]) * (r - l);\n        best = max(best, area);\n        if (height[l] < height[r]) l++;   // shorter wall can never do better\n        else r--;\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-11"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/container-with-most-water/"]],"ask":"Two lines that hold the most water."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-42","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Trapping Rain Water","sub":"LeetCode 42 · Hard · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":42,"q":["Trapping Rain Water","how to solve Trapping Rain Water","Trapping Rain Water approach","Trapping Rain Water solution","leetcode 42","lc 42","Total water trapped between bars.","How much rain water collects between the bars?"],"w":"Two Pointers Trap water → two pointers with side maxima. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Trap water → two pointers with side maxima.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Water at i = min(tallest left, tallest right) − h[i]. Two pointers know the smaller side&#x27;s max for sure.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How much rain water collects between the bars?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> For each bar scan left and right for the tallest: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>l, r at ends; leftMax, rightMax = 0.<\/li><li>If h[l] &lt; h[r]: water += leftMax − h[l] (update leftMax first), l += 1.<\/li><li>Else do the same on the right side.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Formula:<\/b> min(maxL, maxR) − h.<\/li><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> Both ends with lmax, rmax.<\/li><li><b>Move:<\/b> Settle the lower side.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Prefix/suffix max arrays also work in O(n) space; mention both.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def trap(height):\n    l, r = 0, len(height) - 1\n    lmax = rmax = water = 0\n    while l < r:\n        if height[l] < height[r]:\n            lmax = max(lmax, height[l])\n            water += lmax - height[l]     # right side is taller: lmax is the cap\n            l += 1\n        else:\n            rmax = max(rmax, height[r])\n            water += rmax - height[r]\n            r -= 1\n    return water","java":"public int trap(int[] height) {\n    int l = 0, r = height.length - 1, lmax = 0, rmax = 0, water = 0;\n    while (l < r) {\n        if (height[l] < height[r]) {\n            lmax = Math.max(lmax, height[l]);\n            water += lmax - height[l];     // right side is taller: lmax is the cap\n            l++;\n        } else {\n            rmax = Math.max(rmax, height[r]);\n            water += rmax - height[r];\n            r--;\n        }\n    }\n    return water;\n}","cpp":"int trap(vector<int>& height) {\n    int l = 0, r = height.size() - 1, lmax = 0, rmax = 0, water = 0;\n    while (l < r) {\n        if (height[l] < height[r]) {\n            lmax = max(lmax, height[l]);\n            water += lmax - height[l];     // right side is taller: lmax is the cap\n            l++;\n        } else {\n            rmax = max(rmax, height[r]);\n            water += rmax - height[r];\n            r--;\n        }\n    }\n    return water;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-42"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/trapping-rain-water/"]],"ask":"Total water trapped between bars."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-26","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array","sub":"LeetCode 26 · Easy · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":26,"q":["Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array","how to solve Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array","Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array approach","Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array solution","leetcode 26","lc 26","Dedupe in place; return the count of unique values.","Keep one copy of each value at the front of a sorted array; return how many."],"w":"Two Pointers In place → writer and reader. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> In place → writer and reader.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Slow pointer marks where the next unique value goes; fast pointer scans.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Keep one copy of each value at the front of a sorted array; return how many.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Use a set and rebuild: extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>k = 1 (first value is always kept).<\/li><li>For i from 1: if nums[i] ≠ nums[k − 1], write nums[k] = nums[i], k += 1.<\/li><li>Return k.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Writer:<\/b> w = 1.<\/li><li><b>Reader:<\/b> Scans from 1.<\/li><li><b>Keep:<\/b> When it differs from nums[w − 1].<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Compare with the last kept value, not the previous index.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def remove_duplicates(nums):\n    w = 1                          # nums[:w] is the finished answer\n    for r in range(1, len(nums)):\n        if nums[r] != nums[w - 1]:\n            nums[w] = nums[r]      # worth keeping: write it\n            w += 1\n    return w","java":"public int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {\n    int w = 1;                           // nums[0..w) is the finished answer\n    for (int r = 1; r < nums.length; r++)\n        if (nums[r] != nums[w - 1])\n            nums[w++] = nums[r];         // worth keeping: write it\n    return w;\n}","cpp":"int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int w = 1;                           // nums[0..w) is the finished answer\n    for (int r = 1; r < (int)nums.size(); r++)\n        if (nums[r] != nums[w - 1])\n            nums[w++] = nums[r];         // worth keeping: write it\n    return w;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-26"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array/"]],"ask":"Dedupe in place; return the count of unique values."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-283","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Move Zeroes","sub":"LeetCode 283 · Easy · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":283,"q":["Move Zeroes","how to solve Move Zeroes","Move Zeroes approach","Move Zeroes solution","leetcode 283","lc 283","Move zeros to the end, keep order of the rest, in place.","Push all zeros to the end, keeping the other numbers in order."],"w":"Two Pointers In place → writer and reader. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> In place → writer and reader.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Write pointer: copy every non-zero forward, then fill the rest with zeros (or swap).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Push all zeros to the end, keeping the other numbers in order.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Build a new array: extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>w = 0.<\/li><li>For each non-zero x, swap it into position w, w += 1.<\/li><li>Zeros naturally end up after w.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Writer:<\/b> w = 0.<\/li><li><b>Reader:<\/b> Every index.<\/li><li><b>Swap:<\/b> Non-zero to w.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Swapping (not copying) leaves zeros at the end for free.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def move_zeroes(nums):\n    w = 0\n    for r in range(len(nums)):\n        if nums[r] != 0:\n            nums[w], nums[r] = nums[r], nums[w]   # swap non-zero forward\n            w += 1","java":"public void moveZeroes(int[] nums) {\n    int w = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < nums.length; r++)\n        if (nums[r] != 0) {                // swap non-zero forward\n            int t = nums[w]; nums[w] = nums[r]; nums[r] = t;\n            w++;\n        }\n}","cpp":"void moveZeroes(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int w = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)nums.size(); r++)\n        if (nums[r] != 0)\n            swap(nums[w++], nums[r]);      // swap non-zero forward\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-283"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/move-zeroes/"]],"ask":"Move zeros to the end, keep order of the rest, in place."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-75","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Sort Colors","sub":"LeetCode 75 · Medium · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":75,"q":["Sort Colors","how to solve Sort Colors","Sort Colors approach","Sort Colors solution","leetcode 75","lc 75","Sort an array of 0s, 1s and 2s in one pass.","Sort 0s, 1s and 2s in a single pass, in place."],"w":"Two Pointers Three groups → Dutch flag. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Three groups → Dutch flag.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Dutch flag: three regions. low = next 0 spot, high = next 2 spot, mid scans.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sort 0s, 1s and 2s in a single pass, in place.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Counting sort uses two passes.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>low = mid = 0, high = end.<\/li><li>nums[mid] = 0 → swap with low, low++, mid++. = 1 → mid++.<\/li><li>= 2 → swap with high, high−− (don&#x27;t move mid: recheck the swapped value).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> lo, i, hi.<\/li><li><b>0:<\/b> Swap to lo, advance lo and i.<\/li><li><b>2:<\/b> Swap to hi, shrink hi, keep i.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Loop condition is i ≤ hi.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def sort_colors(nums):\n    lo, i, hi = 0, 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while i <= hi:\n        if nums[i] == 0:\n            nums[lo], nums[i] = nums[i], nums[lo]\n            lo += 1\n            i += 1\n        elif nums[i] == 2:\n            nums[i], nums[hi] = nums[hi], nums[i]\n            hi -= 1                 # don't move i: swapped-in value is unchecked\n        else:\n            i += 1","java":"public void sortColors(int[] nums) {\n    int lo = 0, i = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;\n    while (i <= hi) {\n        if (nums[i] == 0) {\n            int t = nums[lo]; nums[lo] = nums[i]; nums[i] = t;\n            lo++; i++;\n        } else if (nums[i] == 2) {\n            int t = nums[i]; nums[i] = nums[hi]; nums[hi] = t;\n            hi--;                  // don't move i: swapped-in value is unchecked\n        } else i++;\n    }\n}","cpp":"void sortColors(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int lo = 0, i = 0, hi = nums.size() - 1;\n    while (i <= hi) {\n        if (nums[i] == 0) swap(nums[lo++], nums[i++]);\n        else if (nums[i] == 2) swap(nums[i], nums[hi--]);   // don't move i: swapped-in value is unchecked\n        else i++;\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-75"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-colors/"]],"ask":"Sort an array of 0s, 1s and 2s in one pass."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-88","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Merge Sorted Array","sub":"LeetCode 88 · Easy · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":88,"q":["Merge Sorted Array","how to solve Merge Sorted Array","Merge Sorted Array approach","Merge Sorted Array solution","leetcode 88","lc 88","Merge nums2 into nums1 in place.","nums1 has room at the end. Merge sorted nums2 into it, keeping it sorted."],"w":"Two Pointers Spare space at the end → fill backwards. Complexity O(m + n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Spare space at the end → fill backwards.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Fill from the back: the largest remaining value goes into the last free slot.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> nums1 has room at the end. Merge sorted nums2 into it, keeping it sorted.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Merge into a new array and copy back: extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>i = m−1, j = n−1, k = m+n−1.<\/li><li>Put the larger of nums1[i], nums2[j] at k; move that pointer and k.<\/li><li>Copy any nums2 leftovers.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Spare space at the end.<\/li><li><b>Fill:<\/b> From the back.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> When nums2 is used up.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(m + n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Guard i ≥ 0 before reading nums1[i].<\/p>","c":{"py":"def merge(nums1, m, nums2, n):\n    i, j, w = m - 1, n - 1, m + n - 1      # fill from the back\n    while j >= 0:\n        if i >= 0 and nums1[i] > nums2[j]:\n            nums1[w] = nums1[i]\n            i -= 1\n        else:\n            nums1[w] = nums2[j]\n            j -= 1\n        w -= 1","java":"public void merge(int[] nums1, int m, int[] nums2, int n) {\n    int i = m - 1, j = n - 1, w = m + n - 1;      // fill from the back\n    while (j >= 0) {\n        if (i >= 0 && nums1[i] > nums2[j]) nums1[w] = nums1[i--];\n        else nums1[w] = nums2[j--];\n        w--;\n    }\n}","cpp":"void merge(vector<int>& nums1, int m, vector<int>& nums2, int n) {\n    int i = m - 1, j = n - 1, w = m + n - 1;      // fill from the back\n    while (j >= 0) {\n        if (i >= 0 && nums1[i] > nums2[j]) nums1[w] = nums1[i--];\n        else nums1[w] = nums2[j--];\n        w--;\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-88"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-sorted-array/"]],"ask":"Merge nums2 into nums1 in place."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-977","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Squares of a Sorted Array","sub":"LeetCode 977 · Easy · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":977,"q":["Squares of a Sorted Array","how to solve Squares of a Sorted Array","Squares of a Sorted Array approach","Squares of a Sorted Array solution","leetcode 977","lc 977","Sorted squares in O(n).","Square each number of a sorted array (may contain negatives) and return them sorted."],"w":"Two Pointers Extremes at the ends → converge, fill backwards. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Extremes at the ends → converge, fill backwards.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> The largest square is at one of the two ends.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Square each number of a sorted array (may contain negatives) and return them sorted.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Square then sort: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>l = 0, r = end, fill result from the back.<\/li><li>Compare |nums[l]| and |nums[r]|; place the bigger square.<\/li><li>Move that pointer inward.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Largest squares at the ends.<\/li><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> Both ends.<\/li><li><b>Fill:<\/b> From the back.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Squaring then sorting is O(n log n).<\/p>","c":{"py":"def sorted_squares(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    res = [0] * n\n    l, r = 0, n - 1\n    for w in range(n - 1, -1, -1):          # biggest square sits at an end\n        if abs(nums[l]) > abs(nums[r]):\n            res[w] = nums[l] ** 2\n            l += 1\n        else:\n            res[w] = nums[r] ** 2\n            r -= 1\n    return res","java":"public int[] sortedSquares(int[] nums) {\n    int n = nums.length, l = 0, r = n - 1;\n    int[] res = new int[n];\n    for (int w = n - 1; w >= 0; w--) {          // biggest square sits at an end\n        if (Math.abs(nums[l]) > Math.abs(nums[r])) { res[w] = nums[l] * nums[l]; l++; }\n        else { res[w] = nums[r] * nums[r]; r--; }\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> sortedSquares(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size(), l = 0, r = n - 1;\n    vector<int> res(n);\n    for (int w = n - 1; w >= 0; w--) {          // biggest square sits at an end\n        if (abs(nums[l]) > abs(nums[r])) { res[w] = nums[l] * nums[l]; l++; }\n        else { res[w] = nums[r] * nums[r]; r--; }\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-977"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/squares-of-a-sorted-array/"]],"ask":"Sorted squares in O(n)."},{"i":"p-two-pointers-680","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Valid Palindrome II","sub":"LeetCode 680 · Easy · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":680,"q":["Valid Palindrome II","how to solve Valid Palindrome II","Valid Palindrome II approach","Valid Palindrome II solution","leetcode 680","lc 680","Can s become a palindrome after deleting at most one character?","Can you make it a palindrome by deleting at most one character?"],"w":"Two Pointers Palindrome with one deletion → branch once on mismatch. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Palindrome with one deletion → branch once on mismatch.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Walk inward; at the first mismatch you get one chance: skip left OR skip right.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Can you make it a palindrome by deleting at most one character?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try deleting each character: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>l, r from both ends while they match.<\/li><li>On mismatch, check if s[l+1..r] or s[l..r−1] is a palindrome.<\/li><li>No mismatch → True.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> Both ends.<\/li><li><b>Mismatch:<\/b> Try skipping left or skipping right.<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> Plain palindrome check on the rest.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Branch only once; nested branching is O(2ⁿ).<\/p>","c":{"py":"def valid_palindrome(s):\n    def is_pal(l, r):\n        while l < r:\n            if s[l] != s[r]:\n                return False\n            l, r = l + 1, r - 1\n        return True\n    l, r = 0, len(s) - 1\n    while l < r:\n        if s[l] != s[r]:\n            return is_pal(l + 1, r) or is_pal(l, r - 1)   # one deletion\n        l, r = l + 1, r - 1\n    return True","java":"public boolean validPalindrome(String s) {\n    int l = 0, r = s.length() - 1;\n    while (l < r) {\n        if (s.charAt(l) != s.charAt(r))\n            return isPal(s, l + 1, r) || isPal(s, l, r - 1);   // one deletion\n        l++; r--;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\nprivate boolean isPal(String s, int l, int r) {\n    while (l < r) {\n        if (s.charAt(l) != s.charAt(r)) return false;\n        l++; r--;\n    }\n    return true;\n}","cpp":"bool validPalindrome(string s) {\n    auto isPal = [&](int l, int r) {\n        while (l < r) {\n            if (s[l] != s[r]) return false;\n            l++; r--;\n        }\n        return true;\n    };\n    int l = 0, r = s.size() - 1;\n    while (l < r) {\n        if (s[l] != s[r]) return isPal(l + 1, r) || isPal(l, r - 1);   // one deletion\n        l++; r--;\n    }\n    return true;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-680"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-palindrome-ii/"]],"ask":"Can s become a palindrome after deleting at most one character?"},{"i":"p-two-pointers-392","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Is Subsequence","sub":"LeetCode 392 · Easy · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":392,"q":["Is Subsequence","how to solve Is Subsequence","Is Subsequence approach","Is Subsequence solution","leetcode 392","lc 392","Is s a subsequence of t?","Can you get s by deleting some letters of t (keeping order)?"],"w":"Two Pointers Subsequence → one pointer per string. Complexity O(len t)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Subsequence → one pointer per string.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Greedy: match s&#x27;s letters in order as you scan t.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Can you get s by deleting some letters of t (keeping order)?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Generate subsequences: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>i over s, j over t.<\/li><li>If s[i] == t[j], i += 1. Always j += 1.<\/li><li>True if i reaches len(s).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pointer:<\/b> i into s.<\/li><li><b>Scan:<\/b> Every char of t.<\/li><li><b>Match:<\/b> Advance i.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(len t)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> For many s queries on one t, precompute next-occurrence tables.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def is_subsequence(s, t):\n    i = 0\n    for ch in t:\n        if i < len(s) and s[i] == ch:\n            i += 1                  # matched the next needed char\n    return i == len(s)","java":"public boolean isSubsequence(String s, String t) {\n    int i = 0;\n    for (char ch : t.toCharArray())\n        if (i < s.length() && s.charAt(i) == ch) i++;   // matched the next needed char\n    return i == s.length();\n}","cpp":"bool isSubsequence(string s, string t) {\n    size_t i = 0;\n    for (char ch : t)\n        if (i < s.size() && s[i] == ch) i++;   // matched the next needed char\n    return i == s.size();\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-392"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/is-subsequence/"]],"ask":"Is s a subsequence of t?"},{"i":"p-two-pointers-881","k":"prob","s":"two-pointers","t":"Boats to Save People","sub":"LeetCode 881 · Medium · Two Pointers","cat":"DSA problems","n":881,"q":["Boats to Save People","how to solve Boats to Save People","Boats to Save People approach","Boats to Save People solution","leetcode 881","lc 881","Each boat carries at most two people under a weight limit. Minimum boats?","Each boat holds at most 2 people and a weight limit. Fewest boats?"],"w":"Two Pointers Pairing extremes → sort + converge. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Pairing extremes → sort + converge.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort. The heaviest person should share with the lightest if possible; otherwise goes alone.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each boat holds at most 2 people and a weight limit. Fewest boats?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try pairings: expensive.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort; l = lightest, r = heaviest.<\/li><li>If people[l] + people[r] ≤ limit, l += 1.<\/li><li>r −= 1 and boats += 1 every step.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> By weight.<\/li><li><b>Pair:<\/b> Heaviest with the lightest if they fit.<\/li><li><b>Count:<\/b> Heaviest always leaves.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Loop while l ≤ r, so a lone middle person gets a boat.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def num_rescue_boats(people, limit):\n    people.sort()\n    l, r, boats = 0, len(people) - 1, 0\n    while l <= r:\n        if people[l] + people[r] <= limit:\n            l += 1                  # lightest rides with the heaviest\n        r -= 1                      # heaviest always leaves\n        boats += 1\n    return boats","java":"public int numRescueBoats(int[] people, int limit) {\n    Arrays.sort(people);\n    int l = 0, r = people.length - 1, boats = 0;\n    while (l <= r) {\n        if (people[l] + people[r] <= limit) l++;   // lightest rides with the heaviest\n        r--;                                       // heaviest always leaves\n        boats++;\n    }\n    return boats;\n}","cpp":"int numRescueBoats(vector<int>& people, int limit) {\n    sort(people.begin(), people.end());\n    int l = 0, r = people.size() - 1, boats = 0;\n    while (l <= r) {\n        if (people[l] + people[r] <= limit) l++;   // lightest rides with the heaviest\n        r--;                                       // heaviest always leaves\n        boats++;\n    }\n    return boats;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","two-pointers.html#p-881"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/boats-to-save-people/"]],"ask":"Each boat carries at most two people under a weight limit. Minimum boats?"},{"i":"p-sliding-window-121","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock","sub":"LeetCode 121 · Easy · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":121,"q":["Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock","how to solve Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock","Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock approach","Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock solution","leetcode 121","lc 121","Max profit from one buy then one later sell.","Buy one day, sell a later day. Biggest profit?"],"w":"Sliding Window Later minus earlier → min so far. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Later minus earlier → min so far.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Track the cheapest price so far; today&#x27;s profit = price − cheapest.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Buy one day, sell a later day. Biggest profit?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Every buy/sell pair: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>minPrice = ∞, best = 0.<\/li><li>For each price: best = max(best, price − minPrice).<\/li><li>minPrice = min(minPrice, price).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Track:<\/b> Cheapest price so far.<\/li><li><b>Each day:<\/b> Profit if selling today.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Max of those.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> You can’t sell before buying, so update the min first or second consistently.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_profit(prices):\n    lo, best = float(\"inf\"), 0\n    for p in prices:\n        lo = min(lo, p)                 # cheapest buy so far\n        best = max(best, p - lo)        # sell today\n    return best","java":"public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n    int lo = Integer.MAX_VALUE, best = 0;\n    for (int p : prices) {\n        lo = Math.min(lo, p);                // cheapest buy so far\n        best = Math.max(best, p - lo);       // sell today\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int maxProfit(vector<int>& prices) {\n    int lo = INT_MAX, best = 0;\n    for (int p : prices) {\n        lo = min(lo, p);                     // cheapest buy so far\n        best = max(best, p - lo);            // sell today\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-121"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock/"]],"ask":"Max profit from one buy then one later sell."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-3","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters","sub":"LeetCode 3 · Medium · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":3,"q":["Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters","how to solve Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters","Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters approach","Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters solution","leetcode 3","lc 3","Length of the longest substring with no repeated characters.","Longest stretch of the string with no repeated character."],"w":"Sliding Window Longest valid → expand, shrink while invalid. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Longest valid → expand, shrink while invalid.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Grow a window to the right; when a repeat enters, shrink from the left until it&#x27;s gone.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Longest stretch of the string with no repeated character.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check every substring: O(n²)–O(n³).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Window [l, r], a set (or last-seen index) of its characters.<\/li><li>Add s[r]; while it&#x27;s a duplicate, remove s[l], l += 1.<\/li><li>Track r − l + 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Window:<\/b> Set of chars.<\/li><li><b>Break:<\/b> New char already inside.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> Shrink until it leaves (or jump via last index).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> In the jump version, only jump if last[ch] ≥ l.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def length_of_longest_substring(s):\n    window = set()\n    l = best = 0\n    for r, ch in enumerate(s):\n        while ch in window:          # repeat: shrink until it leaves\n            window.remove(s[l])\n            l += 1\n        window.add(ch)\n        best = max(best, r - l + 1)\n    return best","java":"public int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {\n    Set<Character> window = new HashSet<>();\n    int l = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < s.length(); r++) {\n        char ch = s.charAt(r);\n        while (window.contains(ch)) {     // repeat: shrink until it leaves\n            window.remove(s.charAt(l));\n            l++;\n        }\n        window.add(ch);\n        best = Math.max(best, r - l + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int lengthOfLongestSubstring(string s) {\n    unordered_set<char> window;\n    int l = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)s.size(); r++) {\n        char ch = s[r];\n        while (window.count(ch)) {        // repeat: shrink until it leaves\n            window.erase(s[l]);\n            l++;\n        }\n        window.insert(ch);\n        best = max(best, r - l + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-3"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-substring-without-repeating-characters/"]],"ask":"Length of the longest substring with no repeated characters."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-424","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Longest Repeating Character Replacement","sub":"LeetCode 424 · Medium · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":424,"q":["Longest Repeating Character Replacement","how to solve Longest Repeating Character Replacement","Longest Repeating Character Replacement approach","Longest Repeating Character Replacement solution","leetcode 424","lc 424","Longest substring you can make one letter with at most k replacements.","Longest substring you can turn into all-one-letter with at most k changes."],"w":"Sliding Window Repaint k → len − max freq ≤ k. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Repaint k → len − max freq ≤ k.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A window is fine if (length − count of its most common letter) ≤ k.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Longest substring you can turn into all-one-letter with at most k changes.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Every substring, count letters: O(n²·26).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Grow r; update counts and maxCount.<\/li><li>If (r − l + 1) − maxCount &gt; k, shrink: counts[s[l]] −= 1, l += 1.<\/li><li>Track the window length.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Rule:<\/b> len − maxf ≤ k.<\/li><li><b>Track:<\/b> Counts and maxf.<\/li><li><b>Shrink:<\/b> While too many repaints.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> maxf never needs to decrease.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def character_replacement(s, k):\n    count = {}\n    l = maxf = best = 0\n    for r, ch in enumerate(s):\n        count[ch] = count.get(ch, 0) + 1\n        maxf = max(maxf, count[ch])\n        while (r - l + 1) - maxf > k:      # too many letters to repaint\n            count[s[l]] -= 1\n            l += 1\n        best = max(best, r - l + 1)\n    return best","java":"public int characterReplacement(String s, int k) {\n    int[] count = new int[26];\n    int l = 0, maxf = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < s.length(); r++) {\n        maxf = Math.max(maxf, ++count[s.charAt(r) - 'A']);\n        while ((r - l + 1) - maxf > k) {      // too many letters to repaint\n            count[s.charAt(l) - 'A']--;\n            l++;\n        }\n        best = Math.max(best, r - l + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int characterReplacement(string s, int k) {\n    int count[26] = {};\n    int l = 0, maxf = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)s.size(); r++) {\n        maxf = max(maxf, ++count[s[r] - 'A']);\n        while ((r - l + 1) - maxf > k) {      // too many letters to repaint\n            count[s[l] - 'A']--;\n            l++;\n        }\n        best = max(best, r - l + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-424"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-repeating-character-replacement/"]],"ask":"Longest substring you can make one letter with at most k replacements."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-567","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Permutation in String","sub":"LeetCode 567 · Medium · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":567,"q":["Permutation in String","how to solve Permutation in String","Permutation in String approach","Permutation in String solution","leetcode 567","lc 567","Does s2 contain a permutation of s1?","Does s2 contain some rearrangement of s1 as a contiguous piece?"],"w":"Sliding Window Anagram inside → fixed count window. Complexity O(n · 26)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Anagram inside → fixed count window.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A fixed window of len(s1) sliding over s2; compare letter counts.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Does s2 contain some rearrangement of s1 as a contiguous piece?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Generate all permutations of s1: factorial.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Count s1&#x27;s letters.<\/li><li>Slide a window of the same size over s2, adding the new letter and removing the old.<\/li><li>If counts match at any point, True.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Size:<\/b> len(s1), fixed.<\/li><li><b>State:<\/b> Letter counts.<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> Counts equal.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · 26)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Delete zero counts so Counter equality works.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import Counter\n\ndef check_inclusion(s1, s2):\n    k = len(s1)\n    need = Counter(s1)\n    window = Counter(s2[:k])\n    if window == need:\n        return True\n    for r in range(k, len(s2)):\n        window[s2[r]] += 1               # new char enters on the right\n        window[s2[r - k]] -= 1           # old char leaves on the left\n        if window[s2[r - k]] == 0:\n            del window[s2[r - k]]\n        if window == need:\n            return True\n    return False","java":"public boolean checkInclusion(String s1, String s2) {\n    int k = s1.length();\n    if (k > s2.length()) return false;\n    int[] need = new int[26], window = new int[26];\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n        need[s1.charAt(i) - 'a']++;\n        window[s2.charAt(i) - 'a']++;\n    }\n    if (Arrays.equals(window, need)) return true;\n    for (int r = k; r < s2.length(); r++) {\n        window[s2.charAt(r) - 'a']++;          // new char enters on the right\n        window[s2.charAt(r - k) - 'a']--;      // old char leaves on the left\n        if (Arrays.equals(window, need)) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}","cpp":"bool checkInclusion(string s1, string s2) {\n    int k = s1.size();\n    if (k > (int)s2.size()) return false;\n    array<int, 26> need{}, window{};\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n        need[s1[i] - 'a']++;\n        window[s2[i] - 'a']++;\n    }\n    if (window == need) return true;\n    for (int r = k; r < (int)s2.size(); r++) {\n        window[s2[r] - 'a']++;                 // new char enters on the right\n        window[s2[r - k] - 'a']--;             // old char leaves on the left\n        if (window == need) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-567"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/permutation-in-string/"]],"ask":"Does s2 contain a permutation of s1?"},{"i":"p-sliding-window-438","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Find All Anagrams in a String","sub":"LeetCode 438 · Medium · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":438,"q":["Find All Anagrams in a String","how to solve Find All Anagrams in a String","Find All Anagrams in a String approach","Find All Anagrams in a String solution","leetcode 438","lc 438","All start indexes of p’s anagrams in s.","Every start index in s where some rearrangement of p begins."],"w":"Sliding Window Anagram inside → fixed count window. Complexity O(n · 26)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Anagram inside → fixed count window.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Same fixed window as 567, but collect every match.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Every start index in s where some rearrangement of p begins.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check each start by sorting: O(n·m log m).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Count p.<\/li><li>Slide a len(p) window over s updating counts.<\/li><li>Record the start index whenever counts are equal.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Size:<\/b> len(p).<\/li><li><b>Slide:<\/b> Add right, drop left.<\/li><li><b>Record:<\/b> r − k + 1 on a match.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · 26)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Start comparing only once the window is full.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import Counter\n\ndef find_anagrams(s, p):\n    k, need, res = len(p), Counter(p), []\n    window = Counter()\n    for r, ch in enumerate(s):\n        window[ch] += 1\n        if r >= k:\n            left = s[r - k]\n            window[left] -= 1\n            if window[left] == 0:\n                del window[left]\n        if window == need:\n            res.append(r - k + 1)\n    return res","java":"public List<Integer> findAnagrams(String s, String p) {\n    int k = p.length();\n    int[] need = new int[26], window = new int[26];\n    for (char ch : p.toCharArray()) need[ch - 'a']++;\n    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int r = 0; r < s.length(); r++) {\n        window[s.charAt(r) - 'a']++;\n        if (r >= k) window[s.charAt(r - k) - 'a']--;   // left char leaves\n        if (Arrays.equals(window, need)) res.add(r - k + 1);\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> findAnagrams(string s, string p) {\n    int k = p.size();\n    array<int, 26> need{}, window{};\n    for (char ch : p) need[ch - 'a']++;\n    vector<int> res;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)s.size(); r++) {\n        window[s[r] - 'a']++;\n        if (r >= k) window[s[r - k] - 'a']--;          // left char leaves\n        if (window == need) res.push_back(r - k + 1);\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-438"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/find-all-anagrams-in-a-string/"]],"ask":"All start indexes of p’s anagrams in s."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-76","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Minimum Window Substring","sub":"LeetCode 76 · Hard · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":76,"q":["Minimum Window Substring","how to solve Minimum Window Substring","Minimum Window Substring approach","Minimum Window Substring solution","leetcode 76","lc 76","Smallest window of s containing all of t.","Smallest substring of s that contains every character of t (with counts)."],"w":"Sliding Window Shortest covering window → shrink while valid, track missing. Complexity O(|s| + |t|)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Shortest covering window → shrink while valid, track missing.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Expand right until the window covers t; then shrink left as long as it still covers it.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Smallest substring of s that contains every character of t (with counts).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Every substring: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>need = counts of t, missing = len(t).<\/li><li>Expand r; when missing hits 0, shrink l while the window stays valid, recording the smallest.<\/li><li>Then move l one more (breaking validity) and keep expanding.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Need:<\/b> Counter(t) and missing.<\/li><li><b>Expand:<\/b> Until missing = 0.<\/li><li><b>Shrink:<\/b> While valid, record.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(|s| + |t|)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Count duplicates in t: &quot;aa&quot; needs two a’s.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import Counter\n\ndef min_window(s, t):\n    need = Counter(t)\n    missing = len(t)                     # chars still needed, with repeats\n    l = 0\n    best = (float(\"inf\"), 0, 0)\n    for r, ch in enumerate(s):\n        if need[ch] > 0:\n            missing -= 1\n        need[ch] -= 1                    # negative = surplus in the window\n        while missing == 0:              # valid: shrink as far as possible\n            if r - l + 1 < best[0]:\n                best = (r - l + 1, l, r + 1)\n            need[s[l]] += 1\n            if need[s[l]] > 0:\n                missing += 1             # just dropped a needed char\n            l += 1\n    return \"\" if best[0] == float(\"inf\") else s[best[1]:best[2]]","java":"public String minWindow(String s, String t) {\n    int[] need = new int[128];\n    for (char ch : t.toCharArray()) need[ch]++;\n    int missing = t.length();                  // chars still needed, with repeats\n    int l = 0, bestLen = Integer.MAX_VALUE, bestL = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < s.length(); r++) {\n        char ch = s.charAt(r);\n        if (need[ch] > 0) missing--;\n        need[ch]--;                            // negative = surplus in the window\n        while (missing == 0) {                 // valid: shrink as far as possible\n            if (r - l + 1 < bestLen) { bestLen = r - l + 1; bestL = l; }\n            need[s.charAt(l)]++;\n            if (need[s.charAt(l)] > 0) missing++;   // just dropped a needed char\n            l++;\n        }\n    }\n    return bestLen == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? \"\" : s.substring(bestL, bestL + bestLen);\n}","cpp":"string minWindow(string s, string t) {\n    vector<int> need(128, 0);\n    for (char ch : t) need[ch]++;\n    int missing = t.size();                    // chars still needed, with repeats\n    int l = 0, bestLen = INT_MAX, bestL = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)s.size(); r++) {\n        char ch = s[r];\n        if (need[ch] > 0) missing--;\n        need[ch]--;                            // negative = surplus in the window\n        while (missing == 0) {                 // valid: shrink as far as possible\n            if (r - l + 1 < bestLen) { bestLen = r - l + 1; bestL = l; }\n            need[s[l]]++;\n            if (need[s[l]] > 0) missing++;     // just dropped a needed char\n            l++;\n        }\n    }\n    return bestLen == INT_MAX ? \"\" : s.substr(bestL, bestLen);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-76"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-window-substring/"]],"ask":"Smallest window of s containing all of t."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-239","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Sliding Window Maximum","sub":"LeetCode 239 · Hard · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":239,"q":["Sliding Window Maximum","how to solve Sliding Window Maximum","Sliding Window Maximum approach","Sliding Window Maximum solution","leetcode 239","lc 239","Max of every window of size k.","For every window of size k, what's the maximum?"],"w":"Sliding Window Window max → monotonic deque. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Window max → monotonic deque.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A deque of indices with decreasing values: the front is always the window&#x27;s max.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For every window of size k, what&#x27;s the maximum?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan each window: O(n·k).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Pop from the back while the new value ≥ the back&#x27;s value; push the index.<\/li><li>Pop the front if it has left the window.<\/li><li>After the first k elements, record nums[front].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Deque:<\/b> Indexes, decreasing values.<\/li><li><b>Back:<\/b> Pop smaller.<\/li><li><b>Front:<\/b> Pop expired; front is the max.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Pop with ≤ so equal older values go too.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n\ndef max_sliding_window(nums, k):\n    dq = deque()                       # indexes, values decreasing\n    res = []\n    for r, x in enumerate(nums):\n        while dq and nums[dq[-1]] <= x:\n            dq.pop()                   # smaller and older: never the max again\n        dq.append(r)\n        if dq[0] <= r - k:\n            dq.popleft()               # front slid out of the window\n        if r >= k - 1:\n            res.append(nums[dq[0]])\n    return res","java":"public int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {\n    Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();      // indexes, values decreasing\n    int[] res = new int[nums.length - k + 1];\n    for (int r = 0; r < nums.length; r++) {\n        while (!dq.isEmpty() && nums[dq.peekLast()] <= nums[r])\n            dq.pollLast();                       // smaller and older: never the max again\n        dq.offerLast(r);\n        if (dq.peekFirst() <= r - k) dq.pollFirst();   // front slid out of the window\n        if (r >= k - 1) res[r - k + 1] = nums[dq.peekFirst()];\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> maxSlidingWindow(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    deque<int> dq;                               // indexes, values decreasing\n    vector<int> res;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)nums.size(); r++) {\n        while (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[r])\n            dq.pop_back();                       // smaller and older: never the max again\n        dq.push_back(r);\n        if (dq.front() <= r - k) dq.pop_front(); // front slid out of the window\n        if (r >= k - 1) res.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-239"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/sliding-window-maximum/"]],"ask":"Max of every window of size k."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-209","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Minimum Size Subarray Sum","sub":"LeetCode 209 · Medium · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":209,"q":["Minimum Size Subarray Sum","how to solve Minimum Size Subarray Sum","Minimum Size Subarray Sum approach","Minimum Size Subarray Sum solution","leetcode 209","lc 209","Shortest subarray with sum ≥ target (positives).","Shortest contiguous piece with sum ≥ target (all numbers positive)."],"w":"Sliding Window Shortest valid → shrink while valid. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Shortest valid → shrink while valid.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Positive numbers: growing increases the sum, shrinking decreases it. So grow, then shrink while valid.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Shortest contiguous piece with sum ≥ target (all numbers positive).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Every subarray: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Add nums[r] to sum.<\/li><li>While sum ≥ target: record length, subtract nums[l], l += 1.<\/li><li>Return the smallest (or 0).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Expand:<\/b> Add to sum.<\/li><li><b>Valid:<\/b> sum ≥ target.<\/li><li><b>Shrink:<\/b> While valid, record.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Return 0 if nothing qualifies.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def min_sub_array_len(target, nums):\n    l = total = 0\n    best = float(\"inf\")\n    for r, x in enumerate(nums):\n        total += x                       # expand\n        while total >= target:           # valid: record, then try smaller\n            best = min(best, r - l + 1)\n            total -= nums[l]\n            l += 1\n    return 0 if best == float(\"inf\") else best","java":"public int minSubArrayLen(int target, int[] nums) {\n    int l = 0, total = 0, best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    for (int r = 0; r < nums.length; r++) {\n        total += nums[r];                    // expand\n        while (total >= target) {            // valid: record, then try smaller\n            best = Math.min(best, r - l + 1);\n            total -= nums[l++];\n        }\n    }\n    return best == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? 0 : best;\n}","cpp":"int minSubArrayLen(int target, vector<int>& nums) {\n    int l = 0, total = 0, best = INT_MAX;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)nums.size(); r++) {\n        total += nums[r];                    // expand\n        while (total >= target) {            // valid: record, then try smaller\n            best = min(best, r - l + 1);\n            total -= nums[l++];\n        }\n    }\n    return best == INT_MAX ? 0 : best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-209"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-size-subarray-sum/"]],"ask":"Shortest subarray with sum ≥ target (positives)."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-643","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Maximum Average Subarray I","sub":"LeetCode 643 · Easy · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":643,"q":["Maximum Average Subarray I","how to solve Maximum Average Subarray I","Maximum Average Subarray I approach","Maximum Average Subarray I solution","leetcode 643","lc 643","Max average of any length-k subarray.","Best average over any k consecutive numbers."],"w":"Sliding Window Fixed size → add one, drop one. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Fixed size → add one, drop one.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Slide: add the new number, subtract the one that left.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Best average over any k consecutive numbers.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Re-sum every window: O(n·k).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sum the first k.<\/li><li>For each next index, sum += new − old.<\/li><li>Return maxSum / k.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Start:<\/b> Sum of first k.<\/li><li><b>Slide:<\/b> Add one, drop one.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> best / k.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Divide only once at the end.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_max_average(nums, k):\n    window = sum(nums[:k])\n    best = window\n    for r in range(k, len(nums)):\n        window += nums[r] - nums[r - k]     # add one, drop one\n        best = max(best, window)\n    return best / k","java":"public double findMaxAverage(int[] nums, int k) {\n    int window = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) window += nums[i];\n    int best = window;\n    for (int r = k; r < nums.length; r++) {\n        window += nums[r] - nums[r - k];     // add one, drop one\n        best = Math.max(best, window);\n    }\n    return (double) best / k;\n}","cpp":"double findMaxAverage(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    int window = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) window += nums[i];\n    int best = window;\n    for (int r = k; r < (int)nums.size(); r++) {\n        window += nums[r] - nums[r - k];     // add one, drop one\n        best = max(best, window);\n    }\n    return (double)best / k;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-643"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-average-subarray-i/"]],"ask":"Max average of any length-k subarray."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-904","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Fruit Into Baskets","sub":"LeetCode 904 · Medium · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":904,"q":["Fruit Into Baskets","how to solve Fruit Into Baskets","Fruit Into Baskets approach","Fruit Into Baskets solution","leetcode 904","lc 904","Longest subarray with at most 2 distinct values.","Longest stretch containing at most 2 different values."],"w":"Sliding Window At most K distinct → window + dict. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> At most K distinct → window + dict.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Window with a count map; shrink when it holds 3 distinct values.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Longest stretch containing at most 2 different values.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Every subarray: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Add fruits[r] to counts.<\/li><li>While len(counts) &gt; 2, remove fruits[l] (delete at 0), l += 1.<\/li><li>Track the window length.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Counts:<\/b> Dict.<\/li><li><b>Break:<\/b> More than 2 keys.<\/li><li><b>Shrink:<\/b> Delete zero counts.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Delete keys at zero, or len(count) lies.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def total_fruit(fruits):\n    count = {}\n    l = best = 0\n    for r, f in enumerate(fruits):\n        count[f] = count.get(f, 0) + 1\n        while len(count) > 2:              # at most 2 kinds allowed\n            count[fruits[l]] -= 1\n            if count[fruits[l]] == 0:\n                del count[fruits[l]]\n            l += 1\n        best = max(best, r - l + 1)\n    return best","java":"public int totalFruit(int[] fruits) {\n    Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();\n    int l = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < fruits.length; r++) {\n        count.merge(fruits[r], 1, Integer::sum);\n        while (count.size() > 2) {               // at most 2 kinds allowed\n            if (count.merge(fruits[l], -1, Integer::sum) == 0) count.remove(fruits[l]);\n            l++;\n        }\n        best = Math.max(best, r - l + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int totalFruit(vector<int>& fruits) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> count;\n    int l = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)fruits.size(); r++) {\n        count[fruits[r]]++;\n        while (count.size() > 2) {               // at most 2 kinds allowed\n            if (--count[fruits[l]] == 0) count.erase(fruits[l]);\n            l++;\n        }\n        best = max(best, r - l + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-904"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/fruit-into-baskets/"]],"ask":"Longest subarray with at most 2 distinct values."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-992","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Subarrays with K Different Integers","sub":"LeetCode 992 · Hard · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":992,"q":["Subarrays with K Different Integers","how to solve Subarrays with K Different Integers","Subarrays with K Different Integers approach","Subarrays with K Different Integers solution","leetcode 992","lc 992","Count subarrays with exactly k distinct values.","Count subarrays with exactly k different values."],"w":"Sliding Window Exactly K → difference of at-mosts. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Exactly K → difference of at-mosts.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> exactly(k) = atMost(k) − atMost(k − 1); atMost is a normal shrinking window.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Count subarrays with exactly k different values.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> All subarrays: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>atMost(K): window with counts; shrink while distinct &gt; K.<\/li><li>Each r adds (r − l + 1) subarrays ending at r.<\/li><li>Return atMost(k) − atMost(k − 1).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> “Exactly” isn’t monotone.<\/li><li><b>Trick:<\/b> atMost(k) − atMost(k − 1).<\/li><li><b>Count:<\/b> r − l + 1 per r.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> r − l + 1 counts all valid windows ending at r.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def subarrays_with_k_distinct(nums, k):\n    def at_most(k):\n        count, l, total = {}, 0, 0\n        for r, x in enumerate(nums):\n            count[x] = count.get(x, 0) + 1\n            while len(count) > k:\n                count[nums[l]] -= 1\n                if count[nums[l]] == 0:\n                    del count[nums[l]]\n                l += 1\n            total += r - l + 1         # every valid window ending at r\n        return total\n    return at_most(k) - at_most(k - 1)","java":"public int subarraysWithKDistinct(int[] nums, int k) {\n    return atMost(nums, k) - atMost(nums, k - 1);\n}\n\nprivate int atMost(int[] nums, int k) {\n    Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();\n    int l = 0, total = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < nums.length; r++) {\n        count.merge(nums[r], 1, Integer::sum);\n        while (count.size() > k) {\n            if (count.merge(nums[l], -1, Integer::sum) == 0) count.remove(nums[l]);\n            l++;\n        }\n        total += r - l + 1;                  // every valid window ending at r\n    }\n    return total;\n}","cpp":"int subarraysWithKDistinct(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    return atMost(nums, k) - atMost(nums, k - 1);\n}\n\nint atMost(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> count;\n    int l = 0, total = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)nums.size(); r++) {\n        count[nums[r]]++;\n        while ((int)count.size() > k) {\n            if (--count[nums[l]] == 0) count.erase(nums[l]);\n            l++;\n        }\n        total += r - l + 1;                  // every valid window ending at r\n    }\n    return total;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-992"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/subarrays-with-k-different-integers/"]],"ask":"Count subarrays with exactly k distinct values."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-1004","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Max Consecutive Ones III","sub":"LeetCode 1004 · Medium · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":1004,"q":["Max Consecutive Ones III","how to solve Max Consecutive Ones III","Max Consecutive Ones III approach","Max Consecutive Ones III solution","leetcode 1004","lc 1004","Longest run of 1s if you can flip at most k zeros.","Longest run of 1s if you may flip at most k zeros."],"w":"Sliding Window At most K → window. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> At most K → window.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Window with at most k zeros inside.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Longest run of 1s if you may flip at most k zeros.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every subarray: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Count zeros as r grows.<\/li><li>While zeros &gt; k: if nums[l] was 0, zeros −= 1; l += 1.<\/li><li>Track r − l + 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Track:<\/b> Zeros in the window.<\/li><li><b>Break:<\/b> zeros &gt; k.<\/li><li><b>Shrink:<\/b> Until zeros ≤ k.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> It’s the same as “longest with at most k zeros”.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def longest_ones(nums, k):\n    l = zeros = best = 0\n    for r, x in enumerate(nums):\n        zeros += x == 0\n        while zeros > k:               # too many flips needed\n            zeros -= nums[l] == 0\n            l += 1\n        best = max(best, r - l + 1)\n    return best","java":"public int longestOnes(int[] nums, int k) {\n    int l = 0, zeros = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < nums.length; r++) {\n        if (nums[r] == 0) zeros++;\n        while (zeros > k) {                  // too many flips needed\n            if (nums[l] == 0) zeros--;\n            l++;\n        }\n        best = Math.max(best, r - l + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int longestOnes(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    int l = 0, zeros = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < (int)nums.size(); r++) {\n        if (nums[r] == 0) zeros++;\n        while (zeros > k) {                  // too many flips needed\n            if (nums[l] == 0) zeros--;\n            l++;\n        }\n        best = max(best, r - l + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-1004"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/max-consecutive-ones-iii/"]],"ask":"Longest run of 1s if you can flip at most k zeros."},{"i":"p-sliding-window-1423","k":"prob","s":"sliding-window","t":"Maximum Points You Can Obtain from Cards","sub":"LeetCode 1423 · Medium · Sliding Window","cat":"DSA problems","n":1423,"q":["Maximum Points You Can Obtain from Cards","how to solve Maximum Points You Can Obtain from Cards","Maximum Points You Can Obtain from Cards approach","Maximum Points You Can Obtain from Cards solution","leetcode 1423","lc 1423","Take exactly k cards from either end to maximize the sum.","Take exactly k cards from the two ends. Max total?"],"w":"Sliding Window Both ends → complement window. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Both ends → complement window.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Taking k from the ends = leaving a contiguous middle of n − k. Minimize that middle (or slide the split).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Take exactly k cards from the two ends. Max total?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every left/right split by summing: O(k²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Start with all k from the left.<\/li><li>Move one card at a time from the left side to the right side.<\/li><li>Track the best total.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Flip:<\/b> Untaken cards are a middle block of n − k.<\/li><li><b>Window:<\/b> Minimize its sum.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> total − min window.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> If k = n, the window is empty: answer is the total.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_score(card_points, k):\n    n = len(card_points)\n    w = n - k                           # cards you DON'T take are contiguous\n    window = sum(card_points[:w])\n    lo = window\n    for r in range(w, n):\n        window += card_points[r] - card_points[r - w]\n        lo = min(lo, window)\n    return sum(card_points) - lo","java":"public int maxScore(int[] cardPoints, int k) {\n    int n = cardPoints.length, w = n - k;    // cards you DON'T take are contiguous\n    int total = 0, window = 0;\n    for (int x : cardPoints) total += x;\n    for (int i = 0; i < w; i++) window += cardPoints[i];\n    int lo = window;\n    for (int r = w; r < n; r++) {\n        window += cardPoints[r] - cardPoints[r - w];\n        lo = Math.min(lo, window);\n    }\n    return total - lo;\n}","cpp":"int maxScore(vector<int>& cardPoints, int k) {\n    int n = cardPoints.size(), w = n - k;    // cards you DON'T take are contiguous\n    int total = accumulate(cardPoints.begin(), cardPoints.end(), 0);\n    int window = accumulate(cardPoints.begin(), cardPoints.begin() + w, 0);\n    int lo = window;\n    for (int r = w; r < n; r++) {\n        window += cardPoints[r] - cardPoints[r - w];\n        lo = min(lo, window);\n    }\n    return total - lo;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sliding-window.html#p-1423"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-points-you-can-obtain-from-cards/"]],"ask":"Take exactly k cards from either end to maximize the sum."},{"i":"p-stack-20","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Valid Parentheses","sub":"LeetCode 20 · Easy · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":20,"q":["Valid Parentheses","how to solve Valid Parentheses","Valid Parentheses approach","Valid Parentheses solution","leetcode 20","lc 20","Are all brackets closed in the correct order?","Is every bracket closed by the right type, in the right order?"],"w":"Stack Nesting → stack. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Nesting → stack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> The most recent unclosed opener must close first → a stack.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Is every bracket closed by the right type, in the right order?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Repeatedly delete &#x27;()&#x27; pairs: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Push openers.<\/li><li>On a closer, the top must be its matching opener; pop it, else False.<\/li><li>Valid only if the stack is empty at the end.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Push:<\/b> Openers.<\/li><li><b>Pop:<\/b> On a matching closer.<\/li><li><b>End:<\/b> Stack must be empty.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> A closer on an empty stack is invalid.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def is_valid(s):\n    pairs = {\")\": \"(\", \"]\": \"[\", \"}\": \"{\"}\n    stack = []\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch in pairs:                          # a closer\n            if not stack or stack[-1] != pairs[ch]:\n                return False\n            stack.pop()\n        else:\n            stack.append(ch)                     # an opener waits\n    return not stack                             # leftovers never closed","java":"public boolean isValid(String s) {\n    Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');\n    Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n        if (pairs.containsKey(ch)) {                       // a closer\n            if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.peek() != pairs.get(ch)) return false;\n            stack.pop();\n        } else stack.push(ch);                             // an opener waits\n    }\n    return stack.isEmpty();                                // leftovers never closed\n}","cpp":"bool isValid(string s) {\n    unordered_map<char, char> pairs{{')', '('}, {']', '['}, {'}', '{'}};\n    vector<char> st;\n    for (char ch : s) {\n        if (pairs.count(ch)) {                             // a closer\n            if (st.empty() || st.back() != pairs[ch]) return false;\n            st.pop_back();\n        } else st.push_back(ch);                           // an opener waits\n    }\n    return st.empty();                                     // leftovers never closed\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-20"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-parentheses/"]],"ask":"Are all brackets closed in the correct order?"},{"i":"p-stack-155","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Min Stack","sub":"LeetCode 155 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":155,"q":["Min Stack","how to solve Min Stack","Min Stack approach","Min Stack solution","leetcode 155","lc 155","Stack with push, pop, top and getMin all O(1).","A stack that can also report its minimum in O(1)."],"w":"Stack O(1) min with pops → store running min per frame. Complexity O(1) per op","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> O(1) min with pops → store running min per frame.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Store, with each element, the minimum at that moment.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A stack that can also report its minimum in O(1).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan the stack for the min: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>push(x): push (x, min(x, current min)).<\/li><li>pop: pop the pair.<\/li><li>getMin: second value of the top pair.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Store:<\/b> (value, min so far).<\/li><li><b>Push:<\/b> min(val, previous min).<\/li><li><b>getMin:<\/b> Top’s second field.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(1) per op<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> A single min variable breaks after popping the min.<\/p>","c":{"py":"class MinStack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stack = []                  # (value, min so far)\n\n    def push(self, val):\n        m = min(val, self.stack[-1][1]) if self.stack else val\n        self.stack.append((val, m))\n\n    def pop(self):\n        self.stack.pop()\n\n    def top(self):\n        return self.stack[-1][0]\n\n    def getMin(self):\n        return self.stack[-1][1]","java":"class MinStack {\n    private final Deque<int[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();   // {value, min so far}\n\n    public void push(int val) {\n        int m = stack.isEmpty() ? val : Math.min(val, stack.peek()[1]);\n        stack.push(new int[]{val, m});\n    }\n\n    public void pop() { stack.pop(); }\n\n    public int top() { return stack.peek()[0]; }\n\n    public int getMin() { return stack.peek()[1]; }\n}","cpp":"class MinStack {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> st;          // (value, min so far)\npublic:\n    void push(int val) {\n        int m = st.empty() ? val : min(val, st.back().second);\n        st.push_back({val, m});\n    }\n    void pop() { st.pop_back(); }\n    int top() { return st.back().first; }\n    int getMin() { return st.back().second; }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-155"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/min-stack/"]],"ask":"Stack with push, pop, top and getMin all O(1)."},{"i":"p-stack-150","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation","sub":"LeetCode 150 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":150,"q":["Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation","how to solve Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation","Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation approach","Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation solution","leetcode 150","lc 150","Evaluate an RPN expression.","Compute an expression written like '2 1 + 3 *'."],"w":"Stack Postfix → operand stack. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Postfix → operand stack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Numbers go on a stack; an operator pops two, applies, pushes the result.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Compute an expression written like &#x27;2 1 + 3 *&#x27;.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Convert to infix first: unnecessary.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each token: number → push.<\/li><li>Operator → b = pop, a = pop, push a op b (truncate division toward 0).<\/li><li>Answer is the last value.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Numbers:<\/b> Push.<\/li><li><b>Operator:<\/b> Pop b, then a; push a op b.<\/li><li><b>Division:<\/b> Truncate toward zero.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Python’s // floors negatives; use int(a / b).<\/p>","c":{"py":"def eval_rpn(tokens):\n    stack = []\n    for t in tokens:\n        if t in \"+-*/\":\n            b, a = stack.pop(), stack.pop()      # order matters: a op b\n            if t == \"+\": stack.append(a + b)\n            elif t == \"-\": stack.append(a - b)\n            elif t == \"*\": stack.append(a * b)\n            else: stack.append(int(a / b))       # truncate toward zero\n        else:\n            stack.append(int(t))\n    return stack[0]","java":"public int evalRPN(String[] tokens) {\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (String t : tokens) {\n        if (\"+-*/\".contains(t)) {\n            int b = stack.pop(), a = stack.pop();          // order matters: a op b\n            switch (t) {\n                case \"+\" -> stack.push(a + b);\n                case \"-\" -> stack.push(a - b);\n                case \"*\" -> stack.push(a * b);\n                default -> stack.push(a / b);              // Java truncates toward zero\n            }\n        } else stack.push(Integer.parseInt(t));\n    }\n    return stack.pop();\n}","cpp":"int evalRPN(vector<string>& tokens) {\n    vector<long long> st;\n    for (auto& t : tokens) {\n        if (t == \"+\" || t == \"-\" || t == \"*\" || t == \"/\") {\n            long long b = st.back(); st.pop_back();\n            long long a = st.back(); st.pop_back();        // order matters: a op b\n            if (t == \"+\") st.push_back(a + b);\n            else if (t == \"-\") st.push_back(a - b);\n            else if (t == \"*\") st.push_back(a * b);\n            else st.push_back(a / b);                      // C++ truncates toward zero\n        } else st.push_back(stoll(t));\n    }\n    return st.back();\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-150"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/evaluate-reverse-polish-notation/"]],"ask":"Evaluate an RPN expression."},{"i":"p-stack-394","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Decode String","sub":"LeetCode 394 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":394,"q":["Decode String","how to solve Decode String","Decode String approach","Decode String solution","leetcode 394","lc 394","Decode k[encoded] strings, possibly nested.","Expand '3[a2[c]]' into 'accaccacc'."],"w":"Stack Nested → stack of contexts. Complexity O(output size)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Nested → stack of contexts.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Stack of (string so far, repeat count) for each &#x27;[&#x27;.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Expand &#x27;3[a2[c]]&#x27; into &#x27;accaccacc&#x27;.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Recursive string building: works, fiddly.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Digits build k; letters append to cur.<\/li><li>&#x27;[&#x27; → push (cur, k), reset both.<\/li><li>&#x27;]&#x27; → pop (prev, k); cur = prev + cur × k.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>[:<\/b> Push (cur, k), reset.<\/li><li><b>]:<\/b> Pop, cur = prev + cur × k.<\/li><li><b>Digits:<\/b> Multi-digit k.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(output size)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Reset k after pushing.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def decode_string(s):\n    stack = []                       # (text before \"[\", repeat count)\n    cur, k = \"\", 0\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch.isdigit():\n            k = k * 10 + int(ch)\n        elif ch == \"[\":\n            stack.append((cur, k))       # save the outer context\n            cur, k = \"\", 0\n        elif ch == \"]\":\n            prev, times = stack.pop()\n            cur = prev + cur * times     # close this layer\n        else:\n            cur += ch\n    return cur","java":"public String decodeString(String s) {\n    Deque<String> texts = new ArrayDeque<>();      // text before \"[\"\n    Deque<Integer> counts = new ArrayDeque<>();    // its repeat count\n    StringBuilder cur = new StringBuilder();\n    int k = 0;\n    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n        if (Character.isDigit(ch)) k = k * 10 + (ch - '0');\n        else if (ch == '[') {\n            texts.push(cur.toString());            // save the outer context\n            counts.push(k);\n            cur = new StringBuilder(); k = 0;\n        } else if (ch == ']') {\n            String inner = cur.toString().repeat(counts.pop());\n            cur = new StringBuilder(texts.pop()).append(inner);   // close this layer\n        } else cur.append(ch);\n    }\n    return cur.toString();\n}","cpp":"string decodeString(string s) {\n    vector<pair<string, int>> st;      // (text before \"[\", repeat count)\n    string cur;\n    int k = 0;\n    for (char ch : s) {\n        if (isdigit(ch)) k = k * 10 + (ch - '0');\n        else if (ch == '[') {\n            st.push_back({cur, k});    // save the outer context\n            cur = \"\"; k = 0;\n        } else if (ch == ']') {\n            auto [prev, times] = st.back(); st.pop_back();\n            string rep;\n            while (times--) rep += cur;\n            cur = prev + rep;          // close this layer\n        } else cur += ch;\n    }\n    return cur;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-394"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/decode-string/"]],"ask":"Decode k[encoded] strings, possibly nested."},{"i":"p-stack-71","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Simplify Path","sub":"LeetCode 71 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":71,"q":["Simplify Path","how to solve Simplify Path","Simplify Path approach","Simplify Path solution","leetcode 71","lc 71","Canonical form of a Unix path.","Turn a Unix path like '/a/./b/../../c/' into '/c'."],"w":"Stack Cancel the last → stack. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Cancel the last → stack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Split by &#x27;/&#x27;, and use a stack of folder names.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Turn a Unix path like &#x27;/a/./b/../../c/&#x27; into &#x27;/c&#x27;.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> String replacements in a loop: error-prone.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Split on &#x27;/&#x27;.<\/li><li>Skip &#x27;&#x27; and &#x27;.&#x27;; &#x27;..&#x27; pops if possible; otherwise push the name.<\/li><li>Join with &#x27;/&#x27; after a leading &#x27;/&#x27;.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Split:<\/b> On “/”.<\/li><li><b>..:<\/b> Pop if possible.<\/li><li><b>Skip:<\/b> Empty parts and “.”.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> “..” at root does nothing.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def simplify_path(path):\n    stack = []\n    for part in path.split(\"/\"):\n        if part == \"..\":\n            if stack:\n                stack.pop()\n        elif part and part != \".\":\n            stack.append(part)\n    return \"/\" + \"/\".join(stack)","java":"public String simplifyPath(String path) {\n    Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (String part : path.split(\"/\")) {\n        if (part.equals(\"..\")) { if (!stack.isEmpty()) stack.pollLast(); }\n        else if (!part.isEmpty() && !part.equals(\".\")) stack.offerLast(part);\n    }\n    return \"/\" + String.join(\"/\", stack);\n}","cpp":"string simplifyPath(string path) {\n    vector<string> st;\n    stringstream ss(path);\n    string part;\n    while (getline(ss, part, '/')) {\n        if (part == \"..\") { if (!st.empty()) st.pop_back(); }\n        else if (!part.empty() && part != \".\") st.push_back(part);\n    }\n    string res;\n    for (auto& p : st) res += \"/\" + p;\n    return res.empty() ? \"/\" : res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-71"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/simplify-path/"]],"ask":"Canonical form of a Unix path."},{"i":"p-stack-844","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Backspace String Compare","sub":"LeetCode 844 · Easy · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":844,"q":["Backspace String Compare","how to solve Backspace String Compare","Backspace String Compare approach","Backspace String Compare solution","leetcode 844","lc 844","Do s and t match after applying “#” as backspace?","Do two strings match after applying '#' as backspace?"],"w":"Stack Undo → stack. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Undo → stack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Walk both from the end, skipping characters that a &#x27;#&#x27; deletes.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Do two strings match after applying &#x27;#&#x27; as backspace?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Build both strings with a stack: O(n) space (fine, but can do O(1)).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>i, j at the ends; count pending backspaces for each.<\/li><li>Find the next real character in each.<\/li><li>Compare; mismatch → False.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Build:<\/b> Stack per string.<\/li><li><b>#:<\/b> Pop if non-empty.<\/li><li><b>Compare:<\/b> Final stacks.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> O(1) space version: walk both from the end counting skips.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def backspace_compare(s, t):\n    def build(x):\n        st = []\n        for ch in x:\n            if ch == \"#\":\n                if st:\n                    st.pop()\n            else:\n                st.append(ch)\n        return st\n    return build(s) == build(t)","java":"public boolean backspaceCompare(String s, String t) {\n    return build(s).equals(build(t));\n}\n\nprivate String build(String x) {\n    StringBuilder st = new StringBuilder();\n    for (char ch : x.toCharArray()) {\n        if (ch == '#') { if (st.length() > 0) st.deleteCharAt(st.length() - 1); }\n        else st.append(ch);\n    }\n    return st.toString();\n}","cpp":"bool backspaceCompare(string s, string t) {\n    auto build = [](const string& x) {\n        string st;\n        for (char ch : x) {\n            if (ch == '#') { if (!st.empty()) st.pop_back(); }\n            else st.push_back(ch);\n        }\n        return st;\n    };\n    return build(s) == build(t);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-844"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/backspace-string-compare/"]],"ask":"Do s and t match after applying “#” as backspace?"},{"i":"p-stack-739","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Daily Temperatures","sub":"LeetCode 739 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":739,"q":["Daily Temperatures","how to solve Daily Temperatures","Daily Temperatures approach","Daily Temperatures solution","leetcode 739","lc 739","Days until a warmer temperature, for each day.","For each day, how many days until a warmer one?"],"w":"Stack Next greater → monotonic stack. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Next greater → monotonic stack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Monotonic stack of days still waiting for a warmer day (temperatures decreasing).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For each day, how many days until a warmer one?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan forward from each day: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each day i: while today is warmer than the top day, pop it and set ans[top] = i − top.<\/li><li>Push i.<\/li><li>Days left in the stack get 0.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Stack:<\/b> Waiting days, decreasing temps.<\/li><li><b>Pop:<\/b> When today is warmer.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> i − j.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Store indexes, not temperatures.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def daily_temperatures(temps):\n    res = [0] * len(temps)\n    stack = []                         # days still waiting for warmer\n    for i, t in enumerate(temps):\n        while stack and temps[stack[-1]] < t:\n            j = stack.pop()\n            res[j] = i - j               # today answers day j\n        stack.append(i)\n    return res","java":"public int[] dailyTemperatures(int[] temps) {\n    int[] res = new int[temps.length];\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();   // days still waiting for warmer\n    for (int i = 0; i < temps.length; i++) {\n        while (!stack.isEmpty() && temps[stack.peek()] < temps[i]) {\n            int j = stack.pop();\n            res[j] = i - j;                      // today answers day j\n        }\n        stack.push(i);\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> dailyTemperatures(vector<int>& temps) {\n    vector<int> res(temps.size(), 0), st;        // st: days still waiting for warmer\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)temps.size(); i++) {\n        while (!st.empty() && temps[st.back()] < temps[i]) {\n            int j = st.back(); st.pop_back();\n            res[j] = i - j;                      // today answers day j\n        }\n        st.push_back(i);\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-739"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-temperatures/"]],"ask":"Days until a warmer temperature, for each day."},{"i":"p-stack-496","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Next Greater Element I","sub":"LeetCode 496 · Easy · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":496,"q":["Next Greater Element I","how to solve Next Greater Element I","Next Greater Element I approach","Next Greater Element I solution","leetcode 496","lc 496","For each x in nums1, next greater element in nums2.","For each number in nums1, the next bigger number to its right in nums2."],"w":"Stack Next greater → monotonic stack + dict. Complexity O(n + m)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Next greater → monotonic stack + dict.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Precompute next-greater for all of nums2 with a decreasing stack, store in a dict.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For each number in nums1, the next bigger number to its right in nums2.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan nums2 for each: O(n·m).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Walk nums2; pop smaller tops and map them to the current number.<\/li><li>Push the current number.<\/li><li>Answer each nums1 value from the dict (default −1).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Scan:<\/b> nums2 with a stack.<\/li><li><b>Map:<\/b> Popped value → its next greater.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Look up nums1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n + m)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Values are unique, so a dict by value works.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def next_greater_element(nums1, nums2):\n    nxt, stack = {}, []\n    for x in nums2:\n        while stack and stack[-1] < x:\n            nxt[stack.pop()] = x\n        stack.append(x)\n    return [nxt.get(x, -1) for x in nums1]","java":"public int[] nextGreaterElement(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {\n    Map<Integer, Integer> nxt = new HashMap<>();\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (int x : nums2) {\n        while (!stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek() < x) nxt.put(stack.pop(), x);\n        stack.push(x);\n    }\n    int[] res = new int[nums1.length];\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums1.length; i++) res[i] = nxt.getOrDefault(nums1[i], -1);\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> nextGreaterElement(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> nxt;\n    vector<int> st;\n    for (int x : nums2) {\n        while (!st.empty() && st.back() < x) { nxt[st.back()] = x; st.pop_back(); }\n        st.push_back(x);\n    }\n    vector<int> res;\n    for (int x : nums1) res.push_back(nxt.count(x) ? nxt[x] : -1);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-496"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/next-greater-element-i/"]],"ask":"For each x in nums1, next greater element in nums2."},{"i":"p-stack-503","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Next Greater Element II","sub":"LeetCode 503 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":503,"q":["Next Greater Element II","how to solve Next Greater Element II","Next Greater Element II approach","Next Greater Element II solution","leetcode 503","lc 503","Next greater in a circular array.","Next greater element when the array wraps around."],"w":"Stack Circular → loop twice. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Circular → loop twice.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Same stack, but loop over the array twice (i % n).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Next greater element when the array wraps around.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan up to n ahead for each: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>for i in 0..2n−1, x = nums[i % n].<\/li><li>Pop smaller tops, setting their answer to x.<\/li><li>Push only during the first pass.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Loop:<\/b> 2n with i % n.<\/li><li><b>Push:<\/b> Only when i &lt; n.<\/li><li><b>Default:<\/b> −1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Pushing in the second pass creates duplicate waits.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def next_greater_elements(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    res = [-1] * n\n    stack = []\n    for i in range(2 * n):             # walk twice to wrap around\n        x = nums[i % n]\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] < x:\n            res[stack.pop()] = x\n        if i < n:\n            stack.append(i)\n    return res","java":"public int[] nextGreaterElements(int[] nums) {\n    int n = nums.length;\n    int[] res = new int[n];\n    Arrays.fill(res, -1);\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {            // walk twice to wrap around\n        int x = nums[i % n];\n        while (!stack.isEmpty() && nums[stack.peek()] < x) res[stack.pop()] = x;\n        if (i < n) stack.push(i);\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> nextGreaterElements(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<int> res(n, -1), st;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {            // walk twice to wrap around\n        int x = nums[i % n];\n        while (!st.empty() && nums[st.back()] < x) { res[st.back()] = x; st.pop_back(); }\n        if (i < n) st.push_back(i);\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-503"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/next-greater-element-ii/"]],"ask":"Next greater in a circular array."},{"i":"p-stack-901","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Online Stock Span","sub":"LeetCode 901 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":901,"q":["Online Stock Span","how to solve Online Stock Span","Online Stock Span approach","Online Stock Span solution","leetcode 901","lc 901","For each new price, count consecutive previous days with price ≤ it.","For each new price, how many consecutive days back (including today) had price ≤ today?"],"w":"Stack Span back to previous greater → monotonic stack. Complexity Amortized O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Span back to previous greater → monotonic stack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Stack of (price, span). Pop smaller-or-equal prices and add their spans.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For each new price, how many consecutive days back (including today) had price ≤ today?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Walk back each day: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>span = 1.<\/li><li>While top price ≤ price, span += popped span.<\/li><li>Push (price, span); return span.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Stack:<\/b> (price, span).<\/li><li><b>Pop:<\/b> Prices ≤ today, adding their spans.<\/li><li><b>Push:<\/b> (today, total span).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> Amortized O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Absorbing spans keeps each price on the stack once.<\/p>","c":{"py":"class StockSpanner:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stack = []                  # (price, span)\n\n    def next(self, price):\n        span = 1\n        while self.stack and self.stack[-1][0] <= price:\n            span += self.stack.pop()[1]  # absorb smaller days' spans\n        self.stack.append((price, span))\n        return span","java":"class StockSpanner {\n    private final Deque<int[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();   // {price, span}\n\n    public int next(int price) {\n        int span = 1;\n        while (!stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek()[0] <= price)\n            span += stack.pop()[1];                          // absorb smaller days' spans\n        stack.push(new int[]{price, span});\n        return span;\n    }\n}","cpp":"class StockSpanner {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> st;          // (price, span)\npublic:\n    int next(int price) {\n        int span = 1;\n        while (!st.empty() && st.back().first <= price) {\n            span += st.back().second;   // absorb smaller days' spans\n            st.pop_back();\n        }\n        st.push_back({price, span});\n        return span;\n    }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-901"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/online-stock-span/"]],"ask":"For each new price, count consecutive previous days with price ≤ it."},{"i":"p-stack-853","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Car Fleet","sub":"LeetCode 853 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":853,"q":["Car Fleet","how to solve Car Fleet","Car Fleet approach","Car Fleet solution","leetcode 853","lc 853","Number of car fleets arriving at the target.","Cars head to a target; faster cars catch up and merge into fleets. How many fleets arrive?"],"w":"Stack Catch up and merge → stack of survivors. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Catch up and merge → stack of survivors.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort by position from the target; a car that would arrive sooner than the fleet ahead joins it.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Cars head to a target; faster cars catch up and merge into fleets. How many fleets arrive?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Simulate time steps: slow and fiddly.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Compute time = (target − pos) / speed.<\/li><li>Go from closest to farthest.<\/li><li>If time &gt; current fleet&#x27;s time, it&#x27;s a new fleet.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> By position, descending.<\/li><li><b>Time:<\/b> (target − p) / s.<\/li><li><b>Stack:<\/b> New fleet if slower than the top.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Equal times merge (≤ doesn’t push).<\/p>","c":{"py":"def car_fleet(target, position, speed):\n    cars = sorted(zip(position, speed), reverse=True)  # closest to target first\n    stack = []                                          # arrival time per fleet\n    for p, s in cars:\n        t = (target - p) / s\n        if not stack or t > stack[-1]:\n            stack.append(t)          # slower than the fleet ahead: new fleet\n        # otherwise it catches up and joins the fleet ahead\n    return len(stack)","java":"public int carFleet(int target, int[] position, int[] speed) {\n    int n = position.length;\n    Integer[] idx = new Integer[n];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) idx[i] = i;\n    Arrays.sort(idx, (a, b) -> position[b] - position[a]);   // closest to target first\n    Deque<Double> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();                 // arrival time per fleet\n    for (int i : idx) {\n        double t = (double) (target - position[i]) / speed[i];\n        if (stack.isEmpty() || t > stack.peek())\n            stack.push(t);          // slower than the fleet ahead: new fleet\n        // otherwise it catches up and joins the fleet ahead\n    }\n    return stack.size();\n}","cpp":"int carFleet(int target, vector<int>& position, vector<int>& speed) {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> cars;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < position.size(); i++) cars.push_back({position[i], speed[i]});\n    sort(cars.rbegin(), cars.rend());                    // closest to target first\n    vector<double> st;                                   // arrival time per fleet\n    for (auto [p, s] : cars) {\n        double t = (double)(target - p) / s;\n        if (st.empty() || t > st.back())\n            st.push_back(t);        // slower than the fleet ahead: new fleet\n        // otherwise it catches up and joins the fleet ahead\n    }\n    return st.size();\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-853"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/car-fleet/"]],"ask":"Number of car fleets arriving at the target."},{"i":"p-stack-84","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Largest Rectangle in Histogram","sub":"LeetCode 84 · Hard · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":84,"q":["Largest Rectangle in Histogram","how to solve Largest Rectangle in Histogram","Largest Rectangle in Histogram approach","Largest Rectangle in Histogram solution","leetcode 84","lc 84","Largest rectangle area under a histogram.","Largest rectangle that fits under the histogram."],"w":"Stack How far a bar extends → increasing stack + sentinel. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> How far a bar extends → increasing stack + sentinel.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Increasing stack: when a lower bar arrives, popped bars can&#x27;t extend further right, so compute their areas.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Largest rectangle that fits under the histogram.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> For each pair of bars, min height × width: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Append a 0 bar to flush the stack.<\/li><li>When h[i] &lt; top height: pop, width = i − newTop − 1, area = h × width.<\/li><li>Push i.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Stack:<\/b> Increasing heights.<\/li><li><b>Pop:<\/b> When a shorter bar arrives.<\/li><li><b>Width:<\/b> i − new top − 1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Use ≥ when popping so equal heights are handled.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def largest_rectangle_area(heights):\n    stack = []                              # indexes, heights increasing\n    best = 0\n    for i, h in enumerate(heights + [0]):   # sentinel 0 flushes everything\n        while stack and heights[stack[-1]] >= h:\n            height = heights[stack.pop()]\n            left = stack[-1] if stack else -1      # previous smaller bar\n            best = max(best, height * (i - left - 1))\n        stack.append(i)\n    return best","java":"public int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();   // indexes, heights increasing\n    int best = 0, n = heights.length;\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n        int h = (i == n) ? 0 : heights[i];       // sentinel 0 flushes everything\n        while (!stack.isEmpty() && heights[stack.peek()] >= h) {\n            int height = heights[stack.pop()];\n            int left = stack.isEmpty() ? -1 : stack.peek();   // previous smaller bar\n            best = Math.max(best, height * (i - left - 1));\n        }\n        stack.push(i);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int largestRectangleArea(vector<int>& heights) {\n    vector<int> st;                              // indexes, heights increasing\n    int best = 0, n = heights.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n        int h = (i == n) ? 0 : heights[i];       // sentinel 0 flushes everything\n        while (!st.empty() && heights[st.back()] >= h) {\n            int height = heights[st.back()]; st.pop_back();\n            int left = st.empty() ? -1 : st.back();   // previous smaller bar\n            best = max(best, height * (i - left - 1));\n        }\n        st.push_back(i);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-84"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-rectangle-in-histogram/"]],"ask":"Largest rectangle area under a histogram."},{"i":"p-stack-85","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Maximal Rectangle","sub":"LeetCode 85 · Hard · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":85,"q":["Maximal Rectangle","how to solve Maximal Rectangle","Maximal Rectangle approach","Maximal Rectangle solution","leetcode 85","lc 85","Largest rectangle of 1s in a binary matrix.","Largest rectangle made only of 1s in a grid."],"w":"Stack 2-D rectangle → histogram per row. Complexity O(R · C)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> 2-D rectangle → histogram per row.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Treat each row as the base of a histogram of consecutive 1s, run problem 84 on it.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Largest rectangle made only of 1s in a grid.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check every rectangle: O(R²C²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>heights[c] = heights[c] + 1 if cell is &#x27;1&#x27; else 0, row by row.<\/li><li>Run the largest-rectangle stack on heights.<\/li><li>Keep the max.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Rows:<\/b> Build a histogram per row.<\/li><li><b>Each row:<\/b> Run Largest Rectangle.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Max over rows.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Reset height to 0 on a “0” cell.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def maximal_rectangle(matrix):\n    if not matrix:\n        return 0\n    heights = [0] * len(matrix[0])\n    best = 0\n    for row in matrix:\n        for c, v in enumerate(row):\n            heights[c] = heights[c] + 1 if v == \"1\" else 0   # a histogram per row\n        best = max(best, largest_rectangle_area(heights))\n    return best","java":"public int maximalRectangle(char[][] matrix) {\n    if (matrix.length == 0) return 0;\n    int[] heights = new int[matrix[0].length];\n    int best = 0;\n    for (char[] row : matrix) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < row.length; c++)\n            heights[c] = row[c] == '1' ? heights[c] + 1 : 0;   // a histogram per row\n        best = Math.max(best, largestRectangleArea(heights));\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n\nprivate int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {   // LeetCode 84\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    int best = 0, n = heights.length;\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n        int h = (i == n) ? 0 : heights[i];\n        while (!stack.isEmpty() && heights[stack.peek()] >= h) {\n            int height = heights[stack.pop()];\n            int left = stack.isEmpty() ? -1 : stack.peek();\n            best = Math.max(best, height * (i - left - 1));\n        }\n        stack.push(i);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int maximalRectangle(vector<vector<char>>& matrix) {\n    if (matrix.empty()) return 0;\n    vector<int> heights(matrix[0].size(), 0);\n    int best = 0;\n    for (auto& row : matrix) {\n        for (size_t c = 0; c < row.size(); c++)\n            heights[c] = row[c] == '1' ? heights[c] + 1 : 0;   // a histogram per row\n        best = max(best, largestRectangleArea(heights));\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n\nint largestRectangleArea(vector<int>& heights) {   // LeetCode 84\n    vector<int> st;\n    int best = 0, n = heights.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n        int h = (i == n) ? 0 : heights[i];\n        while (!st.empty() && heights[st.back()] >= h) {\n            int height = heights[st.back()]; st.pop_back();\n            int left = st.empty() ? -1 : st.back();\n            best = max(best, height * (i - left - 1));\n        }\n        st.push_back(i);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-85"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/maximal-rectangle/"]],"ask":"Largest rectangle of 1s in a binary matrix."},{"i":"p-stack-402","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Remove K Digits","sub":"LeetCode 402 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":402,"q":["Remove K Digits","how to solve Remove K Digits","Remove K Digits approach","Remove K Digits solution","leetcode 402","lc 402","Remove k digits to get the smallest number.","Remove k digits to make the smallest number."],"w":"Stack Smallest after removals → greedy increasing stack. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Smallest after removals → greedy increasing stack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Greedy: a digit bigger than the one after it should go. Increasing stack.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Remove k digits to make the smallest number.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every removal set: combinatorial.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each digit, while k &gt; 0 and the top is bigger, pop (k −= 1).<\/li><li>Push the digit.<\/li><li>Remove remaining k from the end, strip leading zeros, &#x27;0&#x27; if empty.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Stack:<\/b> Increasing digits.<\/li><li><b>Pop:<\/b> Top &gt; current while k.<\/li><li><b>Finish:<\/b> Trim end, strip zeros.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Return &quot;0&quot; for an empty result.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def remove_kdigits(num, k):\n    stack = []\n    for d in num:\n        while k and stack and stack[-1] > d:\n            stack.pop()                  # a bigger digit earlier costs most\n            k -= 1\n        stack.append(d)\n    stack = stack[:len(stack) - k]       # leftover removals come off the end\n    return \"\".join(stack).lstrip(\"0\") or \"0\"","java":"public String removeKdigits(String num, int k) {\n    StringBuilder st = new StringBuilder();          // used as a stack\n    for (char d : num.toCharArray()) {\n        while (k > 0 && st.length() > 0 && st.charAt(st.length() - 1) > d) {\n            st.deleteCharAt(st.length() - 1);        // a bigger digit earlier costs most\n            k--;\n        }\n        st.append(d);\n    }\n    st.setLength(st.length() - k);                   // leftover removals come off the end\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < st.length() && st.charAt(i) == '0') i++;\n    return i == st.length() ? \"0\" : st.substring(i);\n}","cpp":"string removeKdigits(string num, int k) {\n    string st;                                       // used as a stack\n    for (char d : num) {\n        while (k > 0 && !st.empty() && st.back() > d) {\n            st.pop_back();                           // a bigger digit earlier costs most\n            k--;\n        }\n        st.push_back(d);\n    }\n    st.resize(st.size() - k);                        // leftover removals come off the end\n    size_t i = st.find_first_not_of('0');\n    return i == string::npos ? \"0\" : st.substr(i);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-402"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-k-digits/"]],"ask":"Remove k digits to get the smallest number."},{"i":"p-stack-735","k":"prob","s":"stack","t":"Asteroid Collision","sub":"LeetCode 735 · Medium · Stack","cat":"DSA problems","n":735,"q":["Asteroid Collision","how to solve Asteroid Collision","Asteroid Collision approach","Asteroid Collision solution","leetcode 735","lc 735","Simulate collisions; return survivors.","Asteroids move right (+) or left (−); colliding ones explode. What's left?"],"w":"Stack Collisions with the latest → stack. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Collisions with the latest → stack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A stack of survivors; only a right-mover on top can hit a new left-mover.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Asteroids move right (+) or left (−); colliding ones explode. What&#x27;s left?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Repeat passes until nothing changes: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each a: while a &lt; 0 and top &gt; 0: compare sizes.<\/li><li>Top smaller → pop and continue; equal → pop both; bigger → a dies.<\/li><li>If a survives, push it.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Stack:<\/b> Survivors.<\/li><li><b>Fight:<\/b> Left-mover vs right-moving tops.<\/li><li><b>Ties:<\/b> Both explode.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Only a negative meeting a positive top collides.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def asteroid_collision(asteroids):\n    stack = []\n    for a in asteroids:\n        alive = True\n        while alive and a < 0 and stack and stack[-1] > 0:\n            if stack[-1] < -a:\n                stack.pop()              # top explodes; keep checking\n                continue\n            if stack[-1] == -a:\n                stack.pop()              # both explode\n            alive = False\n        if alive:\n            stack.append(a)\n    return stack","java":"public int[] asteroidCollision(int[] asteroids) {\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (int a : asteroids) {\n        boolean alive = true;\n        while (alive && a < 0 && !stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek() > 0) {\n            if (stack.peek() < -a) { stack.pop(); continue; }   // top explodes; keep checking\n            if (stack.peek() == -a) stack.pop();                // both explode\n            alive = false;\n        }\n        if (alive) stack.push(a);\n    }\n    int[] res = new int[stack.size()];\n    for (int i = res.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) res[i] = stack.pop();\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> asteroidCollision(vector<int>& asteroids) {\n    vector<int> st;\n    for (int a : asteroids) {\n        bool alive = true;\n        while (alive && a < 0 && !st.empty() && st.back() > 0) {\n            if (st.back() < -a) { st.pop_back(); continue; }    // top explodes; keep checking\n            if (st.back() == -a) st.pop_back();                 // both explode\n            alive = false;\n        }\n        if (alive) st.push_back(a);\n    }\n    return st;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","stack.html#p-735"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/asteroid-collision/"]],"ask":"Simulate collisions; return survivors."},{"i":"p-binary-search-704","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Binary Search","sub":"LeetCode 704 · Easy · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":704,"q":["Binary Search","how to solve Binary Search","Binary Search approach","Binary Search solution","leetcode 704","lc 704","Return the index of target in a sorted array, or −1.","Find target's index in a sorted array, or −1."],"w":"Binary Search Sorted + find → binary search. Complexity O(log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Sorted + find → binary search.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Look at the middle; the half that can&#x27;t contain target is thrown away.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Find target&#x27;s index in a sorted array, or −1.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan left to right: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = 0, hi = n − 1.<\/li><li>mid = (lo + hi) // 2. Equal → return mid.<\/li><li>Smaller → lo = mid + 1, bigger → hi = mid − 1. Loop ends → −1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Range:<\/b> [0, n − 1] inclusive.<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> lo ≤ hi.<\/li><li><b>Move:<\/b> mid ± 1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Mixing inclusive hi with lo &lt; hi skips the last element.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def search(nums, target):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1          # search [lo, hi], inclusive\n    while lo <= hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if nums[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        if nums[mid] < target:\n            lo = mid + 1               # target is right of mid\n        else:\n            hi = mid - 1               # target is left of mid\n    return -1","java":"public int search(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;      // search [lo, hi], inclusive\n    while (lo <= hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;\n        if (nums[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;   // target is right of mid\n        else hi = mid - 1;                      // target is left of mid\n    }\n    return -1;\n}","cpp":"int search(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.size() - 1;      // search [lo, hi], inclusive\n    while (lo <= hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;\n        if (nums[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;   // target is right of mid\n        else hi = mid - 1;                      // target is left of mid\n    }\n    return -1;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-704"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-search/"]],"ask":"Return the index of target in a sorted array, or −1."},{"i":"p-binary-search-35","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Search Insert Position","sub":"LeetCode 35 · Easy · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":35,"q":["Search Insert Position","how to solve Search Insert Position","Search Insert Position approach","Search Insert Position solution","leetcode 35","lc 35","Index of target, or where it would be inserted.","Where is target, or where would it go to keep the array sorted?"],"w":"Binary Search Insert position → lower bound. Complexity O(log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Insert position → lower bound.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> That&#x27;s &#x27;first index with value ≥ target&#x27;: a lower-bound binary search.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Where is target, or where would it go to keep the array sorted?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan for the first value ≥ target: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = 0, hi = n.<\/li><li>If nums[mid] &lt; target → lo = mid + 1, else hi = mid.<\/li><li>Return lo.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Predicate:<\/b> nums[i] ≥ target.<\/li><li><b>Template:<\/b> First True.<\/li><li><b>Range:<\/b> hi = n.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> hi must start at n so “insert at the end” is possible.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def lower_bound(nums, target):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums)              # hi = len means \"nothing qualifies\"\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if nums[mid] >= target:        # mid could be the answer: keep it\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1               # mid is too small: discard it\n    return lo                          # first index with nums[i] >= target","java":"public int lowerBound(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.length;          // hi = length means \"nothing qualifies\"\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] >= target) hi = mid; // mid could be the answer: keep it\n        else lo = mid + 1;                 // mid is too small: discard it\n    }\n    return lo;                             // first index with nums[i] >= target\n}","cpp":"int lowerBound(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.size();          // hi = size means \"nothing qualifies\"\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] >= target) hi = mid; // mid could be the answer: keep it\n        else lo = mid + 1;                 // mid is too small: discard it\n    }\n    return lo;   // first index with nums[i] >= target (same as std::lower_bound)\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-35"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/search-insert-position/"]],"ask":"Index of target, or where it would be inserted."},{"i":"p-binary-search-34","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"First and Last Position","sub":"LeetCode 34 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":34,"q":["First and Last Position","how to solve First and Last Position","First and Last Position approach","First and Last Position solution","leetcode 34","lc 34","[first, last] index of target.","First and last index of target in a sorted array with repeats."],"w":"Binary Search First / last → two lower bounds. Complexity O(log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> First / last → two lower bounds.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Two lower-bound searches: first ≥ target, and first ≥ target + 1, minus one.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> First and last index of target in a sorted array with repeats.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Find one copy, then walk out: O(n) in the worst case.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>first = lowerBound(target).<\/li><li>If it&#x27;s out of range or ≠ target → [−1, −1].<\/li><li>last = lowerBound(target + 1) − 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>First:<\/b> Lower bound of target.<\/li><li><b>Last:<\/b> Lower bound of target + 1, minus 1.<\/li><li><b>Absent:<\/b> Check nums[first].<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Expanding outward from a found index is O(n) worst case.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from bisect import bisect_left, bisect_right\n\ndef search_range(nums, target):\n    lo = bisect_left(nums, target)                 # first index >= target\n    if lo == len(nums) or nums[lo] != target:\n        return [-1, -1]\n    return [lo, bisect_right(nums, target) - 1]    # last index <= target","java":"public int[] searchRange(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int lo = lowerBound(nums, target);                 // first index >= target\n    if (lo == nums.length || nums[lo] != target) return new int[]{-1, -1};\n    return new int[]{lo, lowerBound(nums, target + 1) - 1};   // last index <= target\n}\n\nprivate int lowerBound(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.length;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] >= target) hi = mid;\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> searchRange(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    int lo = lower_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target) - nums.begin();   // first >= target\n    if (lo == (int)nums.size() || nums[lo] != target) return {-1, -1};\n    int hi = upper_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target) - nums.begin();   // first > target\n    return {lo, hi - 1};\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-34"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array/"]],"ask":"[first, last] index of target."},{"i":"p-binary-search-74","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Search a 2D Matrix","sub":"LeetCode 74 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":74,"q":["Search a 2D Matrix","how to solve Search a 2D Matrix","Search a 2D Matrix approach","Search a 2D Matrix solution","leetcode 74","lc 74","Find target in a row-wise sorted matrix where rows continue each other.","Each row is sorted and starts after the previous row ends. Is target in the matrix?"],"w":"Binary Search Flattenable matrix → index arithmetic. Complexity O(log(R · C))","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Flattenable matrix → index arithmetic.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> It&#x27;s really one sorted list of R·C values. Index i maps to cell (i // C, i % C).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each row is sorted and starts after the previous row ends. Is target in the matrix?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan everything: O(R·C).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = 0, hi = R·C − 1.<\/li><li>Read matrix[mid // C][mid % C].<\/li><li>Normal binary search.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>View:<\/b> One list of R × C.<\/li><li><b>Index:<\/b> mid // C, mid % C.<\/li><li><b>Search:<\/b> Plain binary search.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log(R · C))<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Problem 240 (rows don’t continue) needs the staircase instead.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def search_matrix(matrix, target):\n    R, C = len(matrix), len(matrix[0])\n    lo, hi = 0, R * C - 1                  # treat it as one sorted list\n    while lo <= hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        v = matrix[mid // C][mid % C]\n        if v == target:\n            return True\n        if v < target:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid - 1\n    return False","java":"public boolean searchMatrix(int[][] matrix, int target) {\n    int R = matrix.length, C = matrix[0].length;\n    int lo = 0, hi = R * C - 1;                // treat it as one sorted list\n    while (lo <= hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        int v = matrix[mid / C][mid % C];\n        if (v == target) return true;\n        if (v < target) lo = mid + 1;\n        else hi = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return false;\n}","cpp":"bool searchMatrix(vector<vector<int>>& matrix, int target) {\n    int R = matrix.size(), C = matrix[0].size();\n    int lo = 0, hi = R * C - 1;                // treat it as one sorted list\n    while (lo <= hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        int v = matrix[mid / C][mid % C];\n        if (v == target) return true;\n        if (v < target) lo = mid + 1;\n        else hi = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return false;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-74"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/search-a-2d-matrix/"]],"ask":"Find target in a row-wise sorted matrix where rows continue each other."},{"i":"p-binary-search-278","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"First Bad Version","sub":"LeetCode 278 · Easy · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":278,"q":["First Bad Version","how to solve First Bad Version","First Bad Version approach","First Bad Version solution","leetcode 278","lc 278","Find the first bad version with as few API calls as possible.","Versions go good, good, …, bad, bad. Find the first bad one with few API calls."],"w":"Binary Search First where it flips → lower bound. Complexity O(log n) calls","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> First where it flips → lower bound.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Binary search for the first True in a monotonic True/False sequence.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Versions go good, good, …, bad, bad. Find the first bad one with few API calls.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check each version: O(n) calls.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = 1, hi = n.<\/li><li>isBad(mid) → hi = mid (might be the first), else lo = mid + 1.<\/li><li>Return lo.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Predicate:<\/b> isBadVersion.<\/li><li><b>Template:<\/b> First True.<\/li><li><b>Range:<\/b> [1, n].<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n) calls<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> In other languages, mid = lo + (hi − lo) / 2 avoids overflow.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def first_bad_version(n):\n    lo, hi = 1, n\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if isBadVersion(mid):\n            hi = mid               # first bad is mid or earlier\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo","java":"/* The isBadVersion API is defined in the parent class VersionControl. */\npublic class Solution extends VersionControl {\n    public int firstBadVersion(int n) {\n        int lo = 1, hi = n;\n        while (lo < hi) {\n            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;   // (lo + hi) / 2 would overflow here\n            if (isBadVersion(mid)) hi = mid;   // first bad is mid or earlier\n            else lo = mid + 1;\n        }\n        return lo;\n    }\n}","cpp":"// The API isBadVersion is defined for you.\nint firstBadVersion(int n) {\n    int lo = 1, hi = n;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;     // (lo + hi) / 2 would overflow here\n        if (isBadVersion(mid)) hi = mid;  // first bad is mid or earlier\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-278"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/first-bad-version/"]],"ask":"Find the first bad version with as few API calls as possible."},{"i":"p-binary-search-153","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array","sub":"LeetCode 153 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":153,"q":["Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array","how to solve Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array","Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array approach","Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array solution","leetcode 153","lc 153","Minimum of a rotated sorted array (distinct values).","A sorted array was rotated. Find the smallest value."],"w":"Binary Search Rotated min → compare with hi. Complexity O(log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Rotated min → compare with hi.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Compare mid with the right end: if nums[mid] &gt; nums[hi], the drop (minimum) is to the right.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A sorted array was rotated. Find the smallest value.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo, hi = ends.<\/li><li>nums[mid] &gt; nums[hi] → lo = mid + 1, else hi = mid.<\/li><li>Return nums[lo].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Compare:<\/b> mid with hi.<\/li><li><b>Greater:<\/b> lo = mid + 1.<\/li><li><b>Else:<\/b> hi = mid.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Use lo &lt; hi, not ≤.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_min(nums):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if nums[mid] > nums[hi]:\n            lo = mid + 1          # the drop is right of mid\n        else:\n            hi = mid              # mid might be the min\n    return nums[lo]","java":"public int findMin(int[] nums) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] > nums[hi]) lo = mid + 1;   // the drop is right of mid\n        else hi = mid;                            // mid might be the min\n    }\n    return nums[lo];\n}","cpp":"int findMin(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.size() - 1;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] > nums[hi]) lo = mid + 1;   // the drop is right of mid\n        else hi = mid;                            // mid might be the min\n    }\n    return nums[lo];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-153"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array/"]],"ask":"Minimum of a rotated sorted array (distinct values)."},{"i":"p-binary-search-33","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Search in Rotated Sorted Array","sub":"LeetCode 33 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":33,"q":["Search in Rotated Sorted Array","how to solve Search in Rotated Sorted Array","Search in Rotated Sorted Array approach","Search in Rotated Sorted Array solution","leetcode 33","lc 33","Index of target in a rotated sorted array.","Find target in a rotated sorted array."],"w":"Binary Search Rotated → find the sorted half. Complexity O(log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Rotated → find the sorted half.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> At every mid, one half is properly sorted. Check if target lies in that half&#x27;s range.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Find target in a rotated sorted array.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If nums[lo] ≤ nums[mid], the left half is sorted: target in [nums[lo], nums[mid]) → go left, else right.<\/li><li>Otherwise the right half is sorted: same check on (nums[mid], nums[hi]].<\/li><li>Return mid when found.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sorted half:<\/b> nums[lo] ≤ nums[mid] → left.<\/li><li><b>Range check:<\/b> Is target inside that half?<\/li><li><b>Move:<\/b> Into it, or the other half.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Use ≤ in nums[lo] ≤ nums[mid] for the lo == mid case.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def search_rotated(nums, target):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while lo <= hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if nums[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        if nums[lo] <= nums[mid]:                  # left half is sorted\n            if nums[lo] <= target < nums[mid]:\n                hi = mid - 1\n            else:\n                lo = mid + 1\n        else:                                      # right half is sorted\n            if nums[mid] < target <= nums[hi]:\n                lo = mid + 1\n            else:\n                hi = mid - 1\n    return -1","java":"public int search(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;\n    while (lo <= hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;\n        if (nums[lo] <= nums[mid]) {                       // left half is sorted\n            if (nums[lo] <= target && target < nums[mid]) hi = mid - 1;\n            else lo = mid + 1;\n        } else {                                           // right half is sorted\n            if (nums[mid] < target && target <= nums[hi]) lo = mid + 1;\n            else hi = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}","cpp":"int search(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.size() - 1;\n    while (lo <= hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;\n        if (nums[lo] <= nums[mid]) {                       // left half is sorted\n            if (nums[lo] <= target && target < nums[mid]) hi = mid - 1;\n            else lo = mid + 1;\n        } else {                                           // right half is sorted\n            if (nums[mid] < target && target <= nums[hi]) lo = mid + 1;\n            else hi = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-33"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/search-in-rotated-sorted-array/"]],"ask":"Index of target in a rotated sorted array."},{"i":"p-binary-search-162","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Find Peak Element","sub":"LeetCode 162 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":162,"q":["Find Peak Element","how to solve Find Peak Element","Find Peak Element approach","Find Peak Element solution","leetcode 162","lc 162","Any index whose value is greater than its neighbours.","Find any index that's bigger than both neighbours."],"w":"Binary Search Peak → walk uphill. Complexity O(log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Peak → walk uphill.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> If nums[mid] &lt; nums[mid + 1], you&#x27;re on an uphill: a peak must exist to the right.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Find any index that&#x27;s bigger than both neighbours.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan for a peak: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = 0, hi = n − 1.<\/li><li>Uphill (nums[mid] &lt; nums[mid+1]) → lo = mid + 1, else hi = mid.<\/li><li>Return lo.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Compare:<\/b> mid vs mid + 1.<\/li><li><b>Uphill:<\/b> lo = mid + 1.<\/li><li><b>Downhill:<\/b> hi = mid.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Out-of-bounds neighbours count as −∞.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_peak_element(nums):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1]:\n            lo = mid + 1          # uphill to the right: a peak exists there\n        else:\n            hi = mid              # downhill: mid or something left is a peak\n    return lo","java":"public int findPeakElement(int[] nums) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1]) lo = mid + 1;   // uphill to the right: a peak exists there\n        else hi = mid;                                 // downhill: mid or something left is a peak\n    }\n    return lo;\n}","cpp":"int findPeakElement(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.size() - 1;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1]) lo = mid + 1;   // uphill to the right: a peak exists there\n        else hi = mid;                                 // downhill: mid or something left is a peak\n    }\n    return lo;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-162"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/find-peak-element/"]],"ask":"Any index whose value is greater than its neighbours."},{"i":"p-binary-search-540","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Single Element in a Sorted Array","sub":"LeetCode 540 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":540,"q":["Single Element in a Sorted Array","how to solve Single Element in a Sorted Array","Single Element in a Sorted Array approach","Single Element in a Sorted Array solution","leetcode 540","lc 540","Every value appears twice except one. Find it in O(log n).","Every value appears twice, side by side, except one. Find it in O(log n)."],"w":"Binary Search Pattern flips at the answer → binary search the flip. Complexity O(log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Pattern flips at the answer → binary search the flip.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Before the single element, pairs start at even indexes; after it, at odd ones.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Every value appears twice, side by side, except one. Find it in O(log n).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> XOR everything: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Make mid even (mid −= mid % 2).<\/li><li>If nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1], the single is to the right: lo = mid + 2.<\/li><li>Else hi = mid. Return nums[lo].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Before the single, pairs start on even indexes.<\/li><li><b>Align:<\/b> Make mid even.<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]?<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Jump by 2 when the pair is intact.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def single_non_duplicate(nums):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if mid % 2:\n            mid -= 1                     # look at a pair starting on an even index\n        if nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]:\n            lo = mid + 2                 # pairs intact so far: single is right\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return nums[lo]","java":"public int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (mid % 2 == 1) mid--;               // look at a pair starting on an even index\n        if (nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]) lo = mid + 2;   // pairs intact so far: single is right\n        else hi = mid;\n    }\n    return nums[lo];\n}","cpp":"int singleNonDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.size() - 1;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (mid % 2 == 1) mid--;               // look at a pair starting on an even index\n        if (nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]) lo = mid + 2;   // pairs intact so far: single is right\n        else hi = mid;\n    }\n    return nums[lo];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-540"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/single-element-in-a-sorted-array/"]],"ask":"Every value appears twice except one. Find it in O(log n)."},{"i":"p-binary-search-875","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Koko Eating Bananas","sub":"LeetCode 875 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":875,"q":["Koko Eating Bananas","how to solve Koko Eating Bananas","Koko Eating Bananas approach","Koko Eating Bananas solution","leetcode 875","lc 875","Slowest eating speed to finish all piles in h hours.","Slowest bananas-per-hour speed that finishes all piles within h hours."],"w":"Binary Search Minimum speed that works → search the answer. Complexity O(n log max)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Minimum speed that works → search the answer.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Faster always finishes sooner (monotonic), so binary search the speed.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Slowest bananas-per-hour speed that finishes all piles within h hours.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every speed from 1 up: O(max × n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = 1, hi = max(piles).<\/li><li>hours(mid) = Σ ceil(pile / mid). If ≤ h → hi = mid, else lo = mid + 1.<\/li><li>Return lo.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Range:<\/b> 1 … max(piles).<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> Σ ceil(p / k) ≤ h.<\/li><li><b>Find:<\/b> First speed that works.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log max)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Use ceil, or (p + k − 1) // k.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import math\n\ndef min_eating_speed(piles, h):\n    def hours(k):\n        return sum(math.ceil(p / k) for p in piles)\n    lo, hi = 1, max(piles)             # the answer is somewhere in here\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if hours(mid) <= h:            # fast enough: try slower\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1               # too slow: must go faster\n    return lo","java":"public int minEatingSpeed(int[] piles, int h) {\n    int lo = 1, hi = 0;\n    for (int p : piles) hi = Math.max(hi, p);  // the answer is somewhere in here\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (hours(piles, mid) <= h) hi = mid;  // fast enough: try slower\n        else lo = mid + 1;                     // too slow: must go faster\n    }\n    return lo;\n}\n\nprivate long hours(int[] piles, int k) {\n    long total = 0;\n    for (int p : piles) total += (p + k - 1) / k;   // ceil(p / k)\n    return total;\n}","cpp":"int minEatingSpeed(vector<int>& piles, int h) {\n    auto hours = [&](int k) {\n        long long total = 0;\n        for (int p : piles) total += (p + (long long)k - 1) / k;   // ceil(p / k)\n        return total;\n    };\n    int lo = 1, hi = *max_element(piles.begin(), piles.end());  // answer is in here\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (hours(mid) <= h) hi = mid;         // fast enough: try slower\n        else lo = mid + 1;                     // too slow: must go faster\n    }\n    return lo;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-875"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/koko-eating-bananas/"]],"ask":"Slowest eating speed to finish all piles in h hours."},{"i":"p-binary-search-1011","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Capacity To Ship Packages Within D Days","sub":"LeetCode 1011 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":1011,"q":["Capacity To Ship Packages Within D Days","how to solve Capacity To Ship Packages Within D Days","Capacity To Ship Packages Within D Days approach","Capacity To Ship Packages Within D Days solution","leetcode 1011","lc 1011","Smallest ship capacity to deliver all packages in D days, in order.","Smallest ship capacity that ships everything, in order, within D days."],"w":"Binary Search Minimum capacity → search the answer. Complexity O(n log sum)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Minimum capacity → search the answer.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Bigger capacity never needs more days: binary search between max(weight) and sum(weights).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Smallest ship capacity that ships everything, in order, within D days.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every capacity: slow.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = max(w), hi = sum(w).<\/li><li>Simulate days needed for capacity mid (greedy fill).<\/li><li>≤ D → hi = mid, else lo = mid + 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Range:<\/b> max … sum.<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> Greedy day count.<\/li><li><b>Find:<\/b> First capacity that fits D.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log sum)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> lo must be max(weights), or a single package can never fit.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def ship_within_days(weights, days):\n    def days_needed(cap):\n        d, load = 1, 0\n        for w in weights:\n            if load + w > cap:\n                d, load = d + 1, 0           # start a new day\n            load += w\n        return d\n    lo, hi = max(weights), sum(weights)      # smallest and largest sane capacity\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if days_needed(mid) <= days:\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo","java":"public int shipWithinDays(int[] weights, int days) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = 0;\n    for (int w : weights) { lo = Math.max(lo, w); hi += w; }   // smallest and largest sane capacity\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (daysNeeded(weights, mid) <= days) hi = mid;\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}\n\nprivate int daysNeeded(int[] weights, int cap) {\n    int d = 1, load = 0;\n    for (int w : weights) {\n        if (load + w > cap) { d++; load = 0; }   // start a new day\n        load += w;\n    }\n    return d;\n}","cpp":"int shipWithinDays(vector<int>& weights, int days) {\n    auto daysNeeded = [&](int cap) {\n        int d = 1, load = 0;\n        for (int w : weights) {\n            if (load + w > cap) { d++; load = 0; }   // start a new day\n            load += w;\n        }\n        return d;\n    };\n    int lo = *max_element(weights.begin(), weights.end());     // smallest sane capacity\n    int hi = accumulate(weights.begin(), weights.end(), 0);    // largest\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (daysNeeded(mid) <= days) hi = mid;\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-1011"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/capacity-to-ship-packages-within-d-days/"]],"ask":"Smallest ship capacity to deliver all packages in D days, in order."},{"i":"p-binary-search-410","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Split Array Largest Sum","sub":"LeetCode 410 · Hard · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":410,"q":["Split Array Largest Sum","how to solve Split Array Largest Sum","Split Array Largest Sum approach","Split Array Largest Sum solution","leetcode 410","lc 410","Split into k subarrays minimizing the largest subarray sum.","Split into k contiguous parts so the biggest part's sum is as small as possible."],"w":"Binary Search Minimize the max → binary search the answer. Complexity O(n log sum)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Minimize the max → binary search the answer.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Same as shipping: binary search the answer &#x27;largest sum ≤ X&#x27;, check greedily.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Split into k contiguous parts so the biggest part&#x27;s sum is as small as possible.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all split points: combinatorial (or DP O(k·n²)).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = max(nums), hi = sum(nums).<\/li><li>Count parts needed if no part may exceed mid.<\/li><li>Parts ≤ k → hi = mid, else lo = mid + 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> “Minimize the maximum.”<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> Greedy pieces needed for a limit.<\/li><li><b>Find:<\/b> First limit needing ≤ k pieces.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log sum)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Same code as 1011 with renamed variables.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def split_array(nums, k):\n    def pieces(limit):\n        count, total = 1, 0\n        for x in nums:\n            if total + x > limit:\n                count, total = count + 1, 0\n            total += x\n        return count\n    lo, hi = max(nums), sum(nums)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if pieces(mid) <= k:         # this largest-sum limit is achievable\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo","java":"public int splitArray(int[] nums, int k) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) { lo = Math.max(lo, x); hi += x; }\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (pieces(nums, mid) <= k) hi = mid;   // this largest-sum limit is achievable\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}\n\nprivate int pieces(int[] nums, int limit) {\n    int count = 1, total = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (total + x > limit) { count++; total = 0; }\n        total += x;\n    }\n    return count;\n}","cpp":"int splitArray(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    auto pieces = [&](int limit) {\n        int count = 1, total = 0;\n        for (int x : nums) {\n            if (total + x > limit) { count++; total = 0; }\n            total += x;\n        }\n        return count;\n    };\n    int lo = *max_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    int hi = accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0);\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (pieces(mid) <= k) hi = mid;         // this largest-sum limit is achievable\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-410"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/split-array-largest-sum/"]],"ask":"Split into k subarrays minimizing the largest subarray sum."},{"i":"p-binary-search-69","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Sqrt(x)","sub":"LeetCode 69 · Easy · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":69,"q":["Sqrt(x)","how to solve Sqrt(x)","Sqrt(x) approach","Sqrt(x) solution","leetcode 69","lc 69","Integer square root, rounded down.","Integer square root, rounded down, without sqrt()."],"w":"Binary Search Largest x that works → round mid up. Complexity O(log x)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Largest x that works → round mid up.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Find the largest m with m·m ≤ x.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Integer square root, rounded down, without sqrt().<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try 1, 2, 3…: O(√x).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = 0, hi = x.<\/li><li>m·m ≤ x → answer = m, lo = m + 1.<\/li><li>Else hi = m − 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Predicate:<\/b> mid² ≤ x.<\/li><li><b>Template:<\/b> Last True.<\/li><li><b>Round:<\/b> mid rounded up.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log x)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Rounding down here loops forever.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def my_sqrt(x):\n    lo, hi = 0, x\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2         # round UP: we keep mid on success\n        if mid * mid <= x:\n            lo = mid                     # mid works: answer is mid or bigger\n        else:\n            hi = mid - 1\n    return lo","java":"public int mySqrt(int x) {\n    long lo = 0, hi = x;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        long mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2;    // round UP: we keep mid on success\n        if (mid * mid <= x) lo = mid;    // mid works: answer is mid or bigger\n        else hi = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return (int) lo;\n}","cpp":"int mySqrt(int x) {\n    long long lo = 0, hi = x;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        long long mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2;   // round UP: we keep mid on success\n        if (mid * mid <= x) lo = mid;        // mid works: answer is mid or bigger\n        else hi = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return (int)lo;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-69"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/sqrtx/"]],"ask":"Integer square root, rounded down."},{"i":"p-binary-search-378","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix","sub":"LeetCode 378 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":378,"q":["Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix","how to solve Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix","Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix approach","Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix solution","leetcode 378","lc 378","Kth smallest value in a matrix with sorted rows and columns.","Rows and columns are sorted. Find the kth smallest value."],"w":"Binary Search Kth smallest in structure → binary search the value. Complexity O(n log(range))","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Kth smallest in structure → binary search the value.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Binary search on the value: count how many cells are ≤ mid (walk from the bottom-left).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Rows and columns are sorted. Find the kth smallest value.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Flatten and sort: O(n² log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>lo = smallest, hi = largest.<\/li><li>count(mid) using a staircase walk: O(n).<\/li><li>count ≥ k → hi = mid, else lo = mid + 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Search:<\/b> The value range.<\/li><li><b>Count:<\/b> Values ≤ mid via a staircase.<\/li><li><b>Find:<\/b> First value with count ≥ k.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log(range))<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> A heap solution is O(k log n); both are worth knowing.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def kth_smallest(matrix, k):\n    n = len(matrix)\n    def count_le(x):                     # values <= x, via a staircase walk\n        r, c, cnt = n - 1, 0, 0\n        while r >= 0 and c < n:\n            if matrix[r][c] <= x:\n                cnt += r + 1             # this whole column above is <= x\n                c += 1\n            else:\n                r -= 1\n        return cnt\n    lo, hi = matrix[0][0], matrix[-1][-1]\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if count_le(mid) >= k:\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo","java":"public int kthSmallest(int[][] matrix, int k) {\n    int n = matrix.length;\n    int lo = matrix[0][0], hi = matrix[n - 1][n - 1];\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (countLE(matrix, mid) >= k) hi = mid;\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}\n\nprivate int countLE(int[][] matrix, int x) {    // values <= x, via a staircase walk\n    int n = matrix.length, r = n - 1, c = 0, cnt = 0;\n    while (r >= 0 && c < n) {\n        if (matrix[r][c] <= x) { cnt += r + 1; c++; }   // this whole column above is <= x\n        else r--;\n    }\n    return cnt;\n}","cpp":"int kthSmallest(vector<vector<int>>& matrix, int k) {\n    int n = matrix.size();\n    auto countLE = [&](int x) {                 // values <= x, via a staircase walk\n        int r = n - 1, c = 0, cnt = 0;\n        while (r >= 0 && c < n) {\n            if (matrix[r][c] <= x) { cnt += r + 1; c++; }   // whole column above is <= x\n            else r--;\n        }\n        return cnt;\n    };\n    int lo = matrix[0][0], hi = matrix[n - 1][n - 1];\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (countLE(mid) >= k) hi = mid;\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-378"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-smallest-element-in-a-sorted-matrix/"]],"ask":"Kth smallest value in a matrix with sorted rows and columns."},{"i":"p-binary-search-4","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Median of Two Sorted Arrays","sub":"LeetCode 4 · Hard · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":4,"q":["Median of Two Sorted Arrays","how to solve Median of Two Sorted Arrays","Median of Two Sorted Arrays approach","Median of Two Sorted Arrays solution","leetcode 4","lc 4","Median of two sorted arrays in O(log(min(m, n))).","Median of two sorted arrays combined, in O(log(min(m, n)))."],"w":"Binary Search Median of two → binary search the split. Complexity O(log min(m, n))","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Median of two → binary search the split.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Cut both arrays so the left parts hold half the elements and every left value ≤ every right value. Binary search the cut in the smaller array.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Median of two sorted arrays combined, in O(log(min(m, n))).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Merge them: O(m + n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Binary search i in the smaller array; j = half − i.<\/li><li>If A[i−1] &gt; B[j] move i left; if B[j−1] &gt; A[i] move i right.<\/li><li>When valid, median comes from the max of the lefts / min of the rights.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Search:<\/b> How many from the shorter array.<\/li><li><b>Valid:<\/b> aL ≤ bR and bL ≤ aR.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> From the four border values.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log min(m, n))<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Use ±∞ for empty sides.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_median_sorted_arrays(a, b):\n    if len(a) > len(b):\n        a, b = b, a                              # binary search the shorter one\n    m, n = len(a), len(b)\n    half = (m + n + 1) // 2\n    lo, hi = 0, m\n    while lo <= hi:\n        i = (lo + hi) // 2                       # take i items from a\n        j = half - i                             # and the rest from b\n        a_left = a[i - 1] if i > 0 else float(\"-inf\")\n        a_right = a[i] if i < m else float(\"inf\")\n        b_left = b[j - 1] if j > 0 else float(\"-inf\")\n        b_right = b[j] if j < n else float(\"inf\")\n        if a_left <= b_right and b_left <= a_right:   # valid split\n            if (m + n) % 2:\n                return max(a_left, b_left)\n            return (max(a_left, b_left) + min(a_right, b_right)) / 2\n        if a_left > b_right:\n            hi = i - 1                           # took too many from a\n        else:\n            lo = i + 1","java":"public double findMedianSortedArrays(int[] a, int[] b) {\n    if (a.length > b.length) return findMedianSortedArrays(b, a);   // search the shorter one\n    int m = a.length, n = b.length, half = (m + n + 1) / 2;\n    int lo = 0, hi = m;\n    while (lo <= hi) {\n        int i = (lo + hi) / 2;                   // take i items from a\n        int j = half - i;                        // and the rest from b\n        int aLeft = i > 0 ? a[i - 1] : Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n        int aRight = i < m ? a[i] : Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        int bLeft = j > 0 ? b[j - 1] : Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n        int bRight = j < n ? b[j] : Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        if (aLeft <= bRight && bLeft <= aRight) {          // valid split\n            if ((m + n) % 2 == 1) return Math.max(aLeft, bLeft);\n            return (Math.max(aLeft, bLeft) + (double) Math.min(aRight, bRight)) / 2;\n        }\n        if (aLeft > bRight) hi = i - 1;          // took too many from a\n        else lo = i + 1;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","cpp":"double findMedianSortedArrays(vector<int>& a, vector<int>& b) {\n    if (a.size() > b.size()) return findMedianSortedArrays(b, a);   // search the shorter one\n    int m = a.size(), n = b.size(), half = (m + n + 1) / 2;\n    int lo = 0, hi = m;\n    while (lo <= hi) {\n        int i = (lo + hi) / 2;                   // take i items from a\n        int j = half - i;                        // and the rest from b\n        int aLeft = i > 0 ? a[i - 1] : INT_MIN;\n        int aRight = i < m ? a[i] : INT_MAX;\n        int bLeft = j > 0 ? b[j - 1] : INT_MIN;\n        int bRight = j < n ? b[j] : INT_MAX;\n        if (aLeft <= bRight && bLeft <= aRight) {          // valid split\n            if ((m + n) % 2 == 1) return max(aLeft, bLeft);\n            return (max(aLeft, bLeft) + (double)min(aRight, bRight)) / 2;\n        }\n        if (aLeft > bRight) hi = i - 1;          // took too many from a\n        else lo = i + 1;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-4"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/median-of-two-sorted-arrays/"]],"ask":"Median of two sorted arrays in O(log(min(m, n)))."},{"i":"p-binary-search-981","k":"prob","s":"binary-search","t":"Time Based Key-Value Store","sub":"LeetCode 981 · Medium · Binary Search","cat":"DSA problems","n":981,"q":["Time Based Key-Value Store","how to solve Time Based Key-Value Store","Time Based Key-Value Store approach","Time Based Key-Value Store solution","leetcode 981","lc 981","set(key, value, t) and get(key, t) → value with the largest timestamp ≤ t.","Store values with timestamps; get(key, t) returns the value set at the latest time ≤ t."],"w":"Binary Search Latest ≤ t in sorted data → bisect. Complexity O(log n) per get","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Latest ≤ t in sorted data → bisect.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Timestamps arrive increasing, so each key&#x27;s list is sorted: binary search it.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Store values with timestamps; get(key, t) returns the value set at the latest time ≤ t.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan all versions of the key: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>set: append (t, value) to store[key].<\/li><li>get: find the rightmost t&#x27; ≤ t with bisect.<\/li><li>Return its value or &#x27;&#x27;.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Store:<\/b> Per key, timestamps in order.<\/li><li><b>Query:<\/b> bisect_right(times, t) − 1.<\/li><li><b>Empty:<\/b> Return &quot;&quot;.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n) per get<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Timestamps are strictly increasing per key, so append keeps order.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from bisect import bisect_right\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nclass TimeMap:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.times = defaultdict(list)\n        self.vals = defaultdict(list)\n\n    def set(self, key, value, timestamp):\n        self.times[key].append(timestamp)        # timestamps arrive increasing\n        self.vals[key].append(value)\n\n    def get(self, key, timestamp):\n        i = bisect_right(self.times[key], timestamp)   # entries with time <= t\n        return self.vals[key][i - 1] if i else \"\"","java":"class TimeMap {\n    private final Map<String, TreeMap<Integer, String>> store = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public void set(String key, String value, int timestamp) {\n        store.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new TreeMap<>()).put(timestamp, value);\n    }\n\n    public String get(String key, int timestamp) {\n        TreeMap<Integer, String> t = store.get(key);\n        if (t == null) return \"\";\n        Map.Entry<Integer, String> e = t.floorEntry(timestamp);   // latest time <= t\n        return e == null ? \"\" : e.getValue();\n    }\n}","cpp":"class TimeMap {\n    unordered_map<string, vector<pair<int, string>>> store;   // times arrive increasing\npublic:\n    void set(string key, string value, int timestamp) {\n        store[key].push_back({timestamp, value});\n    }\n    string get(string key, int timestamp) {\n        auto& v = store[key];\n        // first entry with time > timestamp; the one before it is the answer\n        auto it = upper_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), make_pair(timestamp, string(1, '\\x7f')));\n        return it == v.begin() ? \"\" : prev(it)->second;\n    }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","binary-search.html#p-981"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/time-based-key-value-store/"]],"ask":"set(key, value, t) and get(key, t) → value with the largest timestamp ≤ t."},{"i":"p-linked-list-206","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Reverse Linked List","sub":"LeetCode 206 · Easy · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":206,"q":["Reverse Linked List","how to solve Reverse Linked List","Reverse Linked List approach","Reverse Linked List solution","leetcode 206","lc 206","Reverse a singly linked list.","Make the list point backwards and return the new head."],"w":"Linked List Reverse → save, rewire, step. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Reverse → save, rewire, step.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Walk once, flipping each node&#x27;s next pointer to the previous node.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Make the list point backwards and return the new head.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Copy values into an array and rebuild: extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>prev = None, cur = head.<\/li><li>Save nxt = cur.next; set cur.next = prev.<\/li><li>prev = cur, cur = nxt. Return prev.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> prev, curr.<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> Save next, point back, step.<\/li><li><b>Return:<\/b> prev.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Returning head instead of prev returns the old head, now the tail.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def reverse_list(head):\n    prev, curr = None, head\n    while curr:\n        nxt = curr.next          # save the rest before breaking the link\n        curr.next = prev         # point backward\n        prev = curr              # step forward\n        curr = nxt\n    return prev","java":"public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {\n    ListNode prev = null, curr = head;\n    while (curr != null) {\n        ListNode nxt = curr.next;    // save the rest before breaking the link\n        curr.next = prev;            // point backward\n        prev = curr;                 // step forward\n        curr = nxt;\n    }\n    return prev;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* reverseList(ListNode* head) {\n    ListNode *prev = nullptr, *curr = head;\n    while (curr) {\n        ListNode* nxt = curr->next;  // save the rest before breaking the link\n        curr->next = prev;           // point backward\n        prev = curr;                 // step forward\n        curr = nxt;\n    }\n    return prev;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-206"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-linked-list/"]],"ask":"Reverse a singly linked list."},{"i":"p-linked-list-21","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Merge Two Sorted Lists","sub":"LeetCode 21 · Easy · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":21,"q":["Merge Two Sorted Lists","how to solve Merge Two Sorted Lists","Merge Two Sorted Lists approach","Merge Two Sorted Lists solution","leetcode 21","lc 21","Merge two sorted lists.","Merge two sorted lists into one sorted list."],"w":"Linked List Build a list → dummy + tail. Complexity O(m + n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Build a list → dummy + tail.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Dummy head + always attach the smaller front node.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Merge two sorted lists into one sorted list.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Collect all values, sort, rebuild: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>tail = dummy.<\/li><li>Attach the smaller of a, b; advance that list and tail.<\/li><li>Attach whatever is left. Return dummy.next.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Start:<\/b> Dummy + tail.<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> Attach the smaller.<\/li><li><b>Finish:<\/b> Attach the leftover.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(m + n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The leftover list is already sorted: attach it in one step.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def merge_two_lists(a, b):\n    dummy = tail = ListNode()\n    while a and b:\n        if a.val <= b.val:\n            tail.next, a = a, a.next\n        else:\n            tail.next, b = b, b.next\n        tail = tail.next\n    tail.next = a or b              # attach whatever is left\n    return dummy.next","java":"public ListNode mergeTwoLists(ListNode a, ListNode b) {\n    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(), tail = dummy;\n    while (a != null && b != null) {\n        if (a.val <= b.val) { tail.next = a; a = a.next; }\n        else { tail.next = b; b = b.next; }\n        tail = tail.next;\n    }\n    tail.next = (a != null) ? a : b;   // attach whatever is left\n    return dummy.next;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* mergeTwoLists(ListNode* a, ListNode* b) {\n    ListNode dummy, *tail = &dummy;\n    while (a && b) {\n        if (a->val <= b->val) { tail->next = a; a = a->next; }\n        else { tail->next = b; b = b->next; }\n        tail = tail->next;\n    }\n    tail->next = a ? a : b;            // attach whatever is left\n    return dummy.next;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-21"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-two-sorted-lists/"]],"ask":"Merge two sorted lists."},{"i":"p-linked-list-141","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Linked List Cycle","sub":"LeetCode 141 · Easy · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":141,"q":["Linked List Cycle","how to solve Linked List Cycle","Linked List Cycle approach","Linked List Cycle solution","leetcode 141","lc 141","Does the list contain a cycle?","Does the list loop back on itself?"],"w":"Linked List Cycle with O(1) space → Floyd. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Cycle with O(1) space → Floyd.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Floyd: a fast pointer (2 steps) meets a slow pointer (1 step) only if there&#x27;s a cycle.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Does the list loop back on itself?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Store visited nodes in a set: O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>slow = fast = head.<\/li><li>Move slow by 1, fast by 2.<\/li><li>If they meet → True; fast reaches the end → False.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> slow 1, fast 2.<\/li><li><b>Meet:<\/b> Cycle.<\/li><li><b>End:<\/b> No cycle.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Check fast and fast.next before stepping.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def has_cycle(head):\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow, fast = slow.next, fast.next.next\n        if slow is fast:\n            return True\n    return False","java":"public boolean hasCycle(ListNode head) {\n    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;\n    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {\n        slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next;\n        if (slow == fast) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}","cpp":"bool hasCycle(ListNode* head) {\n    ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head;\n    while (fast && fast->next) {\n        slow = slow->next; fast = fast->next->next;\n        if (slow == fast) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-141"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle/"]],"ask":"Does the list contain a cycle?"},{"i":"p-linked-list-142","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Linked List Cycle II","sub":"LeetCode 142 · Medium · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":142,"q":["Linked List Cycle II","how to solve Linked List Cycle II","Linked List Cycle II approach","Linked List Cycle II solution","leetcode 142","lc 142","Return the node where the cycle begins.","Where does the cycle start?"],"w":"Linked List Cycle entrance → restart from head. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Cycle entrance → restart from head.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> After slow and fast meet, a pointer from head and one from the meeting point, moving 1 step each, meet at the cycle start.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Where does the cycle start?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Set of visited nodes: O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Find the meeting point with Floyd.<\/li><li>p = head, q = meeting point.<\/li><li>Advance both by 1 until equal; that&#x27;s the start.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Phase 1:<\/b> Meet inside the cycle.<\/li><li><b>Phase 2:<\/b> Reset one to head.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> Both by 1 until they meet.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Compare nodes with “is”, not values.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def detect_cycle(head):\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow, fast = slow.next, fast.next.next\n        if slow is fast:                 # they met inside the cycle\n            slow = head                  # phase 2: restart one from head\n            while slow is not fast:\n                slow, fast = slow.next, fast.next\n            return slow                  # the cycle's entrance\n    return None","java":"public ListNode detectCycle(ListNode head) {\n    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;\n    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {\n        slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next;\n        if (slow == fast) {                  // they met inside the cycle\n            slow = head;                     // phase 2: restart one from head\n            while (slow != fast) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next; }\n            return slow;                     // the cycle's entrance\n        }\n    }\n    return null;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* detectCycle(ListNode* head) {\n    ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head;\n    while (fast && fast->next) {\n        slow = slow->next; fast = fast->next->next;\n        if (slow == fast) {                  // they met inside the cycle\n            slow = head;                     // phase 2: restart one from head\n            while (slow != fast) { slow = slow->next; fast = fast->next; }\n            return slow;                     // the cycle's entrance\n        }\n    }\n    return nullptr;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-142"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle-ii/"]],"ask":"Return the node where the cycle begins."},{"i":"p-linked-list-876","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Middle of the Linked List","sub":"LeetCode 876 · Easy · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":876,"q":["Middle of the Linked List","how to solve Middle of the Linked List","Middle of the Linked List approach","Middle of the Linked List solution","leetcode 876","lc 876","Return the middle node (second middle if even).","Return the middle node (the second one if there are two)."],"w":"Linked List Middle → fast and slow. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Middle → fast and slow.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Fast moves 2, slow moves 1; when fast ends, slow is in the middle.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Return the middle node (the second one if there are two).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Count length, walk half: two passes.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>slow = fast = head.<\/li><li>While fast and fast.next: slow 1 step, fast 2 steps.<\/li><li>Return slow.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> slow 1, fast 2.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> fast or fast.next is None.<\/li><li><b>Return:<\/b> slow.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Start fast at head.next for the first middle.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def middle_node(head):\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n    return slow","java":"public ListNode middleNode(ListNode head) {\n    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;\n    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {\n        slow = slow.next;\n        fast = fast.next.next;\n    }\n    return slow;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* middleNode(ListNode* head) {\n    ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head;\n    while (fast && fast->next) {\n        slow = slow->next;\n        fast = fast->next->next;\n    }\n    return slow;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-876"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/middle-of-the-linked-list/"]],"ask":"Return the middle node (second middle if even)."},{"i":"p-linked-list-19","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Remove Nth Node From End","sub":"LeetCode 19 · Medium · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":19,"q":["Remove Nth Node From End","how to solve Remove Nth Node From End","Remove Nth Node From End approach","Remove Nth Node From End solution","leetcode 19","lc 19","Remove the nth node from the end in one pass.","Delete the nth node from the end in one pass."],"w":"Linked List Nth from end → fixed gap. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Nth from end → fixed gap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Move a lead pointer n steps ahead; when it hits the end, the trailing pointer is just before the target.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Delete the nth node from the end in one pass.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Count the length, then walk again: two passes.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Dummy before head; fast and slow start at dummy.<\/li><li>Move fast n + 1 steps.<\/li><li>Move both until fast is None; slow.next = slow.next.next.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Dummy:<\/b> Handles removing the head.<\/li><li><b>Gap:<\/b> fast n + 1 ahead.<\/li><li><b>Skip:<\/b> slow.next = slow.next.next.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Without a dummy, removing the head crashes.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def remove_nth_from_end(head, n):\n    dummy = ListNode(0, head)\n    fast = slow = dummy\n    for _ in range(n + 1):\n        fast = fast.next             # open a gap of n + 1\n    while fast:\n        fast, slow = fast.next, slow.next\n    slow.next = slow.next.next       # skip the target\n    return dummy.next","java":"public ListNode removeNthFromEnd(ListNode head, int n) {\n    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0, head), fast = dummy, slow = dummy;\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) fast = fast.next;   // open a gap of n + 1\n    while (fast != null) { fast = fast.next; slow = slow.next; }\n    slow.next = slow.next.next;                      // skip the target\n    return dummy.next;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* removeNthFromEnd(ListNode* head, int n) {\n    ListNode dummy(0, head);\n    ListNode *fast = &dummy, *slow = &dummy;\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) fast = fast->next;  // open a gap of n + 1\n    while (fast) { fast = fast->next; slow = slow->next; }\n    slow->next = slow->next->next;                   // skip the target\n    return dummy.next;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-19"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list/"]],"ask":"Remove the nth node from the end in one pass."},{"i":"p-linked-list-143","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Reorder List","sub":"LeetCode 143 · Medium · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":143,"q":["Reorder List","how to solve Reorder List","Reorder List approach","Reorder List solution","leetcode 143","lc 143","L0 → Ln → L1 → Ln−1 …, in place.","Reorder to first, last, second, second-last, … in place."],"w":"Linked List Pull from the end → middle + reverse. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Pull from the end → middle + reverse.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Three classic moves: find the middle, reverse the second half, weave the two halves.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Reorder to first, last, second, second-last, … in place.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Copy nodes to an array and relink: O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Find the middle (slow/fast) and cut the list.<\/li><li>Reverse the second half.<\/li><li>Alternate nodes from each half.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>1:<\/b> Find the middle.<\/li><li><b>2:<\/b> Reverse the second half.<\/li><li><b>3:<\/b> Weave.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Cut the first half (slow.next = None) or you create a cycle.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def reorder_list(head):\n    slow = fast = head                    # 1. find the middle\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow, fast = slow.next, fast.next.next\n    prev, curr = None, slow.next          # 2. reverse the second half\n    slow.next = None\n    while curr:\n        curr.next, prev, curr = prev, curr, curr.next\n    first, second = head, prev            # 3. weave the halves\n    while second:\n        tmp1, tmp2 = first.next, second.next\n        first.next, second.next = second, tmp1\n        first, second = tmp1, tmp2","java":"public void reorderList(ListNode head) {\n    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;               // 1. find the middle\n    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next; }\n    ListNode prev = null, curr = slow.next;          // 2. reverse the second half\n    slow.next = null;\n    while (curr != null) { ListNode nxt = curr.next; curr.next = prev; prev = curr; curr = nxt; }\n    ListNode first = head, second = prev;            // 3. weave the halves\n    while (second != null) {\n        ListNode t1 = first.next, t2 = second.next;\n        first.next = second; second.next = t1;\n        first = t1; second = t2;\n    }\n}","cpp":"void reorderList(ListNode* head) {\n    ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head;             // 1. find the middle\n    while (fast && fast->next) { slow = slow->next; fast = fast->next->next; }\n    ListNode *prev = nullptr, *curr = slow->next;    // 2. reverse the second half\n    slow->next = nullptr;\n    while (curr) { ListNode* nxt = curr->next; curr->next = prev; prev = curr; curr = nxt; }\n    ListNode *first = head, *second = prev;          // 3. weave the halves\n    while (second) {\n        ListNode *t1 = first->next, *t2 = second->next;\n        first->next = second; second->next = t1;\n        first = t1; second = t2;\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-143"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/reorder-list/"]],"ask":"L0 → Ln → L1 → Ln−1 …, in place."},{"i":"p-linked-list-234","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Palindrome Linked List","sub":"LeetCode 234 · Easy · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":234,"q":["Palindrome Linked List","how to solve Palindrome Linked List","Palindrome Linked List approach","Palindrome Linked List solution","leetcode 234","lc 234","Is the list a palindrome, in O(1) space?","Is the list a palindrome, using O(1) extra space?"],"w":"Linked List Compare with the end → reverse the back half. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Compare with the end → reverse the back half.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Reverse the second half, then compare it with the first half.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Is the list a palindrome, using O(1) extra space?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Copy into an array and compare: O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Find the middle.<\/li><li>Reverse from the middle on.<\/li><li>Walk both halves comparing values.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>1:<\/b> Middle.<\/li><li><b>2:<\/b> Reverse from the middle.<\/li><li><b>3:<\/b> Compare the halves.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Mention that it modifies the input; restore it if asked.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def is_palindrome(head):\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow, fast = slow.next, fast.next.next\n    prev = None                           # reverse the second half\n    while slow:\n        slow.next, prev, slow = prev, slow, slow.next\n    left, right = head, prev\n    while right:\n        if left.val != right.val:\n            return False\n        left, right = left.next, right.next\n    return True","java":"public boolean isPalindrome(ListNode head) {\n    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;\n    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next; }\n    ListNode prev = null;                            // reverse the second half\n    while (slow != null) { ListNode nxt = slow.next; slow.next = prev; prev = slow; slow = nxt; }\n    ListNode left = head, right = prev;\n    while (right != null) {\n        if (left.val != right.val) return false;\n        left = left.next; right = right.next;\n    }\n    return true;\n}","cpp":"bool isPalindrome(ListNode* head) {\n    ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head;\n    while (fast && fast->next) { slow = slow->next; fast = fast->next->next; }\n    ListNode* prev = nullptr;                        // reverse the second half\n    while (slow) { ListNode* nxt = slow->next; slow->next = prev; prev = slow; slow = nxt; }\n    ListNode *left = head, *right = prev;\n    while (right) {\n        if (left->val != right->val) return false;\n        left = left->next; right = right->next;\n    }\n    return true;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-234"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/palindrome-linked-list/"]],"ask":"Is the list a palindrome, in O(1) space?"},{"i":"p-linked-list-2","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Add Two Numbers","sub":"LeetCode 2 · Medium · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":2,"q":["Add Two Numbers","how to solve Add Two Numbers","Add Two Numbers approach","Add Two Numbers solution","leetcode 2","lc 2","Add two numbers stored as reversed digit lists.","Digits are stored in reverse. Add the two numbers and return a list."],"w":"Linked List Digit lists → carry loop with dummy. Complexity O(max(m, n))","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Digit lists → carry loop with dummy.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Grade-school addition, digit by digit with a carry.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Digits are stored in reverse. Add the two numbers and return a list.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Convert to integers: overflows in most languages.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>carry = 0; while either list or carry remains:<\/li><li>s = a + b + carry; append s % 10; carry = s // 10.<\/li><li>Advance the lists.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Loop:<\/b> While l1 or l2 or carry.<\/li><li><b>Digit:<\/b> divmod(s, 10).<\/li><li><b>Build:<\/b> Dummy + tail.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(max(m, n))<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Forgetting “or carry” drops the last digit of 5 + 5.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def add_two_numbers(l1, l2):\n    dummy = tail = ListNode()\n    carry = 0\n    while l1 or l2 or carry:\n        s = carry + (l1.val if l1 else 0) + (l2.val if l2 else 0)\n        carry, digit = divmod(s, 10)\n        tail.next = ListNode(digit)\n        tail = tail.next\n        l1 = l1.next if l1 else None\n        l2 = l2.next if l2 else None\n    return dummy.next","java":"public ListNode addTwoNumbers(ListNode l1, ListNode l2) {\n    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(), tail = dummy;\n    int carry = 0;\n    while (l1 != null || l2 != null || carry != 0) {\n        int s = carry + (l1 != null ? l1.val : 0) + (l2 != null ? l2.val : 0);\n        carry = s / 10;\n        tail.next = new ListNode(s % 10);\n        tail = tail.next;\n        l1 = (l1 != null) ? l1.next : null;\n        l2 = (l2 != null) ? l2.next : null;\n    }\n    return dummy.next;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* addTwoNumbers(ListNode* l1, ListNode* l2) {\n    ListNode dummy, *tail = &dummy;\n    int carry = 0;\n    while (l1 || l2 || carry) {\n        int s = carry + (l1 ? l1->val : 0) + (l2 ? l2->val : 0);\n        carry = s / 10;\n        tail->next = new ListNode(s % 10);\n        tail = tail->next;\n        l1 = l1 ? l1->next : nullptr;\n        l2 = l2 ? l2->next : nullptr;\n    }\n    return dummy.next;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-2"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/add-two-numbers/"]],"ask":"Add two numbers stored as reversed digit lists."},{"i":"p-linked-list-138","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Copy List with Random Pointer","sub":"LeetCode 138 · Medium · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":138,"q":["Copy List with Random Pointer","how to solve Copy List with Random Pointer","Copy List with Random Pointer approach","Copy List with Random Pointer solution","leetcode 138","lc 138","Deep copy a list whose nodes also have a random pointer.","Deep copy a list where nodes also have a random pointer."],"w":"Linked List Deep copy with cross links → dict, two passes. Complexity O(n), O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Deep copy with cross links → dict, two passes.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Map old node → new node; then wire next and random through the map.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Deep copy a list where nodes also have a random pointer.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Copy next pointers, then search for each random target: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Pass 1: create a copy for every node, store in a dict.<\/li><li>Pass 2: copy.next = map[old.next], copy.random = map[old.random].<\/li><li>Return map[head].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Map:<\/b> old → new.<\/li><li><b>Pass 1:<\/b> Create nodes.<\/li><li><b>Pass 2:<\/b> Wire next and random.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Seed the dict with None → None.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def copy_random_list(head):\n    clone = {None: None}                  # old node -> new node\n    node = head\n    while node:                           # pass 1: create every copy\n        clone[node] = Node(node.val)\n        node = node.next\n    node = head\n    while node:                           # pass 2: wire next and random\n        clone[node].next = clone[node.next]\n        clone[node].random = clone[node.random]\n        node = node.next\n    return clone[head]","java":"public Node copyRandomList(Node head) {\n    Map<Node, Node> clone = new HashMap<>();    // old node -> new node\n    for (Node node = head; node != null; node = node.next)   // pass 1: create every copy\n        clone.put(node, new Node(node.val));\n    for (Node node = head; node != null; node = node.next) { // pass 2: wire next and random\n        clone.get(node).next = clone.get(node.next);          // get(null) returns null\n        clone.get(node).random = clone.get(node.random);\n    }\n    return clone.get(head);\n}","cpp":"Node* copyRandomList(Node* head) {\n    unordered_map<Node*, Node*> clone{{nullptr, nullptr}};    // old node -> new node\n    for (Node* node = head; node; node = node->next)          // pass 1: create every copy\n        clone[node] = new Node(node->val);\n    for (Node* node = head; node; node = node->next) {        // pass 2: wire next and random\n        clone[node]->next = clone[node->next];\n        clone[node]->random = clone[node->random];\n    }\n    return clone[head];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-138"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/copy-list-with-random-pointer/"]],"ask":"Deep copy a list whose nodes also have a random pointer."},{"i":"p-linked-list-160","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Intersection of Two Linked Lists","sub":"LeetCode 160 · Easy · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":160,"q":["Intersection of Two Linked Lists","how to solve Intersection of Two Linked Lists","Intersection of Two Linked Lists approach","Intersection of Two Linked Lists solution","leetcode 160","lc 160","Node where two lists intersect, or None.","At which node do two lists merge, if any?"],"w":"Linked List Different lengths → switch tracks. Complexity O(m + n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Different lengths → switch tracks.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Two pointers; when one reaches the end, restart it at the other list&#x27;s head. They meet after equal distances.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> At which node do two lists merge, if any?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Store one list&#x27;s nodes in a set: O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>a = headA, b = headB.<\/li><li>Step both; at None jump to the other head.<\/li><li>They meet at the intersection (or both None).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Walk:<\/b> p through A then B.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> q through B then A.<\/li><li><b>Meet:<\/b> Same node or both None.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(m + n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Compare node identity, not value.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def get_intersection_node(a, b):\n    p, q = a, b\n    while p is not q:\n        p = p.next if p else b            # switch tracks at the end\n        q = q.next if q else a\n    return p                              # meeting node, or None","java":"public ListNode getIntersectionNode(ListNode a, ListNode b) {\n    ListNode p = a, q = b;\n    while (p != q) {\n        p = (p != null) ? p.next : b;     // switch tracks at the end\n        q = (q != null) ? q.next : a;\n    }\n    return p;                             // meeting node, or null\n}","cpp":"ListNode* getIntersectionNode(ListNode* a, ListNode* b) {\n    ListNode *p = a, *q = b;\n    while (p != q) {\n        p = p ? p->next : b;              // switch tracks at the end\n        q = q ? q->next : a;\n    }\n    return p;                             // meeting node, or nullptr\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-160"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/intersection-of-two-linked-lists/"]],"ask":"Node where two lists intersect, or None."},{"i":"p-linked-list-61","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Rotate List","sub":"LeetCode 61 · Medium · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":61,"q":["Rotate List","how to solve Rotate List","Rotate List approach","Rotate List solution","leetcode 61","lc 61","Rotate the list right by k.","Rotate the list to the right by k places."],"w":"Linked List Rotation → ring, then cut. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Rotation → ring, then cut.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Make it a ring, then break it at the right spot (k mod length).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Rotate the list to the right by k places.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Move the last node to the front k times: O(n·k).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Find length and tail; tail.next = head.<\/li><li>Walk length − k % length − 1 steps to the new tail.<\/li><li>New head = newTail.next; newTail.next = None.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Length:<\/b> And the tail.<\/li><li><b>Ring:<\/b> tail.next = head.<\/li><li><b>Cut:<\/b> After n − k % n nodes.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> k can exceed n: take k % n.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def rotate_right(head, k):\n    if not head:\n        return None\n    n, tail = 1, head\n    while tail.next:\n        tail, n = tail.next, n + 1\n    k %= n\n    if k == 0:\n        return head\n    tail.next = head                      # close the ring\n    new_tail = head\n    for _ in range(n - k - 1):\n        new_tail = new_tail.next\n    new_head = new_tail.next\n    new_tail.next = None                  # cut it open\n    return new_head","java":"public ListNode rotateRight(ListNode head, int k) {\n    if (head == null) return null;\n    int n = 1;\n    ListNode tail = head;\n    while (tail.next != null) { tail = tail.next; n++; }\n    k %= n;\n    if (k == 0) return head;\n    tail.next = head;                         // close the ring\n    ListNode newTail = head;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n - k - 1; i++) newTail = newTail.next;\n    ListNode newHead = newTail.next;\n    newTail.next = null;                      // cut it open\n    return newHead;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* rotateRight(ListNode* head, int k) {\n    if (!head) return nullptr;\n    int n = 1;\n    ListNode* tail = head;\n    while (tail->next) { tail = tail->next; n++; }\n    k %= n;\n    if (k == 0) return head;\n    tail->next = head;                        // close the ring\n    ListNode* newTail = head;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n - k - 1; i++) newTail = newTail->next;\n    ListNode* newHead = newTail->next;\n    newTail->next = nullptr;                  // cut it open\n    return newHead;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-61"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/rotate-list/"]],"ask":"Rotate the list right by k."},{"i":"p-linked-list-328","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Odd Even Linked List","sub":"LeetCode 328 · Medium · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":328,"q":["Odd Even Linked List","how to solve Odd Even Linked List","Odd Even Linked List approach","Odd Even Linked List solution","leetcode 328","lc 328","Group odd-position nodes before even-position nodes.","Group nodes at odd positions first, then even positions, keeping relative order."],"w":"Linked List Split into two chains → two pointers + saved head. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Split into two chains → two pointers + saved head.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Relink in place with two chains, odd and even, then join them.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Group nodes at odd positions first, then even positions, keeping relative order.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Build two new lists by copying: extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>odd = head, even = evenHead = head.next.<\/li><li>odd.next = even.next, odd = odd.next; even.next = odd.next, even = even.next.<\/li><li>odd.next = evenHead.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pointers:<\/b> odd, even, even_head.<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> Leapfrog them.<\/li><li><b>Join:<\/b> odd.next = even_head.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Positions, not values.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def odd_even_list(head):\n    if not head:\n        return None\n    odd, even = head, head.next\n    even_head = even\n    while even and even.next:\n        odd.next = even.next\n        odd = odd.next\n        even.next = odd.next\n        even = even.next\n    odd.next = even_head                  # odds first, then evens\n    return head","java":"public ListNode oddEvenList(ListNode head) {\n    if (head == null) return null;\n    ListNode odd = head, even = head.next, evenHead = even;\n    while (even != null && even.next != null) {\n        odd.next = even.next;\n        odd = odd.next;\n        even.next = odd.next;\n        even = even.next;\n    }\n    odd.next = evenHead;                      // odds first, then evens\n    return head;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* oddEvenList(ListNode* head) {\n    if (!head) return nullptr;\n    ListNode *odd = head, *even = head->next, *evenHead = even;\n    while (even && even->next) {\n        odd->next = even->next;\n        odd = odd->next;\n        even->next = odd->next;\n        even = even->next;\n    }\n    odd->next = evenHead;                     // odds first, then evens\n    return head;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-328"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/odd-even-linked-list/"]],"ask":"Group odd-position nodes before even-position nodes."},{"i":"p-linked-list-148","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Sort List","sub":"LeetCode 148 · Medium · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":148,"q":["Sort List","how to solve Sort List","Sort List approach","Sort List solution","leetcode 148","lc 148","Sort a linked list in O(n log n).","Sort a linked list in O(n log n)."],"w":"Linked List Sort a list → merge sort. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Sort a list → merge sort.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Merge sort fits lists perfectly: split at the middle, sort both halves, merge.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sort a linked list in O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Insertion sort: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Base: 0 or 1 node.<\/li><li>Find the middle and cut.<\/li><li>Sort both halves and merge them (problem 21).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Split:<\/b> Fast and slow (first middle).<\/li><li><b>Recurse:<\/b> Both halves.<\/li><li><b>Merge:<\/b> Dummy + tail.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Start fast at head.next, or a two-node list never splits.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def sort_list(head):\n    if not head or not head.next:\n        return head\n    slow, fast = head, head.next          # slow stops at the first middle\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow, fast = slow.next, fast.next.next\n    mid, slow.next = slow.next, None\n    left, right = sort_list(head), sort_list(mid)\n    dummy = tail = ListNode()\n    while left and right:                 # merge\n        if left.val <= right.val:\n            tail.next, left = left, left.next\n        else:\n            tail.next, right = right, right.next\n        tail = tail.next\n    tail.next = left or right\n    return dummy.next","java":"public ListNode sortList(ListNode head) {\n    if (head == null || head.next == null) return head;\n    ListNode slow = head, fast = head.next;   // slow stops at the first middle\n    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next; }\n    ListNode mid = slow.next;\n    slow.next = null;\n    ListNode left = sortList(head), right = sortList(mid);\n    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(), tail = dummy;\n    while (left != null && right != null) {   // merge\n        if (left.val <= right.val) { tail.next = left; left = left.next; }\n        else { tail.next = right; right = right.next; }\n        tail = tail.next;\n    }\n    tail.next = (left != null) ? left : right;\n    return dummy.next;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* sortList(ListNode* head) {\n    if (!head || !head->next) return head;\n    ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head->next;   // slow stops at the first middle\n    while (fast && fast->next) { slow = slow->next; fast = fast->next->next; }\n    ListNode* mid = slow->next;\n    slow->next = nullptr;\n    ListNode *left = sortList(head), *right = sortList(mid);\n    ListNode dummy, *tail = &dummy;\n    while (left && right) {                      // merge\n        if (left->val <= right->val) { tail->next = left; left = left->next; }\n        else { tail->next = right; right = right->next; }\n        tail = tail->next;\n    }\n    tail->next = left ? left : right;\n    return dummy.next;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-148"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-list/"]],"ask":"Sort a linked list in O(n log n)."},{"i":"p-linked-list-23","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Merge k Sorted Lists","sub":"LeetCode 23 · Hard · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":23,"q":["Merge k Sorted Lists","how to solve Merge k Sorted Lists","Merge k Sorted Lists approach","Merge k Sorted Lists solution","leetcode 23","lc 23","Merge k sorted lists.","Merge k sorted lists into one."],"w":"Linked List k sorted streams → heap. Complexity O(N log k)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> k sorted streams → heap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A min-heap of the k current heads always gives the next smallest node.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Merge k sorted lists into one.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Merge one at a time: O(k·N).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Push each list&#x27;s head (value, index, node).<\/li><li>Pop the smallest, attach it, push its next.<\/li><li>Repeat until the heap is empty.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Heap:<\/b> (val, i, node).<\/li><li><b>Pop:<\/b> Smallest, attach.<\/li><li><b>Push:<\/b> Its next.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(N log k)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The index i breaks ties; nodes themselves can’t be compared.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef merge_k_lists(lists):\n    heap = [(node.val, i, node) for i, node in enumerate(lists) if node]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)                   # i breaks ties between equal values\n    dummy = tail = ListNode()\n    while heap:\n        _, i, node = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        tail.next = tail = node\n        if node.next:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (node.next.val, i, node.next))\n    return dummy.next","java":"public ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {\n    PriorityQueue<ListNode> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((x, y) -> Integer.compare(x.val, y.val));\n    for (ListNode node : lists) if (node != null) heap.offer(node);\n    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(), tail = dummy;\n    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n        ListNode node = heap.poll();          // smallest head of all lists\n        tail.next = node;\n        tail = node;\n        if (node.next != null) heap.offer(node.next);\n    }\n    return dummy.next;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* mergeKLists(vector<ListNode*>& lists) {\n    auto cmp = [](ListNode* x, ListNode* y) { return x->val > y->val; };   // min-heap\n    priority_queue<ListNode*, vector<ListNode*>, decltype(cmp)> heap(cmp);\n    for (auto node : lists) if (node) heap.push(node);\n    ListNode dummy, *tail = &dummy;\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        ListNode* node = heap.top(); heap.pop();   // smallest head of all lists\n        tail->next = node;\n        tail = node;\n        if (node->next) heap.push(node->next);\n    }\n    return dummy.next;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-23"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-k-sorted-lists/"]],"ask":"Merge k sorted lists."},{"i":"p-linked-list-25","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"Reverse Nodes in k-Group","sub":"LeetCode 25 · Hard · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":25,"q":["Reverse Nodes in k-Group","how to solve Reverse Nodes in k-Group","Reverse Nodes in k-Group approach","Reverse Nodes in k-Group solution","leetcode 25","lc 25","Reverse every k consecutive nodes.","Reverse every block of k nodes; leave a short tail as is."],"w":"Linked List Group reversal → dummy + per-group reverse. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Group reversal → dummy + per-group reverse.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> For each block: check k nodes exist, reverse them, reconnect before and after.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Reverse every block of k nodes; leave a short tail as is.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Copy to an array: extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>prevGroup = dummy.<\/li><li>Find the kth node; if missing, stop.<\/li><li>Reverse the block, link prevGroup to the new first, move prevGroup to the block&#x27;s end.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Check:<\/b> k nodes remain?<\/li><li><b>Reverse:<\/b> Inside the group.<\/li><li><b>Reconnect:<\/b> group_prev → kth.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Leave a final short group as it is.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def reverse_k_group(head, k):\n    dummy = ListNode(0, head)\n    group_prev = dummy\n    while True:\n        kth = group_prev                  # find this group's k-th node\n        for _ in range(k):\n            kth = kth.next\n            if not kth:\n                return dummy.next         # fewer than k left: done\n        group_next = kth.next\n        prev, curr = group_next, group_prev.next\n        while curr is not group_next:     # reverse inside the group\n            curr.next, prev, curr = prev, curr, curr.next\n        first = group_prev.next           # old first is the new tail\n        group_prev.next = kth\n        group_prev = first","java":"public ListNode reverseKGroup(ListNode head, int k) {\n    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0, head), groupPrev = dummy;\n    while (true) {\n        ListNode kth = groupPrev;                 // find this group's k-th node\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n            kth = kth.next;\n            if (kth == null) return dummy.next;   // fewer than k left: done\n        }\n        ListNode groupNext = kth.next;\n        ListNode prev = groupNext, curr = groupPrev.next;\n        while (curr != groupNext) {               // reverse inside the group\n            ListNode nxt = curr.next; curr.next = prev; prev = curr; curr = nxt;\n        }\n        ListNode first = groupPrev.next;          // old first is the new tail\n        groupPrev.next = kth;\n        groupPrev = first;\n    }\n}","cpp":"ListNode* reverseKGroup(ListNode* head, int k) {\n    ListNode dummy(0, head), *groupPrev = &dummy;\n    while (true) {\n        ListNode* kth = groupPrev;                // find this group's k-th node\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n            kth = kth->next;\n            if (!kth) return dummy.next;          // fewer than k left: done\n        }\n        ListNode* groupNext = kth->next;\n        ListNode *prev = groupNext, *curr = groupPrev->next;\n        while (curr != groupNext) {               // reverse inside the group\n            ListNode* nxt = curr->next; curr->next = prev; prev = curr; curr = nxt;\n        }\n        ListNode* first = groupPrev->next;        // old first is the new tail\n        groupPrev->next = kth;\n        groupPrev = first;\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-25"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-nodes-in-k-group/"]],"ask":"Reverse every k consecutive nodes."},{"i":"p-linked-list-146","k":"prob","s":"linked-list","t":"LRU Cache","sub":"LeetCode 146 · Medium · Linked List","cat":"DSA problems","n":146,"q":["LRU Cache","how to solve LRU Cache","LRU Cache approach","LRU Cache solution","leetcode 146","lc 146","O(1) get/put with least-recently-used eviction.","A cache with get/put in O(1) that evicts the least recently used key."],"w":"Linked List LRU → dict + doubly linked list. Complexity O(1) per op","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> LRU → dict + doubly linked list.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> HashMap for lookups + doubly linked list for recency order.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A cache with get/put in O(1) that evicts the least recently used key.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> List of keys reordered on every access: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>get: move the node to the front, return its value.<\/li><li>put: update/move to front, or insert at front.<\/li><li>Over capacity: remove the node at the back and its key.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Lookup:<\/b> Dict key → node.<\/li><li><b>Order:<\/b> Doubly linked list.<\/li><li><b>Evict:<\/b> head.next.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(1) per op<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Store the key in each node so you can delete it from the dict on eviction.<\/p>","c":{"py":"class Node:\n    def __init__(self, key=0, val=0):\n        self.key, self.val = key, val\n        self.prev = self.next = None\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.cap = capacity\n        self.map = {}                          # key -> node\n        self.head, self.tail = Node(), Node()  # sentinels: LRU end, MRU end\n        self.head.next, self.tail.prev = self.tail, self.head\n\n    def _remove(self, node):\n        node.prev.next, node.next.prev = node.next, node.prev\n\n    def _add(self, node):                      # insert just before tail\n        node.prev, node.next = self.tail.prev, self.tail\n        self.tail.prev.next = node\n        self.tail.prev = node\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self.map:\n            return -1\n        node = self.map[key]\n        self._remove(node)\n        self._add(node)                        # touched: most recent now\n        return node.val\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if key in self.map:\n            self._remove(self.map[key])\n        node = Node(key, value)\n        self.map[key] = node\n        self._add(node)\n        if len(self.map) > self.cap:\n            lru = self.head.next               # least recent is after head\n            self._remove(lru)\n            del self.map[lru.key]","java":"class LRUCache {\n    private static class Node {\n        int key, val;\n        Node prev, next;\n        Node(int k, int v) { key = k; val = v; }\n    }\n    private final int cap;\n    private final Map<Integer, Node> map = new HashMap<>();   // key -> node\n    private final Node head = new Node(0, 0), tail = new Node(0, 0);   // LRU end, MRU end\n\n    public LRUCache(int capacity) {\n        cap = capacity;\n        head.next = tail; tail.prev = head;\n    }\n\n    private void remove(Node node) {\n        node.prev.next = node.next;\n        node.next.prev = node.prev;\n    }\n\n    private void add(Node node) {                 // insert just before tail\n        node.prev = tail.prev; node.next = tail;\n        tail.prev.next = node;\n        tail.prev = node;\n    }\n\n    public int get(int key) {\n        Node node = map.get(key);\n        if (node == null) return -1;\n        remove(node);\n        add(node);                                // touched: most recent now\n        return node.val;\n    }\n\n    public void put(int key, int value) {\n        if (map.containsKey(key)) remove(map.get(key));\n        Node node = new Node(key, value);\n        map.put(key, node);\n        add(node);\n        if (map.size() > cap) {\n            Node lru = head.next;                 // least recent is after head\n            remove(lru);\n            map.remove(lru.key);\n        }\n    }\n}","cpp":"class LRUCache {\n    int cap;\n    list<pair<int, int>> items;                               // front = LRU, back = MRU\n    unordered_map<int, list<pair<int, int>>::iterator> pos;   // key -> node in the list\npublic:\n    LRUCache(int capacity) : cap(capacity) {}\n\n    int get(int key) {\n        auto it = pos.find(key);\n        if (it == pos.end()) return -1;\n        items.splice(items.end(), items, it->second);   // touched: move to MRU end, O(1)\n        return it->second->second;\n    }\n\n    void put(int key, int value) {\n        auto it = pos.find(key);\n        if (it != pos.end()) items.erase(it->second);\n        items.push_back({key, value});\n        pos[key] = prev(items.end());\n        if ((int)pos.size() > cap) {\n            pos.erase(items.front().first);             // least recent is at the front\n            items.pop_front();\n        }\n    }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","linked-list.html#p-146"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/lru-cache/"]],"ask":"O(1) get/put with least-recently-used eviction."},{"i":"p-recursion-50","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Pow(x, n)","sub":"LeetCode 50 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":50,"q":["Pow(x, n)","how to solve Pow(x, n)","Pow(x, n) approach","Pow(x, n) solution","leetcode 50","lc 50","Compute xⁿ for an integer n that may be negative, faster than multiplying n times.","Compute xⁿ faster than n multiplications (n may be negative)."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Halve the input and call once → O(log n). Complexity O(log n) time and stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Halve the input and call once → O(log n).<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> x^n = (x^(n/2))² — compute the half once and square it.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Compute xⁿ faster than n multiplications (n may be negative).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Multiply n times: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>n &lt; 0 → use 1/x and −n.<\/li><li>half = pow(x, n // 2).<\/li><li>Return half × half (× x if n is odd).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> n == 0 → 1. Negative n → 1 / pow(x, −n).<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> pow(x, n // 2), just once.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Square it; multiply by one extra x if n is odd.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n) time and stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Writing my_pow(x, n // 2) * my_pow(x, n // 2) makes two calls per level and drags you back to O(n). Store it in a variable.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def my_pow(x, n):\n    if n < 0:\n        return 1 / my_pow(x, -n)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1.0\n    half = my_pow(x, n // 2)              # one call, reused\n    return half * half * (x if n % 2 else 1)","java":"public double myPow(double x, int n) {\n    long N = n;                              // -2^31 can't be negated as an int\n    if (N < 0) return 1 / pow(x, -N);\n    return pow(x, N);\n}\n\ndouble pow(double x, long n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 1.0;\n    double half = pow(x, n / 2);             // one call, reused\n    return half * half * (n % 2 == 1 ? x : 1);\n}","cpp":"double myPow(double x, int n) {\n    long long N = n;                         // -2^31 can't be negated as an int\n    if (N < 0) return 1 / power(x, -N);\n    return power(x, N);\n}\n\ndouble power(double x, long long n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 1.0;\n    double half = power(x, n / 2);           // one call, reused\n    return half * half * (n % 2 == 1 ? x : 1);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-50"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/powx-n/"]],"ask":"Compute xⁿ for an integer n that may be negative, faster than multiplying n times."},{"i":"p-recursion-206","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Reverse Linked List (recursive)","sub":"LeetCode 206 · Easy · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":206,"q":["Reverse Linked List (recursive)","how to solve Reverse Linked List (recursive)","Reverse Linked List (recursive) approach","Reverse Linked List (recursive) solution","leetcode 206","lc 206","Reverse a singly linked list and return the new head.","Reverse a list recursively."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Recursive call → trust it returned the reversed rest. Complexity O(n) time, O(n) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Recursive call → trust it returned the reversed rest.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Reverse the rest, then hang the current node at its end.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Reverse a list recursively.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Base: empty or one node → return it.<\/li><li>newHead = reverse(head.next).<\/li><li>head.next.next = head; head.next = None; return newHead.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty list or a single node is already reversed.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Reverse everything after head. Trust it to return the new head.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> head.next is now the tail of the reversed part, so hang head after it and cut head.next.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(n) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Forget head.next = None and you create a two-node cycle at the end.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def reverse_list(head):\n    if not head or not head.next:\n        return head\n    new_head = reverse_list(head.next)    # rest reversed; head.next is its tail\n    head.next.next = head                 # hang head after that tail\n    head.next = None                      # head is the new end\n    return new_head","java":"public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {\n    if (head == null || head.next == null) return head;\n    ListNode newHead = reverseList(head.next);   // rest reversed; head.next is its tail\n    head.next.next = head;                       // hang head after that tail\n    head.next = null;                            // head is the new end\n    return newHead;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* reverseList(ListNode* head) {\n    if (!head || !head->next) return head;\n    ListNode* newHead = reverseList(head->next); // rest reversed; head->next is its tail\n    head->next->next = head;                     // hang head after that tail\n    head->next = nullptr;                        // head is the new end\n    return newHead;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-206"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-linked-list/"]],"ask":"Reverse a singly linked list and return the new head."},{"i":"p-recursion-21","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Merge Two Sorted Lists","sub":"LeetCode 21 · Easy · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":21,"q":["Merge Two Sorted Lists","how to solve Merge Two Sorted Lists","Merge Two Sorted Lists approach","Merge Two Sorted Lists solution","leetcode 21","lc 21","Merge two sorted linked lists into one sorted list.","Merge two sorted lists recursively."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Pick one element, trust the call to merge the rest. Complexity O(m + n) time, O(m + n) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Pick one element, trust the call to merge the rest.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> The smaller head goes first; its next is the merge of the rest.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Merge two sorted lists recursively.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If one is empty, return the other.<\/li><li>If a.val &lt; b.val: a.next = merge(a.next, b), return a.<\/li><li>Else symmetric with b.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> One list is empty → the other one is the answer.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Merge everything except the smaller head.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Smaller head points at that merged result.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(m + n) time, O(m + n) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> return a or b handles both “one is empty” cases in one line.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def merge_two_lists(a, b):\n    if not a or not b:\n        return a or b\n    if a.val <= b.val:\n        a.next = merge_two_lists(a.next, b)\n        return a\n    b.next = merge_two_lists(a, b.next)\n    return b","java":"public ListNode mergeTwoLists(ListNode a, ListNode b) {\n    if (a == null || b == null) return a != null ? a : b;\n    if (a.val <= b.val) {\n        a.next = mergeTwoLists(a.next, b);\n        return a;\n    }\n    b.next = mergeTwoLists(a, b.next);\n    return b;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* mergeTwoLists(ListNode* a, ListNode* b) {\n    if (!a || !b) return a ? a : b;\n    if (a->val <= b->val) {\n        a->next = mergeTwoLists(a->next, b);\n        return a;\n    }\n    b->next = mergeTwoLists(a, b->next);\n    return b;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-21"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-two-sorted-lists/"]],"ask":"Merge two sorted linked lists into one sorted list."},{"i":"p-recursion-24","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Swap Nodes in Pairs","sub":"LeetCode 24 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":24,"q":["Swap Nodes in Pairs","how to solve Swap Nodes in Pairs","Swap Nodes in Pairs approach","Swap Nodes in Pairs solution","leetcode 24","lc 24","Swap every two adjacent nodes by relinking, not by changing values.","Swap every two neighbouring nodes by relinking."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Handle one chunk, trust the call for the rest. Complexity O(n) time, O(n) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Handle one chunk, trust the call for the rest.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Swap the first pair, then hang the recursively swapped rest after it.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Swap every two neighbouring nodes by relinking.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Fewer than 2 nodes → return head.<\/li><li>second = head.next; head.next = swap(second.next).<\/li><li>second.next = head; return second.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Fewer than two nodes → nothing to swap.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Swap everything after the first pair.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> second → first → (swapped rest). Return second.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(n) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Grab second.next before you overwrite pointers, or you lose the rest of the list.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def swap_pairs(head):\n    if not head or not head.next:\n        return head\n    first, second = head, head.next\n    first.next = swap_pairs(second.next)  # rest already swapped\n    second.next = first\n    return second","java":"public ListNode swapPairs(ListNode head) {\n    if (head == null || head.next == null) return head;\n    ListNode first = head, second = head.next;\n    first.next = swapPairs(second.next);     // rest already swapped\n    second.next = first;\n    return second;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* swapPairs(ListNode* head) {\n    if (!head || !head->next) return head;\n    ListNode *first = head, *second = head->next;\n    first->next = swapPairs(second->next);   // rest already swapped\n    second->next = first;\n    return second;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-24"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/swap-nodes-in-pairs/"]],"ask":"Swap every two adjacent nodes by relinking, not by changing values."},{"i":"p-recursion-classic","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Tower of Hanoi","sub":"Classic · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":null,"q":["Tower of Hanoi","how to solve Tower of Hanoi","Tower of Hanoi approach","Tower of Hanoi solution","Move n disks from one peg to another, one at a time, never putting a bigger disk on a smaller one.","Move n disks from A to C using B, never putting a big disk on a small one."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Recursive call → trust it. This problem is impossible any other way. Complexity 2ⁿ − 1 moves, O(n) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Recursive call → trust it. This problem is impossible any other way.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> To move n disks: move n − 1 out of the way, move the biggest, move n − 1 back on top.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Move n disks from A to C using B, never putting a big disk on a small one.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>hanoi(n−1, A → B).<\/li><li>Move disk n from A to C.<\/li><li>hanoi(n−1, B → C). Total moves: 2ⁿ − 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> 0 disks → nothing to do.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Move the top n − 1 disks to the spare peg. Don’t think about how.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Move the biggest disk, then move the n − 1 disks back on top of it.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> 2ⁿ − 1 moves, O(n) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Trying to trace this by hand for n = 4 is exactly the habit the leap of faith is meant to break.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def hanoi(n, src, dst, via, moves):\n    if n == 0:\n        return\n    hanoi(n - 1, src, via, dst, moves)    # clear the way (trust it)\n    moves.append((src, dst))              # move the biggest disk\n    hanoi(n - 1, via, dst, src, moves)    # stack the rest back on top","java":"void hanoi(int n, char src, char dst, char via, List<char[]> moves) {\n    if (n == 0) return;\n    hanoi(n - 1, src, via, dst, moves);      // clear the way (trust it)\n    moves.add(new char[]{src, dst});         // move the biggest disk\n    hanoi(n - 1, via, dst, src, moves);      // stack the rest back on top\n}","cpp":"void hanoi(int n, char src, char dst, char via, vector<pair<char, char>>& moves) {\n    if (n == 0) return;\n    hanoi(n - 1, src, via, dst, moves);      // clear the way (trust it)\n    moves.push_back({src, dst});             // move the biggest disk\n    hanoi(n - 1, via, dst, src, moves);      // stack the rest back on top\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-classic"]],"ask":"Move n disks from one peg to another, one at a time, never putting a bigger disk on a smaller one."},{"i":"p-recursion-104","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Maximum Depth of Binary Tree","sub":"LeetCode 104 · Easy · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":104,"q":["Maximum Depth of Binary Tree","how to solve Maximum Depth of Binary Tree","Maximum Depth of Binary Tree approach","Maximum Depth of Binary Tree solution","leetcode 104","lc 104","Return the number of nodes on the longest root-to-leaf path.","How many nodes are on the longest path from the root down to a leaf?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Depends on children → postorder. Complexity O(n) time, O(h) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Depends on children → postorder.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Trust the recursion: depth(node) = 1 + the deeper of its two children.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How many nodes are on the longest path from the root down to a leaf?<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Empty node → 0.<\/li><li>Ask depth(left) and depth(right).<\/li><li>Return 1 + max of the two.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty tree has depth 0.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Depth of the left subtree and of the right subtree.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> 1 + the bigger one.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(h) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Some versions count edges, not nodes. Read which one they want.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_depth(root):\n    if not root:\n        return 0\n    return 1 + max(max_depth(root.left), max_depth(root.right))","java":"public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {\n    if (root == null) return 0;\n    return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(root.left), maxDepth(root.right));\n}","cpp":"int maxDepth(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (!root) return 0;\n    return 1 + max(maxDepth(root->left), maxDepth(root->right));\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-104"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-depth-of-binary-tree/"]],"ask":"Return the number of nodes on the longest root-to-leaf path."},{"i":"p-recursion-226","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Invert Binary Tree","sub":"LeetCode 226 · Easy · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":226,"q":["Invert Binary Tree","how to solve Invert Binary Tree","Invert Binary Tree approach","Invert Binary Tree solution","leetcode 226","lc 226","Mirror the tree: every left child becomes a right child and vice versa.","Mirror the tree: swap left and right everywhere."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Changing shape → reassign what the call returns. Complexity O(n) time, O(h) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Changing shape → reassign what the call returns.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Each node does one tiny job: swap its two children, then let recursion handle the rest.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Mirror the tree: swap left and right everywhere.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Empty → return None.<\/li><li>Swap node.left and node.right.<\/li><li>Invert both children; return node.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty tree inverts to empty.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> The inverted left subtree and the inverted right subtree.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Attach them swapped.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(h) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The one-line swap is safe because Python evaluates the whole right-hand side before assigning.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def invert_tree(root):\n    if not root:\n        return None\n    root.left, root.right = invert_tree(root.right), invert_tree(root.left)\n    return root","java":"public TreeNode invertTree(TreeNode root) {\n    if (root == null) return null;\n    TreeNode left = invertTree(root.left);\n    root.left = invertTree(root.right);\n    root.right = left;\n    return root;\n}","cpp":"TreeNode* invertTree(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (!root) return nullptr;\n    TreeNode* left = invertTree(root->left);\n    root->left = invertTree(root->right);\n    root->right = left;\n    return root;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-226"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/invert-binary-tree/"]],"ask":"Mirror the tree: every left child becomes a right child and vice versa."},{"i":"p-recursion-101","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Symmetric Tree","sub":"LeetCode 101 · Easy · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":101,"q":["Symmetric Tree","how to solve Symmetric Tree","Symmetric Tree approach","Symmetric Tree solution","leetcode 101","lc 101","Is the tree a mirror image of itself around the center?","Is the left side a mirror image of the right side?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Two trees at once → recurse on a pair. Complexity O(n) time, O(h) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Two trees at once → recurse on a pair.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Compare two trees at once: outer children must match and inner children must match.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Is the left side a mirror image of the right side?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Build the mirror and compare: extra work.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>mirror(a, b): both None → True; one None or values differ → False.<\/li><li>Check mirror(a.left, b.right) and mirror(a.right, b.left).<\/li><li>Start with mirror(root.left, root.right).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Two empty nodes mirror; one empty or different values don&#x27;t.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Recurse on a pair: outer pair (a.left, b.right) and inner pair (a.right, b.left).<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Both pairs must mirror.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(h) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Compare outside with outside. Comparing left with left checks “same tree”, a different problem.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def is_symmetric(root):\n    def mirror(a, b):\n        if not a and not b:\n            return True\n        if not a or not b or a.val != b.val:\n            return False\n        return mirror(a.left, b.right) and mirror(a.right, b.left)\n    return mirror(root.left, root.right) if root else True","java":"public boolean isSymmetric(TreeNode root) {\n    return root == null || mirror(root.left, root.right);\n}\n\nboolean mirror(TreeNode a, TreeNode b) {\n    if (a == null && b == null) return true;\n    if (a == null || b == null || a.val != b.val) return false;\n    return mirror(a.left, b.right) && mirror(a.right, b.left);\n}","cpp":"bool isSymmetric(TreeNode* root) {\n    return !root || mirror(root->left, root->right);\n}\n\nbool mirror(TreeNode* a, TreeNode* b) {\n    if (!a && !b) return true;\n    if (!a || !b || a->val != b->val) return false;\n    return mirror(a->left, b->right) && mirror(a->right, b->left);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-101"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/symmetric-tree/"]],"ask":"Is the tree a mirror image of itself around the center?"},{"i":"p-recursion-543","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Diameter of Binary Tree","sub":"LeetCode 543 · Easy · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":543,"q":["Diameter of Binary Tree","how to solve Diameter of Binary Tree","Diameter of Binary Tree approach","Diameter of Binary Tree solution","leetcode 543","lc 543","Length (in edges) of the longest path between any two nodes.","Longest path (in edges) between any two nodes, which may not pass through the root."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Path bends through a node → return one side, track both. Complexity O(n) time, O(h) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Path bends through a node → return one side, track both.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> At each node, the longest path bending there = height(left) + height(right). Return height upward, track the best on the side.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Longest path (in edges) between any two nodes, which may not pass through the root.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> For every node compute both heights separately: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>height(None) = 0.<\/li><li>At a node: L = height(left), R = height(right); best = max(best, L + R).<\/li><li>Return 1 + max(L, R).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty tree has height 0.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Heights of both subtrees.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Return 1 + max to the parent. On the side, the path bending here is left + right; keep the best.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(h) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The longest path doesn&#x27;t have to pass through the root. That&#x27;s why you track the best at every node.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def diameter_of_binary_tree(root):\n    best = 0\n    def height(node):\n        nonlocal best\n        if not node:\n            return 0\n        l, r = height(node.left), height(node.right)\n        best = max(best, l + r)      # path bending at this node\n        return 1 + max(l, r)         # what the parent needs\n    height(root)\n    return best","java":"int best = 0;\n\npublic int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode root) {\n    height(root);\n    return best;\n}\n\nint height(TreeNode node) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    int l = height(node.left), r = height(node.right);\n    best = Math.max(best, l + r);       // path bending at this node\n    return 1 + Math.max(l, r);          // what the parent needs\n}","cpp":"int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode* root) {\n    int best = 0;\n    function<int(TreeNode*)> height = [&](TreeNode* node) {\n        if (!node) return 0;\n        int l = height(node->left), r = height(node->right);\n        best = max(best, l + r);        // path bending at this node\n        return 1 + max(l, r);           // what the parent needs\n    };\n    height(root);\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-543"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/diameter-of-binary-tree/"]],"ask":"Length (in edges) of the longest path between any two nodes."},{"i":"p-recursion-113","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Path Sum II","sub":"LeetCode 113 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":113,"q":["Path Sum II","how to solve Path Sum II","Path Sum II approach","Path Sum II solution","leetcode 113","lc 113","Return every root-to-leaf path whose values add up to the target.","List every root-to-leaf path whose values add up to target."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Info from above → parameter. Listing paths → choose / un-choose. Complexity O(n²) worst case (copying paths)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Info from above → parameter. Listing paths → choose / un-choose.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Backtracking down the tree: carry the path and the remaining sum.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> List every root-to-leaf path whose values add up to target.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Add node to path; remaining −= node.val.<\/li><li>At a leaf with remaining 0, save a copy of path.<\/li><li>Recurse into children, then pop the node (un-choose).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> None: nothing to add.<\/li><li><b>Pass down:<\/b> The remaining sum and the current path (top-down).<\/li><li><b>Record:<\/b> At a leaf with remaining 0. Then un-choose, because this is backtracking on a tree.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n²) worst case (copying paths)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Pop the node on the way out even after recording, or the next path starts dirty.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def path_sum(root, target):\n    res, path = [], []\n    def dfs(node, remaining):\n        if not node:\n            return\n        path.append(node.val)                 # choose\n        remaining -= node.val\n        if not node.left and not node.right and remaining == 0:\n            res.append(path[:])\n        dfs(node.left, remaining)             # explore\n        dfs(node.right, remaining)\n        path.pop()                            # un-choose\n    dfs(root, target)\n    return res","java":"List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\nList<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<List<Integer>> pathSum(TreeNode root, int target) {\n    dfs(root, target);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid dfs(TreeNode node, int remaining) {\n    if (node == null) return;\n    path.add(node.val);                          // choose\n    remaining -= node.val;\n    if (node.left == null && node.right == null && remaining == 0)\n        res.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n    dfs(node.left, remaining);                   // explore\n    dfs(node.right, remaining);\n    path.remove(path.size() - 1);                // un-choose\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> pathSum(TreeNode* root, int target) {\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    vector<int> path;\n    function<void(TreeNode*, int)> dfs = [&](TreeNode* node, int remaining) {\n        if (!node) return;\n        path.push_back(node->val);               // choose\n        remaining -= node->val;\n        if (!node->left && !node->right && remaining == 0) res.push_back(path);\n        dfs(node->left, remaining);              // explore\n        dfs(node->right, remaining);\n        path.pop_back();                         // un-choose\n    };\n    dfs(root, target);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-113"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/path-sum-ii/"]],"ask":"Return every root-to-leaf path whose values add up to the target."},{"i":"p-recursion-236","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Lowest Common Ancestor","sub":"LeetCode 236 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":236,"q":["Lowest Common Ancestor","how to solve Lowest Common Ancestor","Lowest Common Ancestor approach","Lowest Common Ancestor solution","leetcode 236","lc 236","Given nodes p and q, find their deepest shared ancestor.","The deepest node that has both p and q underneath it (a node can be its own descendant)."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking “Find” → return the found node upward. Complexity O(n) time, O(h) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> “Find” → return the found node upward.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Each call reports what it found. If both sides found something, this node is the answer.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The deepest node that has both p and q underneath it (a node can be its own descendant).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Find both root-to-node paths and compare: two passes, extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>None, p or q → return node.<\/li><li>Ask left and right.<\/li><li>Both non-empty → return node; otherwise return whichever side found something.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty, or the node is p or q: return it.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> “Did you find p or q?” to each subtree.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Both sides found something: you are the split point. Otherwise pass up whichever side did.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(h) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Returning the node when it equals p handles the case where p is an ancestor of q.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def lowest_common_ancestor(root, p, q):\n    if not root or root is p or root is q:\n        return root\n    left = lowest_common_ancestor(root.left, p, q)\n    right = lowest_common_ancestor(root.right, p, q)\n    if left and right:\n        return root            # p and q split here\n    return left or right       # pass up whatever was found","java":"public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {\n    if (root == null || root == p || root == q) return root;\n    TreeNode left = lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);\n    TreeNode right = lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);\n    if (left != null && right != null) return root;   // p and q split here\n    return left != null ? left : right;              // pass up whatever was found\n}","cpp":"TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {\n    if (!root || root == p || root == q) return root;\n    TreeNode* left = lowestCommonAncestor(root->left, p, q);\n    TreeNode* right = lowestCommonAncestor(root->right, p, q);\n    if (left && right) return root;          // p and q split here\n    return left ? left : right;              // pass up whatever was found\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-236"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-tree/"]],"ask":"Given nodes p and q, find their deepest shared ancestor."},{"i":"p-recursion-98","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Validate Binary Search Tree","sub":"LeetCode 98 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":98,"q":["Validate Binary Search Tree","how to solve Validate Binary Search Tree","Validate Binary Search Tree approach","Validate Binary Search Tree solution","leetcode 98","lc 98","Check that every node is strictly between all its left and right descendants.","Is this a valid binary search tree (left < node < right for whole subtrees)?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Needs bounds from ancestors → pass (low, high) down. Complexity O(n) time, O(h) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Needs bounds from ancestors → pass (low, high) down.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Pass down the allowed range (low, high) for each node.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Is this a valid binary search tree (left &lt; node &lt; right for whole subtrees)?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Checking only immediate children: wrong (misses deeper violations).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>valid(node, lo, hi): None → True.<\/li><li>Value must satisfy lo &lt; val &lt; hi.<\/li><li>Left gets (lo, val), right gets (val, hi).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty tree is valid.<\/li><li><b>Pass down:<\/b> An allowed range (low, high). Going left lowers high; going right raises low.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> This node in range, and both subtrees valid.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(h) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Checking only parent vs children fails on [5, 4, 6, null, null, 3, 7]: 3 sits in 5&#x27;s right subtree.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def is_valid_bst(root):\n    def ok(node, low, high):\n        if not node:\n            return True\n        if not (low < node.val < high):\n            return False\n        return ok(node.left, low, node.val) and ok(node.right, node.val, high)\n    return ok(root, float(\"-inf\"), float(\"inf\"))","java":"public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {\n    return ok(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);\n}\n\nboolean ok(TreeNode node, long low, long high) {\n    if (node == null) return true;\n    if (!(low < node.val && node.val < high)) return false;\n    return ok(node.left, low, node.val) && ok(node.right, node.val, high);\n}","cpp":"bool isValidBST(TreeNode* root) {\n    return ok(root, LLONG_MIN, LLONG_MAX);\n}\n\nbool ok(TreeNode* node, long long low, long long high) {\n    if (!node) return true;\n    if (!(low < node->val && node->val < high)) return false;\n    return ok(node->left, low, node->val) && ok(node->right, node->val, high);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-98"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/validate-binary-search-tree/"]],"ask":"Check that every node is strictly between all its left and right descendants."},{"i":"p-recursion-105","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Construct Tree from Preorder and Inorder","sub":"LeetCode 105 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":105,"q":["Construct Tree from Preorder and Inorder","how to solve Construct Tree from Preorder and Inorder","Construct Tree from Preorder and Inorder approach","Construct Tree from Preorder and Inorder solution","leetcode 105","lc 105","Rebuild the tree from its preorder and inorder lists (values unique).","Rebuild a tree from its preorder and inorder sequences."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Preorder gives the roots, inorder gives the sides. Complexity O(n) with a value → index map","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Preorder gives the roots, inorder gives the sides.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Preorder&#x27;s first value is the root; its position in inorder splits left and right subtrees. Use a value → index map.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Rebuild a tree from its preorder and inorder sequences.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Search the inorder list for each root: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Map inorder value → index.<\/li><li>Take the next preorder value as root.<\/li><li>Build left from the part before it in inorder, then right from the part after.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty inorder range: no node.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Preorder&#x27;s next value is the root. Its position in inorder splits left part and right part.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Build left from the left part, right from the right part.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) with a value → index map<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Build the left subtree before the right, because preorder lists root, then all of left, then right.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def build_tree(preorder, inorder):\n    idx = {v: i for i, v in enumerate(inorder)}\n    pre = iter(preorder)\n    def build(lo, hi):                    # works on inorder[lo..hi]\n        if lo > hi:\n            return None\n        val = next(pre)                   # preorder gives the root first\n        root = TreeNode(val)\n        mid = idx[val]\n        root.left = build(lo, mid - 1)    # left part of inorder\n        root.right = build(mid + 1, hi)   # right part of inorder\n        return root\n    return build(0, len(inorder) - 1)","java":"Map<Integer, Integer> idx = new HashMap<>();\nint pre = 0;                                     // next root in preorder\n\npublic TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < inorder.length; i++) idx.put(inorder[i], i);\n    return build(preorder, 0, inorder.length - 1);\n}\n\nTreeNode build(int[] preorder, int lo, int hi) {   // works on inorder[lo..hi]\n    if (lo > hi) return null;\n    int val = preorder[pre++];                   // preorder gives the root first\n    TreeNode root = new TreeNode(val);\n    int mid = idx.get(val);\n    root.left = build(preorder, lo, mid - 1);    // left part of inorder\n    root.right = build(preorder, mid + 1, hi);   // right part of inorder\n    return root;\n}","cpp":"TreeNode* buildTree(vector<int>& preorder, vector<int>& inorder) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> idx;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)inorder.size(); i++) idx[inorder[i]] = i;\n    int pre = 0;                                 // next root in preorder\n    function<TreeNode*(int, int)> build = [&](int lo, int hi) -> TreeNode* {\n        if (lo > hi) return nullptr;             // works on inorder[lo..hi]\n        int val = preorder[pre++];               // preorder gives the root first\n        TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(val);\n        int mid = idx[val];\n        root->left = build(lo, mid - 1);         // left part of inorder\n        root->right = build(mid + 1, hi);        // right part of inorder\n        return root;\n    };\n    return build(0, inorder.size() - 1);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-105"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-inorder-traversal/"]],"ask":"Rebuild the tree from its preorder and inorder lists (values unique)."},{"i":"p-recursion-124","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum","sub":"LeetCode 124 · Hard · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":124,"q":["Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum","how to solve Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum","Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum approach","Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum solution","leetcode 124","lc 124","Max sum of any path (any start, any end, can bend once). Values may be negative.","Biggest sum along any path (any start and end), values may be negative."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Path bends → hybrid. Negative branch → max with 0. Complexity O(n) time, O(h) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Path bends → hybrid. Negative branch → max with 0.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> At each node: the best path bending here = node + max(0, leftGain) + max(0, rightGain). Return only one side upward.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Biggest sum along any path (any start and end), values may be negative.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every pair of nodes: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>gain(None) = 0.<\/li><li>L = max(0, gain(left)), R = max(0, gain(right)); best = max(best, val + L + R).<\/li><li>Return val + max(L, R).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty contributes 0.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Best downward gain from each child; treat negative gains as 0.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Track node + left + right as a bending path. Return node + max(left, right), since the parent can extend only one side.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(h) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Start best at −∞, not 0. An all-negative tree still has an answer.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_path_sum(root):\n    best = float(\"-inf\")\n    def gain(node):\n        nonlocal best\n        if not node:\n            return 0\n        l = max(gain(node.left), 0)           # drop negative branches\n        r = max(gain(node.right), 0)\n        best = max(best, node.val + l + r)    # path bending here\n        return node.val + max(l, r)           # parent extends one side\n    gain(root)\n    return best","java":"int best = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n\npublic int maxPathSum(TreeNode root) {\n    gain(root);\n    return best;\n}\n\nint gain(TreeNode node) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    int l = Math.max(gain(node.left), 0);        // drop negative branches\n    int r = Math.max(gain(node.right), 0);\n    best = Math.max(best, node.val + l + r);     // path bending here\n    return node.val + Math.max(l, r);            // parent extends one side\n}","cpp":"int maxPathSum(TreeNode* root) {\n    int best = INT_MIN;\n    function<int(TreeNode*)> gain = [&](TreeNode* node) {\n        if (!node) return 0;\n        int l = max(gain(node->left), 0);        // drop negative branches\n        int r = max(gain(node->right), 0);\n        best = max(best, node->val + l + r);     // path bending here\n        return node->val + max(l, r);            // parent extends one side\n    };\n    gain(root);\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-124"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-maximum-path-sum/"]],"ask":"Max sum of any path (any start, any end, can bend once). Values may be negative."},{"i":"p-recursion-114","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Flatten Binary Tree to Linked List","sub":"LeetCode 114 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":114,"q":["Flatten Binary Tree to Linked List","how to solve Flatten Binary Tree to Linked List","Flatten Binary Tree to Linked List approach","Flatten Binary Tree to Linked List solution","leetcode 114","lc 114","Rewire the tree in place into a “linked list” using right pointers, in preorder. Left pointers become None.","Rearrange the tree into a right-only chain in preorder, in place."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Changing shape and need old pointers → process in reverse with a prev pointer. Complexity O(n) time; O(h) stack, or O(1) with the splice version","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Changing shape and need old pointers → process in reverse with a prev pointer.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Process in reverse preorder (right, left, node) and keep a &#x27;prev&#x27; pointer to hang each node on.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Rearrange the tree into a right-only chain in preorder, in place.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Collect preorder into a list and relink: O(n) extra space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Recurse right, then left.<\/li><li>node.right = prev, node.left = None.<\/li><li>prev = node.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Preorder is root, left, right. Rewiring a node destroys the pointers you still need to visit.<\/li><li><b>Trick:<\/b> Go in reverse: right, left, node. Each node hooks onto the list built so far (prev).<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> node.right = prev, node.left = None, prev = node.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time; O(h) stack, or O(1) with the splice version<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Doing it in normal preorder overwrites node.right before you visit it. Save it first, or go in reverse. Watch the lab above.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def flatten(root):\n    prev = None\n    def dfs(node):\n        nonlocal prev\n        if not node:\n            return\n        dfs(node.right)          # right first: build the list backwards\n        dfs(node.left)\n        node.right = prev        # hook onto the list built so far\n        node.left = None\n        prev = node              # the list now starts here\n    dfs(root)","java":"TreeNode prev = null;\n\npublic void flatten(TreeNode root) {\n    if (root == null) return;\n    flatten(root.right);         // right first: build the list backwards\n    flatten(root.left);\n    root.right = prev;           // hook onto the list built so far\n    root.left = null;\n    prev = root;                 // the list now starts here\n}","cpp":"TreeNode* prev = nullptr;\n\nvoid flatten(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (!root) return;\n    flatten(root->right);        // right first: build the list backwards\n    flatten(root->left);\n    root->right = prev;          // hook onto the list built so far\n    root->left = nullptr;\n    prev = root;                 // the list now starts here\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-114"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/flatten-binary-tree-to-linked-list/"]],"ask":"Rewire the tree in place into a “linked list” using right pointers, in preorder. Left pointers become None."},{"i":"p-recursion-199","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Binary Tree Right Side View","sub":"LeetCode 199 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":199,"q":["Binary Tree Right Side View","how to solve Binary Tree Right Side View","Binary Tree Right Side View approach","Binary Tree Right Side View solution","leetcode 199","lc 199","Return the values you’d see standing on the right of the tree, top to bottom.","Values seen from the right side, top to bottom."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking “Level” wording → BFS, or DFS carrying depth. Complexity O(n) time, O(h) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> “Level” wording → BFS, or DFS carrying depth.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Level-order traversal; the last node of each level is visible (or DFS right-first, first node per depth).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Values seen from the right side, top to bottom.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>BFS level by level.<\/li><li>Record the last node&#x27;s value of each level.<\/li><li>Return the list.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pass down:<\/b> Depth.<\/li><li><b>Order:<\/b> Visit right child before left.<\/li><li><b>Record:<\/b> The first node you meet at each new depth is the visible one.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(h) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> It’s not just the right spine: a deep left subtree peeks out where the right side is shorter.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def right_side_view(root):\n    res = []\n    def dfs(node, depth):\n        if not node:\n            return\n        if depth == len(res):             # first node seen at this depth\n            res.append(node.val)\n        dfs(node.right, depth + 1)        # right before left\n        dfs(node.left, depth + 1)\n    dfs(root, 0)\n    return res","java":"List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {\n    dfs(root, 0);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid dfs(TreeNode node, int depth) {\n    if (node == null) return;\n    if (depth == res.size()) res.add(node.val);   // first node seen at this depth\n    dfs(node.right, depth + 1);                   // right before left\n    dfs(node.left, depth + 1);\n}","cpp":"vector<int> rightSideView(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> res;\n    function<void(TreeNode*, int)> dfs = [&](TreeNode* node, int depth) {\n        if (!node) return;\n        if (depth == (int)res.size()) res.push_back(node->val);   // first node at this depth\n        dfs(node->right, depth + 1);                              // right before left\n        dfs(node->left, depth + 1);\n    };\n    dfs(root, 0);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-199"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-right-side-view/"]],"ask":"Return the values you’d see standing on the right of the tree, top to bottom."},{"i":"p-recursion-103","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order","sub":"LeetCode 103 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":103,"q":["Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order","how to solve Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order","Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order approach","Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order solution","leetcode 103","lc 103","Level order, but alternate left-to-right and right-to-left each level.","Level order, but alternate direction on every level."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking “Level by level” → queue, one level per loop. Complexity O(n) time, O(w) queue","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> “Level by level” → queue, one level per loop.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Normal BFS; reverse every other level&#x27;s list.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Level order, but alternate direction on every level.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>BFS by levels.<\/li><li>Collect each level left to right.<\/li><li>Reverse odd-numbered levels.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Shape:<\/b> BFS with for _ in range(len(q)) to isolate a level.<\/li><li><b>Twist:<\/b> Build the level normally, reverse it every other time.<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> Empty root → [].<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(w) queue<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Don’t change the order you push children; only flip the finished level list.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n\ndef zigzag_level_order(root):\n    if not root:\n        return []\n    res, q, left_to_right = [], deque([root]), True\n    while q:\n        level = []\n        for _ in range(len(q)):           # exactly one level\n            node = q.popleft()\n            level.append(node.val)\n            if node.left:  q.append(node.left)\n            if node.right: q.append(node.right)\n        res.append(level if left_to_right else level[::-1])\n        left_to_right = not left_to_right\n    return res","java":"public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {\n    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    if (root == null) return res;\n    Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    q.offer(root);\n    boolean leftToRight = true;\n    while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n        List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = q.size(); i > 0; i--) {         // exactly one level\n            TreeNode node = q.poll();\n            level.add(node.val);\n            if (node.left != null) q.offer(node.left);\n            if (node.right != null) q.offer(node.right);\n        }\n        if (!leftToRight) Collections.reverse(level);\n        res.add(level);\n        leftToRight = !leftToRight;\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    if (!root) return res;\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(root);\n    bool leftToRight = true;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        vector<int> level;\n        for (int i = q.size(); i > 0; i--) {         // exactly one level\n            TreeNode* node = q.front(); q.pop();\n            level.push_back(node->val);\n            if (node->left) q.push(node->left);\n            if (node->right) q.push(node->right);\n        }\n        if (!leftToRight) reverse(level.begin(), level.end());\n        res.push_back(level);\n        leftToRight = !leftToRight;\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-103"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-zigzag-level-order-traversal/"]],"ask":"Level order, but alternate left-to-right and right-to-left each level."},{"i":"p-recursion-230","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Kth Smallest Element in a BST","sub":"LeetCode 230 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":230,"q":["Kth Smallest Element in a BST","how to solve Kth Smallest Element in a BST","Kth Smallest Element in a BST approach","Kth Smallest Element in a BST solution","leetcode 230","lc 230","Return the kth smallest value in a binary search tree.","The kth smallest value in a BST."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking BST + order → inorder is sorted. Complexity O(h + k) time","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> BST + order → inorder is sorted.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Inorder traversal of a BST visits values in sorted order; stop at the kth.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The kth smallest value in a BST.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Collect all values and sort: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Inorder (left, node, right), counting visits.<\/li><li>When the count hits k, record the value.<\/li><li>Stop early.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> BST + “smallest / sorted” → inorder.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> Iterative inorder: go left as far as possible, pop, go right.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> Count down k on each pop; return at zero.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(h + k) time<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Collecting the whole inorder list works but wastes O(n). Stop early.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def kth_smallest(root, k):\n    stack, node = [], root\n    while stack or node:\n        while node:                       # go as far left as possible\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()                # next value in sorted order\n        k -= 1\n        if k == 0:\n            return node.val\n        node = node.right","java":"public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {\n    Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    TreeNode node = root;\n    while (!stack.isEmpty() || node != null) {\n        while (node != null) {              // go as far left as possible\n            stack.push(node);\n            node = node.left;\n        }\n        node = stack.pop();                 // next value in sorted order\n        if (--k == 0) return node.val;\n        node = node.right;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}","cpp":"int kthSmallest(TreeNode* root, int k) {\n    vector<TreeNode*> st;\n    TreeNode* node = root;\n    while (!st.empty() || node) {\n        while (node) {                      // go as far left as possible\n            st.push_back(node);\n            node = node->left;\n        }\n        node = st.back(); st.pop_back();    // next value in sorted order\n        if (--k == 0) return node->val;\n        node = node->right;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-230"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst/"]],"ask":"Return the kth smallest value in a binary search tree."},{"i":"p-recursion-235","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST","sub":"LeetCode 235 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":235,"q":["Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST","how to solve Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST","Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST approach","Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST solution","leetcode 235","lc 235","Like 236, but the tree is a BST.","Lowest common ancestor when the tree is a BST."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking BST → walk one direction, O(h). Complexity O(h) time, O(1) space","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> BST → walk one direction, O(h).<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> BST order tells you where to go: both smaller → go left; both bigger → go right; otherwise you&#x27;re at the split.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Lowest common ancestor when the tree is a BST.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Use the general solution: O(n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Start at root.<\/li><li>Both p and q &lt; node → left; both &gt; node → right.<\/li><li>Else return node.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Ordering tells you which side p and q live on.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> Both smaller → go left. Both bigger → go right.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> Otherwise they split here (or one of them is here).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(h) time, O(1) space<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Using the general 236 solution works but ignores the BST and costs O(n).<\/p>","c":{"py":"def lowest_common_ancestor_bst(root, p, q):\n    node = root\n    while node:\n        if p.val < node.val and q.val < node.val:\n            node = node.left              # both smaller: go left\n        elif p.val > node.val and q.val > node.val:\n            node = node.right             # both bigger: go right\n        else:\n            return node                   # they split here","java":"public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {\n    TreeNode node = root;\n    while (node != null) {\n        if (p.val < node.val && q.val < node.val) node = node.left;         // both smaller: go left\n        else if (p.val > node.val && q.val > node.val) node = node.right;   // both bigger: go right\n        else return node;                                                   // they split here\n    }\n    return null;\n}","cpp":"TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {\n    TreeNode* node = root;\n    while (node) {\n        if (p->val < node->val && q->val < node->val) node = node->left;         // both smaller\n        else if (p->val > node->val && q->val > node->val) node = node->right;   // both bigger\n        else return node;                                                        // they split here\n    }\n    return nullptr;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-235"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-search-tree/"]],"ask":"Like 236, but the tree is a BST."},{"i":"p-recursion-572","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Subtree of Another Tree","sub":"LeetCode 572 · Easy · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":572,"q":["Subtree of Another Tree","how to solve Subtree of Another Tree","Subtree of Another Tree approach","Subtree of Another Tree solution","leetcode 572","lc 572","Does root contain a subtree identical to sub?","Is some subtree of root exactly equal to sub?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Two trees → pair recursion inside a normal recursion. Complexity O(m · n) worst case","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Two trees → pair recursion inside a normal recursion.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> At every node of root, check &#x27;same tree&#x27; with sub.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Is some subtree of root exactly equal to sub?<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>same(a, b): both None → True; values and both children must match.<\/li><li>If same(node, sub) → True.<\/li><li>Else check node.left and node.right.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Helper:<\/b> same(a, b): the pair recursion from Same Tree (100).<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Is it the same here, or a subtree of my left, or of my right?<\/li><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty root contains nothing.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(m · n) worst case<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> a is b is True only when both are None, a compact base case for the pair.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def is_subtree(root, sub):\n    def same(a, b):\n        if not a or not b:\n            return a is b                 # True only if both are None\n        return a.val == b.val and same(a.left, b.left) and same(a.right, b.right)\n    if not root:\n        return False\n    return same(root, sub) or is_subtree(root.left, sub) or is_subtree(root.right, sub)","java":"public boolean isSubtree(TreeNode root, TreeNode sub) {\n    if (root == null) return false;\n    return same(root, sub) || isSubtree(root.left, sub) || isSubtree(root.right, sub);\n}\n\nboolean same(TreeNode a, TreeNode b) {\n    if (a == null || b == null) return a == b;     // true only if both are null\n    return a.val == b.val && same(a.left, b.left) && same(a.right, b.right);\n}","cpp":"bool isSubtree(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* sub) {\n    if (!root) return false;\n    return same(root, sub) || isSubtree(root->left, sub) || isSubtree(root->right, sub);\n}\n\nbool same(TreeNode* a, TreeNode* b) {\n    if (!a || !b) return a == b;                   // true only if both are null\n    return a->val == b->val && same(a->left, b->left) && same(a->right, b->right);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-572"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/subtree-of-another-tree/"]],"ask":"Does root contain a subtree identical to sub?"},{"i":"p-recursion-297","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree","sub":"LeetCode 297 · Hard · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":297,"q":["Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree","how to solve Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree","Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree approach","Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree solution","leetcode 297","lc 297","Turn a tree into a string and back.","Turn a tree into a string and back."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Rebuild a tree → recurse in the same order it was written. Complexity O(n) both ways","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Rebuild a tree → recurse in the same order it was written.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Preorder with a marker for None makes the shape unambiguous.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Turn a tree into a string and back.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Serialize: preorder values, &#x27;#&#x27; for None, joined by commas.<\/li><li>Deserialize: read tokens in the same preorder.<\/li><li>&#x27;#&#x27; → None; else make a node, then build left, then right.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Preorder alone is ambiguous. Preorder with markers for empty children is not.<\/li><li><b>Serialize:<\/b> Preorder, write # for None.<\/li><li><b>Deserialize:<\/b> Consume the same order with an iterator: value, build left, build right.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) both ways<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Deserialize must read in exactly the order serialize wrote. One shared iterator makes that automatic.<\/p>","c":{"py":"class Codec:\n    def serialize(self, root):\n        out = []\n        def pre(node):\n            if not node:\n                out.append(\"#\")           # mark empty children\n                return\n            out.append(str(node.val))\n            pre(node.left)\n            pre(node.right)\n        pre(root)\n        return \",\".join(out)\n\n    def deserialize(self, data):\n        vals = iter(data.split(\",\"))\n        def build():\n            v = next(vals)\n            if v == \"#\":\n                return None\n            node = TreeNode(int(v))\n            node.left = build()           # same order as serialize\n            node.right = build()\n            return node\n        return build()","java":"public class Codec {\n    public String serialize(TreeNode root) {\n        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();\n        pre(root, out);\n        return out.toString();\n    }\n\n    private void pre(TreeNode node, StringBuilder out) {\n        if (node == null) { out.append(\"#,\"); return; }   // mark empty children\n        out.append(node.val).append(',');\n        pre(node.left, out);\n        pre(node.right, out);\n    }\n\n    public TreeNode deserialize(String data) {\n        Iterator<String> vals = Arrays.asList(data.split(\",\")).iterator();\n        return build(vals);\n    }\n\n    private TreeNode build(Iterator<String> vals) {\n        String v = vals.next();\n        if (v.equals(\"#\")) return null;\n        TreeNode node = new TreeNode(Integer.parseInt(v));\n        node.left = build(vals);                           // same order as serialize\n        node.right = build(vals);\n        return node;\n    }\n}","cpp":"class Codec {\npublic:\n    string serialize(TreeNode* root) {\n        string out;\n        function<void(TreeNode*)> pre = [&](TreeNode* node) {\n            if (!node) { out += \"#,\"; return; }            // mark empty children\n            out += to_string(node->val) + \",\";\n            pre(node->left);\n            pre(node->right);\n        };\n        pre(root);\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    TreeNode* deserialize(string data) {\n        stringstream ss(data);\n        function<TreeNode*()> build = [&]() -> TreeNode* {\n            string v;\n            getline(ss, v, ',');\n            if (v == \"#\") return nullptr;\n            TreeNode* node = new TreeNode(stoi(v));\n            node->left = build();                          // same order as serialize\n            node->right = build();\n            return node;\n        };\n        return build();\n    }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-297"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/serialize-and-deserialize-binary-tree/"]],"ask":"Turn a tree into a string and back."},{"i":"p-recursion-437","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Path Sum III","sub":"LeetCode 437 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":437,"q":["Path Sum III","how to solve Path Sum III","Path Sum III approach","Path Sum III solution","leetcode 437","lc 437","Count downward paths (any start, any end) whose sum equals target.","Count downward paths (any start, any end) that add up to target."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Path sums anywhere → prefix sums + hashmap, added in, removed out. Complexity O(n) time","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Path sums anywhere → prefix sums + hashmap, added in, removed out.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Prefix sums along the current root path: paths ending here = how many earlier prefixes equal current − target.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Count downward paths (any start, any end) that add up to target.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Start a path-sum from every node: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Keep a dict of prefix sums on the current path, starting {0: 1}.<\/li><li>At a node: cur += val; count += seen[cur − target]; seen[cur] += 1.<\/li><li>Recurse, then undo seen[cur] −= 1 on the way back.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> A downward path sums to target when running − earlier = target.<\/li><li><b>State:<\/b> Running sum passed down, plus a hashmap of prefix sums on the current path.<\/li><li><b>Un-choose:<\/b> Remove this node’s prefix when you leave it.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Seed seen[0] = 1, or paths that start at the root are missed.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import defaultdict\n\ndef path_sum3(root, target):\n    seen = defaultdict(int)\n    seen[0] = 1                           # the empty prefix\n    def dfs(node, running):\n        if not node:\n            return 0\n        running += node.val\n        count = seen[running - target]    # paths ending at this node\n        seen[running] += 1                # choose\n        count += dfs(node.left, running) + dfs(node.right, running)\n        seen[running] -= 1                # un-choose: leaving this path\n        return count\n    return dfs(root, 0)","java":"Map<Long, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();\n\npublic int pathSum(TreeNode root, int target) {\n    seen.put(0L, 1);                         // the empty prefix\n    return dfs(root, 0, target);\n}\n\nint dfs(TreeNode node, long running, int target) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    running += node.val;\n    int count = seen.getOrDefault(running - target, 0);   // paths ending at this node\n    seen.merge(running, 1, Integer::sum);                 // choose\n    count += dfs(node.left, running, target) + dfs(node.right, running, target);\n    seen.merge(running, -1, Integer::sum);                // un-choose: leaving this path\n    return count;\n}","cpp":"int pathSum(TreeNode* root, int target) {\n    unordered_map<long long, int> seen{{0, 1}};           // the empty prefix\n    function<int(TreeNode*, long long)> dfs = [&](TreeNode* node, long long running) {\n        if (!node) return 0;\n        running += node->val;\n        int count = seen.count(running - target) ? seen[running - target] : 0;   // ending here\n        seen[running]++;                                  // choose\n        count += dfs(node->left, running) + dfs(node->right, running);\n        seen[running]--;                                  // un-choose: leaving this path\n        return count;\n    };\n    return dfs(root, 0);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-437"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/path-sum-iii/"]],"ask":"Count downward paths (any start, any end) whose sum equals target."},{"i":"p-recursion-863","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"All Nodes Distance K in Binary Tree","sub":"LeetCode 863 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":863,"q":["All Nodes Distance K in Binary Tree","how to solve All Nodes Distance K in Binary Tree","All Nodes Distance K in Binary Tree approach","All Nodes Distance K in Binary Tree solution","leetcode 863","lc 863","Return all values exactly k edges away from a target node.","All nodes exactly k edges away from target."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Need to go upward → parent map, then BFS. Complexity O(n) time and space","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Need to go upward → parent map, then BFS.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Trees only point down. Add parent pointers, then BFS outward from target like a graph.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> All nodes exactly k edges away from target.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>DFS once to record each node&#x27;s parent.<\/li><li>BFS from target over left, right and parent, with a visited set.<\/li><li>After k levels, return the queue&#x27;s values.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Distance can go up through parents, but trees only point down.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> One DFS to record each node’s parent. Now it’s an undirected graph.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> BFS from target for k rings; whatever is in the queue is the answer.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time and space<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Use a seen set, or you’ll bounce child → parent → child forever.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n\ndef distance_k(root, target, k):\n    parent = {}\n    def link(node, par):\n        if node:\n            parent[node] = par\n            link(node.left, node)\n            link(node.right, node)\n    link(root, None)\n    q, seen = deque([target]), {target}\n    for _ in range(k):                    # expand one ring per round\n        for _ in range(len(q)):\n            node = q.popleft()\n            for nb in (node.left, node.right, parent[node]):\n                if nb and nb not in seen:\n                    seen.add(nb)\n                    q.append(nb)\n    return [node.val for node in q]","java":"public List<Integer> distanceK(TreeNode root, TreeNode target, int k) {\n    Map<TreeNode, TreeNode> parent = new HashMap<>();\n    link(root, null, parent);\n    Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    Set<TreeNode> seen = new HashSet<>();\n    q.offer(target); seen.add(target);\n    for (int step = 0; step < k; step++)             // expand one ring per round\n        for (int i = q.size(); i > 0; i--) {\n            TreeNode node = q.poll();\n            for (TreeNode nb : new TreeNode[]{node.left, node.right, parent.get(node)})\n                if (nb != null && seen.add(nb)) q.offer(nb);\n        }\n    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (TreeNode node : q) res.add(node.val);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid link(TreeNode node, TreeNode par, Map<TreeNode, TreeNode> parent) {\n    if (node == null) return;\n    parent.put(node, par);\n    link(node.left, node, parent);\n    link(node.right, node, parent);\n}","cpp":"vector<int> distanceK(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* target, int k) {\n    unordered_map<TreeNode*, TreeNode*> parent;\n    function<void(TreeNode*, TreeNode*)> link = [&](TreeNode* node, TreeNode* par) {\n        if (!node) return;\n        parent[node] = par;\n        link(node->left, node);\n        link(node->right, node);\n    };\n    link(root, nullptr);\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    unordered_set<TreeNode*> seen{target};\n    q.push(target);\n    for (int step = 0; step < k; step++)             // expand one ring per round\n        for (int i = q.size(); i > 0; i--) {\n            TreeNode* node = q.front(); q.pop();\n            for (TreeNode* nb : {node->left, node->right, parent[node]})\n                if (nb && seen.insert(nb).second) q.push(nb);\n        }\n    vector<int> res;\n    while (!q.empty()) { res.push_back(q.front()->val); q.pop(); }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-863"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/all-nodes-distance-k-in-binary-tree/"]],"ask":"Return all values exactly k edges away from a target node."},{"i":"p-recursion-108","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Convert Sorted Array to BST","sub":"LeetCode 108 · Easy · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":108,"q":["Convert Sorted Array to BST","how to solve Convert Sorted Array to BST","Convert Sorted Array to BST approach","Convert Sorted Array to BST solution","leetcode 108","lc 108","Build a height-balanced BST from a sorted array.","Build a height-balanced BST from a sorted array."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Build a tree → choose a root, recurse on the two sides. Complexity O(n) time, O(log n) stack","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Build a tree → choose a root, recurse on the two sides.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> The middle element is the root; recurse on each half.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Build a height-balanced BST from a sorted array.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Insert values one by one: may become a chain.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>build(lo, hi): if lo &gt; hi → None.<\/li><li>mid = (lo + hi) // 2 → root.<\/li><li>left = build(lo, mid−1), right = build(mid+1, hi).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Empty range → None.<\/li><li><b>Ask:<\/b> Build left from the left half, right from the right half.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> The middle element is the root.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time, O(log n) stack<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Pass indexes, not slices, to avoid O(n log n) copying.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def sorted_array_to_bst(nums):\n    def build(lo, hi):\n        if lo > hi:\n            return None\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2              # middle becomes the root\n        node = TreeNode(nums[mid])\n        node.left = build(lo, mid - 1)\n        node.right = build(mid + 1, hi)\n        return node\n    return build(0, len(nums) - 1)","java":"public TreeNode sortedArrayToBST(int[] nums) {\n    return build(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);\n}\n\nTreeNode build(int[] nums, int lo, int hi) {\n    if (lo > hi) return null;\n    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;                 // middle becomes the root\n    TreeNode node = new TreeNode(nums[mid]);\n    node.left = build(nums, lo, mid - 1);\n    node.right = build(nums, mid + 1, hi);\n    return node;\n}","cpp":"TreeNode* sortedArrayToBST(vector<int>& nums) {\n    return build(nums, 0, nums.size() - 1);\n}\n\nTreeNode* build(vector<int>& nums, int lo, int hi) {\n    if (lo > hi) return nullptr;\n    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;                 // middle becomes the root\n    TreeNode* node = new TreeNode(nums[mid]);\n    node->left = build(nums, lo, mid - 1);\n    node->right = build(nums, mid + 1, hi);\n    return node;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-108"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/convert-sorted-array-to-binary-search-tree/"]],"ask":"Build a height-balanced BST from a sorted array."},{"i":"p-recursion-450","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Delete Node in a BST","sub":"LeetCode 450 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":450,"q":["Delete Node in a BST","how to solve Delete Node in a BST","Delete Node in a BST approach","Delete Node in a BST solution","leetcode 450","lc 450","Delete a key from a BST and return the (possibly new) root.","Delete a key from a BST and return the root."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Changing shape → root.left = f(root.left), return root. Complexity O(h) time","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Changing shape → root.left = f(root.left), return root.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Find it by BST order. With two children, replace its value with the smallest in its right subtree, then delete that one.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Delete a key from a BST and return the root.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>key &lt; val → recurse left; &gt; → recurse right.<\/li><li>Found: 0 or 1 child → return the other child.<\/li><li>2 children: copy the successor&#x27;s value, delete the successor from the right subtree.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Find:<\/b> Smaller → recurse left, bigger → recurse right, reassigning the child.<\/li><li><b>Easy case:<\/b> 0 or 1 child → return the other child.<\/li><li><b>Hard case:<\/b> 2 children → copy in the smallest value on the right, then delete that one.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(h) time<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The reassign pattern means deleting the root needs no special case.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def delete_node(root, key):\n    if not root:\n        return None\n    if key < root.val:\n        root.left = delete_node(root.left, key)     # reassign!\n    elif key > root.val:\n        root.right = delete_node(root.right, key)\n    else:\n        if not root.left:\n            return root.right             # 0 or 1 child: lift the other side\n        if not root.right:\n            return root.left\n        succ = root.right                 # 2 children: smallest on the right\n        while succ.left:\n            succ = succ.left\n        root.val = succ.val\n        root.right = delete_node(root.right, succ.val)\n    return root","java":"public TreeNode deleteNode(TreeNode root, int key) {\n    if (root == null) return null;\n    if (key < root.val) root.left = deleteNode(root.left, key);        // reassign!\n    else if (key > root.val) root.right = deleteNode(root.right, key);\n    else {\n        if (root.left == null) return root.right;   // 0 or 1 child: lift the other side\n        if (root.right == null) return root.left;\n        TreeNode succ = root.right;                  // 2 children: smallest on the right\n        while (succ.left != null) succ = succ.left;\n        root.val = succ.val;\n        root.right = deleteNode(root.right, succ.val);\n    }\n    return root;\n}","cpp":"TreeNode* deleteNode(TreeNode* root, int key) {\n    if (!root) return nullptr;\n    if (key < root->val) root->left = deleteNode(root->left, key);         // reassign!\n    else if (key > root->val) root->right = deleteNode(root->right, key);\n    else {\n        if (!root->left) return root->right;        // 0 or 1 child: lift the other side\n        if (!root->right) return root->left;\n        TreeNode* succ = root->right;               // 2 children: smallest on the right\n        while (succ->left) succ = succ->left;\n        root->val = succ->val;\n        root->right = deleteNode(root->right, succ->val);\n    }\n    return root;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-450"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-node-in-a-bst/"]],"ask":"Delete a key from a BST and return the (possibly new) root."},{"i":"p-recursion-337","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"House Robber III","sub":"LeetCode 337 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":337,"q":["House Robber III","how to solve House Robber III","House Robber III approach","House Robber III solution","leetcode 337","lc 337","Houses form a tree. You can’t rob a node and its direct child. Maximize the loot.","Houses form a tree; you can't rob a node and its child. Max loot?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Two states per node → return a tuple. Complexity O(n) time","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Two states per node → return a tuple.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Each node returns two numbers: best if robbed, best if skipped.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Houses form a tree; you can&#x27;t rob a node and its child. Max loot?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every subset: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>dfs returns (rob, skip).<\/li><li>rob = val + left.skip + right.skip.<\/li><li>skip = max(left) + max(right). Answer = max(root pair).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Each node has two futures: robbed or skipped.<\/li><li><b>Return:<\/b> A pair (rob_this, skip_this).<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Rob → children must be skipped. Skip → each child picks its best.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Memoizing on nodes also works, but the tuple version is cleaner and needs no cache.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def rob(root):\n    def dfs(node):                        # returns (rob_this, skip_this)\n        if not node:\n            return (0, 0)\n        l, r = dfs(node.left), dfs(node.right)\n        rob_this = node.val + l[1] + r[1]     # children must be skipped\n        skip_this = max(l) + max(r)           # children choose freely\n        return (rob_this, skip_this)\n    return max(dfs(root))","java":"public int rob(TreeNode root) {\n    int[] r = dfs(root);\n    return Math.max(r[0], r[1]);\n}\n\nint[] dfs(TreeNode node) {                   // returns {robThis, skipThis}\n    if (node == null) return new int[]{0, 0};\n    int[] l = dfs(node.left), r = dfs(node.right);\n    int robThis = node.val + l[1] + r[1];                       // children must be skipped\n    int skipThis = Math.max(l[0], l[1]) + Math.max(r[0], r[1]); // children choose freely\n    return new int[]{robThis, skipThis};\n}","cpp":"int rob(TreeNode* root) {\n    auto [a, b] = dfs(root);\n    return max(a, b);\n}\n\npair<int, int> dfs(TreeNode* node) {         // returns {robThis, skipThis}\n    if (!node) return {0, 0};\n    auto [l0, l1] = dfs(node->left);\n    auto [r0, r1] = dfs(node->right);\n    int robThis = node->val + l1 + r1;                   // children must be skipped\n    int skipThis = max(l0, l1) + max(r0, r1);            // children choose freely\n    return {robThis, skipThis};\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-337"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/house-robber-iii/"]],"ask":"Houses form a tree. You can’t rob a node and its direct child. Maximize the loot."},{"i":"p-recursion-129","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Sum Root to Leaf Numbers","sub":"LeetCode 129 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":129,"q":["Sum Root to Leaf Numbers","how to solve Sum Root to Leaf Numbers","Sum Root to Leaf Numbers approach","Sum Root to Leaf Numbers solution","leetcode 129","lc 129","Each root-to-leaf path spells a number (1 → 2 → 3 is 123). Sum them all.","Each root-to-leaf path spells a number. Add them all."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Info from above → parameter. Complexity O(n) time","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Info from above → parameter.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Carry the number so far down the tree: cur = cur × 10 + val.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each root-to-leaf path spells a number. Add them all.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>dfs(node, cur): None → 0.<\/li><li>cur = cur × 10 + val.<\/li><li>Leaf → return cur; else sum of both children.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pass down:<\/b> The number so far: num * 10 + node.val.<\/li><li><b>Leaf:<\/b> Return that number.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Sum of left and right results.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Check “is leaf” before recursing, or each leaf’s number is counted zero times or twice.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def sum_numbers(root):\n    def dfs(node, num):\n        if not node:\n            return 0\n        num = num * 10 + node.val         # carried down\n        if not node.left and not node.right:\n            return num\n        return dfs(node.left, num) + dfs(node.right, num)\n    return dfs(root, 0)","java":"public int sumNumbers(TreeNode root) {\n    return dfs(root, 0);\n}\n\nint dfs(TreeNode node, int num) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    num = num * 10 + node.val;               // carried down\n    if (node.left == null && node.right == null) return num;\n    return dfs(node.left, num) + dfs(node.right, num);\n}","cpp":"int sumNumbers(TreeNode* root) {\n    return dfs(root, 0);\n}\n\nint dfs(TreeNode* node, int num) {\n    if (!node) return 0;\n    num = num * 10 + node->val;              // carried down\n    if (!node->left && !node->right) return num;\n    return dfs(node->left, num) + dfs(node->right, num);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-129"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/sum-root-to-leaf-numbers/"]],"ask":"Each root-to-leaf path spells a number (1 → 2 → 3 is 123). Sum them all."},{"i":"p-recursion-968","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Binary Tree Cameras","sub":"LeetCode 968 · Hard · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":968,"q":["Binary Tree Cameras","how to solve Binary Tree Cameras","Binary Tree Cameras approach","Binary Tree Cameras solution","leetcode 968","lc 968","A camera watches its parent, itself and its children. Find the minimum cameras to watch every node.","Fewest cameras so every node is watched (a camera covers parent, itself, children)."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Bottom-up with states → greedy from the leaves. Complexity O(n) time","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Bottom-up with states → greedy from the leaves.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Greedy from the leaves up: put cameras on parents of uncovered leaves. Each node returns a state.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fewest cameras so every node is watched (a camera covers parent, itself, children).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every placement: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>States: 0 = needs cover, 1 = has camera, 2 = covered.<\/li><li>If any child is 0 → place camera here (cams += 1), return 1.<\/li><li>If any child is 1 → return 2, else 0. Root returns 0 → one more camera.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Leaves should never hold cameras: their parents cover more.<\/li><li><b>Return:<\/b> A state: 0 = needs cover, 1 = has camera, 2 = covered.<\/li><li><b>Combine:<\/b> Any child needs cover → camera here. Any child has a camera → I’m covered. Else ask my parent.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n) time<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> After the DFS, the root itself may still need a camera. Add one if it returned 0.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def min_camera_cover(root):\n    cams = 0\n    # 0 = needs cover, 1 = has camera, 2 = covered\n    def dfs(node):\n        nonlocal cams\n        if not node:\n            return 2                      # empty: treat as covered\n        l, r = dfs(node.left), dfs(node.right)\n        if l == 0 or r == 0:\n            cams += 1\n            return 1                      # a child needs me: camera here\n        if l == 1 or r == 1:\n            return 2                      # a child's camera covers me\n        return 0                          # leave it to my parent\n    if dfs(root) == 0:\n        cams += 1                 # root still uncovered: it has no parent to ask\n    return cams","java":"int cams = 0;\n// 0 = needs cover, 1 = has camera, 2 = covered\n\npublic int minCameraCover(TreeNode root) {\n    if (dfs(root) == 0) cams++;   // root still uncovered: it has no parent to ask\n    return cams;\n}\n\nint dfs(TreeNode node) {\n    if (node == null) return 2;              // empty: treat as covered\n    int l = dfs(node.left), r = dfs(node.right);\n    if (l == 0 || r == 0) {\n        cams++;\n        return 1;                            // a child needs me: camera here\n    }\n    if (l == 1 || r == 1) return 2;          // a child's camera covers me\n    return 0;                                // leave it to my parent\n}","cpp":"int cams = 0;\n// 0 = needs cover, 1 = has camera, 2 = covered\n\nint minCameraCover(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (dfs(root) == 0) cams++;   // root still uncovered: it has no parent to ask\n    return cams;\n}\n\nint dfs(TreeNode* node) {\n    if (!node) return 2;                     // empty: treat as covered\n    int l = dfs(node->left), r = dfs(node->right);\n    if (l == 0 || r == 0) {\n        cams++;\n        return 1;                            // a child needs me: camera here\n    }\n    if (l == 1 || r == 1) return 2;          // a child's camera covers me\n    return 0;                                // leave it to my parent\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-968"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-cameras/"]],"ask":"A camera watches its parent, itself and its children. Find the minimum cameras to watch every node."},{"i":"p-recursion-77","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Combinations","sub":"LeetCode 77 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":77,"q":["Combinations","how to solve Combinations","Combinations approach","Combinations solution","leetcode 77","lc 77","All ways to choose k numbers from 1..n.","All ways to choose k numbers from 1..n."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Order doesn’t matter → start index. Too few left → break. Complexity O(k · C(n, k))","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Order doesn’t matter → start index. Too few left → break.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Backtrack with a start index so each combination is built in increasing order once.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> All ways to choose k numbers from 1..n.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>bt(start): if len(path) == k, record a copy.<\/li><li>For i from start to n: choose i, bt(i + 1), un-choose.<\/li><li>Prune: stop when not enough numbers remain.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> path, start<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> numbers from start to n<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> len(path) == k<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> not enough numbers left to reach k<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(k · C(n, k))<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The prune n - i + 1 &lt; k - len(path) turns a slow solution into a fast one on big n.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def combine(n, k):\n    res, path = [], []\n    def backtrack(start):\n        if len(path) == k:\n            res.append(path[:])\n            return\n        for i in range(start, n + 1):\n            if n - i + 1 < k - len(path):\n                break                     # not enough numbers left\n            path.append(i)\n            backtrack(i + 1)\n            path.pop()\n    backtrack(1)\n    return res","java":"List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\nList<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<List<Integer>> combine(int n, int k) {\n    backtrack(n, k, 1);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid backtrack(int n, int k, int start) {\n    if (path.size() == k) {\n        res.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int i = start; i <= n; i++) {\n        if (n - i + 1 < k - path.size()) break;   // not enough numbers left\n        path.add(i);\n        backtrack(n, k, i + 1);\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> combine(int n, int k) {\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    vector<int> path;\n    function<void(int)> backtrack = [&](int start) {\n        if ((int)path.size() == k) { res.push_back(path); return; }\n        for (int i = start; i <= n; i++) {\n            if (n - i + 1 < k - (int)path.size()) break;   // not enough numbers left\n            path.push_back(i);\n            backtrack(i + 1);\n            path.pop_back();\n        }\n    };\n    backtrack(1);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-77"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/combinations/"]],"ask":"All ways to choose k numbers from 1..n."},{"i":"p-recursion-40","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Combination Sum II","sub":"LeetCode 40 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":40,"q":["Combination Sum II","how to solve Combination Sum II","Combination Sum II approach","Combination Sum II solution","leetcode 40","lc 40","Each candidate used at most once; candidates may repeat; no duplicate combinations.","Combinations summing to target, each candidate used once, no duplicate combinations."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Duplicates + once each → sort, skip twins, recurse i + 1. Complexity O(2ⁿ · n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Duplicates + once each → sort, skip twins, recurse i + 1.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort, use a start index, and skip a value equal to its left neighbour at the same depth.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Combinations summing to target, each candidate used once, no duplicate combinations.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Generate all then dedupe: wasteful.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort candidates.<\/li><li>For i from start: if i &gt; start and c[i] == c[i−1], skip.<\/li><li>Choose c[i], recurse with i + 1 and target − c[i]; break when c[i] &gt; target.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> path, start, remaining<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> candidates from start<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> remaining == 0<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> sorted break; skip same-level twins<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(2ⁿ · n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> It&#x27;s Combination Sum + Subsets II: recurse with i + 1 and skip i &gt; start duplicates.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def combination_sum2(candidates, target):\n    candidates.sort()\n    res, path = [], []\n    def backtrack(start, remaining):\n        if remaining == 0:\n            res.append(path[:])\n            return\n        for i in range(start, len(candidates)):\n            if i > start and candidates[i] == candidates[i - 1]:\n                continue                          # twin at this level\n            if candidates[i] > remaining:\n                break\n            path.append(candidates[i])\n            backtrack(i + 1, remaining - candidates[i])   # once each\n            path.pop()\n    backtrack(0, target)\n    return res","java":"List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\nList<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {\n    Arrays.sort(candidates);\n    backtrack(candidates, 0, target);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid backtrack(int[] cand, int start, int remaining) {\n    if (remaining == 0) {\n        res.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int i = start; i < cand.length; i++) {\n        if (i > start && cand[i] == cand[i - 1]) continue;   // twin at this level\n        if (cand[i] > remaining) break;\n        path.add(cand[i]);\n        backtrack(cand, i + 1, remaining - cand[i]);         // once each\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> combinationSum2(vector<int>& candidates, int target) {\n    sort(candidates.begin(), candidates.end());\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    vector<int> path;\n    function<void(int, int)> backtrack = [&](int start, int remaining) {\n        if (remaining == 0) { res.push_back(path); return; }\n        for (int i = start; i < (int)candidates.size(); i++) {\n            if (i > start && candidates[i] == candidates[i - 1]) continue;   // twin at this level\n            if (candidates[i] > remaining) break;\n            path.push_back(candidates[i]);\n            backtrack(i + 1, remaining - candidates[i]);                    // once each\n            path.pop_back();\n        }\n    };\n    backtrack(0, target);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-40"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/combination-sum-ii/"]],"ask":"Each candidate used at most once; candidates may repeat; no duplicate combinations."},{"i":"p-recursion-47","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Permutations II","sub":"LeetCode 47 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":47,"q":["Permutations II","how to solve Permutations II","Permutations II approach","Permutations II solution","leetcode 47","lc 47","All unique permutations of a list that may contain duplicates.","All distinct orderings of a list that has duplicates."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Order matters + duplicates → used[] and skip a twin whose earlier copy is unused. Complexity O(n · n!)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Order matters + duplicates → used[] and skip a twin whose earlier copy is unused.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort; skip using a duplicate unless its identical twin before it is already used.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> All distinct orderings of a list that has duplicates.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> All permutations then a set: wasteful.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort; used[] array.<\/li><li>Skip i if used[i], or nums[i] == nums[i−1] and not used[i−1].<\/li><li>Choose, recurse, un-choose; record at full length.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> path, used[]<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> any unused index<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> full length<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> skip a twin if the twin before it is unused<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · n!)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> not used[i-1] means the earlier twin was already tried at this level and released. Using this one now repeats that branch.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def permute_unique(nums):\n    nums.sort()\n    res, path = [], []\n    used = [False] * len(nums)\n    def backtrack():\n        if len(path) == len(nums):\n            res.append(path[:])\n            return\n        for i in range(len(nums)):\n            if used[i]:\n                continue\n            if i > 0 and nums[i] == nums[i - 1] and not used[i - 1]:\n                continue                  # same-level twin\n            used[i] = True\n            path.append(nums[i])\n            backtrack()\n            path.pop()\n            used[i] = False\n    backtrack()\n    return res","java":"List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\nList<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<List<Integer>> permuteUnique(int[] nums) {\n    Arrays.sort(nums);\n    backtrack(nums, new boolean[nums.length]);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid backtrack(int[] nums, boolean[] used) {\n    if (path.size() == nums.length) {\n        res.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        if (used[i]) continue;\n        if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1] && !used[i - 1]) continue;   // same-level twin\n        used[i] = true;\n        path.add(nums[i]);\n        backtrack(nums, used);\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n        used[i] = false;\n    }\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> permuteUnique(vector<int>& nums) {\n    sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    vector<int> path;\n    vector<bool> used(nums.size(), false);\n    function<void()> backtrack = [&]() {\n        if (path.size() == nums.size()) { res.push_back(path); return; }\n        for (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n            if (used[i]) continue;\n            if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1] && !used[i - 1]) continue;   // same-level twin\n            used[i] = true;\n            path.push_back(nums[i]);\n            backtrack();\n            path.pop_back();\n            used[i] = false;\n        }\n    };\n    backtrack();\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-47"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/permutations-ii/"]],"ask":"All unique permutations of a list that may contain duplicates."},{"i":"p-recursion-17","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Letter Combinations of a Phone Number","sub":"LeetCode 17 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":17,"q":["Letter Combinations of a Phone Number","how to solve Letter Combinations of a Phone Number","Letter Combinations of a Phone Number approach","Letter Combinations of a Phone Number solution","leetcode 17","lc 17","Digits 2–9 map to letters like a phone keypad. Return every possible string.","All letter strings a phone number's digits could spell."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking One slot per position → index i, loop that slot’s options. Complexity O(4ⁿ · n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> One slot per position → index i, loop that slot’s options.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> One level of recursion per digit; branch over its letters.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> All letter strings a phone number&#x27;s digits could spell.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Map digits to letters.<\/li><li>bt(i, path): if i == len → record.<\/li><li>For each letter of digits[i]: recurse with path + letter.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> path, digit index i<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> letters of digits[i]<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> i == len(digits)<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> none needed<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(4ⁿ · n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Empty input returns [], not [&quot;&quot;].<\/p>","c":{"py":"def letter_combinations(digits):\n    if not digits:\n        return []\n    pad = {\"2\": \"abc\", \"3\": \"def\", \"4\": \"ghi\", \"5\": \"jkl\",\n           \"6\": \"mno\", \"7\": \"pqrs\", \"8\": \"tuv\", \"9\": \"wxyz\"}\n    res, path = [], []\n    def backtrack(i):\n        if i == len(digits):\n            res.append(\"\".join(path))\n            return\n        for ch in pad[digits[i]]:\n            path.append(ch)\n            backtrack(i + 1)\n            path.pop()\n    backtrack(0)\n    return res","java":"static final String[] PAD = {\"\", \"\", \"abc\", \"def\", \"ghi\", \"jkl\", \"mno\", \"pqrs\", \"tuv\", \"wxyz\"};\nList<String> res = new ArrayList<>();\nStringBuilder path = new StringBuilder();\n\npublic List<String> letterCombinations(String digits) {\n    if (digits.isEmpty()) return res;\n    backtrack(digits, 0);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid backtrack(String digits, int i) {\n    if (i == digits.length()) {\n        res.add(path.toString());\n        return;\n    }\n    for (char ch : PAD[digits.charAt(i) - '0'].toCharArray()) {\n        path.append(ch);\n        backtrack(digits, i + 1);\n        path.deleteCharAt(path.length() - 1);\n    }\n}","cpp":"vector<string> letterCombinations(string digits) {\n    if (digits.empty()) return {};\n    const vector<string> pad = {\"\", \"\", \"abc\", \"def\", \"ghi\", \"jkl\", \"mno\", \"pqrs\", \"tuv\", \"wxyz\"};\n    vector<string> res;\n    string path;\n    function<void(int)> backtrack = [&](int i) {\n        if (i == (int)digits.size()) { res.push_back(path); return; }\n        for (char ch : pad[digits[i] - '0']) {\n            path.push_back(ch);\n            backtrack(i + 1);\n            path.pop_back();\n        }\n    };\n    backtrack(0);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-17"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/letter-combinations-of-a-phone-number/"]],"ask":"Digits 2–9 map to letters like a phone keypad. Return every possible string."},{"i":"p-recursion-131","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Palindrome Partitioning","sub":"LeetCode 131 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":131,"q":["Palindrome Partitioning","how to solve Palindrome Partitioning","Palindrome Partitioning approach","Palindrome Partitioning solution","leetcode 131","lc 131","Split a string so every piece is a palindrome. Return all splits.","Every way to cut the string so all pieces are palindromes."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Split a string → loop the end of the next piece. Complexity O(n · 2ⁿ)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Split a string → loop the end of the next piece.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Backtrack over cut positions: try each palindromic prefix, recurse on the rest.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Every way to cut the string so all pieces are palindromes.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>bt(start): start == n → record path.<\/li><li>For end from start: if s[start..end] is a palindrome, choose it.<\/li><li>Recurse from end + 1, then pop.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> path of pieces, start<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> every end after start<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> start == len(s)<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> piece is not a palindrome<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · 2ⁿ)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Precompute a palindrome table with DP if the string is long; slicing and reversing every time is O(n) per check.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def partition(s):\n    res, path = [], []\n    def backtrack(start):\n        if start == len(s):\n            res.append(path[:])\n            return\n        for end in range(start + 1, len(s) + 1):\n            piece = s[start:end]\n            if piece != piece[::-1]:\n                continue                  # not a palindrome: prune\n            path.append(piece)\n            backtrack(end)\n            path.pop()\n    backtrack(0)\n    return res","java":"List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();\nList<String> path = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<List<String>> partition(String s) {\n    backtrack(s, 0);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid backtrack(String s, int start) {\n    if (start == s.length()) {\n        res.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int end = start + 1; end <= s.length(); end++) {\n        String piece = s.substring(start, end);\n        if (!isPal(piece)) continue;                // not a palindrome: prune\n        path.add(piece);\n        backtrack(s, end);\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nboolean isPal(String p) {\n    for (int l = 0, r = p.length() - 1; l < r; l++, r--)\n        if (p.charAt(l) != p.charAt(r)) return false;\n    return true;\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<string>> partition(string s) {\n    vector<vector<string>> res;\n    vector<string> path;\n    function<void(int)> backtrack = [&](int start) {\n        if (start == (int)s.size()) { res.push_back(path); return; }\n        for (int end = start + 1; end <= (int)s.size(); end++) {\n            string piece = s.substr(start, end - start);\n            if (piece != string(piece.rbegin(), piece.rend())) continue;   // not a palindrome: prune\n            path.push_back(piece);\n            backtrack(end);\n            path.pop_back();\n        }\n    };\n    backtrack(0);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-131"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/palindrome-partitioning/"]],"ask":"Split a string so every piece is a palindrome. Return all splits."},{"i":"p-recursion-37","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Sudoku Solver","sub":"LeetCode 37 · Hard · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":37,"q":["Sudoku Solver","how to solve Sudoku Solver","Sudoku Solver approach","Sudoku Solver solution","leetcode 37","lc 37","Fill the 9×9 board in place.","Fill the Sudoku in place."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Placement under constraints → sets + return True to stop. Complexity Exponential, tiny in practice","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Placement under constraints → sets + return True to stop.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Backtracking with O(1) validity checks via row/column/box sets.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fill the Sudoku in place.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all digits everywhere without checks: astronomical.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Find the next empty cell.<\/li><li>Try digits 1–9 that aren&#x27;t in its row, column or box sets.<\/li><li>Place, recurse; if it fails, remove and try the next digit.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> board, sets for rows, cols, boxes<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> digits 1–9 for the next empty cell<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> no empty cells left<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> digit already in row, column or box<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> Exponential, tiny in practice<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Return True to stop at the first solution. Without it you&#x27;d undo the solved board on the way out.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def solve_sudoku(board):\n    rows = [set() for _ in range(9)]\n    cols = [set() for _ in range(9)]\n    boxes = [set() for _ in range(9)]\n    empty = []\n    for r in range(9):\n        for c in range(9):\n            v = board[r][c]\n            if v == \".\":\n                empty.append((r, c))\n            else:\n                rows[r].add(v); cols[c].add(v); boxes[r // 3 * 3 + c // 3].add(v)\n\n    def backtrack(k):\n        if k == len(empty):\n            return True                   # every blank is filled\n        r, c = empty[k]\n        b = r // 3 * 3 + c // 3\n        for v in \"123456789\":\n            if v in rows[r] or v in cols[c] or v in boxes[b]:\n                continue\n            board[r][c] = v\n            rows[r].add(v); cols[c].add(v); boxes[b].add(v)\n            if backtrack(k + 1):\n                return True               # stop at the first solution\n            board[r][c] = \".\"\n            rows[r].remove(v); cols[c].remove(v); boxes[b].remove(v)\n        return False\n\n    backtrack(0)","java":"boolean[][] rows = new boolean[9][10], cols = new boolean[9][10], boxes = new boolean[9][10];\nList<int[]> empty = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic void solveSudoku(char[][] board) {\n    for (int r = 0; r < 9; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < 9; c++) {\n            if (board[r][c] == '.') empty.add(new int[]{r, c});\n            else {\n                int v = board[r][c] - '0';\n                rows[r][v] = cols[c][v] = boxes[r / 3 * 3 + c / 3][v] = true;\n            }\n        }\n    backtrack(board, 0);\n}\n\nboolean backtrack(char[][] board, int k) {\n    if (k == empty.size()) return true;            // every blank is filled\n    int r = empty.get(k)[0], c = empty.get(k)[1], b = r / 3 * 3 + c / 3;\n    for (int v = 1; v <= 9; v++) {\n        if (rows[r][v] || cols[c][v] || boxes[b][v]) continue;\n        board[r][c] = (char) ('0' + v);\n        rows[r][v] = cols[c][v] = boxes[b][v] = true;\n        if (backtrack(board, k + 1)) return true;  // stop at the first solution\n        board[r][c] = '.';\n        rows[r][v] = cols[c][v] = boxes[b][v] = false;\n    }\n    return false;\n}","cpp":"void solveSudoku(vector<vector<char>>& board) {\n    bool rows[9][10] = {}, cols[9][10] = {}, boxes[9][10] = {};\n    vector<pair<int, int>> empty;\n    for (int r = 0; r < 9; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < 9; c++) {\n            if (board[r][c] == '.') empty.push_back({r, c});\n            else {\n                int v = board[r][c] - '0';\n                rows[r][v] = cols[c][v] = boxes[r / 3 * 3 + c / 3][v] = true;\n            }\n        }\n    function<bool(int)> backtrack = [&](int k) {\n        if (k == (int)empty.size()) return true;   // every blank is filled\n        auto [r, c] = empty[k];\n        int b = r / 3 * 3 + c / 3;\n        for (int v = 1; v <= 9; v++) {\n            if (rows[r][v] || cols[c][v] || boxes[b][v]) continue;\n            board[r][c] = '0' + v;\n            rows[r][v] = cols[c][v] = boxes[b][v] = true;\n            if (backtrack(k + 1)) return true;     // stop at the first solution\n            board[r][c] = '.';\n            rows[r][v] = cols[c][v] = boxes[b][v] = false;\n        }\n        return false;\n    };\n    backtrack(0);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-37"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/sudoku-solver/"]],"ask":"Fill the 9×9 board in place."},{"i":"p-recursion-784","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Letter Case Permutation","sub":"LeetCode 784 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":784,"q":["Letter Case Permutation","how to solve Letter Case Permutation","Letter Case Permutation approach","Letter Case Permutation solution","leetcode 784","lc 784","Return every string formed by changing each letter to lower or upper case.","Every string you can make by switching each letter's case."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking One slot per position → index i, loop that slot’s options. Complexity O(2ᴸ · n), L = letters","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> One slot per position → index i, loop that slot’s options.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Binary choice at each letter (lower/upper); digits have one choice.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Every string you can make by switching each letter&#x27;s case.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>bt(i, path).<\/li><li>Digit → recurse with it.<\/li><li>Letter → recurse with lower, then with upper.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> path, index i<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> lower/upper for letters, the char itself for digits<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> i == len(s)<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> none<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(2ᴸ · n), L = letters<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Digits have one choice, not two. A set avoids duplicates for non-letters.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def letter_case_permutation(s):\n    res, path = [], []\n    def backtrack(i):\n        if i == len(s):\n            res.append(\"\".join(path))\n            return\n        ch = s[i]\n        for c in ({ch.lower(), ch.upper()} if ch.isalpha() else {ch}):\n            path.append(c)\n            backtrack(i + 1)\n            path.pop()\n    backtrack(0)\n    return res","java":"List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<String> letterCasePermutation(String s) {\n    backtrack(s.toCharArray(), 0);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid backtrack(char[] cs, int i) {\n    if (i == cs.length) {\n        res.add(new String(cs));\n        return;\n    }\n    if (Character.isLetter(cs[i])) {\n        cs[i] = Character.toLowerCase(cs[i]);\n        backtrack(cs, i + 1);\n        cs[i] = Character.toUpperCase(cs[i]);    // the other choice\n        backtrack(cs, i + 1);\n    } else backtrack(cs, i + 1);                 // digits have one choice\n}","cpp":"vector<string> letterCasePermutation(string s) {\n    vector<string> res;\n    function<void(int)> backtrack = [&](int i) {\n        if (i == (int)s.size()) { res.push_back(s); return; }\n        if (isalpha(s[i])) {\n            s[i] = tolower(s[i]);\n            backtrack(i + 1);\n            s[i] = toupper(s[i]);                // the other choice\n            backtrack(i + 1);\n        } else backtrack(i + 1);                 // digits have one choice\n    };\n    backtrack(0);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-784"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/letter-case-permutation/"]],"ask":"Return every string formed by changing each letter to lower or upper case."},{"i":"p-recursion-494","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Target Sum","sub":"LeetCode 494 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":494,"q":["Target Sum","how to solve Target Sum","Target Sum approach","Target Sum solution","leetcode 494","lc 494","Put + or − in front of each number. How many ways reach the target?","Number of ways to put + or − before each number to reach target."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Count it → memo. List it → backtrack. Complexity O(n · range of sums) with memo","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Count it → memo. List it → backtrack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Memoize on (index, current sum); or convert to a subset-sum count.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Number of ways to put + or − before each number to reach target.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all 2ⁿ sign choices.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>dfs(i, total): i == n → 1 if total == target else 0.<\/li><li>Return dfs(i+1, total + x) + dfs(i+1, total − x).<\/li><li>Cache by (i, total).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> index i, running total<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> +nums[i] or −nums[i]<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> i == n and total == target<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> none; memo instead<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · range of sums) with memo<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> It asks “how many”, so states repeat. Plain backtracking is 2ⁿ; one decorator makes it polynomial.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from functools import lru_cache\n\ndef find_target_sum_ways(nums, target):\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def ways(i, total):\n        if i == len(nums):\n            return 1 if total == target else 0\n        return ways(i + 1, total + nums[i]) + ways(i + 1, total - nums[i])\n    return ways(0, 0)","java":"Map<String, Integer> memo = new HashMap<>();\n\npublic int findTargetSumWays(int[] nums, int target) {\n    return ways(nums, target, 0, 0);\n}\n\nint ways(int[] nums, int target, int i, int total) {\n    if (i == nums.length) return total == target ? 1 : 0;\n    String key = i + \",\" + total;\n    if (memo.containsKey(key)) return memo.get(key);\n    int r = ways(nums, target, i + 1, total + nums[i]) + ways(nums, target, i + 1, total - nums[i]);\n    memo.put(key, r);\n    return r;\n}","cpp":"int findTargetSumWays(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    map<pair<int, int>, int> memo;\n    function<int(int, int)> ways = [&](int i, int total) {\n        if (i == (int)nums.size()) return total == target ? 1 : 0;\n        auto key = make_pair(i, total);\n        if (memo.count(key)) return memo[key];\n        return memo[key] = ways(i + 1, total + nums[i]) + ways(i + 1, total - nums[i]);\n    };\n    return ways(0, 0);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-494"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/target-sum/"]],"ask":"Put + or − in front of each number. How many ways reach the target?"},{"i":"p-recursion-52","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"N-Queens II","sub":"LeetCode 52 · Hard · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":52,"q":["N-Queens II","how to solve N-Queens II","N-Queens II approach","N-Queens II solution","leetcode 52","lc 52","Return only the number of N-Queens solutions.","How many ways to place n queens with none attacking?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Constraint placement → sets for O(1) checks. Complexity O(n!)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Constraint placement → sets for O(1) checks.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> One queen per row; track used columns and both diagonals (r − c, r + c) in sets.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How many ways to place n queens with none attacking?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every placement: nⁿ.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>place(row): row == n → count += 1.<\/li><li>For each column not attacked: add to the three sets.<\/li><li>Recurse to row + 1, then remove.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> row r, sets of used cols and diagonals<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> columns of row r<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> r == n → count 1<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> attacked squares<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n!)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Return counts up the stack instead of storing boards. Less memory, same search.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def total_n_queens(n):\n    cols, diag, anti = set(), set(), set()\n    def backtrack(r):\n        if r == n:\n            return 1\n        count = 0\n        for c in range(n):\n            if c in cols or r - c in diag or r + c in anti:\n                continue\n            cols.add(c); diag.add(r - c); anti.add(r + c)\n            count += backtrack(r + 1)     # return counts, don't store boards\n            cols.remove(c); diag.remove(r - c); anti.remove(r + c)\n        return count\n    return backtrack(0)","java":"Set<Integer> cols = new HashSet<>(), diag = new HashSet<>(), anti = new HashSet<>();\n\npublic int totalNQueens(int n) {\n    return backtrack(n, 0);\n}\n\nint backtrack(int n, int r) {\n    if (r == n) return 1;\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n        if (cols.contains(c) || diag.contains(r - c) || anti.contains(r + c)) continue;\n        cols.add(c); diag.add(r - c); anti.add(r + c);\n        count += backtrack(n, r + 1);        // return counts, don't store boards\n        cols.remove(c); diag.remove(r - c); anti.remove(r + c);\n    }\n    return count;\n}","cpp":"int totalNQueens(int n) {\n    vector<bool> cols(n), diag(2 * n), anti(2 * n);\n    function<int(int)> backtrack = [&](int r) {\n        if (r == n) return 1;\n        int count = 0;\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n            if (cols[c] || diag[r - c + n] || anti[r + c]) continue;\n            cols[c] = diag[r - c + n] = anti[r + c] = true;\n            count += backtrack(r + 1);       // return counts, don't store boards\n            cols[c] = diag[r - c + n] = anti[r + c] = false;\n        }\n        return count;\n    };\n    return backtrack(0);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-52"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/n-queens-ii/"]],"ask":"Return only the number of N-Queens solutions."},{"i":"p-recursion-698","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Partition to K Equal Sum Subsets","sub":"LeetCode 698 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":698,"q":["Partition to K Equal Sum Subsets","how to solve Partition to K Equal Sum Subsets","Partition to K Equal Sum Subsets approach","Partition to K Equal Sum Subsets solution","leetcode 698","lc 698","Can the array be split into k groups with equal sums?","Can the numbers be split into k groups with equal sums?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Prune before you grow; order choices so failures come early. Complexity O(kⁿ) worst, fast in practice","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Prune before you grow; order choices so failures come early.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Target = total / k. Sort descending and fill buckets; prune empty-bucket repeats.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Can the numbers be split into k groups with equal sums?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Assign each number to any group: kⁿ.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If total % k ≠ 0 → False. Sort descending.<\/li><li>Place nums[i] into a bucket that won&#x27;t exceed target; recurse.<\/li><li>If a bucket stayed empty after failing, stop trying other empty buckets.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> index i, k bucket sums<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> which bucket gets nums[i]<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> all numbers placed<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> bucket would overflow; two buckets with the same sum are interchangeable<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(kⁿ) worst, fast in practice<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Sort descending so big numbers fail early. The same-sum-bucket skip removes symmetric duplicates. Matchsticks to Square (473) is this with k = 4.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def can_partition_k_subsets(nums, k):\n    total = sum(nums)\n    if total % k:\n        return False\n    target = total // k\n    nums.sort(reverse=True)               # big numbers fail fast\n    if nums[0] > target:\n        return False\n    buckets = [0] * k\n    def backtrack(i):\n        if i == len(nums):\n            return True                   # every number placed\n        tried = set()\n        for b in range(k):\n            if buckets[b] + nums[i] > target or buckets[b] in tried:\n                continue                  # overflow, or same-sum bucket already tried\n            tried.add(buckets[b])\n            buckets[b] += nums[i]\n            if backtrack(i + 1):\n                return True\n            buckets[b] -= nums[i]\n        return False\n    return backtrack(0)","java":"int[] nums, buckets;\nint target;\n\npublic boolean canPartitionKSubsets(int[] numsIn, int k) {\n    int total = Arrays.stream(numsIn).sum();\n    if (total % k != 0) return false;\n    target = total / k;\n    nums = Arrays.stream(numsIn).boxed().sorted(Collections.reverseOrder())\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();     // big numbers fail fast\n    if (nums[0] > target) return false;\n    buckets = new int[k];\n    return backtrack(0);\n}\n\nboolean backtrack(int i) {\n    if (i == nums.length) return true;       // every number placed\n    Set<Integer> tried = new HashSet<>();\n    for (int b = 0; b < buckets.length; b++) {\n        if (buckets[b] + nums[i] > target || !tried.add(buckets[b]))\n            continue;                        // overflow, or same-sum bucket already tried\n        buckets[b] += nums[i];\n        if (backtrack(i + 1)) return true;\n        buckets[b] -= nums[i];\n    }\n    return false;\n}","cpp":"bool canPartitionKSubsets(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    int total = accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0);\n    if (total % k) return false;\n    int target = total / k;\n    sort(nums.rbegin(), nums.rend());        // big numbers fail fast\n    if (nums[0] > target) return false;\n    vector<int> buckets(k, 0);\n    function<bool(int)> backtrack = [&](int i) {\n        if (i == (int)nums.size()) return true;   // every number placed\n        unordered_set<int> tried;\n        for (int b = 0; b < k; b++) {\n            if (buckets[b] + nums[i] > target || !tried.insert(buckets[b]).second)\n                continue;                    // overflow, or same-sum bucket already tried\n            buckets[b] += nums[i];\n            if (backtrack(i + 1)) return true;\n            buckets[b] -= nums[i];\n        }\n        return false;\n    };\n    return backtrack(0);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-698"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-to-k-equal-sum-subsets/"]],"ask":"Can the array be split into k groups with equal sums?"},{"i":"p-recursion-1219","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Path with Maximum Gold","sub":"LeetCode 1219 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":1219,"q":["Path with Maximum Gold","how to solve Path with Maximum Gold","Path with Maximum Gold approach","Path with Maximum Gold solution","leetcode 1219","lc 1219","Walk from any cell with gold through non-empty cells, each once. Maximize gold collected.","Walk from any cell through gold cells (each once) collecting the most gold."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Grid + use once → mark, explore 4, restore. Complexity O(R · C · 3ᵍ), g = gold cells","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Grid + use once → mark, explore 4, restore.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> DFS from every gold cell with temporary marking (backtracking on the grid).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Walk from any cell through gold cells (each once) collecting the most gold.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each cell with gold, dfs(r, c).<\/li><li>Take gold, set the cell to 0, explore 4 neighbours.<\/li><li>Restore the gold on the way back; return gold + best neighbour.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> cell (r, c)<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> 4 directions<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> return the best total from here<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> off-grid or empty cell<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C · 3ᵍ), g = gold cells<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Mark by setting the cell to 0, and restore it after. It doubles as the visited set.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def get_maximum_gold(grid):\n    R, C = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    def dfs(r, c):\n        if not (0 <= r < R and 0 <= c < C) or grid[r][c] == 0:\n            return 0\n        gold, grid[r][c] = grid[r][c], 0  # mark by emptying the cell\n        best = max(dfs(r + 1, c), dfs(r - 1, c), dfs(r, c + 1), dfs(r, c - 1))\n        grid[r][c] = gold                 # restore\n        return gold + best\n    return max(dfs(r, c) for r in range(R) for c in range(C))","java":"public int getMaximumGold(int[][] grid) {\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < grid[0].length; c++)\n            best = Math.max(best, dfs(grid, r, c));\n    return best;\n}\n\nint dfs(int[][] grid, int r, int c) {\n    if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= grid.length || c >= grid[0].length || grid[r][c] == 0) return 0;\n    int gold = grid[r][c];\n    grid[r][c] = 0;                          // mark by emptying the cell\n    int best = Math.max(Math.max(dfs(grid, r + 1, c), dfs(grid, r - 1, c)),\n                        Math.max(dfs(grid, r, c + 1), dfs(grid, r, c - 1)));\n    grid[r][c] = gold;                       // restore\n    return gold + best;\n}","cpp":"int getMaximumGold(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {\n    int R = grid.size(), C = grid[0].size();\n    function<int(int, int)> dfs = [&](int r, int c) {\n        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= R || c >= C || grid[r][c] == 0) return 0;\n        int gold = grid[r][c];\n        grid[r][c] = 0;                      // mark by emptying the cell\n        int best = max({dfs(r + 1, c), dfs(r - 1, c), dfs(r, c + 1), dfs(r, c - 1)});\n        grid[r][c] = gold;                   // restore\n        return gold + best;\n    };\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) best = max(best, dfs(r, c));\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-1219"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/path-with-maximum-gold/"]],"ask":"Walk from any cell with gold through non-empty cells, each once. Maximize gold collected."},{"i":"p-recursion-980","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Unique Paths III","sub":"LeetCode 980 · Hard · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":980,"q":["Unique Paths III","how to solve Unique Paths III","Unique Paths III approach","Unique Paths III solution","leetcode 980","lc 980","Count paths from start to end that walk over every empty square exactly once.","Count paths from start to end that step on every empty square exactly once."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Grid + use once → mark, explore 4, restore. Complexity O(3ᴿᶜ) worst","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Grid + use once → mark, explore 4, restore.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Grid backtracking counting remaining empty squares.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Count paths from start to end that step on every empty square exactly once.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Count empty squares (including start).<\/li><li>DFS: mark visited, decrement remaining.<\/li><li>At end with remaining 0 → 1 path. Unmark on return.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> cell, squares left to visit<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> 4 directions<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> reach the end with 0 left<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> walls, visited cells, off-grid<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(3ᴿᶜ) worst<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Count the start square in empty, or every path is off by one.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def unique_paths_iii(grid):\n    R, C = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    empty = 1                             # the start square counts too\n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            if grid[r][c] == 1:\n                sr, sc = r, c\n            elif grid[r][c] == 0:\n                empty += 1\n    def dfs(r, c, left):\n        if not (0 <= r < R and 0 <= c < C) or grid[r][c] == -1:\n            return 0\n        if grid[r][c] == 2:\n            return 1 if left == 0 else 0  # must have covered every square\n        grid[r][c] = -1                   # mark\n        total = (dfs(r + 1, c, left - 1) + dfs(r - 1, c, left - 1) +\n                 dfs(r, c + 1, left - 1) + dfs(r, c - 1, left - 1))\n        grid[r][c] = 0                    # unmark\n        return total\n    return dfs(sr, sc, empty)","java":"public int uniquePathsIII(int[][] grid) {\n    int empty = 1, sr = 0, sc = 0;            // the start square counts too\n    for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < grid[0].length; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == 1) { sr = r; sc = c; }\n            else if (grid[r][c] == 0) empty++;\n        }\n    return dfs(grid, sr, sc, empty);\n}\n\nint dfs(int[][] grid, int r, int c, int left) {\n    if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= grid.length || c >= grid[0].length || grid[r][c] == -1) return 0;\n    if (grid[r][c] == 2) return left == 0 ? 1 : 0;   // must have covered every square\n    grid[r][c] = -1;                          // mark\n    int total = dfs(grid, r + 1, c, left - 1) + dfs(grid, r - 1, c, left - 1)\n              + dfs(grid, r, c + 1, left - 1) + dfs(grid, r, c - 1, left - 1);\n    grid[r][c] = 0;                           // unmark\n    return total;\n}","cpp":"int uniquePathsIII(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {\n    int R = grid.size(), C = grid[0].size(), empty = 1, sr = 0, sc = 0;   // start counts too\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == 1) { sr = r; sc = c; }\n            else if (grid[r][c] == 0) empty++;\n        }\n    function<int(int, int, int)> dfs = [&](int r, int c, int left) {\n        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= R || c >= C || grid[r][c] == -1) return 0;\n        if (grid[r][c] == 2) return left == 0 ? 1 : 0;   // must have covered every square\n        grid[r][c] = -1;                      // mark\n        int total = dfs(r + 1, c, left - 1) + dfs(r - 1, c, left - 1)\n                  + dfs(r, c + 1, left - 1) + dfs(r, c - 1, left - 1);\n        grid[r][c] = 0;                       // unmark\n        return total;\n    };\n    return dfs(sr, sc, empty);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-980"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/unique-paths-iii/"]],"ask":"Count paths from start to end that walk over every empty square exactly once."},{"i":"p-recursion-212","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Word Search II","sub":"LeetCode 212 · Hard · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":212,"q":["Word Search II","how to solve Word Search II","Word Search II approach","Word Search II solution","leetcode 212","lc 212","Find every word from a list that appears on the board.","Find which listed words appear on the board."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Many words on one board → trie + grid backtracking. Complexity O(R · C · 4 · 3ᴸ⁻¹)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Many words on one board → trie + grid backtracking.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Trie of the words + one DFS over the board; prune when the path isn&#x27;t a prefix.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Find which listed words appear on the board.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> DFS per word: slow.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Build a trie.<\/li><li>DFS from each cell following trie children, marking cells.<\/li><li>Record words at word-end nodes; unmark on return.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> cell, current trie node<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> neighbours whose letter is a child in the trie<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> trie node marks a word<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> letter not in trie; delete exhausted trie branches<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C · 4 · 3ᴸ⁻¹)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Running Word Search (79) once per word is too slow. One trie lets every word share the same DFS.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_words(board, words):\n    trie = {}\n    for w in words:\n        node = trie\n        for ch in w:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n        node[\"$\"] = w                     # marks the end of a word\n    R, C, res = len(board), len(board[0]), []\n    def dfs(r, c, parent):\n        ch = board[r][c]\n        node = parent[ch]\n        if \"$\" in node:\n            res.append(node.pop(\"$\"))     # found; pop so it's reported once\n        board[r][c] = \"#\"                 # mark\n        for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n            if 0 <= nr < R and 0 <= nc < C and board[nr][nc] in node:\n                dfs(nr, nc, node)\n        board[r][c] = ch                  # unmark\n        if not node:\n            parent.pop(ch)                # prune a fully used trie branch\n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            if board[r][c] in trie:\n                dfs(r, c, trie)\n    return res","java":"static class TrieNode {\n    Map<Character, TrieNode> next = new HashMap<>();\n    String word;                              // marks the end of a word\n}\n\nList<String> res = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<String> findWords(char[][] board, String[] words) {\n    TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n    for (String w : words) {\n        TrieNode node = root;\n        for (char ch : w.toCharArray()) node = node.next.computeIfAbsent(ch, k -> new TrieNode());\n        node.word = w;\n    }\n    for (int r = 0; r < board.length; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < board[0].length; c++)\n            if (root.next.containsKey(board[r][c])) dfs(board, r, c, root);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid dfs(char[][] board, int r, int c, TrieNode parent) {\n    char ch = board[r][c];\n    TrieNode node = parent.next.get(ch);\n    if (node.word != null) {\n        res.add(node.word);\n        node.word = null;                     // found; clear so it's reported once\n    }\n    board[r][c] = '#';                        // mark\n    int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};\n    for (int[] d : dirs) {\n        int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n        if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < board.length && nc < board[0].length\n                && node.next.containsKey(board[nr][nc]))\n            dfs(board, nr, nc, node);\n    }\n    board[r][c] = ch;                         // unmark\n    if (node.next.isEmpty() && node.word == null)\n        parent.next.remove(ch);               // prune a fully used trie branch\n}","cpp":"struct TrieNode {\n    unordered_map<char, TrieNode*> next;\n    string word;                              // non-empty marks the end of a word\n};\n\nvector<string> findWords(vector<vector<char>>& board, vector<string>& words) {\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n    for (auto& w : words) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char ch : w) {\n            if (!node->next.count(ch)) node->next[ch] = new TrieNode();\n            node = node->next[ch];\n        }\n        node->word = w;\n    }\n    int R = board.size(), C = board[0].size();\n    vector<string> res;\n    function<void(int, int, TrieNode*)> dfs = [&](int r, int c, TrieNode* parent) {\n        char ch = board[r][c];\n        TrieNode* node = parent->next[ch];\n        if (!node->word.empty()) {\n            res.push_back(node->word);\n            node->word.clear();               // found; clear so it's reported once\n        }\n        board[r][c] = '#';                    // mark\n        int dr[] = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc[] = {0, 0, 1, -1};\n        for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < R && nc < C && node->next.count(board[nr][nc]))\n                dfs(nr, nc, node);\n        }\n        board[r][c] = ch;                     // unmark\n        if (node->next.empty() && node->word.empty())\n            parent->next.erase(ch);           // prune a fully used trie branch\n    };\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)\n            if (root->next.count(board[r][c])) dfs(r, c, root);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-212"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/word-search-ii/"]],"ask":"Find every word from a list that appears on the board."},{"i":"p-recursion-282","k":"prob","s":"recursion","t":"Expression Add Operators","sub":"LeetCode 282 · Hard · Recursion & Backtracking","cat":"DSA problems","n":282,"q":["Expression Add Operators","how to solve Expression Add Operators","Expression Add Operators approach","Expression Add Operators solution","leetcode 282","lc 282","Insert +, − or * between digits so the expression equals target. Return all expressions.","Insert +, − or * between digits to hit target. List all expressions."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Split a string → loop the end of the next piece. Complexity O(4ⁿ)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Split a string → loop the end of the next piece.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Backtrack over where each number ends; for *, undo the last term: value − last + last × x.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Insert +, − or * between digits to hit target. List all expressions.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>bt(i, expr, value, last).<\/li><li>Try every next number s[i..j] (no leading zeros).<\/li><li>Recurse with +, − (last = −x) and * (value − last + last·x, last = last·x).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> index, expression string, value, last term<\/li><li><b>Choices:<\/b> next number’s length, then which operator<\/li><li><b>Goal:<\/b> i == len(num) and value == target<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> numbers with a leading zero<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(4ⁿ)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> For *, undo the last term and re-add it multiplied: value − last + last * x. Strings are immutable, so there’s no explicit un-choose.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def add_operators(num, target):\n    res = []\n    def backtrack(i, expr, value, last):\n        if i == len(num):\n            if value == target:\n                res.append(expr)\n            return\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(num) + 1):\n            s = num[i:j]\n            if len(s) > 1 and s[0] == \"0\":\n                break                     # no numbers with leading zeros\n            x = int(s)\n            if i == 0:\n                backtrack(j, s, x, x)\n            else:\n                backtrack(j, expr + \"+\" + s, value + x, x)\n                backtrack(j, expr + \"-\" + s, value - x, -x)\n                # * binds tighter: undo the last term, re-add it multiplied\n                backtrack(j, expr + \"*\" + s, value - last + last * x, last * x)\n    backtrack(0, \"\", 0, 0)\n    return res","java":"List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<String> addOperators(String num, int target) {\n    backtrack(num, target, 0, \"\", 0, 0);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid backtrack(String num, int target, int i, String expr, long value, long last) {\n    if (i == num.length()) {\n        if (value == target) res.add(expr);\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int j = i + 1; j <= num.length(); j++) {\n        String s = num.substring(i, j);\n        if (s.length() > 1 && s.charAt(0) == '0') break;   // no numbers with leading zeros\n        long x = Long.parseLong(s);\n        if (i == 0) backtrack(num, target, j, s, x, x);\n        else {\n            backtrack(num, target, j, expr + \"+\" + s, value + x, x);\n            backtrack(num, target, j, expr + \"-\" + s, value - x, -x);\n            // * binds tighter: undo the last term, re-add it multiplied\n            backtrack(num, target, j, expr + \"*\" + s, value - last + last * x, last * x);\n        }\n    }\n}","cpp":"vector<string> addOperators(string num, int target) {\n    vector<string> res;\n    function<void(int, string, long long, long long)> backtrack =\n        [&](int i, string expr, long long value, long long last) {\n        if (i == (int)num.size()) {\n            if (value == target) res.push_back(expr);\n            return;\n        }\n        for (int j = i + 1; j <= (int)num.size(); j++) {\n            string s = num.substr(i, j - i);\n            if (s.size() > 1 && s[0] == '0') break;   // no numbers with leading zeros\n            long long x = stoll(s);\n            if (i == 0) backtrack(j, s, x, x);\n            else {\n                backtrack(j, expr + \"+\" + s, value + x, x);\n                backtrack(j, expr + \"-\" + s, value - x, -x);\n                // * binds tighter: undo the last term, re-add it multiplied\n                backtrack(j, expr + \"*\" + s, value - last + last * x, last * x);\n            }\n        }\n    };\n    backtrack(0, \"\", 0, 0);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","recursion.html#p-282"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/expression-add-operators/"]],"ask":"Insert +, − or * between digits so the expression equals target. Return all expressions."},{"i":"p-heap-703","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Kth Largest Element in a Stream","sub":"LeetCode 703 · Easy · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":703,"q":["Kth Largest Element in a Stream","how to solve Kth Largest Element in a Stream","Kth Largest Element in a Stream approach","Kth Largest Element in a Stream solution","leetcode 703","lc 703","Class that returns the kth largest after each add.","After each added number, report the kth largest seen so far."],"w":"Heap Kth largest → size-k min-heap. Complexity O(log k) per add","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Kth largest → size-k min-heap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Keep a min-heap of the k largest; its top is the answer.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> After each added number, report the kth largest seen so far.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort after every add: O(n log n) each.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Heap of at most k numbers.<\/li><li>add: push; if size &gt; k, pop the smallest.<\/li><li>Return heap[0].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Heap:<\/b> Min-heap.<\/li><li><b>Cap:<\/b> Size k.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> heap[0].<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log k) per add<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Initial nums may be fewer than k.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\nclass KthLargest:\n    def __init__(self, k, nums):\n        self.k, self.heap = k, []\n        for x in nums:\n            self.add(x)\n\n    def add(self, val):\n        heapq.heappush(self.heap, val)\n        if len(self.heap) > self.k:\n            heapq.heappop(self.heap)\n        return self.heap[0]","java":"class KthLargest {\n    private final int k;\n    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();\n\n    public KthLargest(int k, int[] nums) {\n        this.k = k;\n        for (int x : nums) add(x);\n    }\n\n    public int add(int val) {\n        heap.offer(val);\n        if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();\n        return heap.peek();\n    }\n}","cpp":"class KthLargest {\n    int k;\n    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> heap;\npublic:\n    KthLargest(int k, vector<int>& nums) : k(k) {\n        for (int x : nums) add(x);\n    }\n    int add(int val) {\n        heap.push(val);\n        if ((int)heap.size() > k) heap.pop();\n        return heap.top();\n    }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-703"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-largest-element-in-a-stream/"]],"ask":"Class that returns the kth largest after each add."},{"i":"p-heap-1046","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Last Stone Weight","sub":"LeetCode 1046 · Easy · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":1046,"q":["Last Stone Weight","how to solve Last Stone Weight","Last Stone Weight approach","Last Stone Weight solution","leetcode 1046","lc 1046","Smash the two heaviest stones until at most one remains.","Repeatedly smash the two heaviest stones; return the last weight (or 0)."],"w":"Heap Repeatedly take the two biggest → max-heap. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Repeatedly take the two biggest → max-heap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Max-heap gives the two heaviest instantly (negate values in Python).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Repeatedly smash the two heaviest stones; return the last weight (or 0).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort every round: O(n² log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Push −w for every stone.<\/li><li>Pop two; if different, push −(difference).<\/li><li>Return −heap[0] or 0.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Heap:<\/b> Max (negated).<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> Pop two, push the difference.<\/li><li><b>End:<\/b> 0 or the last stone.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Remember to negate back when reading.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef last_stone_weight(stones):\n    heap = [-s for s in stones]          # negate: heapq is a min-heap\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    while len(heap) > 1:\n        a = -heapq.heappop(heap)         # heaviest\n        b = -heapq.heappop(heap)         # second heaviest\n        if a != b:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, -(a - b))\n    return -heap[0] if heap else 0","java":"public int lastStoneWeight(int[] stones) {\n    PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());   // max-heap\n    for (int s : stones) heap.offer(s);\n    while (heap.size() > 1) {\n        int a = heap.poll();                  // heaviest\n        int b = heap.poll();                  // second heaviest\n        if (a != b) heap.offer(a - b);\n    }\n    return heap.isEmpty() ? 0 : heap.peek();\n}","cpp":"int lastStoneWeight(vector<int>& stones) {\n    priority_queue<int> heap(stones.begin(), stones.end());   // max-heap by default\n    while (heap.size() > 1) {\n        int a = heap.top(); heap.pop();       // heaviest\n        int b = heap.top(); heap.pop();       // second heaviest\n        if (a != b) heap.push(a - b);\n    }\n    return heap.empty() ? 0 : heap.top();\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-1046"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/last-stone-weight/"]],"ask":"Smash the two heaviest stones until at most one remains."},{"i":"p-heap-973","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"K Closest Points to Origin","sub":"LeetCode 973 · Medium · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":973,"q":["K Closest Points to Origin","how to solve K Closest Points to Origin","K Closest Points to Origin approach","K Closest Points to Origin solution","leetcode 973","lc 973","k points closest to (0, 0).","Return the k points nearest the origin."],"w":"Heap k smallest → size-k max-heap. Complexity O(n log k)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> k smallest → size-k max-heap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Keep a max-heap of size k by distance; drop the farthest when it grows.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Return the k points nearest the origin.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort all by distance: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each point push (−dist², point).<\/li><li>If size &gt; k, pop.<\/li><li>Return the points left.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Key:<\/b> x² + y² (no sqrt).<\/li><li><b>Heap:<\/b> Max-heap of size k.<\/li><li><b>Evict:<\/b> The farthest.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log k)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Skip sqrt; it doesn’t change the order.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef k_closest(points, k):\n    heap = []                                   # max-heap by distance (negated)\n    for x, y in points:\n        d = x * x + y * y\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-d, x, y))\n        if len(heap) > k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)                 # drop the farthest\n    return [[x, y] for _, x, y in heap]","java":"public int[][] kClosest(int[][] points, int k) {\n    // max-heap by distance: the farthest of the k sits on top\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(\n        (a, b) -> Integer.compare(b[0] * b[0] + b[1] * b[1], a[0] * a[0] + a[1] * a[1]));\n    for (int[] p : points) {\n        heap.offer(p);\n        if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();     // drop the farthest\n    }\n    return heap.toArray(new int[0][]);\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> kClosest(vector<vector<int>>& points, int k) {\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>> heap;      // max-heap of (distance, index)\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)points.size(); i++) {\n        int d = points[i][0] * points[i][0] + points[i][1] * points[i][1];\n        heap.push({d, i});\n        if ((int)heap.size() > k) heap.pop(); // drop the farthest\n    }\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    while (!heap.empty()) { res.push_back(points[heap.top().second]); heap.pop(); }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-973"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/k-closest-points-to-origin/"]],"ask":"k points closest to (0, 0)."},{"i":"p-heap-215","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Kth Largest Element in an Array","sub":"LeetCode 215 · Medium · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":215,"q":["Kth Largest Element in an Array","how to solve Kth Largest Element in an Array","Kth Largest Element in an Array approach","Kth Largest Element in an Array solution","leetcode 215","lc 215","kth largest element.","The kth largest number (not distinct)."],"w":"Heap Kth largest → size-k min-heap. Complexity O(n log k)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Kth largest → size-k min-heap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Min-heap of size k (or quickselect, average O(n)).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The kth largest number (not distinct).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Push each number.<\/li><li>If size &gt; k, pop the smallest.<\/li><li>Answer is heap[0].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Heap:<\/b> Min-heap.<\/li><li><b>Cap:<\/b> k.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Root.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log k)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Quickselect gives average O(n); mention it.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef find_kth_largest(nums, k):\n    heap = []                          # min-heap of the k largest so far\n    for x in nums:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, x)\n        if len(heap) > k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)        # evict the smallest of the k + 1\n    return heap[0]                     # weakest of the top k = kth largest","java":"public int findKthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {\n    PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();   // min-heap of the k largest so far\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        heap.offer(x);\n        if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();     // evict the smallest of the k + 1\n    }\n    return heap.peek();                       // weakest of the top k = kth largest\n}","cpp":"int findKthLargest(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> heap;   // min-heap of the k largest so far\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        heap.push(x);\n        if ((int)heap.size() > k) heap.pop(); // evict the smallest of the k + 1\n    }\n    return heap.top();                        // weakest of the top k = kth largest\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-215"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-largest-element-in-an-array/"]],"ask":"kth largest element."},{"i":"p-heap-347","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Top K Frequent Elements","sub":"LeetCode 347 · Medium · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":347,"q":["Top K Frequent Elements","how to solve Top K Frequent Elements","Top K Frequent Elements approach","Top K Frequent Elements solution","leetcode 347","lc 347","k most frequent elements.","Return the k most frequent numbers."],"w":"Heap Top k by score → heap. Complexity O(n log k)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Top k by score → heap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Count, then keep a min-heap of size k by count.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Return the k most frequent numbers.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort by frequency: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Count with a dict.<\/li><li>Push (count, num); pop when size &gt; k.<\/li><li>Return the nums in the heap.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Count:<\/b> Counter.<\/li><li><b>Select:<\/b> nlargest by count (a size-k heap).<\/li><li><b>Alt:<\/b> Bucket sort for O(n).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log k)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> nlargest is a size-k heap under the hood.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef top_k_frequent(nums, k):\n    count = Counter(nums)\n    return heapq.nlargest(k, count.keys(), key=count.get)","java":"public int[] topKFrequent(int[] nums, int k) {\n    Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();\n    for (int x : nums) count.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);\n    // min-heap by frequency, kept at size k\n    PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> count.get(a) - count.get(b));\n    for (int x : count.keySet()) {\n        heap.offer(x);\n        if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();\n    }\n    return heap.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();\n}","cpp":"vector<int> topKFrequent(vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> count;\n    for (int x : nums) count[x]++;\n    // min-heap of (frequency, value), kept at size k\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<>> heap;\n    for (auto& [x, c] : count) {\n        heap.push({c, x});\n        if ((int)heap.size() > k) heap.pop();\n    }\n    vector<int> res;\n    while (!heap.empty()) { res.push_back(heap.top().second); heap.pop(); }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-347"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/top-k-frequent-elements/"]],"ask":"k most frequent elements."},{"i":"p-heap-621","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Task Scheduler","sub":"LeetCode 621 · Medium · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":621,"q":["Task Scheduler","how to solve Task Scheduler","Task Scheduler approach","Task Scheduler solution","leetcode 621","lc 621","Min time units to run tasks with cooldown n between same tasks.","Run tasks with at least n idle slots between the same task. Minimum time?"],"w":"Heap Can’t repeat within n → heap + cooldown queue. Complexity O(T log 26)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Can’t repeat within n → heap + cooldown queue.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> The most frequent task decides the frame: (maxCount − 1) × (n + 1) + number of tasks with maxCount.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Run tasks with at least n idle slots between the same task. Minimum time?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Simulate with a priority queue: works but slower to write.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Count tasks; maxCount = highest count.<\/li><li>frames = (maxCount − 1) × (n + 1) + ties.<\/li><li>Answer = max(len(tasks), frames).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Heap:<\/b> Max by remaining count.<\/li><li><b>Cooldown:<\/b> Queue of (count, ready time).<\/li><li><b>Tick:<\/b> Run the best, release ready ones.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(T log 26)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> There’s also an O(T) counting formula: (maxf − 1) × (n + 1) + ties.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\nfrom collections import Counter, deque\n\ndef least_interval(tasks, n):\n    heap = [-c for c in Counter(tasks).values()]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    cooling = deque()                       # (count left, time it's ready)\n    time = 0\n    while heap or cooling:\n        time += 1\n        if heap:\n            c = heapq.heappop(heap) + 1     # run the most frequent task\n            if c:\n                cooling.append((c, time + n))\n        if cooling and cooling[0][1] == time:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, cooling.popleft()[0])\n    return time","java":"public int leastInterval(char[] tasks, int n) {\n    int[] freq = new int[26];\n    for (char t : tasks) freq[t - 'A']++;\n    PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());\n    for (int c : freq) if (c > 0) heap.offer(c);\n    Deque<int[]> cooling = new ArrayDeque<>();    // {count left, time it's ready}\n    int time = 0;\n    while (!heap.isEmpty() || !cooling.isEmpty()) {\n        time++;\n        if (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n            int c = heap.poll() - 1;              // run the most frequent task\n            if (c > 0) cooling.offer(new int[]{c, time + n});\n        }\n        if (!cooling.isEmpty() && cooling.peek()[1] == time)\n            heap.offer(cooling.poll()[0]);\n    }\n    return time;\n}","cpp":"int leastInterval(vector<char>& tasks, int n) {\n    int freq[26] = {};\n    for (char t : tasks) freq[t - 'A']++;\n    priority_queue<int> heap;\n    for (int c : freq) if (c > 0) heap.push(c);\n    queue<pair<int, int>> cooling;                // (count left, time it's ready)\n    int time = 0;\n    while (!heap.empty() || !cooling.empty()) {\n        time++;\n        if (!heap.empty()) {\n            int c = heap.top() - 1; heap.pop();   // run the most frequent task\n            if (c > 0) cooling.push({c, time + n});\n        }\n        if (!cooling.empty() && cooling.front().second == time) {\n            heap.push(cooling.front().first);\n            cooling.pop();\n        }\n    }\n    return time;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-621"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/task-scheduler/"]],"ask":"Min time units to run tasks with cooldown n between same tasks."},{"i":"p-heap-767","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Reorganize String","sub":"LeetCode 767 · Medium · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":767,"q":["Reorganize String","how to solve Reorganize String","Reorganize String approach","Reorganize String solution","leetcode 767","lc 767","No two adjacent characters equal.","Rearrange so no two neighbours are the same letter."],"w":"Heap Can’t repeat → heap + hold out. Complexity O(n log 26)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Can’t repeat → heap + hold out.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Always place the most frequent letter that isn&#x27;t the previous one (max-heap).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Rearrange so no two neighbours are the same letter.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try permutations: factorial.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If any count &gt; (n + 1) // 2 → &#x27;&#x27;.<\/li><li>Pop the top letter, append it, hold it out for one turn.<\/li><li>Push the previously held letter back; repeat.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Heap:<\/b> Max by count.<\/li><li><b>Hold:<\/b> Previous letter sits out a turn.<\/li><li><b>Fail:<\/b> Leftover held letter.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log 26)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Quick check: impossible if maxf &gt; (n + 1) // 2.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef reorganize_string(s):\n    heap = [(-c, ch) for ch, c in Counter(s).items()]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    res, prev = [], None                    # prev sits out for one turn\n    while heap:\n        c, ch = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        res.append(ch)\n        if prev:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, prev)      # the previous letter is allowed again\n        c += 1\n        prev = (c, ch) if c else None\n    return \"\".join(res) if not prev else \"\"","java":"public String reorganizeString(String s) {\n    int[] count = new int[26];\n    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) count[ch - 'a']++;\n    // max-heap of {count, letter}\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b[0] - a[0]);\n    for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) if (count[i] > 0) heap.offer(new int[]{count[i], i});\n    StringBuilder res = new StringBuilder();\n    int[] prev = null;                         // prev sits out for one turn\n    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n        int[] cur = heap.poll();\n        res.append((char) ('a' + cur[1]));\n        if (prev != null) heap.offer(prev);    // the previous letter is allowed again\n        cur[0]--;\n        prev = cur[0] > 0 ? cur : null;\n    }\n    return prev == null ? res.toString() : \"\";\n}","cpp":"string reorganizeString(string s) {\n    int count[26] = {};\n    for (char ch : s) count[ch - 'a']++;\n    priority_queue<pair<int, char>> heap;      // max-heap of (count, letter)\n    for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) if (count[i]) heap.push({count[i], 'a' + i});\n    string res;\n    pair<int, char> prev{0, 0};                // prev sits out for one turn\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        auto cur = heap.top(); heap.pop();\n        res += cur.second;\n        if (prev.first > 0) heap.push(prev);   // the previous letter is allowed again\n        cur.first--;\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev.first > 0 ? \"\" : res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-767"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/reorganize-string/"]],"ask":"No two adjacent characters equal."},{"i":"p-heap-355","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Design Twitter","sub":"LeetCode 355 · Medium · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":355,"q":["Design Twitter","how to solve Design Twitter","Design Twitter approach","Design Twitter solution","leetcode 355","lc 355","Post tweets, follow, and get the 10 most recent tweets from followed users.","Post, follow/unfollow, and fetch the 10 newest tweets from you and people you follow."],"w":"Heap Latest across many sorted lists → merge k with a heap. Complexity O(F + 10 log F)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Latest across many sorted lists → merge k with a heap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Each user&#x27;s tweets are already time-ordered; merge only the newest with a heap.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Post, follow/unfollow, and fetch the 10 newest tweets from you and people you follow.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Merge everyone&#x27;s tweets and sort: slow.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Store tweets per user as (time, id).<\/li><li>Push each followee&#x27;s latest tweet into a max-heap.<\/li><li>Pop 10 times, pushing that user&#x27;s next older tweet.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Store:<\/b> Per-user tweet lists with timestamps.<\/li><li><b>Feed:<\/b> Merge k sorted lists via heap.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> After 10.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(F + 10 log F)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> A user follows themselves for the feed.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom itertools import count\n\nclass Twitter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.clock = count(0, -1)              # decreasing: newest is smallest\n        self.tweets = defaultdict(list)        # user -> [(time, tweet_id)]\n        self.follows = defaultdict(set)\n\n    def postTweet(self, user, tweet_id):\n        self.tweets[user].append((next(self.clock), tweet_id))\n\n    def getNewsFeed(self, user):\n        heap = []\n        for u in self.follows[user] | {user}:\n            if self.tweets[u]:\n                i = len(self.tweets[u]) - 1    # each user's newest tweet\n                t, tid = self.tweets[u][i]\n                heap.append((t, tid, u, i))\n        heapq.heapify(heap)\n        feed = []\n        while heap and len(feed) < 10:\n            t, tid, u, i = heapq.heappop(heap)\n            feed.append(tid)\n            if i > 0:\n                nt, ntid = self.tweets[u][i - 1]\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (nt, ntid, u, i - 1))\n        return feed\n\n    def follow(self, a, b):\n        self.follows[a].add(b)\n\n    def unfollow(self, a, b):\n        self.follows[a].discard(b)","java":"class Twitter {\n    private int clock = 0;                                   // bigger = newer\n    private final Map<Integer, List<int[]>> tweets = new HashMap<>();   // user -> {time, tweetId}\n    private final Map<Integer, Set<Integer>> follows = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public void postTweet(int user, int tweetId) {\n        tweets.computeIfAbsent(user, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(new int[]{clock++, tweetId});\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> getNewsFeed(int user) {\n        // max-heap of {time, tweetId, user, index}\n        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b[0] - a[0]);\n        Set<Integer> people = new HashSet<>(follows.getOrDefault(user, Set.of()));\n        people.add(user);\n        for (int u : people) {\n            List<int[]> t = tweets.get(u);\n            if (t != null && !t.isEmpty()) {\n                int i = t.size() - 1;                        // each user's newest tweet\n                heap.offer(new int[]{t.get(i)[0], t.get(i)[1], u, i});\n            }\n        }\n        List<Integer> feed = new ArrayList<>();\n        while (!heap.isEmpty() && feed.size() < 10) {\n            int[] top = heap.poll();\n            feed.add(top[1]);\n            int u = top[2], i = top[3];\n            if (i > 0) {\n                int[] prev = tweets.get(u).get(i - 1);\n                heap.offer(new int[]{prev[0], prev[1], u, i - 1});\n            }\n        }\n        return feed;\n    }\n\n    public void follow(int a, int b) {\n        follows.computeIfAbsent(a, k -> new HashSet<>()).add(b);\n    }\n\n    public void unfollow(int a, int b) {\n        follows.getOrDefault(a, new HashSet<>()).remove(b);\n    }\n}","cpp":"class Twitter {\n    int clock = 0;                                           // bigger = newer\n    unordered_map<int, vector<pair<int, int>>> tweets;       // user -> (time, tweetId)\n    unordered_map<int, unordered_set<int>> follows;\npublic:\n    void postTweet(int user, int tweetId) {\n        tweets[user].push_back({clock++, tweetId});\n    }\n\n    vector<int> getNewsFeed(int user) {\n        priority_queue<array<int, 4>> heap;                  // max-heap of (time, tweetId, user, index)\n        auto people = follows[user];\n        people.insert(user);\n        for (int u : people) {\n            auto& t = tweets[u];\n            if (!t.empty()) {\n                int i = t.size() - 1;                        // each user's newest tweet\n                heap.push({t[i].first, t[i].second, u, i});\n            }\n        }\n        vector<int> feed;\n        while (!heap.empty() && feed.size() < 10) {\n            auto [time, tid, u, i] = heap.top(); heap.pop();\n            feed.push_back(tid);\n            if (i > 0) heap.push({tweets[u][i - 1].first, tweets[u][i - 1].second, u, i - 1});\n        }\n        return feed;\n    }\n\n    void follow(int a, int b) { follows[a].insert(b); }\n\n    void unfollow(int a, int b) { follows[a].erase(b); }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-355"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/design-twitter/"]],"ask":"Post tweets, follow, and get the 10 most recent tweets from followed users."},{"i":"p-heap-295","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Find Median from Data Stream","sub":"LeetCode 295 · Hard · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":295,"q":["Find Median from Data Stream","how to solve Find Median from Data Stream","Find Median from Data Stream approach","Find Median from Data Stream solution","leetcode 295","lc 295","addNum and findMedian.","Keep adding numbers; return the median any time."],"w":"Heap Running median → two heaps. Complexity O(log n) add, O(1) find","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Running median → two heaps.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Two heaps: a max-heap for the smaller half, a min-heap for the larger half.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Keep adding numbers; return the median any time.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort on every query: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Add to the max-heap, then move its top to the min-heap.<\/li><li>Rebalance so the max-heap has equal or one more.<\/li><li>Median = top of max-heap, or the average of both tops.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>lo:<\/b> Max-heap, smaller half.<\/li><li><b>hi:<\/b> Min-heap, larger half.<\/li><li><b>Balance:<\/b> len(lo) ∈ {len(hi), len(hi) + 1}.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(log n) add, O(1) find<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Always route through lo then hi to keep the order invariant.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\nclass MedianFinder:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.lo = []    # max-heap (negated): the smaller half\n        self.hi = []    # min-heap: the larger half\n\n    def addNum(self, x):\n        heapq.heappush(self.lo, -x)\n        heapq.heappush(self.hi, -heapq.heappop(self.lo))     # lo's max moves up\n        if len(self.hi) > len(self.lo):\n            heapq.heappush(self.lo, -heapq.heappop(self.hi)) # rebalance\n\n    def findMedian(self):\n        if len(self.lo) > len(self.hi):\n            return -self.lo[0]\n        return (-self.lo[0] + self.hi[0]) / 2","java":"class MedianFinder {\n    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> lo = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());   // max-heap: smaller half\n    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> hi = new PriorityQueue<>();                             // min-heap: larger half\n\n    public void addNum(int x) {\n        lo.offer(x);\n        hi.offer(lo.poll());                  // lo's max moves up\n        if (hi.size() > lo.size()) lo.offer(hi.poll());   // rebalance\n    }\n\n    public double findMedian() {\n        if (lo.size() > hi.size()) return lo.peek();\n        return (lo.peek() + (double) hi.peek()) / 2;\n    }\n}","cpp":"class MedianFinder {\n    priority_queue<int> lo;                                 // max-heap: smaller half\n    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> hi;      // min-heap: larger half\npublic:\n    void addNum(int x) {\n        lo.push(x);\n        hi.push(lo.top()); lo.pop();          // lo's max moves up\n        if (hi.size() > lo.size()) { lo.push(hi.top()); hi.pop(); }   // rebalance\n    }\n    double findMedian() {\n        if (lo.size() > hi.size()) return lo.top();\n        return (lo.top() + (double)hi.top()) / 2;\n    }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-295"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/find-median-from-data-stream/"]],"ask":"addNum and findMedian."},{"i":"p-heap-23","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Merge k Sorted Lists","sub":"LeetCode 23 · Hard · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":23,"q":["Merge k Sorted Lists","how to solve Merge k Sorted Lists","Merge k Sorted Lists approach","Merge k Sorted Lists solution","leetcode 23","lc 23","Merge k sorted linked lists.","Merge k sorted lists into one."],"w":"Heap Merge k → heap of frontiers. Complexity O(N log k)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Merge k → heap of frontiers.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Min-heap of the current heads.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Merge k sorted lists into one.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Merge pairwise one by one: O(k·N).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Push each head.<\/li><li>Pop smallest, attach, push its next.<\/li><li>Until empty.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Heap:<\/b> (val, i, node).<\/li><li><b>Pop:<\/b> Attach.<\/li><li><b>Push:<\/b> Next of that list.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(N log k)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Tiebreak with i so nodes are never compared.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef merge_k_lists(lists):\n    heap = [(node.val, i, node) for i, node in enumerate(lists) if node]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)                   # i breaks ties between equal values\n    dummy = tail = ListNode()\n    while heap:\n        _, i, node = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        tail.next = tail = node\n        if node.next:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (node.next.val, i, node.next))\n    return dummy.next","java":"public ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {\n    PriorityQueue<ListNode> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((x, y) -> Integer.compare(x.val, y.val));\n    for (ListNode node : lists) if (node != null) heap.offer(node);\n    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(), tail = dummy;\n    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n        ListNode node = heap.poll();          // smallest head of all lists\n        tail.next = node;\n        tail = node;\n        if (node.next != null) heap.offer(node.next);\n    }\n    return dummy.next;\n}","cpp":"ListNode* mergeKLists(vector<ListNode*>& lists) {\n    auto cmp = [](ListNode* x, ListNode* y) { return x->val > y->val; };   // min-heap\n    priority_queue<ListNode*, vector<ListNode*>, decltype(cmp)> heap(cmp);\n    for (auto node : lists) if (node) heap.push(node);\n    ListNode dummy, *tail = &dummy;\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        ListNode* node = heap.top(); heap.pop();   // smallest head of all lists\n        tail->next = node;\n        tail = node;\n        if (node->next) heap.push(node->next);\n    }\n    return dummy.next;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-23"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-k-sorted-lists/"]],"ask":"Merge k sorted linked lists."},{"i":"p-heap-502","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"IPO","sub":"LeetCode 502 · Hard · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":502,"q":["IPO","how to solve IPO","IPO approach","IPO solution","leetcode 502","lc 502","Maximize capital after at most k projects.","Start with w capital; pick at most k projects (each needs capital, gives profit). Maximize capital."],"w":"Heap Unlock + best available → sort + heap. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Unlock + best available → sort + heap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Greedy: among the projects you can afford now, take the most profitable.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Start with w capital; pick at most k projects (each needs capital, gives profit). Maximize capital.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all orders: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort projects by required capital.<\/li><li>Each round, push every newly affordable project&#x27;s profit into a max-heap.<\/li><li>Take the top profit (if any); repeat k times.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> By capital needed.<\/li><li><b>Unlock:<\/b> Push affordable profits.<\/li><li><b>Pick:<\/b> Pop the max profit.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Break when nothing is affordable.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef find_maximized_capital(k, w, profits, capital):\n    projects = sorted(zip(capital, profits))     # by capital needed\n    heap, i = [], 0                              # max-heap of affordable profits\n    for _ in range(k):\n        while i < len(projects) and projects[i][0] <= w:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, -projects[i][1])\n            i += 1\n        if not heap:\n            break\n        w -= heapq.heappop(heap)                 # take the best affordable one\n    return w","java":"public int findMaximizedCapital(int k, int w, int[] profits, int[] capital) {\n    int n = profits.length;\n    Integer[] order = new Integer[n];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) order[i] = i;\n    Arrays.sort(order, (a, b) -> capital[a] - capital[b]);   // by capital needed\n    PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());   // affordable profits\n    int i = 0;\n    for (int round = 0; round < k; round++) {\n        while (i < n && capital[order[i]] <= w) heap.offer(profits[order[i++]]);\n        if (heap.isEmpty()) break;\n        w += heap.poll();                        // take the best affordable one\n    }\n    return w;\n}","cpp":"int findMaximizedCapital(int k, int w, vector<int>& profits, vector<int>& capital) {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> projects;             // (capital needed, profit)\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < profits.size(); i++) projects.push_back({capital[i], profits[i]});\n    sort(projects.begin(), projects.end());\n    priority_queue<int> heap;                    // max-heap of affordable profits\n    size_t i = 0;\n    for (int round = 0; round < k; round++) {\n        while (i < projects.size() && projects[i].first <= w) heap.push(projects[i++].second);\n        if (heap.empty()) break;\n        w += heap.top(); heap.pop();             // take the best affordable one\n    }\n    return w;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-502"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/ipo/"]],"ask":"Maximize capital after at most k projects."},{"i":"p-heap-1642","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Furthest Building You Can Reach","sub":"LeetCode 1642 · Medium · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":1642,"q":["Furthest Building You Can Reach","how to solve Furthest Building You Can Reach","Furthest Building You Can Reach approach","Furthest Building You Can Reach solution","leetcode 1642","lc 1642","Use bricks or ladders for climbs. How far can you go?","Climb buildings using bricks or ladders. How far can you get?"],"w":"Heap Save the best resource for the biggest needs → min-heap of size ladders. Complexity O(n log L)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Save the best resource for the biggest needs → min-heap of size ladders.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Use ladders on the biggest climbs. A min-heap of climbs: when it exceeds ladder count, pay for the smallest with bricks.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Climb buildings using bricks or ladders. How far can you get?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every assignment: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each climb &gt; 0, push it.<\/li><li>If heap size &gt; ladders, pop the smallest and spend bricks.<\/li><li>Bricks &lt; 0 → return the current index.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Idea:<\/b> Ladders for the biggest climbs.<\/li><li><b>Heap:<\/b> Min-heap of climbs given ladders.<\/li><li><b>Over:<\/b> Brick the smallest one.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log L)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Only positive climbs count.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef furthest_building(heights, bricks, ladders):\n    heap = []                                   # climbs covered by ladders\n    for i in range(len(heights) - 1):\n        d = heights[i + 1] - heights[i]\n        if d <= 0:\n            continue\n        heapq.heappush(heap, d)\n        if len(heap) > ladders:                 # out of ladders: brick the smallest\n            bricks -= heapq.heappop(heap)\n            if bricks < 0:\n                return i\n    return len(heights) - 1","java":"public int furthestBuilding(int[] heights, int bricks, int ladders) {\n    PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();   // climbs covered by ladders\n    for (int i = 0; i < heights.length - 1; i++) {\n        int d = heights[i + 1] - heights[i];\n        if (d <= 0) continue;\n        heap.offer(d);\n        if (heap.size() > ladders) {          // out of ladders: brick the smallest\n            bricks -= heap.poll();\n            if (bricks < 0) return i;\n        }\n    }\n    return heights.length - 1;\n}","cpp":"int furthestBuilding(vector<int>& heights, int bricks, int ladders) {\n    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> heap;   // climbs covered by ladders\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)heights.size() - 1; i++) {\n        int d = heights[i + 1] - heights[i];\n        if (d <= 0) continue;\n        heap.push(d);\n        if ((int)heap.size() > ladders) {     // out of ladders: brick the smallest\n            bricks -= heap.top(); heap.pop();\n            if (bricks < 0) return i;\n        }\n    }\n    return heights.size() - 1;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-1642"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/furthest-building-you-can-reach/"]],"ask":"Use bricks or ladders for climbs. How far can you go?"},{"i":"p-heap-253","k":"prob","s":"heap","t":"Meeting Rooms II","sub":"LeetCode 253 · Medium · Heap","cat":"DSA problems","n":253,"q":["Meeting Rooms II","how to solve Meeting Rooms II","Meeting Rooms II approach","Meeting Rooms II solution","leetcode 253","lc 253","Minimum rooms for a set of meetings.","Minimum number of rooms so no meetings overlap in a room."],"w":"Heap Rooms in use → min-heap of end times. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Rooms in use → min-heap of end times.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort by start; a min-heap of end times tells you if the earliest-ending room is free.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Minimum number of rooms so no meetings overlap in a room.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check every pair of meetings: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort by start.<\/li><li>If heap[0] ≤ start, pop (reuse that room).<\/li><li>Push this meeting&#x27;s end. Answer = heap size.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> By start.<\/li><li><b>Heap:<\/b> End times of busy rooms.<\/li><li><b>Reuse:<\/b> If the earliest end ≤ start.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> heapreplace pops and pushes in one step.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef min_meeting_rooms(intervals):\n    intervals.sort()\n    ends = []                                   # end times of rooms in use\n    for s, e in intervals:\n        if ends and ends[0] <= s:\n            heapq.heapreplace(ends, e)          # reuse the room that frees first\n        else:\n            heapq.heappush(ends, e)\n    return len(ends)","java":"public int minMeetingRooms(int[][] intervals) {\n    Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);\n    PriorityQueue<Integer> ends = new PriorityQueue<>();   // end times of rooms in use\n    for (int[] m : intervals) {\n        if (!ends.isEmpty() && ends.peek() <= m[0]) ends.poll();   // reuse the room that frees first\n        ends.offer(m[1]);\n    }\n    return ends.size();\n}","cpp":"int minMeetingRooms(vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {\n    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end());\n    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> ends;   // end times of rooms in use\n    for (auto& m : intervals) {\n        if (!ends.empty() && ends.top() <= m[0]) ends.pop();   // reuse the room that frees first\n        ends.push(m[1]);\n    }\n    return ends.size();\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","heap.html#p-253"],["LeetCode (Premium)","https://leetcode.com/problems/meeting-rooms-ii/"]],"ask":"Minimum rooms for a set of meetings."},{"i":"p-trie-208","k":"prob","s":"trie","t":"Implement Trie (Prefix Tree)","sub":"LeetCode 208 · Medium · Trie","cat":"DSA problems","n":208,"q":["Implement Trie (Prefix Tree)","how to solve Implement Trie (Prefix Tree)","Implement Trie (Prefix Tree) approach","Implement Trie (Prefix Tree) solution","leetcode 208","lc 208","insert, search and startsWith.","Build a structure with insert, search(word) and startsWith(prefix)."],"w":"Trie Prefix structure → trie node with end flag. Complexity O(L) each","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Prefix structure → trie node with end flag.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A tree of characters: each path from the root spells a prefix; mark word ends.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Build a structure with insert, search(word) and startsWith(prefix).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Store words in a list and scan: O(N·L).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>insert: walk/create one child per character, mark the end.<\/li><li>search: walk; True if path exists and the end is marked.<\/li><li>startsWith: True if the path exists.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Node:<\/b> children + end.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> Shared helper.<\/li><li><b>Differ:<\/b> search checks end.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(L) each<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Forgetting the end flag makes search behave like startsWith.<\/p>","c":{"py":"class TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.children = {}               # char -> TrieNode\n        self.end = False                 # a word ends here\n\nclass Trie:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n\n    def insert(self, word):\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in word:\n            if ch not in node.children:\n                node.children[ch] = TrieNode()   # grow a new branch\n            node = node.children[ch]\n        node.end = True\n\n    def _walk(self, s):\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in s:\n            if ch not in node.children:\n                return None              # path breaks: no such prefix\n            node = node.children[ch]\n        return node\n\n    def search(self, word):\n        node = self._walk(word)\n        return node is not None and node.end     # must END here\n\n    def startsWith(self, prefix):\n        return self._walk(prefix) is not None    # any path will do","java":"class TrieNode {\n    TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];   // one slot per letter\n    boolean end;                              // a word ends here\n}\n\nclass Trie {\n    private final TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n\n    public void insert(String word) {\n        TrieNode node = root;\n        for (char ch : word.toCharArray()) {\n            int i = ch - 'a';\n            if (node.children[i] == null) node.children[i] = new TrieNode();   // grow a new branch\n            node = node.children[i];\n        }\n        node.end = true;\n    }\n\n    private TrieNode walk(String s) {\n        TrieNode node = root;\n        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n            node = node.children[ch - 'a'];\n            if (node == null) return null;    // path breaks: no such prefix\n        }\n        return node;\n    }\n\n    public boolean search(String word) {\n        TrieNode node = walk(word);\n        return node != null && node.end;      // must END here\n    }\n\n    public boolean startsWith(String prefix) {\n        return walk(prefix) != null;          // any path will do\n    }\n}","cpp":"struct TrieNode {\n    TrieNode* children[26] = {};              // one slot per letter\n    bool end = false;                         // a word ends here\n};\n\nclass Trie {\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n\n    TrieNode* walk(const string& s) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char ch : s) {\n            node = node->children[ch - 'a'];\n            if (!node) return nullptr;        // path breaks: no such prefix\n        }\n        return node;\n    }\npublic:\n    void insert(string word) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char ch : word) {\n            int i = ch - 'a';\n            if (!node->children[i]) node->children[i] = new TrieNode();   // grow a new branch\n            node = node->children[i];\n        }\n        node->end = true;\n    }\n    bool search(string word) {\n        TrieNode* node = walk(word);\n        return node && node->end;             // must END here\n    }\n    bool startsWith(string prefix) {\n        return walk(prefix) != nullptr;       // any path will do\n    }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","trie.html#p-208"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/implement-trie-prefix-tree/"]],"ask":"insert, search and startsWith."},{"i":"p-trie-211","k":"prob","s":"trie","t":"Design Add and Search Words","sub":"LeetCode 211 · Medium · Trie","cat":"DSA problems","n":211,"q":["Design Add and Search Words","how to solve Design Add and Search Words","Design Add and Search Words approach","Design Add and Search Words solution","leetcode 211","lc 211","Support “.” matching any letter in search.","Like a trie, but search may contain '.' matching any letter."],"w":"Trie Wildcard → DFS all children. Complexity O(L) insert, up to O(26ᵈ L) search","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Wildcard → DFS all children.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Trie + DFS: on &#x27;.&#x27;, try every child.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Like a trie, but search may contain &#x27;.&#x27; matching any letter.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan all words: slow.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Insert as usual.<\/li><li>search(i, node): if word[i] is &#x27;.&#x27;, recurse into every child.<\/li><li>Else follow that one child.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Insert:<\/b> Normal.<\/li><li><b>Search:<\/b> DFS.<\/li><li><b>Dot:<\/b> Try all children.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(L) insert, up to O(26ᵈ L) search<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Return node.end at the end of the pattern, not True.<\/p>","c":{"py":"class WordDictionary:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n\n    def addWord(self, word):\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in word:\n            node = node.children.setdefault(ch, TrieNode())\n        node.end = True\n\n    def search(self, word):\n        def dfs(node, i):\n            if i == len(word):\n                return node.end\n            ch = word[i]\n            if ch == \".\":\n                return any(dfs(child, i + 1) for child in node.children.values())\n            if ch not in node.children:\n                return False\n            return dfs(node.children[ch], i + 1)\n        return dfs(self.root, 0)","java":"class WordDictionary {\n    private static class TrieNode {\n        TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];\n        boolean end;\n    }\n    private final TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n\n    public void addWord(String word) {\n        TrieNode node = root;\n        for (char ch : word.toCharArray()) {\n            int i = ch - 'a';\n            if (node.children[i] == null) node.children[i] = new TrieNode();\n            node = node.children[i];\n        }\n        node.end = true;\n    }\n\n    public boolean search(String word) {\n        return dfs(root, word, 0);\n    }\n\n    private boolean dfs(TrieNode node, String word, int i) {\n        if (i == word.length()) return node.end;\n        char ch = word.charAt(i);\n        if (ch == '.') {                          // wildcard: try every child\n            for (TrieNode child : node.children)\n                if (child != null && dfs(child, word, i + 1)) return true;\n            return false;\n        }\n        TrieNode next = node.children[ch - 'a'];\n        return next != null && dfs(next, word, i + 1);\n    }\n}","cpp":"class WordDictionary {\n    struct TrieNode {\n        TrieNode* children[26] = {};\n        bool end = false;\n    };\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n\n    bool dfs(TrieNode* node, const string& word, int i) {\n        if (i == (int)word.size()) return node->end;\n        char ch = word[i];\n        if (ch == '.') {                          // wildcard: try every child\n            for (auto child : node->children)\n                if (child && dfs(child, word, i + 1)) return true;\n            return false;\n        }\n        TrieNode* next = node->children[ch - 'a'];\n        return next && dfs(next, word, i + 1);\n    }\npublic:\n    void addWord(string word) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char ch : word) {\n            int i = ch - 'a';\n            if (!node->children[i]) node->children[i] = new TrieNode();\n            node = node->children[i];\n        }\n        node->end = true;\n    }\n    bool search(string word) { return dfs(root, word, 0); }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","trie.html#p-211"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/design-add-and-search-words-data-structure/"]],"ask":"Support “.” matching any letter in search."},{"i":"p-trie-212","k":"prob","s":"trie","t":"Word Search II","sub":"LeetCode 212 · Hard · Trie","cat":"DSA problems","n":212,"q":["Word Search II","how to solve Word Search II","Word Search II approach","Word Search II solution","leetcode 212","lc 212","Find all dictionary words in a letter grid.","Find every listed word that can be traced on the letter grid."],"w":"Trie Many words, one board → trie + grid DFS. Complexity O(R · C · 4 · 3ᴸ⁻¹)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Many words, one board → trie + grid DFS.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Put all words in a trie and DFS the grid once; stop when the path isn&#x27;t a prefix.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Find every listed word that can be traced on the letter grid.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Run a DFS for each word: O(words × cells × 4^L).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Build a trie of the words.<\/li><li>DFS from every cell, following trie children.<\/li><li>When a node marks a word, record it (and remove it to avoid duplicates).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Trie:<\/b> All words.<\/li><li><b>DFS:<\/b> From each cell, following trie children.<\/li><li><b>Prune:<\/b> Pop found words and empty branches.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C · 4 · 3ᴸ⁻¹)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Running Word Search (79) per word times out.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_words(board, words):\n    trie = {}\n    for w in words:\n        node = trie\n        for ch in w:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n        node[\"$\"] = w                     # marks the end of a word\n    R, C, res = len(board), len(board[0]), []\n    def dfs(r, c, parent):\n        ch = board[r][c]\n        node = parent[ch]\n        if \"$\" in node:\n            res.append(node.pop(\"$\"))     # found; pop so it's reported once\n        board[r][c] = \"#\"                 # mark\n        for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n            if 0 <= nr < R and 0 <= nc < C and board[nr][nc] in node:\n                dfs(nr, nc, node)\n        board[r][c] = ch                  # unmark\n        if not node:\n            parent.pop(ch)                # prune a fully used branch\n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            if board[r][c] in trie:\n                dfs(r, c, trie)\n    return res","java":"static class TrieNode {\n    TrieNode[] next = new TrieNode[26];\n    String word;                              // marks the end of a word\n}\n\nList<String> res = new ArrayList<>();\n\npublic List<String> findWords(char[][] board, String[] words) {\n    TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n    for (String w : words) {\n        TrieNode node = root;\n        for (char ch : w.toCharArray()) {\n            if (node.next[ch - 'a'] == null) node.next[ch - 'a'] = new TrieNode();\n            node = node.next[ch - 'a'];\n        }\n        node.word = w;\n    }\n    for (int r = 0; r < board.length; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < board[0].length; c++)\n            dfs(board, r, c, root);\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid dfs(char[][] board, int r, int c, TrieNode parent) {\n    if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= board.length || c >= board[0].length) return;\n    char ch = board[r][c];\n    if (ch == '#' || parent.next[ch - 'a'] == null) return;\n    TrieNode node = parent.next[ch - 'a'];\n    if (node.word != null) {\n        res.add(node.word);\n        node.word = null;                     // found; clear so it's reported once\n    }\n    board[r][c] = '#';                        // mark\n    dfs(board, r + 1, c, node); dfs(board, r - 1, c, node);\n    dfs(board, r, c + 1, node); dfs(board, r, c - 1, node);\n    board[r][c] = ch;                         // unmark\n}","cpp":"struct TrieNode {\n    TrieNode* next[26] = {};\n    string word;                              // non-empty marks the end of a word\n};\n\nvector<string> findWords(vector<vector<char>>& board, vector<string>& words) {\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n    for (auto& w : words) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char ch : w) {\n            if (!node->next[ch - 'a']) node->next[ch - 'a'] = new TrieNode();\n            node = node->next[ch - 'a'];\n        }\n        node->word = w;\n    }\n    int R = board.size(), C = board[0].size();\n    vector<string> res;\n    function<void(int, int, TrieNode*)> dfs = [&](int r, int c, TrieNode* parent) {\n        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= R || c >= C) return;\n        char ch = board[r][c];\n        if (ch == '#' || !parent->next[ch - 'a']) return;\n        TrieNode* node = parent->next[ch - 'a'];\n        if (!node->word.empty()) {\n            res.push_back(node->word);\n            node->word.clear();               // found; clear so it's reported once\n        }\n        board[r][c] = '#';                    // mark\n        dfs(r + 1, c, node); dfs(r - 1, c, node);\n        dfs(r, c + 1, node); dfs(r, c - 1, node);\n        board[r][c] = ch;                     // unmark\n    };\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) dfs(r, c, root);\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","trie.html#p-212"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/word-search-ii/"]],"ask":"Find all dictionary words in a letter grid."},{"i":"p-trie-14","k":"prob","s":"trie","t":"Longest Common Prefix","sub":"LeetCode 14 · Easy · Trie","cat":"DSA problems","n":14,"q":["Longest Common Prefix","how to solve Longest Common Prefix","Longest Common Prefix approach","Longest Common Prefix solution","leetcode 14","lc 14","Longest common prefix of all strings.","Longest prefix shared by every string."],"w":"Trie Shared beginning → walk the single path. Complexity O(total characters)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Shared beginning → walk the single path.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Vertical scan: check column i across all strings until a mismatch.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Longest prefix shared by every string.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Compare all strings character by character: fine, O(total chars).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Take the first string as reference.<\/li><li>For i, check every string has the same char at i.<\/li><li>Stop at the first mismatch or end.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Trie:<\/b> Of all words.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> While exactly one child.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> At a word end too.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(total characters)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Sorting and comparing the first and last strings is simpler; know both.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def longest_common_prefix(strs):\n    root = {}\n    for w in strs:                      # build a trie of all words\n        node = root\n        for ch in w:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n        node[\"$\"] = True\n    prefix, node = [], root\n    while len(node) == 1 and \"$\" not in node:   # one path, no word ends yet\n        ch = next(iter(node))\n        prefix.append(ch)\n        node = node[ch]\n    return \"\".join(prefix)","java":"static class Node {\n    Map<Character, Node> kids = new TreeMap<>();\n    boolean end;\n}\n\npublic String longestCommonPrefix(String[] strs) {\n    Node root = new Node();\n    for (String w : strs) {                     // build a trie of all words\n        Node node = root;\n        for (char ch : w.toCharArray()) node = node.kids.computeIfAbsent(ch, k -> new Node());\n        node.end = true;\n    }\n    StringBuilder prefix = new StringBuilder();\n    Node node = root;\n    while (node.kids.size() == 1 && !node.end) {   // one path, no word ends yet\n        var e = node.kids.entrySet().iterator().next();\n        prefix.append(e.getKey());\n        node = e.getValue();\n    }\n    return prefix.toString();\n}","cpp":"string longestCommonPrefix(vector<string>& strs) {\n    struct Node { map<char, Node*> kids; bool end = false; };\n    Node* root = new Node();\n    for (auto& w : strs) {                    // build a trie of all words\n        Node* node = root;\n        for (char ch : w) {\n            if (!node->kids.count(ch)) node->kids[ch] = new Node();\n            node = node->kids[ch];\n        }\n        node->end = true;\n    }\n    string prefix;\n    Node* node = root;\n    while (node->kids.size() == 1 && !node->end) {   // one path, no word ends yet\n        auto [ch, next] = *node->kids.begin();\n        prefix += ch;\n        node = next;\n    }\n    return prefix;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","trie.html#p-14"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-common-prefix/"]],"ask":"Longest common prefix of all strings."},{"i":"p-trie-648","k":"prob","s":"trie","t":"Replace Words","sub":"LeetCode 648 · Medium · Trie","cat":"DSA problems","n":648,"q":["Replace Words","how to solve Replace Words","Replace Words approach","Replace Words solution","leetcode 648","lc 648","Replace each word with its shortest dictionary root.","Replace each word in a sentence with the shortest dictionary root it starts with."],"w":"Trie Shortest root → first end flag. Complexity O(total characters)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Shortest root → first end flag.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Trie of roots; walk the word until you hit a root end.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Replace each word in a sentence with the shortest dictionary root it starts with.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> For each word, try every root: O(words × roots × L).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Insert all roots.<\/li><li>For each word, follow characters; stop at the first word-end mark.<\/li><li>Replace if found, else keep the word.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Trie:<\/b> Of roots, storing the root at its end.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> Each word.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> First end found.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(total characters)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> If no root matches, keep the word.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def replace_words(dictionary, sentence):\n    root = {}\n    for w in dictionary:\n        node = root\n        for ch in w:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n        node[\"$\"] = w                       # store the root word at its end\n    def shortest_root(word):\n        node = root\n        for ch in word:\n            if ch not in node:\n                break\n            node = node[ch]\n            if \"$\" in node:\n                return node[\"$\"]            # first (shortest) root wins\n        return word\n    return \" \".join(shortest_root(w) for w in sentence.split())","java":"static class Node {\n    Node[] next = new Node[26];\n    String word;                               // the root word ending here\n}\n\npublic String replaceWords(List<String> dictionary, String sentence) {\n    Node root = new Node();\n    for (String w : dictionary) {\n        Node node = root;\n        for (char ch : w.toCharArray()) {\n            if (node.next[ch - 'a'] == null) node.next[ch - 'a'] = new Node();\n            node = node.next[ch - 'a'];\n        }\n        node.word = w;\n    }\n    StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();\n    for (String w : sentence.split(\" \")) {\n        if (out.length() > 0) out.append(' ');\n        out.append(shortestRoot(root, w));\n    }\n    return out.toString();\n}\n\nString shortestRoot(Node root, String word) {\n    Node node = root;\n    for (char ch : word.toCharArray()) {\n        node = node.next[ch - 'a'];\n        if (node == null) break;\n        if (node.word != null) return node.word;   // first (shortest) root wins\n    }\n    return word;\n}","cpp":"string replaceWords(vector<string>& dictionary, string sentence) {\n    struct Node { Node* next[26] = {}; string word; };\n    Node* root = new Node();\n    for (auto& w : dictionary) {\n        Node* node = root;\n        for (char ch : w) {\n            if (!node->next[ch - 'a']) node->next[ch - 'a'] = new Node();\n            node = node->next[ch - 'a'];\n        }\n        node->word = w;                        // the root word ending here\n    }\n    auto shortestRoot = [&](const string& word) {\n        Node* node = root;\n        for (char ch : word) {\n            node = node->next[ch - 'a'];\n            if (!node) break;\n            if (!node->word.empty()) return node->word;   // first (shortest) root wins\n        }\n        return word;\n    };\n    stringstream ss(sentence);\n    string w, out;\n    while (ss >> w) out += (out.empty() ? \"\" : \" \") + shortestRoot(w);\n    return out;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","trie.html#p-648"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/replace-words/"]],"ask":"Replace each word with its shortest dictionary root."},{"i":"p-trie-1268","k":"prob","s":"trie","t":"Search Suggestions System","sub":"LeetCode 1268 · Medium · Trie","cat":"DSA problems","n":1268,"q":["Search Suggestions System","how to solve Search Suggestions System","Search Suggestions System approach","Search Suggestions System solution","leetcode 1268","lc 1268","Top 3 suggestions after each typed character.","After each typed letter, suggest up to 3 products (alphabetical) with that prefix."],"w":"Trie Type-ahead → trie with top 3 per node. Complexity O(total characters)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Type-ahead → trie with top 3 per node.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort products; for each prefix binary search the first match and take 3 (or store top-3 in trie nodes).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> After each typed letter, suggest up to 3 products (alphabetical) with that prefix.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Filter all products for each prefix: O(L·n·L).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort products.<\/li><li>For each prefix, bisect to the first product ≥ prefix.<\/li><li>Take up to 3 that start with the prefix.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> Products.<\/li><li><b>Store:<\/b> First 3 per node.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> The typed prefix.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(total characters)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> After the prefix breaks, keep appending [].<\/p>","c":{"py":"def suggested_products(products, search_word):\n    products.sort()\n    root = {}\n    for p in products:\n        node = root\n        for ch in p:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n            node.setdefault(\"#\", [])\n            if len(node[\"#\"]) < 3:\n                node[\"#\"].append(p)          # sorted: the first 3 are the answer\n    res, node = [], root\n    for ch in search_word:\n        node = node.get(ch) if node else None\n        res.append(node[\"#\"] if node else [])\n    return res","java":"static class Node {\n    Node[] next = new Node[26];\n    List<String> top = new ArrayList<>();      // up to 3 products through this node\n}\n\npublic List<List<String>> suggestedProducts(String[] products, String searchWord) {\n    Arrays.sort(products);\n    Node root = new Node();\n    for (String p : products) {\n        Node node = root;\n        for (char ch : p.toCharArray()) {\n            if (node.next[ch - 'a'] == null) node.next[ch - 'a'] = new Node();\n            node = node.next[ch - 'a'];\n            if (node.top.size() < 3) node.top.add(p);   // sorted: the first 3 are the answer\n        }\n    }\n    List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    Node node = root;\n    for (char ch : searchWord.toCharArray()) {\n        node = (node == null) ? null : node.next[ch - 'a'];\n        res.add(node == null ? new ArrayList<>() : node.top);\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<string>> suggestedProducts(vector<string>& products, string searchWord) {\n    struct Node { Node* next[26] = {}; vector<string> top; };   // up to 3 products per node\n    sort(products.begin(), products.end());\n    Node* root = new Node();\n    for (auto& p : products) {\n        Node* node = root;\n        for (char ch : p) {\n            if (!node->next[ch - 'a']) node->next[ch - 'a'] = new Node();\n            node = node->next[ch - 'a'];\n            if (node->top.size() < 3) node->top.push_back(p);   // sorted: first 3 are the answer\n        }\n    }\n    vector<vector<string>> res;\n    Node* node = root;\n    for (char ch : searchWord) {\n        node = node ? node->next[ch - 'a'] : nullptr;\n        res.push_back(node ? node->top : vector<string>{});\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","trie.html#p-1268"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/search-suggestions-system/"]],"ask":"Top 3 suggestions after each typed character."},{"i":"p-trie-421","k":"prob","s":"trie","t":"Maximum XOR of Two Numbers","sub":"LeetCode 421 · Medium · Trie","cat":"DSA problems","n":421,"q":["Maximum XOR of Two Numbers","how to solve Maximum XOR of Two Numbers","Maximum XOR of Two Numbers approach","Maximum XOR of Two Numbers solution","leetcode 421","lc 421","Max XOR of any pair.","Max XOR of any two numbers in the list."],"w":"Trie Max XOR → binary trie. Complexity O(32 n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Max XOR → binary trie.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Bitwise trie from the highest bit: for each number, try to go the opposite bit at every level.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Max XOR of any two numbers in the list.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> All pairs: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Insert every number&#x27;s 32 bits.<\/li><li>For each number, at each bit prefer the opposite child (adds that bit to the XOR).<\/li><li>Track the best.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Trie:<\/b> Binary, 32 levels.<\/li><li><b>Greedy:<\/b> Opposite bit first.<\/li><li><b>Each x:<\/b> Walk and build cur.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(32 n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Insert high bit first, or greedy breaks.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_maximum_xor(nums):\n    root = {}\n    for x in nums:                          # insert 32-bit paths, high bit first\n        node = root\n        for b in range(31, -1, -1):\n            node = node.setdefault((x >> b) & 1, {})\n    best = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        node, cur = root, 0\n        for b in range(31, -1, -1):\n            bit = (x >> b) & 1\n            want = 1 - bit                  # opposite bit makes this xor bit 1\n            if want in node:\n                cur |= 1 << b\n                node = node[want]\n            else:\n                node = node[bit]\n        best = max(best, cur)\n    return best","java":"public int findMaximumXOR(int[] nums) {\n    int[][] trie = new int[nums.length * 32 + 1][2];   // node -> child per bit (0 = none)\n    int size = 1;\n    for (int x : nums) {                        // insert 32-bit paths, high bit first\n        int node = 0;\n        for (int b = 31; b >= 0; b--) {\n            int bit = (x >> b) & 1;\n            if (trie[node][bit] == 0) trie[node][bit] = size++;\n            node = trie[node][bit];\n        }\n    }\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        int node = 0, cur = 0;\n        for (int b = 31; b >= 0; b--) {\n            int bit = (x >> b) & 1, want = 1 - bit;   // opposite bit makes this xor bit 1\n            if (trie[node][want] != 0) { cur |= 1 << b; node = trie[node][want]; }\n            else node = trie[node][bit];\n        }\n        best = Math.max(best, cur);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int findMaximumXOR(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<array<int, 2>> trie(1, {0, 0});      // node -> child per bit (0 = none)\n    for (int x : nums) {                        // insert 32-bit paths, high bit first\n        int node = 0;\n        for (int b = 31; b >= 0; b--) {\n            int bit = (x >> b) & 1;\n            if (!trie[node][bit]) { trie[node][bit] = trie.size(); trie.push_back({0, 0}); }\n            node = trie[node][bit];\n        }\n    }\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        int node = 0, cur = 0;\n        for (int b = 31; b >= 0; b--) {\n            int bit = (x >> b) & 1, want = 1 - bit;   // opposite bit makes this xor bit 1\n            if (trie[node][want]) { cur |= 1 << b; node = trie[node][want]; }\n            else node = trie[node][bit];\n        }\n        best = max(best, cur);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","trie.html#p-421"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-xor-of-two-numbers-in-an-array/"]],"ask":"Max XOR of any pair."},{"i":"p-trie-720","k":"prob","s":"trie","t":"Longest Word in Dictionary","sub":"LeetCode 720 · Medium · Trie","cat":"DSA problems","n":720,"q":["Longest Word in Dictionary","how to solve Longest Word in Dictionary","Longest Word in Dictionary approach","Longest Word in Dictionary solution","leetcode 720","lc 720","Longest word built one letter at a time by other words (ties: smallest).","Longest word whose every prefix is also a word (ties: smallest)."],"w":"Trie Built letter by letter → walk only through end flags. Complexity O(total characters)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Built letter by letter → walk only through end flags.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Trie: only follow nodes that are word ends.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Longest word whose every prefix is also a word (ties: smallest).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> For each word check all prefixes in a set: fine (O(n·L)).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Insert all words.<\/li><li>DFS/BFS only through nodes marked as word ends.<\/li><li>Keep the longest (lexicographically smallest on ties).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Trie:<\/b> Store each word at its end.<\/li><li><b>Walk:<\/b> Only through word ends.<\/li><li><b>Best:<\/b> Longest, then smallest.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(total characters)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Handle the tie-break explicitly.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def longest_word(words):\n    root = {}\n    for w in words:\n        node = root\n        for ch in w:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n        node[\"$\"] = w\n    best = \"\"\n    stack = [root]\n    while stack:                               # only walk through word ends\n        node = stack.pop()\n        for ch, child in node.items():\n            if ch != \"$\" and \"$\" in child:\n                w = child[\"$\"]\n                if len(w) > len(best) or (len(w) == len(best) and w < best):\n                    best = w\n                stack.append(child)\n    return best","java":"static class Node {\n    Node[] next = new Node[26];\n    String word;\n}\n\npublic String longestWord(String[] words) {\n    Node root = new Node();\n    for (String w : words) {\n        Node node = root;\n        for (char ch : w.toCharArray()) {\n            if (node.next[ch - 'a'] == null) node.next[ch - 'a'] = new Node();\n            node = node.next[ch - 'a'];\n        }\n        node.word = w;\n    }\n    String best = \"\";\n    Deque<Node> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    stack.push(root);\n    while (!stack.isEmpty()) {                 // only walk through word ends\n        Node node = stack.pop();\n        for (Node child : node.next)\n            if (child != null && child.word != null) {\n                String w = child.word;\n                if (w.length() > best.length() || (w.length() == best.length() && w.compareTo(best) < 0))\n                    best = w;\n                stack.push(child);\n            }\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"string longestWord(vector<string>& words) {\n    struct Node { Node* next[26] = {}; string word; };\n    Node* root = new Node();\n    for (auto& w : words) {\n        Node* node = root;\n        for (char ch : w) {\n            if (!node->next[ch - 'a']) node->next[ch - 'a'] = new Node();\n            node = node->next[ch - 'a'];\n        }\n        node->word = w;\n    }\n    string best;\n    vector<Node*> st{root};\n    while (!st.empty()) {                      // only walk through word ends\n        Node* node = st.back(); st.pop_back();\n        for (Node* child : node->next)\n            if (child && !child->word.empty()) {\n                const string& w = child->word;\n                if (w.size() > best.size() || (w.size() == best.size() && w < best)) best = w;\n                st.push_back(child);\n            }\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","trie.html#p-720"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-word-in-dictionary/"]],"ask":"Longest word built one letter at a time by other words (ties: smallest)."},{"i":"p-trie-677","k":"prob","s":"trie","t":"Map Sum Pairs","sub":"LeetCode 677 · Medium · Trie","cat":"DSA problems","n":677,"q":["Map Sum Pairs","how to solve Map Sum Pairs","Map Sum Pairs approach","Map Sum Pairs solution","leetcode 677","lc 677","insert(key, val) and sum(prefix) of all values whose key starts with prefix.","insert(key, value) and sum(prefix) of values for keys starting with prefix."],"w":"Trie Prefix aggregate → sum on each node. Complexity O(L) each","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Prefix aggregate → sum on each node.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Each trie node stores the sum of values passing through it; update by the delta on insert.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> insert(key, value) and sum(prefix) of values for keys starting with prefix.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Scan all keys for each sum: O(n·L).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>delta = val − old value of key (if any).<\/li><li>Walk the key adding delta to every node.<\/li><li>sum(prefix) = stored value at the prefix&#x27;s node.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Nodes:<\/b> Store a running sum.<\/li><li><b>Overwrite:<\/b> Apply the delta.<\/li><li><b>Query:<\/b> Walk the prefix, read the sum.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(L) each<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Re-inserting a key must replace, not add.<\/p>","c":{"py":"class MapSum:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = {}\n        self.vals = {}\n\n    def insert(self, key, val):\n        delta = val - self.vals.get(key, 0)   # handles overwrites\n        self.vals[key] = val\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in key:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {\"#\": 0})\n            node[\"#\"] += delta                # every prefix node keeps a sum\n\n    def sum(self, prefix):\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in prefix:\n            if ch not in node:\n                return 0\n            node = node[ch]\n        return node[\"#\"]","java":"class MapSum {\n    private static class Node {\n        Node[] next = new Node[26];\n        int sum;                               // every prefix node keeps a sum\n    }\n    private final Node root = new Node();\n    private final Map<String, Integer> vals = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public void insert(String key, int val) {\n        int delta = val - vals.getOrDefault(key, 0);   // handles overwrites\n        vals.put(key, val);\n        Node node = root;\n        for (char ch : key.toCharArray()) {\n            if (node.next[ch - 'a'] == null) node.next[ch - 'a'] = new Node();\n            node = node.next[ch - 'a'];\n            node.sum += delta;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public int sum(String prefix) {\n        Node node = root;\n        for (char ch : prefix.toCharArray()) {\n            node = node.next[ch - 'a'];\n            if (node == null) return 0;\n        }\n        return node.sum;\n    }\n}","cpp":"class MapSum {\n    struct Node { Node* next[26] = {}; int sum = 0; };   // every prefix node keeps a sum\n    Node* root = new Node();\n    unordered_map<string, int> vals;\npublic:\n    void insert(string key, int val) {\n        int delta = val - vals[key];           // handles overwrites\n        vals[key] = val;\n        Node* node = root;\n        for (char ch : key) {\n            if (!node->next[ch - 'a']) node->next[ch - 'a'] = new Node();\n            node = node->next[ch - 'a'];\n            node->sum += delta;\n        }\n    }\n    int sum(string prefix) {\n        Node* node = root;\n        for (char ch : prefix) {\n            node = node->next[ch - 'a'];\n            if (!node) return 0;\n        }\n        return node->sum;\n    }\n};"},"L":[["Open in the app","trie.html#p-677"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/map-sum-pairs/"]],"ask":"insert(key, val) and sum(prefix) of all values whose key starts with prefix."},{"i":"p-graphs-200","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Number of Islands","sub":"LeetCode 200 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":200,"q":["Number of Islands","how to solve Number of Islands","Number of Islands approach","Number of Islands solution","leetcode 200","lc 200","Count islands of “1” in a grid.","How many separate groups of land ('1') are there? Cells connect up/down/left/right."],"w":"Graphs Components → count floods. Complexity O(R · C)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Components → count floods.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Every unvisited land cell starts a new island; flood-fill (DFS/BFS) marks the whole island.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How many separate groups of land (&#x27;1&#x27;) are there? Cells connect up/down/left/right.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Scan every cell.<\/li><li>On an unvisited &#x27;1&#x27;: count += 1, then flood-fill it, marking cells visited.<\/li><li>Return count.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Nodes:<\/b> Cells.<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> Each unvisited land cell starts a flood.<\/li><li><b>Count:<\/b> Floods.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Mark before recursing.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def num_islands(grid):\n    R, C = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    def sink(r, c):                              # flood-fill one island\n        if r < 0 or c < 0 or r >= R or c >= C or grid[r][c] != \"1\":\n            return\n        grid[r][c] = \"0\"                         # mark visited by sinking it\n        sink(r + 1, c); sink(r - 1, c); sink(r, c + 1); sink(r, c - 1)\n    count = 0\n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            if grid[r][c] == \"1\":\n                count += 1                       # a new island\n                sink(r, c)\n    return count","java":"public int numIslands(char[][] grid) {\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < grid[0].length; c++)\n            if (grid[r][c] == '1') {\n                count++;                         // a new island\n                sink(grid, r, c);\n            }\n    return count;\n}\n\nvoid sink(char[][] grid, int r, int c) {         // flood-fill one island\n    if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= grid.length || c >= grid[0].length || grid[r][c] != '1') return;\n    grid[r][c] = '0';                            // mark visited by sinking it\n    sink(grid, r + 1, c); sink(grid, r - 1, c); sink(grid, r, c + 1); sink(grid, r, c - 1);\n}","cpp":"int numIslands(vector<vector<char>>& grid) {\n    int R = grid.size(), C = grid[0].size(), count = 0;\n    function<void(int, int)> sink = [&](int r, int c) {   // flood-fill one island\n        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= R || c >= C || grid[r][c] != '1') return;\n        grid[r][c] = '0';                        // mark visited by sinking it\n        sink(r + 1, c); sink(r - 1, c); sink(r, c + 1); sink(r, c - 1);\n    };\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)\n            if (grid[r][c] == '1') { count++; sink(r, c); }   // a new island\n    return count;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-200"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-islands/"]],"ask":"Count islands of “1” in a grid."},{"i":"p-graphs-695","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Max Area of Island","sub":"LeetCode 695 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":695,"q":["Max Area of Island","how to solve Max Area of Island","Max Area of Island approach","Max Area of Island solution","leetcode 695","lc 695","Largest island area.","Size of the biggest island."],"w":"Graphs Components with size → flood returns a count. Complexity O(R · C)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Components with size → flood returns a count.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Same flood fill, but return the number of cells filled.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Size of the biggest island.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each unvisited land cell, dfs returns 1 + sum of neighbour dfs.<\/li><li>Mark cells as you go.<\/li><li>Track the max.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Flood:<\/b> Return the area.<\/li><li><b>Area:<\/b> 1 + four neighbours.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Max.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> max() needs default=0 for an all-water grid.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_area_of_island(grid):\n    R, C = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    def area(r, c):\n        if r < 0 or c < 0 or r >= R or c >= C or grid[r][c] != 1:\n            return 0\n        grid[r][c] = 0                            # visited\n        return 1 + area(r + 1, c) + area(r - 1, c) + area(r, c + 1) + area(r, c - 1)\n    return max((area(r, c) for r in range(R) for c in range(C)), default=0)","java":"public int maxAreaOfIsland(int[][] grid) {\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < grid[0].length; c++)\n            best = Math.max(best, area(grid, r, c));\n    return best;\n}\n\nint area(int[][] grid, int r, int c) {\n    if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= grid.length || c >= grid[0].length || grid[r][c] != 1) return 0;\n    grid[r][c] = 0;                              // visited\n    return 1 + area(grid, r + 1, c) + area(grid, r - 1, c) + area(grid, r, c + 1) + area(grid, r, c - 1);\n}","cpp":"int maxAreaOfIsland(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {\n    int R = grid.size(), C = grid[0].size(), best = 0;\n    function<int(int, int)> area = [&](int r, int c) {\n        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= R || c >= C || grid[r][c] != 1) return 0;\n        grid[r][c] = 0;                          // visited\n        return 1 + area(r + 1, c) + area(r - 1, c) + area(r, c + 1) + area(r, c - 1);\n    };\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) best = max(best, area(r, c));\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-695"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/max-area-of-island/"]],"ask":"Largest island area."},{"i":"p-graphs-994","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Rotting Oranges","sub":"LeetCode 994 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":994,"q":["Rotting Oranges","how to solve Rotting Oranges","Rotting Oranges approach","Rotting Oranges solution","leetcode 994","lc 994","Minutes until every orange is rotten, or −1.","Each minute rot spreads to neighbours. Minutes until all oranges rot, or −1?"],"w":"Graphs Many sources → multi-source BFS. Complexity O(R · C)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Many sources → multi-source BFS.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Multi-source BFS: start from all rotten oranges at once; BFS levels = minutes.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each minute rot spreads to neighbours. Minutes until all oranges rot, or −1?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Simulate minute by minute rescanning the grid: O((RC)²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Queue all rotten cells; count fresh ones.<\/li><li>BFS level by level, rotting fresh neighbours (fresh −= 1).<\/li><li>fresh == 0 → minutes, else −1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Seed:<\/b> All rotten at once.<\/li><li><b>Level:<\/b> One minute.<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> Fresh left.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> If there are no fresh oranges, the answer is 0.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n\ndef oranges_rotting(grid):\n    R, C = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    q, fresh = deque(), 0\n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            if grid[r][c] == 2:\n                q.append((r, c))                 # every rotten orange starts at once\n            elif grid[r][c] == 1:\n                fresh += 1\n    minutes = 0\n    while q and fresh:\n        for _ in range(len(q)):                  # one BFS level = one minute\n            r, c = q.popleft()\n            for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n                if 0 <= nr < R and 0 <= nc < C and grid[nr][nc] == 1:\n                    grid[nr][nc] = 2\n                    fresh -= 1\n                    q.append((nr, nc))\n        minutes += 1\n    return minutes if fresh == 0 else -1","java":"public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {\n    int R = grid.length, C = grid[0].length, fresh = 0;\n    Deque<int[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == 2) q.offer(new int[]{r, c});   // every rotten orange starts at once\n            else if (grid[r][c] == 1) fresh++;\n        }\n    int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};\n    int minutes = 0;\n    while (!q.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {\n        for (int i = q.size(); i > 0; i--) {     // one BFS level = one minute\n            int[] cell = q.poll();\n            for (int[] d : dirs) {\n                int nr = cell[0] + d[0], nc = cell[1] + d[1];\n                if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < R && nc < C && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {\n                    grid[nr][nc] = 2;\n                    fresh--;\n                    q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        minutes++;\n    }\n    return fresh == 0 ? minutes : -1;\n}","cpp":"int orangesRotting(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {\n    int R = grid.size(), C = grid[0].size(), fresh = 0;\n    queue<pair<int, int>> q;\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == 2) q.push({r, c});   // every rotten orange starts at once\n            else if (grid[r][c] == 1) fresh++;\n        }\n    int dr[] = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc[] = {0, 0, 1, -1}, minutes = 0;\n    while (!q.empty() && fresh > 0) {\n        for (int i = q.size(); i > 0; i--) {     // one BFS level = one minute\n            auto [r, c] = q.front(); q.pop();\n            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n                if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < R && nc < C && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {\n                    grid[nr][nc] = 2;\n                    fresh--;\n                    q.push({nr, nc});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        minutes++;\n    }\n    return fresh == 0 ? minutes : -1;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-994"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/rotting-oranges/"]],"ask":"Minutes until every orange is rotten, or −1."},{"i":"p-graphs-286","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Walls and Gates","sub":"LeetCode 286 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":286,"q":["Walls and Gates","how to solve Walls and Gates","Walls and Gates approach","Walls and Gates solution","leetcode 286","lc 286","Fill each room with its distance to the nearest gate.","Fill each empty room with the distance to the nearest gate."],"w":"Graphs Nearest of many sources → multi-source BFS. Complexity O(R · C)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Nearest of many sources → multi-source BFS.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Multi-source BFS from all gates together: first time a room is reached is its shortest distance.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fill each empty room with the distance to the nearest gate.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> BFS from each gate separately: O(gates × cells).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Queue every gate.<\/li><li>Pop a cell; for each INF neighbour set dist + 1 and enqueue.<\/li><li>Walls (−1) are never entered.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Seed:<\/b> Every gate.<\/li><li><b>Spread:<\/b> Only into INF rooms.<\/li><li><b>Distance:<\/b> Parent + 1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> BFS from each gate separately is O((R·C)²).<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n\ndef walls_and_gates(rooms):\n    INF = 2 ** 31 - 1\n    R, C = len(rooms), len(rooms[0])\n    q = deque((r, c) for r in range(R) for c in range(C) if rooms[r][c] == 0)\n    while q:                                  # multi-source BFS from every gate\n        r, c = q.popleft()\n        for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n            if 0 <= nr < R and 0 <= nc < C and rooms[nr][nc] == INF:\n                rooms[nr][nc] = rooms[r][c] + 1\n                q.append((nr, nc))","java":"public void wallsAndGates(int[][] rooms) {\n    final int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    int R = rooms.length, C = rooms[0].length;\n    Deque<int[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)\n            if (rooms[r][c] == 0) q.offer(new int[]{r, c});\n    int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};\n    while (!q.isEmpty()) {                       // multi-source BFS from every gate\n        int[] cell = q.poll();\n        for (int[] d : dirs) {\n            int nr = cell[0] + d[0], nc = cell[1] + d[1];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < R && nc < C && rooms[nr][nc] == INF) {\n                rooms[nr][nc] = rooms[cell[0]][cell[1]] + 1;\n                q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","cpp":"void wallsAndGates(vector<vector<int>>& rooms) {\n    const int INF = INT_MAX;\n    int R = rooms.size(), C = rooms[0].size();\n    queue<pair<int, int>> q;\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)\n            if (rooms[r][c] == 0) q.push({r, c});\n    int dr[] = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc[] = {0, 0, 1, -1};\n    while (!q.empty()) {                         // multi-source BFS from every gate\n        auto [r, c] = q.front(); q.pop();\n        for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < R && nc < C && rooms[nr][nc] == INF) {\n                rooms[nr][nc] = rooms[r][c] + 1;\n                q.push({nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-286"],["LeetCode (Premium)","https://leetcode.com/problems/walls-and-gates/"]],"ask":"Fill each room with its distance to the nearest gate."},{"i":"p-graphs-417","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Pacific Atlantic Water Flow","sub":"LeetCode 417 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":417,"q":["Pacific Atlantic Water Flow","how to solve Pacific Atlantic Water Flow","Pacific Atlantic Water Flow approach","Pacific Atlantic Water Flow solution","leetcode 417","lc 417","Cells that can drain to both oceans.","Which cells can drain water to both the Pacific (top/left) and Atlantic (bottom/right)?"],"w":"Graphs Reach the edge → flood from the edge. Complexity O(R · C)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Reach the edge → flood from the edge.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Reverse it: from each ocean&#x27;s border, walk &#x27;uphill&#x27; (to cells ≥ current). Answer = cells reached from both.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Which cells can drain water to both the Pacific (top/left) and Atlantic (bottom/right)?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Simulate water from every cell: O((RC)²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>BFS/DFS from Pacific borders to equal-or-higher cells → set P.<\/li><li>Same for Atlantic → set A.<\/li><li>Return P ∩ A.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Flip:<\/b> Flood uphill from each ocean.<\/li><li><b>Twice:<\/b> Pacific and Atlantic.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Intersection.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Uphill means ≥, not &gt;.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def pacific_atlantic(heights):\n    R, C = len(heights), len(heights[0])\n    def flood(starts):                            # walk UPHILL from the ocean\n        seen = set(starts)\n        stack = list(starts)\n        while stack:\n            r, c = stack.pop()\n            for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n                if (0 <= nr < R and 0 <= nc < C and (nr, nc) not in seen\n                        and heights[nr][nc] >= heights[r][c]):\n                    seen.add((nr, nc))\n                    stack.append((nr, nc))\n        return seen\n    pac = flood([(0, c) for c in range(C)] + [(r, 0) for r in range(R)])\n    atl = flood([(R - 1, c) for c in range(C)] + [(r, C - 1) for r in range(R)])\n    return [list(p) for p in pac & atl]","java":"public List<List<Integer>> pacificAtlantic(int[][] heights) {\n    int R = heights.length, C = heights[0].length;\n    boolean[][] pac = new boolean[R][C], atl = new boolean[R][C];\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) { flood(heights, r, 0, pac); flood(heights, r, C - 1, atl); }\n    for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) { flood(heights, 0, c, pac); flood(heights, R - 1, c, atl); }\n    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)\n            if (pac[r][c] && atl[r][c]) res.add(List.of(r, c));\n    return res;\n}\n\nvoid flood(int[][] h, int r, int c, boolean[][] seen) {   // walk UPHILL from the ocean\n    if (seen[r][c]) return;\n    seen[r][c] = true;\n    int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};\n    for (int[] d : dirs) {\n        int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n        if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < h.length && nc < h[0].length && h[nr][nc] >= h[r][c])\n            flood(h, nr, nc, seen);\n    }\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> pacificAtlantic(vector<vector<int>>& h) {\n    int R = h.size(), C = h[0].size();\n    vector<vector<bool>> pac(R, vector<bool>(C)), atl(R, vector<bool>(C));\n    function<void(int, int, vector<vector<bool>>&)> flood =\n        [&](int r, int c, vector<vector<bool>>& seen) {       // walk UPHILL from the ocean\n        if (seen[r][c]) return;\n        seen[r][c] = true;\n        int dr[] = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc[] = {0, 0, 1, -1};\n        for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < R && nc < C && h[nr][nc] >= h[r][c])\n                flood(nr, nc, seen);\n        }\n    };\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) { flood(r, 0, pac); flood(r, C - 1, atl); }\n    for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) { flood(0, c, pac); flood(R - 1, c, atl); }\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)\n            if (pac[r][c] && atl[r][c]) res.push_back({r, c});\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-417"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/pacific-atlantic-water-flow/"]],"ask":"Cells that can drain to both oceans."},{"i":"p-graphs-130","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Surrounded Regions","sub":"LeetCode 130 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":130,"q":["Surrounded Regions","how to solve Surrounded Regions","Surrounded Regions approach","Surrounded Regions solution","leetcode 130","lc 130","Capture O regions not connected to the border.","Flip every 'O' region that doesn't touch the border into 'X'."],"w":"Graphs Not touching the border → flood from the border. Complexity O(R · C)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Not touching the border → flood from the border.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Mark the safe ones: flood-fill from border &#x27;O&#x27;s; everything else gets captured.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Flip every &#x27;O&#x27; region that doesn&#x27;t touch the border into &#x27;X&#x27;.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check each region for border contact separately: repeated work.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>DFS from every border &#x27;O&#x27;, marking it safe.<\/li><li>Turn unmarked &#x27;O&#x27; into &#x27;X&#x27;.<\/li><li>Turn safe marks back into &#x27;O&#x27;.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Flip:<\/b> Protect border-connected Os.<\/li><li><b>Mark:<\/b> As “S”.<\/li><li><b>Sweep:<\/b> S → O, others → X.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(R · C)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Deep recursion on big boards: an explicit stack is safer.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def solve(board):\n    R, C = len(board), len(board[0])\n    def keep(r, c):                               # O connected to the border survives\n        if 0 <= r < R and 0 <= c < C and board[r][c] == \"O\":\n            board[r][c] = \"S\"\n            keep(r + 1, c); keep(r - 1, c); keep(r, c + 1); keep(r, c - 1)\n    for r in range(R):\n        keep(r, 0); keep(r, C - 1)\n    for c in range(C):\n        keep(0, c); keep(R - 1, c)\n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            board[r][c] = \"O\" if board[r][c] == \"S\" else \"X\"","java":"public void solve(char[][] board) {\n    int R = board.length, C = board[0].length;\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) { keep(board, r, 0); keep(board, r, C - 1); }\n    for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) { keep(board, 0, c); keep(board, R - 1, c); }\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)\n            board[r][c] = board[r][c] == 'S' ? 'O' : 'X';\n}\n\nvoid keep(char[][] board, int r, int c) {        // O connected to the border survives\n    if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= board.length || c >= board[0].length || board[r][c] != 'O') return;\n    board[r][c] = 'S';\n    keep(board, r + 1, c); keep(board, r - 1, c); keep(board, r, c + 1); keep(board, r, c - 1);\n}","cpp":"void solve(vector<vector<char>>& board) {\n    int R = board.size(), C = board[0].size();\n    function<void(int, int)> keep = [&](int r, int c) {   // O connected to the border survives\n        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= R || c >= C || board[r][c] != 'O') return;\n        board[r][c] = 'S';\n        keep(r + 1, c); keep(r - 1, c); keep(r, c + 1); keep(r, c - 1);\n    };\n    for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) { keep(r, 0); keep(r, C - 1); }\n    for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) { keep(0, c); keep(R - 1, c); }\n    for (auto& row : board)\n        for (char& ch : row) ch = (ch == 'S') ? 'O' : 'X';\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-130"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/surrounded-regions/"]],"ask":"Capture O regions not connected to the border."},{"i":"p-graphs-133","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Clone Graph","sub":"LeetCode 133 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":133,"q":["Clone Graph","how to solve Clone Graph","Clone Graph approach","Clone Graph solution","leetcode 133","lc 133","Deep copy an undirected graph.","Deep copy a connected undirected graph."],"w":"Graphs Deep copy → dict + DFS. Complexity O(V + E)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Deep copy → dict + DFS.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> DFS/BFS with a map old → copy, so cycles don&#x27;t loop forever.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Deep copy a connected undirected graph.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If node in map, return its copy.<\/li><li>Create the copy, store it in the map.<\/li><li>Copy each neighbour recursively into copy.neighbors.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Map:<\/b> old → new.<\/li><li><b>Register:<\/b> Before recursing.<\/li><li><b>Wire:<\/b> Clone neighbours.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(V + E)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Without the map, cycles recurse forever.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def clone_graph(node):\n    if not node:\n        return None\n    copies = {}                                   # old -> new\n    def clone(n):\n        if n in copies:\n            return copies[n]                      # already made (handles cycles)\n        copies[n] = Node(n.val)\n        for nb in n.neighbors:\n            copies[n].neighbors.append(clone(nb))\n        return copies[n]\n    return clone(node)","java":"Map<Node, Node> copies = new HashMap<>();       // old -> new\n\npublic Node cloneGraph(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return null;\n    if (copies.containsKey(node)) return copies.get(node);   // already made (handles cycles)\n    Node copy = new Node(node.val);\n    copies.put(node, copy);\n    for (Node nb : node.neighbors) copy.neighbors.add(cloneGraph(nb));\n    return copy;\n}","cpp":"unordered_map<Node*, Node*> copies;              // old -> new\n\nNode* cloneGraph(Node* node) {\n    if (!node) return nullptr;\n    if (copies.count(node)) return copies[node];  // already made (handles cycles)\n    Node* copy = new Node(node->val);\n    copies[node] = copy;\n    for (Node* nb : node->neighbors) copy->neighbors.push_back(cloneGraph(nb));\n    return copy;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-133"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/clone-graph/"]],"ask":"Deep copy an undirected graph."},{"i":"p-graphs-785","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Is Graph Bipartite?","sub":"LeetCode 785 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":785,"q":["Is Graph Bipartite?","how to solve Is Graph Bipartite?","Is Graph Bipartite? approach","Is Graph Bipartite? solution","leetcode 785","lc 785","Can nodes be split into two sets with every edge crossing?","Can you colour nodes in two colours so every edge joins different colours?"],"w":"Graphs Two groups → two-colouring. Complexity O(V + E)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Two groups → two-colouring.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> BFS/DFS colouring; a neighbour with the same colour means an odd cycle → not bipartite.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Can you colour nodes in two colours so every edge joins different colours?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all colourings: 2ⁿ.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each uncoloured node, colour it 0 and BFS.<\/li><li>Neighbours get the opposite colour.<\/li><li>Same-colour neighbour → False.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Colour:<\/b> 0 / 1 via BFS.<\/li><li><b>Clash:<\/b> Same colour neighbour.<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> Every component.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(V + E)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The graph may be disconnected.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n\ndef is_bipartite(graph):\n    color = {}\n    for start in range(len(graph)):          # the graph may be disconnected\n        if start in color:\n            continue\n        color[start] = 0\n        q = deque([start])\n        while q:\n            node = q.popleft()\n            for nb in graph[node]:\n                if nb not in color:\n                    color[nb] = 1 - color[node]\n                    q.append(nb)\n                elif color[nb] == color[node]:\n                    return False             # an edge inside one color\n    return True","java":"public boolean isBipartite(int[][] graph) {\n    int[] color = new int[graph.length];\n    Arrays.fill(color, -1);\n    for (int start = 0; start < graph.length; start++) {   // the graph may be disconnected\n        if (color[start] != -1) continue;\n        color[start] = 0;\n        Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(start));\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int node = q.poll();\n            for (int nb : graph[node]) {\n                if (color[nb] == -1) {\n                    color[nb] = 1 - color[node];\n                    q.offer(nb);\n                } else if (color[nb] == color[node]) return false;   // an edge inside one color\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}","cpp":"bool isBipartite(vector<vector<int>>& graph) {\n    vector<int> color(graph.size(), -1);\n    for (int start = 0; start < (int)graph.size(); start++) {   // may be disconnected\n        if (color[start] != -1) continue;\n        color[start] = 0;\n        queue<int> q;\n        q.push(start);\n        while (!q.empty()) {\n            int node = q.front(); q.pop();\n            for (int nb : graph[node]) {\n                if (color[nb] == -1) {\n                    color[nb] = 1 - color[node];\n                    q.push(nb);\n                } else if (color[nb] == color[node]) return false;   // an edge inside one color\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-785"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/is-graph-bipartite/"]],"ask":"Can nodes be split into two sets with every edge crossing?"},{"i":"p-graphs-127","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Word Ladder","sub":"LeetCode 127 · Hard · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":127,"q":["Word Ladder","how to solve Word Ladder","Word Ladder approach","Word Ladder solution","leetcode 127","lc 127","Shortest transformation sequence length.","Fewest words in a chain from beginWord to endWord, changing one letter each step."],"w":"Graphs Fewest moves between states → BFS. Complexity O(n · L²)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Fewest moves between states → BFS.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> BFS over words; generate neighbours by wildcard patterns like &#x27;h*t&#x27;.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fewest words in a chain from beginWord to endWord, changing one letter each step.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Compare every pair of words to build the graph: O(n²·L).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Index words by pattern (each position replaced by &#x27;*&#x27;).<\/li><li>BFS from beginWord, counting levels.<\/li><li>Neighbours = words sharing a pattern; stop at endWord.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Nodes:<\/b> Words.<\/li><li><b>Edges:<\/b> One-letter changes via patterns.<\/li><li><b>BFS:<\/b> Steps to end.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · L²)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Return 0 if end isn’t in the list.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque, defaultdict\n\ndef ladder_length(begin, end, words):\n    if end not in words:\n        return 0\n    buckets = defaultdict(list)              # \"h*t\" -> [\"hot\", \"hit\", ...]\n    for w in words + [begin]:\n        for i in range(len(w)):\n            buckets[w[:i] + \"*\" + w[i + 1:]].append(w)\n    q, seen = deque([(begin, 1)]), {begin}\n    while q:\n        w, steps = q.popleft()\n        if w == end:\n            return steps\n        for i in range(len(w)):\n            for nb in buckets[w[:i] + \"*\" + w[i + 1:]]:\n                if nb not in seen:\n                    seen.add(nb)\n                    q.append((nb, steps + 1))\n    return 0","java":"public int ladderLength(String begin, String end, List<String> words) {\n    if (!words.contains(end)) return 0;\n    Map<String, List<String>> buckets = new HashMap<>();   // \"h*t\" -> [hot, hit, ...]\n    List<String> all = new ArrayList<>(words);\n    all.add(begin);\n    for (String w : all)\n        for (int i = 0; i < w.length(); i++)\n            buckets.computeIfAbsent(w.substring(0, i) + \"*\" + w.substring(i + 1), k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(w);\n    Deque<String> q = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(begin));\n    Set<String> seen = new HashSet<>(List.of(begin));\n    for (int steps = 1; !q.isEmpty(); steps++)\n        for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {     // one BFS level per step\n            String w = q.poll();\n            if (w.equals(end)) return steps;\n            for (int i = 0; i < w.length(); i++)\n                for (String nb : buckets.get(w.substring(0, i) + \"*\" + w.substring(i + 1)))\n                    if (seen.add(nb)) q.offer(nb);\n        }\n    return 0;\n}","cpp":"int ladderLength(string begin, string end, vector<string>& words) {\n    if (find(words.begin(), words.end(), end) == words.end()) return 0;\n    unordered_map<string, vector<string>> buckets;   // \"h*t\" -> [hot, hit, ...]\n    auto all = words;\n    all.push_back(begin);\n    for (auto& w : all)\n        for (size_t i = 0; i < w.size(); i++) {\n            string key = w;\n            key[i] = '*';\n            buckets[key].push_back(w);\n        }\n    queue<string> q;\n    unordered_set<string> seen{begin};\n    q.push(begin);\n    for (int steps = 1; !q.empty(); steps++)\n        for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {     // one BFS level per step\n            string w = q.front(); q.pop();\n            if (w == end) return steps;\n            for (size_t i = 0; i < w.size(); i++) {\n                string key = w;\n                key[i] = '*';\n                for (auto& nb : buckets[key])\n                    if (seen.insert(nb).second) q.push(nb);\n            }\n        }\n    return 0;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-127"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/word-ladder/"]],"ask":"Shortest transformation sequence length."},{"i":"p-graphs-207","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Course Schedule","sub":"LeetCode 207 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":207,"q":["Course Schedule","how to solve Course Schedule","Course Schedule approach","Course Schedule solution","leetcode 207","lc 207","Can all courses be finished?","Courses have prerequisites. Can you finish all of them?"],"w":"Graphs Prerequisites → topo sort. Complexity O(V + E)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Prerequisites → topo sort.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> It&#x27;s possible iff the prerequisite graph has no cycle. Kahn&#x27;s algorithm: repeatedly take courses with no remaining prerequisites.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Courses have prerequisites. Can you finish all of them?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every order: factorial.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Build the graph and in-degree counts.<\/li><li>Queue all courses with in-degree 0.<\/li><li>Pop, count it, decrement neighbours; True if count == n.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Graph:<\/b> pre → course.<\/li><li><b>Kahn:<\/b> Process in-degree 0.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> All processed?<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(V + E)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The pair is [course, pre]: the edge goes pre → course.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n\ndef can_finish(n, prereqs):\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    indeg = [0] * n\n    for course, pre in prereqs:\n        graph[pre].append(course)\n        indeg[course] += 1\n    q = deque(i for i in range(n) if indeg[i] == 0)\n    taken = 0\n    while q:\n        node = q.popleft()\n        taken += 1\n        for nxt in graph[node]:\n            indeg[nxt] -= 1\n            if indeg[nxt] == 0:\n                q.append(nxt)\n    return taken == n                        # anything left is stuck in a cycle","java":"public boolean canFinish(int n, int[][] prereqs) {\n    List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) graph.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    int[] indeg = new int[n];\n    for (int[] p : prereqs) {\n        graph.get(p[1]).add(p[0]);\n        indeg[p[0]]++;\n    }\n    Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] == 0) q.offer(i);\n    int taken = 0;\n    while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n        int node = q.poll();\n        taken++;\n        for (int nxt : graph.get(node))\n            if (--indeg[nxt] == 0) q.offer(nxt);\n    }\n    return taken == n;                           // anything left is stuck in a cycle\n}","cpp":"bool canFinish(int n, vector<vector<int>>& prereqs) {\n    vector<vector<int>> graph(n);\n    vector<int> indeg(n, 0);\n    for (auto& p : prereqs) {\n        graph[p[1]].push_back(p[0]);\n        indeg[p[0]]++;\n    }\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n    int taken = 0;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int node = q.front(); q.pop();\n        taken++;\n        for (int nxt : graph[node])\n            if (--indeg[nxt] == 0) q.push(nxt);\n    }\n    return taken == n;                           // anything left is stuck in a cycle\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-207"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule/"]],"ask":"Can all courses be finished?"},{"i":"p-graphs-210","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Course Schedule II","sub":"LeetCode 210 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":210,"q":["Course Schedule II","how to solve Course Schedule II","Course Schedule II approach","Course Schedule II solution","leetcode 210","lc 210","Return a valid order, or [].","Return one valid order to take all courses, or [] if impossible."],"w":"Graphs Ordering with dependencies → Kahn. Complexity O(V + E)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Ordering with dependencies → Kahn.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Same as 207, but record the order in which courses leave the queue.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Return one valid order to take all courses, or [] if impossible.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Kahn&#x27;s algorithm.<\/li><li>Append each popped course to the order.<\/li><li>If order has n courses return it, else [].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Kahn:<\/b> Same.<\/li><li><b>Record:<\/b> Pop order.<\/li><li><b>Cycle:<\/b> Fewer than n → [].<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(V + E)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Any valid order is accepted.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n\ndef find_order(n, prereqs):\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    indeg = [0] * n\n    for course, pre in prereqs:\n        graph[pre].append(course)            # pre must come before course\n        indeg[course] += 1\n    q = deque(i for i in range(n) if indeg[i] == 0)\n    order = []\n    while q:\n        node = q.popleft()\n        order.append(node)\n        for nxt in graph[node]:\n            indeg[nxt] -= 1                  # one prerequisite done\n            if indeg[nxt] == 0:\n                q.append(nxt)\n    return order if len(order) == n else []  # leftovers = a cycle","java":"public int[] findOrder(int n, int[][] prereqs) {\n    List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) graph.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    int[] indeg = new int[n];\n    for (int[] p : prereqs) {\n        graph.get(p[1]).add(p[0]);               // pre must come before course\n        indeg[p[0]]++;\n    }\n    Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] == 0) q.offer(i);\n    int[] order = new int[n];\n    int k = 0;\n    while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n        int node = q.poll();\n        order[k++] = node;\n        for (int nxt : graph.get(node))\n            if (--indeg[nxt] == 0) q.offer(nxt); // one prerequisite done\n    }\n    return k == n ? order : new int[0];          // leftovers = a cycle\n}","cpp":"vector<int> findOrder(int n, vector<vector<int>>& prereqs) {\n    vector<vector<int>> graph(n);\n    vector<int> indeg(n, 0), order;\n    for (auto& p : prereqs) {\n        graph[p[1]].push_back(p[0]);             // pre must come before course\n        indeg[p[0]]++;\n    }\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int node = q.front(); q.pop();\n        order.push_back(node);\n        for (int nxt : graph[node])\n            if (--indeg[nxt] == 0) q.push(nxt);  // one prerequisite done\n    }\n    return (int)order.size() == n ? order : vector<int>{};   // leftovers = a cycle\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-210"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule-ii/"]],"ask":"Return a valid order, or []."},{"i":"p-graphs-269","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Alien Dictionary","sub":"LeetCode 269 · Hard · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":269,"q":["Alien Dictionary","how to solve Alien Dictionary","Alien Dictionary approach","Alien Dictionary solution","leetcode 269","lc 269","Derive letter order from a sorted alien word list.","Given words sorted in an alien language, deduce the letter order."],"w":"Graphs Hidden ordering → build edges, topo sort. Complexity O(total characters)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Hidden ordering → build edges, topo sort.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Each pair of neighbouring words gives one edge (first differing letter). Then topological sort.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Given words sorted in an alien language, deduce the letter order.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Compare adjacent words; first difference a ≠ b → edge a → b.<\/li><li>If a word is followed by its own prefix → invalid &#x27;&#x27;.<\/li><li>Topological sort; a cycle → &#x27;&#x27;.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Edges:<\/b> First differing letters of adjacent words.<\/li><li><b>Invalid:<\/b> A word before its own prefix.<\/li><li><b>Kahn:<\/b> Over letters.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(total characters)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Include letters that never get an edge.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import deque\n\ndef alien_order(words):\n    graph = {ch: set() for w in words for ch in w}\n    indeg = {ch: 0 for ch in graph}\n    for a, b in zip(words, words[1:]):\n        for x, y in zip(a, b):\n            if x != y:                       # first difference gives an order\n                if y not in graph[x]:\n                    graph[x].add(y)\n                    indeg[y] += 1\n                break\n        else:\n            if len(a) > len(b):\n                return \"\"                    # \"abc\" before \"ab\" is impossible\n    q = deque(ch for ch in indeg if indeg[ch] == 0)\n    order = []\n    while q:\n        ch = q.popleft()\n        order.append(ch)\n        for nxt in graph[ch]:\n            indeg[nxt] -= 1\n            if indeg[nxt] == 0:\n                q.append(nxt)\n    return \"\".join(order) if len(order) == len(graph) else \"\"","java":"public String alienOrder(String[] words) {\n    Map<Character, Set<Character>> graph = new HashMap<>();\n    Map<Character, Integer> indeg = new HashMap<>();\n    for (String w : words)\n        for (char ch : w.toCharArray()) { graph.putIfAbsent(ch, new HashSet<>()); indeg.putIfAbsent(ch, 0); }\n    for (int i = 0; i + 1 < words.length; i++) {\n        String a = words[i], b = words[i + 1];\n        int j = 0;\n        while (j < a.length() && j < b.length() && a.charAt(j) == b.charAt(j)) j++;\n        if (j < a.length() && j < b.length()) {          // first difference gives an order\n            if (graph.get(a.charAt(j)).add(b.charAt(j)))\n                indeg.merge(b.charAt(j), 1, Integer::sum);\n        } else if (a.length() > b.length()) return \"\";   // \"abc\" before \"ab\" is impossible\n    }\n    Deque<Character> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (char ch : indeg.keySet()) if (indeg.get(ch) == 0) q.offer(ch);\n    StringBuilder order = new StringBuilder();\n    while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n        char ch = q.poll();\n        order.append(ch);\n        for (char nxt : graph.get(ch))\n            if (indeg.merge(nxt, -1, Integer::sum) == 0) q.offer(nxt);\n    }\n    return order.length() == graph.size() ? order.toString() : \"\";\n}","cpp":"string alienOrder(vector<string>& words) {\n    unordered_map<char, unordered_set<char>> graph;\n    unordered_map<char, int> indeg;\n    for (auto& w : words)\n        for (char ch : w) { graph[ch]; indeg[ch]; }\n    for (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < words.size(); i++) {\n        const string &a = words[i], &b = words[i + 1];\n        size_t j = 0;\n        while (j < a.size() && j < b.size() && a[j] == b[j]) j++;\n        if (j < a.size() && j < b.size()) {              // first difference gives an order\n            if (graph[a[j]].insert(b[j]).second) indeg[b[j]]++;\n        } else if (a.size() > b.size()) return \"\";       // \"abc\" before \"ab\" is impossible\n    }\n    queue<char> q;\n    for (auto& [ch, d] : indeg) if (d == 0) q.push(ch);\n    string order;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        char ch = q.front(); q.pop();\n        order += ch;\n        for (char nxt : graph[ch])\n            if (--indeg[nxt] == 0) q.push(nxt);\n    }\n    return order.size() == graph.size() ? order : \"\";\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-269"],["LeetCode (Premium)","https://leetcode.com/problems/alien-dictionary/"]],"ask":"Derive letter order from a sorted alien word list."},{"i":"p-graphs-323","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Number of Connected Components","sub":"LeetCode 323 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":323,"q":["Number of Connected Components","how to solve Number of Connected Components","Number of Connected Components approach","Number of Connected Components solution","leetcode 323","lc 323","Count components in an undirected graph.","How many connected pieces does the undirected graph have?"],"w":"Graphs Connectivity → union-find. Complexity ≈ O(E)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Connectivity → union-find.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Union-Find: every successful union merges two pieces.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How many connected pieces does the undirected graph have?<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>components = n.<\/li><li>For each edge, if find(a) ≠ find(b): union, components −= 1.<\/li><li>Return components.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Start:<\/b> n components.<\/li><li><b>Union:<\/b> Each edge.<\/li><li><b>Merge:<\/b> Successful union: −1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> ≈ O(E)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> DFS works too; mention both.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def count_components(n, edges):\n    parent = list(range(n))\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]    # path halving\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    def union(a, b):\n        ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n        if ra == rb:\n            return False                     # already connected: redundant edge\n        parent[rb] = ra\n        return True\n    components = n\n    for a, b in edges:\n        if union(a, b):\n            components -= 1\n    return components","java":"int[] parent;\n\nint find(int x) {\n    while (parent[x] != x) {\n        parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];           // path halving\n        x = parent[x];\n    }\n    return x;\n}\n\nboolean union(int a, int b) {\n    int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);\n    if (ra == rb) return false;                  // already connected: redundant edge\n    parent[rb] = ra;\n    return true;\n}\n\npublic int countComponents(int n, int[][] edges) {\n    parent = new int[n];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;\n    int components = n;\n    for (int[] e : edges)\n        if (union(e[0], e[1])) components--;\n    return components;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> parent;\n\nint find(int x) {\n    while (parent[x] != x) {\n        parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];           // path halving\n        x = parent[x];\n    }\n    return x;\n}\n\nbool unite(int a, int b) {\n    int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);\n    if (ra == rb) return false;                  // already connected: redundant edge\n    parent[rb] = ra;\n    return true;\n}\n\nint countComponents(int n, vector<vector<int>>& edges) {\n    parent.resize(n);\n    iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);\n    int components = n;\n    for (auto& e : edges)\n        if (unite(e[0], e[1])) components--;\n    return components;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-323"],["LeetCode (Premium)","https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph/"]],"ask":"Count components in an undirected graph."},{"i":"p-graphs-684","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Redundant Connection","sub":"LeetCode 684 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":684,"q":["Redundant Connection","how to solve Redundant Connection","Redundant Connection approach","Redundant Connection solution","leetcode 684","lc 684","Return the edge that makes the tree have a cycle.","Which edge creates the cycle in this tree-plus-one-edge?"],"w":"Graphs Edge closing a cycle → shared root. Complexity ≈ O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Edge closing a cycle → shared root.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Union-Find: the first edge whose ends are already connected is the redundant one.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Which edge creates the cycle in this tree-plus-one-edge?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Remove each edge and test: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each edge (a, b):<\/li><li>find(a) == find(b) → return this edge.<\/li><li>Else union them.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Process:<\/b> Edges in order.<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> Same root?<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> First such edge.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> ≈ O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Nodes are 1-indexed: size parent n + 1.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_redundant_connection(edges):\n    parent = list(range(len(edges) + 1))\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    for a, b in edges:\n        ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n        if ra == rb:\n            return [a, b]                    # already connected: closes a cycle\n        parent[rb] = ra","java":"public int[] findRedundantConnection(int[][] edges) {\n    int[] parent = new int[edges.length + 1];\n    for (int i = 0; i < parent.length; i++) parent[i] = i;\n    for (int[] e : edges) {\n        int ra = find(parent, e[0]), rb = find(parent, e[1]);\n        if (ra == rb) return e;                  // already connected: closes a cycle\n        parent[rb] = ra;\n    }\n    return new int[0];\n}\n\nint find(int[] parent, int x) {\n    while (parent[x] != x) {\n        parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];\n        x = parent[x];\n    }\n    return x;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> findRedundantConnection(vector<vector<int>>& edges) {\n    vector<int> parent(edges.size() + 1);\n    iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);\n    auto find = [&](int x) {\n        while (parent[x] != x) {\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];\n            x = parent[x];\n        }\n        return x;\n    };\n    for (auto& e : edges) {\n        int ra = find(e[0]), rb = find(e[1]);\n        if (ra == rb) return e;                  // already connected: closes a cycle\n        parent[rb] = ra;\n    }\n    return {};\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-684"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/redundant-connection/"]],"ask":"Return the edge that makes the tree have a cycle."},{"i":"p-graphs-261","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Graph Valid Tree","sub":"LeetCode 261 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":261,"q":["Graph Valid Tree","how to solve Graph Valid Tree","Graph Valid Tree approach","Graph Valid Tree solution","leetcode 261","lc 261","Do these edges form a valid tree?","Do these edges form exactly one tree (connected, no cycles)?"],"w":"Graphs Tree check → n − 1 edges + no cycle. Complexity ≈ O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Tree check → n − 1 edges + no cycle.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A tree has exactly n − 1 edges and no cycle.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Do these edges form exactly one tree (connected, no cycles)?<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If edges ≠ n − 1 → False.<\/li><li>Union-Find each edge; a cycle → False.<\/li><li>Otherwise True.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Count:<\/b> Exactly n − 1 edges.<\/li><li><b>Cycle:<\/b> Union-find.<\/li><li><b>Both:<\/b> Then it’s connected.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> ≈ O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Check the edge count first: it’s free.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def valid_tree(n, edges):\n    if len(edges) != n - 1:\n        return False                         # a tree has exactly n - 1 edges\n    parent = list(range(n))\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    for a, b in edges:\n        ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n        if ra == rb:\n            return False                     # cycle\n        parent[rb] = ra\n    return True","java":"public boolean validTree(int n, int[][] edges) {\n    if (edges.length != n - 1) return false;     // a tree has exactly n - 1 edges\n    int[] parent = new int[n];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;\n    for (int[] e : edges) {\n        int ra = find(parent, e[0]), rb = find(parent, e[1]);\n        if (ra == rb) return false;              // cycle\n        parent[rb] = ra;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\nint find(int[] parent, int x) {\n    while (parent[x] != x) {\n        parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];\n        x = parent[x];\n    }\n    return x;\n}","cpp":"bool validTree(int n, vector<vector<int>>& edges) {\n    if ((int)edges.size() != n - 1) return false;   // a tree has exactly n - 1 edges\n    vector<int> parent(n);\n    iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);\n    auto find = [&](int x) {\n        while (parent[x] != x) {\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];\n            x = parent[x];\n        }\n        return x;\n    };\n    for (auto& e : edges) {\n        int ra = find(e[0]), rb = find(e[1]);\n        if (ra == rb) return false;              // cycle\n        parent[rb] = ra;\n    }\n    return true;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-261"],["LeetCode (Premium)","https://leetcode.com/problems/graph-valid-tree/"]],"ask":"Do these edges form a valid tree?"},{"i":"p-graphs-743","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Network Delay Time","sub":"LeetCode 743 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":743,"q":["Network Delay Time","how to solve Network Delay Time","Network Delay Time approach","Network Delay Time solution","leetcode 743","lc 743","Time for a signal from k to reach every node.","A signal starts at k. How long until every node receives it?"],"w":"Graphs Weighted shortest path → Dijkstra. Complexity O(E log V)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Weighted shortest path → Dijkstra.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Dijkstra: always expand the closest unsettled node; answer is the largest shortest distance.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A signal starts at k. How long until every node receives it?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Bellman-Ford: O(V·E).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Heap with (0, k); dist map.<\/li><li>Pop the closest; skip if already settled; relax its edges.<\/li><li>All reached → max distance, else −1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Dijkstra:<\/b> From k.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Max distance.<\/li><li><b>Unreached:<\/b> −1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(E log V)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Directed edges.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef network_delay_time(times, n, k):\n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    for u, v, w in times:\n        graph[u].append((v, w))\n    dist = {}\n    heap = [(0, k)]\n    while heap:\n        d, node = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if node in dist:\n            continue                         # settled earlier with a smaller d\n        dist[node] = d\n        for nb, w in graph[node]:\n            if nb not in dist:\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (d + w, nb))\n    return max(dist.values()) if len(dist) == n else -1","java":"public int networkDelayTime(int[][] times, int n, int k) {\n    Map<Integer, List<int[]>> graph = new HashMap<>();\n    for (int[] t : times) graph.computeIfAbsent(t[0], x -> new ArrayList<>()).add(new int[]{t[1], t[2]});\n    Map<Integer, Integer> dist = new HashMap<>();\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);   // {dist, node}\n    heap.offer(new int[]{0, k});\n    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n        int[] top = heap.poll();\n        int d = top[0], node = top[1];\n        if (dist.containsKey(node)) continue;    // settled earlier with a smaller d\n        dist.put(node, d);\n        for (int[] e : graph.getOrDefault(node, List.of()))\n            if (!dist.containsKey(e[0])) heap.offer(new int[]{d + e[1], e[0]});\n    }\n    return dist.size() == n ? Collections.max(dist.values()) : -1;\n}","cpp":"int networkDelayTime(vector<vector<int>>& times, int n, int k) {\n    vector<vector<pair<int, int>>> graph(n + 1);\n    for (auto& t : times) graph[t[0]].push_back({t[1], t[2]});\n    vector<int> dist(n + 1, -1);\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<>> heap;   // (dist, node)\n    heap.push({0, k});\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        auto [d, node] = heap.top(); heap.pop();\n        if (dist[node] != -1) continue;          // settled earlier with a smaller d\n        dist[node] = d;\n        for (auto [nb, w] : graph[node])\n            if (dist[nb] == -1) heap.push({d + w, nb});\n    }\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        if (dist[i] == -1) return -1;\n        best = max(best, dist[i]);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-743"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/network-delay-time/"]],"ask":"Time for a signal from k to reach every node."},{"i":"p-graphs-787","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Cheapest Flights Within K Stops","sub":"LeetCode 787 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":787,"q":["Cheapest Flights Within K Stops","how to solve Cheapest Flights Within K Stops","Cheapest Flights Within K Stops approach","Cheapest Flights Within K Stops solution","leetcode 787","lc 787","Cheapest price with at most k stops.","Cheapest flight from src to dst using at most k stops."],"w":"Graphs Stop limit → Bellman-Ford rounds. Complexity O(k · E)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Stop limit → Bellman-Ford rounds.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Bellman-Ford limited to k + 1 rounds (copy the distances each round).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Cheapest flight from src to dst using at most k stops.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Plain Dijkstra ignores the stop limit.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>prices[src] = 0.<\/li><li>Repeat k + 1 times: relax every flight using last round&#x27;s prices.<\/li><li>Return prices[dst] or −1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Rounds:<\/b> k + 1.<\/li><li><b>Copy:<\/b> Previous costs.<\/li><li><b>Relax:<\/b> Every flight.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(k · E)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Without the copy, one round can chain several flights.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_cheapest_price(n, flights, src, dst, k):\n    cost = [float(\"inf\")] * n\n    cost[src] = 0\n    for _ in range(k + 1):                   # at most k stops = k + 1 edges\n        nxt = cost[:]                        # copy: read only last round's values\n        for u, v, w in flights:\n            if cost[u] + w < nxt[v]:\n                nxt[v] = cost[u] + w\n        cost = nxt\n    return cost[dst] if cost[dst] < float(\"inf\") else -1","java":"public int findCheapestPrice(int n, int[][] flights, int src, int dst, int k) {\n    int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    int[] cost = new int[n];\n    Arrays.fill(cost, INF);\n    cost[src] = 0;\n    for (int round = 0; round <= k; round++) {   // at most k stops = k + 1 edges\n        int[] nxt = cost.clone();                // copy: read only last round's values\n        for (int[] f : flights)\n            if (cost[f[0]] != INF && cost[f[0]] + f[2] < nxt[f[1]])\n                nxt[f[1]] = cost[f[0]] + f[2];\n        cost = nxt;\n    }\n    return cost[dst] == INF ? -1 : cost[dst];\n}","cpp":"int findCheapestPrice(int n, vector<vector<int>>& flights, int src, int dst, int k) {\n    const int INF = INT_MAX;\n    vector<int> cost(n, INF);\n    cost[src] = 0;\n    for (int round = 0; round <= k; round++) {   // at most k stops = k + 1 edges\n        vector<int> nxt = cost;                  // copy: read only last round's values\n        for (auto& f : flights)\n            if (cost[f[0]] != INF && cost[f[0]] + f[2] < nxt[f[1]])\n                nxt[f[1]] = cost[f[0]] + f[2];\n        cost = nxt;\n    }\n    return cost[dst] == INF ? -1 : cost[dst];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-787"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/cheapest-flights-within-k-stops/"]],"ask":"Cheapest price with at most k stops."},{"i":"p-graphs-1584","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Min Cost to Connect All Points","sub":"LeetCode 1584 · Medium · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":1584,"q":["Min Cost to Connect All Points","how to solve Min Cost to Connect All Points","Min Cost to Connect All Points approach","Min Cost to Connect All Points solution","leetcode 1584","lc 1584","Minimum total Manhattan distance to connect all points.","Connect all points with minimum total Manhattan distance."],"w":"Graphs Connect all cheaply → MST. Complexity O(n² log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Connect all cheaply → MST.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Minimum spanning tree: Prim&#x27;s (grow from one point, always add the cheapest edge out).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Connect all points with minimum total Manhattan distance.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all spanning trees: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Start from point 0 with cost 0.<\/li><li>Pop the cheapest unvisited point from a heap; add its cost.<\/li><li>Push edges from it to all unvisited points.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>MST:<\/b> Prim from point 0.<\/li><li><b>Heap:<\/b> (cost, point).<\/li><li><b>Skip:<\/b> Already connected.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n² log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Dense graph: an O(n²) Prim without a heap is also fine.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef min_cost_connect_points(points):\n    n = len(points)\n    seen, total = set(), 0\n    heap = [(0, 0)]                          # (cost to attach, point)\n    while len(seen) < n:\n        cost, i = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if i in seen:\n            continue\n        seen.add(i)\n        total += cost\n        xi, yi = points[i]\n        for j in range(n):\n            if j not in seen:\n                xj, yj = points[j]\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (abs(xi - xj) + abs(yi - yj), j))\n    return total","java":"public int minCostConnectPoints(int[][] points) {\n    int n = points.length, total = 0, taken = 0;\n    boolean[] seen = new boolean[n];\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);   // {cost to attach, point}\n    heap.offer(new int[]{0, 0});\n    while (taken < n) {\n        int[] top = heap.poll();\n        int i = top[1];\n        if (seen[i]) continue;\n        seen[i] = true;\n        taken++;\n        total += top[0];\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)\n            if (!seen[j])\n                heap.offer(new int[]{Math.abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0])\n                                   + Math.abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1]), j});\n    }\n    return total;\n}","cpp":"int minCostConnectPoints(vector<vector<int>>& points) {\n    int n = points.size(), total = 0, taken = 0;\n    vector<bool> seen(n, false);\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<>> heap;   // (cost to attach, point)\n    heap.push({0, 0});\n    while (taken < n) {\n        auto [cost, i] = heap.top(); heap.pop();\n        if (seen[i]) continue;\n        seen[i] = true;\n        taken++;\n        total += cost;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)\n            if (!seen[j])\n                heap.push({abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0]) + abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1]), j});\n    }\n    return total;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-1584"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/min-cost-to-connect-all-points/"]],"ask":"Minimum total Manhattan distance to connect all points."},{"i":"p-graphs-778","k":"prob","s":"graphs","t":"Swim in Rising Water","sub":"LeetCode 778 · Hard · Graphs","cat":"DSA problems","n":778,"q":["Swim in Rising Water","how to solve Swim in Rising Water","Swim in Rising Water approach","Swim in Rising Water solution","leetcode 778","lc 778","Earliest time to swim from top-left to bottom-right.","Water rises over time; earliest time you can swim from top-left to bottom-right?"],"w":"Graphs Minimize the max → Dijkstra with max. Complexity O(n² log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Minimize the max → Dijkstra with max.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Minimize the maximum height on the path: Dijkstra where a path&#x27;s cost is its highest cell.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Water rises over time; earliest time you can swim from top-left to bottom-right?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every time t with a BFS: O(maxT × n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Heap with (grid[0][0], 0, 0).<\/li><li>Pop the lowest; cost = max(cost, cell).<\/li><li>Reach the end → return its cost.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Cost:<\/b> Max height on the path.<\/li><li><b>Dijkstra:<\/b> Pop the lowest max.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> At the target.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n² log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Binary search on time + BFS also works.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef swim_in_water(grid):\n    n = len(grid)\n    heap, seen = [(grid[0][0], 0, 0)], {(0, 0)}\n    while heap:\n        t, r, c = heapq.heappop(heap)        # lowest \"highest point so far\"\n        if (r, c) == (n - 1, n - 1):\n            return t\n        for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n            if 0 <= nr < n and 0 <= nc < n and (nr, nc) not in seen:\n                seen.add((nr, nc))\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (max(t, grid[nr][nc]), nr, nc))","java":"public int swimInWater(int[][] grid) {\n    int n = grid.length;\n    boolean[][] seen = new boolean[n][n];\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);   // {time, r, c}\n    heap.offer(new int[]{grid[0][0], 0, 0});\n    seen[0][0] = true;\n    int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};\n    while (true) {\n        int[] top = heap.poll();                 // lowest \"highest point so far\"\n        int t = top[0], r = top[1], c = top[2];\n        if (r == n - 1 && c == n - 1) return t;\n        for (int[] d : dirs) {\n            int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < n && nc < n && !seen[nr][nc]) {\n                seen[nr][nc] = true;\n                heap.offer(new int[]{Math.max(t, grid[nr][nc]), nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","cpp":"int swimInWater(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {\n    int n = grid.size();\n    vector<vector<bool>> seen(n, vector<bool>(n, false));\n    priority_queue<array<int, 3>, vector<array<int, 3>>, greater<>> heap;   // (time, r, c)\n    heap.push({grid[0][0], 0, 0});\n    seen[0][0] = true;\n    int dr[] = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc[] = {0, 0, 1, -1};\n    while (true) {\n        auto [t, r, c] = heap.top(); heap.pop(); // lowest \"highest point so far\"\n        if (r == n - 1 && c == n - 1) return t;\n        for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < n && nc < n && !seen[nr][nc]) {\n                seen[nr][nc] = true;\n                heap.push({max(t, grid[nr][nc]), nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","graphs.html#p-778"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/swim-in-rising-water/"]],"ask":"Earliest time to swim from top-left to bottom-right."},{"i":"p-dp-70","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Climbing Stairs","sub":"LeetCode 70 · Easy · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":70,"q":["Climbing Stairs","how to solve Climbing Stairs","Climbing Stairs approach","Climbing Stairs solution","leetcode 70","lc 70","Ways to climb n stairs with 1 or 2 steps.","Ways to climb n stairs taking 1 or 2 steps at a time."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Last move options → sum of smaller states. Complexity O(n), O(1) with two variables","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Last move options → sum of smaller states.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> ways(n) = ways(n − 1) + ways(n − 2): your last step was 1 or 2.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Ways to climb n stairs taking 1 or 2 steps at a time.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Recursion without memory: O(2ⁿ).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>a, b = 1, 1.<\/li><li>Repeat n − 1 times: a, b = b, a + b.<\/li><li>Return b.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> dp[i] = ways to reach i.<\/li><li><b>Recurrence:<\/b> dp[i − 1] + dp[i − 2].<\/li><li><b>Base:<\/b> dp[0] = dp[1] = 1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1) with two variables<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> It’s Fibonacci shifted by one.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def climb_stairs(n):             # 3. table (bottom-up): O(n)\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0], dp[1] = 1, 1          # one way to stand still / take one step\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2]    # last move: 1 step or 2 steps\n    return dp[n]","java":"public int climbStairs(int n) {     // 3. table (bottom-up): O(n)\n    int[] dp = new int[n + 1];\n    dp[0] = 1; dp[1] = 1;              // one way to stand still / take one step\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++)\n        dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2]; // last move: 1 step or 2 steps\n    return dp[n];\n}","cpp":"int climbStairs(int n) {            // 3. table (bottom-up): O(n)\n    vector<int> dp(n + 1);\n    dp[0] = 1; dp[1] = 1;              // one way to stand still / take one step\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++)\n        dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2]; // last move: 1 step or 2 steps\n    return dp[n];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-70"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/climbing-stairs/"]],"ask":"Ways to climb n stairs with 1 or 2 steps."},{"i":"p-dp-746","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Min Cost Climbing Stairs","sub":"LeetCode 746 · Easy · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":746,"q":["Min Cost Climbing Stairs","how to solve Min Cost Climbing Stairs","Min Cost Climbing Stairs approach","Min Cost Climbing Stairs solution","leetcode 746","lc 746","Cheapest way to reach the top past the last step.","Each step has a cost; start at step 0 or 1. Cheapest way past the top?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Min over the last move. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Min over the last move.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> cost to reach i = cost[i] + min(reach i − 1, reach i − 2).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each step has a cost; start at step 0 or 1. Cheapest way past the top?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every path: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>dp[i] = cost[i] + min(dp[i−1], dp[i−2]).<\/li><li>Only two previous values needed.<\/li><li>Answer = min(dp[n−1], dp[n−2]).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> Cheapest to stand on step i.<\/li><li><b>Recurrence:<\/b> min(from i − 1, from i − 2) + its cost.<\/li><li><b>Base:<\/b> Steps 0 and 1 are free to start on.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The top is one past the last index.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def min_cost_climbing_stairs(cost):\n    a = b = 0                       # cheapest way to stand on step i - 2, i - 1\n    for i in range(2, len(cost) + 1):\n        a, b = b, min(b + cost[i - 1], a + cost[i - 2])\n    return b","java":"public int minCostClimbingStairs(int[] cost) {\n    int a = 0, b = 0;                  // cheapest way to stand on step i - 2, i - 1\n    for (int i = 2; i <= cost.length; i++) {\n        int c = Math.min(b + cost[i - 1], a + cost[i - 2]);\n        a = b;\n        b = c;\n    }\n    return b;\n}","cpp":"int minCostClimbingStairs(vector<int>& cost) {\n    int a = 0, b = 0;                  // cheapest way to stand on step i - 2, i - 1\n    for (int i = 2; i <= (int)cost.size(); i++) {\n        int c = min(b + cost[i - 1], a + cost[i - 2]);\n        a = b;\n        b = c;\n    }\n    return b;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-746"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/min-cost-climbing-stairs/"]],"ask":"Cheapest way to reach the top past the last step."},{"i":"p-dp-198","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"House Robber","sub":"LeetCode 198 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":198,"q":["House Robber","how to solve House Robber","House Robber approach","House Robber solution","leetcode 198","lc 198","Max money without robbing adjacent houses.","Rob houses in a row, never two neighbours. Max money?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Neighbour conflict → take or skip. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Neighbour conflict → take or skip.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> At each house: rob it (+ best up to i − 2) or skip it (best up to i − 1).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Rob houses in a row, never two neighbours. Max money?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every subset: 2ⁿ.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>prev2 = best up to i − 2, prev1 = best up to i − 1.<\/li><li>cur = max(prev1, prev2 + nums[i]).<\/li><li>Shift and return prev1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> Best from the first i.<\/li><li><b>Skip:<\/b> dp[i − 1].<\/li><li><b>Take:<\/b> dp[i − 2] + x.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Handle a single house.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def rob(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)                   # dp[i] = best using the first i houses\n    dp[1] = nums[0]\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        skip = dp[i - 1]                 # leave house i alone\n        take = dp[i - 2] + nums[i - 1]   # rob it, so its neighbour is off-limits\n        dp[i] = max(skip, take)\n    return dp[n]","java":"public int rob(int[] nums) {\n    int n = nums.length;\n    int[] dp = new int[n + 1];             // dp[i] = best using the first i houses\n    dp[1] = nums[0];\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        int skip = dp[i - 1];              // leave house i alone\n        int take = dp[i - 2] + nums[i - 1];// rob it, so its neighbour is off-limits\n        dp[i] = Math.max(skip, take);\n    }\n    return dp[n];\n}","cpp":"int rob(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);              // dp[i] = best using the first i houses\n    dp[1] = nums[0];\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        int skip = dp[i - 1];              // leave house i alone\n        int take = dp[i - 2] + nums[i - 1];// rob it, so its neighbour is off-limits\n        dp[i] = max(skip, take);\n    }\n    return dp[n];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-198"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/house-robber/"]],"ask":"Max money without robbing adjacent houses."},{"i":"p-dp-213","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"House Robber II","sub":"LeetCode 213 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":213,"q":["House Robber II","how to solve House Robber II","House Robber II approach","House Robber II solution","leetcode 213","lc 213","Houses in a circle.","House Robber, but the houses form a circle."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Circular → solve two linear cases. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Circular → solve two linear cases.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> The first and last houses can&#x27;t both be robbed: run House Robber twice, once without the first, once without the last.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> House Robber, but the houses form a circle.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If one house, return it.<\/li><li>rob(nums[1:]) and rob(nums[:−1]).<\/li><li>Return the max.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> First and last are neighbours.<\/li><li><b>Split:<\/b> Run without the first, and without the last.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Max of both.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> One house: return it directly.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def rob(nums):\n    def line(houses):\n        prev = cur = 0\n        for x in houses:\n            prev, cur = cur, max(cur, prev + x)   # skip or take\n        return cur\n    if len(nums) == 1:\n        return nums[0]\n    return max(line(nums[:-1]), line(nums[1:]))   # first and last can't both go","java":"public int rob(int[] nums) {\n    int n = nums.length;\n    if (n == 1) return nums[0];\n    return Math.max(line(nums, 0, n - 2), line(nums, 1, n - 1));   // first and last can't both go\n}\n\nint line(int[] nums, int lo, int hi) {\n    int prev = 0, cur = 0;\n    for (int i = lo; i <= hi; i++) {\n        int next = Math.max(cur, prev + nums[i]);   // skip or take\n        prev = cur;\n        cur = next;\n    }\n    return cur;\n}","cpp":"int rob(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    if (n == 1) return nums[0];\n    auto line = [&](int lo, int hi) {\n        int prev = 0, cur = 0;\n        for (int i = lo; i <= hi; i++) {\n            int next = max(cur, prev + nums[i]);    // skip or take\n            prev = cur;\n            cur = next;\n        }\n        return cur;\n    };\n    return max(line(0, n - 2), line(1, n - 1));     // first and last can't both go\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-213"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/house-robber-ii/"]],"ask":"Houses in a circle."},{"i":"p-dp-91","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Decode Ways","sub":"LeetCode 91 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":91,"q":["Decode Ways","how to solve Decode Ways","Decode Ways approach","Decode Ways solution","leetcode 91","lc 91","Ways to decode digits where 1–26 map to letters.","How many ways to decode a digit string (1 → A … 26 → Z)?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Split a string → dp over prefixes. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Split a string → dp over prefixes.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> ways(i) = ways(i−1) if the 1-digit code is valid + ways(i−2) if the 2-digit code is 10–26.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How many ways to decode a digit string (1 → A … 26 → Z)?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all splits: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>dp[0] = 1.<\/li><li>Add dp[i−1] if s[i−1] ≠ &#x27;0&#x27;.<\/li><li>Add dp[i−2] if s[i−2..i−1] is between 10 and 26.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> dp[i] = ways for s[:i].<\/li><li><b>One digit:<\/b> If not “0”.<\/li><li><b>Two digits:<\/b> If 10–26.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> “0” alone is invalid; “06” is not 6.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def num_decodings(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)                   # dp[i] = ways to decode s[:i]\n    dp[0] = 1\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if s[i - 1] != \"0\":\n            dp[i] += dp[i - 1]           # last piece is one digit\n        if i > 1 and \"10\" <= s[i - 2:i] <= \"26\":\n            dp[i] += dp[i - 2]           # last piece is two digits\n    return dp[n]","java":"public int numDecodings(String s) {\n    int n = s.length();\n    int[] dp = new int[n + 1];             // dp[i] = ways to decode s[0..i)\n    dp[0] = 1;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        if (s.charAt(i - 1) != '0') dp[i] += dp[i - 1];        // last piece is one digit\n        if (i > 1) {\n            int two = Integer.parseInt(s.substring(i - 2, i));\n            if (two >= 10 && two <= 26) dp[i] += dp[i - 2];     // last piece is two digits\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[n];\n}","cpp":"int numDecodings(string s) {\n    int n = s.size();\n    vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);              // dp[i] = ways to decode s[0..i)\n    dp[0] = 1;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        if (s[i - 1] != '0') dp[i] += dp[i - 1];               // last piece is one digit\n        if (i > 1) {\n            int two = (s[i - 2] - '0') * 10 + (s[i - 1] - '0');\n            if (two >= 10 && two <= 26) dp[i] += dp[i - 2];     // last piece is two digits\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[n];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-91"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/decode-ways/"]],"ask":"Ways to decode digits where 1–26 map to letters."},{"i":"p-dp-152","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Maximum Product Subarray","sub":"LeetCode 152 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":152,"q":["Maximum Product Subarray","how to solve Maximum Product Subarray","Maximum Product Subarray approach","Maximum Product Subarray solution","leetcode 152","lc 152","Largest product of a contiguous subarray.","Largest product of a contiguous subarray (negatives allowed)."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Negatives flip → track max and min. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Negatives flip → track max and min.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A negative flips max and min, so track both the largest and smallest product ending here.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Largest product of a contiguous subarray (negatives allowed).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Every subarray: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>hi = lo = best = nums[0].<\/li><li>For x: candidates x, hi·x, lo·x → new hi = max, lo = min.<\/li><li>best = max(best, hi).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Track:<\/b> Max and min ending here.<\/li><li><b>Each x:<\/b> Candidates x, hi·x, lo·x.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Best hi.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Compute both from the old hi/lo at once.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_product(nums):\n    best = hi = lo = nums[0]             # max and min product ending here\n    for x in nums[1:]:\n        candidates = (x, hi * x, lo * x)   # a negative flips min into max\n        hi, lo = max(candidates), min(candidates)\n        best = max(best, hi)\n    return best","java":"public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {\n    int best = nums[0], hi = nums[0], lo = nums[0];   // max and min product ending here\n    for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        int x = nums[i];\n        int a = hi * x, b = lo * x;                    // a negative flips min into max\n        hi = Math.max(x, Math.max(a, b));\n        lo = Math.min(x, Math.min(a, b));\n        best = Math.max(best, hi);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int maxProduct(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int best = nums[0], hi = nums[0], lo = nums[0];   // max and min product ending here\n    for (size_t i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n        int x = nums[i];\n        int a = hi * x, b = lo * x;                    // a negative flips min into max\n        hi = max({x, a, b});\n        lo = min({x, a, b});\n        best = max(best, hi);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-152"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-product-subarray/"]],"ask":"Largest product of a contiguous subarray."},{"i":"p-dp-300","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Longest Increasing Subsequence","sub":"LeetCode 300 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":300,"q":["Longest Increasing Subsequence","how to solve Longest Increasing Subsequence","Longest Increasing Subsequence approach","Longest Increasing Subsequence solution","leetcode 300","lc 300","Length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.","Length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Best ending here → dp[i] over earlier j. Complexity O(n²) or O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Best ending here → dp[i] over earlier j.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> O(n²) DP, or O(n log n): keep tails[k] = smallest tail of an increasing subsequence of length k + 1.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> All subsequences: 2ⁿ.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each x, binary search the first tail ≥ x.<\/li><li>Replace it with x (or append if none).<\/li><li>Answer = len(tails).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> Best ending at i.<\/li><li><b>Extend:<\/b> From any smaller earlier value.<\/li><li><b>Fast:<\/b> Tails + bisect.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n²) or O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> tails isn’t the subsequence itself; only its length is right.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def length_of_lis(nums):\n    dp = [1] * len(nums)                 # dp[i] = longest increasing run ending at i\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if nums[j] < nums[i]:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)   # extend the run ending at j\n    return max(dp)","java":"public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {\n    int[] dp = new int[nums.length];       // dp[i] = longest increasing run ending at i\n    Arrays.fill(dp, 1);\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)\n            if (nums[j] < nums[i])\n                dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);   // extend the run ending at j\n        best = Math.max(best, dp[i]);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int lengthOfLIS(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<int> dp(nums.size(), 1);        // dp[i] = longest increasing run ending at i\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++)\n        for (size_t j = 0; j < i; j++)\n            if (nums[j] < nums[i])\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);        // extend the run ending at j\n    return *max_element(dp.begin(), dp.end());\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-300"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-increasing-subsequence/"]],"ask":"Length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence."},{"i":"p-dp-5","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Longest Palindromic Substring","sub":"LeetCode 5 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":5,"q":["Longest Palindromic Substring","how to solve Longest Palindromic Substring","Longest Palindromic Substring approach","Longest Palindromic Substring solution","leetcode 5","lc 5","Longest palindrome inside s.","Longest palindrome inside s."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Palindromic substrings → expand around centers. Complexity O(n²), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Palindromic substrings → expand around centers.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Every palindrome has a centre. Expand around each of the 2n − 1 centres.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Longest palindrome inside s.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check every substring: O(n³).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each i, expand around (i, i) and (i, i + 1).<\/li><li>While ends match, widen.<\/li><li>Keep the longest.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Centers:<\/b> 2n − 1 of them.<\/li><li><b>Expand:<\/b> While ends match.<\/li><li><b>Track:<\/b> Longest.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n²), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Don’t forget even-length centers.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def longest_palindrome(s):\n    best = \"\"\n    for center in range(len(s)):\n        for l, r in ((center, center), (center, center + 1)):   # odd, even\n            while l >= 0 and r < len(s) and s[l] == s[r]:\n                l, r = l - 1, r + 1\n            if r - l - 1 > len(best):\n                best = s[l + 1:r]\n    return best","java":"public String longestPalindrome(String s) {\n    int bestL = 0, bestLen = 0;\n    for (int center = 0; center < s.length(); center++)\n        for (int odd = 0; odd < 2; odd++) {            // odd, even\n            int l = center, r = center + odd;\n            while (l >= 0 && r < s.length() && s.charAt(l) == s.charAt(r)) { l--; r++; }\n            if (r - l - 1 > bestLen) { bestLen = r - l - 1; bestL = l + 1; }\n        }\n    return s.substring(bestL, bestL + bestLen);\n}","cpp":"string longestPalindrome(string s) {\n    int n = s.size(), bestL = 0, bestLen = 0;\n    for (int center = 0; center < n; center++)\n        for (int odd = 0; odd < 2; odd++) {            // odd, even\n            int l = center, r = center + odd;\n            while (l >= 0 && r < n && s[l] == s[r]) { l--; r++; }\n            if (r - l - 1 > bestLen) { bestLen = r - l - 1; bestL = l + 1; }\n        }\n    return s.substr(bestL, bestLen);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-5"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-palindromic-substring/"]],"ask":"Longest palindrome inside s."},{"i":"p-dp-647","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Palindromic Substrings","sub":"LeetCode 647 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":647,"q":["Palindromic Substrings","how to solve Palindromic Substrings","Palindromic Substrings approach","Palindromic Substrings solution","leetcode 647","lc 647","Count palindromic substrings.","How many substrings are palindromes?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Palindromes → expand around centers. Complexity O(n²)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Palindromes → expand around centers.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Expand around each centre and count every successful expansion.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How many substrings are palindromes?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check every substring: O(n³).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each centre (odd and even):<\/li><li>While l ≥ 0, r &lt; n and s[l] == s[r]: count += 1, widen.<\/li><li>Return count.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Centers:<\/b> Each index and each gap.<\/li><li><b>Expand:<\/b> Count each success.<\/li><li><b>Total:<\/b> Sum.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n²)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Single characters count.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def count_substrings(s):\n    count = 0\n    for center in range(len(s)):\n        for l, r in ((center, center), (center, center + 1)):\n            while l >= 0 and r < len(s) and s[l] == s[r]:\n                count += 1                  # every expansion is one palindrome\n                l, r = l - 1, r + 1\n    return count","java":"public int countSubstrings(String s) {\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int center = 0; center < s.length(); center++)\n        for (int odd = 0; odd < 2; odd++) {\n            int l = center, r = center + odd;\n            while (l >= 0 && r < s.length() && s.charAt(l) == s.charAt(r)) {\n                count++;                       // every expansion is one palindrome\n                l--; r++;\n            }\n        }\n    return count;\n}","cpp":"int countSubstrings(string s) {\n    int n = s.size(), count = 0;\n    for (int center = 0; center < n; center++)\n        for (int odd = 0; odd < 2; odd++) {\n            int l = center, r = center + odd;\n            while (l >= 0 && r < n && s[l] == s[r]) {\n                count++;                       // every expansion is one palindrome\n                l--; r++;\n            }\n        }\n    return count;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-647"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/palindromic-substrings/"]],"ask":"Count palindromic substrings."},{"i":"p-dp-139","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Word Break","sub":"LeetCode 139 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":139,"q":["Word Break","how to solve Word Break","Word Break approach","Word Break solution","leetcode 139","lc 139","Can s be segmented into dictionary words?","Can s be split into dictionary words?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Segmentation → dp over prefixes. Complexity O(n²)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Segmentation → dp over prefixes.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> dp[i] = can the first i characters be split? True if some dp[j] is True and s[j:i] is a word.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Can s be split into dictionary words?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all splits: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>dp[0] = True.<\/li><li>For i, check j &lt; i with dp[j] and s[j:i] in the set.<\/li><li>Return dp[n].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> dp[i] for prefix i.<\/li><li><b>Try:<\/b> Every last word s[j:i].<\/li><li><b>Base:<\/b> dp[0] = True.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n²)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Limit j to the max word length for speed.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def word_break(s, word_dict):\n    words = set(word_dict)\n    n = len(s)\n    dp = [True] + [False] * n            # dp[i]: s[:i] can be segmented\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if dp[j] and s[j:i] in words:  # a good prefix + one dictionary word\n                dp[i] = True\n                break\n    return dp[n]","java":"public boolean wordBreak(String s, List<String> wordDict) {\n    Set<String> words = new HashSet<>(wordDict);\n    int n = s.length();\n    boolean[] dp = new boolean[n + 1];     // dp[i]: s[0..i) can be segmented\n    dp[0] = true;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)\n        for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)\n            if (dp[j] && words.contains(s.substring(j, i))) {   // a good prefix + one word\n                dp[i] = true;\n                break;\n            }\n    return dp[n];\n}","cpp":"bool wordBreak(string s, vector<string>& wordDict) {\n    unordered_set<string> words(wordDict.begin(), wordDict.end());\n    int n = s.size();\n    vector<bool> dp(n + 1, false);         // dp[i]: s[0..i) can be segmented\n    dp[0] = true;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)\n        for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)\n            if (dp[j] && words.count(s.substr(j, i - j))) {     // a good prefix + one word\n                dp[i] = true;\n                break;\n            }\n    return dp[n];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-139"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/word-break/"]],"ask":"Can s be segmented into dictionary words?"},{"i":"p-dp-322","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Coin Change","sub":"LeetCode 322 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":322,"q":["Coin Change","how to solve Coin Change","Coin Change approach","Coin Change solution","leetcode 322","lc 322","Fewest coins for amount.","Fewest coins to make the amount (unlimited coins)."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Fewest reusable items → unbounded knapsack. Complexity O(amount · coins)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Fewest reusable items → unbounded knapsack.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> dp[a] = 1 + min(dp[a − coin]).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fewest coins to make the amount (unlimited coins).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Greedy biggest-first: fails (e.g. coins 1, 3, 4 for 6).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>dp[0] = 0, others ∞.<\/li><li>For a from 1 to amount, for each coin ≤ a: dp[a] = min(dp[a], dp[a − c] + 1).<\/li><li>∞ → −1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> dp[a].<\/li><li><b>Recurrence:<\/b> min dp[a − c] + 1.<\/li><li><b>Base:<\/b> dp[0] = 0, others ∞.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(amount · coins)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Greedy fails for coins like [1, 3, 4].<\/p>","c":{"py":"def coin_change(coins, amount):\n    INF = float(\"inf\")\n    dp = [0] + [INF] * amount            # dp[a] = fewest coins to make a\n    for a in range(1, amount + 1):\n        for c in coins:\n            if c <= a and dp[a - c] + 1 < dp[a]:\n                dp[a] = dp[a - c] + 1    # the last coin was c\n    return dp[amount] if dp[amount] != INF else -1","java":"public int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {\n    int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];        // dp[a] = fewest coins to make a\n    Arrays.fill(dp, INF);\n    dp[0] = 0;\n    for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++)\n        for (int c : coins)\n            if (c <= a && dp[a - c] != INF && dp[a - c] + 1 < dp[a])\n                dp[a] = dp[a - c] + 1;     // the last coin was c\n    return dp[amount] == INF ? -1 : dp[amount];\n}","cpp":"int coinChange(vector<int>& coins, int amount) {\n    const int INF = INT_MAX;\n    vector<int> dp(amount + 1, INF);       // dp[a] = fewest coins to make a\n    dp[0] = 0;\n    for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++)\n        for (int c : coins)\n            if (c <= a && dp[a - c] != INF && dp[a - c] + 1 < dp[a])\n                dp[a] = dp[a - c] + 1;     // the last coin was c\n    return dp[amount] == INF ? -1 : dp[amount];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-322"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/coin-change/"]],"ask":"Fewest coins for amount."},{"i":"p-dp-518","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Coin Change II","sub":"LeetCode 518 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":518,"q":["Coin Change II","how to solve Coin Change II","Coin Change II approach","Coin Change II solution","leetcode 518","lc 518","Number of combinations making amount.","How many coin combinations make the amount? (Order doesn't matter.)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Count combinations → items outer. Complexity O(amount · coins)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Count combinations → items outer.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Loop coins on the outside so each combination is counted once.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> How many coin combinations make the amount? (Order doesn&#x27;t matter.)<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Count sequences: counts orders too.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>dp[0] = 1.<\/li><li>For each coin, for a from coin to amount: dp[a] += dp[a − coin].<\/li><li>Return dp[amount].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> dp[a] = ways.<\/li><li><b>Loops:<\/b> Coins outer.<\/li><li><b>Base:<\/b> dp[0] = 1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(amount · coins)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Swapping loops counts orderings instead.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def change(amount, coins):\n    dp = [1] + [0] * amount              # dp[a] = combinations making a\n    for c in coins:                      # coins OUTER: each combination once\n        for a in range(c, amount + 1):\n            dp[a] += dp[a - c]\n    return dp[amount]","java":"public int change(int amount, int[] coins) {\n    int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];        // dp[a] = combinations making a\n    dp[0] = 1;\n    for (int c : coins)                    // coins OUTER: each combination once\n        for (int a = c; a <= amount; a++)\n            dp[a] += dp[a - c];\n    return dp[amount];\n}","cpp":"int change(int amount, vector<int>& coins) {\n    vector<unsigned long long> dp(amount + 1, 0);   // dp[a] = combinations making a\n    dp[0] = 1;\n    for (int c : coins)                    // coins OUTER: each combination once\n        for (int a = c; a <= amount; a++)\n            dp[a] += dp[a - c];\n    return dp[amount];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-518"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/coin-change-ii/"]],"ask":"Number of combinations making amount."},{"i":"p-dp-416","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Partition Equal Subset Sum","sub":"LeetCode 416 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":416,"q":["Partition Equal Subset Sum","how to solve Partition Equal Subset Sum","Partition Equal Subset Sum approach","Partition Equal Subset Sum solution","leetcode 416","lc 416","Split into two subsets with equal sums?","Can the array be split into two subsets with equal sums?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Equal halves → subset sum. Complexity O(n · total)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Equal halves → subset sum.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Equivalent to: is there a subset summing to total / 2? 0/1 knapsack on reachable sums.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Can the array be split into two subsets with equal sums?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all subsets: 2ⁿ.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If total is odd → False.<\/li><li>dp = set/booleans of reachable sums, starting {0}.<\/li><li>For each number, add it to every reachable sum (backwards for arrays). Check target.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Reduce:<\/b> Subset summing to total / 2.<\/li><li><b>State:<\/b> dp[s] reachable.<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> Sums backwards.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · total)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Odd total → False immediately.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def can_partition(nums):\n    total = sum(nums)\n    if total % 2:\n        return False\n    target = total // 2\n    dp = [True] + [False] * target       # dp[s]: some subset sums to s\n    for x in nums:\n        for s in range(target, x - 1, -1):   # backwards: use x at most once\n            dp[s] = dp[s] or dp[s - x]\n    return dp[target]","java":"public boolean canPartition(int[] nums) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) total += x;\n    if (total % 2 == 1) return false;\n    int target = total / 2;\n    boolean[] dp = new boolean[target + 1];    // dp[s]: some subset sums to s\n    dp[0] = true;\n    for (int x : nums)\n        for (int s = target; s >= x; s--)      // backwards: use x at most once\n            dp[s] = dp[s] || dp[s - x];\n    return dp[target];\n}","cpp":"bool canPartition(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int total = accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0);\n    if (total % 2) return false;\n    int target = total / 2;\n    vector<bool> dp(target + 1, false);        // dp[s]: some subset sums to s\n    dp[0] = true;\n    for (int x : nums)\n        for (int s = target; s >= x; s--)      // backwards: use x at most once\n            dp[s] = dp[s] || dp[s - x];\n    return dp[target];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-416"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-equal-subset-sum/"]],"ask":"Split into two subsets with equal sums?"},{"i":"p-dp-494","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Target Sum","sub":"LeetCode 494 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":494,"q":["Target Sum","how to solve Target Sum","Target Sum approach","Target Sum solution","leetcode 494","lc 494","Ways to assign + and − to reach target.","Ways to put + / − before each number to reach target."],"w":"Dynamic Programming ± signs → count subsets. Complexity O(n · total)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> ± signs → count subsets.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Pick a subset P to be positive: sum(P) = (total + target) / 2 → count subsets with that sum.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Ways to put + / − before each number to reach target.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> 2ⁿ sign choices.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>If (total + target) is odd or |target| &gt; total → 0.<\/li><li>dp[0] = 1; for each x, for s from goal down to x: dp[s] += dp[s − x].<\/li><li>Return dp[goal].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Reduce:<\/b> Plus-subset sums to (total + target) / 2.<\/li><li><b>Count:<\/b> Counting knapsack.<\/li><li><b>Loop:<\/b> Backwards.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · total)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Check parity and |target| ≤ total.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_target_sum_ways(nums, target):\n    total = sum(nums)\n    if abs(target) > total or (total + target) % 2:\n        return 0\n    want = (total + target) // 2         # the subset that gets a + sign\n    dp = [1] + [0] * want\n    for x in nums:\n        for s in range(want, x - 1, -1):\n            dp[s] += dp[s - x]\n    return dp[want]","java":"public int findTargetSumWays(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) total += x;\n    if (Math.abs(target) > total || (total + target) % 2 != 0) return 0;\n    int want = (total + target) / 2;           // the subset that gets a + sign\n    int[] dp = new int[want + 1];\n    dp[0] = 1;\n    for (int x : nums)\n        for (int s = want; s >= x; s--)\n            dp[s] += dp[s - x];\n    return dp[want];\n}","cpp":"int findTargetSumWays(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    int total = accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0);\n    if (abs(target) > total || (total + target) % 2) return 0;\n    int want = (total + target) / 2;           // the subset that gets a + sign\n    vector<int> dp(want + 1, 0);\n    dp[0] = 1;\n    for (int x : nums)\n        for (int s = want; s >= x; s--)\n            dp[s] += dp[s - x];\n    return dp[want];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-494"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/target-sum/"]],"ask":"Ways to assign + and − to reach target."},{"i":"p-dp-62","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Unique Paths","sub":"LeetCode 62 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":62,"q":["Unique Paths","how to solve Unique Paths","Unique Paths approach","Unique Paths solution","leetcode 62","lc 62","Paths from top-left to bottom-right moving right/down.","Paths from top-left to bottom-right moving only right or down."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Grid paths → above + left. Complexity O(m · n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Grid paths → above + left.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> paths(r, c) = paths(r − 1, c) + paths(r, c − 1).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Paths from top-left to bottom-right moving only right or down.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Enumerate paths: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>First row and column are 1.<\/li><li>Fill the rest with top + left.<\/li><li>Return the bottom-right (one row of memory is enough).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> dp[r][c].<\/li><li><b>Recurrence:<\/b> Above + left.<\/li><li><b>Base:<\/b> First row/col = 1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(m · n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Combinatorics: C(m + n − 2, m − 1) also works.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def unique_paths(m, n):\n    dp = [[1] * n for _ in range(m)]     # first row and column: one way\n    for r in range(1, m):\n        for c in range(1, n):\n            dp[r][c] = dp[r - 1][c] + dp[r][c - 1]   # from above or the left\n    return dp[m - 1][n - 1]","java":"public int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {\n    int[][] dp = new int[m][n];\n    for (int[] row : dp) Arrays.fill(row, 1);   // first row and column: one way\n    for (int r = 1; r < m; r++)\n        for (int c = 1; c < n; c++)\n            dp[r][c] = dp[r - 1][c] + dp[r][c - 1];   // from above or the left\n    return dp[m - 1][n - 1];\n}","cpp":"int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(m, vector<int>(n, 1));   // first row and column: one way\n    for (int r = 1; r < m; r++)\n        for (int c = 1; c < n; c++)\n            dp[r][c] = dp[r - 1][c] + dp[r][c - 1];   // from above or the left\n    return dp[m - 1][n - 1];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-62"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/unique-paths/"]],"ask":"Paths from top-left to bottom-right moving right/down."},{"i":"p-dp-1143","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Longest Common Subsequence","sub":"LeetCode 1143 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":1143,"q":["Longest Common Subsequence","how to solve Longest Common Subsequence","Longest Common Subsequence approach","Longest Common Subsequence solution","leetcode 1143","lc 1143","Length of the LCS of two strings.","Length of the longest subsequence common to both strings."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Two strings → prefix table. Complexity O(m · n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Two strings → prefix table.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Grid dp[i][j]: if letters match, 1 + diagonal; else max(top, left).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Length of the longest subsequence common to both strings.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Compare all subsequences: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>(m + 1) × (n + 1) zeros.<\/li><li>a[i−1] == b[j−1] → dp[i−1][j−1] + 1.<\/li><li>Else max(dp[i−1][j], dp[i][j−1]).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>State:<\/b> dp[i][j] over prefixes.<\/li><li><b>Match:<\/b> Diagonal + 1.<\/li><li><b>Else:<\/b> max(above, left).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(m · n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Index with i − 1 and j − 1 inside the table.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def longest_common_subsequence(a, b):\n    m, n = len(a), len(b)\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]   # dp[i][j]: a[:i] vs b[:j]\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]:\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1             # match: extend diagonal\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])  # drop one char\n    return dp[m][n]","java":"public int longestCommonSubsequence(String a, String b) {\n    int m = a.length(), n = b.length();\n    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];        // dp[i][j]: a[0..i) vs b[0..j)\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)\n            if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1))\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;              // match: extend diagonal\n            else\n                dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);   // drop one char\n    return dp[m][n];\n}","cpp":"int longestCommonSubsequence(string a, string b) {\n    int m = a.size(), n = b.size();\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(m + 1, vector<int>(n + 1, 0));   // dp[i][j]: a[0..i) vs b[0..j)\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)\n            if (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1])\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;              // match: extend diagonal\n            else\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);   // drop one char\n    return dp[m][n];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-1143"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-common-subsequence/"]],"ask":"Length of the LCS of two strings."},{"i":"p-dp-72","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Edit Distance","sub":"LeetCode 72 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":72,"q":["Edit Distance","how to solve Edit Distance","Edit Distance approach","Edit Distance solution","leetcode 72","lc 72","Min insert/delete/replace operations to turn a into b.","Fewest inserts, deletes or replacements to turn word a into word b."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Edits → above / left / diagonal. Complexity O(m · n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Edits → above / left / diagonal.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> dp[i][j] = cost for prefixes; match → diagonal, else 1 + min(insert, delete, replace).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fewest inserts, deletes or replacements to turn word a into word b.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all edit sequences: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>dp[i][0] = i, dp[0][j] = j.<\/li><li>Same letters → dp[i−1][j−1].<\/li><li>Else 1 + min(dp[i−1][j], dp[i][j−1], dp[i−1][j−1]).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Row = inserts, col = deletes.<\/li><li><b>Match:<\/b> Diagonal.<\/li><li><b>Else:<\/b> 1 + min of three.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(m · n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Initialize the borders, not zeros.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def min_distance(a, b):\n    m, n = len(a), len(b)\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n    for i in range(m + 1):\n        dp[i][0] = i                     # delete everything\n    for j in range(n + 1):\n        dp[0][j] = j                     # insert everything\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]:\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]              # free\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + min(dp[i - 1][j],         # delete\n                                   dp[i][j - 1],         # insert\n                                   dp[i - 1][j - 1])     # replace\n    return dp[m][n]","java":"public int minDistance(String a, String b) {\n    int m = a.length(), n = b.length();\n    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\n    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;     // delete everything\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;     // insert everything\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)\n            if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1))\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];       // free\n            else\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(dp[i - 1][j - 1],             // replace\n                               Math.min(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])); // delete, insert\n    return dp[m][n];\n}","cpp":"int minDistance(string a, string b) {\n    int m = a.size(), n = b.size();\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(m + 1, vector<int>(n + 1, 0));\n    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;     // delete everything\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;     // insert everything\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)\n            if (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1])\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];       // free\n            else\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + min({dp[i - 1][j],          // delete\n                                    dp[i][j - 1],          // insert\n                                    dp[i - 1][j - 1]});    // replace\n    return dp[m][n];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-72"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/edit-distance/"]],"ask":"Min insert/delete/replace operations to turn a into b."},{"i":"p-dp-309","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown","sub":"LeetCode 309 · Medium · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":309,"q":["Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown","how to solve Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown","Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown approach","Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown solution","leetcode 309","lc 309","Max profit with a one-day cooldown after selling.","Max stock profit with unlimited trades, but a 1-day rest after each sell."],"w":"Dynamic Programming Stock rules → state machine. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Stock rules → state machine.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Three states per day: holding, just sold, resting.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Max stock profit with unlimited trades, but a 1-day rest after each sell.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every buy/sell set: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>hold = max(hold, rest − price).<\/li><li>sold = hold + price (from yesterday&#x27;s hold).<\/li><li>rest = max(rest, yesterday&#x27;s sold). Answer max(sold, rest).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>States:<\/b> hold, sold, rest.<\/li><li><b>Transitions:<\/b> From yesterday.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> max(sold, rest).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Update all three simultaneously.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_profit(prices):\n    hold, sold, rest = float(\"-inf\"), 0, 0    # best profit in each state\n    for p in prices:\n        hold, sold, rest = (max(hold, rest - p),   # keep holding, or buy after resting\n                            hold + p,              # sell today\n                            max(rest, sold))       # do nothing; cooldown ends\n    return max(sold, rest)","java":"public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n    int hold = Integer.MIN_VALUE / 2, sold = 0, rest = 0;   // best profit in each state\n    for (int p : prices) {\n        int newHold = Math.max(hold, rest - p);   // keep holding, or buy after resting\n        int newSold = hold + p;                   // sell today\n        int newRest = Math.max(rest, sold);       // do nothing; cooldown ends\n        hold = newHold; sold = newSold; rest = newRest;\n    }\n    return Math.max(sold, rest);\n}","cpp":"int maxProfit(vector<int>& prices) {\n    int hold = INT_MIN / 2, sold = 0, rest = 0;   // best profit in each state\n    for (int p : prices) {\n        int newHold = max(hold, rest - p);        // keep holding, or buy after resting\n        int newSold = hold + p;                   // sell today\n        int newRest = max(rest, sold);            // do nothing; cooldown ends\n        hold = newHold; sold = newSold; rest = newRest;\n    }\n    return max(sold, rest);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-309"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-cooldown/"]],"ask":"Max profit with a one-day cooldown after selling."},{"i":"p-dp-312","k":"prob","s":"dp","t":"Burst Balloons","sub":"LeetCode 312 · Hard · Dynamic Programming","cat":"DSA problems","n":312,"q":["Burst Balloons","how to solve Burst Balloons","Burst Balloons approach","Burst Balloons solution","leetcode 312","lc 312","Max coins from bursting all balloons.","Burst balloons for coins (left × self × right). Max coins?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Range splitting → interval DP by length. Complexity O(n³)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Range splitting → interval DP by length.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Think of the LAST balloon to burst in an interval: its neighbours are the interval&#x27;s fixed ends.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Burst balloons for coins (left × self × right). Max coins?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every order: n!.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Pad with 1 on both sides.<\/li><li>dp[l][r] = max over k in (l, r) of dp[l][k] + a[l]·a[k]·a[r] + dp[k][r].<\/li><li>Fill by increasing interval length; answer dp[0][n+1].<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pad:<\/b> 1s on both ends.<\/li><li><b>State:<\/b> dp[l][r], exclusive range.<\/li><li><b>Choose:<\/b> k burst LAST.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n³)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Choosing the first balloon creates dependent sides; choose the last.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_coins(nums):\n    a = [1] + nums + [1]\n    n = len(a)\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]          # dp[l][r]: best strictly inside (l, r)\n    for length in range(2, n):\n        for l in range(n - length):\n            r = l + length\n            for k in range(l + 1, r):         # k is the LAST balloon burst in (l, r)\n                dp[l][r] = max(dp[l][r], dp[l][k] + a[l] * a[k] * a[r] + dp[k][r])\n    return dp[0][n - 1]","java":"public int maxCoins(int[] nums) {\n    int n = nums.length + 2;\n    int[] a = new int[n];\n    a[0] = a[n - 1] = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) a[i + 1] = nums[i];\n    int[][] dp = new int[n][n];                // dp[l][r]: best strictly inside (l, r)\n    for (int len = 2; len < n; len++)\n        for (int l = 0; l + len < n; l++) {\n            int r = l + len;\n            for (int k = l + 1; k < r; k++)    // k is the LAST balloon burst in (l, r)\n                dp[l][r] = Math.max(dp[l][r], dp[l][k] + a[l] * a[k] * a[r] + dp[k][r]);\n        }\n    return dp[0][n - 1];\n}","cpp":"int maxCoins(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<int> a = {1};\n    a.insert(a.end(), nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    a.push_back(1);\n    int n = a.size();\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(n, vector<int>(n, 0));   // dp[l][r]: best strictly inside (l, r)\n    for (int len = 2; len < n; len++)\n        for (int l = 0; l + len < n; l++) {\n            int r = l + len;\n            for (int k = l + 1; k < r; k++)    // k is the LAST balloon burst in (l, r)\n                dp[l][r] = max(dp[l][r], dp[l][k] + a[l] * a[k] * a[r] + dp[k][r]);\n        }\n    return dp[0][n - 1];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","dp.html#p-312"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/burst-balloons/"]],"ask":"Max coins from bursting all balloons."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-53","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Maximum Subarray","sub":"LeetCode 53 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":53,"q":["Maximum Subarray","how to solve Maximum Subarray","Maximum Subarray approach","Maximum Subarray solution","leetcode 53","lc 53","Largest sum of a contiguous subarray.","Largest sum of any contiguous subarray."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Max subarray → Kadane. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Max subarray → Kadane.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Kadane: a running sum that has gone negative only hurts; restart from the current number.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Largest sum of any contiguous subarray.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Every subarray: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>cur = best = nums[0].<\/li><li>cur = max(x, cur + x).<\/li><li>best = max(best, cur).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Run:<\/b> cur = max(x, cur + x).<\/li><li><b>Best:<\/b> Track max.<\/li><li><b>All negative:<\/b> Still works.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Initialize with nums[0], not 0.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def max_sub_array(nums):\n    best = cur = nums[0]\n    for x in nums[1:]:\n        cur = max(x, cur + x)            # extend, or drop a negative prefix\n        best = max(best, cur)\n    return best","java":"public int maxSubArray(int[] nums) {\n    int best = nums[0], cur = nums[0];\n    for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        cur = Math.max(nums[i], cur + nums[i]);   // extend, or drop a negative prefix\n        best = Math.max(best, cur);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int maxSubArray(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int best = nums[0], cur = nums[0];\n    for (size_t i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n        cur = max(nums[i], cur + nums[i]);   // extend, or drop a negative prefix\n        best = max(best, cur);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-53"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-subarray/"]],"ask":"Largest sum of a contiguous subarray."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-55","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Jump Game","sub":"LeetCode 55 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":55,"q":["Jump Game","how to solve Jump Game","Jump Game approach","Jump Game solution","leetcode 55","lc 55","Can you reach the last index?","Each value is your max jump length. Can you reach the last index?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Reach the end → farthest reach. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Reach the end → farthest reach.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Track the farthest index reachable so far.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each value is your max jump length. Can you reach the last index?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all jumps: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>far = 0.<\/li><li>For i: if i &gt; far → False; far = max(far, i + nums[i]).<\/li><li>True.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Track:<\/b> Farthest reach.<\/li><li><b>Fail:<\/b> i &gt; reach.<\/li><li><b>Update:<\/b> max(reach, i + x).<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> DP works but is O(n²).<\/p>","c":{"py":"def can_jump(nums):\n    reach = 0                            # farthest index reachable so far\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        if i > reach:\n            return False                 # stuck before reaching i\n        reach = max(reach, i + x)\n    return True","java":"public boolean canJump(int[] nums) {\n    int reach = 0;                         // farthest index reachable so far\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        if (i > reach) return false;       // stuck before reaching i\n        reach = Math.max(reach, i + nums[i]);\n    }\n    return true;\n}","cpp":"bool canJump(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int reach = 0;                         // farthest index reachable so far\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); i++) {\n        if (i > reach) return false;       // stuck before reaching i\n        reach = max(reach, i + nums[i]);\n    }\n    return true;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-55"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/jump-game/"]],"ask":"Can you reach the last index?"},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-45","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Jump Game II","sub":"LeetCode 45 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":45,"q":["Jump Game II","how to solve Jump Game II","Jump Game II approach","Jump Game II solution","leetcode 45","lc 45","Fewest jumps to the last index.","Fewest jumps to reach the last index."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Fewest jumps → implicit BFS levels. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Fewest jumps → implicit BFS levels.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Greedy BFS by levels: the current jump covers [start, end]; the next covers up to the farthest reach.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fewest jumps to reach the last index.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> BFS over indexes: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>jumps = 0, end = 0, far = 0.<\/li><li>For i &lt; n − 1: far = max(far, i + nums[i]).<\/li><li>If i == end: jumps += 1, end = far.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Levels:<\/b> Indexes reachable in k jumps.<\/li><li><b>far:<\/b> Next level’s edge.<\/li><li><b>Jump:<\/b> When i == end.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Loop to n − 2, or you count an extra jump at the end.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def jump(nums):\n    jumps, end, far = 0, 0, 0            # indexes up to end need 'jumps' jumps\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 1):\n        far = max(far, i + nums[i])\n        if i == end:                     # this jump's range is used up\n            jumps += 1\n            end = far\n    return jumps","java":"public int jump(int[] nums) {\n    int jumps = 0, end = 0, far = 0;       // indexes up to end need 'jumps' jumps\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {\n        far = Math.max(far, i + nums[i]);\n        if (i == end) {                    // this jump's range is used up\n            jumps++;\n            end = far;\n        }\n    }\n    return jumps;\n}","cpp":"int jump(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int jumps = 0, end = 0, far = 0;       // indexes up to end need 'jumps' jumps\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size() - 1; i++) {\n        far = max(far, i + nums[i]);\n        if (i == end) {                    // this jump's range is used up\n            jumps++;\n            end = far;\n        }\n    }\n    return jumps;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-45"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/jump-game-ii/"]],"ask":"Fewest jumps to the last index."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-134","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Gas Station","sub":"LeetCode 134 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":134,"q":["Gas Station","how to solve Gas Station","Gas Station approach","Gas Station solution","leetcode 134","lc 134","Start index to complete the circuit.","Find the start station that lets you drive the whole circle (or −1)."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Negative tank → restart after it. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Negative tank → restart after it.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> If total gas ≥ total cost, a start exists. Whenever the tank goes negative, the next station is the new candidate.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Find the start station that lets you drive the whole circle (or −1).<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try every start: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>total += gas − cost; tank += gas − cost.<\/li><li>If tank &lt; 0: start = i + 1, tank = 0.<\/li><li>total &lt; 0 → −1, else start.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Check:<\/b> sum(gas) ≥ sum(cost).<\/li><li><b>Scan:<\/b> Tank of gains.<\/li><li><b>Reset:<\/b> On negative tank.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The total check is what makes the single pass correct.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def can_complete_circuit(gas, cost):\n    if sum(gas) < sum(cost):\n        return -1                        # not enough fuel in total\n    tank, start = 0, 0\n    for i in range(len(gas)):\n        tank += gas[i] - cost[i]\n        if tank < 0:                     # can't reach i + 1 from start\n            start, tank = i + 1, 0       # so nothing in [start, i] works\n    return start","java":"public int canCompleteCircuit(int[] gas, int[] cost) {\n    int total = 0, tank = 0, start = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < gas.length; i++) {\n        total += gas[i] - cost[i];\n        tank += gas[i] - cost[i];\n        if (tank < 0) {                    // can't reach i + 1 from start\n            start = i + 1;                 // so nothing in [start, i] works\n            tank = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return total < 0 ? -1 : start;         // not enough fuel in total -> -1\n}","cpp":"int canCompleteCircuit(vector<int>& gas, vector<int>& cost) {\n    int total = 0, tank = 0, start = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)gas.size(); i++) {\n        total += gas[i] - cost[i];\n        tank += gas[i] - cost[i];\n        if (tank < 0) {                    // can't reach i + 1 from start\n            start = i + 1;                 // so nothing in [start, i] works\n            tank = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return total < 0 ? -1 : start;         // not enough fuel in total -> -1\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-134"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/gas-station/"]],"ask":"Start index to complete the circuit."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-846","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Hand of Straights","sub":"LeetCode 846 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":846,"q":["Hand of Straights","how to solve Hand of Straights","Hand of Straights approach","Hand of Straights solution","leetcode 846","lc 846","Can the hand be split into groups of consecutive cards?","Can the cards be split into groups of groupSize consecutive values?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Consecutive groups → smallest must start one. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Consecutive groups → smallest must start one.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> The smallest remaining card must start a group. Count with a map and go in sorted order.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Can the cards be split into groups of groupSize consecutive values?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all groupings: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Count cards; sort distinct values.<\/li><li>For the smallest with count c &gt; 0, the next groupSize values must each have ≥ c.<\/li><li>Subtract c from each; any shortfall → False.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Count:<\/b> Counter.<\/li><li><b>Start:<\/b> Smallest remaining card.<\/li><li><b>Take:<\/b> The next size − 1 values.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Subtract n copies at once, not one at a time.<\/p>","c":{"py":"from collections import Counter\n\ndef is_n_straight_hand(hand, size):\n    if len(hand) % size:\n        return False\n    count = Counter(hand)\n    for x in sorted(count):                  # the smallest card must start a group\n        n = count[x]\n        if n:\n            for y in range(x, x + size):\n                if count[y] < n:\n                    return False\n                count[y] -= n\n    return True","java":"public boolean isNStraightHand(int[] hand, int size) {\n    if (hand.length % size != 0) return false;\n    TreeMap<Integer, Integer> count = new TreeMap<>();   // sorted keys\n    for (int x : hand) count.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);\n    for (int x : count.keySet()) {         // the smallest card must start a group\n        int n = count.get(x);\n        if (n == 0) continue;\n        for (int y = x; y < x + size; y++) {\n            int have = count.getOrDefault(y, 0);\n            if (have < n) return false;\n            count.put(y, have - n);\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}","cpp":"bool isNStraightHand(vector<int>& hand, int size) {\n    if (hand.size() % size) return false;\n    map<int, int> count;                   // sorted keys\n    for (int x : hand) count[x]++;\n    for (auto& [x, n0] : count) {          // the smallest card must start a group\n        int n = n0;\n        if (n == 0) continue;\n        for (int y = x; y < x + size; y++) {\n            if (count[y] < n) return false;\n            count[y] -= n;\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-846"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/hand-of-straights/"]],"ask":"Can the hand be split into groups of consecutive cards?"},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-1899","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Merge Triplets to Form Target","sub":"LeetCode 1899 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":1899,"q":["Merge Triplets to Form Target","how to solve Merge Triplets to Form Target","Merge Triplets to Form Target approach","Merge Triplets to Form Target solution","leetcode 1899","lc 1899","Can max-merging some triplets reach target exactly?","Can max-merging some triplets produce exactly the target triplet?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Max-merge → discard anything that would overshoot. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Max-merge → discard anything that would overshoot.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Ignore any triplet with a value above target in any position; check the rest cover each target value.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Can max-merging some triplets produce exactly the target triplet?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all subsets: 2ⁿ.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Skip triplets exceeding the target anywhere.<\/li><li>For good ones, note which positions equal the target.<\/li><li>True if all three positions are matched.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Filter:<\/b> Drop triplets that overshoot anywhere.<\/li><li><b>Collect:<\/b> Positions that match target.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> All three covered.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> A triplet with any value above target can never be used.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def merge_triplets(triplets, target):\n    good = set()\n    for t in triplets:\n        if all(t[i] <= target[i] for i in range(3)):   # never overshoots\n            for i in range(3):\n                if t[i] == target[i]:\n                    good.add(i)\n    return len(good) == 3","java":"public boolean mergeTriplets(int[][] triplets, int[] target) {\n    boolean[] good = new boolean[3];\n    for (int[] t : triplets)\n        if (t[0] <= target[0] && t[1] <= target[1] && t[2] <= target[2])   // never overshoots\n            for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)\n                if (t[i] == target[i]) good[i] = true;\n    return good[0] && good[1] && good[2];\n}","cpp":"bool mergeTriplets(vector<vector<int>>& triplets, vector<int>& target) {\n    bool good[3] = {false, false, false};\n    for (auto& t : triplets)\n        if (t[0] <= target[0] && t[1] <= target[1] && t[2] <= target[2])   // never overshoots\n            for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)\n                if (t[i] == target[i]) good[i] = true;\n    return good[0] && good[1] && good[2];\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-1899"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-triplets-to-form-target-triplet/"]],"ask":"Can max-merging some triplets reach target exactly?"},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-763","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Partition Labels","sub":"LeetCode 763 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":763,"q":["Partition Labels","how to solve Partition Labels","Partition Labels approach","Partition Labels solution","leetcode 763","lc 763","Most parts with each letter in one part.","Split the string into as many parts as possible so each letter appears in only one part."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Letter in one part → last occurrence. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Letter in one part → last occurrence.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A part can&#x27;t end before the last occurrence of any letter inside it.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Split the string into as many parts as possible so each letter appears in only one part.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Record each letter&#x27;s last index.<\/li><li>Extend end = max(end, last[c]) while scanning.<\/li><li>When i == end, cut a part.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Last:<\/b> Last index per letter.<\/li><li><b>Stretch:<\/b> end = max(end, last[ch]).<\/li><li><b>Cut:<\/b> i == end.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Return sizes, not the parts.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def partition_labels(s):\n    last = {ch: i for i, ch in enumerate(s)}\n    res, start, end = [], 0, 0\n    for i, ch in enumerate(s):\n        end = max(end, last[ch])             # this part must reach ch's last use\n        if i == end:\n            res.append(end - start + 1)\n            start = i + 1\n    return res","java":"public List<Integer> partitionLabels(String s) {\n    int[] last = new int[26];\n    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) last[s.charAt(i) - 'a'] = i;\n    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    int start = 0, end = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\n        end = Math.max(end, last[s.charAt(i) - 'a']);   // this part must reach ch's last use\n        if (i == end) {\n            res.add(end - start + 1);\n            start = i + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> partitionLabels(string s) {\n    int last[26] = {};\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)s.size(); i++) last[s[i] - 'a'] = i;\n    vector<int> res;\n    int start = 0, end = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)s.size(); i++) {\n        end = max(end, last[s[i] - 'a']);  // this part must reach ch's last use\n        if (i == end) {\n            res.push_back(end - start + 1);\n            start = i + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-763"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-labels/"]],"ask":"Most parts with each letter in one part."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-678","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Valid Parenthesis String","sub":"LeetCode 678 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":678,"q":["Valid Parenthesis String","how to solve Valid Parenthesis String","Valid Parenthesis String approach","Valid Parenthesis String solution","leetcode 678","lc 678","Is s valid if “*” can be “(”, “)” or empty?","Is the string valid if '*' can be '(', ')' or empty?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Wildcard choices → range of states. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Wildcard choices → range of states.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Track the range [lo, hi] of possible open-bracket counts.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Is the string valid if &#x27;*&#x27; can be &#x27;(&#x27;, &#x27;)&#x27; or empty?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all 3^stars options.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>&#x27;(&#x27; → lo += 1, hi += 1. &#x27;)&#x27; → both −= 1. &#x27;*&#x27; → lo −= 1, hi += 1.<\/li><li>If hi &lt; 0 → False; lo = max(lo, 0).<\/li><li>Valid if lo == 0 at the end.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Range:<\/b> lo, hi open counts.<\/li><li><b>Fail:<\/b> hi &lt; 0.<\/li><li><b>Clamp:<\/b> lo ≥ 0.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Clamp lo at 0, but check hi first.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def check_valid_string(s):\n    lo = hi = 0                              # range of possible open counts\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch == \"(\":\n            lo, hi = lo + 1, hi + 1\n        elif ch == \")\":\n            lo, hi = lo - 1, hi - 1\n        else:                                # \"*\" is \")\", \"\" or \"(\"\n            lo, hi = lo - 1, hi + 1\n        if hi < 0:\n            return False                     # too many \")\" whatever we choose\n        lo = max(lo, 0)\n    return lo == 0","java":"public boolean checkValidString(String s) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = 0;                    // range of possible open counts\n    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n        if (ch == '(') { lo++; hi++; }\n        else if (ch == ')') { lo--; hi--; }\n        else { lo--; hi++; }               // '*' is ')', '' or '('\n        if (hi < 0) return false;          // too many ')' whatever we choose\n        lo = Math.max(lo, 0);\n    }\n    return lo == 0;\n}","cpp":"bool checkValidString(string s) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = 0;                    // range of possible open counts\n    for (char ch : s) {\n        if (ch == '(') { lo++; hi++; }\n        else if (ch == ')') { lo--; hi--; }\n        else { lo--; hi++; }               // '*' is ')', '' or '('\n        if (hi < 0) return false;          // too many ')' whatever we choose\n        lo = max(lo, 0);\n    }\n    return lo == 0;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-678"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-parenthesis-string/"]],"ask":"Is s valid if “*” can be “(”, “)” or empty?"},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-135","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Candy","sub":"LeetCode 135 · Hard · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":135,"q":["Candy","how to solve Candy","Candy approach","Candy solution","leetcode 135","lc 135","Minimum candies so higher-rated children beat their neighbours.","Give candies so higher-rated kids get more than their neighbours. Minimum total?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Both neighbours → two passes. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Both neighbours → two passes.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Two passes: left-to-right handles left neighbours, right-to-left handles right neighbours.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Give candies so higher-rated kids get more than their neighbours. Minimum total?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Repeated adjustment passes: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>c = [1] × n.<\/li><li>Left pass: rating up → c[i] = c[i−1] + 1.<\/li><li>Right pass: rating up from the right → c[i] = max(c[i], c[i+1] + 1). Sum.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Pass 1:<\/b> Left neighbour.<\/li><li><b>Pass 2:<\/b> Right neighbour, with max.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Sum.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Use max in pass 2 or you break pass 1’s guarantee.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def candy(ratings):\n    n = len(ratings)\n    c = [1] * n\n    for i in range(1, n):                    # beat the left neighbour\n        if ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]:\n            c[i] = c[i - 1] + 1\n    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):           # beat the right neighbour too\n        if ratings[i] > ratings[i + 1]:\n            c[i] = max(c[i], c[i + 1] + 1)\n    return sum(c)","java":"public int candy(int[] ratings) {\n    int n = ratings.length;\n    int[] c = new int[n];\n    Arrays.fill(c, 1);\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)            // beat the left neighbour\n        if (ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]) c[i] = c[i - 1] + 1;\n    for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--)       // beat the right neighbour too\n        if (ratings[i] > ratings[i + 1]) c[i] = Math.max(c[i], c[i + 1] + 1);\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int x : c) sum += x;\n    return sum;\n}","cpp":"int candy(vector<int>& ratings) {\n    int n = ratings.size();\n    vector<int> c(n, 1);\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)            // beat the left neighbour\n        if (ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]) c[i] = c[i - 1] + 1;\n    for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--)       // beat the right neighbour too\n        if (ratings[i] > ratings[i + 1]) c[i] = max(c[i], c[i + 1] + 1);\n    return accumulate(c.begin(), c.end(), 0);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-135"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/candy/"]],"ask":"Minimum candies so higher-rated children beat their neighbours."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-1029","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Two City Scheduling","sub":"LeetCode 1029 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":1029,"q":["Two City Scheduling","how to solve Two City Scheduling","Two City Scheduling approach","Two City Scheduling solution","leetcode 1029","lc 1029","Send exactly n people to each city at minimum cost.","Send exactly half the people to city A and half to B, minimum total cost."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Two choices with a quota → sort by difference. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Two choices with a quota → sort by difference.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort by how much cheaper A is than B (costA − costB); first half go to A.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Send exactly half the people to city A and half to B, minimum total cost.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all splits: C(2n, n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort by a − b.<\/li><li>First n → A, rest → B.<\/li><li>Sum the chosen costs.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Key:<\/b> costA − costB.<\/li><li><b>Sort:<\/b> Ascending.<\/li><li><b>Split:<\/b> First half to A.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Sorting by costA alone ignores what B would cost.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def two_city_sched_cost(costs):\n    costs.sort(key=lambda c: c[0] - c[1])    # most \"prefers A\" first\n    n = len(costs) // 2\n    return sum(c[0] for c in costs[:n]) + sum(c[1] for c in costs[n:])","java":"public int twoCitySchedCost(int[][] costs) {\n    Arrays.sort(costs, (a, b) -> (a[0] - a[1]) - (b[0] - b[1]));   // most \"prefers A\" first\n    int n = costs.length / 2, total = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < costs.length; i++) total += i < n ? costs[i][0] : costs[i][1];\n    return total;\n}","cpp":"int twoCitySchedCost(vector<vector<int>>& costs) {\n    sort(costs.begin(), costs.end(), [](auto& a, auto& b) {\n        return a[0] - a[1] < b[0] - b[1];                  // most \"prefers A\" first\n    });\n    int n = costs.size() / 2, total = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)costs.size(); i++) total += i < n ? costs[i][0] : costs[i][1];\n    return total;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-1029"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/two-city-scheduling/"]],"ask":"Send exactly n people to each city at minimum cost."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-56","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Merge Intervals","sub":"LeetCode 56 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":56,"q":["Merge Intervals","how to solve Merge Intervals","Merge Intervals approach","Merge Intervals solution","leetcode 56","lc 56","Merge all overlapping intervals.","Merge all overlapping intervals."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Merge → sort by start. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Merge → sort by start.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort by start; then each interval either extends the last merged one or starts a new one.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Merge all overlapping intervals.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Compare every pair repeatedly: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort by start.<\/li><li>If start ≤ last.end → last.end = max(last.end, end).<\/li><li>Else append it.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> By start.<\/li><li><b>Overlap:<\/b> s ≤ last end.<\/li><li><b>Extend:<\/b> max of ends.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Extend with max, since a contained interval ends earlier.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def merge(intervals):\n    intervals.sort()                     # by start\n    res = [intervals[0]]\n    for s, e in intervals[1:]:\n        if s <= res[-1][1]:              # overlaps the last merged interval\n            res[-1][1] = max(res[-1][1], e)\n        else:\n            res.append([s, e])\n    return res","java":"public int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {\n    Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);   // by start\n    List<int[]> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int[] iv : intervals) {\n        int[] last = res.isEmpty() ? null : res.get(res.size() - 1);\n        if (last != null && iv[0] <= last[1])        // overlaps the last merged interval\n            last[1] = Math.max(last[1], iv[1]);\n        else\n            res.add(iv);\n    }\n    return res.toArray(new int[0][]);\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> merge(vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {\n    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end());        // by start\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    for (auto& iv : intervals) {\n        if (!res.empty() && iv[0] <= res.back()[1])  // overlaps the last merged interval\n            res.back()[1] = max(res.back()[1], iv[1]);\n        else\n            res.push_back(iv);\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-56"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-intervals/"]],"ask":"Merge all overlapping intervals."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-57","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Insert Interval","sub":"LeetCode 57 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":57,"q":["Insert Interval","how to solve Insert Interval","Insert Interval approach","Insert Interval solution","leetcode 57","lc 57","Insert into sorted, non-overlapping intervals and merge.","Insert a new interval into a sorted, non-overlapping list and merge."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Already sorted → three phases. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Already sorted → three phases.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Three phases: copy intervals ending before it, merge the overlapping ones, copy the rest.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Insert a new interval into a sorted, non-overlapping list and merge.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Append and re-merge everything: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Add intervals with end &lt; new.start.<\/li><li>While intervals overlap new, widen new.<\/li><li>Add new, then the remaining intervals.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Before:<\/b> End &lt; new start.<\/li><li><b>Absorb:<\/b> Start ≤ new end.<\/li><li><b>After:<\/b> The rest.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> No need to sort again.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def insert(intervals, new):\n    res, i, n = [], 0, len(intervals)\n    while i < n and intervals[i][1] < new[0]:      # 1. entirely before\n        res.append(intervals[i])\n        i += 1\n    while i < n and intervals[i][0] <= new[1]:     # 2. overlapping: absorb\n        new = [min(new[0], intervals[i][0]), max(new[1], intervals[i][1])]\n        i += 1\n    res.append(new)\n    res.extend(intervals[i:])                      # 3. entirely after\n    return res","java":"public int[][] insert(int[][] intervals, int[] add) {\n    List<int[]> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    int i = 0, n = intervals.length;\n    while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < add[0]) res.add(intervals[i++]);   // 1. entirely before\n    int lo = add[0], hi = add[1];\n    while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= hi) {                             // 2. overlapping: absorb\n        lo = Math.min(lo, intervals[i][0]);\n        hi = Math.max(hi, intervals[i][1]);\n        i++;\n    }\n    res.add(new int[]{lo, hi});\n    while (i < n) res.add(intervals[i++]);                               // 3. entirely after\n    return res.toArray(new int[0][]);\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> insert(vector<vector<int>>& intervals, vector<int>& add) {\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    size_t i = 0, n = intervals.size();\n    while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < add[0]) res.push_back(intervals[i++]);   // 1. before\n    int lo = add[0], hi = add[1];\n    while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= hi) {                                   // 2. absorb\n        lo = min(lo, intervals[i][0]);\n        hi = max(hi, intervals[i][1]);\n        i++;\n    }\n    res.push_back({lo, hi});\n    while (i < n) res.push_back(intervals[i++]);                               // 3. after\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-57"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/insert-interval/"]],"ask":"Insert into sorted, non-overlapping intervals and merge."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-435","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Non-overlapping Intervals","sub":"LeetCode 435 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":435,"q":["Non-overlapping Intervals","how to solve Non-overlapping Intervals","Non-overlapping Intervals approach","Non-overlapping Intervals solution","leetcode 435","lc 435","Fewest removals to make the rest non-overlapping.","Remove the fewest intervals so the rest don't overlap."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Remove the fewest → sort by end. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Remove the fewest → sort by end.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Keep as many as possible: always keep the interval that ends earliest (classic activity selection).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Remove the fewest intervals so the rest don&#x27;t overlap.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Try all subsets: 2ⁿ.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort by end.<\/li><li>Keep an interval if start ≥ last kept end.<\/li><li>Answer = n − kept.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> By end.<\/li><li><b>Keep:<\/b> If s ≥ last end.<\/li><li><b>Count:<\/b> The rest.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Touching ends don’t overlap here.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def erase_overlap_intervals(intervals):\n    intervals.sort(key=lambda x: x[1])   # by END: finish early, leave room\n    removed, end = 0, float(\"-inf\")\n    for s, e in intervals:\n        if s >= end:\n            end = e                      # keep it\n        else:\n            removed += 1                 # overlaps a kept one: drop it\n    return removed","java":"public int eraseOverlapIntervals(int[][] intervals) {\n    Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));   // by END: finish early\n    int removed = 0;\n    long end = Long.MIN_VALUE;\n    for (int[] iv : intervals) {\n        if (iv[0] >= end) end = iv[1];     // keep it\n        else removed++;                    // overlaps a kept one: drop it\n    }\n    return removed;\n}","cpp":"int eraseOverlapIntervals(vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {\n    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end(),\n         [](auto& a, auto& b) { return a[1] < b[1]; });   // by END: finish early\n    int removed = 0;\n    long long end = LLONG_MIN;\n    for (auto& iv : intervals) {\n        if (iv[0] >= end) end = iv[1];     // keep it\n        else removed++;                    // overlaps a kept one: drop it\n    }\n    return removed;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-435"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/non-overlapping-intervals/"]],"ask":"Fewest removals to make the rest non-overlapping."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-452","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons","sub":"LeetCode 452 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":452,"q":["Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons","how to solve Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons","Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons approach","Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons solution","leetcode 452","lc 452","Fewest vertical arrows to burst all balloons.","Fewest vertical arrows to burst all balloons (intervals)."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Fewest stabbing points → sort by end. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Fewest stabbing points → sort by end.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort by end; shoot at the end of the first balloon; it pops everything starting before that point.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fewest vertical arrows to burst all balloons (intervals).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort by end.<\/li><li>arrows = 1, pos = first end.<\/li><li>A balloon starting after pos needs a new arrow at its end.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sort:<\/b> By end.<\/li><li><b>Shoot:<\/b> At the end of the first unburst balloon.<\/li><li><b>Skip:<\/b> Balloons that start ≤ that point.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Touching counts as hit here: use s &gt; pos for a new arrow.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def find_min_arrow_shots(points):\n    points.sort(key=lambda p: p[1])          # by end\n    arrows, pos = 0, float(\"-inf\")\n    for s, e in points:\n        if s > pos:                          # not hit by the last arrow\n            arrows += 1\n            pos = e                          # shoot as far right as possible\n    return arrows","java":"public int findMinArrowShots(int[][] points) {\n    Arrays.sort(points, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));   // by end\n    int arrows = 0;\n    long pos = Long.MIN_VALUE;\n    for (int[] p : points)\n        if (p[0] > pos) {                  // not hit by the last arrow\n            arrows++;\n            pos = p[1];                    // shoot as far right as possible\n        }\n    return arrows;\n}","cpp":"int findMinArrowShots(vector<vector<int>>& points) {\n    sort(points.begin(), points.end(),\n         [](auto& a, auto& b) { return a[1] < b[1]; });   // by end\n    int arrows = 0;\n    long long pos = LLONG_MIN;\n    for (auto& p : points)\n        if (p[0] > pos) {                  // not hit by the last arrow\n            arrows++;\n            pos = p[1];                    // shoot as far right as possible\n        }\n    return arrows;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-452"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-arrows-to-burst-balloons/"]],"ask":"Fewest vertical arrows to burst all balloons."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-253","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Meeting Rooms II","sub":"LeetCode 253 · Medium · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":253,"q":["Meeting Rooms II","how to solve Meeting Rooms II","Meeting Rooms II approach","Meeting Rooms II solution","leetcode 253","lc 253","Minimum rooms for all meetings.","Minimum meeting rooms."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Max overlap → sweep. Complexity O(n log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Max overlap → sweep.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort starts and ends separately; sweep: a start needs a room, an end frees one.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Minimum meeting rooms.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check overlaps pairwise: O(n²).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort starts, sort ends.<\/li><li>For each start: if it&#x27;s ≥ the earliest end, reuse (move the end pointer).<\/li><li>Else rooms += 1.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Events:<\/b> Sorted starts and ends.<\/li><li><b>Sweep:<\/b> +1 start, −1 end.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> Max in use.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Free rooms whose end ≤ the new start first.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def min_meeting_rooms(intervals):\n    starts = sorted(s for s, e in intervals)\n    ends = sorted(e for s, e in intervals)\n    rooms = best = 0\n    j = 0\n    for s in starts:\n        while j < len(ends) and ends[j] <= s:\n            rooms -= 1                   # a meeting ended: free its room\n            j += 1\n        rooms += 1                       # this meeting needs a room\n        best = max(best, rooms)\n    return best","java":"public int minMeetingRooms(int[][] intervals) {\n    int n = intervals.length;\n    int[] starts = new int[n], ends = new int[n];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { starts[i] = intervals[i][0]; ends[i] = intervals[i][1]; }\n    Arrays.sort(starts);\n    Arrays.sort(ends);\n    int rooms = 0, best = 0, j = 0;\n    for (int s : starts) {\n        while (j < n && ends[j] <= s) { rooms--; j++; }   // a meeting ended: free its room\n        rooms++;                                          // this meeting needs a room\n        best = Math.max(best, rooms);\n    }\n    return best;\n}","cpp":"int minMeetingRooms(vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {\n    vector<int> starts, ends;\n    for (auto& iv : intervals) { starts.push_back(iv[0]); ends.push_back(iv[1]); }\n    sort(starts.begin(), starts.end());\n    sort(ends.begin(), ends.end());\n    int rooms = 0, best = 0;\n    size_t j = 0;\n    for (int s : starts) {\n        while (j < ends.size() && ends[j] <= s) { rooms--; j++; }   // a meeting ended\n        rooms++;                                                    // this one needs a room\n        best = max(best, rooms);\n    }\n    return best;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-253"],["LeetCode (Premium)","https://leetcode.com/problems/meeting-rooms-ii/"]],"ask":"Minimum rooms for all meetings."},{"i":"p-greedy-intervals-1851","k":"prob","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Minimum Interval to Include Each Query","sub":"LeetCode 1851 · Hard · Greedy & Intervals","cat":"DSA problems","n":1851,"q":["Minimum Interval to Include Each Query","how to solve Minimum Interval to Include Each Query","Minimum Interval to Include Each Query approach","Minimum Interval to Include Each Query solution","leetcode 1851","lc 1851","For each query, size of the smallest interval containing it.","For each query point, the size of the smallest interval containing it."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Many interval queries → offline sweep + heap. Complexity O((n + q) log n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Many interval queries → offline sweep + heap.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Sort queries and intervals; add intervals that start before the query to a min-heap by size; drop ones that ended.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For each query point, the size of the smallest interval containing it.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Check every interval per query: O(n·q).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>Sort intervals by start, queries by value (remember original order).<\/li><li>Push (size, end) for intervals with start ≤ q.<\/li><li>Pop while end &lt; q; heap top is the answer (or −1).<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Offline:<\/b> Sort queries and intervals.<\/li><li><b>Add:<\/b> Intervals that started.<\/li><li><b>Drop:<\/b> Heap tops that ended.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O((n + q) log n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Map answers back to the original query order.<\/p>","c":{"py":"import heapq\n\ndef min_interval(intervals, queries):\n    intervals.sort()\n    res, heap, i = {}, [], 0\n    for q in sorted(queries):                         # answer queries in order\n        while i < len(intervals) and intervals[i][0] <= q:\n            l, r = intervals[i]\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (r - l + 1, r))      # (size, end)\n            i += 1\n        while heap and heap[0][1] < q:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)                       # ended before q\n        res[q] = heap[0][0] if heap else -1\n    return [res[q] for q in queries]","java":"public int[] minInterval(int[][] intervals, int[] queries) {\n    Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);\n    Integer[] order = new Integer[queries.length];\n    for (int i = 0; i < order.length; i++) order[i] = i;\n    Arrays.sort(order, (a, b) -> queries[a] - queries[b]);    // answer queries in order\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);   // {size, end}\n    int[] res = new int[queries.length];\n    int i = 0;\n    for (int qi : order) {\n        int q = queries[qi];\n        while (i < intervals.length && intervals[i][0] <= q) {\n            heap.offer(new int[]{intervals[i][1] - intervals[i][0] + 1, intervals[i][1]});\n            i++;\n        }\n        while (!heap.isEmpty() && heap.peek()[1] < q) heap.poll();   // ended before q\n        res[qi] = heap.isEmpty() ? -1 : heap.peek()[0];\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> minInterval(vector<vector<int>>& intervals, vector<int>& queries) {\n    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end());\n    vector<int> order(queries.size());\n    iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);\n    sort(order.begin(), order.end(), [&](int a, int b) { return queries[a] < queries[b]; });\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<>> heap;   // (size, end)\n    vector<int> res(queries.size());\n    size_t i = 0;\n    for (int qi : order) {                 // answer queries in order\n        int q = queries[qi];\n        while (i < intervals.size() && intervals[i][0] <= q) {\n            heap.push({intervals[i][1] - intervals[i][0] + 1, intervals[i][1]});\n            i++;\n        }\n        while (!heap.empty() && heap.top().second < q) heap.pop();   // ended before q\n        res[qi] = heap.empty() ? -1 : heap.top().first;\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","greedy-intervals.html#p-1851"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-interval-to-include-each-query/"]],"ask":"For each query, size of the smallest interval containing it."},{"i":"p-bits-136","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Single Number","sub":"LeetCode 136 · Easy · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":136,"q":["Single Number","how to solve Single Number","Single Number approach","Single Number solution","leetcode 136","lc 136","Every element twice except one. Find it with O(1) space.","Every number appears twice except one. Find it with O(1) space."],"w":"Bit Manipulation Pairs cancel → XOR. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Pairs cancel → XOR.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x, so XOR-ing everything leaves the single number.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Every number appears twice except one. Find it with O(1) space.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Count with a dict: O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>res = 0.<\/li><li>res ^= each number.<\/li><li>Return res.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Pairs.<\/li><li><b>Trick:<\/b> XOR all.<\/li><li><b>Result:<\/b> The single.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> A set works but costs O(n) space.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def single_number(nums):\n    acc = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        acc ^= x                 # pairs cancel: a ^ a = 0\n    return acc","java":"public int singleNumber(int[] nums) {\n    int acc = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) acc ^= x;     // pairs cancel: a ^ a = 0\n    return acc;\n}","cpp":"int singleNumber(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int acc = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) acc ^= x;     // pairs cancel: a ^ a = 0\n    return acc;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-136"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/single-number/"]],"ask":"Every element twice except one. Find it with O(1) space."},{"i":"p-bits-191","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Number of 1 Bits","sub":"LeetCode 191 · Easy · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":191,"q":["Number of 1 Bits","how to solve Number of 1 Bits","Number of 1 Bits approach","Number of 1 Bits solution","leetcode 191","lc 191","Count set bits.","Count the 1 bits in a number."],"w":"Bit Manipulation Count set bits → n & (n − 1). Complexity O(set bits)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Count set bits → n &amp; (n − 1).<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> n &amp; (n − 1) removes the lowest set bit; count how many times you can do it.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Count the 1 bits in a number.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Convert to a binary string and count: fine but not the bit trick.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>count = 0.<\/li><li>While n: n &amp;= n − 1, count += 1.<\/li><li>Return count.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Loop:<\/b> While n.<\/li><li><b>Erase:<\/b> n &amp;= n − 1.<\/li><li><b>Count:<\/b> Iterations.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(set bits)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> bin(n).count(&quot;1&quot;) is fine in Python too.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def hamming_weight(n):\n    count = 0\n    while n:\n        n &= n - 1               # erase the lowest set bit\n        count += 1\n    return count","java":"// n is treated as unsigned (Java has no unsigned int)\npublic int hammingWeight(int n) {\n    int count = 0;\n    while (n != 0) {\n        n &= n - 1;                  // erase the lowest set bit\n        count++;\n    }\n    return count;\n}","cpp":"int hammingWeight(uint32_t n) {\n    int count = 0;\n    while (n) {\n        n &= n - 1;                  // erase the lowest set bit\n        count++;\n    }\n    return count;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-191"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-1-bits/"]],"ask":"Count set bits."},{"i":"p-bits-338","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Counting Bits","sub":"LeetCode 338 · Easy · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":338,"q":["Counting Bits","how to solve Counting Bits","Counting Bits approach","Counting Bits solution","leetcode 338","lc 338","Set-bit counts for every number 0…n.","For every i from 0 to n, how many 1 bits does i have?"],"w":"Bit Manipulation Counts for a range → bit DP. Complexity O(n)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Counts for a range → bit DP.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> bits[i] = bits[i &gt;&gt; 1] + (i &amp; 1): drop the last bit and reuse a smaller answer.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For every i from 0 to n, how many 1 bits does i have?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Count bits of each number separately: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>bits[0] = 0.<\/li><li>For i from 1: bits[i] = bits[i &gt;&gt; 1] + (i &amp; 1).<\/li><li>Return bits.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>DP:<\/b> dp[i &gt;&gt; 1].<\/li><li><b>Plus:<\/b> i &amp; 1.<\/li><li><b>Order:<\/b> Increasing i.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Also works: dp[i] = dp[i &amp; (i − 1)] + 1.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def count_bits(n):\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        dp[i] = dp[i >> 1] + (i & 1)     # i is (i >> 1) shifted, plus its last bit\n    return dp","java":"public int[] countBits(int n) {\n    int[] dp = new int[n + 1];\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)\n        dp[i] = dp[i >> 1] + (i & 1);   // i is (i >> 1) shifted, plus its last bit\n    return dp;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> countBits(int n) {\n    vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)\n        dp[i] = dp[i >> 1] + (i & 1);   // i is (i >> 1) shifted, plus its last bit\n    return dp;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-338"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/counting-bits/"]],"ask":"Set-bit counts for every number 0…n."},{"i":"p-bits-190","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Reverse Bits","sub":"LeetCode 190 · Easy · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":190,"q":["Reverse Bits","how to solve Reverse Bits","Reverse Bits approach","Reverse Bits solution","leetcode 190","lc 190","Reverse the bits of a 32-bit unsigned integer.","Reverse the 32 bits of an unsigned integer."],"w":"Bit Manipulation Bit by bit → shift and mask. Complexity O(32)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Bit by bit → shift and mask.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Take bits off the right of n and push them onto the right of the result, 32 times.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Reverse the 32 bits of an unsigned integer.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> String reversal: works, slower.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>res = 0.<\/li><li>32 times: res = (res &lt;&lt; 1) | (n &amp; 1); n &gt;&gt;= 1.<\/li><li>Return res.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Loop:<\/b> 32 times.<\/li><li><b>Shift:<\/b> res left, append n &amp; 1.<\/li><li><b>Consume:<\/b> n &gt;&gt;= 1.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(32)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Loop exactly 32 times, even after n becomes 0.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def reverse_bits(n):\n    res = 0\n    for _ in range(32):\n        res = (res << 1) | (n & 1)       # move n's lowest bit onto res\n        n >>= 1\n    return res","java":"// n is treated as unsigned\npublic int reverseBits(int n) {\n    int res = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {\n        res = (res << 1) | (n & 1);  // move n's lowest bit onto res\n        n >>>= 1;                    // >>> : unsigned shift, fills with 0\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"uint32_t reverseBits(uint32_t n) {\n    uint32_t res = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {\n        res = (res << 1) | (n & 1);  // move n's lowest bit onto res\n        n >>= 1;\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-190"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-bits/"]],"ask":"Reverse the bits of a 32-bit unsigned integer."},{"i":"p-bits-268","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Missing Number","sub":"LeetCode 268 · Easy · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":268,"q":["Missing Number","how to solve Missing Number","Missing Number approach","Missing Number solution","leetcode 268","lc 268","One number from 0…n is missing.","Numbers 0..n with one missing. Which one?"],"w":"Bit Manipulation Missing from a range → XOR indexes and values. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Missing from a range → XOR indexes and values.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> XOR all indexes 0..n with all values; pairs cancel, the missing one remains (or use n(n+1)/2 − sum).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Numbers 0..n with one missing. Which one?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Sort and scan: O(n log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>res = n.<\/li><li>For i, x: res ^= i ^ x.<\/li><li>Return res.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Seed:<\/b> acc = n.<\/li><li><b>XOR:<\/b> Each index and value.<\/li><li><b>Left:<\/b> The missing one.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> n(n + 1)/2 − sum works too; XOR can’t overflow in other languages.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def missing_number(nums):\n    acc = len(nums)\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        acc ^= i ^ x             # every index meets its value, except one\n    return acc","java":"public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {\n    int acc = nums.length;\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++)\n        acc ^= i ^ nums[i];          // every index meets its value, except one\n    return acc;\n}","cpp":"int missingNumber(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int acc = nums.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); i++)\n        acc ^= i ^ nums[i];          // every index meets its value, except one\n    return acc;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-268"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/missing-number/"]],"ask":"One number from 0…n is missing."},{"i":"p-bits-371","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Sum of Two Integers","sub":"LeetCode 371 · Medium · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":371,"q":["Sum of Two Integers","how to solve Sum of Two Integers","Sum of Two Integers approach","Sum of Two Integers solution","leetcode 371","lc 371","Add without + or −.","Add two integers without + or −."],"w":"Bit Manipulation Add with bits → XOR + carry loop. Complexity O(32)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Add with bits → XOR + carry loop.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> XOR adds without carrying; AND &lt;&lt; 1 gives the carries. Repeat until no carry (mask to 32 bits in Python).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Add two integers without + or −.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>While b ≠ 0: carry = (a &amp; b) &lt;&lt; 1, a = a ^ b, b = carry (masked).<\/li><li>Convert back from 32-bit if negative.<\/li><li>Return a.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Sum:<\/b> a ^ b.<\/li><li><b>Carry:<\/b> (a &amp; b) &lt;&lt; 1.<\/li><li><b>Python:<\/b> Mask to 32 bits.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(32)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Without the mask, negatives loop forever in Python.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def get_sum(a, b):\n    MASK, MAX = 0xFFFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF\n    while b:\n        a, b = (a ^ b) & MASK, ((a & b) << 1) & MASK   # sum without carry, carry\n    return a if a <= MAX else ~(a ^ MASK)              # back to a signed int","java":"public int getSum(int a, int b) {\n    while (b != 0) {\n        int carry = (a & b) << 1;    // carry bits (Java ints wrap at 32 bits)\n        a = a ^ b;                   // sum without carry\n        b = carry;\n    }\n    return a;\n}","cpp":"int getSum(int a, int b) {\n    while (b != 0) {\n        unsigned carry = (unsigned)(a & b) << 1;   // unsigned: shifting a negative is UB\n        a = a ^ b;                                 // sum without carry\n        b = (int)carry;\n    }\n    return a;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-371"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/sum-of-two-integers/"]],"ask":"Add without + or −."},{"i":"p-bits-231","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Power of Two","sub":"LeetCode 231 · Easy · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":231,"q":["Power of Two","how to solve Power of Two","Power of Two approach","Power of Two solution","leetcode 231","lc 231","Is n a power of two?","Is n a power of two?"],"w":"Bit Manipulation Power of two → one set bit. Complexity O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Power of two → one set bit.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> A power of two has exactly one 1 bit, so n &amp; (n − 1) == 0.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Is n a power of two?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Divide by 2 repeatedly: O(log n).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>n must be &gt; 0.<\/li><li>Return n &amp; (n − 1) == 0.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Bits:<\/b> Exactly one set.<\/li><li><b>Test:<\/b> n &amp; (n − 1) == 0.<\/li><li><b>Guard:<\/b> n &gt; 0.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> 0 and negatives are not powers of two.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def is_power_of_two(n):\n    return n > 0 and n & (n - 1) == 0    # exactly one set bit","java":"public boolean isPowerOfTwo(int n) {\n    return n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0;   // exactly one set bit\n}","cpp":"bool isPowerOfTwo(int n) {\n    return n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0;   // exactly one set bit\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-231"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/power-of-two/"]],"ask":"Is n a power of two?"},{"i":"p-bits-461","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Hamming Distance","sub":"LeetCode 461 · Easy · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":461,"q":["Hamming Distance","how to solve Hamming Distance","Hamming Distance approach","Hamming Distance solution","leetcode 461","lc 461","Number of differing bit positions.","In how many bit positions do x and y differ?"],"w":"Bit Manipulation Differing bits → XOR then count. Complexity O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Differing bits → XOR then count.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> x ^ y has a 1 exactly where they differ; count its bits.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> In how many bit positions do x and y differ?<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Compare bit by bit: fine.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>z = x ^ y.<\/li><li>Count bits of z (n &amp; (n − 1) trick).<\/li><li>Return the count.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Differ:<\/b> x ^ y.<\/li><li><b>Count:<\/b> Its set bits.<\/li><li><b>Done:<\/b> One line.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Same trick for Total Hamming Distance, counted per bit.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def hamming_distance(x, y):\n    return bin(x ^ y).count(\"1\")         # 1s mark the differing bits","java":"public int hammingDistance(int x, int y) {\n    return Integer.bitCount(x ^ y);  // 1s mark the differing bits\n}","cpp":"int hammingDistance(int x, int y) {\n    return __builtin_popcount(x ^ y);   // 1s mark the differing bits\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-461"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/hamming-distance/"]],"ask":"Number of differing bit positions."},{"i":"p-bits-137","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Single Number II","sub":"LeetCode 137 · Medium · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":137,"q":["Single Number II","how to solve Single Number II","Single Number II approach","Single Number II solution","leetcode 137","lc 137","Every element three times except one.","Every number appears three times except one. Find it."],"w":"Bit Manipulation Triples → count bits mod 3. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Triples → count bits mod 3.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Count each bit position across all numbers mod 3; the leftover bits form the answer (or the ones/twos trick).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Every number appears three times except one. Find it.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Dict counts: O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For each of 32 bits, sum that bit over all numbers.<\/li><li>If sum % 3 ≠ 0, set the bit in the result.<\/li><li>Fix the sign for negatives.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Per bit:<\/b> Count mod 3.<\/li><li><b>Compact:<\/b> ones / twos masks.<\/li><li><b>Answer:<\/b> ones.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Summing each of 32 bits mod 3 is easier to explain; mention both.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def single_number_ii(nums):\n    ones = twos = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        ones = (ones ^ x) & ~twos        # bits seen once (mod 3)\n        twos = (twos ^ x) & ~ones        # bits seen twice (mod 3)\n    return ones","java":"public int singleNumber(int[] nums) {\n    int ones = 0, twos = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        ones = (ones ^ x) & ~twos;   // bits seen once (mod 3)\n        twos = (twos ^ x) & ~ones;   // bits seen twice (mod 3)\n    }\n    return ones;\n}","cpp":"int singleNumber(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int ones = 0, twos = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        ones = (ones ^ x) & ~twos;   // bits seen once (mod 3)\n        twos = (twos ^ x) & ~ones;   // bits seen twice (mod 3)\n    }\n    return ones;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-137"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/single-number-ii/"]],"ask":"Every element three times except one."},{"i":"p-bits-260","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Single Number III","sub":"LeetCode 260 · Medium · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":260,"q":["Single Number III","how to solve Single Number III","Single Number III approach","Single Number III solution","leetcode 260","lc 260","Two elements appear once, the rest twice.","Two numbers appear once, the rest twice. Find both."],"w":"Bit Manipulation Two singles → split by a differing bit. Complexity O(n), O(1)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Two singles → split by a differing bit.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> XOR all → a ^ b. Any set bit of it splits numbers into two groups, each with one of the singles.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Two numbers appear once, the rest twice. Find both.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Dict counts: O(n) space.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>x = XOR of everything.<\/li><li>bit = x &amp; −x (lowest set bit).<\/li><li>XOR numbers with that bit set → a; b = x ^ a.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>XOR:<\/b> All → a ^ b.<\/li><li><b>Split:<\/b> By its lowest set bit.<\/li><li><b>XOR:<\/b> Each group.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> In other languages, x &amp; −x can overflow for INT_MIN.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def single_number_iii(nums):\n    xy = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        xy ^= x                          # = a ^ b, the two singles\n    low = xy & -xy                       # a bit where a and b differ\n    a = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        if x & low:\n            a ^= x                       # XOR only a's group\n    return [a, xy ^ a]","java":"public int[] singleNumber(int[] nums) {\n    int xy = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) xy ^= x;      // = a ^ b, the two singles\n    int low = xy & -xy;              // a bit where a and b differ\n    int a = 0;\n    for (int x : nums)\n        if ((x & low) != 0) a ^= x;  // XOR only a's group\n    return new int[]{a, xy ^ a};\n}","cpp":"vector<int> singleNumber(vector<int>& nums) {\n    unsigned xy = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) xy ^= x;      // = a ^ b, the two singles\n    unsigned low = xy & -xy;         // a bit where a and b differ\n    int a = 0;\n    for (int x : nums)\n        if (x & low) a ^= x;         // XOR only a's group\n    return {a, (int)(xy ^ a)};\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-260"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/single-number-iii/"]],"ask":"Two elements appear once, the rest twice."},{"i":"p-bits-78","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Subsets (bitmask)","sub":"LeetCode 78 · Medium · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":78,"q":["Subsets (bitmask)","how to solve Subsets (bitmask)","Subsets (bitmask) approach","Subsets (bitmask) solution","leetcode 78","lc 78","All subsets, without recursion.","All subsets of a list, without recursion."],"w":"Bit Manipulation All subsets → masks. Complexity O(n · 2ⁿ)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> All subsets → masks.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Each number 0..2ⁿ − 1 is a bitmask: bit i set means include nums[i].<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> All subsets of a list, without recursion.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For mask in 0..2ⁿ − 1:<\/li><li>Collect nums[i] where mask &gt;&gt; i &amp; 1.<\/li><li>Append the subset.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Masks:<\/b> 0 … 2ⁿ − 1.<\/li><li><b>Bit i:<\/b> Item i chosen.<\/li><li><b>Build:<\/b> One list per mask.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(n · 2ⁿ)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Fine up to n ≈ 20.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def subsets(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    res = []\n    for mask in range(1 << n):           # 0 .. 2^n - 1\n        res.append([nums[i] for i in range(n) if mask >> i & 1])\n    return res","java":"public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {\n    int n = nums.length;\n    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {    // 0 .. 2^n - 1\n        List<Integer> subset = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n            if ((mask >> i & 1) == 1) subset.add(nums[i]);\n        res.add(subset);\n    }\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> subsets(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {    // 0 .. 2^n - 1\n        vector<int> subset;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n            if (mask >> i & 1) subset.push_back(nums[i]);\n        res.push_back(subset);\n    }\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-78"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/subsets/"]],"ask":"All subsets, without recursion."},{"i":"p-bits-201","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Bitwise AND of Numbers Range","sub":"LeetCode 201 · Medium · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":201,"q":["Bitwise AND of Numbers Range","how to solve Bitwise AND of Numbers Range","Bitwise AND of Numbers Range approach","Bitwise AND of Numbers Range solution","leetcode 201","lc 201","AND of every integer in [left, right].","Bitwise AND of every integer from left to right."],"w":"Bit Manipulation AND of a range → common prefix. Complexity O(32)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> AND of a range → common prefix.<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> The answer is the common binary prefix of left and right; any differing lower bits get a 0 somewhere in the range.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Bitwise AND of every integer from left to right.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> AND them all: O(range).<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>shift = 0.<\/li><li>While left ≠ right: shift both right by 1, shift += 1.<\/li><li>Return left &lt;&lt; shift.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Notice:<\/b> Varying bits become 0.<\/li><li><b>Shift:<\/b> Until left == right.<\/li><li><b>Restore:<\/b> Shift back.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(32)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> Looping through the range is far too slow.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def range_bitwise_and(left, right):\n    shift = 0\n    while left != right:                 # strip bits until only the common prefix is left\n        left >>= 1\n        right >>= 1\n        shift += 1\n    return left << shift","java":"public int rangeBitwiseAnd(int left, int right) {\n    int shift = 0;\n    while (left != right) {          // strip bits until only the common prefix is left\n        left >>= 1;\n        right >>= 1;\n        shift++;\n    }\n    return left << shift;\n}","cpp":"int rangeBitwiseAnd(int left, int right) {\n    int shift = 0;\n    while (left != right) {          // strip bits until only the common prefix is left\n        left >>= 1;\n        right >>= 1;\n        shift++;\n    }\n    return left << shift;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-201"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/bitwise-and-of-numbers-range/"]],"ask":"AND of every integer in [left, right]."},{"i":"p-bits-89","k":"prob","s":"bits","t":"Gray Code","sub":"LeetCode 89 · Medium · Bit Manipulation","cat":"DSA problems","n":89,"q":["Gray Code","how to solve Gray Code","Gray Code approach","Gray Code solution","leetcode 89","lc 89","Sequence of 2ⁿ numbers where neighbours differ in one bit.","List 2ⁿ numbers where neighbours differ in exactly one bit."],"w":"Bit Manipulation One-bit neighbours → i ^ (i >> 1). Complexity O(2ⁿ)","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> One-bit neighbours → i ^ (i &gt;&gt; 1).<\/p><p><b>Key idea:<\/b> Gray code formula: g(i) = i ^ (i &gt;&gt; 1).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>In plain words:<\/b> List 2ⁿ numbers where neighbours differ in exactly one bit.<\/p><p><b>Brute force:<\/b> Search for a valid order: exponential.<\/p><p><b>Steps<\/b><\/p><ol><li>For i in 0..2ⁿ − 1:<\/li><li>Append i ^ (i &gt;&gt; 1).<\/li><li>Return the list.<\/li><\/ol><p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Formula:<\/b> i ^ (i &gt;&gt; 1).<\/li><li><b>Range:<\/b> 0 … 2ⁿ − 1.<\/li><li><b>Why:<\/b> Consecutive i differ in a suffix.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Complexity:<\/b> O(2ⁿ)<\/p><p><b>⚠ Gotcha:<\/b> The reflect-and-prefix construction also works.<\/p>","c":{"py":"def gray_code(n):\n    return [i ^ (i >> 1) for i in range(1 << n)]   # neighbours differ by one bit","java":"public List<Integer> grayCode(int n) {\n    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < (1 << n); i++)\n        res.add(i ^ (i >> 1));       // neighbours differ by one bit\n    return res;\n}","cpp":"vector<int> grayCode(int n) {\n    vector<int> res;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (1 << n); i++)\n        res.push_back(i ^ (i >> 1)); // neighbours differ by one bit\n    return res;\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","bits.html#p-89"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/gray-code/"]],"ask":"Sequence of 2ⁿ numbers where neighbours differ in one bit."},{"i":"p-sd-java-pillars","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"The Four Pillars","sub":"Foundation 01 · Easy · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["The Four Pillars","how to solve The Four Pillars","The Four Pillars approach","The Four Pillars solution","Encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism."],"w":"Java & OOP Give an example for each pillar. Use it when Opening question in almost every OOP round.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Give an example for each pillar.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Private state, guarded by methods.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Encapsulation:<\/b> Private state, guarded by methods.<\/li><li><b>Abstraction:<\/b> Expose what, hide how.<\/li><li><b>Polymorphism:<\/b> One call, many behaviours.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Opening question in almost every OOP round.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Reciting definitions without an example sounds memorised.<\/p>","c":{"java":"public class BankAccount {                      // encapsulation: state is private\n    private long balancePaise;\n \n    public void withdraw(long amount) {         // the object guards its own rule\n        if (amount <= 0 || amount > balancePaise)\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"invalid amount\");\n        balancePaise -= amount;\n    }\n    public long balance() { return balancePaise; }\n}\n \ninterface Shape { double area(); }              // abstraction: what, not how\nclass Circle implements Shape {                 // inheritance of a contract\n    private final double r;\n    Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }\n    public double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }\n}\n// polymorphism: shapes.forEach(s -> total += s.area());"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-pillars"]],"ask":"Encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism."},{"i":"p-sd-java-refs","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"Objects vs References","sub":"Foundation 02 · Easy · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Objects vs References","how to solve Objects vs References","Objects vs References approach","Objects vs References solution","What a variable actually holds, and what pass-by-value means for objects."],"w":"Java & OOP Two variables, one object → changes are shared. Use it when Reasoning about shared state, defensive copies, aliasing bugs.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Two variables, one object → changes are shared.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Holds an arrow to a heap object.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Variable:<\/b> Holds an arrow to a heap object.<\/li><li><b>Assignment:<\/b> Copies the arrow.<\/li><li><b>Method call:<\/b> Copies the arrow into the parameter.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Reasoning about shared state, defensive copies, aliasing bugs.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Saying “Java is pass-by-reference”.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class Dog { String name; Dog(String n) { name = n; } }\n \nstatic void rename(Dog d) {\n    d.name = \"Rio\";        // changes the shared object\n    d = new Dog(\"Zed\");    // only moves d's own arrow\n}\n \npublic static void main(String[] args) {\n    Dog a = new Dog(\"Rex\");\n    Dog b = a;             // copies the arrow, not the dog\n    b.name = \"Max\";\n    System.out.println(a.name);   // Max\n    b = new Dog(\"Bo\");     // b points somewhere else now\n    rename(a);\n    System.out.println(a.name);   // Rio\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-refs"]],"ask":"What a variable actually holds, and what pass-by-value means for objects."},{"i":"p-sd-java-equals","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"== vs equals & hashCode","sub":"Foundation · Medium · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["== vs equals & hashCode","how to solve == vs equals & hashCode","== vs equals & hashCode approach","== vs equals & hashCode solution","Comparing objects and using them as map keys."],"w":"Java & OOP Override equals → override hashCode. Use it when Value objects, HashMap/HashSet keys.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Override equals → override hashCode.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Same object?<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>==:<\/b> Same object?<\/li><li><b>equals:<\/b> Same value?<\/li><li><b>hashCode:<\/b> Must agree with equals.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Value objects, HashMap/HashSet keys.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Mutable fields in hashCode: change them after inserting and the key is lost.<\/p>","c":{"java":"public final class Point {\n    private final int x, y;\n    public Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }\n \n    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (this == o) return true;                    // same object\n        if (!(o instanceof Point p)) return false;\n        return x == p.x && y == p.y;                   // same value\n    }\n    @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(x, y); }   // must match equals\n}\n// new Point(1,2) == new Point(1,2)       -> false (two objects)\n// new Point(1,2).equals(new Point(1,2))  -> true"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-equals"]],"ask":"Comparing objects and using them as map keys."},{"i":"p-sd-java-access","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"Access Modifiers","sub":"Foundation 03 · Easy · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Access Modifiers","how to solve Access Modifiers","Access Modifiers approach","Access Modifiers solution","private, package-private, protected, public."],"w":"Java & OOP Start private, widen on demand. Use it when Designing a class’s public surface.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Start private, widen on demand.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> This class only.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>private:<\/b> This class only.<\/li><li><b>default:<\/b> Same package.<\/li><li><b>protected:<\/b> Package + subclasses.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Designing a class’s public surface.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Public fields: you can never add validation later without breaking callers.<\/p>","c":{"java":"public class Order {\n    private final String id;          // only this class\n    String note;                      // package-private (no keyword): same package\n    protected double discount;        // same package + subclasses\n    public Order(String id) { this.id = id; }   // everyone\n \n    public String id() { return id; }  // expose behaviour, not fields\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-access"]],"ask":"private, package-private, protected, public."},{"i":"p-sd-java-staticfinal","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"static · final · this","sub":"Foundation 06 · Easy · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["static · final · this","how to solve static · final · this","static · final · this approach","static · final · this solution","Three keywords in every pattern."],"w":"Java & OOP Singleton → static; immutable → final; builder → return this. Use it when Singletons, constants, immutable value objects, fluent builders.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Singleton → static; immutable → final; builder → return this.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> One per class.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>static:<\/b> One per class.<\/li><li><b>final:<\/b> Assign once / no override.<\/li><li><b>this:<\/b> Current object.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Singletons, constants, immutable value objects, fluent builders.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Static mutable state is global state: hard to test and not thread-safe.<\/p>","c":{"java":"public class Counter {\n    private static int created = 0;   // static: one copy shared by the class\n    private final int id;             // final: assigned once\n    private int hits;\n \n    public Counter() {\n        created++;\n        this.id = created;            // this: the current object\n    }\n    public static int created() { return created; }   // no 'this' inside static\n    public void hit() { this.hits++; }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-staticfinal"]],"ask":"Three keywords in every pattern."},{"i":"p-sd-java-ifaceabs","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"Interface vs Abstract Class","sub":"Foundation 04 · Medium · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Interface vs Abstract Class","how to solve Interface vs Abstract Class","Interface vs Abstract Class approach","Interface vs Abstract Class solution","A contract versus a half-built machine."],"w":"Java & OOP Capability → interface; family with shared steps → abstract. Use it when Strategy/State use interfaces; Template Method uses an abstract class.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Capability → interface; family with shared steps → abstract.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Many per class, no state.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Interface:<\/b> Many per class, no state.<\/li><li><b>Abstract:<\/b> One per class, shared state.<\/li><li><b>Default:<\/b> Interface unless sharing state.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Strategy/State use interfaces; Template Method uses an abstract class.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Default methods don’t make interfaces stateful.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface Notifier {                         // contract: many unrelated classes\n    void send(String to, String msg);\n    default void sendAll(List<String> to, String msg) {   // optional shared default\n        to.forEach(t -> send(t, msg));\n    }\n}\n \nabstract class Report {                      // half-built machine with shared state\n    protected final String title;\n    Report(String title) { this.title = title; }\n    public final String render() {           // fixed algorithm\n        return header() + body() + \"\\n-- end --\";\n    }\n    protected String header() { return \"# \" + title + \"\\n\"; }\n    protected abstract String body();        // subclasses fill this in\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-ifaceabs"]],"ask":"A contract versus a half-built machine."},{"i":"p-sd-java-poly","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"Polymorphism & Dispatch","sub":"Foundation 05 · Medium · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Polymorphism & Dispatch","how to solve Polymorphism & Dispatch","Polymorphism & Dispatch approach","Polymorphism & Dispatch solution","Which method actually runs?"],"w":"Java & OOP Replace type switches with overridden methods. Use it when Every behavioural pattern.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Replace type switches with overridden methods.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Runtime, by object.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Overriding:<\/b> Runtime, by object.<\/li><li><b>Overloading:<\/b> Compile time, by declared type.<\/li><li><b>Upcast:<\/b> Animal a = new Dog().<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Every behavioural pattern.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Static and private methods are not overridden, only hidden.<\/p>","c":{"java":"abstract class Animal { abstract String speak(); }\nclass Dog extends Animal { String speak() { return \"Woof\"; } }\nclass Cat extends Animal { String speak() { return \"Meow\"; } }\n \nstatic String greet(Animal x) { return \"animal\"; }\nstatic String greet(Dog x)    { return \"dog\"; }\n \nAnimal a = new Dog();\na.speak();      // \"Woof\"   overriding: decided at runtime by the object\ngreet(a);       // \"animal\" overloading: decided at compile time by the declared type"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-poly"]],"ask":"Which method actually runs?"},{"i":"p-sd-java-generics","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"Generics","sub":"Foundation 07 · Medium · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Generics","how to solve Generics","Generics approach","Generics solution","Type safety at compile time, and the erasure trap."],"w":"Java & OOP Generic container or algorithm → <T>. Use it when Reusable collections, repositories, typed factories.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Generic container or algorithm → &lt;T&gt;.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Caller picks the type.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>&lt;T&gt;:<\/b> Caller picks the type.<\/li><li><b>Bounds:<\/b> T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;.<\/li><li><b>PECS:<\/b> extends to read, super to write.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Reusable collections, repositories, typed factories.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> List&lt;Dog&gt; is not a List&lt;Animal&gt;; use List&lt;? extends Animal&gt;.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class Box<T> {                                  // T chosen by the caller\n    private T value;\n    void put(T v) { value = v; }\n    T get() { return value; }                    // no cast needed\n}\n \n// bounded type: only Comparable things\nstatic <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(List<T> xs) {\n    T best = xs.get(0);\n    for (T x : xs) if (x.compareTo(best) > 0) best = x;\n    return best;\n}\n \n// PECS: Producer Extends, Consumer Super\nstatic double sum(List<? extends Number> nums) { ... }   // read Numbers out\nstatic void fill(List<? super Integer> out) { out.add(1); }  // put Integers in"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-generics"]],"ask":"Type safety at compile time, and the erasure trap."},{"i":"p-sd-java-enums","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"Enums","sub":"Foundation 08 · Easy · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Enums","how to solve Enums","Enums approach","Enums solution","Constants with state and behaviour."],"w":"Java & OOP Fixed set of types or states → enum. Use it when Vehicle types, order status, simple state machines, singletons.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Fixed set of types or states → enum.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Each constant can carry data.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Fields:<\/b> Each constant can carry data.<\/li><li><b>Methods:<\/b> Constant-specific bodies.<\/li><li><b>EnumMap:<\/b> Fast, ordered lookups.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Vehicle types, order status, simple state machines, singletons.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Persisting ordinal(): reordering constants corrupts data. Store name().<\/p>","c":{"java":"enum VehicleType {\n    BIKE(1), CAR(2), TRUCK(4);                 // each constant is an object\n \n    private final int slotsNeeded;             // enums can hold state...\n    VehicleType(int slots) { this.slotsNeeded = slots; }\n    int slotsNeeded() { return slotsNeeded; }\n}\n \nenum Op {\n    ADD { int apply(int a, int b) { return a + b; } },   // ...and behaviour\n    MUL { int apply(int a, int b) { return a * b; } };\n    abstract int apply(int a, int b);\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-enums"]],"ask":"Constants with state and behaviour."},{"i":"p-sd-java-hasa","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"IS-A vs HAS-A","sub":"Foundation 09 · Medium · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["IS-A vs HAS-A","how to solve IS-A vs HAS-A","IS-A vs HAS-A approach","IS-A vs HAS-A solution","Inheritance or composition?"],"w":"Java & OOP Reuse → composition + delegation. Use it when Decorator, Strategy, State, Bridge all choose HAS-A.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Reuse → composition + delegation.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Fixed at compile time.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>IS-A:<\/b> Fixed at compile time.<\/li><li><b>HAS-A:<\/b> Swappable at runtime.<\/li><li><b>Test:<\/b> Would every parent use still make sense?<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Decorator, Strategy, State, Bridge all choose HAS-A.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Stack extends Vector in the JDK is the famous mistake.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// IS-A: rigid, decided at compile time\nclass LoggingList extends ArrayList<String> { /* inherits 40+ methods */ }\n \n// HAS-A: flexible, swappable at runtime\nclass Car {\n    private Engine engine;                 // composition\n    Car(Engine engine) { this.engine = engine; }\n    void upgrade(Engine better) { this.engine = better; }\n    void start() { engine.ignite(); }      // delegation\n}\ninterface Engine { void ignite(); }\nclass PetrolEngine implements Engine { public void ignite() { /* ... */ } }\nclass ElectricEngine implements Engine { public void ignite() { /* ... */ } }"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-hasa"]],"ask":"Inheritance or composition?"},{"i":"p-sd-java-coupling","k":"prob","s":"sd-java","t":"Coupling & Cohesion","sub":"Foundation 10 · Medium · Java & OOP","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Coupling & Cohesion","how to solve Coupling & Cohesion","Coupling & Cohesion approach","Coupling & Cohesion solution","The vocabulary for judging any design."],"w":"Java & OOP new ConcreteThing() in business logic → inject an interface. Use it when Justifying any design decision.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> new ConcreteThing() in business logic → inject an interface.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Knowledge between classes: keep low.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Coupling:<\/b> Knowledge between classes: keep low.<\/li><li><b>Cohesion:<\/b> Focus within a class: keep high.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> Interfaces + injection; split classes.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Justifying any design decision.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> “Loose coupling” doesn’t mean zero coupling; depend on stable abstractions.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Tight coupling: OrderService knows the concrete class and builds it\nclass OrderService {\n    private final MySqlOrderRepo repo = new MySqlOrderRepo();\n}\n \n// Loose coupling: depends on an interface, gets it from outside\ninterface OrderRepo { void save(Order o); }\nclass OrderService2 {\n    private final OrderRepo repo;\n    OrderService2(OrderRepo repo) { this.repo = repo; }   // injected\n}\n// High cohesion: OrderService2 only does order logic, nothing about emails or PDFs"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-java.html#p-coupling"]],"ask":"The vocabulary for judging any design."},{"i":"p-sd-solid-srp","k":"prob","s":"sd-solid","t":"Single Responsibility","sub":"SOLID · S · Easy · SOLID","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Single Responsibility","how to solve Single Responsibility","Single Responsibility approach","Single Responsibility solution","A class should have one reason to change."],"w":"SOLID Several actors → several classes. Smell it cures God classes, “Manager” classes, controllers doing business logic.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Several actors → several classes.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Unrelated edits to one file.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Smell:<\/b> Unrelated edits to one file.<\/li><li><b>Test:<\/b> Who could ask for a change?<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> Split; original delegates.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Smell it:<\/b> cures God classes, “Manager” classes, controllers doing business logic.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> “One responsibility” is not “one method”.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// BEFORE: three reasons to change in one class\nclass Invoice {\n    double total() { /* tax rules */ }\n    byte[] toPdf() { /* layout */ }\n    void save()    { /* SQL */ }\n}\n \n// AFTER: one reason each\nclass Invoice           { double total() { /* tax rules */ } }\nclass InvoicePrinter    { byte[] toPdf(Invoice i) { /* layout */ } }\nclass InvoiceRepository { void save(Invoice i) { /* SQL */ } }"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-solid.html#p-srp"]],"ask":"A class should have one reason to change."},{"i":"p-sd-solid-ocp","k":"prob","s":"sd-solid","t":"Open / Closed","sub":"SOLID · O · Easy · SOLID","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Open / Closed","how to solve Open / Closed","Open / Closed approach","Open / Closed solution","Open for extension, closed for modification."],"w":"SOLID Switch on type → polymorphism. Smell it cures Payments, notification channels, file formats, pricing rules.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Switch on type → polymorphism.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Growing switch on type.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Smell:<\/b> Growing switch on type.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> Interface + class per case.<\/li><li><b>Result:<\/b> New feature = new file.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Smell it:<\/b> cures Payments, notification channels, file formats, pricing rules.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> You can’t be closed against every change; pick the axis that actually varies.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// BEFORE: every new method edits this class\ndouble fee(String type, double amt) {\n    if (type.equals(\"CARD\")) return amt * 0.02;\n    else if (type.equals(\"UPI\")) return 0;\n    else if (type.equals(\"WALLET\")) return amt * 0.01;\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException(type);\n}\n \n// AFTER: closed for modification, open for extension\ninterface PaymentMethod { double fee(double amt); }\nclass Card   implements PaymentMethod { public double fee(double a) { return a * 0.02; } }\nclass Upi    implements PaymentMethod { public double fee(double a) { return 0; } }\nclass Wallet implements PaymentMethod { public double fee(double a) { return a * 0.01; } }\nclass PayLater implements PaymentMethod { public double fee(double a) { return a * 0.03; } } // new: no edits"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-solid.html#p-ocp"]],"ask":"Open for extension, closed for modification."},{"i":"p-sd-solid-lsp","k":"prob","s":"sd-solid","t":"Liskov Substitution","sub":"SOLID · L · Medium · SOLID","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Liskov Substitution","how to solve Liskov Substitution","Liskov Substitution approach","Liskov Substitution solution","Subtypes must be usable anywhere the parent is."],"w":"SOLID Can’t keep the promise → not a subtype. Smell it cures Designing class hierarchies.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Can’t keep the promise → not a subtype.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Override throws or changes meaning.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Smell:<\/b> Override throws or changes meaning.<\/li><li><b>Example:<\/b> Square/Rectangle, Penguin/Bird.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> Siblings under a smaller interface.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Smell it:<\/b> cures Designing class hierarchies.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> “IS-A in real life” doesn’t mean IS-A in code; behaviour decides.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class Rectangle {\n    protected int w, h;\n    void setWidth(int w)  { this.w = w; }\n    void setHeight(int h) { this.h = h; }\n    int area() { return w * h; }\n}\nclass Square extends Rectangle {               // looks right, IS-A in maths...\n    @Override void setWidth(int w)  { this.w = w; this.h = w; }\n    @Override void setHeight(int h) { this.w = h; this.h = h; }\n}\n \nvoid resize(Rectangle r) {\n    r.setWidth(5); r.setHeight(4);\n    assert r.area() == 20;                     // fails for Square: 16\n}\n// Fix: no inheritance. interface Shape { int area(); } with Rectangle and Square as siblings."},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-solid.html#p-lsp"]],"ask":"Subtypes must be usable anywhere the parent is."},{"i":"p-sd-solid-isp","k":"prob","s":"sd-solid","t":"Interface Segregation","sub":"SOLID · I · Easy · SOLID","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Interface Segregation","how to solve Interface Segregation","Interface Segregation approach","Interface Segregation solution","Clients shouldn’t depend on methods they don’t use."],"w":"SOLID Fat interface → split by client need. Smell it cures Device capabilities, repository interfaces, plugin APIs.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Fat interface → split by client need.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Empty or throwing implementations.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Smell:<\/b> Empty or throwing implementations.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> Role interfaces.<\/li><li><b>Bonus:<\/b> Smaller mocks in tests.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Smell it:<\/b> cures Device capabilities, repository interfaces, plugin APIs.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Splitting into one-method interfaces everywhere is overkill; group by client.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// BEFORE: one fat interface\ninterface Worker { void work(); void eat(); void sleep(); }\nclass Robot implements Worker {\n    public void work() { /* ... */ }\n    public void eat()   { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }  // smell\n    public void sleep() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }\n}\n \n// AFTER: role interfaces\ninterface Workable  { void work(); }\ninterface Eatable   { void eat(); }\ninterface Sleepable { void sleep(); }\nclass Human implements Workable, Eatable, Sleepable { /* ... */ }\nclass Robot implements Workable { public void work() { /* ... */ } }"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-solid.html#p-isp"]],"ask":"Clients shouldn’t depend on methods they don’t use."},{"i":"p-sd-solid-dip","k":"prob","s":"sd-solid","t":"Dependency Inversion","sub":"SOLID · D · Medium · SOLID","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Dependency Inversion","how to solve Dependency Inversion","Dependency Inversion approach","Dependency Inversion solution","Depend on abstractions, not concretions."],"w":"SOLID Business logic never news its infrastructure. Smell it cures Repositories, gateways, clients; makes unit tests trivial.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Business logic never news its infrastructure.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> new Concrete() in policy code.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Smell:<\/b> new Concrete() in policy code.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> Interface owned by the caller.<\/li><li><b>Wire:<\/b> Constructor injection.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Smell it:<\/b> cures Repositories, gateways, clients; makes unit tests trivial.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> DIP (a principle) ≠ DI (a technique) ≠ IoC container (a tool).<\/p>","c":{"java":"// BEFORE: high-level policy welded to a low-level detail\nclass OrderService {\n    private final MySqlOrderRepo repo = new MySqlOrderRepo();\n}\n \n// AFTER: both depend on the abstraction\ninterface OrderRepo { void save(Order o); Optional<Order> find(long id); }\n \nclass MySqlOrderRepo implements OrderRepo { /* JDBC */ }\nclass InMemoryOrderRepo implements OrderRepo { /* tests */ }\n \nclass OrderService {\n    private final OrderRepo repo;\n    OrderService(OrderRepo repo) { this.repo = repo; }   // constructor injection\n}\n \n// Composition root (main or Spring) decides\nOrderService svc = new OrderService(new MySqlOrderRepo());"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-solid.html#p-dip"]],"ask":"Depend on abstractions, not concretions."},{"i":"p-sd-solid-solidreview","k":"prob","s":"sd-solid","t":"Refactor a Notification Service","sub":"Applied · Medium · SOLID","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Refactor a Notification Service","how to solve Refactor a Notification Service","Refactor a Notification Service approach","Refactor a Notification Service solution","Apply S, O and D to a messy service."],"w":"SOLID Name the smell before you name the principle. Smell it cures The most common “review this code” prompt.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Name the smell before you name the principle.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Renderer separate from sending.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>S:<\/b> Renderer separate from sending.<\/li><li><b>O:<\/b> Channel interface.<\/li><li><b>D:<\/b> Inject channels.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Smell it:<\/b> cures The most common “review this code” prompt.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Don’t over-engineer: no interface until a second implementation is real.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface Channel { void send(User u, String msg); }          // O, I\nclass EmailChannel implements Channel { ... }\nclass SmsChannel   implements Channel { ... }\nclass PushChannel  implements Channel { ... }                // new channel = new class\n \nclass TemplateRenderer { String render(String key, Map<String,Object> data) { ... } }  // S\n \nclass NotificationService {\n    private final List<Channel> channels;                     // D: injected\n    private final TemplateRenderer renderer;\n    NotificationService(List<Channel> channels, TemplateRenderer r) { ... }\n \n    void notify(User u, String key, Map<String,Object> data) {\n        String msg = renderer.render(key, data);\n        for (Channel c : channels) if (u.wants(c)) c.send(u, msg);\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-solid.html#p-solidreview"]],"ask":"Apply S, O and D to a messy service."},{"i":"p-sd-creational-singleton","k":"prob","s":"sd-creational","t":"Singleton","sub":"Creational · Easy · Creational Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Singleton","how to solve Singleton","Singleton approach","Singleton solution","One instance, globally reachable, thread-safe."],"w":"Creational Patterns Enum singleton unless you need lazy init with arguments. Use it when Config, registries, pools, caches.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Enum singleton unless you need lazy init with arguments.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Default choice.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Enum:<\/b> Default choice.<\/li><li><b>Holder:<\/b> Lazy, lock-free.<\/li><li><b>DCL:<\/b> Needs volatile.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Config, registries, pools, caches.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Breakable via reflection and serialization unless it’s an enum.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Best default: enum singleton (thread-safe, serialization-safe, reflection-safe)\npublic enum Registry {\n    INSTANCE;\n    private final Map<String, Integer> counts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    public void hit(String k) { counts.merge(k, 1, Integer::sum); }\n}\n \n// Lazy alternative: holder idiom (class loading is thread-safe)\npublic class Config {\n    private Config() {}\n    private static class Holder { static final Config INSTANCE = new Config(); }\n    public static Config get() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-creational.html#p-singleton"]],"ask":"One instance, globally reachable, thread-safe."},{"i":"p-sd-creational-dcl","k":"prob","s":"sd-creational","t":"Double-Checked Locking","sub":"Creational · concurrency · Hard · Creational Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Double-Checked Locking","how to solve Double-Checked Locking","Double-Checked Locking approach","Double-Checked Locking solution","Lazy singleton without locking on every call."],"w":"Creational Patterns Two checks + synchronized + volatile. Use it when Favourite follow-up after “write a singleton”.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Two checks + synchronized + volatile.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Fast path, no lock.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>1st check:<\/b> Fast path, no lock.<\/li><li><b>Lock:<\/b> Only on first creation.<\/li><li><b>2nd check:<\/b> Someone may have won the race.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Favourite follow-up after “write a singleton”.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Without volatile another thread can see a half-constructed object.<\/p>","c":{"java":"public class Config {\n    private static volatile Config instance;   // volatile: no half-built object\n    private Config() { load(); }\n \n    public static Config get() {\n        Config c = instance;\n        if (c == null) {                       // 1st check, no lock (fast path)\n            synchronized (Config.class) {\n                c = instance;\n                if (c == null) {               // 2nd check, under the lock\n                    instance = c = new Config();\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return c;\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-creational.html#p-dcl"]],"ask":"Lazy singleton without locking on every call."},{"i":"p-sd-creational-factory","k":"prob","s":"sd-creational","t":"Simple Factory","sub":"Creational · Easy · Creational Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Simple Factory","how to solve Simple Factory","Simple Factory approach","Simple Factory solution","Create one of several classes based on input."],"w":"Creational Patterns All the news in one place. Use it when Payment methods, notification channels, vehicle types.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> All the news in one place.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Type string or enum.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Input:<\/b> Type string or enum.<\/li><li><b>Return:<\/b> The interface.<\/li><li><b>Extend:<\/b> Map of suppliers.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Payment methods, notification channels, vehicle types.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> A huge switch in the factory is fine; a switch in every caller is not.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface Payment { void pay(long paise); }\nclass CardPayment   implements Payment { public void pay(long p) { /* ... */ } }\nclass UpiPayment    implements Payment { public void pay(long p) { /* ... */ } }\nclass WalletPayment implements Payment { public void pay(long p) { /* ... */ } }\n \nclass PaymentFactory {\n    static Payment of(String method) {\n        return switch (method) {\n            case \"CARD\"   -> new CardPayment();\n            case \"UPI\"    -> new UpiPayment();\n            case \"WALLET\" -> new WalletPayment();\n            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(method);\n        };\n    }\n}\n \n// caller only knows the interface\nPayment p = PaymentFactory.of(request.method());\np.pay(amount);"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-creational.html#p-factory"]],"ask":"Create one of several classes based on input."},{"i":"p-sd-creational-factorymethod","k":"prob","s":"sd-creational","t":"Factory Method","sub":"Creational · GoF · Medium · Creational Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Factory Method","how to solve Factory Method","Factory Method approach","Factory Method solution","Let subclasses choose the product."],"w":"Creational Patterns Template Method + a creation hook. Use it when Frameworks letting users plug in their own classes.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Template Method + a creation hook.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Fixed algorithm.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Base:<\/b> Fixed algorithm.<\/li><li><b>Hook:<\/b> abstract create().<\/li><li><b>Sub:<\/b> Returns its product.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Frameworks letting users plug in their own classes.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Don’t confuse with a static factory method like List.of().<\/p>","c":{"java":"abstract class Logistics {\n    abstract Transport createTransport();          // the factory method\n    void deliver(Parcel p) {                       // shared algorithm\n        Transport t = createTransport();\n        t.load(p); t.go();\n    }\n}\nclass RoadLogistics extends Logistics { Transport createTransport() { return new Truck(); } }\nclass SeaLogistics  extends Logistics { Transport createTransport() { return new Ship(); } }"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-creational.html#p-factorymethod"]],"ask":"Let subclasses choose the product."},{"i":"p-sd-creational-abstractfactory","k":"prob","s":"sd-creational","t":"Abstract Factory","sub":"Creational · GoF · Medium · Creational Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Abstract Factory","how to solve Abstract Factory","Abstract Factory approach","Abstract Factory solution","Create families of related objects."],"w":"Creational Patterns Must match → one factory per family. Use it when UI themes, cloud providers (AWS vs GCP clients), DB dialects.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Must match → one factory per family.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> One create per product.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Interface:<\/b> One create per product.<\/li><li><b>Impl:<\/b> One per family.<\/li><li><b>Swap:<\/b> Whole family at once.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when UI themes, cloud providers (AWS vs GCP clients), DB dialects.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Adding a new product type changes every factory.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface Button { void render(); }\ninterface Checkbox { void render(); }\n \ninterface UIFactory {                // one factory = one consistent family\n    Button button();\n    Checkbox checkbox();\n}\nclass MacFactory implements UIFactory {\n    public Button button()     { return new MacButton(); }\n    public Checkbox checkbox() { return new MacCheckbox(); }\n}\nclass WinFactory implements UIFactory {\n    public Button button()     { return new WinButton(); }\n    public Checkbox checkbox() { return new WinCheckbox(); }\n}\nUIFactory ui = os.isMac() ? new MacFactory() : new WinFactory();"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-creational.html#p-abstractfactory"]],"ask":"Create families of related objects."},{"i":"p-sd-creational-builder","k":"prob","s":"sd-creational","t":"Builder","sub":"Creational · GoF · Easy · Creational Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Builder","how to solve Builder","Builder approach","Builder solution","Step-by-step construction of a complex, immutable object."],"w":"Creational Patterns More than ~4 params or several optional → Builder. Use it when Requests, configs, domain objects with optional parts.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> More than ~4 params or several optional → Builder.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> return this.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Fluent:<\/b> return this.<\/li><li><b>Defaults:<\/b> In the builder.<\/li><li><b>build():<\/b> Validate, then freeze.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Requests, configs, domain objects with optional parts.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Copy mutable collections in the constructor or the object isn’t really immutable.<\/p>","c":{"java":"public final class Pizza {\n    private final Size size; private final boolean cheese; private final List<String> toppings;\n \n    private Pizza(Builder b) { size = b.size; cheese = b.cheese; toppings = List.copyOf(b.toppings); }\n    public static Builder builder() { return new Builder(); }\n \n    public static class Builder {\n        private Size size = Size.MEDIUM;              // defaults\n        private boolean cheese = true;\n        private final List<String> toppings = new ArrayList<>();\n \n        public Builder size(Size s)       { this.size = s; return this; }   // return this -> chaining\n        public Builder cheese(boolean c)  { this.cheese = c; return this; }\n        public Builder topping(String t)  { toppings.add(t); return this; }\n \n        public Pizza build() {\n            if (toppings.size() > 5) throw new IllegalStateException(\"max 5 toppings\");\n            return new Pizza(this);                   // validate once, then immutable\n        }\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-creational.html#p-builder"]],"ask":"Step-by-step construction of a complex, immutable object."},{"i":"p-sd-creational-prototype","k":"prob","s":"sd-creational","t":"Prototype","sub":"Creational · GoF · Medium · Creational Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Prototype","how to solve Prototype","Prototype approach","Prototype solution","Create objects by copying a configured instance."],"w":"Creational Patterns Copy, don’t rebuild. Use it when Document templates, game entities, cached configs.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Copy, don’t rebuild.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Expensive setup once.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Template:<\/b> Expensive setup once.<\/li><li><b>Copy:<\/b> Copy constructor.<\/li><li><b>Deep:<\/b> Copy mutable fields.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Document templates, game entities, cached configs.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Object.clone() is shallow and awkward; prefer a copy constructor.<\/p>","c":{"java":"public class Document implements Cloneable {\n    private String title;\n    private List<String> pages = new ArrayList<>();\n \n    // copy constructor: preferred over clone()\n    public Document(Document other) {\n        this.title = other.title;\n        this.pages = new ArrayList<>(other.pages);   // DEEP copy of the list\n    }\n    public Document copy() { return new Document(this); }\n}\n \nDocument template = loadTemplate();     // expensive, done once\nDocument a = template.copy();           // cheap copies\nDocument b = template.copy();\na.addPage(\"A only\");                     // b and template unaffected"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-creational.html#p-prototype"]],"ask":"Create objects by copying a configured instance."},{"i":"p-sd-structural-decorator","k":"prob","s":"sd-structural","t":"Decorator","sub":"Structural · GoF · Medium · Structural Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Decorator","how to solve Decorator","Decorator approach","Decorator solution","Attach responsibilities to an object dynamically."],"w":"Structural Patterns Combinations of features → wrap. Use it when Java I/O streams, HTTP middleware, pricing add-ons.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Combinations of features → wrap.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> IS-A and HAS-A the same interface.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Shape:<\/b> IS-A and HAS-A the same interface.<\/li><li><b>Call:<\/b> Delegate, then add.<\/li><li><b>Stack:<\/b> Any order, any count.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Java I/O streams, HTTP middleware, pricing add-ons.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Order of wrapping can change the result.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface Beverage { int cost(); String description(); }\n \nclass Espresso implements Beverage {\n    public int cost() { return 100; }\n    public String description() { return \"Espresso\"; }\n}\n \nabstract class AddOn implements Beverage {            // IS-A Beverage...\n    protected final Beverage inner;                    // ...and HAS-A Beverage\n    AddOn(Beverage inner) { this.inner = inner; }\n}\nclass Milk extends AddOn {\n    Milk(Beverage b) { super(b); }\n    public int cost() { return inner.cost() + 10; }   // delegate, then add\n    public String description() { return inner.description() + \", milk\"; }\n}\nclass Whip extends AddOn {\n    Whip(Beverage b) { super(b); }\n    public int cost() { return inner.cost() + 20; }\n    public String description() { return inner.description() + \", whip\"; }\n}\n \nBeverage order = new Whip(new Milk(new Espresso()));\nint total = order.cost();                              // 130"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-structural.html#p-decorator"]],"ask":"Attach responsibilities to an object dynamically."},{"i":"p-sd-structural-proxy","k":"prob","s":"sd-structural","t":"Proxy","sub":"Structural · GoF · Medium · Structural Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Proxy","how to solve Proxy","Proxy approach","Proxy solution","A stand-in that controls access to the real object."],"w":"Structural Patterns Same interface + guard → Proxy. Use it when Spring AOP, Hibernate lazy loading, gRPC stubs.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Same interface + guard → Proxy.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Create on first use.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Virtual:<\/b> Create on first use.<\/li><li><b>Protection:<\/b> Check permissions.<\/li><li><b>Caching/remote:<\/b> Avoid or hide the trip.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Spring AOP, Hibernate lazy loading, gRPC stubs.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Calling a method on this inside the bean bypasses Spring’s proxy (@Transactional ignored).<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface ImageService { byte[] load(String id); }\n \nclass S3ImageService implements ImageService {          // the real, slow subject\n    public byte[] load(String id) { return s3.get(id); }  // ~80 ms\n}\n \nclass CachingImageProxy implements ImageService {       // same interface\n    private final ImageService real;\n    private final Map<String, byte[]> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    CachingImageProxy(ImageService real) { this.real = real; }\n \n    public byte[] load(String id) {\n        return cache.computeIfAbsent(id, real::load);     // guard access to the real one\n    }\n}\n \nImageService svc = new CachingImageProxy(new S3ImageService());\nsvc.load(\"cat\");   // miss -> S3\nsvc.load(\"cat\");   // hit  -> cache"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-structural.html#p-proxy"]],"ask":"A stand-in that controls access to the real object."},{"i":"p-sd-structural-adapter","k":"prob","s":"sd-structural","t":"Adapter","sub":"Structural · GoF · Easy · Structural Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Adapter","how to solve Adapter","Adapter approach","Adapter solution","Make an incompatible interface fit yours."],"w":"Structural Patterns Vendor SDK → adapter behind your interface. Use it when Payment gateways, legacy systems, Arrays.asList().","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Vendor SDK → adapter behind your interface.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Your interface.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Target:<\/b> Your interface.<\/li><li><b>Adaptee:<\/b> Their class.<\/li><li><b>Adapter:<\/b> Translates.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Payment gateways, legacy systems, Arrays.asList().<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Don’t leak vendor types (Receipt) through your interface.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Yours\ninterface PaymentGateway { boolean charge(long paise); }\n \n// Theirs (can't change it)\nclass StripeSdk { Receipt makePayment(double amount, String currency) { ... } }\n \n// The adapter speaks both\nclass StripeAdapter implements PaymentGateway {\n    private final StripeSdk sdk;\n    StripeAdapter(StripeSdk sdk) { this.sdk = sdk; }\n    public boolean charge(long paise) {\n        Receipt r = sdk.makePayment(paise / 100.0, \"INR\");   // translate\n        return r.status().equals(\"succeeded\");\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-structural.html#p-adapter"]],"ask":"Make an incompatible interface fit yours."},{"i":"p-sd-structural-facade","k":"prob","s":"sd-structural","t":"Facade","sub":"Structural · GoF · Easy · Structural Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Facade","how to solve Facade","Facade approach","Facade solution","One simple entry point to a complex subsystem."],"w":"Structural Patterns Many calls in a fixed order → Facade. Use it when Order placement, SDKs, service layers.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Many calls in a fixed order → Facade.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Ordering and wiring.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Hide:<\/b> Ordering and wiring.<\/li><li><b>Expose:<\/b> A few high-level calls.<\/li><li><b>Keep:<\/b> Subsystems still usable directly.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Order placement, SDKs, service layers.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> A facade that grows every feature becomes a god class.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class OrderFacade {\n    private final Inventory inventory; private final Payments payments;\n    private final Shipping shipping;   private final Mailer mailer;\n \n    public OrderId placeOrder(Cart cart, Card card) {\n        inventory.reserve(cart.items());\n        PaymentId pid = payments.charge(card, cart.total());\n        OrderId id = shipping.schedule(cart);\n        mailer.sendConfirmation(cart.user(), id);\n        return id;\n    }\n}\n// clients call one method instead of orchestrating four subsystems"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-structural.html#p-facade"]],"ask":"One simple entry point to a complex subsystem."},{"i":"p-sd-structural-composite","k":"prob","s":"sd-structural","t":"Composite","sub":"Structural · GoF · Medium · Structural Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Composite","how to solve Composite","Composite approach","Composite solution","Treat individual objects and groups uniformly."],"w":"Structural Patterns Part-whole tree → Composite. Use it when File systems, UI trees, org charts, expression trees.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Part-whole tree → Composite.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Base case.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Leaf:<\/b> Base case.<\/li><li><b>Group:<\/b> Loops children.<\/li><li><b>Client:<\/b> One call.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when File systems, UI trees, org charts, expression trees.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Leaf-only methods (add child) on the shared interface hurt type safety; decide deliberately.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface Node { long size(); }\n \nclass File implements Node {\n    private final long bytes;\n    File(long bytes) { this.bytes = bytes; }\n    public long size() { return bytes; }                 // leaf: base case\n}\n \nclass Folder implements Node {\n    private final List<Node> children = new ArrayList<>();\n    Folder add(Node n) { children.add(n); return this; }\n    public long size() {\n        long total = 0;\n        for (Node c : children) total += c.size();       // same call for file or folder\n        return total;\n    }\n}\n \nNode root = new Folder()\n    .add(new File(4))\n    .add(new Folder().add(new File(10)).add(new File(6)))\n    .add(new File(2));\nroot.size();   // 22"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-structural.html#p-composite"]],"ask":"Treat individual objects and groups uniformly."},{"i":"p-sd-structural-bridge","k":"prob","s":"sd-structural","t":"Bridge","sub":"Structural · GoF · Hard · Structural Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Bridge","how to solve Bridge","Bridge approach","Bridge solution","Separate an abstraction from its implementation so both vary."],"w":"Structural Patterns M × N classes → M + N. Use it when Shapes × renderers, notifications × channels, JDBC drivers.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> M × N classes → M + N.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Abstraction hierarchy.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Axis 1:<\/b> Abstraction hierarchy.<\/li><li><b>Axis 2:<\/b> Implementation hierarchy.<\/li><li><b>Link:<\/b> HAS-A reference.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Shapes × renderers, notifications × channels, JDBC drivers.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Often confused with Adapter: Bridge is designed up front; Adapter is a retrofit.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface Renderer { void circle(float r); void square(float s); }   // implementation axis\nclass VectorRenderer implements Renderer { ... }\nclass RasterRenderer implements Renderer { ... }\n \nabstract class Shape {                                                 // abstraction axis\n    protected final Renderer renderer;                                 // the bridge\n    Shape(Renderer r) { this.renderer = r; }\n    abstract void draw();\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    private final float r;\n    Circle(Renderer rd, float r) { super(rd); this.r = r; }\n    void draw() { renderer.circle(r); }\n}\n// 2 shapes + 2 renderers = 4 classes, not 2 x 2 subclasses; both sides grow independently"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-structural.html#p-bridge"]],"ask":"Separate an abstraction from its implementation so both vary."},{"i":"p-sd-structural-flyweight","k":"prob","s":"sd-structural","t":"Flyweight","sub":"Structural · GoF · Hard · Structural Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Flyweight","how to solve Flyweight","Flyweight approach","Flyweight solution","Share common state across huge numbers of objects."],"w":"Structural Patterns Many objects, few distinct values → share. Use it when Text editors, game sprites, Integer/String caches.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Many objects, few distinct values → share.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Shared, immutable.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Intrinsic:<\/b> Shared, immutable.<\/li><li><b>Extrinsic:<\/b> Passed per use.<\/li><li><b>Factory:<\/b> Caches instances.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Text editors, game sprites, Integer/String caches.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Flyweights must be immutable or sharing becomes a bug.<\/p>","c":{"java":"record GlyphStyle(String font, int size, String color) {}   // intrinsic, immutable, shared\n \nclass StyleFactory {\n    private static final Map<GlyphStyle, GlyphStyle> pool = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    static GlyphStyle of(String f, int s, String c) {\n        return pool.computeIfAbsent(new GlyphStyle(f, s, c), k -> k);\n    }\n}\n \nclass Char {                                                  // extrinsic: per character\n    final char value; final int row, col; final GlyphStyle style;\n    Char(char v, int r, int c, GlyphStyle st) { value = v; row = r; col = c; style = st; }\n}\n// 1,000,000 chars, maybe 20 distinct styles"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-structural.html#p-flyweight"]],"ask":"Share common state across huge numbers of objects."},{"i":"p-sd-behavioral-strategy","k":"prob","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Strategy","sub":"Behavioral · GoF · Easy · Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Strategy","how to solve Strategy","Strategy approach","Strategy solution","Interchangeable algorithms behind one interface."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns if-else on algorithm → Strategy. Use it when Pricing, payment, sorting, routing, rate-limit algorithms.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> if-else on algorithm → Strategy.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> One method.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Interface:<\/b> One method.<\/li><li><b>Variants:<\/b> Class or lambda each.<\/li><li><b>Inject:<\/b> Constructor or setter.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Pricing, payment, sorting, routing, rate-limit algorithms.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> A strategy with only one implementation is ceremony.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface FareStrategy { Money fare(Trip t); }\n \nclass NormalFare implements FareStrategy {\n    public Money fare(Trip t) { return Money.of(50 + t.km() * 12); }\n}\nclass SurgeFare implements FareStrategy {\n    private final double mult;\n    SurgeFare(double m) { mult = m; }\n    public Money fare(Trip t) { return new NormalFare().fare(t).times(mult); }\n}\nclass PoolFare implements FareStrategy {\n    public Money fare(Trip t) { return new NormalFare().fare(t).times(0.6); }\n}\n \n// chosen at runtime, no if-else in RideService\nFareStrategy s = demand.isHigh() ? new SurgeFare(1.8) : new NormalFare();"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-behavioral.html#p-strategy"]],"ask":"Interchangeable algorithms behind one interface."},{"i":"p-sd-behavioral-observer","k":"prob","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Observer","sub":"Behavioral · GoF · Easy · Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Observer","how to solve Observer","Observer approach","Observer solution","Notify dependents automatically when state changes."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns One event, many reactions → Observer. Use it when Notifications, UI updates, domain events.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> One event, many reactions → Observer.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Keeps listeners.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Subject:<\/b> Keeps listeners.<\/li><li><b>Listener:<\/b> One callback.<\/li><li><b>Scale:<\/b> Event bus / Kafka.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Notifications, UI updates, domain events.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Synchronous listeners block the subject; forgotten unsubscribes leak memory.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface OrderListener { void onPlaced(Order o); }\n \nclass OrderService {\n    private final List<OrderListener> listeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n    void subscribe(OrderListener l) { listeners.add(l); }\n \n    void place(Order o) {\n        repo.save(o);\n        for (OrderListener l : listeners) l.onPlaced(o);   // fan-out\n    }\n}\n \norders.subscribe(o -> email.send(o.user(), \"Order placed\"));\norders.subscribe(o -> sms.send(o.phone(), \"Order placed\"));\norders.subscribe(o -> analytics.track(\"order\", o.id()));\norders.place(order);"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-behavioral.html#p-observer"]],"ask":"Notify dependents automatically when state changes."},{"i":"p-sd-behavioral-command","k":"prob","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Command","sub":"Behavioral · GoF · Medium · Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Command","how to solve Command","Command approach","Command solution","Encapsulate a request as an object."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Undo, queue, log → Command. Use it when Editors, job queues, transactions, remote controls.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Undo, queue, log → Command.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Do it.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>execute:<\/b> Do it.<\/li><li><b>undo:<\/b> Reverse it.<\/li><li><b>Invoker:<\/b> Stacks or queue.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Editors, job queues, transactions, remote controls.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> New command after undo must clear the redo stack.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface Command { void execute(); void undo(); }\n \nclass InsertText implements Command {\n    private final Editor ed; private final int pos; private final String text;\n    InsertText(Editor ed, int pos, String text) { this.ed = ed; this.pos = pos; this.text = text; }\n    public void execute() { ed.insert(pos, text); }\n    public void undo()    { ed.delete(pos, text.length()); }\n}\n \nclass History {\n    private final Deque<Command> undo = new ArrayDeque<>(), redo = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    void run(Command c) { c.execute(); undo.push(c); redo.clear(); }\n    void undo() { if (!undo.isEmpty()) { Command c = undo.pop(); c.undo(); redo.push(c); } }\n    void redo() { if (!redo.isEmpty()) { Command c = redo.pop(); c.execute(); undo.push(c); } }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-behavioral.html#p-command"]],"ask":"Encapsulate a request as an object."},{"i":"p-sd-behavioral-iterator","k":"prob","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Iterator","sub":"Behavioral · GoF · Easy · Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Iterator","how to solve Iterator","Iterator approach","Iterator solution","Sequential access without exposing the structure."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Custom collection → implement Iterable. Use it when Custom collections, paginated API clients.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Custom collection → implement Iterable.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> iterator().<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Iterable:<\/b> iterator().<\/li><li><b>hasNext/next:<\/b> The protocol.<\/li><li><b>for-each:<\/b> Free syntax.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Custom collections, paginated API clients.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Modifying a collection while iterating → ConcurrentModificationException.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class Playlist implements Iterable<Song> {\n    private final Song[] songs;                       // storage is private\n    Playlist(Song... s) { songs = s; }\n \n    public Iterator<Song> iterator() {\n        return new Iterator<>() {\n            int i = 0;\n            public boolean hasNext() { return i < songs.length; }\n            public Song next() { return songs[i++]; }\n        };\n    }\n}\nfor (Song s : playlist) play(s);                       // works with for-each"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-behavioral.html#p-iterator"]],"ask":"Sequential access without exposing the structure."},{"i":"p-sd-behavioral-state","k":"prob","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"State","sub":"Behavioral · GoF · Medium · Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["State","how to solve State","State approach","State solution","Change behaviour when internal state changes."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Mode-dependent behaviour → State. Use it when Vending machine, ATM, order lifecycle, TCP connection.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Mode-dependent behaviour → State.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Holds current state.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Context:<\/b> Holds current state.<\/li><li><b>State:<\/b> One class per mode.<\/li><li><b>Transition:<\/b> Methods return next state.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Vending machine, ATM, order lifecycle, TCP connection.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> For 2–3 simple states an enum with a switch can be clearer; say so.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface VendingState {\n    VendingState insertCoin(Machine m);\n    VendingState select(Machine m, String item);\n    VendingState dispense(Machine m);\n}\n \nclass Idle implements VendingState {\n    public VendingState insertCoin(Machine m) { return new HasCoin(); }\n    public VendingState select(Machine m, String i) { m.show(\"Insert coin first\"); return this; }\n    public VendingState dispense(Machine m) { return this; }\n}\nclass HasCoin implements VendingState {\n    public VendingState insertCoin(Machine m) { m.refund(); return this; }\n    public VendingState select(Machine m, String i) {\n        if (!m.inStock(i)) { m.show(\"Sold out\"); return this; }\n        m.choose(i); return new Dispensing();\n    }\n    public VendingState dispense(Machine m) { return this; }\n}\nclass Dispensing implements VendingState {\n    public VendingState insertCoin(Machine m) { m.show(\"Busy\"); return this; }\n    public VendingState select(Machine m, String i) { return this; }\n    public VendingState dispense(Machine m) { m.drop(); return new Idle(); }\n}\n \nclass Machine {\n    private VendingState state = new Idle();\n    void insertCoin() { state = state.insertCoin(this); }   // delegate, then switch\n    void select(String i) { state = state.select(this, i); }\n    void dispense() { state = state.dispense(this); }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-behavioral.html#p-state"]],"ask":"Change behaviour when internal state changes."},{"i":"p-sd-behavioral-cor","k":"prob","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Chain of Responsibility","sub":"Behavioral · GoF · Medium · Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Chain of Responsibility","how to solve Chain of Responsibility","Chain of Responsibility approach","Chain of Responsibility solution","Pass a request along a chain until handled or stopped."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Sequential checks → chain. Use it when Servlet filters, auth pipelines, approval flows, ATM cash dispenser.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Sequential checks → chain.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> process + next.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Handler:<\/b> process + next.<\/li><li><b>Stop:<\/b> Return false.<\/li><li><b>Build:<\/b> Link in order.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Servlet filters, auth pipelines, approval flows, ATM cash dispenser.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Make sure an unhandled request has a defined outcome.<\/p>","c":{"java":"abstract class Handler {\n    private Handler next;\n    Handler then(Handler n) { this.next = n; return n; }\n    void handle(Request r) {\n        if (!process(r)) return;                 // stop the chain\n        if (next != null) next.handle(r);        // or pass it on\n    }\n    protected abstract boolean process(Request r);\n}\n \nclass Auth extends Handler      { protected boolean process(Request r) { return r.hasValidToken(); } }\nclass RateLimit extends Handler { protected boolean process(Request r) { return bucket.tryAcquire(r.user()); } }\nclass Validate extends Handler  { protected boolean process(Request r) { return r.body() != null; } }\nclass Controller extends Handler{ protected boolean process(Request r) { service.run(r); return true; } }\n \nHandler chain = new Auth();\nchain.then(new RateLimit()).then(new Validate()).then(new Controller());\nchain.handle(request);"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-behavioral.html#p-cor"]],"ask":"Pass a request along a chain until handled or stopped."},{"i":"p-sd-behavioral-template","k":"prob","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Template Method","sub":"Behavioral · GoF · Easy · Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Template Method","how to solve Template Method","Template Method approach","Template Method solution","Algorithm skeleton in a base class, steps in subclasses."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Same steps, one differs → Template. Use it when Importers, game loops, framework lifecycles.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Same steps, one differs → Template.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Skeleton can’t be overridden.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>final:<\/b> Skeleton can’t be overridden.<\/li><li><b>abstract:<\/b> Required steps.<\/li><li><b>hooks:<\/b> Optional steps.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Importers, game loops, framework lifecycles.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Inheritance-based: prefer Strategy if the step must change at runtime.<\/p>","c":{"java":"abstract class DataImporter {\n    public final void run(Path file) {          // the template: fixed order, final\n        validate(file);\n        List<Row> rows = parse(file);           // step varies\n        save(rows);\n        audit(file, rows.size());\n    }\n    protected abstract List<Row> parse(Path file);\n    protected void validate(Path f) { /* shared default */ }\n    private void save(List<Row> rows) { /* shared */ }\n    private void audit(Path f, int n) { /* shared */ }\n}\nclass CsvImporter extends DataImporter   { protected List<Row> parse(Path f) { ... } }\nclass ExcelImporter extends DataImporter { protected List<Row> parse(Path f) { ... } }"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-behavioral.html#p-template"]],"ask":"Algorithm skeleton in a base class, steps in subclasses."},{"i":"p-sd-behavioral-mediator","k":"prob","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Mediator","sub":"Behavioral · GoF · Medium · Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Mediator","how to solve Mediator","Mediator approach","Mediator solution","Centralise communication between objects."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Objects chatting with each other → hub. Use it when Chat rooms, air traffic control, UI dialogs.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Objects chatting with each other → hub.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Routes messages.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Hub:<\/b> Routes messages.<\/li><li><b>Colleagues:<\/b> Know only the hub.<\/li><li><b>Win:<\/b> N×N → N.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Chat rooms, air traffic control, UI dialogs.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> The mediator itself can become a god class.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface ChatRoom { void send(String from, String msg); void join(User u); }\n \nclass Room implements ChatRoom {\n    private final Map<String, User> users = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    public void join(User u) { users.put(u.name(), u); }\n    public void send(String from, String msg) {\n        users.values().stream()\n             .filter(u -> !u.name().equals(from))\n             .forEach(u -> u.receive(from, msg));       // users never reference each other\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-behavioral.html#p-mediator"]],"ask":"Centralise communication between objects."},{"i":"p-sd-behavioral-memento","k":"prob","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Memento","sub":"Behavioral · GoF · Medium · Behavioral Patterns","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Memento","how to solve Memento","Memento approach","Memento solution","Capture and restore state without breaking encapsulation."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Checkpoint / undo state → Memento. Use it when Editors, games, form drafts.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Checkpoint / undo state → Memento.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> save/restore.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Originator:<\/b> save/restore.<\/li><li><b>Memento:<\/b> Opaque snapshot.<\/li><li><b>Caretaker:<\/b> Stores history.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Editors, games, form drafts.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Full snapshots of big objects are memory-heavy; store diffs or cap history.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class Editor {\n    private String text = \"\"; private int cursor;\n    public Snapshot save() { return new Snapshot(text, cursor); }\n    public void restore(Snapshot s) { text = s.text(); cursor = s.cursor(); }\n    public record Snapshot(String text, int cursor) {}      // opaque, immutable\n}\n \nDeque<Editor.Snapshot> history = new ArrayDeque<>();\nhistory.push(editor.save());   // before each change\neditor.type(\"hello\");\neditor.restore(history.pop()); // undo"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-behavioral.html#p-memento"]],"ask":"Capture and restore state without breaking encapsulation."},{"i":"p-sd-lld-parkinglot","k":"prob","s":"sd-lld","t":"Parking Lot","sub":"LLD classic · Medium · LLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Parking Lot","how to solve Parking Lot","Parking Lot approach","Parking Lot solution","Levels, spot sizes, tickets, fees, multiple gates."],"w":"LLD Problems Enums for types, strategies for rules. Patterns used Strategy, enum, (injected) Singleton.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Enums for types, strategies for rules.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Lot, Level, Spot, Ticket, Vehicle.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Classes:<\/b> Lot, Level, Spot, Ticket, Vehicle.<\/li><li><b>Patterns:<\/b> SpotFinder + FeePolicy strategies.<\/li><li><b>Concurrency:<\/b> Atomic tryOccupy.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Patterns used:<\/b> Strategy, enum, (injected) Singleton.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Putting fee logic in Ticket or Vehicle; forgetting two gates can race for one spot.<\/p>","c":{"java":"enum VehicleType { BIKE, CAR, TRUCK }\nenum SpotSize { SMALL, MEDIUM, LARGE;\n    boolean fits(VehicleType v) { return ordinal() >= v.ordinal(); }   // bike fits anywhere\n}\n \nclass Spot {\n    final String id; final SpotSize size; private Vehicle parked;\n    synchronized boolean tryOccupy(Vehicle v) {            // atomic per spot\n        if (parked != null || !size.fits(v.type())) return false;\n        parked = v; return true;\n    }\n    synchronized void free() { parked = null; }\n}\n \ninterface SpotFinder { Optional<Spot> find(List<Level> levels, Vehicle v); }\nclass NearestFirst implements SpotFinder {\n    public Optional<Spot> find(List<Level> levels, Vehicle v) {\n        for (Level l : levels) for (Spot s : l.spots())\n            if (s.tryOccupy(v)) return Optional.of(s);       // scan in order, claim atomically\n        return Optional.empty();\n    }\n}\n \ninterface FeePolicy { Money fee(Ticket t, Instant out); }\nclass Hourly implements FeePolicy {\n    public Money fee(Ticket t, Instant out) {\n        long h = Math.max(1, Duration.between(t.in(), out).toHours());\n        return Money.of(h * t.rate());\n    }\n}\n \nclass ParkingLot {\n    private final List<Level> levels; private final SpotFinder finder; private final FeePolicy fees;\n    private final Map<String, Ticket> active = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n \n    Ticket park(Vehicle v) {\n        Spot s = finder.find(levels, v).orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException(\"Lot full\"));\n        Ticket t = new Ticket(UUID.randomUUID().toString(), v, s, Instant.now());\n        active.put(t.id(), t);\n        return t;\n    }\n    Money exit(String ticketId) {\n        Ticket t = active.remove(ticketId);\n        t.spot().free();\n        return fees.fee(t, Instant.now());\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-lld.html#p-parkinglot"]],"ask":"Levels, spot sizes, tickets, fees, multiple gates."},{"i":"p-sd-lld-atm","k":"prob","s":"sd-lld","t":"ATM","sub":"LLD classic · Medium · LLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["ATM","how to solve ATM","ATM approach","ATM solution","Card, PIN, withdraw, dispense notes."],"w":"LLD Problems Mode-driven → State; denominations → chain. Patterns used State, Chain of Responsibility.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Mode-driven → State; denominations → chain.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Idle, HasCard, Authenticated.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>States:<\/b> Idle, HasCard, Authenticated.<\/li><li><b>Dispense:<\/b> Chain 2000 → 500 → 100.<\/li><li><b>Bank:<\/b> Interface for verify/debit.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Patterns used:<\/b> State, Chain of Responsibility.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Debit before checking the ATM has enough notes; check both, then commit.<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface AtmState {\n    default AtmState insertCard(Atm a, Card c) { throw new IllegalStateException(); }\n    default AtmState enterPin(Atm a, String pin) { throw new IllegalStateException(); }\n    default AtmState withdraw(Atm a, int amount) { throw new IllegalStateException(); }\n    default AtmState eject(Atm a) { a.returnCard(); return new Idle(); }\n}\nclass Idle implements AtmState { public AtmState insertCard(Atm a, Card c) { a.hold(c); return new HasCard(); } }\nclass HasCard implements AtmState {\n    public AtmState enterPin(Atm a, String pin) { return a.bank().verify(a.card(), pin) ? new Authenticated() : eject(a); }\n}\nclass Authenticated implements AtmState {\n    public AtmState withdraw(Atm a, int amt) {\n        if (!a.bank().debit(a.card(), amt)) return this;\n        a.dispenser().dispense(amt);\n        return eject(a);\n    }\n}\n \n// Cash dispenser: Chain of Responsibility\nabstract class NoteHandler {\n    private final int note; private NoteHandler next;\n    NoteHandler(int note) { this.note = note; }\n    NoteHandler then(NoteHandler n) { next = n; return n; }\n    void dispense(int amt) {\n        int count = amt / note, rest = amt % note;\n        if (count > 0) System.out.println(count + \" x \" + note);\n        if (rest > 0) next.dispense(rest);\n    }\n}\n// 2000 -> 500 -> 100 : 3700 = 1x2000 + 3x500 + 2x100"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-lld.html#p-atm"]],"ask":"Card, PIN, withdraw, dispense notes."},{"i":"p-sd-lld-elevator","k":"prob","s":"sd-lld","t":"Elevator","sub":"LLD classic · Hard · LLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Elevator","how to solve Elevator","Elevator approach","Elevator solution","One or many cars, internal and hall requests."],"w":"LLD Problems Keep direction while requests remain ahead. Patterns used Strategy (dispatch), State (moving/idle/maintenance).","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Keep direction while requests remain ahead.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Up/down TreeSets.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Per car:<\/b> Up/down TreeSets.<\/li><li><b>Algorithm:<\/b> SCAN.<\/li><li><b>Many cars:<\/b> Dispatcher strategy.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Patterns used:<\/b> Strategy (dispatch), State (moving/idle/maintenance).<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> FCFS scheduling: correct but wasteful; mention starvation for nearest-first.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class Elevator {\n    private int floor = 0; private Dir dir = Dir.IDLE;\n    private final TreeSet<Integer> up = new TreeSet<>(), down = new TreeSet<>();\n \n    synchronized void request(int f) {\n        if (f > floor) up.add(f); else if (f < floor) down.add(f);\n        if (dir == Dir.IDLE) dir = f > floor ? Dir.UP : Dir.DOWN;\n    }\n \n    synchronized void step() {                         // called every tick\n        if (dir == Dir.UP) {\n            Integer next = up.ceiling(floor);           // nearest request ahead\n            if (next == null) { dir = down.isEmpty() ? Dir.IDLE : Dir.DOWN; return; }\n            floor++;\n            if (up.remove(floor)) openDoors();\n        } else if (dir == Dir.DOWN) {\n            Integer next = down.floor(floor);\n            if (next == null) { dir = up.isEmpty() ? Dir.IDLE : Dir.UP; return; }\n            floor--;\n            if (down.remove(floor)) openDoors();\n        }\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-lld.html#p-elevator"]],"ask":"One or many cars, internal and hall requests."},{"i":"p-sd-lld-bookmyshow","k":"prob","s":"sd-lld","t":"BookMyShow","sub":"LLD + concurrency · Hard · LLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["BookMyShow","how to solve BookMyShow","BookMyShow approach","BookMyShow solution","Browse shows, pick seats, pay, confirm."],"w":"LLD Problems Hold atomically, confirm on payment, expire holds. Patterns used Conditional update, idempotency, Observer for notifications.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Hold atomically, confirm on payment, expire holds.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> ShowSeat per show.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Model:<\/b> ShowSeat per show.<\/li><li><b>Hold:<\/b> HELD with expiry.<\/li><li><b>Confirm:<\/b> After payment, idempotent.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Patterns used:<\/b> Conditional update, idempotency, Observer for notifications.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Holding a DB lock during payment; forgetting to release partial holds.<\/p>","c":{"java":"enum SeatStatus { FREE, HELD, BOOKED }\n \nclass ShowSeat {                       // a seat's status for ONE show\n    final String showId, seatId; SeatStatus status; String heldBy; Instant holdUntil;\n}\n \nclass BookingService {\n    private final ShowSeatRepo seats; private final PaymentGateway pay;\n \n    // atomic: UPDATE show_seat SET status='HELD', held_by=?, hold_until=now()+5min\n    //         WHERE show_id=? AND seat_id IN (...) AND (status='FREE' OR hold_until < now())\n    Booking hold(String user, String showId, List<String> seatIds) {\n        int updated = seats.holdIfFree(showId, seatIds, user, Duration.ofMinutes(5));\n        if (updated != seatIds.size()) {\n            seats.release(showId, seatIds, user);       // all or nothing\n            throw new SeatUnavailableException();\n        }\n        return Booking.pending(user, showId, seatIds);\n    }\n \n    void confirm(Booking b, PaymentInfo p) {\n        pay.charge(p, b.amount(), b.id());               // idempotency key = booking id\n        seats.markBooked(b.showId(), b.seatIds(), b.user());   // WHERE held_by = user\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-lld.html#p-bookmyshow"]],"ask":"Browse shows, pick seats, pay, confirm."},{"i":"p-sd-lld-race","k":"prob","s":"sd-lld","t":"Race Conditions","sub":"Concurrency · Medium · LLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Race Conditions","how to solve Race Conditions","Race Conditions approach","Race Conditions solution","Why check-then-act fails and how to fix it."],"w":"LLD Problems Make check+act one atomic step. Patterns used Bookings, counters, inventory, singletons.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Make check+act one atomic step.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Gap between read and write.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Bug:<\/b> Gap between read and write.<\/li><li><b>JVM:<\/b> synchronized / CAS.<\/li><li><b>Servers:<\/b> DB or distributed lock.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Patterns used:<\/b> Bookings, counters, inventory, singletons.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> volatile gives visibility, not atomicity: count++ is still a race.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// BUG: check-then-act\nboolean book(String seat, String user) {\n    if (status.get(seat) == FREE) {            // 1. check\n        status.put(seat, BOOKED);              // 2. act  (another thread can run between 1 and 2)\n        owner.put(seat, user);\n        return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n \n// FIX (one JVM): make check+act a single atomic step\nConcurrentHashMap<String, String> owner = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\nboolean book(String seat, String user) {\n    return owner.putIfAbsent(seat, user) == null;   // atomic compare-and-set\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-lld.html#p-race"]],"ask":"Why check-then-act fails and how to fix it."},{"i":"p-sd-lld-seatsql","k":"prob","s":"sd-lld","t":"Conditional Update & Row Locks","sub":"Concurrency · SQL · Medium · LLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Conditional Update & Row Locks","how to solve Conditional Update & Row Locks","Conditional Update & Row Locks approach","Conditional Update & Row Locks solution","Database as the referee."],"w":"LLD Problems 0 rows updated = you lost the race. Patterns used Multi-server booking and inventory.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> 0 rows updated = you lost the race.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> WHERE status = FREE.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Conditional:<\/b> WHERE status = FREE.<\/li><li><b>Pessimistic:<\/b> SELECT … FOR UPDATE.<\/li><li><b>Result:<\/b> Check rows affected.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Patterns used:<\/b> Multi-server booking and inventory.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Forgetting the WHERE condition turns it back into last-write-wins.<\/p>","c":{"java":"-- FIX (many servers): let the database do check+act atomically\nUPDATE show_seat\n   SET status = 'BOOKED', user_id = :user\n WHERE show_id = :show AND seat_id = :seat\n   AND status = 'FREE';\n-- rows affected = 1 -> you got it;  0 -> someone else did\n \n-- Pessimistic alternative: lock the row first\nBEGIN;\nSELECT status FROM show_seat WHERE show_id = :show AND seat_id = :seat FOR UPDATE;\n-- other transactions wait here until COMMIT\nUPDATE show_seat SET status = 'BOOKED', user_id = :user WHERE show_id = :show AND seat_id = :seat;\nCOMMIT;"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-lld.html#p-seatsql"]],"ask":"Database as the referee."},{"i":"p-sd-lld-optimistic","k":"prob","s":"sd-lld","t":"Optimistic Locking","sub":"Concurrency · SQL · Medium · LLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Optimistic Locking","how to solve Optimistic Locking","Optimistic Locking approach","Optimistic Locking solution","Detect conflicting updates without holding locks."],"w":"LLD Problems Rare conflicts → version column. Patterns used Wallets, profiles, carts, admin edits.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Rare conflicts → version column.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Value + version.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Read:<\/b> Value + version.<\/li><li><b>Write:<\/b> WHERE version = old.<\/li><li><b>Conflict:<\/b> Retry or tell the user.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Patterns used:<\/b> Wallets, profiles, carts, admin edits.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Under heavy contention retries pile up; switch to pessimistic or queue.<\/p>","c":{"java":"-- Optimistic locking: version column, no locks held while the user thinks\nSELECT id, balance, version FROM wallet WHERE id = 42;          -- balance 500, version 7\n \nUPDATE wallet SET balance = 400, version = 8\n WHERE id = 42 AND version = 7;                                 -- 0 rows -> someone changed it, retry\n \n-- JPA does this for you with @Version on a field"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-lld.html#p-optimistic"]],"ask":"Detect conflicting updates without holding locks."},{"i":"p-sd-basics-framework","k":"prob","s":"sd-basics","t":"Interview Framework","sub":"SD Basics 01 · Easy · System Design Basics","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Interview Framework","how to solve Interview Framework","Interview Framework approach","Interview Framework solution","How to spend 45 minutes."],"w":"System Design Basics Requirements before boxes. Use it when Every HLD round.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Requirements before boxes.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Requirements.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>5 min:<\/b> Requirements.<\/li><li><b>10 min:<\/b> Estimates, API, data.<\/li><li><b>30 min:<\/b> Design + deep dive.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Every HLD round.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Spending 20 minutes on requirements and never reaching the deep dive.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// API for a URL shortener (sketch)\nPOST /v1/urls        { \"longUrl\": \"...\", \"alias\": null }   -> 201 { \"code\": \"aZ3k9Q\" }\nGET  /{code}                                               -> 301/302 Location: longUrl\n \n// Estimates: 100M new URLs/month -> ~40 writes/s; reads 100:1 -> ~4k reads/s\n// Storage: 100M x 12 months x 5 years x 500 B ~ 3 TB\n// Design: LB -> stateless app -> Redis cache (code -> url) -> key-value DB\n// Deep dive: generating unique 7-char codes (counter + base62, or random + retry on collision)"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-basics.html#p-framework"]],"ask":"How to spend 45 minutes."},{"i":"p-sd-basics-estimation","k":"prob","s":"sd-basics","t":"Back-of-the-envelope","sub":"SD Basics · Easy · System Design Basics","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Back-of-the-envelope","how to solve Back-of-the-envelope","Back-of-the-envelope approach","Back-of-the-envelope solution","QPS, storage, bandwidth in a minute."],"w":"System Design Basics Round aggressively, then find the bottleneck. Use it when Choosing cache, sharding, queue.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Round aggressively, then find the bottleneck.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> ≈ 10^5 s.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Day:<\/b> ≈ 10^5 s.<\/li><li><b>Peak:<\/b> 2–3× average.<\/li><li><b>Storage:<\/b> count × size × time.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Choosing cache, sharding, queue.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Forgetting replication factor (×3) and indexes when sizing storage.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Estimation in five lines\nlong dau = 100_000_000L, reqPerUser = 10;\nlong perDay = dau * reqPerUser;             // 1e9 requests/day\nlong avgQps = perDay / 100_000;             // 1 day ~ 1e5 s  -> ~10k QPS\nlong peakQps = avgQps * 3;                  // plan for peak  -> ~30k QPS\nlong storagePerYear = 5_000_000L * 1_000 * 365;  // 5M writes/day x 1 KB -> ~1.8 TB/yr"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-basics.html#p-estimation"]],"ask":"QPS, storage, bandwidth in a minute."},{"i":"p-sd-basics-scaling","k":"prob","s":"sd-basics","t":"Scaling 1 → 1M","sub":"SD Basics 02 · Medium · System Design Basics","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Scaling 1 → 1M","how to solve Scaling 1 → 1M","Scaling 1 → 1M approach","Scaling 1 → 1M solution","Evolve one server into a scalable system."],"w":"System Design Basics Fix the current bottleneck only. Use it when The narrative behind every HLD.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Fix the current bottleneck only.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> App and DB.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Split:<\/b> App and DB.<\/li><li><b>Out:<\/b> Stateless + LB.<\/li><li><b>Reads:<\/b> Cache + replicas + CDN.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when The narrative behind every HLD.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Jumping to microservices and sharding for 10k users.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Stateless app server: nothing user-specific lives in memory\n@RestController\nclass CartController {\n    private final RedisTemplate<String, Cart> sessions;   // shared session store\n    private final CartService carts;\n \n    @GetMapping(\"/cart\")\n    Cart get(@CookieValue(\"sid\") String sid) {\n        return sessions.opsForValue().get(\"cart:\" + sid); // any server can answer\n    }\n}\n// Files -> S3, sessions -> Redis or JWT, config -> env vars.\n// Now: add servers behind the LB, add read replicas, add a cache, add a CDN."},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-basics.html#p-scaling"]],"ask":"Evolve one server into a scalable system."},{"i":"p-sd-basics-loadbalancer","k":"prob","s":"sd-basics","t":"Load Balancers","sub":"SD Basics 03 · Medium · System Design Basics","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Load Balancers","how to solve Load Balancers","Load Balancers approach","Load Balancers solution","L4 vs L7, algorithms, health checks."],"w":"System Design Basics Variable cost → least connections. Use it when Every horizontally scaled tier.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Variable cost → least connections.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> IP/port, fast.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>L4:<\/b> IP/port, fast.<\/li><li><b>L7:<\/b> HTTP-aware routing.<\/li><li><b>Algo:<\/b> RR, least-conn, hashing.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Every horizontally scaled tier.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> The LB itself must be redundant (active-passive pair or managed).<\/p>","c":{"java":"interface Balancer { Server pick(); }\n \nclass RoundRobin implements Balancer {\n    private final List<Server> servers; private final AtomicInteger i = new AtomicInteger();\n    public Server pick() {\n        return servers.get(Math.floorMod(i.getAndIncrement(), servers.size()));\n    }\n}\n \nclass LeastConnections implements Balancer {\n    private final List<Server> servers;\n    public Server pick() {\n        return servers.stream().filter(Server::healthy)\n                      .min(Comparator.comparingInt(Server::activeConnections))\n                      .orElseThrow();\n    }\n}\n// Consistent hashing on user id when you need stickiness (caches, WebSocket shards)"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-basics.html#p-loadbalancer"]],"ask":"L4 vs L7, algorithms, health checks."},{"i":"p-sd-basics-apigateway","k":"prob","s":"sd-basics","t":"API Gateway","sub":"SD Basics 04 · Medium · System Design Basics","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["API Gateway","how to solve API Gateway","API Gateway approach","API Gateway solution","Single entry point for microservices."],"w":"System Design Basics Cross-cutting concerns at the edge. Use it when Microservice architectures, public APIs.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Cross-cutting concerns at the edge.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Verify once.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Auth:<\/b> Verify once.<\/li><li><b>Limit:<\/b> Per user/key.<\/li><li><b>Route:<\/b> Path → service.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Microservice architectures, public APIs.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Business logic creeping into the gateway.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// The gateway runs a chain of filters before routing\nList<GatewayFilter> chain = List.of(\n    new AuthFilter(jwtVerifier),           // 401 if token invalid\n    new RateLimitFilter(tokenBuckets),     // 429 if over quota\n    new RequestIdFilter(),                 // trace id for logs\n    new RouteFilter(Map.of(\n        \"/api/orders/**\",   \"http://order-service\",\n        \"/api/payments/**\", \"http://payment-service\",\n        \"/api/users/**\",    \"http://user-service\"))\n);"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-basics.html#p-apigateway"]],"ask":"Single entry point for microservices."},{"i":"p-sd-basics-apidesign","k":"prob","s":"sd-basics","t":"API Design","sub":"SD Basics 05 · Medium · System Design Basics","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["API Design","how to solve API Design","API Design approach","API Design solution","REST resources, codes, pagination, idempotency, versions."],"w":"System Design Basics Design for retries and growth. Use it when Every service boundary.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Design for retries and growth.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> POST /orders.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Nouns:<\/b> POST /orders.<\/li><li><b>Cursor:<\/b> after + limit.<\/li><li><b>Idempotency:<\/b> Key header.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Every service boundary.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Returning 200 with an error body; offset pagination on feeds.<\/p>","c":{"java":"@RestController @RequestMapping(\"/v1/orders\")\nclass OrderController {\n \n    @PostMapping\n    ResponseEntity<Order> create(@RequestHeader(\"Idempotency-Key\") String key,\n                                 @Valid @RequestBody CreateOrder req) {\n        Order o = idempotency.computeIfAbsent(key, () -> orders.create(req));  // retry-safe\n        return ResponseEntity.status(201).body(o);\n    }\n \n    @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")\n    Order get(@PathVariable long id) {\n        return orders.find(id).orElseThrow(NotFound::new);                   // 404\n    }\n \n    @GetMapping\n    Page<Order> list(@RequestParam(required = false) Long after,\n                     @RequestParam(defaultValue = \"20\") int limit) {\n        // WHERE user_id = ? AND id < :after ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT :limit\n        List<Order> rows = orders.pageBefore(currentUser(), after, limit);\n        Long next = rows.size() == limit ? rows.get(rows.size() - 1).id() : null;\n        return new Page<>(rows, next);                                       // cursor\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-basics.html#p-apidesign"]],"ask":"REST resources, codes, pagination, idempotency, versions."},{"i":"p-sd-basics-realtime","k":"prob","s":"sd-basics","t":"Polling, SSE, WebSockets","sub":"SD Basics 06 · Medium · System Design Basics","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Polling, SSE, WebSockets","how to solve Polling, SSE, WebSockets","Polling, SSE, WebSockets approach","Polling, SSE, WebSockets solution","Getting updates to clients."],"w":"System Design Basics Direction + frequency pick the tool. Use it when Chat, live scores, notifications, collaboration.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Direction + frequency pick the tool.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Simple, wasteful.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Poll:<\/b> Simple, wasteful.<\/li><li><b>SSE:<\/b> One-way stream.<\/li><li><b>WS:<\/b> Two-way, stateful.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Chat, live scores, notifications, collaboration.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Forgetting how to route a message to the server holding the socket.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// WebSocket: one persistent, two-way connection per client\n@Component\nclass ChatSocket extends TextWebSocketHandler {\n    private final Map<String, WebSocketSession> online = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n \n    public void afterConnectionEstablished(WebSocketSession s) {\n        online.put(userOf(s), s);\n    }\n    protected void handleTextMessage(WebSocketSession s, TextMessage m) throws IOException {\n        Msg msg = parse(m);\n        WebSocketSession to = online.get(msg.to());\n        if (to != null) to.sendMessage(new TextMessage(msg.json()));   // server pushes\n        else offlineQueue.add(msg);\n    }\n}\n// SSE alternative for one-way pushes: return an SseEmitter and call emitter.send(event)"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-basics.html#p-realtime"]],"ask":"Getting updates to clients."},{"i":"p-sd-databases-sqlnosql","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"SQL vs NoSQL","sub":"Data 02 · Easy · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["SQL vs NoSQL","how to solve SQL vs NoSQL","SQL vs NoSQL approach","SQL vs NoSQL solution","When would you pick each?"],"w":"Databases Name the access pattern, then the store. Use it when Opening question of most data discussions.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Name the access pattern, then the store.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Relations, transactions, ad-hoc queries.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>SQL:<\/b> Relations, transactions, ad-hoc queries.<\/li><li><b>NoSQL:<\/b> Known queries at huge scale.<\/li><li><b>Both:<\/b> Polyglot by workload.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Opening question of most data discussions.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> “NoSQL scales, SQL doesn’t” is wrong and interviewers will push back.<\/p>","c":{"java":"-- Cassandra table for chat: partition by conversation, cluster by time\nCREATE TABLE messages (\n  conversation_id uuid,\n  message_id      timeuuid,          -- sortable by time, unique\n  sender_id       uuid,\n  body            text,\n  PRIMARY KEY ((conversation_id), message_id)\n) WITH CLUSTERING ORDER BY (message_id DESC);\n \n-- \"last 50 messages in this chat\" = one partition, already sorted\nSELECT * FROM messages WHERE conversation_id = ? LIMIT 50;"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-sqlnosql"]],"ask":"When would you pick each?"},{"i":"p-sd-databases-normalize","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"Normalisation vs Denormalisation","sub":"Data · Medium · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Normalisation vs Denormalisation","how to solve Normalisation vs Denormalisation","Normalisation vs Denormalisation approach","Normalisation vs Denormalisation solution","Duplicate data or join at read time?"],"w":"Databases Write-heavy → normalise; hot reads → denormalise. Use it when Feeds, dashboards, NoSQL modelling.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Write-heavy → normalise; hot reads → denormalise.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> One fact, one place.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Normalise:<\/b> One fact, one place.<\/li><li><b>Denormalise:<\/b> Copy for fast reads.<\/li><li><b>Sync:<\/b> Async jobs / events.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Feeds, dashboards, NoSQL modelling.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Denormalising without a plan to update copies.<\/p>","c":{"java":"-- Normalised (3NF): no duplicated facts, joins at read time\nCREATE TABLE users  (id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT);\nCREATE TABLE posts  (id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY, user_id BIGINT REFERENCES users(id), body TEXT);\n \n-- Denormalised for a hot read path: copy the author name into the post row\nALTER TABLE posts ADD COLUMN author_name TEXT;\n-- fast feed reads with no join, but renaming a user must update many posts (async job)"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-normalize"]],"ask":"Duplicate data or join at read time?"},{"i":"p-sd-databases-indexes","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"Indexes","sub":"Data 03 · Easy · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Indexes","how to solve Indexes","Indexes approach","Indexes solution","How an index speeds up reads and what it costs."],"w":"Databases Slow filter → index → EXPLAIN. Use it when Every slow-query question.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Slow filter → index → EXPLAIN.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Sorted pages, O(log n).<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>B-tree:<\/b> Sorted pages, O(log n).<\/li><li><b>Cost:<\/b> Slower writes, more disk.<\/li><li><b>Check:<\/b> EXPLAIN.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Every slow-query question.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Indexing low-cardinality columns (gender) rarely helps.<\/p>","c":{"java":"-- No index on city: the database reads every row\nSELECT id, name FROM users WHERE city = 'Pune';\n \nEXPLAIN SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE city = 'Pune';\n-- Seq Scan on users  (rows=1000000)   <- the red flag\n \nCREATE INDEX idx_users_city ON users (city);\n-- Index Scan using idx_users_city     <- now O(log n) + matches"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-indexes"]],"ask":"How an index speeds up reads and what it costs."},{"i":"p-sd-databases-compositeindex","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"Composite Index","sub":"Data 03 · Medium · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Composite Index","how to solve Composite Index","Composite Index approach","Composite Index solution","Multi-column indexes and the leftmost-prefix rule."],"w":"Databases Index matches WHERE then ORDER BY. Use it when Feeds, “orders for user by date”.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Index matches WHERE then ORDER BY.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Equality first, range last.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Order:<\/b> Equality first, range last.<\/li><li><b>Prefix:<\/b> (A,B) serves A and A+B.<\/li><li><b>Covering:<\/b> Include selected columns.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Feeds, “orders for user by date”.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> (created_at, user_id) for a per-user query is the wrong order.<\/p>","c":{"java":"CREATE INDEX idx_orders_id ON orders (id);       -- B-tree by default\n \nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = 70;              -- root -> internal -> leaf: ~3 page reads\n \n-- Composite index: sorted by user_id, then created_at\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_time ON orders (user_id, created_at);\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 42 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;   -- uses it\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at > now() - interval '1 day';            -- can't (no leftmost column)"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-compositeindex"]],"ask":"Multi-column indexes and the leftmost-prefix rule."},{"i":"p-sd-databases-nplus1","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"N+1 Queries","sub":"Data · ORM · Medium · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["N+1 Queries","how to solve N+1 Queries","N+1 Queries approach","N+1 Queries solution","Why the ORM fires 101 queries."],"w":"Databases Loop + lazy relation → fetch eagerly once. Use it when Hibernate/JPA codebases.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Loop + lazy relation → fetch eagerly once.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Lazy loading in a loop.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Cause:<\/b> Lazy loading in a loop.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> JOIN FETCH / batch.<\/li><li><b>Spot:<\/b> Query logs, counts.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Hibernate/JPA codebases.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Making everything EAGER instead, which loads too much elsewhere.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// N+1: 1 query for orders, then 1 query per order for its items\nList<Order> orders = orderRepo.findByUser(42);           // 1 query\nfor (Order o : orders) o.getItems().size();              // +N lazy queries\n \n// Fix: fetch in one go\n@Query(\"SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items WHERE o.userId = :uid\")\nList<Order> findWithItems(@Param(\"uid\") long uid);       // 1 query\n// or batch: WHERE order_id IN (:ids)"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-nplus1"]],"ask":"Why the ORM fires 101 queries."},{"i":"p-sd-databases-acid","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"ACID Transactions","sub":"Data 03 · Easy · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["ACID Transactions","how to solve ACID Transactions","ACID Transactions approach","ACID Transactions solution","What each letter guarantees."],"w":"Databases Must-all-happen writes → one transaction. Use it when Payments, orders, inventory.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Must-all-happen writes → one transaction.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> All or nothing.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>A:<\/b> All or nothing.<\/li><li><b>I:<\/b> Levels of isolation.<\/li><li><b>D:<\/b> WAL survives crash.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Payments, orders, inventory.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Calling a remote API inside a DB transaction holds locks for seconds.<\/p>","c":{"java":"@Service\nclass TransferService {\n    private final AccountRepo accounts;\n \n    @Transactional(isolation = Isolation.READ_COMMITTED)\n    public void transfer(long from, long to, long paise) {\n        int debited = accounts.debitIfEnough(from, paise);   // UPDATE ... WHERE balance >= ?\n        if (debited == 0) throw new InsufficientFunds();      // throws -> whole txn rolls back\n        accounts.credit(to, paise);\n    }   // commit here: both updates or neither\n}\n// Lock rows in a consistent order (lower id first) to avoid deadlocks between A->B and B->A transfers"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-acid"]],"ask":"What each letter guarantees."},{"i":"p-sd-databases-isolation","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"Isolation Levels & Lost Update","sub":"Data 03 · Hard · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Isolation Levels & Lost Update","how to solve Isolation Levels & Lost Update","Isolation Levels & Lost Update approach","Isolation Levels & Lost Update solution","Dirty, non-repeatable, phantom, lost update."],"w":"Databases Read-modify-write → make it atomic. Use it when Wallets, counters, stock.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Read-modify-write → make it atomic.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Postgres default.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>RC:<\/b> Postgres default.<\/li><li><b>RR:<\/b> MySQL default.<\/li><li><b>Fix:<\/b> Atomic UPDATE / FOR UPDATE.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Wallets, counters, stock.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Assuming @Transactional alone prevents lost updates at Read Committed.<\/p>","c":{"java":"-- BUG: read in the app, write back (lost update under READ COMMITTED)\nSELECT balance FROM wallet WHERE id = 7;           -- both transactions read 100\nUPDATE wallet SET balance = 70 WHERE id = 7;       -- T1 wrote 100 - 30\nUPDATE wallet SET balance = 50 WHERE id = 7;       -- T2 wrote 100 - 50, T1's debit is lost\n \n-- FIX 1: let the database compute it atomically\nUPDATE wallet SET balance = balance - 30 WHERE id = 7 AND balance >= 30;\n \n-- FIX 2: lock the row while you decide\nBEGIN;\nSELECT balance FROM wallet WHERE id = 7 FOR UPDATE;   -- T2 waits here\nUPDATE wallet SET balance = 70 WHERE id = 7;\nCOMMIT;"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-isolation"]],"ask":"Dirty, non-repeatable, phantom, lost update."},{"i":"p-sd-databases-replication","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"Replication","sub":"Data 04 · Medium · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Replication","how to solve Replication","Replication approach","Replication solution","Primary/replica, sync vs async, lag."],"w":"Databases Read-heavy → replicas; lag → read-your-writes. Use it when Every production database.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Read-heavy → replicas; lag → read-your-writes.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Fast, may lag.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Async:<\/b> Fast, may lag.<\/li><li><b>Sync:<\/b> Safe, slower.<\/li><li><b>RYW:<\/b> Primary after write.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Every production database.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Failover with async replication can lose the last few writes.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Route reads to replicas, but read-your-writes from the primary for a short window\nclass RoutingDataSource {\n    DataSource pick(Request r) {\n        if (r.isWrite()) return primary;\n        Instant lastWrite = recentWrites.get(r.userId());          // set on every write\n        if (lastWrite != null && lastWrite.isAfter(Instant.now().minusSeconds(5)))\n            return primary;                                         // avoid replica lag surprises\n        return replicas.get(ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(replicas.size()));\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-replication"]],"ask":"Primary/replica, sync vs async, lag."},{"i":"p-sd-databases-sharding","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"Sharding","sub":"Data 05 · Hard · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Sharding","how to solve Sharding","Sharding approach","Sharding solution","Partition data across machines."],"w":"Databases Shard last, by user/tenant. Use it when Huge write volume or data size.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Shard last, by user/tenant.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> High cardinality, co-located.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Key:<\/b> High cardinality, co-located.<\/li><li><b>Map:<\/b> Hash, range or directory.<\/li><li><b>Pain:<\/b> Cross-shard joins, rebalancing.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Huge write volume or data size.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Auto-increment IDs collide across shards; use Snowflake/UUIDs.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Naive sharding: hash mod N\nint shardFor(String key, int n) {\n    return Math.floorMod(key.hashCode(), n);\n}\n// n = 3 -> 4 : most keys change shard, so most data must move"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-sharding"]],"ask":"Partition data across machines."},{"i":"p-sd-databases-consistenthashing","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"Consistent Hashing","sub":"Data 05 · Hard · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Consistent Hashing","how to solve Consistent Hashing","Consistent Hashing approach","Consistent Hashing solution","Assign keys to servers so few move on change."],"w":"Databases Changing server set → ring + vnodes. Use it when Distributed caches, Cassandra, Dynamo, LBs.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Changing server set → ring + vnodes.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Hash servers and keys.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Ring:<\/b> Hash servers and keys.<\/li><li><b>Lookup:<\/b> Next clockwise.<\/li><li><b>vnodes:<\/b> Even load.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Distributed caches, Cassandra, Dynamo, LBs.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Without virtual nodes, load is uneven and one failure dumps on one neighbour.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class ConsistentHash<T> {\n    private final TreeMap<Long, T> ring = new TreeMap<>();\n    private final int vnodes;                                  // virtual nodes per server\n \n    ConsistentHash(int vnodes) { this.vnodes = vnodes; }\n \n    void add(T server) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < vnodes; i++) ring.put(hash(server + \"#\" + i), server);\n    }\n    void remove(T server) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < vnodes; i++) ring.remove(hash(server + \"#\" + i));\n    }\n    T get(String key) {\n        Map.Entry<Long, T> e = ring.ceilingEntry(hash(key));   // next server clockwise\n        return (e != null ? e : ring.firstEntry()).getValue(); // wrap around\n    }\n    private long hash(String s) { return murmur3_64(s); }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-consistenthashing"]],"ask":"Assign keys to servers so few move on change."},{"i":"p-sd-databases-secondsalary","k":"prob","s":"sd-databases","t":"Second Highest Salary","sub":"SQL classic · Easy · Databases","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Second Highest Salary","how to solve Second Highest Salary","Second Highest Salary approach","Second Highest Salary solution","Write the query; handle duplicates and no result."],"w":"Databases Nth highest → DENSE_RANK. Use it when Warm-up SQL round question.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Nth highest → DENSE_RANK.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> MAX below MAX.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Subquery:<\/b> MAX below MAX.<\/li><li><b>Window:<\/b> DENSE_RANK.<\/li><li><b>Edge:<\/b> NULL if one salary.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Warm-up SQL round question.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> ORDER BY … LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1 breaks with duplicate top salaries.<\/p>","c":{"java":"-- Second highest salary (handles duplicates and returns NULL if none)\nSELECT MAX(salary) AS second_highest\nFROM employees\nWHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);\n \n-- Nth highest with a window function\nSELECT salary FROM (\n  SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees\n) t WHERE rnk = 2 LIMIT 1;"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-databases.html#p-secondsalary"]],"ask":"Write the query; handle duplicates and no result."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-caching","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Caching Strategies","sub":"Data 06 · Medium · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Caching Strategies","how to solve Caching Strategies","Caching Strategies approach","Caching Strategies solution","Cache-aside, read/write-through, write-back; eviction; invalidation."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Read-heavy → cache-aside. Use it when Product pages, profiles, sessions, URL lookups.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Read-heavy → cache-aside.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Cache-aside + TTL.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Default:<\/b> Cache-aside + TTL.<\/li><li><b>Write:<\/b> DB then delete key.<\/li><li><b>Evict:<\/b> LRU.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Product pages, profiles, sessions, URL lookups.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Cache stampede on hot-key expiry; caching without a TTL.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class ProductService {\n    private final Cache<String, Product> cache;   // Redis\n    private final ProductRepo db;\n \n    Product get(long id) {\n        String key = \"product:\" + id;\n        Product p = cache.get(key);                       // 1. try cache\n        if (p != null) return p;                          //    hit: ~1 ms\n        p = db.findById(id).orElseThrow();                // 2. miss: DB, ~10 ms\n        cache.set(key, p, Duration.ofMinutes(10));        // 3. fill with TTL\n        return p;\n    }\n \n    void updatePrice(long id, long price) {\n        db.updatePrice(id, price);                        // 1. write the source of truth\n        cache.delete(\"product:\" + id);                    // 2. invalidate, don't update\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-caching"]],"ask":"Cache-aside, read/write-through, write-back; eviction; invalidation."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-cdn","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"CDN & Object Storage","sub":"Data 07 · Easy · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["CDN & Object Storage","how to solve CDN & Object Storage","CDN & Object Storage approach","CDN & Object Storage solution","Where big files live and how they reach users."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Bytes → S3 + CDN, metadata → DB. Use it when Media apps, static sites, downloads.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Bytes → S3 + CDN, metadata → DB.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> S3, key in DB.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Store:<\/b> S3, key in DB.<\/li><li><b>Upload:<\/b> Pre-signed URL.<\/li><li><b>Serve:<\/b> CDN edge.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Media apps, static sites, downloads.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Storing blobs in the database.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// 1. Client asks your API for an upload URL (small JSON request)\n@PostMapping(\"/uploads\")\nUploadTicket start(@RequestBody NewUpload req) {\n    String key = \"u/\" + currentUser() + \"/\" + UUID.randomUUID();\n    URL put = s3Presigner.presignPutObject(r -> r\n        .signatureDuration(Duration.ofMinutes(10))\n        .putObjectRequest(o -> o.bucket(\"media\").key(key).contentType(req.type()))).url();\n    media.saveMetadata(key, currentUser(), req.type());      // DB stores only metadata + key\n    return new UploadTicket(key, put);\n}\n// 2. Client PUTs the bytes straight to S3 (your servers never see them)\n// 3. Viewers load https://cdn.example.com/u/... : CDN edge caches, S3 is the origin"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-cdn"]],"ask":"Where big files live and how they reach users."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-kafka","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Message Queues & Kafka","sub":"Systems 01 · Medium · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Message Queues & Kafka","how to solve Message Queues & Kafka","Message Queues & Kafka approach","Message Queues & Kafka solution","Partitions, consumer groups, ordering, delivery semantics."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Order per entity → key by entity. Use it when Event-driven systems, notifications, analytics pipelines.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Order per entity → key by entity.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Same key → same partition.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Key:<\/b> Same key → same partition.<\/li><li><b>Group:<\/b> Partition → one consumer.<\/li><li><b>Delivery:<\/b> At-least-once + idempotent.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Event-driven systems, notifications, analytics pipelines.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> More consumers than partitions doesn’t add throughput.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Producer: key decides the partition -> per-key ordering\nproducer.send(new ProducerRecord<>(\"orders\", order.id(), order.toJson()));\n \n// Consumer in a group: partitions are split among members\nprops.put(\"group.id\", \"email-service\");\nprops.put(\"enable.auto.commit\", \"false\");\nKafkaConsumer<String, String> c = new KafkaConsumer<>(props);\nc.subscribe(List.of(\"orders\"));\nwhile (true) {\n    for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> r : c.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500))) {\n        if (processed.add(r.key() + \":\" + r.offset())) handle(r);   // idempotent\n    }\n    c.commitSync();                                                   // at-least-once\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-kafka"]],"ask":"Partitions, consumer groups, ordering, delivery semantics."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-saga","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Sagas","sub":"Systems 02 · Hard · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Sagas","how to solve Sagas","Sagas approach","Sagas solution","Distributed transactions without 2PC."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Cross-service txn → saga. Use it when Order → payment → inventory → shipping.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Cross-service txn → saga.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Each step commits.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Local:<\/b> Each step commits.<\/li><li><b>Compensate:<\/b> Undo in reverse.<\/li><li><b>Style:<\/b> Orchestrated vs choreographed.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Order → payment → inventory → shipping.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Compensations must be idempotent and can themselves fail; persist saga state.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class OrderSaga {                                   // orchestrated saga\n    void run(Order o) {\n        orders.create(o);                           // local txn 1\n        try {\n            payments.charge(o);                     // local txn 2\n            try {\n                inventory.reserve(o);               // local txn 3\n                shipping.schedule(o);               // local txn 4\n                orders.confirm(o);\n            } catch (Exception e) {\n                payments.refund(o);                 // compensate 2\n                throw e;\n            }\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            orders.cancel(o);                       // compensate 1\n        }\n    }\n}\n// Real systems persist saga state and drive steps from events, so a crash can resume."},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-saga"]],"ask":"Distributed transactions without 2PC."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-outbox","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Transactional Outbox","sub":"Systems 02 · Hard · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Transactional Outbox","how to solve Transactional Outbox","Transactional Outbox approach","Transactional Outbox solution","Reliably publish events with DB changes."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience DB + event must agree → outbox. Use it when Any service publishing domain events.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> DB + event must agree → outbox.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Row + event.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Same txn:<\/b> Row + event.<\/li><li><b>Relay:<\/b> Poll or CDC.<\/li><li><b>Consumers:<\/b> Dedupe.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Any service publishing domain events.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Publishing to Kafka inside the DB transaction doesn’t make it atomic.<\/p>","c":{"java":"@Transactional\nvoid placeOrder(Order o) {\n    orderRepo.save(o);                                          // business row\n    outboxRepo.save(new OutboxEvent(\"OrderPlaced\", o.id(), json(o)));  // same transaction\n}   // both rows commit together, or neither\n \n// Relay (poller or CDC like Debezium) publishes and marks sent\n@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 500)\nvoid relay() {\n    for (OutboxEvent e : outboxRepo.findUnsent(100)) {\n        kafka.send(\"orders\", e.aggregateId(), e.payload());\n        outboxRepo.markSent(e.id());                            // crash here -> resent -> consumers dedupe\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-outbox"]],"ask":"Reliably publish events with DB changes."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-circuitbreaker","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Circuit Breaker & Retries","sub":"Systems 03 · Medium · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Circuit Breaker & Retries","how to solve Circuit Breaker & Retries","Circuit Breaker & Retries approach","Circuit Breaker & Retries solution","Survive a failing dependency."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Repeated failures → open the circuit. Use it when Payments, third-party APIs, internal service calls.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Repeated failures → open the circuit.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Always.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Timeout:<\/b> Always.<\/li><li><b>Retry:<\/b> Backoff + jitter.<\/li><li><b>Breaker:<\/b> Closed/Open/Half-open.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Payments, third-party APIs, internal service calls.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Retrying non-idempotent calls; retries multiplying across layers.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class CircuitBreaker {\n    enum State { CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN }\n    private State state = State.CLOSED; private int failures; private Instant openedAt;\n    private final int threshold = 3; private final Duration cooldown = Duration.ofSeconds(10);\n \n    synchronized <T> T call(Supplier<T> remote, Supplier<T> fallback) {\n        if (state == State.OPEN) {\n            if (Instant.now().isBefore(openedAt.plus(cooldown))) return fallback.get();  // fail fast\n            state = State.HALF_OPEN;                                                    // try one\n        }\n        try {\n            T r = remote.get();\n            failures = 0; state = State.CLOSED;                                         // healthy again\n            return r;\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            if (++failures >= threshold || state == State.HALF_OPEN) { state = State.OPEN; openedAt = Instant.now(); }\n            return fallback.get();\n        }\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-circuitbreaker"]],"ask":"Survive a failing dependency."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-bloom","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Bloom Filter","sub":"Systems 04 · Medium · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Bloom Filter","how to solve Bloom Filter","Bloom Filter approach","Bloom Filter solution","Probabilistic set membership."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience “Definitely not” fast path. Use it when Usernames, crawlers, LSM-tree reads, malicious URL checks.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> “Definitely not” fast path.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Set k bits.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Add:<\/b> Set k bits.<\/li><li><b>Query:<\/b> Any 0 → no.<\/li><li><b>Trade:<\/b> Small, false positives.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Usernames, crawlers, LSM-tree reads, malicious URL checks.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Can’t delete from a plain Bloom filter.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class BloomFilter {\n    private final BitSet bits; private final int m, k;\n    BloomFilter(int m, int k) { this.bits = new BitSet(m); this.m = m; this.k = k; }\n \n    void add(String s) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) bits.set(index(s, i));\n    }\n    boolean mightContain(String s) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) if (!bits.get(index(s, i))) return false;   // definitely not\n        return true;                                                             // probably yes\n    }\n    private int index(String s, int i) {\n        int h1 = s.hashCode(), h2 = Integer.reverse(h1) | 1;\n        return Math.floorMod(h1 + i * h2, m);                // double hashing\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-bloom"]],"ask":"Probabilistic set membership."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-geohash","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Geohash & Proximity","sub":"Systems 04 · Medium · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Geohash & Proximity","how to solve Geohash & Proximity","Geohash & Proximity approach","Geohash & Proximity solution","Find things near a point fast."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Nearby → geohash / quadtree. Use it when Uber, Zomato, Airbnb.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Nearby → geohash / quadtree.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> lat/lng → prefix.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Encode:<\/b> lat/lng → prefix.<\/li><li><b>Search:<\/b> Cell + neighbours.<\/li><li><b>Filter:<\/b> Exact distance.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Uber, Zomato, Airbnb.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Points near a cell edge: always include neighbour cells.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Geohash: interleave longitude/latitude bits, base32-encode.\n// Shared prefix = nearby cell (precision 6 ~ 1.2 km x 0.6 km)\nString cell = GeoHash.encode(lat, lng, 6);           // e.g. \"tdr1y5\"\n \n// Nearby drivers: query the cell and its 8 neighbours\nSet<String> cells = GeoHash.neighbours(cell);  cells.add(cell);\nList<Driver> nearby = cells.stream()\n    .flatMap(c -> redis.smembers(\"drivers:\" + c).stream())\n    .filter(d -> distance(d, lat, lng) < 2_000)\n    .toList();\n// Redis has this built in: GEOADD drivers lng lat id ; GEOSEARCH drivers FROMLONLAT ... BYRADIUS 2 km"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-geohash"]],"ask":"Find things near a point fast."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-hyperloglog","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"HyperLogLog","sub":"Systems 04 · Medium · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["HyperLogLog","how to solve HyperLogLog","HyperLogLog approach","HyperLogLog solution","Count distinct with tiny memory."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Unique count at scale → HLL. Use it when Unique visitors, distinct searches.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Unique count at scale → HLL.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Leading zeros of hashes.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Idea:<\/b> Leading zeros of hashes.<\/li><li><b>Size:<\/b> ~12 KB.<\/li><li><b>Error:<\/b> ~1%.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Unique visitors, distinct searches.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Approximate only; not for billing.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Unique visitors per day in ~12 KB regardless of traffic\nredis.pfadd(\"uv:2026-10-15\", userId);          // HyperLogLog add\nlong approxUnique = redis.pfcount(\"uv:2026-10-15\");   // ~0.81% standard error\n \n// vs exact: a Set of 50M user IDs needs gigabytes"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-hyperloglog"]],"ask":"Count distinct with tiny memory."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-auth","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Auth: JWT & OAuth","sub":"Systems 06 · Medium · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Auth: JWT & OAuth","how to solve Auth: JWT & OAuth","Auth: JWT & OAuth approach","Auth: JWT & OAuth solution","Authenticate and authorise across services."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Stateless auth → JWT + refresh token. Use it when Every API.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Stateless auth → JWT + refresh token.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Who you are.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>AuthN:<\/b> Who you are.<\/li><li><b>AuthZ:<\/b> What you may do.<\/li><li><b>JWT:<\/b> Signed, short-lived.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Every API.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Putting secrets in a JWT payload (it’s only signed, not encrypted).<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Login: verify password, issue a short-lived signed token\nString token = Jwts.builder()\n    .subject(user.id()).claim(\"roles\", user.roles())\n    .expiration(Date.from(Instant.now().plus(15, ChronoUnit.MINUTES)))\n    .signWith(privateKey).compact();\n \n// Any service: verify signature + expiry locally, no DB call\nClaims c = Jwts.parser().verifyWith(publicKey).build()\n               .parseSignedClaims(token).getPayload();\nif (!c.get(\"roles\", List.class).contains(\"ADMIN\")) throw new Forbidden();   // authorisation"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-auth"]],"ask":"Authenticate and authorise across services."},{"i":"p-sd-distributed-payments","k":"prob","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Reliable Payments","sub":"Applied · Hard · Caching, Queues & Resilience","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Reliable Payments","how to solve Reliable Payments","Reliable Payments approach","Reliable Payments solution","Idempotency, timeouts, reconciliation."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Money + network → idempotent + reconcilable. Use it when Checkout, wallets, payouts.","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Money + network → idempotent + reconcilable.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Idempotency everywhere.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Key:<\/b> Idempotency everywhere.<\/li><li><b>Unknown:<\/b> Reconcile, don’t guess.<\/li><li><b>Events:<\/b> Outbox.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Use it:<\/b> when Checkout, wallets, payouts.<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Treating a timeout as a failure and charging again.<\/p>","c":{"java":"@PostMapping(\"/payments\")\nPayment pay(@RequestHeader(\"Idempotency-Key\") String key, @RequestBody PayRequest r) {\n    return idempotencyStore.getOrCompute(key, () -> {               // 1. retry-safe\n        Payment p = payments.createPending(r);                       // 2. local txn + outbox\n        PspResult res = breaker.call(() -> psp.charge(r, key),       // 3. timeout + breaker,\n                                     () -> PspResult.unknown());    //    PSP also gets the key\n        return res.isUnknown() ? p.markUnknown()                     // 4. reconcile later\n                               : p.complete(res);\n    });\n}\n// A reconciliation job polls the PSP for UNKNOWN payments; events flow via the outbox."},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-distributed.html#p-payments"]],"ask":"Idempotency, timeouts, reconciliation."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-urlshortener","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"URL Shortener","sub":"HLD 01 · Medium · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["URL Shortener","how to solve URL Shortener","URL Shortener approach","URL Shortener solution","Shorten, redirect, custom aliases, expiry."],"w":"HLD Problems Unique codes without collisions. Key numbers 40 writes/s · 4k reads/s · ~3 TB / 5 yrs","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Unique codes without collisions.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Counter blocks + Base62.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>IDs:<\/b> Counter blocks + Base62.<\/li><li><b>Read:<\/b> Redis cache, 302.<\/li><li><b>Store:<\/b> KV store.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> 40 writes/s · 4k reads/s · ~3 TB / 5 yrs<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Hash-and-truncate collisions; 301 killing analytics.<\/p>","c":{"java":"@RestController\nclass ShortenerController {\n    private final IdBlock ids;                     // leased range, e.g. [5_000_000, 5_001_000)\n    private final UrlRepo repo;                    // key-value store: code -> longUrl\n    private final Cache<String, String> cache;     // Redis\n \n    @PostMapping(\"/v1/urls\")\n    ResponseEntity<Map<String, String>> shorten(@RequestBody NewUrl req) {\n        String code = req.alias() != null ? req.alias() : Base62.encode(ids.next());\n        if (!repo.putIfAbsent(code, req.longUrl())) return ResponseEntity.status(409).build();\n        return ResponseEntity.status(201).body(Map.of(\"code\", code));\n    }\n \n    @GetMapping(\"/{code}\")\n    ResponseEntity<Void> go(@PathVariable String code) {\n        String url = cache.get(code, c -> repo.find(c).orElse(null));   // cache-aside\n        if (url == null) return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();\n        analytics.publishAsync(code);                                    // queue, off the hot path\n        return ResponseEntity.status(302).location(URI.create(url)).build();\n    }\n}\n \nclass IdBlock {   // when the block runs out: UPDATE counters SET next = next + 1000 RETURNING next\n    synchronized long next() { if (cur == end) lease(); return cur++; }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-urlshortener"]],"ask":"Shorten, redirect, custom aliases, expiry."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-ratelimiter","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"Rate Limiter","sub":"HLD 02 · Medium · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Rate Limiter","how to solve Rate Limiter","Rate Limiter approach","Rate Limiter solution","Limit requests per user/key across many servers."],"w":"HLD Problems Shared atomic counter per key. Key numbers ~100 B per key · O(1) per request","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Shared atomic counter per key.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Token bucket.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Algo:<\/b> Token bucket.<\/li><li><b>State:<\/b> Redis + Lua.<\/li><li><b>Where:<\/b> Gateway.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> ~100 B per key · O(1) per request<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Per-server counters let users multiply their limit by the server count.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class TokenBucket {\n    private final long capacity; private final double refillPerSec;\n    private double tokens; private long lastNanos = System.nanoTime();\n \n    TokenBucket(long capacity, double refillPerSec) {\n        this.capacity = capacity; this.refillPerSec = refillPerSec; this.tokens = capacity;\n    }\n \n    synchronized boolean tryAcquire() {\n        long now = System.nanoTime();\n        tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + (now - lastNanos) / 1e9 * refillPerSec);  // lazy refill\n        lastNanos = now;\n        if (tokens >= 1) { tokens -= 1; return true; }   // allowed\n        return false;                                    // 429 Too Many Requests\n    }\n}\n// Many servers: keep tokens + timestamp per user in Redis, update atomically with a Lua script."},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-ratelimiter"]],"ask":"Limit requests per user/key across many servers."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-notification","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"Notification System","sub":"HLD 03 · Medium · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Notification System","how to solve Notification System","Notification System approach","Notification System solution","Email, SMS, push at scale with preferences."],"w":"HLD Problems Accept fast, deliver async. Key numbers 10M+/day · bursts 10× during campaigns","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Accept fast, deliver async.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> 202 + idempotency.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Ingest:<\/b> 202 + idempotency.<\/li><li><b>Route:<\/b> Topic per channel.<\/li><li><b>Deliver:<\/b> Retries, DLQ, rate limits.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> 10M+/day · bursts 10× during campaigns<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Sending synchronously in the API; no dedupe → double notifications.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Notification service: accept fast, deliver asynchronously per channel\n@PostMapping(\"/v1/notifications\")\nResponseEntity<Void> send(@RequestBody Notify n, @RequestHeader(\"Idempotency-Key\") String key) {\n    if (!dedupe.firstTime(key)) return ResponseEntity.accepted().build();\n    Prefs p = prefs.of(n.userId());                       // opted-in channels, quiet hours\n    for (Channel c : p.channels(n.type()))\n        kafka.send(\"notify.\" + c, n.userId(), render(n, c));   // one topic per channel\n    return ResponseEntity.accepted().build();             // 202: queued, not delivered\n}\n \n// Channel workers: rate limit per provider, retry with backoff, dead-letter after N tries\nclass PushWorker { void handle(Msg m) { limiter.acquire(); fcm.send(m); status.mark(m, SENT); } }"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-notification"]],"ask":"Email, SMS, push at scale with preferences."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-autocomplete","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"Autocomplete","sub":"HLD 04 · Medium · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Autocomplete","how to solve Autocomplete","Autocomplete approach","Autocomplete solution","Top suggestions as the user types."],"w":"HLD Problems Precompute; never rank per keystroke. Key numbers ~50 ms per keystroke · 100k+ QPS","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Precompute; never rank per keystroke.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Aggregate logs → top-K.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Offline:<\/b> Aggregate logs → top-K.<\/li><li><b>Online:<\/b> Prefix → list in memory.<\/li><li><b>Client:<\/b> Debounce + cache.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> ~50 ms per keystroke · 100k+ QPS<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Walking a huge trie and sorting at request time.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Offline: aggregate query logs hourly -> top 10 completions per prefix\n// Online: prefix -> precomputed list, served from memory/Redis\nclass Typeahead {\n    private final Map<String, List<String>> topK;   // built by a batch job from a trie\n \n    List<String> suggest(String prefix) {\n        String p = prefix.toLowerCase().strip();\n        if (p.length() < 2) return List.of();\n        return topK.getOrDefault(p, List.of());     // O(1), no trie walk at request time\n    }\n}\n// Redis alternative: ZADD \"ac:<prefix>\" score term ... ; ZREVRANGE \"ac:ip\" 0 9\n// Client: debounce keystrokes (~150 ms), cache results per prefix in the browser"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-autocomplete"]],"ask":"Top suggestions as the user types."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-whatsapp","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"WhatsApp","sub":"HLD 05 · Hard · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["WhatsApp","how to solve WhatsApp","WhatsApp approach","WhatsApp solution","1:1 and group chat, delivery ticks, offline delivery."],"w":"HLD Problems Find the recipient’s socket. Key numbers 100B msgs/day · ~1M sockets per ~10 servers","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Find the recipient’s socket.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> WebSocket servers.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Conn:<\/b> WebSocket servers.<\/li><li><b>Route:<\/b> Presence + pub/sub.<\/li><li><b>Store:<\/b> Cassandra by chat.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> 100B msgs/day · ~1M sockets per ~10 servers<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Assuming sender and receiver share a server; forgetting offline sync.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// 1:1 message flow (WhatsApp-style)\nvoid onMessage(WebSocketSession from, ChatMsg m) {\n    m = m.withId(snowflake.next()).withStatus(SENT);\n    messages.save(m);                                    // Cassandra: (chat_id) partition, time-ordered\n    ack(from, m.id(), SENT);                             // single tick\n \n    String server = presence.serverOf(m.to());           // Redis: user -> connection server\n    if (server != null) pubsub.publish(\"conn:\" + server, m);   // that server pushes over the socket\n    else push.notifyOffline(m.to(), m);                  // APNs/FCM; synced on reconnect\n}\nvoid onDelivered(long msgId, String user) { status.update(msgId, DELIVERED); notifySender(msgId); }   // double tick"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-whatsapp"]],"ask":"1:1 and group chat, delivery ticks, offline delivery."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-twitterfeed","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"Twitter Feed","sub":"HLD 06 · Hard · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Twitter Feed","how to solve Twitter Feed","Twitter Feed approach","Twitter Feed solution","Post, follow, home timeline."],"w":"HLD Problems Hybrid fan-out. Key numbers Reads ≫ writes · timeline = list of post IDs","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Hybrid fan-out.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Fan-out to timelines.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Write:<\/b> Fan-out to timelines.<\/li><li><b>Celebs:<\/b> Pull at read.<\/li><li><b>Read:<\/b> Merge, hydrate, cursor.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> Reads ≫ writes · timeline = list of post IDs<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Pure fan-out on write breaks on celebrities.<\/p>","c":{"java":"class FeedService {\n    static final int CELEBRITY = 100_000;\n \n    void onPost(Post p) {                                     // fan-out on WRITE\n        if (followers.count(p.author()) >= CELEBRITY) return; // celebrities: skip\n        for (long f : followers.of(p.author()))\n            timelines.push(\"tl:\" + f, p.id(), 800);           // Redis list, keep last 800\n    }\n \n    List<Post> home(long user) {                              // fan-out on READ for celebrities\n        List<Long> ids = timelines.range(\"tl:\" + user, 0, 200);\n        for (long celeb : following.celebritiesOf(user))\n            ids.addAll(posts.recentIds(celeb, 20));\n        return posts.hydrate(mergeByTime(ids)).subList(0, 50);\n    }\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-twitterfeed"]],"ask":"Post, follow, home timeline."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-uber","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"Uber","sub":"HLD 07 · Hard · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Uber","how to solve Uber","Uber approach","Uber solution","Match riders to nearby drivers; live tracking."],"w":"HLD Problems Moving points → in-memory geo index. Key numbers ~1M drivers × 1 update/4 s ≈ 250k writes/s","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Moving points → in-memory geo index.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Every 4 s → geo index.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Location:<\/b> Every 4 s → geo index.<\/li><li><b>Match:<\/b> Nearest + lock driver.<\/li><li><b>Trip:<\/b> State machine.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> ~1M drivers × 1 update/4 s ≈ 250k writes/s<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Writing every location update to the main DB.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Drivers send location every 4 s -> in-memory geo index\nvoid onLocation(String driverId, double lat, double lng) {\n    redis.geoadd(\"drivers:available\", lng, lat, driverId);       // geohash under the hood\n}\n \n// Rider requests: nearest available drivers, offer one at a time\nOptional<String> match(Ride r) {\n    List<String> near = redis.geosearch(\"drivers:available\",\n            r.lng(), r.lat(), 3, KM, 10, ASC);                     // 10 nearest within 3 km\n    for (String d : near) {\n        if (locks.tryLock(\"driver:\" + d, Duration.ofSeconds(15))) {   // one ride offer at a time\n            if (offer(d, r)) return Optional.of(d);\n            locks.unlock(\"driver:\" + d);\n        }\n    }\n    return Optional.empty();\n}"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-uber"]],"ask":"Match riders to nearby drivers; live tracking."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-zomato","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"Zomato / Food Delivery","sub":"HLD 08 · Hard · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Zomato / Food Delivery","how to solve Zomato / Food Delivery","Zomato / Food Delivery approach","Zomato / Food Delivery solution","Search restaurants, order, assign rider, track."],"w":"HLD Problems Order lifecycle = State + events. Key numbers Lunch/dinner peaks 5–10× average","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Order lifecycle = State + events.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Elasticsearch + geo.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Search:<\/b> Elasticsearch + geo.<\/li><li><b>Order:<\/b> State machine + saga.<\/li><li><b>Track:<\/b> WebSocket/SSE.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> Lunch/dinner peaks 5–10× average<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Ignoring partial failures between payment, restaurant and rider.<\/p>","c":{"java":"enum OrderStatus { PLACED, ACCEPTED, PREPARING, PICKED_UP, DELIVERED, CANCELLED }\n \nclass OrderService {\n    Order place(Cart c, String idemKey) {\n        return idem.once(idemKey, () -> {\n            Order o = orders.create(c);                       // + outbox event\n            payments.authorize(o);                            // hold, capture on delivery\n            return o;\n        });\n    }\n    void transition(long id, OrderStatus to) {\n        orders.updateStatusIf(id, allowedFrom(to), to);       // UPDATE ... WHERE status IN (...)\n        events.publish(new StatusChanged(id, to));            // rider app, customer app, ETA service\n    }\n}\n// Search: Elasticsearch with geo_distance filter on restaurant location + \"open now\"\n// Rider assignment: same geo matching as Uber; live tracking over WebSocket/SSE"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-zomato"]],"ask":"Search restaurants, order, assign rider, track."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-youtube","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"YouTube","sub":"HLD 09 · Hard · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["YouTube","how to solve YouTube","YouTube approach","YouTube solution","Upload, process and stream video."],"w":"HLD Problems Video delivery is a CDN problem. Key numbers 500 h uploaded/min · egress dominates cost","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Video delivery is a CDN problem.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> Resumable to S3.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Upload:<\/b> Resumable to S3.<\/li><li><b>Process:<\/b> Transcode jobs.<\/li><li><b>Serve:<\/b> HLS on CDN, ABR.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> 500 h uploaded/min · egress dominates cost<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Streaming video through application servers.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Upload -> transcode -> serve via CDN\nUploadTicket startUpload(VideoMeta m) {\n    Video v = videos.create(m, Status.UPLOADING);\n    return new UploadTicket(v.id(), s3.presignMultipartUpload(\"raw/\" + v.id()));   // resumable chunks\n}\nvoid onRawUploaded(String videoId) {\n    for (Rendition r : List.of(R_240P, R_480P, R_720P, R_1080P))\n        queue.send(\"transcode\", new Job(videoId, r));      // parallel workers (ffmpeg)\n}\nvoid onAllRenditionsDone(String videoId) {\n    manifests.writeHls(videoId);                          // .m3u8 + 4-second segments per bitrate\n    videos.setStatus(videoId, Status.READY);\n}\n// Playback: player fetches the manifest from the CDN and switches bitrate per segment (ABR)"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-youtube"]],"ask":"Upload, process and stream video."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-airbnb","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"Airbnb","sub":"HLD 10 · Hard · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Airbnb","how to solve Airbnb","Airbnb approach","Airbnb solution","Search stays and book date ranges."],"w":"HLD Problems No double booking: one conditional update. Key numbers Search ≫ bookings · strong consistency only for booking","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> No double booking: one conditional update.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> ES geo + filters.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Search:<\/b> ES geo + filters.<\/li><li><b>Avail:<\/b> Row per night.<\/li><li><b>Book:<\/b> Atomic hold all nights.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> Search ≫ bookings · strong consistency only for booking<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Checking availability in one query and booking in another.<\/p>","c":{"java":"-- Availability per listing per night\nCREATE TABLE availability (\n  listing_id BIGINT, night DATE, status TEXT DEFAULT 'FREE', booking_id BIGINT,\n  PRIMARY KEY (listing_id, night)\n);\n \n-- Book 3 nights atomically: succeed only if all 3 are free\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE availability SET status = 'HELD', booking_id = :b\n WHERE listing_id = :l AND night >= :checkin AND night < :checkout AND status = 'FREE';\n-- rows affected must equal number of nights, else ROLLBACK\nCOMMIT;\n \n-- Search: Elasticsearch (geo + filters + price) -> candidate ids -> check availability"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-airbnb"]],"ask":"Search stays and book date ranges."},{"i":"p-sd-hld-dropbox","k":"prob","s":"sd-hld","t":"Dropbox","sub":"HLD 11 · Hard · HLD Problems","cat":"System design problems","n":null,"q":["Dropbox","how to solve Dropbox","Dropbox approach","Dropbox solution","Store and sync files across devices."],"w":"HLD Problems Move only changed chunks. Key numbers Metadata DB small; blobs in S3; most uploads dedupe","h":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> Move only changed chunks.<\/p><p><b>First move:<\/b> 4 MB, by hash.<\/p>","a":"<p><b>Think it through<\/b><\/p><ul><li><b>Chunk:<\/b> 4 MB, by hash.<\/li><li><b>Dedupe:<\/b> Upload missing only.<\/li><li><b>Sync:<\/b> Versions + notify.<\/li><\/ul><p><b>Key numbers:<\/b> Metadata DB small; blobs in S3; most uploads dedupe<\/p><p><b>⚠ Interview trap:<\/b> Re-uploading whole files; ignoring edit conflicts.<\/p>","c":{"java":"// Client splits files into 4 MB chunks, identified by content hash\nList<String> chunkHashes(Path file) {\n    List<String> hashes = new ArrayList<>();\n    try (InputStream in = Files.newInputStream(file)) {\n        byte[] buf = new byte[4 * 1024 * 1024]; int n;\n        while ((n = in.readNBytes(buf, 0, buf.length)) > 0) hashes.add(sha256(buf, n));\n    }\n    return hashes;\n}\n \nvoid sync(Path file) {\n    List<String> hashes = chunkHashes(file);\n    Set<String> missing = api.missingChunks(hashes);           // server already has most: dedupe\n    for (String h : missing) s3.put(\"chunks/\" + h, readChunk(file, h));   // upload only changes\n    api.commitFileVersion(file.toString(), hashes, baseVersion);         // conflict if base is stale\n}\n// Other devices: long-poll / WebSocket \"changes since cursor\" -> download missing chunks"},"L":[["Open in the app","sd-hld.html#p-dropbox"]],"ask":"Store and sync files across devices."},{"i":"rx-hashing-seen","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"“Contains duplicate”, “have we seen this before?”, “first repeated”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Contains duplicate”, “have we seen this before?”, “first repeated”","when I see “Contains duplicate”, “have we seen this before?”, “first repeated”","what to do when “Contains duplicate”, “have we seen this before?”, “first repeated”","which pattern for “Contains duplicate”, “have we seen this before?”, “first repeated”"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Contains duplicate”, “have we seen this before?”, “first repeated”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>A set. Check, then add. One pass.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If the question is really “has this value shown up before?”, keep a set of everything you’ve already looked at. For each new item, check the set first: if it’s there, you found a repeat. If not, add it and move on. You only walk the array once.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-seen"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-complement","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Two numbers that combine to a target (sum, difference)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Two numbers that combine to a target (sum, difference)","when I see Two numbers that combine to a target (sum, difference)","what to do when Two numbers that combine to a target (sum, difference)","which pattern for Two numbers that combine to a target (sum, difference)"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Two numbers that combine to a target (sum, difference)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dict value → index. For each x, look up <code>target − x<\/code> <b>before<\/b> storing x.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When two numbers must add up to a target, each number already tells you its partner: partner = target − x. Keep a dictionary of the numbers you’ve passed (value → index). At each x, first ask “is my partner in the dictionary?”, and only then store x.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-complement"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-group","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Group things that are “the same” after rearranging (anagrams, shifted strings)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Group things that are “the same” after rearranging (anagrams, shifted strings)","when I see Group things that are “the same” after rearranging (anagrams, shifted strings)","what to do when Group things that are “the same” after rearranging (anagrams, shifted strings)","which pattern for Group things that are “the same” after rearranging (anagrams, shifted strings)"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Group things that are “the same” after rearranging (anagrams, shifted strings)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dict keyed by a canonical form: sorted string, or a tuple of 26 counts.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Words that are “the same after rearranging” should land in the same bucket. Give every word a label that ignores the order of its letters, for example the word with its letters sorted. Use that label as a dictionary key and append the word to its list.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-group"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-count","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Frequencies: “most common”, “top k frequent”, “is it an anagram”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Frequencies: “most common”, “top k frequent”, “is it an anagram”","when I see Frequencies: “most common”, “top k frequent”, “is it an anagram”","what to do when Frequencies: “most common”, “top k frequent”, “is it an anagram”","which pattern for Frequencies: “most common”, “top k frequent”, “is it an anagram”"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Frequencies: “most common”, “top k frequent”, “is it an anagram”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>Counter<\/code>. For top k without sorting, bucket by frequency (index = count).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Any question about “how often” starts with counting: one dictionary of value → count. To get the k most frequent without sorting, make buckets where the position is the count (bucket 3 holds everything seen 3 times), then read the buckets from the highest count down.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-count"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-consec","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Longest consecutive run in an unsorted array, in O(n)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Longest consecutive run in an unsorted array, in O(n)","when I see Longest consecutive run in an unsorted array, in O(n)","what to do when Longest consecutive run in an unsorted array, in O(n)","which pattern for Longest consecutive run in an unsorted array, in O(n)"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Longest consecutive run in an unsorted array, in O(n)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Put everything in a set. Only start counting at x where x − 1 is missing.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sorting would find runs but costs O(n log n). Instead put every number in a set. A run can only start at a number whose left neighbour (x − 1) is missing; from there count up x + 1, x + 2, … while they exist. Every number is counted once, so it’s O(n).<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-consec"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-range","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Many “sum of range” queries on a fixed array","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Many “sum of range” queries on a fixed array","when I see Many “sum of range” queries on a fixed array","what to do when Many “sum of range” queries on a fixed array","which pattern for Many “sum of range” queries on a fixed array"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Many “sum of range” queries on a fixed array<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Prefix array with a leading 0: <code>sum(l..r) = pre[r+1] − pre[l]<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If you’ll ask “what’s the sum from l to r?” many times, add up once in advance. pre[i] is the sum of the first i numbers, starting with pre[0] = 0. Then any range is a subtraction: everything up to r, minus everything before l.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-range"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-subk","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Count subarrays whose sum is k (negatives allowed)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Count subarrays whose sum is k (negatives allowed)","when I see Count subarrays whose sum is k (negatives allowed)","what to do when Count subarrays whose sum is k (negatives allowed)","which pattern for Count subarrays whose sum is k (negatives allowed)"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Count subarrays whose sum is k (negatives allowed)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Running sum + dict of prefix counts seeded <code>{0: 1}<\/code>. Add <code>seen[running − k]<\/code> each step.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Keep a running total as you walk. A subarray ending here sums to k exactly when some earlier running total equals running − k. So keep a dictionary of how many times each running total has appeared (start with {0: 1} for the empty prefix) and add that count at each step.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-subk"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-leftright","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Each answer depends on everything to its left and right (product except self, trapping water)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Each answer depends on everything to its left and right (product except self, trapping water)","when I see Each answer depends on everything to its left and right (product except self, trapping water)","what to do when Each answer depends on everything to its left and right (product except self, trapping water)","which pattern for Each answer depends on everything to its left and right (product except self, trapping water)"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Each answer depends on everything to its left and right (product except self, trapping water)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Two passes: left-to-right with a running value, then right-to-left.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When each answer needs “everything on my left” and “everything on my right”, don’t look both ways from every position (that’s O(n²)). Sweep once left → right keeping a running value, sweep once right → left keeping another, and combine.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-leftright"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-indexhash","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"O(1) extra space and values in the range 1…n","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["O(1) extra space and values in the range 1…n","when I see O(1) extra space and values in the range 1…n","what to do when O(1) extra space and values in the range 1…n","which pattern for O(1) extra space and values in the range 1…n"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>O(1) extra space and values in the range 1…n<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Use the array as its own hash: mark value v by flipping the sign at index v − 1.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With values from 1 to n and no extra memory allowed, the array itself can be your “seen” set: position v − 1 stands for value v. To mark v as seen, make the number at index v − 1 negative. A position still positive at the end means its value never appeared.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-indexhash"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-rotate","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Rotate a square matrix 90° clockwise in place","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Rotate a square matrix 90° clockwise in place","when I see Rotate a square matrix 90° clockwise in place","what to do when Rotate a square matrix 90° clockwise in place","which pattern for Rotate a square matrix 90° clockwise in place"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Rotate a square matrix 90° clockwise in place<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Transpose, then reverse each row.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A 90° clockwise turn is two easy moves. Transpose (swap across the diagonal, so rows become columns), then reverse each row. Both happen in place.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-rotate"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-spiral","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Walk a matrix in a spiral","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Walk a matrix in a spiral","when I see Walk a matrix in a spiral","what to do when Walk a matrix in a spiral","which pattern for Walk a matrix in a spiral"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Walk a matrix in a spiral<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Four walls: top, bottom, left, right. Walk one edge, move that wall in, repeat while they haven’t crossed.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Picture four walls closing in. Walk the top row left → right then move the top wall down; walk the right column down then move the right wall left; then the bottom row right → left, then the left column up. Repeat while top ≤ bottom and left ≤ right.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-spiral"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-markers","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Mark rows / columns of a matrix with O(1) extra space","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Mark rows / columns of a matrix with O(1) extra space","when I see Mark rows / columns of a matrix with O(1) extra space","what to do when Mark rows / columns of a matrix with O(1) extra space","which pattern for Mark rows / columns of a matrix with O(1) extra space"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Mark rows / columns of a matrix with O(1) extra space<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Store the flags in row 0 and column 0. Keep two booleans for whether they had zeros themselves.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To remember which rows and columns contain a zero without another matrix, write the notes into the matrix itself: the first row stores “this column has a zero”, the first column stores “this row has a zero”. Two extra booleans remember whether the first row and column needed zeroing themselves.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-markers"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-cxhinner","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"An inner loop that searches the input for a partner or a duplicate","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["An inner loop that searches the input for a partner or a duplicate","when I see An inner loop that searches the input for a partner or a duplicate","what to do when An inner loop that searches the input for a partner or a duplicate","which pattern for An inner loop that searches the input for a partner or a duplicate"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>An inner loop that searches the input for a partner or a duplicate<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Replace it with a set/dict lookup: O(n²) → O(n) time, at the price of O(n) space.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If your code has a loop inside a loop just to find a matching item, the inner loop is a search. A set or dictionary answers “is it here?” in O(1) on average, so the inner loop disappears. You pay with memory for the stored items.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-cxhinner"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-cxhspace","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"“What’s the space complexity?” for a hashing solution","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“What’s the space complexity?” for a hashing solution","when I see “What’s the space complexity?” for a hashing solution","what to do when “What’s the space complexity?” for a hashing solution","which pattern for “What’s the space complexity?” for a hashing solution"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“What’s the space complexity?” for a hashing solution<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Count what the dict can hold: up to n keys → O(n). A fixed 26-letter count array → O(1).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Space is “how big can my dictionary get?”. Storing each input value can grow to n keys → O(n). Counting letters of the alphabet never exceeds 26 keys, so it’s O(1), no matter how long the input is.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-cxhspace"]]},{"i":"rx-hashing-cxhkey","k":"rx","s":"hashing","t":"Building a key for each item (sorted string, tuple, serialised path)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Building a key for each item (sorted string, tuple, serialised path)","when I see Building a key for each item (sorted string, tuple, serialised path)","what to do when Building a key for each item (sorted string, tuple, serialised path)","which pattern for Building a key for each item (sorted string, tuple, serialised path)"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Building a key for each item (sorted string, tuple, serialised path)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Multiply: n items × cost of one key. Sorted key O(n·k log k), count key O(n·k).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When you build a key per item, the cost is (number of items) × (cost of one key). For n words of length k: sorting each word costs k log k, counting its letters costs k.<\/p><p>From <b>Arrays & Hashing<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Arrays & Hashing","hashing.html#rx-cxhkey"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-pairsum","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"Sorted array + find a pair with a target sum","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Sorted array + find a pair with a target sum","when I see Sorted array + find a pair with a target sum","what to do when Sorted array + find a pair with a target sum","which pattern for Sorted array + find a pair with a target sum"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Sorted array + find a pair with a target sum<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>l<\/code> at the start, <code>r<\/code> at the end. Too small → l += 1. Too big → r −= 1.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> In a sorted array, put one finger at each end. If the pair is too small, only moving the left finger right can make it bigger; if too big, move the right finger left. Every step throws away one number for good, so it’s one pass.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-pairsum"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-container","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"Max area / water between two lines chosen from an array","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Max area / water between two lines chosen from an array","when I see Max area / water between two lines chosen from an array","what to do when Max area / water between two lines chosen from an array","which pattern for Max area / water between two lines chosen from an array"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Max area / water between two lines chosen from an array<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Start widest. Always move the shorter wall inward.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Area = width × the shorter wall. Start as wide as possible. Moving the taller wall can never help (the short wall still limits you and the width shrinks), so always move the shorter one inward and keep the best area seen.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-container"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-threesum","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"3Sum, 4Sum, “triplets that sum to …”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["3Sum, 4Sum, “triplets that sum to …”","when I see 3Sum, 4Sum, “triplets that sum to …”","what to do when 3Sum, 4Sum, “triplets that sum to …”","which pattern for 3Sum, 4Sum, “triplets that sum to …”"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>3Sum, 4Sum, “triplets that sum to …”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sort. Fix the first number(s) in a loop, two-pointer the rest. Skip equal neighbours to kill duplicates.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sort first. Then pick the first number with a normal loop and find the other two with the pair-sum fingers on the part to its right. Skipping a number equal to the one before stops the same triplet appearing twice.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-threesum"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-palin","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"Palindrome check (maybe ignoring symbols, maybe one deletion allowed)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Palindrome check (maybe ignoring symbols, maybe one deletion allowed)","when I see Palindrome check (maybe ignoring symbols, maybe one deletion allowed)","what to do when Palindrome check (maybe ignoring symbols, maybe one deletion allowed)","which pattern for Palindrome check (maybe ignoring symbols, maybe one deletion allowed)"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Palindrome check (maybe ignoring symbols, maybe one deletion allowed)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Pointers at both ends, move inward comparing. On a mismatch with one deletion allowed, try skipping either side once.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A palindrome reads the same from both ends, so compare the outermost pair and walk inward. If one deletion is allowed and you hit a mismatch, try deleting the left character or the right one; if either remainder is a palindrome, answer yes.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-palin"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-trap","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"Trapping rain water","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Trapping rain water","when I see Trapping rain water","what to do when Trapping rain water","which pattern for Trapping rain water"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Trapping rain water<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Two pointers with <code>lmax<\/code> and <code>rmax<\/code>. Process whichever side has the lower bar.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Water above a bar = (the lower of the tallest wall on its left and on its right) − its height. With a pointer at each end, the side whose wall is lower is the limit, so you can settle that side right away and step inward.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-trap"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-slowfast","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"“In place”: remove, dedupe, compact, move zeroes","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“In place”: remove, dedupe, compact, move zeroes","when I see “In place”: remove, dedupe, compact, move zeroes","what to do when “In place”: remove, dedupe, compact, move zeroes","which pattern for “In place”: remove, dedupe, compact, move zeroes"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“In place”: remove, dedupe, compact, move zeroes<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Writer <code>w<\/code> and reader <code>r<\/code>. Copy to w only when the reader finds something worth keeping.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To change an array in place, use two indexes: a reader r that looks at every item, and a writer w that marks where the next kept item goes. When the reader finds something worth keeping, copy it to w and move w on. Everything before w is the answer.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-slowfast"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-dutch","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"Partition into three groups (0/1/2, low/mid/high) in one pass","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Partition into three groups (0/1/2, low/mid/high) in one pass","when I see Partition into three groups (0/1/2, low/mid/high) in one pass","what to do when Partition into three groups (0/1/2, low/mid/high) in one pass","which pattern for Partition into three groups (0/1/2, low/mid/high) in one pass"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Partition into three groups (0/1/2, low/mid/high) in one pass<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>lo<\/code>, <code>i<\/code>, <code>hi<\/code>. 0 → swap to lo, advance both. 2 → swap to hi, shrink hi, <b>don’t<\/b> advance i. 1 → advance i.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Keep three zones: everything before lo is 0, everything after hi is 2, and i scans the middle. A 0 is swapped to lo (both move on). A 2 is swapped to hi (hi shrinks, but i stays because the swapped-in value hasn’t been checked). A 1 just lets i move on.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-dutch"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-mergeback","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"Merge into an array that has empty space at the end","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Merge into an array that has empty space at the end","when I see Merge into an array that has empty space at the end","what to do when Merge into an array that has empty space at the end","which pattern for Merge into an array that has empty space at the end"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Merge into an array that has empty space at the end<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Fill from the back: compare the largest remaining of each, write at the end.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If the first array has empty space at its end, merge from the back. Compare the largest remaining number of each array and write the bigger one into the last free slot. Nothing you still need ever gets overwritten.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-mergeback"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-subseq","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"Is s a subsequence of t? Compare two sequences in order","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Is s a subsequence of t? Compare two sequences in order","when I see Is s a subsequence of t? Compare two sequences in order","what to do when Is s a subsequence of t? Compare two sequences in order","which pattern for Is s a subsequence of t? Compare two sequences in order"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Is s a subsequence of t? Compare two sequences in order<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>One pointer per sequence. Advance the s pointer only on a match; always advance t.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To check that s appears inside t in order (gaps allowed), walk t once with one pointer and keep a second pointer at the next letter of s you still need. Advance it only when they match. If you use up all of s, the answer is yes.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-subseq"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-cycle","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"Cycle in a linked list or in repeated function application (happy number, duplicate number 287)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Cycle in a linked list or in repeated function application (happy number, duplicate number 287)","when I see Cycle in a linked list or in repeated function application (happy number, duplicate number 287)","what to do when Cycle in a linked list or in repeated function application (happy number, duplicate number 287)","which pattern for Cycle in a linked list or in repeated function application (happy number, duplicate number 287)"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Cycle in a linked list or in repeated function application (happy number, duplicate number 287)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Fast and slow pointers. Fast moves 2, slow moves 1. If they meet, there’s a cycle.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Send two runners around: slow takes 1 step, fast takes 2. Without a loop, fast falls off the end. With a loop, fast eventually laps slow and they land on the same node, like runners on a circular track.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-cycle"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-cxtpmoves","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"A while loop with two pointers moving towards each other (or both forward)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A while loop with two pointers moving towards each other (or both forward)","when I see A while loop with two pointers moving towards each other (or both forward)","what to do when A while loop with two pointers moving towards each other (or both forward)","which pattern for A while loop with two pointers moving towards each other (or both forward)"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A while loop with two pointers moving towards each other (or both forward)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Count total moves: each pointer travels at most n → O(n), not O(n²).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Two pointers inside one while loop look like nested work, but count moves instead: each pointer only moves forward (or inward) and never comes back, so together they make at most about 2n moves. That’s O(n).<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-cxtpmoves"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-cxtpsort","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"“Sort the array first, then two pointers”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Sort the array first, then two pointers”","when I see “Sort the array first, then two pointers”","what to do when “Sort the array first, then two pointers”","which pattern for “Sort the array first, then two pointers”"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Sort the array first, then two pointers”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Say O(n log n) for the sort, O(n) for the scan → O(n log n) overall; space O(1) or O(n) depending on the sort.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When you sort first, the total is the sort plus the scan. Sorting is O(n log n) and the scan is O(n); the bigger term wins, so say O(n log n). Mention space too: O(1) if you sort in place.<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-cxtpsort"]]},{"i":"rx-two-pointers-cxtp3","k":"rx","s":"two-pointers","t":"k-Sum with two pointers inside k − 2 loops","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["k-Sum with two pointers inside k − 2 loops","when I see k-Sum with two pointers inside k − 2 loops","what to do when k-Sum with two pointers inside k − 2 loops","which pattern for k-Sum with two pointers inside k − 2 loops"],"w":"Two Pointers","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>k-Sum with two pointers inside k − 2 loops<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(n^(k−1)): 3Sum O(n²), 4Sum O(n³).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> k-Sum = (k − 2) ordinary loops around one two-pointer scan. Each loop multiplies by n, and the scan is n, so it’s n to the power (k − 1).<\/p><p>From <b>Two Pointers<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Two Pointers","two-pointers.html#rx-cxtp3"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-longest","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Longest substring / subarray such that …” (contiguous)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Longest substring / subarray such that …” (contiguous)","when I see “Longest substring / subarray such that …” (contiguous)","what to do when “Longest substring / subarray such that …” (contiguous)","which pattern for “Longest substring / subarray such that …” (contiguous)"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Longest substring / subarray such that …” (contiguous)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Expand r every step. <b>While<\/b> the window is invalid, shrink l. Record after shrinking.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A window is a stretch of neighbours from l to r. Grow it by moving r one step every time. If the rule breaks, move l right until it holds again, and only then record the length. The longest valid window you ever saw is the answer.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-longest"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-shortest","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Shortest / minimum length subarray such that …”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Shortest / minimum length subarray such that …”","when I see “Shortest / minimum length subarray such that …”","what to do when “Shortest / minimum length subarray such that …”","which pattern for “Shortest / minimum length subarray such that …”"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Shortest / minimum length subarray such that …”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Expand r. <b>While<\/b> the window is valid, record it, then shrink l.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For the shortest window, flip the order: grow r until the window is valid, then shrink from the left as long as it stays valid, recording the length every time. You stop shrinking the moment it breaks.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-shortest"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-counts","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"The window rule depends on letter counts (covers t, at most k distinct, anagram)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["The window rule depends on letter counts (covers t, at most k distinct, anagram)","when I see The window rule depends on letter counts (covers t, at most k distinct, anagram)","what to do when The window rule depends on letter counts (covers t, at most k distinct, anagram)","which pattern for The window rule depends on letter counts (covers t, at most k distinct, anagram)"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>The window rule depends on letter counts (covers t, at most k distinct, anagram)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Keep a count dict for the window. Track one number (missing, distinct, formed) so validity is O(1).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When “valid” depends on which letters are inside, keep a count dictionary for the window. Also keep one summary number (for example, how many required letters are still missing) so you can tell valid from invalid without rescanning.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-counts"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-replace","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Replace at most k characters to make them all the same”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Replace at most k characters to make them all the same”","when I see “Replace at most k characters to make them all the same”","what to do when “Replace at most k characters to make them all the same”","which pattern for “Replace at most k characters to make them all the same”"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Replace at most k characters to make them all the same”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Valid when <code>window length − max frequency ≤ k<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To make a window all one letter with at most k changes, keep its most common letter and change the rest. The number of changes is length − (count of the most common letter). The window is fine while that is ≤ k.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-replace"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-atmostk","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"“At most K distinct”, “at most K zeros”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“At most K distinct”, “at most K zeros”","when I see “At most K distinct”, “at most K zeros”","what to do when “At most K distinct”, “at most K zeros”","which pattern for “At most K distinct”, “at most K zeros”"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“At most K distinct”, “at most K zeros”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Window with a count dict; shrink while the rule exceeds K.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> “At most K” is a growing window with a count dictionary. Add the new item on the right; while the rule is exceeded (more than K kinds, more than K zeros), remove items from the left. Record the length after fixing.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-atmostk"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-exactk","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Exactly K distinct”, “exactly K odd numbers” (counting subarrays)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Exactly K distinct”, “exactly K odd numbers” (counting subarrays)","when I see “Exactly K distinct”, “exactly K odd numbers” (counting subarrays)","what to do when “Exactly K distinct”, “exactly K odd numbers” (counting subarrays)","which pattern for “Exactly K distinct”, “exactly K odd numbers” (counting subarrays)"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Exactly K distinct”, “exactly K odd numbers” (counting subarrays)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>atMost(K) − atMost(K − 1)<\/code>. Count windows ending at r as <code>r − l + 1<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> “Exactly K” is awkward for a window, but “at most K” is easy. Exactly K = (at most K) − (at most K − 1). Each at-most count adds r − l + 1 at every step: the number of valid windows ending at r.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-exactk"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-fixed","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"Window size k is given (average, sum, max vowels in k)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Window size k is given (average, sum, max vowels in k)","when I see Window size k is given (average, sum, max vowels in k)","what to do when Window size k is given (average, sum, max vowels in k)","which pattern for Window size k is given (average, sum, max vowels in k)"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Window size k is given (average, sum, max vowels in k)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Add <code>nums[r]<\/code>, subtract <code>nums[r − k]<\/code>. Never recompute.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When the window size k is fixed, don’t re-add k numbers every step. Slide: add the number entering on the right and subtract the one leaving on the left. Each step is O(1).<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-fixed"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-anagram","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Permutation of p inside s”, “all anagram start indexes”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Permutation of p inside s”, “all anagram start indexes”","when I see “Permutation of p inside s”, “all anagram start indexes”","what to do when “Permutation of p inside s”, “all anagram start indexes”","which pattern for “Permutation of p inside s”, “all anagram start indexes”"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Permutation of p inside s”, “all anagram start indexes”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Fixed window of size len(p) with letter counts; compare to p’s counts.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> An anagram of p has the same letter counts as p. So slide a window of exactly len(p) across s, keep its letter counts up to date, and compare them with p’s counts at every position.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-anagram"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-deque","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Max (or min) of every window of size k”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Max (or min) of every window of size k”","when I see “Max (or min) of every window of size k”","what to do when “Max (or min) of every window of size k”","which pattern for “Max (or min) of every window of size k”"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Max (or min) of every window of size k”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Deque of indexes with decreasing values. Pop smaller from the back, pop expired from the front. Front is the max.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Keep a short list (deque) of indexes whose values are decreasing. When a new number arrives, remove smaller numbers from the back: they can never be a maximum again. Drop the front if it has slid out of the window. The front is always the current maximum.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-deque"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-negatives","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"Subarray sum target, but the array has negative numbers","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Subarray sum target, but the array has negative numbers","when I see Subarray sum target, but the array has negative numbers","what to do when Subarray sum target, but the array has negative numbers","which pattern for Subarray sum target, but the array has negative numbers"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Subarray sum target, but the array has negative numbers<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Not a window. Use prefix sums + a dict.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A sliding window assumes that adding a number moves the sum one way. With negative numbers, growing the window can make the sum smaller, so shrinking decisions become wrong. Use prefix sums with a dictionary instead (see Arrays &amp; Hashing).<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-negatives"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-complement","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Take k items from the two ends” (cards, operations to reduce x to zero)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Take k items from the two ends” (cards, operations to reduce x to zero)","when I see “Take k items from the two ends” (cards, operations to reduce x to zero)","what to do when “Take k items from the two ends” (cards, operations to reduce x to zero)","which pattern for “Take k items from the two ends” (cards, operations to reduce x to zero)"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Take k items from the two ends” (cards, operations to reduce x to zero)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Flip it: the items you <b>don’t<\/b> take form a contiguous middle window of size n − k.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Taking k cards from the two ends means the cards you leave form one block in the middle, of length n − k. So find the middle block with the smallest sum (a fixed-size window), and subtract it from the total.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-complement"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-stock","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"Best single buy-then-sell, or “max later minus earlier”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Best single buy-then-sell, or “max later minus earlier”","when I see Best single buy-then-sell, or “max later minus earlier”","what to do when Best single buy-then-sell, or “max later minus earlier”","which pattern for Best single buy-then-sell, or “max later minus earlier”"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Best single buy-then-sell, or “max later minus earlier”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Track the minimum so far; at each step compare today minus that minimum.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> You must buy before you sell, so for each day ask: “what if I sell today?” The best buy is the cheapest day so far. Keep that minimum as you go and compare today − minimum with the best profit.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-stock"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-cxswamort","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"while (shrink) inside for (grow) over the same array","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["while (shrink) inside for (grow) over the same array","when I see while (shrink) inside for (grow) over the same array","what to do when while (shrink) inside for (grow) over the same array","which pattern for while (shrink) inside for (grow) over the same array"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>while (shrink) inside for (grow) over the same array<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(n): count that each index enters once and leaves at most once.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A shrinking while-loop inside the growing for-loop looks like O(n²), but count what happens to each index: it joins the window once (r passes it) and leaves at most once (l passes it). That’s at most 2n steps in total: O(n).<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-cxswamort"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-cxswspace","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"Space of a window solution","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Space of a window solution","when I see Space of a window solution","what to do when Space of a window solution","which pattern for Space of a window solution"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Space of a window solution<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Size of the window’s bookkeeping: count map bounded by distinct values (O(26) for letters), a deque up to O(k).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The extra space is whatever the window remembers. Letter counts are capped at 26 keys (O(1)); counts of arbitrary values can grow with the window; a max-deque holds at most k indexes.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-cxswspace"]]},{"i":"rx-sliding-window-cxswfixed","k":"rx","s":"sliding-window","t":"Fixed-size window recomputed from scratch each step","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Fixed-size window recomputed from scratch each step","when I see Fixed-size window recomputed from scratch each step","what to do when Fixed-size window recomputed from scratch each step","which pattern for Fixed-size window recomputed from scratch each step"],"w":"Sliding Window","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Fixed-size window recomputed from scratch each step<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Make it incremental: add the new element, remove the old one → O(n) instead of O(n·k).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Re-adding all k numbers for each of the n windows does k work n times. Updating the previous window (add the new number, drop the old) is 1 unit of work per step.<\/p><p>From <b>Sliding Window<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Sliding Window","sliding-window.html#rx-cxswfixed"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-brackets","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Brackets, tags, nesting, “valid parentheses”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Brackets, tags, nesting, “valid parentheses”","when I see Brackets, tags, nesting, “valid parentheses”","what to do when Brackets, tags, nesting, “valid parentheses”","which pattern for Brackets, tags, nesting, “valid parentheses”"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Brackets, tags, nesting, “valid parentheses”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Push openers. On a closer, the top must be its partner; pop it. Valid if the stack ends empty.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Every closing bracket must match the most recent unmatched opening one, which is exactly what a stack’s top gives you. Push each opener. On a closer, the top must be its partner (pop it), otherwise it’s invalid. At the end nothing may be left open.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-brackets"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-expr","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Evaluate an expression (RPN, calculator, + − × with precedence)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Evaluate an expression (RPN, calculator, + − × with precedence)","when I see Evaluate an expression (RPN, calculator, + − × with precedence)","what to do when Evaluate an expression (RPN, calculator, + − × with precedence)","which pattern for Evaluate an expression (RPN, calculator, + − × with precedence)"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Evaluate an expression (RPN, calculator, + − × with precedence)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Stack of numbers. RPN: operators pop two. Infix: push signed terms, apply × ÷ to the top immediately, sum at the end.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> In Reverse Polish notation the operator comes after its two numbers. Push numbers onto a stack; when an operator appears, pop the last two numbers, apply it, and push the result back. The final number on the stack is the answer.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-expr"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-decode","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Nested repeats like 3[a2[c]] , nested lists, mini-parsers","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Nested repeats like 3[a2[c]] , nested lists, mini-parsers","when I see Nested repeats like 3[a2[c]] , nested lists, mini-parsers","what to do when Nested repeats like 3[a2[c]] , nested lists, mini-parsers","which pattern for Nested repeats like 3[a2[c]] , nested lists, mini-parsers"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Nested repeats like 3[a2[c]] , nested lists, mini-parsers<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>On “[” push (current text, count) and reset. On “]” pop and build <code>prev + cur × count<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Nested brackets mean “pause what I was building, build the inside, then come back”. On “[”, save (text so far, repeat count) on a stack and start fresh. On “]”, pop the saved pair and set text = saved text + inside × count.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-decode"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-undo","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Backspace, undo, “..” in a path, cancel the last thing","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Backspace, undo, “..” in a path, cancel the last thing","when I see Backspace, undo, “..” in a path, cancel the last thing","what to do when Backspace, undo, “..” in a path, cancel the last thing","which pattern for Backspace, undo, “..” in a path, cancel the last thing"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Backspace, undo, “..” in a path, cancel the last thing<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>A stack: push normal items, pop on the cancel action.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Whenever something can cancel the most recent thing (backspace, undo, “..” in a path), keep the kept items on a stack. Normal items are pushed; the cancel action pops the latest one.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-undo"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-minstack","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Stack that also returns min (or max) in O(1)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Stack that also returns min (or max) in O(1)","when I see Stack that also returns min (or max) in O(1)","what to do when Stack that also returns min (or max) in O(1)","which pattern for Stack that also returns min (or max) in O(1)"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Stack that also returns min (or max) in O(1)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Store pairs <code>(value, min so far)<\/code> on every push.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A stack can’t search for its minimum quickly. So when you push a value, also store what the minimum is at that moment. The top pair always knows the current minimum, and popping restores the previous one for free.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-minstack"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-nextgreater","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"“Next greater / warmer / higher element” for every position","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Next greater / warmer / higher element” for every position","when I see “Next greater / warmer / higher element” for every position","what to do when “Next greater / warmer / higher element” for every position","which pattern for “Next greater / warmer / higher element” for every position"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Next greater / warmer / higher element” for every position<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Stack of indexes with decreasing values. When x beats the top, pop: x is that index’s answer.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Walk left to right keeping a stack of positions still waiting for a bigger number. When the current number is bigger than the one on top, that position just found its answer: pop it and record. The stack stays in decreasing order.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-nextgreater"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-prevsmaller","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"“Previous smaller / greater element”, “span of days with price ≤ today”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Previous smaller / greater element”, “span of days with price ≤ today”","when I see “Previous smaller / greater element”, “span of days with price ≤ today”","what to do when “Previous smaller / greater element”, “span of days with price ≤ today”","which pattern for “Previous smaller / greater element”, “span of days with price ≤ today”"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Previous smaller / greater element”, “span of days with price ≤ today”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Same scan. After popping everything that x beats, whatever remains on top is x’s previous greater (or smaller).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> It’s the same scan, but you read the answer from what survives. After popping everything the current value beats, the item left on top is the nearest earlier value that’s bigger. For stock span, add up the spans you popped.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-prevsmaller"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-histogram","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Largest rectangle in a histogram, max area under bars","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Largest rectangle in a histogram, max area under bars","when I see Largest rectangle in a histogram, max area under bars","what to do when Largest rectangle in a histogram, max area under bars","which pattern for Largest rectangle in a histogram, max area under bars"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Largest rectangle in a histogram, max area under bars<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Increasing stack of indexes plus a 0 sentinel. When popping bar j: width = i − new top − 1.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each bar’s best rectangle stretches until a lower bar on each side. Keep bars in increasing height on a stack. When a lower bar arrives, the bar you pop has found its right edge (here) and its left edge (the new top), so width = i − new top − 1.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-histogram"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-circular","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Next greater in a circular array","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Next greater in a circular array","when I see Next greater in a circular array","what to do when Next greater in a circular array","which pattern for Next greater in a circular array"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Next greater in a circular array<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Loop i over 2n and use <code>nums[i % n]<\/code>; only push during the first pass.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> In a circular array the next bigger number may sit before you. Walk the array twice (index i % n): the second lap gives the early positions a chance to find their answer. Only push positions during the first lap.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-circular"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-removek","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"“Remove k digits to make the smallest number”, smallest subsequence","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Remove k digits to make the smallest number”, smallest subsequence","when I see “Remove k digits to make the smallest number”, smallest subsequence","what to do when “Remove k digits to make the smallest number”, smallest subsequence","which pattern for “Remove k digits to make the smallest number”, smallest subsequence"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Remove k digits to make the smallest number”, smallest subsequence<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Increasing stack: while k and top &amp;gt; current, pop. Trim leftovers from the end, strip leading zeros.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To make the smallest number, earlier digits matter most. Walk the digits keeping a stack; while you still may delete and the top digit is bigger than the current one, delete the top. Delete any leftovers from the end and strip leading zeros.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-removek"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-asteroid","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Things moving and colliding (asteroids, cancelling pairs)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Things moving and colliding (asteroids, cancelling pairs)","when I see Things moving and colliding (asteroids, cancelling pairs)","what to do when Things moving and colliding (asteroids, cancelling pairs)","which pattern for Things moving and colliding (asteroids, cancelling pairs)"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Things moving and colliding (asteroids, cancelling pairs)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Stack of survivors. A new left-mover fights the top right-movers until it dies or none are left.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Keep the survivors on a stack. A collision only happens when something moving left meets the right-moving things before it. Let the new one fight the top: the smaller explodes, equal sizes both explode, and repeat until it dies or nothing is left to hit.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-asteroid"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-carfleet","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Cars catching up and merging into fleets","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Cars catching up and merging into fleets","when I see Cars catching up and merging into fleets","what to do when Cars catching up and merging into fleets","which pattern for Cars catching up and merging into fleets"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Cars catching up and merging into fleets<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sort by position, closest to target first. Keep a stack of arrival times; a slower (larger) time starts a new fleet.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A car can’t overtake, so it joins the fleet ahead if it would arrive no later than that fleet. Sort cars by position, closest to the target first, and work out each one’s arrival time. A car that arrives later than the fleet ahead starts a new fleet.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-carfleet"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-cxstamort","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"while stack and …: pop inside a for loop","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["while stack and …: pop inside a for loop","when I see while stack and …: pop inside a for loop","what to do when while stack and …: pop inside a for loop","which pattern for while stack and …: pop inside a for loop"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>while stack and …: pop inside a for loop<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(n): every element is pushed once and popped at most once.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A while-pop inside a for loop looks quadratic, but follow one element: it is pushed once and popped at most once. Over the whole run that’s at most 2n stack operations, so the time is O(n).<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-cxstamort"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-cxstspace","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"Space of a stack solution","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Space of a stack solution","when I see Space of a stack solution","what to do when Space of a stack solution","which pattern for Space of a stack solution"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Space of a stack solution<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Worst-case stack height: O(n) (sorted input never pops, or nested brackets).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Space is the tallest the stack can get. If nothing ever gets popped (an increasing list for next-greater, or “((((((”), every item stays on it: O(n).<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-cxstspace"]]},{"i":"rx-stack-cxstbrute","k":"rx","s":"stack","t":"For each element, scanning left or right for the first bigger/smaller one","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["For each element, scanning left or right for the first bigger/smaller one","when I see For each element, scanning left or right for the first bigger/smaller one","what to do when For each element, scanning left or right for the first bigger/smaller one","which pattern for For each element, scanning left or right for the first bigger/smaller one"],"w":"Stack","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>For each element, scanning left or right for the first bigger/smaller one<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>That’s O(n²). A monotonic stack does it in O(n).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Scanning outward from every position to find the first bigger or smaller one does up to n steps for each of n positions. The monotonic stack finds all the answers in one pass.<\/p><p>From <b>Stack<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Stack","stack.html#rx-cxstbrute"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-sorted","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"Sorted input and “find”, “search”, “insert position”, or a required O(log n)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Sorted input and “find”, “search”, “insert position”, or a required O(log n)","when I see Sorted input and “find”, “search”, “insert position”, or a required O(log n)","what to do when Sorted input and “find”, “search”, “insert position”, or a required O(log n)","which pattern for Sorted input and “find”, “search”, “insert position”, or a required O(log n)"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Sorted input and “find”, “search”, “insert position”, or a required O(log n)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Binary search.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sorted data lets you throw away half the candidates with one comparison: look at the middle; if it’s too small, the answer can only be to the right, otherwise to the left. A million items need about 20 looks.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-sorted"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-firsttrue","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"“First / smallest index where …”, “insert position”, “first bad version”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“First / smallest index where …”, “insert position”, “first bad version”","when I see “First / smallest index where …”, “insert position”, “first bad version”","what to do when “First / smallest index where …”, “insert position”, “first bad version”","which pattern for “First / smallest index where …”, “insert position”, “first bad version”"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“First / smallest index where …”, “insert position”, “first bad version”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>lo, hi = 0, n<\/code>; <code>while lo &lt; hi<\/code>; true → <code>hi = mid<\/code>, false → <code>lo = mid + 1<\/code>. Return lo.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Many questions are “where does false turn into true?” (first bad version, insert position). Keep lo and hi around that switch point. If mid is true, the first true is at mid or before it (hi = mid); if false, it’s after mid (lo = mid + 1). When lo meets hi, that’s the answer.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-firsttrue"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-invariant","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"You’re unsure about < vs ≤ , mid vs mid ± 1","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You’re unsure about < vs ≤ , mid vs mid ± 1","when I see You’re unsure about < vs ≤ , mid vs mid ± 1","what to do when You’re unsure about < vs ≤ , mid vs mid ± 1","which pattern for You’re unsure about < vs ≤ , mid vs mid ± 1"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You’re unsure about &lt; vs ≤ , mid vs mid ± 1<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Decide what [lo, hi] means first (“answer is inside”), then make every branch keep that true.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Off-by-one bugs come from not deciding what lo and hi mean. Write one sentence first, for example “the answer is somewhere in [lo, hi]”. Then each branch must keep that sentence true: if mid might be the answer keep it (hi = mid); if it can’t be, skip it (lo = mid + 1).<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-invariant"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-rotated","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"“Rotated sorted array”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Rotated sorted array”","when I see “Rotated sorted array”","what to do when “Rotated sorted array”","which pattern for “Rotated sorted array”"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Rotated sorted array”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Compare nums[lo] and nums[mid]: one half is sorted. If the target fits that half’s range go there, else the other half.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A rotated sorted array is two sorted pieces. Whatever the middle is, one side of it is still in order (check nums[lo] ≤ nums[mid]). If the target fits inside that ordered side’s range, search there; otherwise search the other side.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-rotated"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-findmin","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"Minimum of a rotated sorted array","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Minimum of a rotated sorted array","when I see Minimum of a rotated sorted array","what to do when Minimum of a rotated sorted array","which pattern for Minimum of a rotated sorted array"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Minimum of a rotated sorted array<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Compare <code>nums[mid]<\/code> with <code>nums[hi]<\/code>. Greater → lo = mid + 1, else hi = mid.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The minimum is where the rotation “drop” happens. Compare the middle with the last element: if the middle is bigger, the drop (and the minimum) is to its right; otherwise the minimum is the middle or to its left.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-findmin"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-bisect","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"Python and you need a first/last position in a sorted list","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Python and you need a first/last position in a sorted list","when I see Python and you need a first/last position in a sorted list","what to do when Python and you need a first/last position in a sorted list","which pattern for Python and you need a first/last position in a sorted list"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Python and you need a first/last position in a sorted list<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>bisect_left(a, x)<\/code> = first index ≥ x. <code>bisect_right(a, x)<\/code> = first index &gt; x.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Python already has binary search. bisect_left gives the first position where x could go (the first index ≥ x). bisect_right gives the position just after the last x. Together they give the range of all copies of x.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-bisect"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-answer","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"“Minimum capacity / speed / largest-sum limit such that it’s possible”, “minimize the maximum”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Minimum capacity / speed / largest-sum limit such that it’s possible”, “minimize the maximum”","when I see “Minimum capacity / speed / largest-sum limit such that it’s possible”, “minimize the maximum”","what to do when “Minimum capacity / speed / largest-sum limit such that it’s possible”, “minimize the maximum”","which pattern for “Minimum capacity / speed / largest-sum limit such that it’s possible”, “minimize the maximum”"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Minimum capacity / speed / largest-sum limit such that it’s possible”, “minimize the maximum”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Binary search the answer in [lo, hi] with a greedy <code>feasible(x)<\/code>. Find the first x that works.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sometimes you can’t search the input, but you can search the answer. Ask “could speed k work?”. If k works, any bigger k works too, so the answers form ✗✗✗✓✓✓. Binary search for the first ✓, checking each guess with a quick greedy simulation.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-answer"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-peak","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"“Find a peak”, “mountain array”, local max in O(log n)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Find a peak”, “mountain array”, local max in O(log n)","when I see “Find a peak”, “mountain array”, local max in O(log n)","what to do when “Find a peak”, “mountain array”, local max in O(log n)","which pattern for “Find a peak”, “mountain array”, local max in O(log n)"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Find a peak”, “mountain array”, local max in O(log n)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Compare mid with mid + 1. Uphill to the right → lo = mid + 1, else hi = mid.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Compare the middle with its right neighbour. If the right one is higher you’re walking uphill, and a peak must exist to the right. Otherwise one exists at mid or to its left. Every step halves the search.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-peak"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-matrix","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"Matrix sorted row by row, each row starting above the previous row’s end","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Matrix sorted row by row, each row starting above the previous row’s end","when I see Matrix sorted row by row, each row starting above the previous row’s end","what to do when Matrix sorted row by row, each row starting above the previous row’s end","which pattern for Matrix sorted row by row, each row starting above the previous row’s end"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Matrix sorted row by row, each row starting above the previous row’s end<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Treat it as one list of R × C: <code>matrix[mid // C][mid % C]<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If each row continues where the previous one ended, the matrix is really one sorted list split into rows. Binary search positions 0 … R·C − 1 and turn a position into a cell with row = mid // C, col = mid % C.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-matrix"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-kth","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"“Kth smallest” in a sorted matrix / multiplication table / pair distances","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Kth smallest” in a sorted matrix / multiplication table / pair distances","when I see “Kth smallest” in a sorted matrix / multiplication table / pair distances","what to do when “Kth smallest” in a sorted matrix / multiplication table / pair distances","which pattern for “Kth smallest” in a sorted matrix / multiplication table / pair distances"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Kth smallest” in a sorted matrix / multiplication table / pair distances<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Binary search on the <b>value<\/b>. Count how many items are ≤ mid; first value with count ≥ k.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Instead of searching positions, search the value itself. Guess a value and count how many items are ≤ it (quick in a sorted matrix). The k-th smallest is the first value whose count reaches k.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-kth"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-median","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"Median of two sorted arrays in O(log)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Median of two sorted arrays in O(log)","when I see Median of two sorted arrays in O(log)","what to do when Median of two sorted arrays in O(log)","which pattern for Median of two sorted arrays in O(log)"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Median of two sorted arrays in O(log)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Binary search how many items to take from the shorter array; the rest come from the other. Valid when both left maxes ≤ both right mins.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The median splits all numbers into a left half and a right half. Binary search how many of the left half come from the shorter array (the rest come from the other). The split is right when every left number ≤ every right number.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-median"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-rounding","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"Template that does lo = mid (largest x that works: sqrt, last true)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Template that does lo = mid (largest x that works: sqrt, last true)","when I see Template that does lo = mid (largest x that works: sqrt, last true)","what to do when Template that does lo = mid (largest x that works: sqrt, last true)","which pattern for Template that does lo = mid (largest x that works: sqrt, last true)"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Template that does lo = mid (largest x that works: sqrt, last true)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Round mid <b>up<\/b>: <code>mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If a branch sets lo = mid (you’re finding the largest value that works), mid must round up. Otherwise, with lo and hi next to each other, mid equals lo again and the loop never moves.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-rounding"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-cxbslog","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"A loop that halves the range (lo/hi/mid), or n //= 2","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A loop that halves the range (lo/hi/mid), or n //= 2","when I see A loop that halves the range (lo/hi/mid), or n //= 2","what to do when A loop that halves the range (lo/hi/mid), or n //= 2","which pattern for A loop that halves the range (lo/hi/mid), or n //= 2"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A loop that halves the range (lo/hi/mid), or n //= 2<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(log n) iterations. Multiply by the work inside.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A loop that halves the range each time runs about log₂ n times: 1,000,000 shrinks to 1 in about 20 steps. Multiply by the work done in each step.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-cxbslog"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-cxbsanswer","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"“Minimum speed / capacity / days such that …”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Minimum speed / capacity / days such that …”","when I see “Minimum speed / capacity / days such that …”","what to do when “Minimum speed / capacity / days such that …”","which pattern for “Minimum speed / capacity / days such that …”"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Minimum speed / capacity / days such that …”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Binary search the answer range: O(log(range) × cost of the check).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Searching the answer costs (number of guesses) × (cost of one check). The guesses are log of the size of the answer range; each check usually walks the input once.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-cxbsanswer"]]},{"i":"rx-binary-search-cxbssort","k":"rx","s":"binary-search","t":"Unsorted data and one lookup","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Unsorted data and one lookup","when I see Unsorted data and one lookup","what to do when Unsorted data and one lookup","which pattern for Unsorted data and one lookup"],"w":"Binary Search","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Unsorted data and one lookup<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Just scan, O(n). Sort (O(n log n)) only if you’ll do many binary searches afterwards.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Binary search needs sorted data. Sorting costs O(n log n), more than simply scanning once (O(n)). Sort only when you’ll search many times afterwards.<\/p><p>From <b>Binary Search<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Binary Search","binary-search.html#rx-cxbssort"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-reverse","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Reverse the list” (or part of it)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Reverse the list” (or part of it)","when I see “Reverse the list” (or part of it)","what to do when “Reverse the list” (or part of it)","which pattern for “Reverse the list” (or part of it)"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Reverse the list” (or part of it)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>prev, curr = None, head<\/code>. Loop: save next, point curr back to prev, step both.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Walk the list turning every arrow around. Keep three names: prev (the part already reversed), curr (the node you’re fixing) and next (saved first, so you don’t lose the rest). Point curr at prev, then step both forward.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-reverse"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-dummy","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"The head might change: delete, insert at front, merge, partition, build a new list","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["The head might change: delete, insert at front, merge, partition, build a new list","when I see The head might change: delete, insert at front, merge, partition, build a new list","what to do when The head might change: delete, insert at front, merge, partition, build a new list","which pattern for The head might change: delete, insert at front, merge, partition, build a new list"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>The head might change: delete, insert at front, merge, partition, build a new list<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>dummy = ListNode(0, head)<\/code>, work from it, return <code>dummy.next<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When the first node might be deleted or replaced, put a fake node in front of it. Now every real node has a node before it, so there are no special cases for the head. At the end return dummy.next.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-dummy"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-nth","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Nth node from the end” in one pass","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Nth node from the end” in one pass","when I see “Nth node from the end” in one pass","what to do when “Nth node from the end” in one pass","which pattern for “Nth node from the end” in one pass"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Nth node from the end” in one pass<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dummy, then move fast n + 1 steps ahead. Move both until fast is None. slow is just before the target.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To find the n-th node from the end in one pass, give one pointer a head start of n + 1 steps (from a dummy). Then move both together. When the leader falls off the end, the follower is right before the node to remove.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-nth"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-addtwo","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"Numbers stored as digit lists (add, multiply)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Numbers stored as digit lists (add, multiply)","when I see Numbers stored as digit lists (add, multiply)","what to do when Numbers stored as digit lists (add, multiply)","which pattern for Numbers stored as digit lists (add, multiply)"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Numbers stored as digit lists (add, multiply)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dummy + tail, loop while l1 or l2 or carry. <code>carry, digit = divmod(s, 10)<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Add like on paper, one digit column at a time, carrying the extra. The lists store digits in reverse, so the head is the ones digit. Keep going while either list has digits or a carry is left.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-addtwo"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-reversek","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Reverse nodes in groups of k”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Reverse nodes in groups of k”","when I see “Reverse nodes in groups of k”","what to do when “Reverse nodes in groups of k”","which pattern for “Reverse nodes in groups of k”"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Reverse nodes in groups of k”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dummy. For each group: find the kth node (stop if missing), reverse inside, reconnect group_prev → kth, group_prev = old first.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Cut the list into groups of k. For each full group: reverse it on its own, then reconnect the node before the group to the new first node, and the old first node (now last) to what follows. A short final group stays as it is.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-reversek"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-copyrandom","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"Deep copy a list with random pointers (or any graph)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Deep copy a list with random pointers (or any graph)","when I see Deep copy a list with random pointers (or any graph)","what to do when Deep copy a list with random pointers (or any graph)","which pattern for Deep copy a list with random pointers (or any graph)"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Deep copy a list with random pointers (or any graph)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dict old → new. Pass 1 creates copies, pass 2 wires next and random through the dict.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Random pointers can point anywhere, even ahead, so build the copies first and connect them second. Pass 1: create a copy of every node and remember old → new in a dictionary. Pass 2: copy.next = map[old.next], copy.random = map[old.random].<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-copyrandom"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-lru","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“LRU cache”, “O(1) get and put with eviction”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“LRU cache”, “O(1) get and put with eviction”","when I see “LRU cache”, “O(1) get and put with eviction”","what to do when “LRU cache”, “O(1) get and put with eviction”","which pattern for “LRU cache”, “O(1) get and put with eviction”"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“LRU cache”, “O(1) get and put with eviction”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dict key → node plus a doubly linked list with head/tail sentinels. Touch = move to the tail; evict head.next.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A dictionary finds a key in O(1), and a doubly linked list keeps keys in “last used” order, also changeable in O(1). Every get or put moves that key to the most-recent end. When over capacity, remove from the least-recent end.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-lru"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-middle","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Middle of the list”, split a list in half","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Middle of the list”, split a list in half","when I see “Middle of the list”, split a list in half","what to do when “Middle of the list”, split a list in half","which pattern for “Middle of the list”, split a list in half"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Middle of the list”, split a list in half<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>slow 1 step, fast 2 steps, while <code>fast and fast.next<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fast moves two nodes for every one that slow moves. When fast reaches the end, slow has gone half as far: the middle.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-middle"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-cycle","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Does the list have a cycle?” with O(1) space","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Does the list have a cycle?” with O(1) space","when I see “Does the list have a cycle?” with O(1) space","what to do when “Does the list have a cycle?” with O(1) space","which pattern for “Does the list have a cycle?” with O(1) space"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Does the list have a cycle?” with O(1) space<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Floyd: slow 1, fast 2. If they ever meet, there’s a cycle.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Two runners: slow takes 1 step, fast takes 2. Without a loop fast reaches the end. With a loop, fast eventually laps slow and they land on the same node.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-cycle"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-cyclestart","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Where does the cycle begin?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Where does the cycle begin?”","when I see “Where does the cycle begin?”","what to do when “Where does the cycle begin?”","which pattern for “Where does the cycle begin?”"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Where does the cycle begin?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>After they meet, reset slow to head. Move both one step at a time. They meet at the entrance.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> After slow and fast meet inside the loop, put slow back at the head and move both one step at a time. The maths works out so they meet again exactly where the loop starts.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-cyclestart"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-reorder","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Reorder L0 → Ln → L1 → Ln−1 …”, palindrome list","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Reorder L0 → Ln → L1 → Ln−1 …”, palindrome list","when I see “Reorder L0 → Ln → L1 → Ln−1 …”, palindrome list","what to do when “Reorder L0 → Ln → L1 → Ln−1 …”, palindrome list","which pattern for “Reorder L0 → Ln → L1 → Ln−1 …”, palindrome list"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Reorder L0 → Ln → L1 → Ln−1 …”, palindrome list<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Three steps: find the middle, reverse the second half, then weave or compare.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Weaving the front with the back is three standard moves you already know: find the middle (slow/fast), reverse the second half, then take one node from each half in turn.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-reorder"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-palin","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Is the linked list a palindrome?” in O(1) space","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Is the linked list a palindrome?” in O(1) space","when I see “Is the linked list a palindrome?” in O(1) space","what to do when “Is the linked list a palindrome?” in O(1) space","which pattern for “Is the linked list a palindrome?” in O(1) space"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Is the linked list a palindrome?” in O(1) space<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Middle, reverse the second half, compare the halves.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A list can’t be read backwards, so turn its second half around. Find the middle, reverse the second half, then walk both halves side by side comparing values.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-palin"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-intersect","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Where do two lists intersect?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Where do two lists intersect?”","when I see “Where do two lists intersect?”","what to do when “Where do two lists intersect?”","which pattern for “Where do two lists intersect?”"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Where do two lists intersect?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>p walks A then B, q walks B then A. They meet at the intersection (or both reach None).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The lists have different lengths before they join. If pointer p walks A then B, and q walks B then A, both cover the same total distance, so they arrive at the shared node at the same moment (or both reach the end together if there is none).<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-intersect"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-mergek","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"“Merge k sorted lists”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Merge k sorted lists”","when I see “Merge k sorted lists”","what to do when “Merge k sorted lists”","which pattern for “Merge k sorted lists”"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Merge k sorted lists”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Min-heap of (value, list index, node). Pop the smallest, push its next.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The next smallest overall is always one of the k current list heads. Keep those heads in a min-heap. Take the smallest, add it to the result, and push the node after it from the same list.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-mergek"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-cxllhops","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"Walking a linked list","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Walking a linked list","when I see Walking a linked list","what to do when Walking a linked list","which pattern for Walking a linked list"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Walking a linked list<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Count hops: a full walk is O(n); reaching index k is O(k); there is no O(1) random access.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A linked list has no jump-to-index. Getting to the k-th node means following k arrows, so it costs O(k). A full walk is O(n).<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-cxllhops"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-cxllfast","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"Fast/slow pointers (middle, cycle detection, k-th from end)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Fast/slow pointers (middle, cycle detection, k-th from end)","when I see Fast/slow pointers (middle, cycle detection, k-th from end)","what to do when Fast/slow pointers (middle, cycle detection, k-th from end)","which pattern for Fast/slow pointers (middle, cycle detection, k-th from end)"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Fast/slow pointers (middle, cycle detection, k-th from end)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(n) time, O(1) space.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Slow/fast pointer tricks walk the list a constant number of times and only keep two pointers, so they’re O(n) time and O(1) extra space.<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-cxllfast"]]},{"i":"rx-linked-list-cxllrec","k":"rx","s":"linked-list","t":"A recursive linked-list function","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A recursive linked-list function","when I see A recursive linked-list function","what to do when A recursive linked-list function","which pattern for A recursive linked-list function"],"w":"Linked List","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A recursive linked-list function<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Add O(n) stack space and mention the recursion limit; offer the iterative O(1)-space version.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A recursive solution keeps one call frame per node until it returns, so the call stack uses O(n) memory, and very long lists can hit Python’s recursion limit. Mention that, and offer the iterative version that uses O(1).<\/p><p>From <b>Linked List<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Linked List","linked-list.html#rx-cxllrec"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-trust","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"A recursive call, like fact(n - 1) or solve(node.left)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A recursive call, like fact(n - 1) or solve(node.left)","when I see A recursive call, like fact(n - 1) or solve(node.left)","what to do when A recursive call, like fact(n - 1) or solve(node.left)","which pattern for A recursive call, like fact(n - 1) or solve(node.left)"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A recursive call, like fact(n - 1) or solve(node.left)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Don’t step inside. Read it as “the correct answer for the smaller input” and keep reading.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Don’t try to follow a recursive call down into all its levels; your head will overflow. Treat the call as a finished helper that already returns the right answer for the smaller input, and just use that answer.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-trust"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-write","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"A blank function you need to make recursive","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A blank function you need to make recursive","when I see A blank function you need to make recursive","what to do when A blank function you need to make recursive","which pattern for A blank function you need to make recursive"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A blank function you need to make recursive<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Write the base case first. Then write the final <code>return<\/code> as if the recursive call already works. Fill in the middle last.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Write a recursive function in this order. First the base case (the smallest input you can answer directly). Then the last line, pretending the recursive calls already work. Only then the middle: what you need from the smaller calls.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-write"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-memo","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"The same arguments showing up in more than one branch of the call tree","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["The same arguments showing up in more than one branch of the call tree","when I see The same arguments showing up in more than one branch of the call tree","what to do when The same arguments showing up in more than one branch of the call tree","which pattern for The same arguments showing up in more than one branch of the call tree"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>The same arguments showing up in more than one branch of the call tree<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Put <code>@lru_cache(None)<\/code> on it, or keep a dict keyed by the arguments.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If the call tree asks the same question in several branches, you’re redoing work, often exponentially. Remember each answer the first time (a dict, or @lru_cache) and return the saved one afterwards.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-memo"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cost","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“What’s the time complexity?” for a recursive solution","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“What’s the time complexity?” for a recursive solution","when I see “What’s the time complexity?” for a recursive solution","what to do when “What’s the time complexity?” for a recursive solution","which pattern for “What’s the time complexity?” for a recursive solution"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“What’s the time complexity?” for a recursive solution<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Count the boxes in the call tree, multiply by the work inside one box. Space is the height of the tree.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Draw the call tree. Time = (number of calls, the boxes) × (work done inside one call). Space is not the number of boxes: it’s how deep the tree goes, because only one path of calls is open at a time.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cost"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-none","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"if not node:","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["if not node:","when I see if not node:","what to do when if not node:","which pattern for if not node:"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>if not node:<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Return the empty-tree answer: <code>0<\/code> for counts and heights, <code>True<\/code> for “is it valid”, <code>None<\/code> for build or search, <code>[]<\/code> for lists.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The empty tree still needs an answer, and it’s the value that makes the parent’s formula work without special cases. A height of 0 makes leaves get 1. “Valid?” is True, so it never breaks an and-chain.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-none"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-children","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"A node’s answer depends on its children’s answers (height, size, balanced, sum)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A node’s answer depends on its children’s answers (height, size, balanced, sum)","when I see A node’s answer depends on its children’s answers (height, size, balanced, sum)","what to do when A node’s answer depends on its children’s answers (height, size, balanced, sum)","which pattern for A node’s answer depends on its children’s answers (height, size, balanced, sum)"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A node’s answer depends on its children’s answers (height, size, balanced, sum)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>l = f(node.left)<\/code>, <code>r = f(node.right)<\/code>, combine. Postorder, every time.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When a node’s answer is built from its children’s answers, ask both children first and then combine. Answers flow from the leaves up to the root (postorder).<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-children"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-above","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"A node needs something from above: running sum, max so far, allowed range, depth","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A node needs something from above: running sum, max so far, allowed range, depth","when I see A node needs something from above: running sum, max so far, allowed range, depth","what to do when A node needs something from above: running sum, max so far, allowed range, depth","which pattern for A node needs something from above: running sum, max so far, allowed range, depth"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A node needs something from above: running sum, max so far, allowed range, depth<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Add a parameter. Pass the updated value into both recursive calls.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When a node needs information from above (the sum so far, the allowed range, the depth), pass it down as an extra parameter. Each call hands its children an updated copy.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-above"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-bend","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"The answer is a path that can bend through a node (diameter, max path sum)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["The answer is a path that can bend through a node (diameter, max path sum)","when I see The answer is a path that can bend through a node (diameter, max path sum)","what to do when The answer is a path that can bend through a node (diameter, max path sum)","which pattern for The answer is a path that can bend through a node (diameter, max path sum)"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>The answer is a path that can bend through a node (diameter, max path sum)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Return the best one-sided path to the parent. Update a <code>nonlocal best<\/code> with left + right + node.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A path may go up into a node and down the other side, but a parent can only continue one side of it. So each node does two things: it tries the bent path left + node + right as a candidate for the global best, and it returns only the better single side to its parent.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-bend"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-bst","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“Binary search tree”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Binary search tree”","when I see “Binary search tree”","what to do when “Binary search tree”","which pattern for “Binary search tree”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Binary search tree”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Inorder gives sorted order. And the ordering lets you go only left or only right: O(h) instead of O(n).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> In a BST everything left is smaller and everything right is bigger. Two consequences: an inorder walk (left, node, right) lists values in sorted order; and to find a value you only go one way at each node, so it costs the height, not the size.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-bst"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-pair","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"Two trees at once: same tree, symmetric, merge, subtree","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Two trees at once: same tree, symmetric, merge, subtree","when I see Two trees at once: same tree, symmetric, merge, subtree","what to do when Two trees at once: same tree, symmetric, merge, subtree","which pattern for Two trees at once: same tree, symmetric, merge, subtree"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Two trees at once: same tree, symmetric, merge, subtree<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Recurse on a pair <code>f(a, b)<\/code>. Base: both None, or exactly one None.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To compare two trees, recurse on a pair of nodes. If both are empty they match. If only one is empty, or the values differ, they don’t. Otherwise compare their children pairwise (for a mirror, pair left with right).<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-pair"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-level","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“Level”, “row”, “right side view”, “zigzag”, “minimum depth”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Level”, “row”, “right side view”, “zigzag”, “minimum depth”","when I see “Level”, “row”, “right side view”, “zigzag”, “minimum depth”","what to do when “Level”, “row”, “right side view”, “zigzag”, “minimum depth”","which pattern for “Level”, “row”, “right side view”, “zigzag”, “minimum depth”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Level”, “row”, “right side view”, “zigzag”, “minimum depth”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>BFS with a deque, and <code>for _ in range(len(q))<\/code> to process one level at a time.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Anything about “levels” is breadth-first: use a queue. At the start of each round the queue holds exactly one level, so loop len(queue) times to process just that level before moving on.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-level"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-found","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“Find” a node in a tree, or the lowest common ancestor","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Find” a node in a tree, or the lowest common ancestor","when I see “Find” a node in a tree, or the lowest common ancestor","what to do when “Find” a node in a tree, or the lowest common ancestor","which pattern for “Find” a node in a tree, or the lowest common ancestor"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Find” a node in a tree, or the lowest common ancestor<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Return the found node (or None) upward. Parent: both sides found → it’s me; one side → pass it up.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each call reports upward “I found p or q here” (the node) or nothing (None). A node that hears back from both sides is where the two paths meet: the lowest common ancestor. If only one side reports, pass that report up.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-found"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-reshape","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"You’re changing the tree’s shape: delete, insert, trim, flatten","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You’re changing the tree’s shape: delete, insert, trim, flatten","when I see You’re changing the tree’s shape: delete, insert, trim, flatten","what to do when You’re changing the tree’s shape: delete, insert, trim, flatten","which pattern for You’re changing the tree’s shape: delete, insert, trim, flatten"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You’re changing the tree’s shape: delete, insert, trim, flatten<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Return the new subtree root and reassign: <code>root.left = f(root.left)<\/code>. Need old pointers? Process in reverse with a <code>prev<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When a call may replace a subtree (deleting a node, trimming), let it return the new subtree root and assign it back: root.left = f(root.left). The parent never needs to know what changed.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-reshape"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-upward","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“Distance K”, “nodes within reach”, anything that walks up toward the parent","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Distance K”, “nodes within reach”, anything that walks up toward the parent","when I see “Distance K”, “nodes within reach”, anything that walks up toward the parent","what to do when “Distance K”, “nodes within reach”, anything that walks up toward the parent","which pattern for “Distance K”, “nodes within reach”, anything that walks up toward the parent"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Distance K”, “nodes within reach”, anything that walks up toward the parent<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>One DFS to build a parent map. Now it’s a graph: BFS from the target.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Tree nodes only point down. If you need to move up too (nodes within distance k), first walk the tree once and record each node’s parent. Now each node has up to three neighbours (left, right, parent) and it’s an ordinary graph search.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-upward"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-prefix","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“Number of paths that sum to k”, starting anywhere and going down","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Number of paths that sum to k”, starting anywhere and going down","when I see “Number of paths that sum to k”, starting anywhere and going down","what to do when “Number of paths that sum to k”, starting anywhere and going down","which pattern for “Number of paths that sum to k”, starting anywhere and going down"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Number of paths that sum to k”, starting anywhere and going down<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Running prefix sum + a hashmap of prefixes on the current path. Add on the way in, remove on the way out.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Paths can start at any node above you. Track the running sum from the root to the current node and a count of earlier running sums on this path. If running − k appeared before, the part between them sums to k. Remove the current sum when you return (backtrack).<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-prefix"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-tuple","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"Each node has two states: rob or skip, take or leave, camera or not","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Each node has two states: rob or skip, take or leave, camera or not","when I see Each node has two states: rob or skip, take or leave, camera or not","what to do when Each node has two states: rob or skip, take or leave, camera or not","which pattern for Each node has two states: rob or skip, take or leave, camera or not"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Each node has two states: rob or skip, take or leave, camera or not<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Return a tuple with one answer per state. The parent combines them.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When each node can be in one of two states (rob it or skip it, camera or no camera), return both answers from every call as a pair. The parent picks the combination it needs, so nothing is recomputed.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-tuple"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-all","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“Return all…”, “list every…”, “generate all possible…”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Return all…”, “list every…”, “generate all possible…”","when I see “Return all…”, “list every…”, “generate all possible…”","what to do when “Return all…”, “list every…”, “generate all possible…”","which pattern for “Return all…”, “list every…”, “generate all possible…”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Return all…”, “list every…”, “generate all possible…”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Backtracking. Start writing the template before you finish reading.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> “Return all” means trying every choice and undoing it afterwards. Choose an option (add it to the path), explore everything after it with a recursive call, then un-choose it (pop) so the next option starts clean.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-all"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-copy","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"res.append(path)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["res.append(path)","when I see res.append(path)","what to do when res.append(path)","which pattern for res.append(path)"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>res.append(path)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Make it <code>res.append(path[:])<\/code>. Every time, no exceptions.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> path is one list that keeps changing as you backtrack. Appending path itself stores a reference to that same changing list, so every saved answer ends up identical (usually empty). Save a copy of how it looks right now.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-copy"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-order","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"Combinations, subsets, “in any order”, {1,2} same as {2,1}","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Combinations, subsets, “in any order”, {1,2} same as {2,1}","when I see Combinations, subsets, “in any order”, {1,2} same as {2,1}","what to do when Combinations, subsets, “in any order”, {1,2} same as {2,1}","which pattern for Combinations, subsets, “in any order”, {1,2} same as {2,1}"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Combinations, subsets, “in any order”, {1,2} same as {2,1}<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Start index: <code>for i in range(start, n)<\/code>. If order matters (permutations), use <code>used[]<\/code> and loop from 0.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If {1, 2} and {2, 1} count as the same answer, only look forward: the next choice starts after the current index. If order matters (permutations), loop over everything each time and skip what’s already used.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-order"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-reuse","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“Unlimited”, “may be chosen more than once”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Unlimited”, “may be chosen more than once”","when I see “Unlimited”, “may be chosen more than once”","what to do when “Unlimited”, “may be chosen more than once”","which pattern for “Unlimited”, “may be chosen more than once”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Unlimited”, “may be chosen more than once”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Recurse with <code>i<\/code>. Otherwise recurse with <code>i + 1<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If a number may be picked again, the next call starts at the same index i. If each can be used once, it starts at i + 1.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-reuse"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-dups","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"Input “may contain duplicates” but results must be unique","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Input “may contain duplicates” but results must be unique","when I see Input “may contain duplicates” but results must be unique","what to do when Input “may contain duplicates” but results must be unique","which pattern for Input “may contain duplicates” but results must be unique"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Input “may contain duplicates” but results must be unique<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>nums.sort()<\/code>, then <code>if i &gt; start and nums[i] == nums[i-1]: continue<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With repeated numbers, two branches at the same level could make the same choice and produce identical answers. Sort first so equal numbers sit together, then at each level skip a number equal to the one just tried.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-dups"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-sortbreak","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"A target sum with positive numbers","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A target sum with positive numbers","when I see A target sum with positive numbers","what to do when A target sum with positive numbers","which pattern for A target sum with positive numbers"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A target sum with positive numbers<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sort first. In the loop: <code>if nums[i] &gt; remaining: break<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With only positive numbers, once a number is bigger than what’s left of the target, every later number in sorted order is too. So sort, and break out of the loop instead of trying them.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-sortbreak"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-constraint","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“Place them so no two conflict” (queens, sudoku, coloring)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Place them so no two conflict” (queens, sudoku, coloring)","when I see “Place them so no two conflict” (queens, sudoku, coloring)","what to do when “Place them so no two conflict” (queens, sudoku, coloring)","which pattern for “Place them so no two conflict” (queens, sudoku, coloring)"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Place them so no two conflict” (queens, sudoku, coloring)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Fill one slot at a time. Keep sets of what’s taken for O(1) checks. Return <code>True<\/code> up the chain if one solution is enough.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Fill one slot at a time (one queen per row). Keep sets of what’s already taken, such as columns and both diagonals, so “is this allowed?” is a quick lookup. Undo the choice when you come back.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-constraint"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-grid","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"A grid and “each cell may be used once”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A grid and “each cell may be used once”","when I see A grid and “each cell may be used once”","what to do when A grid and “each cell may be used once”","which pattern for A grid and “each cell may be used once”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A grid and “each cell may be used once”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Mark the cell (<code>board[r][c] = \"#\"<\/code>), try 4 directions, restore it.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To use each cell at most once, mark it as visited before going deeper (for example, write “#” into it), try the four directions, then put the original letter back so other paths can use it.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-grid"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-split","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“Partition”, “split into pieces”, “restore”, “segment” a string","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Partition”, “split into pieces”, “restore”, “segment” a string","when I see “Partition”, “split into pieces”, “restore”, “segment” a string","what to do when “Partition”, “split into pieces”, “restore”, “segment” a string","which pattern for “Partition”, “split into pieces”, “restore”, “segment” a string"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Partition”, “split into pieces”, “restore”, “segment” a string<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>for end in range(start + 1, len(s) + 1)<\/code>: take <code>s[start:end]<\/code>, validate, recurse from <code>end<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To cut a string into valid pieces, try every possible first piece s[start:end]. If it’s valid, recurse on the rest from end. Reaching the end of the string means one complete answer.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-split"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cxcalls","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“What’s the time complexity of this recursion?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“What’s the time complexity of this recursion?”","when I see “What’s the time complexity of this recursion?”","what to do when “What’s the time complexity of this recursion?”","which pattern for “What’s the time complexity of this recursion?”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“What’s the time complexity of this recursion?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Ask three things: how many calls per call (on what size), how much work per call, then add the tree up level by level.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Three questions give you the complexity of any recursion. How many calls does each call make, and on what size? How much work happens inside one call? Then add up the cost of each level of the call tree.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cxcalls"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cxbranch","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"b recursive calls per call, each shrinking by 1","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["b recursive calls per call, each shrinking by 1","when I see b recursive calls per call, each shrinking by 1","what to do when b recursive calls per call, each shrinking by 1","which pattern for b recursive calls per call, each shrinking by 1"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>b recursive calls per call, each shrinking by 1<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Tree has depth n and ~bⁿ boxes → O(bⁿ) × work per box.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If every call makes b more calls and the size only drops by 1, the tree is n levels deep and roughly bⁿ boxes wide at the bottom: exponential.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cxbranch"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cxhalve","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"One recursive call on half the input (binary search, fast pow)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["One recursive call on half the input (binary search, fast pow)","when I see One recursive call on half the input (binary search, fast pow)","what to do when One recursive call on half the input (binary search, fast pow)","which pattern for One recursive call on half the input (binary search, fast pow)"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>One recursive call on half the input (binary search, fast pow)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>log n levels × work per call → O(log n) if the work is O(1).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> One call on half the input means the size goes n, n/2, n/4, … 1: about log₂ n levels. With constant work per level that’s O(log n).<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cxhalve"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cxtwohalves","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"Two recursive calls on halves","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Two recursive calls on halves","when I see Two recursive calls on halves","what to do when Two recursive calls on halves","which pattern for Two recursive calls on halves"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Two recursive calls on halves<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(n) work per call → every level costs n → O(n log n). O(1) work per call → leaves dominate → O(n).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Two calls on halves give log n levels. What matters is the work per level: if each call does O(n) work, every level costs n in total → O(n log n) (merge sort). If each does O(1), the n leaves dominate → O(n).<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cxtwohalves"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cxtrap","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"f(n//2) * f(n//2) : the same half computed twice","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["f(n//2) * f(n//2) : the same half computed twice","when I see f(n//2) * f(n//2) : the same half computed twice","what to do when f(n//2) * f(n//2) : the same half computed twice","which pattern for f(n//2) * f(n//2) : the same half computed twice"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>f(n//2) * f(n//2) : the same half computed twice<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Store it once: <code>h = f(n//2); h * h<\/code>. Two calls on n/2 is O(n), one is O(log n).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Calling f(n // 2) twice computes the same value twice. Each level doubles the calls, which cancels the halving and gives O(n). Store it once and reuse it for O(log n).<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cxtrap"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cxspace","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"“What’s the space complexity of this recursion?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“What’s the space complexity of this recursion?”","when I see “What’s the space complexity of this recursion?”","what to do when “What’s the space complexity of this recursion?”","which pattern for “What’s the space complexity of this recursion?”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“What’s the space complexity of this recursion?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Max depth of the call tree × frame size, plus memo tables, path lists and other extras. Not the total number of calls.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Only the calls on the current path are in memory at once, so stack space = the depth of the call tree, not the number of calls. Add any extra storage you keep: memo tables, path lists.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cxspace"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cxmemo","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"Memoized recursion or top-down DP","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Memoized recursion or top-down DP","when I see Memoized recursion or top-down DP","what to do when Memoized recursion or top-down DP","which pattern for Memoized recursion or top-down DP"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Memoized recursion or top-down DP<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Time = distinct states × work per state. Space = states (cache) + depth (stack).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With memoization each distinct input is solved once. Time = (number of different inputs) × (work per input). Space = the cache plus the recursion depth.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cxmemo"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cxslice","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"Slicing or copying inside a recursive call ( s[1:] , a[:mid] , path[:] )","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Slicing or copying inside a recursive call ( s[1:] , a[:mid] , path[:] )","when I see Slicing or copying inside a recursive call ( s[1:] , a[:mid] , path[:] )","what to do when Slicing or copying inside a recursive call ( s[1:] , a[:mid] , path[:] )","which pattern for Slicing or copying inside a recursive call ( s[1:] , a[:mid] , path[:] )"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Slicing or copying inside a recursive call ( s[1:] , a[:mid] , path[:] )<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Add O(length) work per call. <code>f(s[1:])<\/code> turns O(n) into O(n²); pass an index instead.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Slicing copies. f(s[1:]) copies n − 1 characters, then n − 2, … which adds up to O(n²). Pass an index instead of a new string or list.<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cxslice"]]},{"i":"rx-recursion-cxbt","k":"rx","s":"recursion","t":"Backtracking that outputs every answer","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Backtracking that outputs every answer","when I see Backtracking that outputs every answer","what to do when Backtracking that outputs every answer","which pattern for Backtracking that outputs every answer"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Backtracking that outputs every answer<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Time ≈ number of answers × cost to copy one: subsets O(n·2ⁿ), permutations O(n·n!), combinations O(k·C(n,k)).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When you must output every answer, the time is at least (number of answers) × (cost to copy one).<\/p><p>From <b>Recursion & Backtracking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html#rx-cxbt"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-python","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"You need a max-heap in Python","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You need a max-heap in Python","when I see You need a max-heap in Python","what to do when You need a max-heap in Python","which pattern for You need a max-heap in Python"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You need a max-heap in Python<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>heapq<\/code> is min-only: push <code>-x<\/code>, read <code>-heap[0]<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Python’s heapq always keeps the smallest on top. To get the largest instead, store negated numbers: the most negative is the largest original. Flip the sign back when you read it.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-python"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-heapify","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"You already have all the items up front","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You already have all the items up front","when I see You already have all the items up front","what to do when You already have all the items up front","which pattern for You already have all the items up front"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You already have all the items up front<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>heapq.heapify(list)<\/code> in O(n), not n pushes.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If you already have all the items, heapify arranges the list into a heap in O(n). Pushing them one by one costs O(log n) each, O(n log n) in total.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-heapify"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-tuples","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"Heap entries need a priority plus payload (nodes, strings)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Heap entries need a priority plus payload (nodes, strings)","when I see Heap entries need a priority plus payload (nodes, strings)","what to do when Heap entries need a priority plus payload (nodes, strings)","which pattern for Heap entries need a priority plus payload (nodes, strings)"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Heap entries need a priority plus payload (nodes, strings)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Push tuples <code>(priority, tiebreak, payload)<\/code>. Use a counter or index as the tiebreak.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A heap compares whole entries. Put the priority first. If two priorities tie, Python compares the next field, and objects like nodes can’t be compared. So add a unique counter as the second field to break ties.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-tuples"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-lazy","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"You need to delete or update an item that’s deep inside the heap","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You need to delete or update an item that’s deep inside the heap","when I see You need to delete or update an item that’s deep inside the heap","what to do when You need to delete or update an item that’s deep inside the heap","which pattern for You need to delete or update an item that’s deep inside the heap"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You need to delete or update an item that’s deep inside the heap<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Lazy deletion: push the new version, and when an outdated entry reaches the top, skip it.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> You can’t cheaply find and change an entry deep inside a heap. Instead push the updated entry, and leave the old one. When an outdated entry reaches the top, notice it’s stale and skip it.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-lazy"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-topk","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"“k largest”, “kth largest”, “top k” from a big list or a stream","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“k largest”, “kth largest”, “top k” from a big list or a stream","when I see “k largest”, “kth largest”, “top k” from a big list or a stream","what to do when “k largest”, “kth largest”, “top k” from a big list or a stream","which pattern for “k largest”, “kth largest”, “top k” from a big list or a stream"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“k largest”, “kth largest”, “top k” from a big list or a stream<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><b>Min<\/b>-heap of size k. Push, and pop when size &gt; k. Root = kth largest.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To keep the k largest, hold a small min-heap of size k. Push every number, and when the heap grows past k, pop the smallest. What stays is the k largest, and the top is the k-th largest.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-topk"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-kclosest","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"“k smallest”, “k closest points”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“k smallest”, “k closest points”","when I see “k smallest”, “k closest points”","what to do when “k smallest”, “k closest points”","which pattern for “k smallest”, “k closest points”"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“k smallest”, “k closest points”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><b>Max<\/b>-heap of size k (negate the key). Pop the farthest when size &gt; k.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For the k smallest it’s the mirror image: keep a max-heap of size k (negate distances in Python). When it overflows, pop the farthest. The k closest remain.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-kclosest"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-mergek","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"“Merge k sorted lists / arrays”, “smallest range covering k lists”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Merge k sorted lists / arrays”, “smallest range covering k lists”","when I see “Merge k sorted lists / arrays”, “smallest range covering k lists”","what to do when “Merge k sorted lists / arrays”, “smallest range covering k lists”","which pattern for “Merge k sorted lists / arrays”, “smallest range covering k lists”"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Merge k sorted lists / arrays”, “smallest range covering k lists”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Heap of (value, which list, index). Pop the min, push that list’s next.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The next smallest overall is always one of the k current fronts. Put the fronts in a min-heap, take the smallest, then push the next item from the list it came from.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-mergek"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-median","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"“Median of a data stream”, sliding median","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Median of a data stream”, sliding median","when I see “Median of a data stream”, sliding median","what to do when “Median of a data stream”, sliding median","which pattern for “Median of a data stream”, sliding median"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Median of a data stream”, sliding median<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Two heaps: max-heap for the lower half, min-heap for the upper. Keep sizes equal or lo one bigger.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Split the numbers into a lower half and an upper half. Keep the lower half in a max-heap and the upper half in a min-heap, with sizes equal or the lower half one bigger. The median is the top of the lower half, or the average of both tops.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-median"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-schedule","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"Resources unlock over time and you always take the best available (IPO, meeting rooms, CPU tasks)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Resources unlock over time and you always take the best available (IPO, meeting rooms, CPU tasks)","when I see Resources unlock over time and you always take the best available (IPO, meeting rooms, CPU tasks)","what to do when Resources unlock over time and you always take the best available (IPO, meeting rooms, CPU tasks)","which pattern for Resources unlock over time and you always take the best available (IPO, meeting rooms, CPU tasks)"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Resources unlock over time and you always take the best available (IPO, meeting rooms, CPU tasks)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sort by unlock time. Push newly available items into a heap keyed by “best”, pop when you act.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When options become available over time and you always want the best available one, sort the options by when they unlock. As time (or money) moves on, push newly unlocked options into a heap ordered by “best”, and pop the best when you act.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-schedule"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-cooldown","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"“Same item can’t repeat within n” (task scheduler, reorganize string)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Same item can’t repeat within n” (task scheduler, reorganize string)","when I see “Same item can’t repeat within n” (task scheduler, reorganize string)","what to do when “Same item can’t repeat within n” (task scheduler, reorganize string)","which pattern for “Same item can’t repeat within n” (task scheduler, reorganize string)"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Same item can’t repeat within n” (task scheduler, reorganize string)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Max-heap by remaining count. Take the most frequent; hold it out (a queue or a prev slot) until it’s allowed back.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Use the most common item first, since it’s the hardest to space out. Keep a max-heap by remaining count. After using an item, hold it aside until its cooldown ends, then put it back.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-cooldown"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-dijkstra","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"Shortest path with non-negative weights","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Shortest path with non-negative weights","when I see Shortest path with non-negative weights","what to do when Shortest path with non-negative weights","which pattern for Shortest path with non-negative weights"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Shortest path with non-negative weights<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Min-heap of (distance, node). Pop the closest, relax its edges, skip stale entries.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Always settle the closest unsettled node next; with no negative edges, nobody can later find a shorter way to it. Keep (distance, node) in a min-heap, and when you pop an entry whose distance is out of date, skip it.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-dijkstra"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-cxhplog","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"heappush / heappop","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["heappush / heappop","when I see heappush / heappop","what to do when heappush / heappop","which pattern for heappush / heappop"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>heappush / heappop<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(log n) each (height of a complete binary tree). heap[0] is O(1).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A heap is a complete binary tree, so its height is about log₂ n. A push or pop moves one item up or down that height. Looking at the top doesn’t move anything.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-cxhplog"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-cxhptopk","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"“k largest / k closest / k most frequent”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“k largest / k closest / k most frequent”","when I see “k largest / k closest / k most frequent”","what to do when “k largest / k closest / k most frequent”","which pattern for “k largest / k closest / k most frequent”"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“k largest / k closest / k most frequent”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Heap capped at size k: O(n log k) time, O(k) space.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With the heap capped at k items, each of the n pushes costs log k, not log n. Better than sorting everything when k is small.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-cxhptopk"]]},{"i":"rx-heap-cxhpify","k":"rx","s":"heap","t":"Building a heap from a full list","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Building a heap from a full list","when I see Building a heap from a full list","what to do when Building a heap from a full list","which pattern for Building a heap from a full list"],"w":"Heap","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Building a heap from a full list<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>heapq.heapify(list)<\/code> is O(n). n separate pushes would be O(n log n).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> heapify fixes the list from the bottom up. Most nodes are near the bottom and barely move, so the total work is O(n), cheaper than n separate pushes.<\/p><p>From <b>Heap<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Heap","heap.html#rx-cxhpify"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-prefix","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"“starts with”, “prefix”, “autocomplete”, many lookups sharing beginnings","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“starts with”, “prefix”, “autocomplete”, many lookups sharing beginnings","when I see “starts with”, “prefix”, “autocomplete”, many lookups sharing beginnings","what to do when “starts with”, “prefix”, “autocomplete”, many lookups sharing beginnings","which pattern for “starts with”, “prefix”, “autocomplete”, many lookups sharing beginnings"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“starts with”, “prefix”, “autocomplete”, many lookups sharing beginnings<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>A trie.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A trie stores words letter by letter in a tree, so words with the same beginning share the same path. “Is there any word starting with ca?” becomes: walk c → a and see whether anything is below. You don’t check every word.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-prefix"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-node","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"You need to write a trie fast in an interview","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You need to write a trie fast in an interview","when I see You need to write a trie fast in an interview","what to do when You need to write a trie fast in an interview","which pattern for You need to write a trie fast in an interview"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You need to write a trie fast in an interview<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Node = <code>children<\/code> dict + <code>end<\/code> flag. Or plain nested dicts with a <code>\"$\"<\/code> key for the end.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A trie node needs only two things: a map from letter to child node, and a flag saying “a word ends here”. In Python, nested dictionaries with a special &quot;$&quot; key work just as well.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-node"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-endflag","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"Both “do” and “dog” are words","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Both “do” and “dog” are words","when I see Both “do” and “dog” are words","what to do when Both “do” and “dog” are words","which pattern for Both “do” and “dog” are words"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Both “do” and “dog” are words<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Mark the end node. <code>search<\/code> checks the flag, <code>startsWith<\/code> doesn’t.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> After inserting “dog”, the path d → o exists too, but “do” was never inserted. The end flag tells them apart: search needs the flag on the last node, startsWith only needs the path.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-endflag"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-counts","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"“How many words start with …”, “sum of values with prefix”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“How many words start with …”, “sum of values with prefix”","when I see “How many words start with …”, “sum of values with prefix”","what to do when “How many words start with …”, “sum of values with prefix”","which pattern for “How many words start with …”, “sum of values with prefix”"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“How many words start with …”, “sum of values with prefix”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Store a counter (or running sum) on every node, updated during insert.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If you’ll ask “how many words start with this?” or “sum of values with this prefix”, keep a running number on every node and update it as each word passes through during insert. A query is then just a walk plus reading one number.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-counts"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-replace","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"“Replace each word with the shortest root from a dictionary”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Replace each word with the shortest root from a dictionary”","when I see “Replace each word with the shortest root from a dictionary”","what to do when “Replace each word with the shortest root from a dictionary”","which pattern for “Replace each word with the shortest root from a dictionary”"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Replace each word with the shortest root from a dictionary”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Trie of roots; walk each word and stop at the first end flag.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Put the root words in a trie. For each word in the sentence, walk its letters down the trie and stop at the first node marked as the end of a root. That shortest root replaces the word; if you fall off the trie, keep the word.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-replace"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-lcp","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"“Longest common prefix” of many strings","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Longest common prefix” of many strings","when I see “Longest common prefix” of many strings","what to do when “Longest common prefix” of many strings","which pattern for “Longest common prefix” of many strings"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Longest common prefix” of many strings<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Walk down while the node has exactly one child and no word ends there.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Insert all the strings. The common prefix is the path from the root as long as there’s only one way to continue and no word has ended yet.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-lcp"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-wildcard","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"Pattern search with “.” matching any letter","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Pattern search with “.” matching any letter","when I see Pattern search with “.” matching any letter","what to do when Pattern search with “.” matching any letter","which pattern for Pattern search with “.” matching any letter"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Pattern search with “.” matching any letter<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>DFS: on a letter follow one child; on “.” try every child; at the end check the flag.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A normal letter means follow that one child. A “.” could be any letter, so try every child with a small DFS. At the end of the pattern, the node must carry the end flag.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-wildcard"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-suggest","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"Type-ahead: “top 3 suggestions after each character”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Type-ahead: “top 3 suggestions after each character”","when I see Type-ahead: “top 3 suggestions after each character”","what to do when Type-ahead: “top 3 suggestions after each character”","which pattern for Type-ahead: “top 3 suggestions after each character”"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Type-ahead: “top 3 suggestions after each character”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sort words, then store up to 3 words on every node during insert. Walk the typed prefix.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For type-ahead, do the work once at insert time. Insert words in sorted order and keep the first 3 that pass through each node. Then each typed prefix is just a walk to a node and a read of its list.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-suggest"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-manywords","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"Find many words in one grid","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Find many words in one grid","when I see Find many words in one grid","what to do when Find many words in one grid","which pattern for Find many words in one grid"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Find many words in one grid<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>One trie of all words + grid DFS that follows trie children.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Searching the grid once per word repeats the same walks. Put all the words in one trie and run one DFS over the grid that follows trie children. A path stops the moment no word has that prefix.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-manywords"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-prune","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"Trie-guided search keeps revisiting finished branches","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Trie-guided search keeps revisiting finished branches","when I see Trie-guided search keeps revisiting finished branches","what to do when Trie-guided search keeps revisiting finished branches","which pattern for Trie-guided search keeps revisiting finished branches"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Trie-guided search keeps revisiting finished branches<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Remove found words (pop the end marker) and delete empty child nodes on the way back.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Once a word is found, it shouldn’t be found again. Remove its end flag, and on the way back delete nodes that no longer have children, so later searches don’t walk dead branches.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-prune"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-xor","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"“Maximum XOR of two numbers”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Maximum XOR of two numbers”","when I see “Maximum XOR of two numbers”","what to do when “Maximum XOR of two numbers”","which pattern for “Maximum XOR of two numbers”"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Maximum XOR of two numbers”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Binary trie of 32-bit numbers, high bit first. For each number, greedily walk the opposite bit when possible.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> XOR is biggest when the two numbers differ in the highest bits. Store the numbers in a trie of bits, highest bit first. For each number, walk down choosing the opposite bit whenever that child exists; that greedily builds the best partner.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-xor"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-notneeded","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"Only exact-word lookups, no prefixes","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Only exact-word lookups, no prefixes","when I see Only exact-word lookups, no prefixes","what to do when Only exact-word lookups, no prefixes","which pattern for Only exact-word lookups, no prefixes"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Only exact-word lookups, no prefixes<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Use a set. Mention the trie only if prefixes or wildcards appear.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A trie earns its keep only when prefixes matter. If you only ask “is this exact word in the list?”, a set is simpler and just as fast.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-notneeded"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-cxtrlen","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"Trie insert / search / startsWith","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Trie insert / search / startsWith","when I see Trie insert / search / startsWith","what to do when Trie insert / search / startsWith","which pattern for Trie insert / search / startsWith"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Trie insert / search / startsWith<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(L) for a word or prefix of length L, independent of how many words are stored.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Insert, search and startsWith each walk one node per letter, so they cost the length of the word. How many words are stored doesn’t matter.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-cxtrlen"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-cxtrspace","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"Space of a trie","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Space of a trie","when I see Space of a trie","what to do when Space of a trie","which pattern for Space of a trie"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Space of a trie<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(total characters) nodes in the worst case, less with shared prefixes; mention the per-node children map (26 array or dict).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> In the worst case every inserted character needs its own node: O(total characters). Shared prefixes reuse nodes, so real tries are smaller. Each node also carries a children map (a dict, or an array of 26).<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-cxtrspace"]]},{"i":"rx-trie-cxtrhash","k":"rx","s":"trie","t":"Trie vs hash set of words","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Trie vs hash set of words","when I see Trie vs hash set of words","what to do when Trie vs hash set of words","which pattern for Trie vs hash set of words"],"w":"Trie","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Trie vs hash set of words<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Exact lookups: a set is simpler (O(L) to hash). Prefix queries, autocomplete, word-search pruning: trie.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For exact lookups a set does the same O(L) work (hashing the word) with less code. Choose a trie for prefix questions, autocomplete and pruning word searches.<\/p><p>From <b>Trie<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Trie","trie.html#rx-cxtrhash"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-gridgraph","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"A 2-D grid with moves to neighbouring cells","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A 2-D grid with moves to neighbouring cells","when I see A 2-D grid with moves to neighbouring cells","what to do when A 2-D grid with moves to neighbouring cells","which pattern for A 2-D grid with moves to neighbouring cells"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A 2-D grid with moves to neighbouring cells<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Treat cells as nodes and the 4 directions as edges. No adjacency list needed.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A grid is already a graph: every cell is a node and it connects to its up, down, left and right neighbours. You don’t build an adjacency list; you generate the 4 neighbours on the fly and skip ones outside the grid.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-gridgraph"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-components","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Number of islands / provinces / connected groups”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Number of islands / provinces / connected groups”","when I see “Number of islands / provinces / connected groups”","what to do when “Number of islands / provinces / connected groups”","which pattern for “Number of islands / provinces / connected groups”"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Number of islands / provinces / connected groups”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Loop over every node; on an unvisited one, count += 1 and flood-fill (DFS/BFS) its whole group.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Visit every cell or node. When you hit one that isn’t visited yet, you’ve found a new group: count it and flood-fill the whole group so it’s marked. The count at the end is the number of groups.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-components"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-multisource","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"Several starting points spreading at once (rotting oranges, walls and gates, distance to nearest 0)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Several starting points spreading at once (rotting oranges, walls and gates, distance to nearest 0)","when I see Several starting points spreading at once (rotting oranges, walls and gates, distance to nearest 0)","what to do when Several starting points spreading at once (rotting oranges, walls and gates, distance to nearest 0)","which pattern for Several starting points spreading at once (rotting oranges, walls and gates, distance to nearest 0)"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Several starting points spreading at once (rotting oranges, walls and gates, distance to nearest 0)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Multi-source BFS: put <b>all<\/b> sources in the queue before the loop.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When several places spread at the same time (all rotten oranges, all gates), put all of them in the queue first. BFS then grows outward from all of them together, one ring per minute.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-multisource"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-bfsshortest","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Minimum steps / moves / transformations” with equal-cost moves","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Minimum steps / moves / transformations” with equal-cost moves","when I see “Minimum steps / moves / transformations” with equal-cost moves","what to do when “Minimum steps / moves / transformations” with equal-cost moves","which pattern for “Minimum steps / moves / transformations” with equal-cost moves"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Minimum steps / moves / transformations” with equal-cost moves<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>BFS. The first time you reach the target is the shortest.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> BFS explores in rings: everything 1 move away, then 2 moves, and so on. So the first time it reaches the target, that’s the fewest moves, as long as every move costs the same.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-bfsshortest"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-markenqueue","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"Writing BFS","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Writing BFS","when I see Writing BFS","what to do when Writing BFS","which pattern for Writing BFS"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Writing BFS<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Mark a node as seen when you <b>enqueue<\/b> it, not when you pop it.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Mark a node as seen at the moment you put it in the queue. If you wait until it’s popped, other nodes can add it again in the meantime, so it gets processed several times.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-markenqueue"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-clone","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Clone / deep copy a graph”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Clone / deep copy a graph”","when I see “Clone / deep copy a graph”","what to do when “Clone / deep copy a graph”","which pattern for “Clone / deep copy a graph”"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Clone / deep copy a graph”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dict old → new. Create the copy before recursing into neighbours; return the existing copy if seen.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Keep a dictionary original → copy. Create a node’s copy and store it before visiting its neighbours. When a neighbour is already in the dictionary, reuse its copy; that also stops infinite loops on cycles.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-clone"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-border","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Surrounded regions”, “which cells reach the ocean / edge”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Surrounded regions”, “which cells reach the ocean / edge”","when I see “Surrounded regions”, “which cells reach the ocean / edge”","what to do when “Surrounded regions”, “which cells reach the ocean / edge”","which pattern for “Surrounded regions”, “which cells reach the ocean / edge”"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Surrounded regions”, “which cells reach the ocean / edge”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Flip it: flood from the border (or ocean) inward, then everything not reached is the answer.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Asking “which cells can’t reach the edge?” is hard. Turn it around: start from the border and flood inward. Everything the flood touches is safe; everything it never reached is the answer.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-border"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-bipartite","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Split into two groups so every edge crosses”, “two-colour the graph”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Split into two groups so every edge crosses”, “two-colour the graph”","when I see “Split into two groups so every edge crosses”, “two-colour the graph”","what to do when “Split into two groups so every edge crosses”, “two-colour the graph”","which pattern for “Split into two groups so every edge crosses”, “two-colour the graph”"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Split into two groups so every edge crosses”, “two-colour the graph”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>BFS/DFS colouring with 0/1. A neighbour with the same colour means False.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Try to colour nodes with two colours so every edge joins different colours. Colour a start node A, its neighbours B, their neighbours A, and so on. If you ever find an edge with the same colour on both ends, it can’t be done.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-bipartite"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-implicit","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"States that transform into other states (word ladder, lock combinations, puzzles)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["States that transform into other states (word ladder, lock combinations, puzzles)","when I see States that transform into other states (word ladder, lock combinations, puzzles)","what to do when States that transform into other states (word ladder, lock combinations, puzzles)","which pattern for States that transform into other states (word ladder, lock combinations, puzzles)"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>States that transform into other states (word ladder, lock combinations, puzzles)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>States are nodes, one move is an edge. BFS over states, generating neighbours on the fly.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sometimes the graph isn’t given. Each possible state (a word, a lock combination) is a node, and one allowed move connects two states. Run BFS over states, creating the neighbours of a state only when you reach it.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-implicit"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-topo","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Prerequisites”, “order of tasks”, “build order”, dependencies","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Prerequisites”, “order of tasks”, “build order”, dependencies","when I see “Prerequisites”, “order of tasks”, “build order”, dependencies","what to do when “Prerequisites”, “order of tasks”, “build order”, dependencies","which pattern for “Prerequisites”, “order of tasks”, “build order”, dependencies"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Prerequisites”, “order of tasks”, “build order”, dependencies<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Kahn’s algorithm: in-degrees, queue of zeros, pop and decrement.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Count how many prerequisites each task still has (its in-degree). Tasks with 0 can be done now, so queue them. Each time you finish one, reduce the counts of the tasks that depended on it; any that drop to 0 join the queue.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-topo"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-cycle","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Is it possible to finish?” / cycle in a directed graph","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Is it possible to finish?” / cycle in a directed graph","when I see “Is it possible to finish?” / cycle in a directed graph","what to do when “Is it possible to finish?” / cycle in a directed graph","which pattern for “Is it possible to finish?” / cycle in a directed graph"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Is it possible to finish?” / cycle in a directed graph<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Run Kahn’s: if fewer than n nodes come out, there’s a cycle. (Or DFS with 3 states.)<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> In a cycle every task waits for another task in the cycle, so none of them ever reaches in-degree 0. If Kahn’s algorithm outputs fewer than n tasks, there’s a cycle and the schedule is impossible.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-cycle"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-unionfind","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"Groups merging as edges arrive; “are x and y connected?” many times","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Groups merging as edges arrive; “are x and y connected?” many times","when I see Groups merging as edges arrive; “are x and y connected?” many times","what to do when Groups merging as edges arrive; “are x and y connected?” many times","which pattern for Groups merging as edges arrive; “are x and y connected?” many times"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Groups merging as edges arrive; “are x and y connected?” many times<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Union-find with path compression (and union by rank/size).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Union-find keeps a “parent” for every item; following parents leads to the group’s representative (root). Merging two groups = point one root at the other. Two items are connected if they have the same root.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-unionfind"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-redundant","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Which edge creates a cycle?”, “redundant connection”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Which edge creates a cycle?”, “redundant connection”","when I see “Which edge creates a cycle?”, “redundant connection”","what to do when “Which edge creates a cycle?”, “redundant connection”","which pattern for “Which edge creates a cycle?”, “redundant connection”"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Which edge creates a cycle?”, “redundant connection”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Process edges in order; the first edge whose endpoints already share a root is the answer.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Add edges one by one with union-find. An edge whose two ends already have the same root connects nodes that were already connected, so it closes a cycle: that’s the extra edge.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-redundant"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-tree","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Is this graph a valid tree?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Is this graph a valid tree?”","when I see “Is this graph a valid tree?”","what to do when “Is this graph a valid tree?”","which pattern for “Is this graph a valid tree?”"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Is this graph a valid tree?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Exactly n − 1 edges and no cycle (union-find never sees a shared root).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A tree on n nodes is connected with no cycles, which means exactly n − 1 edges and union-find never meets an edge whose ends are already joined.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-tree"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-dijkstra","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"Shortest path with non-negative weights","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Shortest path with non-negative weights","when I see Shortest path with non-negative weights","what to do when Shortest path with non-negative weights","which pattern for Shortest path with non-negative weights"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Shortest path with non-negative weights<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Min-heap of (dist, node). Pop, skip if stale, relax neighbours.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Always settle the closest unsettled node next; with no negative edges, nothing found later can be shorter. Keep (distance, node) in a min-heap, relax the edges of each popped node, and skip popped entries that are out of date.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-dijkstra"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-kstops","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"Cheapest path with “at most k stops” (or negative edges)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Cheapest path with “at most k stops” (or negative edges)","when I see Cheapest path with “at most k stops” (or negative edges)","what to do when Cheapest path with “at most k stops” (or negative edges)","which pattern for Cheapest path with “at most k stops” (or negative edges)"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Cheapest path with “at most k stops” (or negative edges)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Bellman-Ford for k + 1 rounds, each round reading a copy of the previous costs.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With a limit on stops, “shortest so far” isn’t enough; a cheaper route might use too many flights. Bellman-Ford in rounds fixes this: round i finds the cheapest prices using at most i flights. Each round reads a copy of the previous round, so no route takes two new flights in one round.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-kstops"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-minimax","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"Minimize the maximum along a path (swim in rising water, min effort path)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Minimize the maximum along a path (swim in rising water, min effort path)","when I see Minimize the maximum along a path (swim in rising water, min effort path)","what to do when Minimize the maximum along a path (swim in rising water, min effort path)","which pattern for Minimize the maximum along a path (swim in rising water, min effort path)"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Minimize the maximum along a path (swim in rising water, min effort path)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dijkstra where a path’s cost is the max edge so far, not the sum.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sometimes a path’s cost is its highest point, not the sum (how deep the water must be to swim it). Dijkstra still works: the cost of reaching a neighbour is max(cost so far, neighbour’s height) instead of a sum.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-minimax"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-mst","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"“Connect all points / cities with minimum total cost”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Connect all points / cities with minimum total cost”","when I see “Connect all points / cities with minimum total cost”","what to do when “Connect all points / cities with minimum total cost”","which pattern for “Connect all points / cities with minimum total cost”"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Connect all points / cities with minimum total cost”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Minimum spanning tree: Prim (heap from one node) or Kruskal (sort edges + union-find).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To connect everything as cheaply as possible, choose cheap edges that don’t form loops. Kruskal: sort all edges by cost and take each one that joins two different groups. Prim: grow one tree, always adding the cheapest edge that leaves it.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-mst"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-cxgrve","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components","when I see BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components","what to do when BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components","which pattern for BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(V + E) time, O(V) space.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> BFS and DFS visit every node once and look at every edge once (twice for undirected graphs): O(V + E). The visited set and the queue or stack hold up to V nodes.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-cxgrve"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-cxgrgrid","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"Traversal on an R × C grid","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Traversal on an R × C grid","when I see Traversal on an R × C grid","what to do when Traversal on an R × C grid","which pattern for Traversal on an R × C grid"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Traversal on an R × C grid<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(R·C) time; O(R·C) visited (or in-place marking); recursion depth up to R·C for DFS.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> On a grid every cell is a node with at most 4 edges, so V + E is about R·C. A DFS can go as deep as the number of cells, which matters for Python’s recursion limit.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-cxgrgrid"]]},{"i":"rx-graphs-cxgrdijk","k":"rx","s":"graphs","t":"Dijkstra with a binary heap","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Dijkstra with a binary heap","when I see Dijkstra with a binary heap","what to do when Dijkstra with a binary heap","which pattern for Dijkstra with a binary heap"],"w":"Graphs","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Dijkstra with a binary heap<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O((V + E) log V) time, O(V + E) space.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each edge can push one entry onto the heap, and each push or pop costs log of the heap size.<\/p><p>From <b>Graphs<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Graphs","graphs.html#rx-cxgrdijk"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-isdp","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"“How many ways”, “minimum / maximum”, “is it possible” + a sequence of choices","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“How many ways”, “minimum / maximum”, “is it possible” + a sequence of choices","when I see “How many ways”, “minimum / maximum”, “is it possible” + a sequence of choices","what to do when “How many ways”, “minimum / maximum”, “is it possible” + a sequence of choices","which pattern for “How many ways”, “minimum / maximum”, “is it possible” + a sequence of choices"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“How many ways”, “minimum / maximum”, “is it possible” + a sequence of choices<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>DP. Before coding, write the state as a sentence.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Dynamic programming fits when you make a sequence of choices and the question is “how many ways”, “what’s the best” or “is it possible”. The same smaller questions come up again and again, so you answer each once and reuse it.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-isdp"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-state","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"You’re stuck on a DP problem","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You’re stuck on a DP problem","when I see You’re stuck on a DP problem","what to do when You’re stuck on a DP problem","which pattern for You’re stuck on a DP problem"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You’re stuck on a DP problem<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Ask: “what’s the minimum I need to know about the past to decide the future?” That’s the state.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The state is the smallest summary of the past that is enough to decide what comes next. For climbing stairs, how you got here doesn’t matter, only which step you’re on, so the state is just i.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-state"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-memofirst","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"The recurrence is clear but the table order isn’t","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["The recurrence is clear but the table order isn’t","when I see The recurrence is clear but the table order isn’t","what to do when The recurrence is clear but the table order isn’t","which pattern for The recurrence is clear but the table order isn’t"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>The recurrence is clear but the table order isn’t<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Write the recursion, add <code>@lru_cache<\/code>, and convert to a table later if needed.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If you can write the recursion but not the table order, don’t fight it. Write the recursive function, add a cache, and you already have a correct DP. Convert it to a loop later only if needed.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-memofirst"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-order","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Writing a bottom-up loop","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Writing a bottom-up loop","when I see Writing a bottom-up loop","what to do when Writing a bottom-up loop","which pattern for Writing a bottom-up loop"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Writing a bottom-up loop<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Fill in the order your recurrence reads: if dp[i] reads dp[i − 1], go up; if it reads dp[i + 1], go down.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A cell can only be filled after the cells it reads. If dp[i] uses dp[i − 1], fill from small i upwards; if it uses dp[i + 1], fill from the end downwards.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-order"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-twovars","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"dp[i] reads only dp[i − 1] and dp[i − 2] (or one previous row)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["dp[i] reads only dp[i − 1] and dp[i − 2] (or one previous row)","when I see dp[i] reads only dp[i − 1] and dp[i − 2] (or one previous row)","what to do when dp[i] reads only dp[i − 1] and dp[i − 2] (or one previous row)","which pattern for dp[i] reads only dp[i − 1] and dp[i − 2] (or one previous row)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>dp[i] reads only dp[i − 1] and dp[i − 2] (or one previous row)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Replace the table with rolling variables or one row.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If each step only looks back one or two cells, you don’t need the whole table. Keep just those last values in variables and shift them forward each step.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-twovars"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-takeskip","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Choose items with a conflict between neighbours (house robber, delete and earn)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Choose items with a conflict between neighbours (house robber, delete and earn)","when I see Choose items with a conflict between neighbours (house robber, delete and earn)","what to do when Choose items with a conflict between neighbours (house robber, delete and earn)","which pattern for Choose items with a conflict between neighbours (house robber, delete and earn)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Choose items with a conflict between neighbours (house robber, delete and earn)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>dp[i] = max(dp[i − 1], dp[i − 2] + value[i])<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> At each item you either skip it (keep the best answer up to the previous item) or take it, which forbids the previous item, so add it to the best answer up to two items back. Take whichever is bigger.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-takeskip"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-unbounded","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Fewest / most items to reach an amount, items reusable (coin change, perfect squares)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Fewest / most items to reach an amount, items reusable (coin change, perfect squares)","when I see Fewest / most items to reach an amount, items reusable (coin change, perfect squares)","what to do when Fewest / most items to reach an amount, items reusable (coin change, perfect squares)","which pattern for Fewest / most items to reach an amount, items reusable (coin change, perfect squares)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Fewest / most items to reach an amount, items reusable (coin change, perfect squares)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>dp[a] = min over c of dp[a − c] + 1<\/code>, with dp[0] = 0 and others ∞.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> dp[a] is the fewest coins to make amount a. The last coin used was some coin c, leaving a − c to make, so try every coin and take the best dp[a − c] + 1. Start with dp[0] = 0 and everything else “impossible” (∞).<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-unbounded"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-combos","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Count ways to reach an amount","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Count ways to reach an amount","when I see Count ways to reach an amount","what to do when Count ways to reach an amount","which pattern for Count ways to reach an amount"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Count ways to reach an amount<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Combinations (order doesn’t matter): loop items <b>outer<\/b>, amounts inner. Orderings count: amounts outer.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When counting ways to reach an amount, the loop order decides whether 1 + 2 and 2 + 1 are the same way. Coins in the outer loop counts each combination once; amounts in the outer loop counts every ordering.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-combos"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-endinghere","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Best subsequence / subarray with a property (LIS, longest chain)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Best subsequence / subarray with a property (LIS, longest chain)","when I see Best subsequence / subarray with a property (LIS, longest chain)","what to do when Best subsequence / subarray with a property (LIS, longest chain)","which pattern for Best subsequence / subarray with a property (LIS, longest chain)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Best subsequence / subarray with a property (LIS, longest chain)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>dp[i]<\/code> = best that <b>ends at<\/b> i. Answer is <code>max(dp)<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Let dp[i] be the best answer that ends exactly at position i. To compute it, look at earlier positions j that can come before i and extend the best one. The final answer is the best over all i, because the best sequence can end anywhere.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-endinghere"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-minmax","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Max product subarray (negatives flip signs)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Max product subarray (negatives flip signs)","when I see Max product subarray (negatives flip signs)","what to do when Max product subarray (negatives flip signs)","which pattern for Max product subarray (negatives flip signs)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Max product subarray (negatives flip signs)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Track both the max and the min ending here.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Multiplying by a negative number turns the smallest product into the largest. So at every position keep both the largest and the smallest product ending there; a negative number swaps their roles.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-minmax"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-segment","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"“Can the string be split into dictionary words?”, decode ways","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Can the string be split into dictionary words?”, decode ways","when I see “Can the string be split into dictionary words?”, decode ways","what to do when “Can the string be split into dictionary words?”, decode ways","which pattern for “Can the string be split into dictionary words?”, decode ways"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Can the string be split into dictionary words?”, decode ways<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>dp[i]<\/code> = answer for the prefix of length i; try every last piece <code>s[j:i]<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> dp[i] answers the question for the first i characters. To fill it, try every possible last piece s[j:i]: if that piece is valid and dp[j] is true (or has ways), the first i characters work too.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-segment"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-palin","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Palindromic substrings / longest palindromic substring","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Palindromic substrings / longest palindromic substring","when I see Palindromic substrings / longest palindromic substring","what to do when Palindromic substrings / longest palindromic substring","which pattern for Palindromic substrings / longest palindromic substring"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Palindromic substrings / longest palindromic substring<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Expand around every center (2n − 1 of them).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Every palindrome has a middle. Try each middle (on a letter, or between two letters: 2n − 1 middles) and expand outward while both ends match. No table is needed.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-palin"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-twostrings","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Two strings: common subsequence, edit distance, interleaving","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Two strings: common subsequence, edit distance, interleaving","when I see Two strings: common subsequence, edit distance, interleaving","what to do when Two strings: common subsequence, edit distance, interleaving","which pattern for Two strings: common subsequence, edit distance, interleaving"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Two strings: common subsequence, edit distance, interleaving<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>dp[i][j]<\/code> over prefixes, table (m + 1) × (n + 1). Compare the last characters.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With two strings, dp[i][j] is the answer for the first i letters of one and the first j letters of the other. Look at the last letters of each: if they match, extend the diagonal answer; otherwise take the best of dropping one letter from either string.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-twostrings"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-gridpaths","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Count paths or min cost in a grid moving right/down","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Count paths or min cost in a grid moving right/down","when I see Count paths or min cost in a grid moving right/down","what to do when Count paths or min cost in a grid moving right/down","which pattern for Count paths or min cost in a grid moving right/down"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Count paths or min cost in a grid moving right/down<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>dp[r][c] = combine(dp[r − 1][c], dp[r][c − 1])<\/code>. First row and column are base cases.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If you can only move right or down, every cell is reached from the cell above or the cell to the left. So its value combines those two (add them for counting, min for cost). The first row and column have only one way in.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-gridpaths"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-editops","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Minimum edits (insert, delete, replace)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Minimum edits (insert, delete, replace)","when I see Minimum edits (insert, delete, replace)","what to do when Minimum edits (insert, delete, replace)","which pattern for Minimum edits (insert, delete, replace)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Minimum edits (insert, delete, replace)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Match: diagonal. Else 1 + min(above = delete, left = insert, diagonal = replace).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Compare the last letters of the two prefixes. If they match, no edit is needed: take the diagonal. If not, try the three edits (delete = cell above, insert = cell to the left, replace = diagonal), each costing 1, and keep the cheapest.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-editops"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-knap","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"0/1 knapsack in one row","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["0/1 knapsack in one row","when I see 0/1 knapsack in one row","what to do when 0/1 knapsack in one row","which pattern for 0/1 knapsack in one row"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>0/1 knapsack in one row<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Loop capacity <b>backwards<\/b>: <code>for c in range(cap, w − 1, −1)<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> In a one-row knapsack, going through capacities from high to low means each cell still reads values from before this item, so each item is used at most once. Going low to high would let the same item be added again.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-knap"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-subsetsum","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"“Partition into equal sums”, “target sum with + and −”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Partition into equal sums”, “target sum with + and −”","when I see “Partition into equal sums”, “target sum with + and −”","what to do when “Partition into equal sums”, “target sum with + and −”","which pattern for “Partition into equal sums”, “target sum with + and −”"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Partition into equal sums”, “target sum with + and −”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Turn it into subset sum: boolean (or counting) 0/1 knapsack over sums.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Splitting into two equal halves means finding a subset that adds up to half the total. Keep track of which sums are reachable; each number either joins a subset or doesn’t.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-subsetsum"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-statemachine","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Stock problems with cooldowns, fees, or k transactions","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Stock problems with cooldowns, fees, or k transactions","when I see Stock problems with cooldowns, fees, or k transactions","what to do when Stock problems with cooldowns, fees, or k transactions","which pattern for Stock problems with cooldowns, fees, or k transactions"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Stock problems with cooldowns, fees, or k transactions<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>One variable per state (holding, sold, resting) and transitions for each day.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Stock problems are easiest as a few states you can be in each day: holding a stock, just sold, or resting. Each day, compute the best profit for every state from yesterday’s states.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-statemachine"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-interval","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"Burst balloons, merge stones, best way to split a range","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Burst balloons, merge stones, best way to split a range","when I see Burst balloons, merge stones, best way to split a range","what to do when Burst balloons, merge stones, best way to split a range","which pattern for Burst balloons, merge stones, best way to split a range"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Burst balloons, merge stones, best way to split a range<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>dp[l][r]<\/code> over ranges, filled by increasing length; try every k as the last (or first) action.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> For problems on a range (burst balloons, split a range), let dp[l][r] be the best answer for just that range. Try every position k as the last thing done inside it, combine the two smaller ranges, and fill shorter ranges before longer ones.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-interval"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-cxdpstates","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"“What’s the complexity of this DP?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“What’s the complexity of this DP?”","when I see “What’s the complexity of this DP?”","what to do when “What’s the complexity of this DP?”","which pattern for “What’s the complexity of this DP?”"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“What’s the complexity of this DP?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Count states (table size), multiply by work per state (how many earlier cells one cell reads).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> DP time = (number of cells in the table) × (work to fill one cell, i.e. how many earlier cells it reads).<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-cxdpstates"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-cxdptrans","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"An inner loop choosing among earlier states (LIS, word break, partition)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["An inner loop choosing among earlier states (LIS, word break, partition)","when I see An inner loop choosing among earlier states (LIS, word break, partition)","what to do when An inner loop choosing among earlier states (LIS, word break, partition)","which pattern for An inner loop choosing among earlier states (LIS, word break, partition)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>An inner loop choosing among earlier states (LIS, word break, partition)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Work per state is O(n) (or O(k) choices) → multiply it in: LIS O(n²), coin change O(amount × coins).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If filling one cell loops over many earlier choices, that loop multiplies the time.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-cxdptrans"]]},{"i":"rx-dp-cxdpspace","k":"rx","s":"dp","t":"A 2-D table where row i only reads row i − 1 (or 1-D reading the last few)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A 2-D table where row i only reads row i − 1 (or 1-D reading the last few)","when I see A 2-D table where row i only reads row i − 1 (or 1-D reading the last few)","what to do when A 2-D table where row i only reads row i − 1 (or 1-D reading the last few)","which pattern for A 2-D table where row i only reads row i − 1 (or 1-D reading the last few)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A 2-D table where row i only reads row i − 1 (or 1-D reading the last few)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Keep only the rows you need: O(m·n) → O(n), or O(n) → O(1).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If each row only reads the row before it, keep two rows (or one) instead of the whole table. If each cell only reads the last one or two, keep two variables.<\/p><p>From <b>Dynamic Programming<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Dynamic Programming","dp.html#rx-cxdpspace"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-greedy","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"You’re tempted to always take the biggest / earliest / cheapest","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You’re tempted to always take the biggest / earliest / cheapest","when I see You’re tempted to always take the biggest / earliest / cheapest","what to do when You’re tempted to always take the biggest / earliest / cheapest","which pattern for You’re tempted to always take the biggest / earliest / cheapest"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You’re tempted to always take the biggest / earliest / cheapest<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Before coding, state why it’s safe (an exchange argument). Can’t? Use DP.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Greedy means taking the locally best option each time and never looking back. It’s only correct if you can argue that swapping the greedy choice into any optimal solution doesn’t make it worse. If you can’t make that argument, or you find a counterexample, use DP.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-greedy"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-reach","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Can you reach the last index?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Can you reach the last index?”","when I see “Can you reach the last index?”","what to do when “Can you reach the last index?”","which pattern for “Can you reach the last index?”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Can you reach the last index?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Track the farthest reachable index; fail if i ever exceeds it.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> You don’t need to try every path. Walk left to right remembering the farthest index you can reach so far. If you ever stand on an index beyond that, you’re stuck.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-reach"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-levels","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Minimum number of jumps”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Minimum number of jumps”","when I see “Minimum number of jumps”","what to do when “Minimum number of jumps”","which pattern for “Minimum number of jumps”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Minimum number of jumps”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>BFS levels without a queue: when i hits the current level’s end, jump and set end = far.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Think of BFS levels. With j jumps you can reach a window of indexes; while scanning that window, compute the farthest point reachable with one more jump. When you reach the window’s end, take the jump and the next window ends at that farthest point.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-levels"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-kadane","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Maximum subarray sum”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Maximum subarray sum”","when I see “Maximum subarray sum”","what to do when “Maximum subarray sum”","which pattern for “Maximum subarray sum”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Maximum subarray sum”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>cur = max(x, cur + x)<\/code>; track the best cur.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> At each number, decide: is the best subarray ending here “just me” or “me added to the best one ending just before”? If the previous run is negative, it only drags you down, so start fresh. Keep the best value you ever see.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-kadane"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-gasreset","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Circular route with gains and costs (gas station)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Circular route with gains and costs (gas station)","when I see Circular route with gains and costs (gas station)","what to do when Circular route with gains and costs (gas station)","which pattern for Circular route with gains and costs (gas station)"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Circular route with gains and costs (gas station)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>If total ≥ 0 an answer exists. Scan once; when the tank goes negative, start after this station.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If total gas ≥ total cost, some start works. Drive from station 0. If the tank goes negative at station i, no station from your start up to i can be the start, so restart just after i with an empty tank.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-gasreset"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-twopass","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Constraints with both neighbours (candy, trapping water)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Constraints with both neighbours (candy, trapping water)","when I see Constraints with both neighbours (candy, trapping water)","what to do when Constraints with both neighbours (candy, trapping water)","which pattern for Constraints with both neighbours (candy, trapping water)"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Constraints with both neighbours (candy, trapping water)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Left-to-right pass for the left neighbour, right-to-left pass for the right, combine with max.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A rule involving both neighbours is easier as two one-sided rules. The left-to-right pass satisfies “more than my left neighbour”, the right-to-left pass satisfies “more than my right neighbour”, and taking the max of both satisfies both.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-twopass"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-lastocc","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Partition so each letter appears in at most one part”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Partition so each letter appears in at most one part”","when I see “Partition so each letter appears in at most one part”","what to do when “Partition so each letter appears in at most one part”","which pattern for “Partition so each letter appears in at most one part”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Partition so each letter appears in at most one part”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Record each letter’s last index. Extend the current part’s end to the last index of every letter seen; cut when i == end.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A part can only end once every letter in it has appeared for the last time. Record the last position of each letter. Walk the string, stretching the current part’s end to the last position of every letter you see; when you reach that end, cut.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-lastocc"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-smallestfirst","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Group cards into consecutive runs (hand of straights)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Group cards into consecutive runs (hand of straights)","when I see Group cards into consecutive runs (hand of straights)","what to do when Group cards into consecutive runs (hand of straights)","which pattern for Group cards into consecutive runs (hand of straights)"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Group cards into consecutive runs (hand of straights)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sorted counter; the smallest remaining card must start a run.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The smallest card left can’t be in the middle of a run (nothing smaller is left), so it must start one. Take it and the next size − 1 consecutive cards, and repeat.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-smallestfirst"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-lohi","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Wildcards that could be several things (“*” as “(”, “)” or empty)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Wildcards that could be several things (“*” as “(”, “)” or empty)","when I see Wildcards that could be several things (“*” as “(”, “)” or empty)","what to do when Wildcards that could be several things (“*” as “(”, “)” or empty)","which pattern for Wildcards that could be several things (“*” as “(”, “)” or empty)"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Wildcards that could be several things (“*” as “(”, “)” or empty)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Track a range [lo, hi] of possible states instead of branching.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A “*” could be “(”, “)” or nothing; branching on all three explodes. Instead track the range of possible open-bracket counts: lo (fewest) and hi (most). If hi drops below 0 it’s invalid; clamp lo at 0; at the end lo must be 0.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-lohi"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-sortdiff","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Split people between two options at different costs (two city scheduling)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Split people between two options at different costs (two city scheduling)","when I see Split people between two options at different costs (two city scheduling)","what to do when Split people between two options at different costs (two city scheduling)","which pattern for Split people between two options at different costs (two city scheduling)"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Split people between two options at different costs (two city scheduling)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sort by cost difference; the first half takes option A.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Ask how much each person saves by going to A instead of B (cost A − cost B). Sort by that difference: the people with the most negative values save the most by going to A, so the first half goes to A and the rest to B.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-sortdiff"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-mergestart","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Merge overlapping intervals”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Merge overlapping intervals”","when I see “Merge overlapping intervals”","what to do when “Merge overlapping intervals”","which pattern for “Merge overlapping intervals”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Merge overlapping intervals”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sort by start. If s ≤ last end, extend; else append.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sort by start time; then overlapping intervals sit next to each other. Walk them: if the next one starts before (or when) the last merged one ends, stretch that end. Otherwise start a new merged interval.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-mergestart"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-keepend","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Max non-overlapping”, “remove the fewest”, “fewest arrows”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Max non-overlapping”, “remove the fewest”, “fewest arrows”","when I see “Max non-overlapping”, “remove the fewest”, “fewest arrows”","what to do when “Max non-overlapping”, “remove the fewest”, “fewest arrows”","which pattern for “Max non-overlapping”, “remove the fewest”, “fewest arrows”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Max non-overlapping”, “remove the fewest”, “fewest arrows”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sort by <b>end<\/b>. Keep an interval if it starts after the last kept end.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To fit the most non-overlapping intervals, always keep the one that ends earliest; it leaves the most room for the rest. Sort by end, and keep each interval that starts after the last kept one ends.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-keepend"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-overlap","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Do two intervals overlap?","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Do two intervals overlap?","when I see Do two intervals overlap?","what to do when Do two intervals overlap?","which pattern for Do two intervals overlap?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Do two intervals overlap?<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>a.start &lt;= b.end and b.start &lt;= a.end<\/code>. Check whether touching ends count (≤ vs &lt;).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Two intervals overlap when each one starts before the other ends. Decide up front whether intervals that only touch (one ends exactly when the next starts) count as overlapping.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-overlap"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-rooms","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Minimum rooms / platforms / max overlap at once”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Minimum rooms / platforms / max overlap at once”","when I see “Minimum rooms / platforms / max overlap at once”","what to do when “Minimum rooms / platforms / max overlap at once”","which pattern for “Minimum rooms / platforms / max overlap at once”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Minimum rooms / platforms / max overlap at once”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Sweep sorted starts and ends (or a min-heap of end times).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Count how many meetings are running at once at their busiest moment. Sort start times and end times separately and sweep through time: a start needs a room, an end frees one. The largest count you see is the number of rooms.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-rooms"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-insert3","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Insert an interval into a sorted, non-overlapping list","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Insert an interval into a sorted, non-overlapping list","when I see Insert an interval into a sorted, non-overlapping list","what to do when Insert an interval into a sorted, non-overlapping list","which pattern for Insert an interval into a sorted, non-overlapping list"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Insert an interval into a sorted, non-overlapping list<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Three phases: copy those ending before it, absorb those overlapping it, copy the rest.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The list is already sorted and non-overlapping, so the new interval splits it into three parts: intervals entirely before it (copy them), intervals touching it (merge them all into one), and intervals entirely after it (copy them).<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-insert3"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-queries","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Many queries about intervals (smallest interval containing q)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Many queries about intervals (smallest interval containing q)","when I see Many queries about intervals (smallest interval containing q)","what to do when Many queries about intervals (smallest interval containing q)","which pattern for Many queries about intervals (smallest interval containing q)"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Many queries about intervals (smallest interval containing q)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Answer offline: sort queries and intervals, sweep, keep a heap of candidates and drop expired ones.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Answer the queries in sorted order instead of the order asked (then put answers back). As the query value grows, add intervals that have started into a heap keyed by size, and drop ones that have already ended. The heap top answers the query.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-queries"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-cxgisort","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Sort intervals by start/end, then one pass”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Sort intervals by start/end, then one pass”","when I see “Sort intervals by start/end, then one pass”","what to do when “Sort intervals by start/end, then one pass”","which pattern for “Sort intervals by start/end, then one pass”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Sort intervals by start/end, then one pass”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(n log n) total: the sort dominates the O(n) sweep.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Sorting costs O(n log n) and the sweep afterwards is O(n). The sort is the bigger cost, so the total is O(n log n).<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-cxgisort"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-cxgiheap","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Interval scheduling with a min-heap of end times (meeting rooms, CPU tasks)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Interval scheduling with a min-heap of end times (meeting rooms, CPU tasks)","when I see Interval scheduling with a min-heap of end times (meeting rooms, CPU tasks)","what to do when Interval scheduling with a min-heap of end times (meeting rooms, CPU tasks)","which pattern for Interval scheduling with a min-heap of end times (meeting rooms, CPU tasks)"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Interval scheduling with a min-heap of end times (meeting rooms, CPU tasks)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(n log n) time, O(n) heap space.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each interval is pushed onto the heap and popped at most once, and each heap operation costs log n.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-cxgiheap"]]},{"i":"rx-greedy-intervals-cxgipair","k":"rx","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Checking every interval against every other","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Checking every interval against every other","when I see Checking every interval against every other","what to do when Checking every interval against every other","which pattern for Checking every interval against every other"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Checking every interval against every other<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>That’s O(n²). Sort so related intervals sit next to each other, then sweep.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Comparing every interval with every other is n × n work. After sorting, intervals that interact are neighbours, so one sweep is enough.<\/p><p>From <b>Greedy & Intervals<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Greedy & Intervals","greedy-intervals.html#rx-cxgipair"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-xorpairs","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"“Every element appears twice except one”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Every element appears twice except one”","when I see “Every element appears twice except one”","what to do when “Every element appears twice except one”","which pattern for “Every element appears twice except one”"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Every element appears twice except one”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>XOR everything together.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> XOR has two handy rules: x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x, and order doesn’t matter. So XOR-ing everything makes every pair cancel out, and only the single number is left.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-xorpairs"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-lowclear","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"Count set bits, or remove them one by one","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Count set bits, or remove them one by one","when I see Count set bits, or remove them one by one","what to do when Count set bits, or remove them one by one","which pattern for Count set bits, or remove them one by one"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Count set bits, or remove them one by one<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>n &amp;= n − 1<\/code> in a loop.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> n − 1 flips the lowest 1-bit of n to 0 and the zeros below it to 1s. So n &amp; (n − 1) is n with its lowest 1 removed. Repeat until n is 0, counting the loops: that’s the number of 1s.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-lowclear"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-lowbit","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"You need the lowest set bit of x (Fenwick trees, splitting groups)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You need the lowest set bit of x (Fenwick trees, splitting groups)","when I see You need the lowest set bit of x (Fenwick trees, splitting groups)","what to do when You need the lowest set bit of x (Fenwick trees, splitting groups)","which pattern for You need the lowest set bit of x (Fenwick trees, splitting groups)"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You need the lowest set bit of x (Fenwick trees, splitting groups)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>x &amp; −x<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> In two’s complement, −x is “flip every bit, then add 1”. Every bit above the lowest 1 ends up opposite in x and −x, so x &amp; −x keeps only the lowest 1-bit.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-lowbit"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-pow2","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"“Is n a power of two?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Is n a power of two?”","when I see “Is n a power of two?”","what to do when “Is n a power of two?”","which pattern for “Is n a power of two?”"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Is n a power of two?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>n &gt; 0 and n &amp; (n − 1) == 0<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A power of two has exactly one 1-bit. Removing the lowest 1 (n &amp; (n − 1)) leaves 0 only in that case. Check n &gt; 0 too, because 0 also gives 0.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-pow2"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-checkbit","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"Test, set, clear or toggle bit i","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Test, set, clear or toggle bit i","when I see Test, set, clear or toggle bit i","what to do when Test, set, clear or toggle bit i","which pattern for Test, set, clear or toggle bit i"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Test, set, clear or toggle bit i<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Test <code>x &gt;&gt; i &amp; 1<\/code>. Set <code>x | 1 &lt;&lt; i<\/code>. Clear <code>x &amp; ~(1 &lt;&lt; i)<\/code>. Toggle <code>x ^ 1 &lt;&lt; i<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> 1 &lt;&lt; i is a number with only bit i set. AND with it to test the bit, OR to set it, AND with its inverse to clear it, XOR to flip it.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-checkbit"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-missingxor","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"“One number missing from 0…n”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“One number missing from 0…n”","when I see “One number missing from 0…n”","what to do when “One number missing from 0…n”","which pattern for “One number missing from 0…n”"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“One number missing from 0…n”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>XOR all indexes 0…n with all values.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> XOR every index 0 … n together with every value in the array. Each number that’s present appears twice (once as an index, once as a value) and cancels; the missing one appears only once and survives.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-missingxor"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-pymask","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"Bit tricks with negatives in Python (sum without +, reverse bits)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Bit tricks with negatives in Python (sum without +, reverse bits)","when I see Bit tricks with negatives in Python (sum without +, reverse bits)","what to do when Bit tricks with negatives in Python (sum without +, reverse bits)","which pattern for Bit tricks with negatives in Python (sum without +, reverse bits)"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Bit tricks with negatives in Python (sum without +, reverse bits)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Mask with <code>0xFFFFFFFF<\/code> to emulate 32 bits; convert back with <code>~(a ^ MASK)<\/code> if above 0x7FFFFFFF.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Python integers never overflow, and a negative number behaves as if it had infinitely many 1s in front. To imitate 32-bit integers, keep only the low 32 bits with &amp; 0xFFFFFFFF, and convert results above 0x7FFFFFFF back to negative.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-pymask"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-masks","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"Every subset of a small set (n ≤ 20), or “visited set” as part of a DP state","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Every subset of a small set (n ≤ 20), or “visited set” as part of a DP state","when I see Every subset of a small set (n ≤ 20), or “visited set” as part of a DP state","what to do when Every subset of a small set (n ≤ 20), or “visited set” as part of a DP state","which pattern for Every subset of a small set (n ≤ 20), or “visited set” as part of a DP state"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Every subset of a small set (n ≤ 20), or “visited set” as part of a DP state<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Loop <code>mask<\/code> over 0 … 2ⁿ − 1; item i is chosen when <code>mask &gt;&gt; i &amp; 1<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With n small items, each subset is an n-bit number: bit i = 1 means item i is in the subset. Counting from 0 to 2ⁿ − 1 lists every subset exactly once.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-masks"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-dpbits","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"“Count bits for every number 0…n”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Count bits for every number 0…n”","when I see “Count bits for every number 0…n”","what to do when “Count bits for every number 0…n”","which pattern for “Count bits for every number 0…n”"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Count bits for every number 0…n”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>dp[i] = dp[i &gt;&gt; 1] + (i &amp; 1)<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> i &gt;&gt; 1 is i without its last bit, a smaller number you’ve already counted. So bits(i) = bits(i &gt;&gt; 1) + (the last bit of i).<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-dpbits"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-split","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"“Two numbers appear once, the rest twice”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Two numbers appear once, the rest twice”","when I see “Two numbers appear once, the rest twice”","what to do when “Two numbers appear once, the rest twice”","which pattern for “Two numbers appear once, the rest twice”"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Two numbers appear once, the rest twice”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>XOR all → a ^ b. Take its lowest set bit; XOR each group (bit set / not set) separately.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> XOR everything to get a ^ b (the two singles). They differ in at least one bit, the lowest 1-bit of a ^ b. Split all numbers by that bit: a and b fall into different groups, pairs stay together, and XOR-ing each group gives one single.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-split"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-mod3","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"“Every element appears three times except one”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Every element appears three times except one”","when I see “Every element appears three times except one”","what to do when “Every element appears three times except one”","which pattern for “Every element appears three times except one”"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Every element appears three times except one”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Count each bit position mod 3 (or the ones/twos trick).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Look at one bit position at a time and add up that bit over all numbers. Numbers that appear three times contribute a multiple of 3, so the count mod 3 is exactly the single number’s bit.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-mod3"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-commonprefix","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"“Bitwise AND of all numbers in [left, right]”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Bitwise AND of all numbers in [left, right]”","when I see “Bitwise AND of all numbers in [left, right]”","what to do when “Bitwise AND of all numbers in [left, right]”","which pattern for “Bitwise AND of all numbers in [left, right]”"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Bitwise AND of all numbers in [left, right]”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Shift both right until equal, then shift back.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> ANDing a whole range of numbers keeps only the leading bits that all of them share; every lower bit is 0 somewhere in the range. So shift left and right until they’re equal (that’s the shared prefix), then shift it back.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-commonprefix"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-cxbtlog","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"A loop doing n >>= 1 or n //= 2","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A loop doing n >>= 1 or n //= 2","when I see A loop doing n >>= 1 or n //= 2","what to do when A loop doing n >>= 1 or n //= 2","which pattern for A loop doing n >>= 1 or n //= 2"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A loop doing n &gt;&gt;= 1 or n //= 2<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>O(log n) rounds; O(1) if n is a fixed 32/64-bit integer.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each n &gt;&gt;= 1 removes one binary digit, and n has about log₂ n digits. For a fixed 32- or 64-bit integer that’s at most 32 or 64 steps, a constant.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-cxbtlog"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-cxbtkern","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"n &= n - 1 in a loop","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["n &= n - 1 in a loop","when I see n &= n - 1 in a loop","what to do when n &= n - 1 in a loop","which pattern for n &= n - 1 in a loop"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>n &amp;= n - 1 in a loop<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Runs once per set bit: O(popcount) ≤ O(log n).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> n &amp;= n − 1 removes one 1-bit per loop, so the loop runs once per 1-bit, not once per bit position.<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-cxbtkern"]]},{"i":"rx-bits-cxbtrange","k":"rx","s":"bits","t":"A bit answer for every number 0…n","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A bit answer for every number 0…n","when I see A bit answer for every number 0…n","what to do when A bit answer for every number 0…n","which pattern for A bit answer for every number 0…n"],"w":"Bit Manipulation","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A bit answer for every number 0…n<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Reuse smaller answers (<code>i &gt;&gt; 1<\/code> or <code>i &amp; (i − 1)<\/code>) for O(n) total.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When you need an answer for every number up to n, build each one from a smaller number you’ve already solved (i &gt;&gt; 1, or i &amp; (i − 1)). Each takes O(1), so all of them take O(n).<\/p><p>From <b>Bit Manipulation<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Bit Manipulation","bits.html#rx-cxbtrange"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-start-whatchanges","k":"rx","s":"sd-start","t":"Any design question, big or small","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Any design question, big or small","when I see Any design question, big or small","what to do when Any design question, big or small","which pattern for Any design question, big or small"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Any design question, big or small<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Ask “what will change?” first, and name the exact thing (channel, pricing rule, storage, traffic).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Good design starts by spotting what is likely to change. Before drawing anything, name it concretely: “new notification channels will be added”, “pricing rules differ per city”, “traffic will grow 10×”. That decides where you need flexibility.<\/p><p>From <b>Start Here: Design Thinking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Start Here: Design Thinking","sd-start.html#rx-whatchanges"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-start-hideit","k":"rx","s":"sd-start","t":"You’ve found the part that changes","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["You’ve found the part that changes","when I see You’ve found the part that changes","what to do when You’ve found the part that changes","which pattern for You’ve found the part that changes"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>You’ve found the part that changes<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Put it behind an interface (code) or an API/queue (system), and pass the implementation in from outside.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Once you know what changes, wrap it so the rest of the code doesn’t care which version it gets. In code that’s an interface; in a system it’s an API or a queue. Hand the actual implementation in from outside, so swapping it needs no other edits.<\/p><p>From <b>Start Here: Design Thinking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Start Here: Design Thinking","sd-start.html#rx-hideit"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-start-readuml","k":"rx","s":"sd-start","t":"A class diagram","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A class diagram","when I see A class diagram","what to do when A class diagram","which pattern for A class diagram"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A class diagram<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Read each line as Java: ▷ solid = extends, ▷ dashed = implements, ◆ = owns a field, ⇢ dashed = uses as a parameter.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each arrow in a class diagram is a line of Java. Read them one by one: a solid line with a hollow triangle means extends, a dashed one means implements, a filled diamond means “owns this as a field”, and a dashed open arrow means “uses it, e.g. as a parameter”.<\/p><p>From <b>Start Here: Design Thinking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Start Here: Design Thinking","sd-start.html#rx-readuml"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-start-diamond","k":"rx","s":"sd-start","t":"Composition (◆) or aggregation (◇)?","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Composition (◆) or aggregation (◇)?","when I see Composition (◆) or aggregation (◇)?","what to do when Composition (◆) or aggregation (◇)?","which pattern for Composition (◆) or aggregation (◇)?"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Composition (◆) or aggregation (◇)?<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Does the owner create the part and does the part die with it? Yes → ◆ composition. Passed in and shared → ◇ aggregation.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Ask who creates the part and whether it can outlive the owner. If the owner creates it and it dies with the owner (a car’s engine), that’s composition ◆. If it’s passed in and can be shared or exist alone (a team’s players), that’s aggregation ◇.<\/p><p>From <b>Start Here: Design Thinking<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Start Here: Design Thinking","sd-start.html#rx-diamond"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-pillars","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"“Explain the four pillars of OOP”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Explain the four pillars of OOP”","when I see “Explain the four pillars of OOP”","what to do when “Explain the four pillars of OOP”","which pattern for “Explain the four pillars of OOP”"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Explain the four pillars of OOP”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>One line each with an example: encapsulation (private balance + withdraw rule), abstraction (interface hides how), inheritance (reuse a parent), polymorphism (one call, many behaviours).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Give one sentence and one tiny example per pillar. Encapsulation: data is private and changed only through methods with rules. Abstraction: callers use what it does, not how. Inheritance: a child reuses a parent. Polymorphism: one call does different things depending on the object.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-pillars"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-passvalue","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"“Is Java pass-by-reference?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Is Java pass-by-reference?”","when I see “Is Java pass-by-reference?”","what to do when “Is Java pass-by-reference?”","which pattern for “Is Java pass-by-reference?”"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Is Java pass-by-reference?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>No. Always pass-by-value. For objects, the value copied is the reference, so a method can mutate the object but can’t repoint the caller’s variable.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Java always copies the argument. For an object, what gets copied is the reference (the address), so both variables point at the same object: changes to the object are visible to the caller, but reassigning the parameter only changes the copy.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-passvalue"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-equals","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"Comparing objects, or using objects as HashMap keys","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Comparing objects, or using objects as HashMap keys","when I see Comparing objects, or using objects as HashMap keys","what to do when Comparing objects, or using objects as HashMap keys","which pattern for Comparing objects, or using objects as HashMap keys"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Comparing objects, or using objects as HashMap keys<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b><code>==<\/code> compares references; <code>equals()<\/code> compares values. Override <code>equals<\/code> and <code>hashCode<\/code> together.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> == asks “are these the very same object?”. equals() asks “do they mean the same value?”. Hash maps first use hashCode() to find the bucket, then equals() inside it, so if you override one you must override the other, or equal keys get lost.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-equals"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-access","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"Choosing an access modifier","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Choosing an access modifier","when I see Choosing an access modifier","what to do when Choosing an access modifier","which pattern for Choosing an access modifier"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Choosing an access modifier<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Start <code>private<\/code>. Widen only when needed: package-private → protected → public. Expose methods, not fields.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Make everything private by default and open it up only when something outside truly needs it. Let callers go through methods, so you can add checks or change the internals later without breaking them.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-access"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-static","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"static , final , this","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["static , final , this","when I see static , final , this","what to do when static , final , this","which pattern for static , final , this"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>static , final , this<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>static = belongs to the class (one copy). final = assign once (variable), no override (method), no subclass (class). this = the current object.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> static belongs to the class itself: one shared copy, usable without an object. final means “fixed”: a variable can’t be reassigned, a method can’t be overridden, a class can’t be extended. this is the current object.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-static"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-ifaceabs","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"Interface or abstract class?","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Interface or abstract class?","when I see Interface or abstract class?","what to do when Interface or abstract class?","which pattern for Interface or abstract class?"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Interface or abstract class?<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Capability many unrelated classes share → interface. Shared state or a fixed algorithm across a family → abstract class.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Use an interface for a capability that unrelated classes can have (Comparable, Payable). Use an abstract class when related classes share fields or a fixed series of steps (a base Report with a shared header/footer).<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-ifaceabs"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-dispatch","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"Overriding vs overloading","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Overriding vs overloading","when I see Overriding vs overloading","what to do when Overriding vs overloading","which pattern for Overriding vs overloading"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Overriding vs overloading<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Overriding: same signature in a subclass, chosen at runtime by the object. Overloading: same name, different parameters, chosen at compile time by declared types.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Overriding is the same method redefined in a subclass; Java picks the version by the real object at runtime. Overloading is several methods with the same name but different parameters; Java picks one by the declared types when compiling.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-dispatch"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-generics","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"A collection or helper that should work for any type, safely","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A collection or helper that should work for any type, safely","when I see A collection or helper that should work for any type, safely","what to do when A collection or helper that should work for any type, safely","which pattern for A collection or helper that should work for any type, safely"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A collection or helper that should work for any type, safely<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Generics <code>&lt;T&gt;<\/code>. Bound with <code>T extends X<\/code> when you need X’s methods. PECS: <code>? extends<\/code> to read, <code>? super<\/code> to write.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Generics let one class or method work with any type while the compiler still checks types for you. Add a bound when you need methods of a type; for wildcards remember PECS: a Producer you read from is ? extends, a Consumer you write into is ? super.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-generics"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-enums","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"A fixed set of values (vehicle types, states, operations)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A fixed set of values (vehicle types, states, operations)","when I see A fixed set of values (vehicle types, states, operations)","what to do when A fixed set of values (vehicle types, states, operations)","which pattern for A fixed set of values (vehicle types, states, operations)"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A fixed set of values (vehicle types, states, operations)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Enum with fields and methods, or constant-specific bodies. Use <code>EnumMap<\/code> for lookups.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When the values are a fixed list known in advance, use an enum instead of strings or ints: typos become compile errors. Enums can carry fields and methods, and EnumMap is a fast map keyed by them.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-enums"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-hasa","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"Tempted to extend a class just to reuse code","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Tempted to extend a class just to reuse code","when I see Tempted to extend a class just to reuse code","what to do when Tempted to extend a class just to reuse code","which pattern for Tempted to extend a class just to reuse code"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Tempted to extend a class just to reuse code<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Prefer composition (HAS-A) and delegate. Inherit only for a true IS-A that respects the parent’s contract.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Inheritance ties you to everything the parent does and promises. If you only want to reuse some code, keep an instance of the other class as a field and call it (HAS-A). Inherit only when the child truly IS-A parent everywhere the parent is used.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-hasa"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-java-coupling","k":"rx","s":"sd-java","t":"“Is this a good design?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Is this a good design?”","when I see “Is this a good design?”","what to do when “Is this a good design?”","which pattern for “Is this a good design?”"],"w":"Java & OOP","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Is this a good design?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Aim for low coupling (depend on interfaces, inject dependencies) and high cohesion (each class does one focused job).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Low coupling: classes know little about each other (they depend on interfaces and receive their dependencies), so a change in one doesn’t ripple. High cohesion: everything inside a class serves one purpose.<\/p><p>From <b>Java & OOP<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Java & OOP","sd-java.html#rx-coupling"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-srp","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"A class that changes for unrelated reasons (maths, formatting, storage)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A class that changes for unrelated reasons (maths, formatting, storage)","when I see A class that changes for unrelated reasons (maths, formatting, storage)","what to do when A class that changes for unrelated reasons (maths, formatting, storage)","which pattern for A class that changes for unrelated reasons (maths, formatting, storage)"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A class that changes for unrelated reasons (maths, formatting, storage)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Split by reason to change: one class per actor who could request a change.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Ask who could request a change to this class. If the finance team (calculations), the design team (formatting) and the DBA (storage) could each force edits, it has three jobs. Split it so each class has one reason to change.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-srp"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-godclass","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"A 1,000-line Manager / Util / Service class","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A 1,000-line Manager / Util / Service class","when I see A 1,000-line Manager / Util / Service class","what to do when A 1,000-line Manager / Util / Service class","which pattern for A 1,000-line Manager / Util / Service class"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A 1,000-line Manager / Util / Service class<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>List its responsibilities, then extract each into a focused class the original delegates to.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A giant Manager or Util class is many classes glued together. List what it does, give each responsibility its own small class, and let the old class delegate to them, so callers don’t break while you refactor.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-godclass"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-ocp","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"“We’ll need to add more types later”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“We’ll need to add more types later”","when I see “We’ll need to add more types later”","what to do when “We’ll need to add more types later”","which pattern for “We’ll need to add more types later”"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“We’ll need to add more types later”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Put the varying behaviour behind an interface. New type = new class, no edits to existing code.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Open for extension, closed for modification. If new types will arrive, put the varying behaviour behind an interface. Adding a type then means writing a new class, not editing code that already works and is tested.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-ocp"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-switchtype","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"switch / if-else on a type field that keeps growing","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["switch / if-else on a type field that keeps growing","when I see switch / if-else on a type field that keeps growing","what to do when switch / if-else on a type field that keeps growing","which pattern for switch / if-else on a type field that keeps growing"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>switch / if-else on a type field that keeps growing<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Replace conditional with polymorphism: interface + one class per branch (Strategy).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A switch on a type field grows with every new type and is copied into several places. Replace it with an interface and one class per case; each class holds its own behaviour, and adding a case touches nothing else.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-switchtype"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-lsp","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"A subclass that weakens the parent’s promise (throws, ignores input, changes meaning)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A subclass that weakens the parent’s promise (throws, ignores input, changes meaning)","when I see A subclass that weakens the parent’s promise (throws, ignores input, changes meaning)","what to do when A subclass that weakens the parent’s promise (throws, ignores input, changes meaning)","which pattern for A subclass that weakens the parent’s promise (throws, ignores input, changes meaning)"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A subclass that weakens the parent’s promise (throws, ignores input, changes meaning)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>It isn’t a true IS-A. Use separate classes under a smaller shared interface.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A subclass must work everywhere its parent is expected, keeping every promise the parent makes. If it throws for a normal call, ignores input or changes the meaning, it isn’t really a subtype; give both classes a smaller shared interface instead.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-lsp"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-throwoverride","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"An override that throws UnsupportedOperationException","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["An override that throws UnsupportedOperationException","when I see An override that throws UnsupportedOperationException","what to do when An override that throws UnsupportedOperationException","which pattern for An override that throws UnsupportedOperationException"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>An override that throws UnsupportedOperationException<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Smell for LSP and ISP. Split the interface so the class never inherits that method.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> An override that throws UnsupportedOperationException says “I was forced to have a method I can’t support”. That’s a sign the interface is too big or the inheritance is wrong. Split the interface so the class never receives that method.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-throwoverride"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-isp","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"An interface where implementers leave methods empty","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["An interface where implementers leave methods empty","when I see An interface where implementers leave methods empty","what to do when An interface where implementers leave methods empty","which pattern for An interface where implementers leave methods empty"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>An interface where implementers leave methods empty<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Break it into small role interfaces; a class implements only the roles it plays.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If classes implement an interface but leave some methods empty, the interface is doing too much. Break it into small role interfaces, so each class implements only what it really does.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-isp"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-dip","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"Business logic depends on a concrete DB, API client or framework class","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Business logic depends on a concrete DB, API client or framework class","when I see Business logic depends on a concrete DB, API client or framework class","what to do when Business logic depends on a concrete DB, API client or framework class","which pattern for Business logic depends on a concrete DB, API client or framework class"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Business logic depends on a concrete DB, API client or framework class<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Define an interface on the business side; inject the implementation through the constructor.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Business rules shouldn’t depend on a specific database or API. The business side defines the interface it needs (OrderRepository), the details implement it (MySqlOrderRepository), and the implementation is passed in through the constructor.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-dip"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-newconcrete","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"new ConcreteThing() inside a service method","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["new ConcreteThing() inside a service method","when I see new ConcreteThing() inside a service method","what to do when new ConcreteThing() inside a service method","which pattern for new ConcreteThing() inside a service method"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>new ConcreteThing() inside a service method<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Move creation out: constructor injection, or a factory if creation depends on input.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Calling new ConcreteThing() inside a service glues the service to that class and makes it hard to test. Receive the object through the constructor instead; if what to create depends on input, ask a factory.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-newconcrete"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-solid-overdo","k":"rx","s":"sd-solid","t":"Tempted to add an interface for every class “for SOLID”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Tempted to add an interface for every class “for SOLID”","when I see Tempted to add an interface for every class “for SOLID”","what to do when Tempted to add an interface for every class “for SOLID”","which pattern for Tempted to add an interface for every class “for SOLID”"],"w":"SOLID","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Tempted to add an interface for every class “for SOLID”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Add abstraction where variation is real or likely (payments, storage, channels). Keep the rest concrete.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Interfaces are useful where things really vary (payment providers, storage, channels). An interface for every class with exactly one implementation just adds files to jump through. Abstract the parts that change, keep the rest simple.<\/p><p>From <b>SOLID<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open SOLID","sd-solid.html#rx-overdo"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-singleton","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"Exactly one instance per process (config, pool, registry)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Exactly one instance per process (config, pool, registry)","when I see Exactly one instance per process (config, pool, registry)","what to do when Exactly one instance per process (config, pool, registry)","which pattern for Exactly one instance per process (config, pool, registry)"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Exactly one instance per process (config, pool, registry)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Singleton, but prefer injecting that single instance over calling <code>X.getInstance()<\/code> everywhere.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Some things must exist exactly once per process (configuration, a connection pool). A singleton enforces that. Still, give it to the classes that need it through their constructors rather than calling X.getInstance() everywhere; hidden global access is hard to test.<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-singleton"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-singletonimpl","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"“Write a thread-safe singleton”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Write a thread-safe singleton”","when I see “Write a thread-safe singleton”","what to do when “Write a thread-safe singleton”","which pattern for “Write a thread-safe singleton”"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Write a thread-safe singleton”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Enum singleton (simplest, safe against reflection and serialization) or the holder idiom for lazy init. Mention DCL with <code>volatile<\/code> if asked.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The simplest thread-safe singleton in Java is an enum with one value: the JVM guarantees one instance, even against reflection and serialization tricks. For lazy creation, the holder idiom loads the instance only when first asked.<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-singletonimpl"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-volatile","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"Double-checked locking without volatile","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Double-checked locking without volatile","when I see Double-checked locking without volatile","what to do when Double-checked locking without volatile","which pattern for Double-checked locking without volatile"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Double-checked locking without volatile<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Bug. Add <code>volatile<\/code> so the reference is published only after the constructor finishes.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Without volatile, another thread can see the instance reference before the constructor has finished writing its fields, and use a half-built object. volatile makes the reference visible only after construction completes.<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-volatile"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-simplefactory","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"Which class to create depends on input (a string, an enum, config)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Which class to create depends on input (a string, an enum, config)","when I see Which class to create depends on input (a string, an enum, config)","what to do when Which class to create depends on input (a string, an enum, config)","which pattern for Which class to create depends on input (a string, an enum, config)"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Which class to create depends on input (a string, an enum, config)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Factory method <code>of(type)<\/code> returning the interface. Keep all the <code>new<\/code> calls in one place.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When which class to create depends on input (a string, an enum, a config value), put that decision in one factory method that returns the interface. The rest of the code never writes new SpecificThing() or repeats the if-else.<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-simplefactory"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-factorymethod","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"A base class runs a fixed algorithm but subclasses decide which object it uses","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A base class runs a fixed algorithm but subclasses decide which object it uses","when I see A base class runs a fixed algorithm but subclasses decide which object it uses","what to do when A base class runs a fixed algorithm but subclasses decide which object it uses","which pattern for A base class runs a fixed algorithm but subclasses decide which object it uses"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A base class runs a fixed algorithm but subclasses decide which object it uses<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Factory Method: an abstract <code>createX()<\/code> the subclass overrides.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The parent class runs a fixed process but leaves one creation step abstract. Each subclass overrides that step to create its own kind of object, so the process is shared and only the object differs.<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-factorymethod"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-abstractfactory","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"Several related objects that must match (theme, platform, region)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Several related objects that must match (theme, platform, region)","when I see Several related objects that must match (theme, platform, region)","what to do when Several related objects that must match (theme, platform, region)","which pattern for Several related objects that must match (theme, platform, region)"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Several related objects that must match (theme, platform, region)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Abstract Factory: one interface with a create method per product; one implementation per family.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When several objects must come from the same family (all dark-theme widgets, all AWS services), one factory interface creates each of them, and one implementation exists per family. Choosing the factory chooses a consistent set.<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-abstractfactory"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-builder","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"Constructor with many optional parameters, or several booleans in a row","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Constructor with many optional parameters, or several booleans in a row","when I see Constructor with many optional parameters, or several booleans in a row","what to do when Constructor with many optional parameters, or several booleans in a row","which pattern for Constructor with many optional parameters, or several booleans in a row"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Constructor with many optional parameters, or several booleans in a row<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Builder: fluent setters returning <code>this<\/code>, validate in <code>build()<\/code>, produce an immutable object.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A constructor with many optional parameters is unreadable: new Pizza(true, false, true, 2, null). A builder names each option, lets you set only what you need, checks everything in build(), and returns a finished, unchangeable object.<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-builder"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-prototype","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"Many similar objects that are expensive to set up","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Many similar objects that are expensive to set up","when I see Many similar objects that are expensive to set up","what to do when Many similar objects that are expensive to set up","which pattern for Many similar objects that are expensive to set up"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Many similar objects that are expensive to set up<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Prototype: configure one, then copy it (copy constructor or <code>copy()<\/code>).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If creating an object is expensive or fiddly (lots of configuration, loading data), set one up carefully and make copies of it. Each copy is then adjusted a little.<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-prototype"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-deepcopy","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"Copying an object that holds lists, maps or other mutable objects","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Copying an object that holds lists, maps or other mutable objects","when I see Copying an object that holds lists, maps or other mutable objects","what to do when Copying an object that holds lists, maps or other mutable objects","which pattern for Copying an object that holds lists, maps or other mutable objects"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Copying an object that holds lists, maps or other mutable objects<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Deep copy the mutable parts (new list, new map, copied children). Prefer a copy constructor to <code>clone()<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A shallow copy shares the inner lists and maps with the original, so changing one changes the other. For a real copy, create new lists or maps and copy their contents too. A copy constructor makes this explicit and is safer than clone().<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-deepcopy"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-creational-newinlogic","k":"rx","s":"sd-creational","t":"Business code full of new ConcreteX()","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Business code full of new ConcreteX()","when I see Business code full of new ConcreteX()","what to do when Business code full of new ConcreteX()","which pattern for Business code full of new ConcreteX()"],"w":"Creational Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Business code full of new ConcreteX()<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Move creation to a factory or to the composition root and inject.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If business methods are full of new ConcreteX(), creation is mixed with logic. Do the creating in one place (a factory, or the main setup code) and pass the finished objects in.<\/p><p>From <b>Creational Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Creational Patterns","sd-creational.html#rx-newinlogic"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-structural-decorator","k":"rx","s":"sd-structural","t":"Add optional features in any combination, at runtime","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Add optional features in any combination, at runtime","when I see Add optional features in any combination, at runtime","what to do when Add optional features in any combination, at runtime","which pattern for Add optional features in any combination, at runtime"],"w":"Structural Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Add optional features in any combination, at runtime<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Decorator: each feature wraps the same interface, delegates inward, then adds its part.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each optional feature is a wrapper that implements the same interface as the thing it wraps. It forwards the call inward, then adds its own bit. Stack wrappers in any combination at runtime.<\/p><p>From <b>Structural Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html#rx-decorator"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-structural-proxy","k":"rx","s":"sd-structural","t":"Control access to an object: lazy loading, caching, permissions, remote calls","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Control access to an object: lazy loading, caching, permissions, remote calls","when I see Control access to an object: lazy loading, caching, permissions, remote calls","what to do when Control access to an object: lazy loading, caching, permissions, remote calls","which pattern for Control access to an object: lazy loading, caching, permissions, remote calls"],"w":"Structural Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Control access to an object: lazy loading, caching, permissions, remote calls<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Proxy: same interface as the real object, holds it, and decides when or whether to forward.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A proxy looks exactly like the real object (same interface) but stands in front of it, deciding when or whether to pass calls on: create it only when first needed, return a cached answer, check permissions, or forward over the network.<\/p><p>From <b>Structural Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html#rx-proxy"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-structural-adapter","k":"rx","s":"sd-structural","t":"A third-party or legacy class has the wrong interface","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A third-party or legacy class has the wrong interface","when I see A third-party or legacy class has the wrong interface","what to do when A third-party or legacy class has the wrong interface","which pattern for A third-party or legacy class has the wrong interface"],"w":"Structural Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A third-party or legacy class has the wrong interface<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Adapter: implement your interface, translate calls to theirs.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When a library’s class doesn’t match the interface your code expects, write a small adapter that implements your interface and translates each call into the library’s calls. Your code stays unaware of the library.<\/p><p>From <b>Structural Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html#rx-adapter"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-structural-facade","k":"rx","s":"sd-structural","t":"Clients must call many subsystems in the right order","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Clients must call many subsystems in the right order","when I see Clients must call many subsystems in the right order","what to do when Clients must call many subsystems in the right order","which pattern for Clients must call many subsystems in the right order"],"w":"Structural Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Clients must call many subsystems in the right order<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Facade: one higher-level method that orchestrates them.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If clients must call several subsystems in a careful order (reserve stock, charge, ship), give them one simple method that does the whole sequence. The subsystems remain available for advanced use.<\/p><p>From <b>Structural Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html#rx-facade"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-structural-composite","k":"rx","s":"sd-structural","t":"A tree where leaves and groups support the same operation (files/folders, menu/submenu, org chart)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A tree where leaves and groups support the same operation (files/folders, menu/submenu, org chart)","when I see A tree where leaves and groups support the same operation (files/folders, menu/submenu, org chart)","what to do when A tree where leaves and groups support the same operation (files/folders, menu/submenu, org chart)","which pattern for A tree where leaves and groups support the same operation (files/folders, menu/submenu, org chart)"],"w":"Structural Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A tree where leaves and groups support the same operation (files/folders, menu/submenu, org chart)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Composite: common interface; the group implements it by looping over children.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When a tree mixes single items and groups (files and folders), give both the same interface. A group implements it by asking each child and combining the answers, so callers never check which kind they hold.<\/p><p>From <b>Structural Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html#rx-composite"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-structural-bridge","k":"rx","s":"sd-structural","t":"Two independent dimensions of variation (shape × renderer, notification × channel)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Two independent dimensions of variation (shape × renderer, notification × channel)","when I see Two independent dimensions of variation (shape × renderer, notification × channel)","what to do when Two independent dimensions of variation (shape × renderer, notification × channel)","which pattern for Two independent dimensions of variation (shape × renderer, notification × channel)"],"w":"Structural Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Two independent dimensions of variation (shape × renderer, notification × channel)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Bridge: one hierarchy holds a reference to the other.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If something varies along two independent axes (shape × renderer, message type × channel), subclasses for every pair explode. Make one hierarchy hold a reference to the other: shapes have a renderer, and the two grow separately.<\/p><p>From <b>Structural Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html#rx-bridge"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-structural-flyweight","k":"rx","s":"sd-structural","t":"Huge numbers of objects that repeat the same heavy data","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Huge numbers of objects that repeat the same heavy data","when I see Huge numbers of objects that repeat the same heavy data","what to do when Huge numbers of objects that repeat the same heavy data","which pattern for Huge numbers of objects that repeat the same heavy data"],"w":"Structural Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Huge numbers of objects that repeat the same heavy data<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Flyweight: share immutable intrinsic state from a factory cache; pass extrinsic state per use.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When thousands of objects repeat the same heavy data (font glyphs, tree textures), store that shared data once in a cache and let objects point to it. Pass the per-object bits (position, colour) in when used.<\/p><p>From <b>Structural Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html#rx-flyweight"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-structural-combos","k":"rx","s":"sd-structural","t":"Class names like MilkSugarWhipCoffee or RedCircleVector","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Class names like MilkSugarWhipCoffee or RedCircleVector","when I see Class names like MilkSugarWhipCoffee or RedCircleVector","what to do when Class names like MilkSugarWhipCoffee or RedCircleVector","which pattern for Class names like MilkSugarWhipCoffee or RedCircleVector"],"w":"Structural Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Class names like MilkSugarWhipCoffee or RedCircleVector<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Combination explosion. Use composition: Decorator for stackable add-ons, Bridge for two axes.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Class names that list feature combinations (MilkSugarWhipCoffee) multiply: every new feature doubles the classes. Use objects that combine at runtime instead: decorators for stackable extras, a bridge when there are two separate dimensions.<\/p><p>From <b>Structural Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Structural Patterns","sd-structural.html#rx-combos"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-behavioral-strategy","k":"rx","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"An if-else or switch choosing an algorithm (pricing, sorting, routing, compression)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["An if-else or switch choosing an algorithm (pricing, sorting, routing, compression)","when I see An if-else or switch choosing an algorithm (pricing, sorting, routing, compression)","what to do when An if-else or switch choosing an algorithm (pricing, sorting, routing, compression)","which pattern for An if-else or switch choosing an algorithm (pricing, sorting, routing, compression)"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>An if-else or switch choosing an algorithm (pricing, sorting, routing, compression)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Strategy: interface for the algorithm, one class (or lambda) per variant, injected.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When a method picks between algorithms with if-else (pricing per city, sort order, route type), make each algorithm its own small class behind one interface, and pass in the one to use. Adding an algorithm never touches the others.<\/p><p>From <b>Behavioral Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html#rx-strategy"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-behavioral-observer","k":"rx","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"“When X happens, notify A, B and C” (and more later)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“When X happens, notify A, B and C” (and more later)","when I see “When X happens, notify A, B and C” (and more later)","what to do when “When X happens, notify A, B and C” (and more later)","which pattern for “When X happens, notify A, B and C” (and more later)"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“When X happens, notify A, B and C” (and more later)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Observer: listeners subscribe to the subject; the subject loops and calls them.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When one event must trigger reactions in several places (and more will be added), let them subscribe. The subject keeps a list of listeners and calls each one when the event happens; it doesn’t need to know what they do.<\/p><p>From <b>Behavioral Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html#rx-observer"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-behavioral-command","k":"rx","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Undo/redo, queues of jobs, macros, audit log of actions","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Undo/redo, queues of jobs, macros, audit log of actions","when I see Undo/redo, queues of jobs, macros, audit log of actions","what to do when Undo/redo, queues of jobs, macros, audit log of actions","which pattern for Undo/redo, queues of jobs, macros, audit log of actions"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Undo/redo, queues of jobs, macros, audit log of actions<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Command: wrap each action in an object with <code>execute()<\/code> (and <code>undo()<\/code>).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Turn each action into an object with execute() (and undo()). Now actions can be stored, queued, logged, replayed, or undone by keeping a history stack.<\/p><p>From <b>Behavioral Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html#rx-command"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-behavioral-iterator","k":"rx","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Traverse a custom collection without exposing its internals","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Traverse a custom collection without exposing its internals","when I see Traverse a custom collection without exposing its internals","what to do when Traverse a custom collection without exposing its internals","which pattern for Traverse a custom collection without exposing its internals"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Traverse a custom collection without exposing its internals<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Implement <code>Iterable&lt;T&gt;<\/code> and return an <code>Iterator<\/code>.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Let callers loop over your collection without knowing how it’s stored inside. Implement Iterable&lt;T&gt; and return an Iterator, and then a for-each loop just works.<\/p><p>From <b>Behavioral Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html#rx-iterator"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-behavioral-state","k":"rx","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Behaviour depends on a mode, with rules about which mode comes next","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Behaviour depends on a mode, with rules about which mode comes next","when I see Behaviour depends on a mode, with rules about which mode comes next","what to do when Behaviour depends on a mode, with rules about which mode comes next","which pattern for Behaviour depends on a mode, with rules about which mode comes next"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Behaviour depends on a mode, with rules about which mode comes next<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>State: one class per state; each method returns the next state; the context delegates.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If behaviour depends on a mode with rules about which mode comes next (a vending machine: idle → has coin → dispensing), make each mode a class. Each handles the actions its own way and returns the next mode; the main object just delegates to its current mode.<\/p><p>From <b>Behavioral Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html#rx-state"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-behavioral-cor","k":"rx","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"A request must pass a sequence of checks, any of which may stop it","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A request must pass a sequence of checks, any of which may stop it","when I see A request must pass a sequence of checks, any of which may stop it","what to do when A request must pass a sequence of checks, any of which may stop it","which pattern for A request must pass a sequence of checks, any of which may stop it"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A request must pass a sequence of checks, any of which may stop it<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Chain of Responsibility: handlers linked with <code>next<\/code>; each processes or stops.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When a request goes through a series of checks (auth → rate limit → validation), make each check a handler that either stops the request or passes it to the next one. You can reorder, add or remove checks without touching the others.<\/p><p>From <b>Behavioral Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html#rx-cor"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-behavioral-template","k":"rx","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Several classes share the same algorithm skeleton but differ in a step","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Several classes share the same algorithm skeleton but differ in a step","when I see Several classes share the same algorithm skeleton but differ in a step","what to do when Several classes share the same algorithm skeleton but differ in a step","which pattern for Several classes share the same algorithm skeleton but differ in a step"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Several classes share the same algorithm skeleton but differ in a step<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Template Method: <code>final<\/code> skeleton in an abstract class, abstract hook methods.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When several classes run the same steps and differ only in one or two of them, write the steps once in a parent method (marked final) and leave the differing steps as abstract methods that subclasses fill in.<\/p><p>From <b>Behavioral Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html#rx-template"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-behavioral-mediator","k":"rx","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Many objects talking to each other directly (N×N links)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Many objects talking to each other directly (N×N links)","when I see Many objects talking to each other directly (N×N links)","what to do when Many objects talking to each other directly (N×N links)","which pattern for Many objects talking to each other directly (N×N links)"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Many objects talking to each other directly (N×N links)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Mediator: they all talk to one hub that routes messages.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If many objects all talk to each other directly, the links multiply (every pair is connected) and every change ripples. Route them through one hub that knows the rules: each object only talks to the hub.<\/p><p>From <b>Behavioral Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html#rx-mediator"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-behavioral-memento","k":"rx","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Save and restore an object’s state (undo, checkpoints) without exposing internals","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Save and restore an object’s state (undo, checkpoints) without exposing internals","when I see Save and restore an object’s state (undo, checkpoints) without exposing internals","what to do when Save and restore an object’s state (undo, checkpoints) without exposing internals","which pattern for Save and restore an object’s state (undo, checkpoints) without exposing internals"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Save and restore an object’s state (undo, checkpoints) without exposing internals<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Memento: the object creates an opaque snapshot and can restore from it; a caretaker stores them.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To support undo without exposing an object’s private fields, the object itself creates a sealed snapshot (memento) and can later restore from one. Someone else (the caretaker) just stores the snapshots without looking inside.<\/p><p>From <b>Behavioral Patterns<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Behavioral Patterns","sd-behavioral.html#rx-memento"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-clarify","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"“Design a parking lot” (or any LLD prompt)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Design a parking lot” (or any LLD prompt)","when I see “Design a parking lot” (or any LLD prompt)","what to do when “Design a parking lot” (or any LLD prompt)","which pattern for “Design a parking lot” (or any LLD prompt)"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Design a parking lot” (or any LLD prompt)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Spend 3–5 minutes on scope: actors, 3–5 use cases, what’s out of scope. Write them down.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Don’t start drawing classes straight away. Spend a few minutes agreeing on the scope: who uses the system, the 3–5 things it must do, and what you’re leaving out. Write that list down; it stops you from designing the wrong thing.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-clarify"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-nouns","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"A list of use cases","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A list of use cases","when I see A list of use cases","what to do when A list of use cases","which pattern for A list of use cases"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A list of use cases<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Nouns → classes, verbs → methods on the class that owns the data.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Read your use cases out loud. The nouns (lot, level, spot, ticket, vehicle) become classes; the verbs (park, pay, exit) become methods, placed on the class that owns the data the method needs.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-nouns"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-enums","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"A fixed set of categories (vehicle type, spot size, seat class, coin)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A fixed set of categories (vehicle type, spot size, seat class, coin)","when I see A fixed set of categories (vehicle type, spot size, seat class, coin)","what to do when A fixed set of categories (vehicle type, spot size, seat class, coin)","which pattern for A fixed set of categories (vehicle type, spot size, seat class, coin)"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A fixed set of categories (vehicle type, spot size, seat class, coin)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Use an <code>enum<\/code>, optionally with fields and methods.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Categories that come from a fixed list (vehicle type, spot size, seat class) should be enums, not strings. The compiler catches typos, and the enum can carry data like a rate.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-enums"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-policy","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"Rules the business will change (pricing, spot allocation, dispatch)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Rules the business will change (pricing, spot allocation, dispatch)","when I see Rules the business will change (pricing, spot allocation, dispatch)","what to do when Rules the business will change (pricing, spot allocation, dispatch)","which pattern for Rules the business will change (pricing, spot allocation, dispatch)"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Rules the business will change (pricing, spot allocation, dispatch)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Strategy interface, injected into the main class.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Anything the business will tweak (pricing, which spot to give, which elevator answers) goes behind a strategy interface passed into the main class. Changing the rule then means swapping one object, not editing the lot.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-policy"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-modes","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"An object whose allowed actions depend on what happened before (ATM, vending machine, order)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["An object whose allowed actions depend on what happened before (ATM, vending machine, order)","when I see An object whose allowed actions depend on what happened before (ATM, vending machine, order)","what to do when An object whose allowed actions depend on what happened before (ATM, vending machine, order)","which pattern for An object whose allowed actions depend on what happened before (ATM, vending machine, order)"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>An object whose allowed actions depend on what happened before (ATM, vending machine, order)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>State pattern: one class per state, transitions returned by methods.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If what’s allowed depends on what happened earlier (an ATM can’t dispense before a PIN is entered), make each situation a state class. Each one handles actions its own way and says which state comes next.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-modes"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-lotmanager","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"Temptation to make ParkingLot a singleton","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Temptation to make ParkingLot a singleton","when I see Temptation to make ParkingLot a singleton","what to do when Temptation to make ParkingLot a singleton","which pattern for Temptation to make ParkingLot a singleton"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Temptation to make ParkingLot a singleton<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Mention one instance per process, but create it once and inject it. Don’t call <code>getInstance()<\/code> everywhere.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> It’s fine that only one ParkingLot exists per process, but don’t make every class fetch it via getInstance(). Create it once at startup and pass it to whoever needs it; tests can then use a different one.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-lotmanager"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-atmchain","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"Dispense an amount using several note denominations","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Dispense an amount using several note denominations","when I see Dispense an amount using several note denominations","what to do when Dispense an amount using several note denominations","which pattern for Dispense an amount using several note denominations"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Dispense an amount using several note denominations<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Chain of Responsibility from the largest note down; each takes <code>amount / note<\/code> notes and passes the remainder.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To pay out an amount in notes, start with the largest note: take as many as fit, then pass the remainder to the next smaller note’s handler, and so on. Each handler only knows its own note.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-atmchain"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-scan","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"Elevator request scheduling","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Elevator request scheduling","when I see Elevator request scheduling","what to do when Elevator request scheduling","which pattern for Elevator request scheduling"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Elevator request scheduling<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>SCAN: keep direction, serve requests ahead in sorted order (TreeSet), reverse when none are ahead.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> An elevator works like a lift sweeping a building: keep going in the current direction, stopping at requested floors ahead in order, and turn around only when nothing is left ahead. A sorted set of requested floors makes “next stop ahead” quick.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-scan"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-showseat","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"Seat booking for movies, flights, events","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Seat booking for movies, flights, events","when I see Seat booking for movies, flights, events","what to do when Seat booking for movies, flights, events","which pattern for Seat booking for movies, flights, events"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Seat booking for movies, flights, events<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Model <code>ShowSeat<\/code> (seat × show) with status FREE/HELD/BOOKED and a hold expiry.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A seat exists once, but its availability differs per show. So model ShowSeat (seat × show) with a status FREE / HELD / BOOKED and a hold expiry time, so abandoned holds free themselves.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-showseat"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-checkact","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"if (available) take() on shared state","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["if (available) take() on shared state","when I see if (available) take() on shared state","what to do when if (available) take() on shared state","which pattern for if (available) take() on shared state"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>if (available) take() on shared state<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Race. Make it atomic: lock, CAS (<code>putIfAbsent<\/code>, <code>AtomicInteger<\/code>) or a conditional DB update.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> “If it’s free, take it” written as two steps lets two threads both see “free” before either takes it, and both succeed. Make the check and the take a single atomic step.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-checkact"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-multiserver","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"The same race across several app servers","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["The same race across several app servers","when I see The same race across several app servers","what to do when The same race across several app servers","which pattern for The same race across several app servers"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>The same race across several app servers<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>JVM locks don’t help. Use the DB (conditional UPDATE / row lock / unique constraint) or a distributed lock with TTL.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Java locks only protect threads inside one server. With several servers, the shared truth is the database: let it decide with a conditional UPDATE, a row lock or a unique constraint. Use a distributed lock with an expiry if there’s no database to lean on.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-multiserver"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-optimistic","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"Conflicts are rare and you don’t want to hold locks","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Conflicts are rare and you don’t want to hold locks","when I see Conflicts are rare and you don’t want to hold locks","what to do when Conflicts are rare and you don’t want to hold locks","which pattern for Conflicts are rare and you don’t want to hold locks"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Conflicts are rare and you don’t want to hold locks<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Optimistic locking: version column, <code>UPDATE … WHERE version = ?<\/code>, retry on 0 rows.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When conflicts are rare, don’t lock while the user thinks. Read the row with its version number; when saving, update only if the version is still the same. If 0 rows change, someone else won: reload and retry or tell the user.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-optimistic"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-lld-idempotent","k":"rx","s":"sd-lld","t":"Retries on payment or booking (network timeouts, double clicks)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Retries on payment or booking (network timeouts, double clicks)","when I see Retries on payment or booking (network timeouts, double clicks)","what to do when Retries on payment or booking (network timeouts, double clicks)","which pattern for Retries on payment or booking (network timeouts, double clicks)"],"w":"LLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Retries on payment or booking (network timeouts, double clicks)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Send an idempotency key (booking id); the server stores results by key and returns the same result on repeats.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Networks time out and users double-click, so the same payment or booking request can arrive twice. The client sends a unique key with it; the server stores the result under that key and, if the key shows up again, returns the saved result instead of doing it twice.<\/p><p>From <b>LLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open LLD Problems","sd-lld.html#rx-idempotent"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-reqfirst","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"“Design X” and a blank whiteboard","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Design X” and a blank whiteboard","when I see “Design X” and a blank whiteboard","what to do when “Design X” and a blank whiteboard","which pattern for “Design X” and a blank whiteboard"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Design X” and a blank whiteboard<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Requirements first: 3–4 core features, then scale, latency and consistency needs. Confirm before drawing.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Before drawing boxes, agree on what you’re building: the 3–4 core features and the scale and quality targets. Say them back to the interviewer and get a nod. A design for the wrong requirements scores nothing.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-reqfirst"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-nfr","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"Non-functional requirements","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Non-functional requirements","when I see Non-functional requirements","what to do when Non-functional requirements","which pattern for Non-functional requirements"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Non-functional requirements<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Ask: DAU and peak QPS, read/write ratio, latency target, availability vs strong consistency, data size and retention.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Non-functional requirements are the numbers and qualities that shape the design: how many users and requests at peak, reads vs writes, how fast it must respond, whether it must always be up or always be exactly correct, and how much data it keeps.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-nfr"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-estimate","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"Need numbers fast","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Need numbers fast","when I see Need numbers fast","what to do when Need numbers fast","which pattern for Need numbers fast"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Need numbers fast<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>1 day ≈ 10<sup>5<\/sup> s. QPS = daily requests / 10<sup>5<\/sup>, peak ×2–3. Storage = writes/day × size × 365 × years.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A day has about 100,000 seconds (86,400). So requests per second ≈ requests per day ÷ 100,000, and peak is 2–3× that. Storage per year ≈ writes per day × size × 365.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-estimate"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-stateless","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"App servers must scale horizontally","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["App servers must scale horizontally","when I see App servers must scale horizontally","what to do when App servers must scale horizontally","which pattern for App servers must scale horizontally"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>App servers must scale horizontally<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Make them stateless: sessions in Redis or JWT, files in object storage, no local caches that must agree.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> To add servers freely, any server must be able to handle any request. So keep nothing on the server that the next request needs: sessions go to Redis or a signed token, files to object storage.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-stateless"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-lb","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"More than one app server, or a single point of failure","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["More than one app server, or a single point of failure","when I see More than one app server, or a single point of failure","what to do when More than one app server, or a single point of failure","which pattern for More than one app server, or a single point of failure"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>More than one app server, or a single point of failure<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Put a load balancer in front with health checks; run at least two of everything.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> One server is a single point of failure and a ceiling. Put a load balancer in front of several servers; it spreads traffic and stops sending to servers that fail health checks. Run at least two of everything, including the load balancer.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-lb"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-lbalgo","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"Choosing a load-balancing algorithm","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Choosing a load-balancing algorithm","when I see Choosing a load-balancing algorithm","what to do when Choosing a load-balancing algorithm","which pattern for Choosing a load-balancing algorithm"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Choosing a load-balancing algorithm<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Round robin for uniform requests; least connections when request cost varies; consistent hashing when you need stickiness (caches, sockets).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Round robin takes turns, fine when requests cost about the same. Least connections sends to the least busy server, better when some requests are slow. Consistent hashing sends the same key to the same server, needed when a server holds that key’s cache or socket.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-lbalgo"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-gateway","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"Many microservices, each needing auth, rate limiting, logging","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Many microservices, each needing auth, rate limiting, logging","when I see Many microservices, each needing auth, rate limiting, logging","what to do when Many microservices, each needing auth, rate limiting, logging","which pattern for Many microservices, each needing auth, rate limiting, logging"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Many microservices, each needing auth, rate limiting, logging<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>API gateway at the edge: authenticate, rate limit, route, add trace IDs.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Instead of every microservice re-implementing login checks, rate limits and logging, put one gateway at the edge that does them once and routes each request to the right service.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-gateway"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-cursor","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"Paginating a feed or a large, changing list","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Paginating a feed or a large, changing list","when I see Paginating a feed or a large, changing list","what to do when Paginating a feed or a large, changing list","which pattern for Paginating a feed or a large, changing list"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Paginating a feed or a large, changing list<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Cursor pagination: <code>?after=lastId&amp;limit=20<\/code> with an index on the sort key.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Page numbers (OFFSET) get slow deep into a list and skip or repeat items when new ones are added. A cursor says “give me 20 items after the last one I saw”, which uses the index and stays correct while the list changes.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-cursor"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-idem","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"A write that must not happen twice (payment, order) and clients that retry","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A write that must not happen twice (payment, order) and clients that retry","when I see A write that must not happen twice (payment, order) and clients that retry","what to do when A write that must not happen twice (payment, order) and clients that retry","which pattern for A write that must not happen twice (payment, order) and clients that retry"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A write that must not happen twice (payment, order) and clients that retry<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Require an <code>Idempotency-Key<\/code>; store the result by key; return it on repeats.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Clients retry when they don’t hear back, which can repeat a payment. Make the client send an Idempotency-Key header; the server stores the result for that key and returns it again on a repeat instead of charging twice.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-idem"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-protocol","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL","when I see REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL","what to do when REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL","which pattern for REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>REST for public CRUD, gRPC for internal low-latency calls (protobuf, HTTP/2, streaming), GraphQL for many clients needing different shapes.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Pick by who’s calling. REST is simple and universal for public APIs. gRPC is fast and typed for service-to-service calls. GraphQL lets many different clients ask for exactly the fields they need.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-protocol"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-realtime","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"Server must push updates to clients","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Server must push updates to clients","when I see Server must push updates to clients","what to do when Server must push updates to clients","which pattern for Server must push updates to clients"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Server must push updates to clients<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Rare updates → polling. One-way stream → SSE. Two-way, low latency (chat, games, collaborative editing) → WebSocket.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Match the tool to how often and in which direction updates flow. Occasional updates: just poll. A one-way stream from server to client: Server-Sent Events. Constant two-way traffic (chat, games): WebSockets.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-realtime"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-basics-wsscale","k":"rx","s":"sd-basics","t":"WebSockets at millions of users","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["WebSockets at millions of users","when I see WebSockets at millions of users","what to do when WebSockets at millions of users","which pattern for WebSockets at millions of users"],"w":"System Design Basics","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>WebSockets at millions of users<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Dedicated connection servers + a registry (user → server) in Redis + pub/sub between servers; heartbeats; reconnect with resume.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Millions of open sockets need dedicated connection servers. Keep a registry of which server holds each user’s connection, and use pub/sub so any server can push a message to a user connected elsewhere. Heartbeats detect dead connections; clients reconnect and resume.<\/p><p>From <b>System Design Basics<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open System Design Basics","sd-basics.html#rx-wsscale"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-default","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"Not sure which database","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Not sure which database","when I see Not sure which database","what to do when Not sure which database","which pattern for Not sure which database"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Not sure which database<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Start with Postgres/MySQL. Move a specific workload to another store only when an access pattern or scale number demands it.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A relational database (Postgres or MySQL) handles most products well: transactions, joins, indexes, and it scales further than people expect. Move a specific workload elsewhere only when a real access pattern or number demands it.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-default"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-nosql","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"Massive write volume, simple key-based access, or flexible schema","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Massive write volume, simple key-based access, or flexible schema","when I see Massive write volume, simple key-based access, or flexible schema","what to do when Massive write volume, simple key-based access, or flexible schema","which pattern for Massive write volume, simple key-based access, or flexible schema"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Massive write volume, simple key-based access, or flexible schema<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Key-value (Redis/Dynamo) for lookups by key, Cassandra for append-heavy time-ordered data, Mongo for whole-document reads.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> NoSQL stores trade joins and flexible queries for scale on one access pattern. Pick by how you’ll read: by key (Redis/DynamoDB), huge append-only time series (Cassandra), or whole documents at once (MongoDB).<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-nosql"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-denorm","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"A hot read path that needs several joins","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A hot read path that needs several joins","when I see A hot read path that needs several joins","what to do when A hot read path that needs several joins","which pattern for A hot read path that needs several joins"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A hot read path that needs several joins<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Denormalise: copy the few fields you need into the read model, update copies asynchronously.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If a page is read constantly and needs several joins each time, store the needed fields together in a read-friendly copy. Writes then update the copies (often asynchronously), trading some write work and staleness for fast reads.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-denorm"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-index","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"Slow query filtering or sorting on a column","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Slow query filtering or sorting on a column","when I see Slow query filtering or sorting on a column","what to do when Slow query filtering or sorting on a column","which pattern for Slow query filtering or sorting on a column"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Slow query filtering or sorting on a column<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Add a B-tree index on that column; verify with <code>EXPLAIN<\/code> that the plan uses it.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Without an index, the database reads every row to find matches. A B-tree index on the column is like a book index: it jumps straight to the matching rows. Check with EXPLAIN that the query actually uses it.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-index"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-composite","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"Query filters on A and sorts or filters on B","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Query filters on A and sorts or filters on B","when I see Query filters on A and sorts or filters on B","what to do when Query filters on A and sorts or filters on B","which pattern for Query filters on A and sorts or filters on B"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Query filters on A and sorts or filters on B<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Composite index (A, B), equality columns first, range/sort column last.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A composite index is sorted by its first column, then the second within it. Put columns you filter with = first and the range or ORDER BY column last, so the database can jump to the right block and read it already sorted.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-composite"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-explain","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"“This query is slow, what do you do?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“This query is slow, what do you do?”","when I see “This query is slow, what do you do?”","what to do when “This query is slow, what do you do?”","which pattern for “This query is slow, what do you do?”"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“This query is slow, what do you do?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Run <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE<\/code>: look for Seq Scan on big tables, bad row estimates, sorts and nested loops; then index, rewrite or cache.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Don’t guess why a query is slow; ask the database. EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows the plan and real timings. Look for full table scans on big tables, wildly wrong row estimates, big sorts and nested loops over many rows. Then add an index, rewrite or cache.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-explain"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-txn","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"Several writes that must all happen or none (transfer, order + payment record)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Several writes that must all happen or none (transfer, order + payment record)","when I see Several writes that must all happen or none (transfer, order + payment record)","what to do when Several writes that must all happen or none (transfer, order + payment record)","which pattern for Several writes that must all happen or none (transfer, order + payment record)"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Several writes that must all happen or none (transfer, order + payment record)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Wrap them in one transaction; guard invariants inside the UPDATE (<code>WHERE balance &gt;= ?<\/code>).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Group writes that must succeed or fail together into one transaction. Put the business rule inside the UPDATE itself, so the database checks it atomically.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-txn"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-lostupdate","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"Read a value in code, change it, write it back","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Read a value in code, change it, write it back","when I see Read a value in code, change it, write it back","what to do when Read a value in code, change it, write it back","which pattern for Read a value in code, change it, write it back"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Read a value in code, change it, write it back<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Atomic UPDATE (<code>SET x = x - ?<\/code>), <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE<\/code>, or a version column.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Reading a value into code, changing it and writing it back lets two requests overwrite each other (both read 10, both write 9). Let the database do the change in one statement, lock the row while you work, or use a version check.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-lostupdate"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-replicas","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"Read-heavy load on one database","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Read-heavy load on one database","when I see Read-heavy load on one database","what to do when Read-heavy load on one database","which pattern for Read-heavy load on one database"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Read-heavy load on one database<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Add read replicas; send reads there, writes to the primary; route a user’s reads to the primary just after they write.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If reads overwhelm one database, copy it to read replicas and send reads there; writes still go to the primary. Replicas lag slightly, so right after a user writes, read their data from the primary so they see their own change.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-replicas"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-quorum","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"Leaderless replication (Cassandra/Dynamo) with N copies","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Leaderless replication (Cassandra/Dynamo) with N copies","when I see Leaderless replication (Cassandra/Dynamo) with N copies","what to do when Leaderless replication (Cassandra/Dynamo) with N copies","which pattern for Leaderless replication (Cassandra/Dynamo) with N copies"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Leaderless replication (Cassandra/Dynamo) with N copies<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Choose W and R so that W + R &amp;gt; N for strong reads (e.g. N=3, W=2, R=2).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With N copies, write to W of them and read from R. If W + R &gt; N, every read overlaps at least one copy that has the latest write, so reads are up to date.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-quorum"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-shardkey","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"One primary can’t take the writes or hold the data","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["One primary can’t take the writes or hold the data","when I see One primary can’t take the writes or hold the data","what to do when One primary can’t take the writes or hold the data","which pattern for One primary can’t take the writes or hold the data"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>One primary can’t take the writes or hold the data<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Shard by a high-cardinality key that keeps a request’s data together (user_id, tenant_id); avoid hot keys.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When one database can’t handle the writes or the size, split the rows across several by a key. Choose a key with many different values that keeps one request’s data on one shard (user_id), and avoid keys where one value is very hot.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-shardkey"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-databases-ring","k":"rx","s":"sd-databases","t":"Mapping keys to a changing set of servers (caches, shards)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Mapping keys to a changing set of servers (caches, shards)","when I see Mapping keys to a changing set of servers (caches, shards)","what to do when Mapping keys to a changing set of servers (caches, shards)","which pattern for Mapping keys to a changing set of servers (caches, shards)"],"w":"Databases","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Mapping keys to a changing set of servers (caches, shards)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Consistent hashing with virtual nodes.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> With plain hash % n, adding one server moves almost every key. Consistent hashing places servers and keys on a ring; each key belongs to the next server clockwise, so adding a server only moves the keys next to it. Virtual nodes spread the load evenly.<\/p><p>From <b>Databases<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Databases","sd-databases.html#rx-ring"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-cacheaside","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Read-heavy data that tolerates seconds of staleness","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Read-heavy data that tolerates seconds of staleness","when I see Read-heavy data that tolerates seconds of staleness","what to do when Read-heavy data that tolerates seconds of staleness","which pattern for Read-heavy data that tolerates seconds of staleness"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Read-heavy data that tolerates seconds of staleness<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Cache-aside: read cache → miss → DB → set with TTL.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> The app checks the cache first. On a miss, it reads the database, stores the result in the cache with an expiry (TTL), and returns it. Later reads are fast until the entry expires.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-cacheaside"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-invalidate","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Updating data that is cached","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Updating data that is cached","when I see Updating data that is cached","what to do when Updating data that is cached","which pattern for Updating data that is cached"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Updating data that is cached<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Write the DB, then delete the cache key (plus a TTL as a safety net).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> After changing data, update the database first, then delete the cache entry so the next read fetches the fresh value. A TTL covers the rare case where the delete is lost.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-invalidate"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-stampede","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"A hot key expires and thousands of requests hit the DB at once","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A hot key expires and thousands of requests hit the DB at once","when I see A hot key expires and thousands of requests hit the DB at once","what to do when A hot key expires and thousands of requests hit the DB at once","which pattern for A hot key expires and thousands of requests hit the DB at once"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A hot key expires and thousands of requests hit the DB at once<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Lock/single-flight so one request reloads, others wait or serve stale; add TTL jitter; refresh hot keys early.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When a popular cache entry expires, thousands of requests miss at once and all hit the database. Let only one request rebuild the entry while the others wait or get the slightly stale value, and randomise expiries so hot keys don’t all expire together.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-stampede"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-blob","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Images, videos, PDFs, uploads","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Images, videos, PDFs, uploads","when I see Images, videos, PDFs, uploads","what to do when Images, videos, PDFs, uploads","which pattern for Images, videos, PDFs, uploads"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Images, videos, PDFs, uploads<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Object storage (S3) with pre-signed upload/download URLs; store only the key and metadata in the DB.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Big files don’t belong in the database. Store them in object storage (S3) and keep only the file’s key and metadata in the DB. Clients upload and download directly using short-lived pre-signed URLs, so file bytes skip your servers.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-blob"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-cdn","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Static assets or media served to users worldwide","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Static assets or media served to users worldwide","when I see Static assets or media served to users worldwide","what to do when Static assets or media served to users worldwide","which pattern for Static assets or media served to users worldwide"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Static assets or media served to users worldwide<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Put a CDN in front with long cache headers and versioned file names.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A CDN keeps copies of static files on servers near users, so they download from nearby. Give files long cache lifetimes and put a version in the file name (app.v42.js); a new version gets a new name, so nobody sees a stale file.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-cdn"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-queue","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Work that doesn’t need to finish before replying (email, thumbnails, reports), or spiky traffic","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Work that doesn’t need to finish before replying (email, thumbnails, reports), or spiky traffic","when I see Work that doesn’t need to finish before replying (email, thumbnails, reports), or spiky traffic","what to do when Work that doesn’t need to finish before replying (email, thumbnails, reports), or spiky traffic","which pattern for Work that doesn’t need to finish before replying (email, thumbnails, reports), or spiky traffic"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Work that doesn’t need to finish before replying (email, thumbnails, reports), or spiky traffic<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Put it on a queue; workers process at their own pace.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If the user doesn’t need to wait for a job (sending email, making thumbnails), reply immediately and put the job on a queue. Workers process it at their own pace, and traffic spikes just make the queue longer instead of overloading servers.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-queue"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-kafkaorder","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Events for the same entity must be processed in order","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Events for the same entity must be processed in order","when I see Events for the same entity must be processed in order","what to do when Events for the same entity must be processed in order","which pattern for Events for the same entity must be processed in order"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Events for the same entity must be processed in order<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Use the entity ID as the Kafka message key so they share a partition.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Kafka only keeps order within one partition. Messages with the same key go to the same partition, so use the entity’s ID (order id, user id) as the key and all of that entity’s events stay in order.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-kafkaorder"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-atleastonce","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Consuming from Kafka/SQS","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Consuming from Kafka/SQS","when I see Consuming from Kafka/SQS","what to do when Consuming from Kafka/SQS","which pattern for Consuming from Kafka/SQS"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Consuming from Kafka/SQS<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Assume at-least-once delivery: make handlers idempotent (dedupe by event ID, upserts, conditional updates).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Queues may deliver the same message more than once (after a crash or retry). Make processing safe to repeat: skip event IDs you’ve already handled, or write in a way where doing it twice has the same effect (upserts, conditional updates).<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-atleastonce"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-saga","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"A business transaction spanning several services’ databases","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A business transaction spanning several services’ databases","when I see A business transaction spanning several services’ databases","what to do when A business transaction spanning several services’ databases","which pattern for A business transaction spanning several services’ databases"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A business transaction spanning several services’ databases<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Saga: sequence of local transactions with compensating actions on failure (orchestrated or choreographed).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A business process that spans several services can’t use one database transaction. Run it as a series of local steps, and for each step define an undo (compensation). If a later step fails, run the undos for the steps already done.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-saga"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-outbox","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Save to DB and publish an event, both must happen","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Save to DB and publish an event, both must happen","when I see Save to DB and publish an event, both must happen","what to do when Save to DB and publish an event, both must happen","which pattern for Save to DB and publish an event, both must happen"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Save to DB and publish an event, both must happen<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Transactional outbox: event row in the same transaction; a relay/CDC publishes it.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Saving to the database and publishing an event are two systems; one can fail after the other succeeded. Write the event as a row in an outbox table inside the same transaction as the data, and let a separate relay publish outbox rows to the queue.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-outbox"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-cqrs","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Reads need very different shapes or scale than writes","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Reads need very different shapes or scale than writes","when I see Reads need very different shapes or scale than writes","what to do when Reads need very different shapes or scale than writes","which pattern for Reads need very different shapes or scale than writes"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Reads need very different shapes or scale than writes<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>CQRS: write model for commands, separate read models (built from events) for queries.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> When reads and writes need very different shapes or scale, separate them. Commands update the write model; events from it build read models shaped exactly for each query.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-cqrs"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-cap","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Consistency vs availability decision","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Consistency vs availability decision","when I see Consistency vs availability decision","what to do when Consistency vs availability decision","which pattern for Consistency vs availability decision"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Consistency vs availability decision<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Name it per feature: money, stock, uniqueness → CP. Feeds, likes, presence, carts → AP with eventual consistency.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> During a network split you must choose: refuse some requests to stay correct (consistency) or keep answering with possibly stale data (availability). Decide per feature: money, stock and uniqueness need correctness; likes, feeds and presence can be eventually consistent.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-cap"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-timeouts","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Any remote call","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Any remote call","when I see Any remote call","what to do when Any remote call","which pattern for Any remote call"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Any remote call<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Timeout + bounded retries with exponential backoff and jitter, only for idempotent operations.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A remote call without a timeout can hang forever and tie up your threads. Always set one. Retry a few times with growing, randomised waits (backoff with jitter), but only for operations that are safe to repeat.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-timeouts"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-breaker","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"A dependency failing or slow repeatedly","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["A dependency failing or slow repeatedly","when I see A dependency failing or slow repeatedly","what to do when A dependency failing or slow repeatedly","which pattern for A dependency failing or slow repeatedly"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>A dependency failing or slow repeatedly<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Circuit breaker (CLOSED → OPEN → HALF_OPEN) with a fallback; bulkhead its thread pool.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> If a dependency keeps failing, stop calling it for a while (open circuit) and return a fallback immediately, so you don’t pile up waiting requests. After a pause, let a few test calls through (half-open); if they succeed, close the circuit.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-breaker"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-micro","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"“Should we use microservices?”","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Should we use microservices?”","when I see “Should we use microservices?”","what to do when “Should we use microservices?”","which pattern for “Should we use microservices?”"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Should we use microservices?”<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Start with a modular monolith; split a service when a team, scaling need or deployment cadence demands it.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Microservices add network calls, deployments and data consistency problems. Start with one well-organised application (a modular monolith). Split out a service when there’s a concrete reason: a separate team, very different scaling, or a different release pace.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-micro"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-bloom","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"“Have we seen this before?” over a huge set, where a rare false yes is OK","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["“Have we seen this before?” over a huge set, where a rare false yes is OK","when I see “Have we seen this before?” over a huge set, where a rare false yes is OK","what to do when “Have we seen this before?” over a huge set, where a rare false yes is OK","which pattern for “Have we seen this before?” over a huge set, where a rare false yes is OK"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>“Have we seen this before?” over a huge set, where a rare false yes is OK<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Bloom filter in front of the real lookup.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A Bloom filter is a tiny bit array that answers “definitely not seen” or “maybe seen”. Put it in front of an expensive lookup: “definitely not” skips the lookup entirely; “maybe” does the real check.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-bloom"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-geo","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Find nearby drivers, restaurants, stays","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Find nearby drivers, restaurants, stays","when I see Find nearby drivers, restaurants, stays","what to do when Find nearby drivers, restaurants, stays","which pattern for Find nearby drivers, restaurants, stays"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Find nearby drivers, restaurants, stays<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Geohash (prefix = area) or quadtree; query your cell plus 8 neighbours, then filter by exact distance. Redis GEO does this.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Comparing distances to every driver is too slow. Divide the map into cells (a geohash: longer shared prefix = closer). Look in your cell and its 8 neighbours, then compute exact distances only for those candidates.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-geo"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-hll","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Count distinct users/IPs at huge scale","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Count distinct users/IPs at huge scale","when I see Count distinct users/IPs at huge scale","what to do when Count distinct users/IPs at huge scale","which pattern for Count distinct users/IPs at huge scale"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Count distinct users/IPs at huge scale<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>HyperLogLog (Redis PFADD/PFCOUNT).<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Counting exact unique visitors at huge scale needs memory for every ID. HyperLogLog estimates the count within about 1% using around 12 KB, no matter how many IDs.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-hll"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-nines","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Availability target","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Availability target","when I see Availability target","what to do when Availability target","which pattern for Availability target"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Availability target<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>99.9% ≈ 8.8 h/yr down, 99.99% ≈ 53 min/yr. Serial deps multiply; redundancy in parallel adds nines.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Availability is quoted in nines. 99.9% still means about 9 hours down a year; 99.99% about 53 minutes. Services in a chain multiply their availabilities (worse); redundant copies in parallel push it up.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-nines"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-distributed-jwt","k":"rx","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Authenticating requests across many services","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Authenticating requests across many services","when I see Authenticating requests across many services","what to do when Authenticating requests across many services","which pattern for Authenticating requests across many services"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Authenticating requests across many services<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Short-lived signed JWT access token + refresh token; verify the signature locally; check roles for authorisation.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> After login, give the client a short-lived signed token (JWT) listing who they are and their roles. Every service can check the signature locally without calling the auth server. A longer-lived refresh token gets new access tokens.<\/p><p>From <b>Caching, Queues & Resilience<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open Caching, Queues & Resilience","sd-distributed.html#rx-jwt"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-shortcode","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"Generate short unique IDs at scale (URL codes, order numbers)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Generate short unique IDs at scale (URL codes, order numbers)","when I see Generate short unique IDs at scale (URL codes, order numbers)","what to do when Generate short unique IDs at scale (URL codes, order numbers)","which pattern for Generate short unique IDs at scale (URL codes, order numbers)"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Generate short unique IDs at scale (URL codes, order numbers)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Counter ranges leased per server + Base62 (or Snowflake IDs). Avoid hash-and-truncate.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each server leases a block of numbers from a counter (say 1,000,000 to 1,000,999) and hands them out locally; no collisions, no coordination per request. Encode each number in Base62 (0–9, a–z, A–Z) for a short code.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-shortcode"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-redirect","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"301 or 302 for the redirect?","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["301 or 302 for the redirect?","when I see 301 or 302 for the redirect?","what to do when 301 or 302 for the redirect?","which pattern for 301 or 302 for the redirect?"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>301 or 302 for the redirect?<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>302 (temporary) if you want analytics on every click; 301 if you want browsers to cache and reduce load.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A 301 tells browsers the move is permanent, so they cache it and skip your server next time: less load, but you can’t count those clicks. A 302 is temporary, so every click comes through you: you can count every click.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-redirect"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-ratelimit","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"Rate limiter design","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Rate limiter design","when I see Rate limiter design","what to do when Rate limiter design","which pattern for Rate limiter design"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Rate limiter design<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Token bucket per user/API key in Redis, atomic via Lua; return 429 with Retry-After; enforce at the gateway.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> A token bucket gives each user a bucket that refills at a steady rate; each request takes a token, and an empty bucket means “429 Too Many Requests”. Keep buckets in Redis, update them atomically (a Lua script), and enforce at the gateway.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-ratelimit"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-fanout","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"News feed / timeline","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["News feed / timeline","when I see News feed / timeline","what to do when News feed / timeline","which pattern for News feed / timeline"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>News feed / timeline<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Hybrid: fan-out on write into per-user timeline caches; fan-out on read for celebrity accounts; merge at read.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Push each new post into followers’ ready-made timelines when it’s written (fast reads). That breaks for celebrities with millions of followers, so pull their posts at read time and merge them in.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-fanout"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-notify","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"Notification system","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Notification system","when I see Notification system","what to do when Notification system","which pattern for Notification system"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Notification system<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Accept → 202, dedupe by idempotency key, check preferences, queue per channel, workers with rate limits, retries and a dead-letter queue.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Accept the request quickly (202), drop duplicates with an idempotency key, check the user’s preferences, then queue it per channel. Channel workers respect provider rate limits, retry failures, and move hopeless messages to a dead-letter queue.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-notify"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-typeahead","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"Autocomplete / typeahead","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Autocomplete / typeahead","when I see Autocomplete / typeahead","what to do when Autocomplete / typeahead","which pattern for Autocomplete / typeahead"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Autocomplete / typeahead<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Precompute top-K completions per prefix offline from query logs (trie), serve from memory/Redis; debounce and cache on the client.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Don’t search on every keystroke. Precompute, offline from query logs, the top suggestions for each prefix and keep them in memory or Redis. Typing a prefix then becomes a single lookup; the client also waits for a pause and caches results.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-typeahead"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-chat","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"Chat app (WhatsApp)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Chat app (WhatsApp)","when I see Chat app (WhatsApp)","what to do when Chat app (WhatsApp)","which pattern for Chat app (WhatsApp)"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Chat app (WhatsApp)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>WebSocket connection servers + presence registry (user → server) + pub/sub between servers + Cassandra for messages + push for offline users.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Each phone keeps a WebSocket open to a connection server. A registry records which server holds each user. To deliver, look up the recipient’s server and publish to it; store messages durably (Cassandra); if the recipient is offline, send a push notification.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-chat"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-nearby","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"Ride matching / nearby search (Uber, Zomato)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Ride matching / nearby search (Uber, Zomato)","when I see Ride matching / nearby search (Uber, Zomato)","what to do when Ride matching / nearby search (Uber, Zomato)","which pattern for Ride matching / nearby search (Uber, Zomato)"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Ride matching / nearby search (Uber, Zomato)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Drivers stream locations into a geo index (Redis GEO / geohash / quadtree); query nearby; lock a driver while offering.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Drivers send their location every few seconds into a geo index (Redis GEO, geohash cells). A ride request queries nearby drivers, then locks one driver while the offer is pending so two riders can’t get the same driver.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-nearby"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-video","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"Video platform (YouTube)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Video platform (YouTube)","when I see Video platform (YouTube)","what to do when Video platform (YouTube)","which pattern for Video platform (YouTube)"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Video platform (YouTube)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Resumable upload to object storage → queue transcoding jobs into several bitrates → HLS/DASH segments on a CDN → adaptive bitrate playback.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Upload the original in resumable chunks to object storage. A queue triggers transcoding into several qualities, cut into short segments (HLS/DASH) and served from a CDN. The player switches quality as bandwidth changes.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-video"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-booking","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"Booking system (Airbnb, BookMyShow, hotels)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["Booking system (Airbnb, BookMyShow, hotels)","when I see Booking system (Airbnb, BookMyShow, hotels)","what to do when Booking system (Airbnb, BookMyShow, hotels)","which pattern for Booking system (Airbnb, BookMyShow, hotels)"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>Booking system (Airbnb, BookMyShow, hotels)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Availability row per resource per slot; atomic conditional update to HOLD all slots; confirm after payment; expire holds.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Keep one availability row per resource per time slot. To book, atomically switch all the needed rows from FREE to HELD in one conditional update; if any was taken, nothing changes. Confirm after payment, and let holds expire on their own.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-booking"]]},{"i":"rx-sd-hld-sync","k":"rx","s":"sd-hld","t":"File sync (Dropbox, Drive)","cat":"Reflexes: see this → do that","q":["File sync (Dropbox, Drive)","when I see File sync (Dropbox, Drive)","what to do when File sync (Dropbox, Drive)","which pattern for File sync (Dropbox, Drive)"],"w":"HLD Problems","a":"<p class=\"ap-rx\"><span>When you see<\/span><b>File sync (Dropbox, Drive)<\/b><span>Do this<\/span><b>Chunk files (~4 MB), address chunks by hash, upload only missing chunks, store metadata/versions separately, notify devices of changes.<\/b><\/p><p><b>In plain words:<\/b> Split files into chunks (~4 MB) named by their hash. To sync, upload only chunks the server doesn’t already have, which makes small edits cheap. Keep file metadata and versions separately, and notify the user’s other devices to pull the changes.<\/p><p>From <b>HLD Problems<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Open HLD Problems","sd-hld.html#rx-sync"]]},{"i":"po-hashing-0","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"Memory buys time","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Have I seen this before?","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Memory buys time","explain Memory buys time","what is Memory buys time"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Have I seen this before?","a":"<p>The brute force for “find two numbers that add to a target” checks every pair: for each element, scan everything before it. That inner scan is asking one question over and over: <i>is the partner somewhere behind me?<\/i><\/p><p>A dict answers that question in O(1). Walk the array once, and before moving on, write down what you saw. Now the inner loop is a lookup, and O(n²) becomes O(n), paid for with O(n) memory.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#s-memory-buys-time"]]},{"i":"po-hashing-1","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"Canonical keys","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Have I seen this before?","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Canonical keys","explain Canonical keys","what is Canonical keys"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Have I seen this before?","a":"<p>“Group the anagrams” sounds like comparing every pair of words. Instead, give each word a <b>canonical form<\/b> that's identical for all its anagrams, and let a dict do the grouping. Sorted letters work; a tuple of 26 letter counts works in O(k) per word.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#s-canonical-keys"]]},{"i":"po-hashing-2","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"Running totals","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Prefix sums","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Running totals","explain Running totals","what is Running totals"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Prefix sums","a":"<p>Let <code>pre[i]<\/code> be the sum of the first i elements, with <code>pre[0] = 0<\/code>. Then the sum of <code>nums[l..r]<\/code> is <code>pre[r + 1] − pre[l]<\/code>: everything up to r, minus everything before l. One O(n) pass buys O(1) answers forever.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#s-running-totals"]]},{"i":"po-hashing-3","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"Watch the thinking happen","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Prefix sums","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Watch the thinking happen","explain Watch the thinking happen","what is Watch the thinking happen"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Prefix sums","a":"<p>Try to say each thought before you reveal it.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#s-watch-the-thinking-happen"]]},{"i":"po-hashing-4","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"Decompose the move","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Matrix moves & the full set","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Decompose the move","explain Decompose the move","what is Decompose the move"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Matrix moves & the full set","a":"<p>Matrix problems look fiddly because you try to do the whole transformation at once. Break it into simple moves you can do in place: a rotation is a transpose plus a mirror, a spiral is four edges with shrinking walls, and “set zeroes” stores its flags in the matrix's own first row and column.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#s-decompose-the-move"]]},{"i":"po-hashing-5","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Matrix moves & the full set","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Matrix moves & the full set","a":"<p>Filter by pattern. For each one, name the reflex before you reveal the code.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-hashing-6","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"How to price a hashing solution","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a hashing solution","explain How to price a hashing solution","what is How to price a hashing solution"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Count passes over the input.<\/b> One loop over n items is O(n). A loop inside a loop over the same items is O(n²).<\/li><li><b>Price each dict/set operation at O(1)<\/b> on average. (Worst case O(n) if everything collides; say “average” out loud.)<\/li><li><b>Add the cost of building keys.<\/b> Sorting a word of length k for a key is O(k log k); a 26-count tuple is O(k).<\/li><li><b>Space<\/b> is the size of the dict: up to n entries → O(n). That’s the trade: memory for speed.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#s-how-to-price-a-hashing-solution"]]},{"i":"po-hashing-8","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technique<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Set / dict lookup pass<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code> average<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Group by canonical key<\/td><td><code>O(n · k)<\/code> with count tuples<\/td><td><code>O(n · k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Bucket sort by frequency<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Prefix sums, then queries<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code> build, <code>O(1)<\/code> query<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Index-as-hash marking<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> extra<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Rotate / spiral / set zeroes<\/td><td><code>O(R · C)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> extra<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"po-hashing-10","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this app, shuffled. Say the move, then flip. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-hashing-0","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"Every hash trick is the same trade.","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Every hash trick is the same trade."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing insight","a":"<p>The brute force asks “is there an earlier element that …?” by scanning, which is O(n) per question. A dict pre-answers that question for every earlier element, so each question costs O(1).<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">inner loop  →  one lookup        O(n²) → O(n) time, + O(n) memory<\/pre><p>When an interviewer asks “can you do better than O(n²)?”, the first thing to try is: what is my inner loop searching for, and can I keep it in a dict?<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#in-trade"]]},{"i":"in-hashing-1","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"A while inside a for can still be O(n).","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["A while inside a for can still be O(n)."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing insight","a":"<p>Longest Consecutive Sequence has a while loop inside a for loop, yet it runs in O(n). The guard <code>if x − 1 in s: continue<\/code> means only run starts ever enter the while, and each number belongs to exactly one run.<\/p><p>So count total work, not nesting depth: every number is stepped over by the inner loop at most once across the entire program. This “amortized” argument shows up again in sliding windows and monotonic stacks.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#in-amort"]]},{"i":"in-hashing-2","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"Subarray sum = k is Two Sum in disguise.","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Subarray sum = k is Two Sum in disguise."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing insight","a":"<p>Two Sum looks for an earlier value equal to <code>target − x<\/code>. Subarray Sum looks for an earlier <i>prefix<\/i> equal to <code>running − k<\/code>, because a subarray sum is a difference of two prefixes.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">sum(j+1 .. i) = pre[i] − pre[j] = k   ⇔   pre[j] = pre[i] − k<\/pre><p>Same dict, same one pass. Change “sum” to “count of odds”, “balance of 0s and 1s” or “sum mod k” and the trick still works.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#in-twosumprefix"]]},{"i":"in-hashing-3","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"In-place matrix work = find the mapping, then split it.","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["In-place matrix work = find the mapping, then split it."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing insight","a":"<p>Write down where cell (r, c) must end up. Rotation clockwise: (c, n − 1 − r). That's hard to do in one move without a temp grid, but it splits into two moves that are each easy in place: swap across the diagonal, then flip each row.<\/p><p>Counterclockwise? (n − 1 − c, r): transpose, then flip each column. 180°? Flip rows and columns. Any time a transformation looks messy, decompose it.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#in-decompose"]]},{"i":"in-hashing-4","k":"post","s":"hashing","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Arrays & Hashing insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","hashing.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-h1","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"In Two Sum, what goes wrong if you write seen[x] = i before checking target − x in seen ?","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In Two Sum, what goes wrong if you write seen[x] = i before checking target − x in seen ?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Nothing An element can pair with itself, e.g. target 6 with x = 3 returns [i, i] It becomes O(n²) It misses valid pairs","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> An element can pair with itself, e.g. target 6 with x = 3 returns [i, i]<\/p><p>Storing first means the lookup can find x itself. Check first, then store.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-h1"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-h2","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"Which dict key groups anagrams in O(k) per word, without sorting?","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Which dict key groups anagrams in O(k) per word, without sorting?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing sorted(word) tuple of 26 letter counts len(word) set(word)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> tuple of 26 letter counts<\/p><p>Sorting is O(k log k). A count tuple is O(k). <code>set<\/code> loses repeats (\"aab\" vs \"ab\"), and length groups non-anagrams.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-h2"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-h3","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"Why does subarray_sum start with seen = {0: 1} ?","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Why does subarray_sum start with seen = {0: 1} ?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing To avoid a KeyError So subarrays that start at index 0 are counted To handle negative numbers It’s optional","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> So subarrays that start at index 0 are counted<\/p><p>A subarray from index 0 to i has sum = running. Looking up running − k = 0 needs the empty prefix to be there.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-h3"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-h4","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"Subarray sum equals k, with negative numbers. Can a sliding window replace the prefix-sum dict?","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Subarray sum equals k, with negative numbers. Can a sliding window replace the prefix-sum dict?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Yes, always No: with negatives, shrinking the window can make the sum go up, so the shrink rule breaks Only if k > 0 Only if the array is sorted","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> No: with negatives, shrinking the window can make the sum go up, so the shrink rule breaks<\/p><p>Sliding windows need “adding makes it bigger, removing makes it smaller”. Negatives break that monotonicity; prefix sums don’t care.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-h4"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"Time and space of this?","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Time and space of this?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing O(n²) time, O(1) space O(n) time, O(n) space O(n log n) time, O(1) space O(n) time, O(1) space","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">def has_dup(a):\n    seen = set()\n    for x in a:\n        if x in seen:\n            return True\n        seen.add(x)\n    return False<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n) time, O(n) space<\/p><p>One pass with O(1) average set operations; the set can hold all n items.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"All n keys land in the same hash bucket. What does one lookup cost now?","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["All n keys land in the same hash bucket. What does one lookup cost now?"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing O(1) O(log n) O(n) O(n²)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p>The bucket becomes a list of n items to scan. That’s why we say O(1) <i>average<\/i>. (Java 8+ HashMap turns long buckets into trees: O(log n).)<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"“Is there a pair whose difference is exactly k?”","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Is there a pair whose difference is exactly k?”"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Set: for each x check x − k (or x + k) Prefix sums Bucket sort Transpose","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Set: for each x check x − k (or x + k)<\/p><p>A complement lookup, just with subtraction. One pass with a set.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"“Return the k most frequent words.”","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Return the k most frequent words.”"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Two pointers Counter, then buckets or a heap of size k Prefix sums Index-as-hash","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Counter, then buckets or a heap of size k<\/p><p>Frequencies → Counter. Then bucket sort for O(n), or a size-k heap for O(n log k).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"“Count subarrays whose sum is divisible by k.”","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Count subarrays whose sum is divisible by k.”"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Sliding window Prefix sums with a dict of remainders Sort and two pointers Spiral walk","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Prefix sums with a dict of remainders<\/p><p>Two prefixes with the same remainder mod k bound a subarray divisible by k. Count remainders as you go.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"qz-hashing-q-d4","k":"quiz","s":"hashing","t":"“Rotate an n × n image 90° counterclockwise, in place.”","sub":"Arrays & Hashing · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Rotate an n × n image 90° counterclockwise, in place.”"],"w":"Arrays & Hashing Transpose, then reverse each column Reverse each row only Spiral walk Prefix sums","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Transpose, then reverse each column<\/p><p>Counterclockwise maps (r, c) to (n − 1 − c, r): transpose, then flip vertically.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","hashing.html#q-d4"]]},{"i":"po-two-pointers-0","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"Sorted means you know which way to move","sub":"Two Pointers · Walk toward each other","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Sorted means you know which way to move","explain Sorted means you know which way to move","what is Sorted means you know which way to move"],"w":"Two Pointers Walk toward each other","a":"<p>Pair sum on a sorted array. Put <code>l<\/code> at the smallest and <code>r<\/code> at the largest. If the sum is too small, the only move that can make it bigger is <code>l += 1<\/code>. Too big? Only <code>r -= 1<\/code> helps. Every step is forced, so there's nothing to search.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#s-sorted-means-you-know-which-way-to-move"]]},{"i":"po-two-pointers-2","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"The writer and the reader","sub":"Two Pointers · Read fast, write slow","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The writer and the reader","explain The writer and the reader","what is The writer and the reader"],"w":"Two Pointers Read fast, write slow","a":"<p>“Remove duplicates in place” sounds like deleting, but deleting from an array is O(n) each time. Instead, keep a <b>write<\/b> pointer. Everything before it is the finished answer. The <b>read<\/b> pointer scans ahead, and only when it finds something worth keeping does it copy it to the writer and advance it.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#s-the-writer-and-the-reader"]]},{"i":"po-two-pointers-3","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Two Pointers · Read fast, write slow","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Two Pointers Read fast, write slow","a":"<p>Filter by pattern. Name the reflex before you reveal.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-two-pointers-4","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"How to price a two-pointer solution","sub":"Two Pointers · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a two-pointer solution","explain How to price a two-pointer solution","what is How to price a two-pointer solution"],"w":"Two Pointers Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Count how far each pointer can travel in total.<\/b> Left only moves right, right only moves left, and they stop when they meet: at most n moves combined → O(n).<\/li><li><b>Add the sort<\/b> if you sorted first: O(n log n), which then dominates.<\/li><li><b>Nested two pointers<\/b> (3Sum): an outer loop of n, each running an O(n) two-pointer scan → O(n²). Still better than O(n³).<\/li><li><b>Space<\/b>: two indexes → O(1), plus whatever the sort uses.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#s-how-to-price-a-two-pointer-solution"]]},{"i":"po-two-pointers-6","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Two Pointers · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Two Pointers The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Converging pair on sorted input<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code> (+ <code>O(n log n)<\/code> to sort)<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>3Sum / 4Sum<\/td><td><code>O(n²)<\/code> / <code>O(n³)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> extra<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Read / write in place<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Dutch national flag<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>, one pass<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Trapping rain water<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"po-two-pointers-8","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Two Pointers · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Two Pointers The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-two-pointers-0","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"Each move deletes a whole row of pairs.","sub":"Two Pointers · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Each move deletes a whole row of pairs."],"w":"Two Pointers insight","a":"<p>Picture all pairs (i, j) as an n × n grid. Brute force checks every cell. In Two Sum II, when <code>nums[l] + nums[r] &lt; target<\/code>, every pair using <code>l<\/code> is too small, because <code>nums[r]<\/code> is the largest partner left. That kills a whole row of the grid in one comparison.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">one comparison  →  one row or column of pairs eliminated  →  n steps<\/pre><p>This is the proof you should say in an interview when asked “why doesn't this miss the answer?”.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#in-elim"]]},{"i":"in-two-pointers-1","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"Why the lower side is always safe to settle.","sub":"Two Pointers · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Why the lower side is always safe to settle."],"w":"Two Pointers insight","a":"<p>Water above bar i is <code>min(maxLeft, maxRight) − height[i]<\/code>. When <code>height[l] &lt; height[r]<\/code>, we know a bar at least as tall as height[r] exists on l's right. So maxRight(l) ≥ height[r] &gt; height[l]… and lmax is what limits it.<\/p><p>You don't need to know the exact right max, only that it isn't the bottleneck. Proving “this side is the bottleneck” is the core move behind many two-pointer answers.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#in-trapproof"]]},{"i":"in-two-pointers-2","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"Name the region each pointer guards.","sub":"Two Pointers · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Name the region each pointer guards."],"w":"Two Pointers insight","a":"<p>Read/write pointers are easy to get wrong until you say the invariant out loud:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">[0, w) finished answer     [w, r) junk     [r, n) not read yet<\/pre><p>Sort Colors has four regions: 0s, 1s, unknown, 2s. Each branch of the loop must keep every region true. If you can state the regions, the code writes itself, and the off-by-one bugs disappear.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#in-invariant"]]},{"i":"in-two-pointers-3","k":"post","s":"two-pointers","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Two Pointers · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Two Pointers insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","two-pointers.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-two-pointers-q-t1","k":"quiz","s":"two-pointers","t":"Container With Most Water: why move the shorter wall inward?","sub":"Two Pointers · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Container With Most Water: why move the shorter wall inward?"],"w":"Two Pointers It doesn’t matter which The shorter wall caps the height; any narrower container with it can only be worse Moving the taller one is O(n²) To keep the array sorted","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> The shorter wall caps the height; any narrower container with it can only be worse<\/p><p>Area = min(h[l], h[r]) × width. Keeping the shorter wall, the min can’t grow and width shrinks, so every container using it is already beaten.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","two-pointers.html#q-t1"]]},{"i":"qz-two-pointers-q-t2","k":"quiz","s":"two-pointers","t":"In 3Sum, after recording a triple, why skip nums[l] values equal to the one just used?","sub":"Two Pointers · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In 3Sum, after recording a triple, why skip nums[l] values equal to the one just used?"],"w":"Two Pointers For speed only To avoid recording the same triple twice Because the array is unsorted It’s required to terminate","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> To avoid recording the same triple twice<\/p><p>With the same first number and the same second number, the third is forced, so the triple would repeat.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","two-pointers.html#q-t2"]]},{"i":"qz-two-pointers-q-t3","k":"quiz","s":"two-pointers","t":"Sort Colors: after swapping nums[i] (a 2) with nums[hi] , why not i += 1 ?","sub":"Two Pointers · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Sort Colors: after swapping nums[i] (a 2) with nums[hi] , why not i += 1 ?"],"w":"Two Pointers It would be out of bounds The value that came from hi hasn’t been examined yet 2s must be checked twice No reason, it’s fine","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> The value that came from hi hasn’t been examined yet<\/p><p>hi’s old value could be 0, 1 or 2. It must be classified before i moves past it.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","two-pointers.html#q-t3"]]},{"i":"qz-two-pointers-q-t4","k":"quiz","s":"two-pointers","t":"Merge Sorted Array (88): why fill nums1 from the back?","sub":"Two Pointers · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Merge Sorted Array (88): why fill nums1 from the back?"],"w":"Two Pointers It’s faster The back is empty space, so writing there never overwrites unread values Python lists require it To keep it stable","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> The back is empty space, so writing there never overwrites unread values<\/p><p>Filling from the front would clobber nums1’s own unmerged values. The tail has m + n − m free slots.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","two-pointers.html#q-t4"]]},{"i":"qz-two-pointers-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"two-pointers","t":"Remove duplicates in-place from a sorted array with a slow and a fast pointer. Time and extra space?","sub":"Two Pointers · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Remove duplicates in-place from a sorted array with a slow and a fast pointer. Time and extra space?"],"w":"Two Pointers O(n²), O(1) O(n), O(1) O(n), O(n) O(n log n), O(1)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p>The fast pointer visits each element once; the slow one never passes it. Two integers of extra space.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","two-pointers.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-two-pointers-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"two-pointers","t":"Container With Most Water: two pointers from both ends. Why is it O(n) and not O(n²)?","sub":"Two Pointers · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Container With Most Water: two pointers from both ends. Why is it O(n) and not O(n²)?"],"w":"Two Pointers It skips half the pairs Every step moves one pointer inward, and they meet after n − 1 steps It sorts first Heights are bounded","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Every step moves one pointer inward, and they meet after n − 1 steps<\/p><p>Each iteration permanently discards one line, so there are at most n − 1 iterations.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","two-pointers.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-two-pointers-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"two-pointers","t":"“Sorted array: count pairs with sum less than target.”","sub":"Two Pointers · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Sorted array: count pairs with sum less than target.”"],"w":"Two Pointers Converging pointers: when nums[l] + nums[r] < target, add r − l, then l += 1 Hash set Read/write pointers Fast and slow","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Converging pointers: when nums[l] + nums[r] &lt; target, add r − l, then l += 1<\/p><p>If l pairs with r, it also pairs with everything between, so r − l pairs are counted at once.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","two-pointers.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-two-pointers-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"two-pointers","t":"“Remove every occurrence of val in place and return the new length.”","sub":"Two Pointers · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Remove every occurrence of val in place and return the new length.”"],"w":"Two Pointers Converging pointers Writer and reader Fast and slow Prefix sums","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Writer and reader<\/p><p>Keep everything that isn’t val by writing it at w.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","two-pointers.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-two-pointers-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"two-pointers","t":"“Find the duplicate number in [1..n] with O(1) space, without modifying the array.” (287)","sub":"Two Pointers · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Find the duplicate number in [1..n] with O(1) space, without modifying the array.” (287)"],"w":"Two Pointers Sort it Hash set Fast and slow pointers on i → nums[i] Dutch flag","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Fast and slow pointers on i → nums[i]<\/p><p>Treat the array as a linked list i → nums[i]. The duplicate is the cycle entrance (Floyd’s algorithm).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","two-pointers.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sliding-window-0","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"The caterpillar","sub":"Sliding Window · Expand right, shrink left","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The caterpillar","explain The caterpillar","what is The caterpillar"],"w":"Sliding Window Expand right, shrink left","a":"<p>A window is a contiguous slice <code>[l, r]<\/code>. The right edge always moves forward, eating the next element. If that breaks the rule (a repeated letter, too many distinct values), the left edge crawls forward until the rule holds again. Then you record the answer.<\/p><p>Brute force tries every (l, r) pair: O(n²). The caterpillar never moves backward, so it's O(n).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#s-the-caterpillar"]]},{"i":"po-sliding-window-1","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"Watch the thinking happen","sub":"Sliding Window · Expand right, shrink left","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Watch the thinking happen","explain Watch the thinking happen","what is Watch the thinking happen"],"w":"Sliding Window Expand right, shrink left","a":"<p>The hardest window problem, one thought at a time.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#s-watch-the-thinking-happen"]]},{"i":"po-sliding-window-2","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"Add one, drop one","sub":"Sliding Window · Fixed windows & the deque","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Add one, drop one","explain Add one, drop one","what is Add one, drop one"],"w":"Sliding Window Fixed windows & the deque","a":"<p>With a fixed size k, there's nothing to decide about shrinking: every step adds <code>nums[r]<\/code> and removes <code>nums[r − k]<\/code>. Keep a running sum or a running count, and never recompute the window from scratch.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#s-add-one-drop-one"]]},{"i":"po-sliding-window-3","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Sliding Window · Fixed windows & the deque","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Sliding Window Fixed windows & the deque","a":"<p>Filter by window type. Name the reflex before revealing.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-sliding-window-4","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"How to price a sliding-window solution","sub":"Sliding Window · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a sliding-window solution","explain How to price a sliding-window solution","what is How to price a sliding-window solution"],"w":"Sliding Window Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Don't multiply the loops.<\/b> A <code>while<\/code> that shrinks the window inside a <code>for<\/code> that grows it looks like O(n²).<\/li><li><b>Count entries and exits instead.<\/b> Each index enters the window once (right moves) and leaves at most once (left moves). Total ≤ 2n → O(n).<\/li><li><b>Space<\/b> is whatever describes the window: a count map is O(distinct items in the window), bounded by the alphabet (O(26) = O(1) for lowercase letters).<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#s-how-to-price-a-sliding-window-solution"]]},{"i":"po-sliding-window-6","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Sliding Window · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Sliding Window The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Variable window<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>: each index enters and leaves once<\/td><td><code>O(k)<\/code> or <code>O(alphabet)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Fixed window, running sum<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Fixed window, count compare<\/td><td><code>O(n · 26)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(26)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Sliding max with deque<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Exactly K via atMost<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>, two passes<\/td><td><code>O(k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"in-sliding-window-0","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"Two nested loops, still O(n).","sub":"Sliding Window · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Two nested loops, still O(n)."],"w":"Sliding Window insight","a":"<p>The <code>while<\/code> inside the <code>for<\/code> looks quadratic. It isn't: <code>l<\/code> only ever increases, and it can increase at most n times over the whole run. So the total work is n moves of r plus at most n moves of l.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">total pointer moves ≤ 2n   →   O(n)<\/pre><p>Same argument as the monotonic stack and as Longest Consecutive Sequence: count how often each element is touched, not how deep the loops nest.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#in-amort2"]]},{"i":"in-sliding-window-1","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"A window only works if the rule is monotone.","sub":"Sliding Window · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["A window only works if the rule is monotone."],"w":"Sliding Window insight","a":"<p>The whole trick depends on one property. For “longest valid”: if [l, r] is invalid, every window that contains it is invalid too. That's why we never move l backward.<\/p><p>“At most k distinct” has this property. “Sum ≥ target” with positives has the mirror property. “Exactly k distinct” and “sum = k with negatives” don't, which is why they need atMost tricks or prefix sums. Before writing a window, check monotonicity in one sentence.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#in-monotone"]]},{"i":"in-sliding-window-2","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"The deque is a waiting line of possible champions.","sub":"Sliding Window · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The deque is a waiting line of possible champions."],"w":"Sliding Window insight","a":"<p>When a bigger value arrives, every smaller value before it is doomed: it'll leave the window earlier and it's smaller, so it can never be the max. Throw them out from the back.<\/p><p>What remains is decreasing from front to back: the current champion, then the runner-up who takes over when the champion expires, and so on. The same idea, with a stack instead of a deque, gives “next greater element”.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#in-deque"]]},{"i":"in-sliding-window-3","k":"post","s":"sliding-window","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Sliding Window · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Sliding Window insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sliding-window.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-sliding-window-q-s1","k":"quiz","s":"sliding-window","t":"Longest Substring Without Repeating: why while ch in window instead of if ?","sub":"Sliding Window · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Longest Substring Without Repeating: why while ch in window instead of if ?"],"w":"Sliding Window Style only The repeat may be several positions back, so several letters must leave To avoid an infinite loop if would be O(n²)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> The repeat may be several positions back, so several letters must leave<\/p><p>In \"abca…\" arriving at a second \"b\" after \"abc\" needs \"a\" and \"b\" to leave. One removal isn’t enough.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sliding-window.html#q-s1"]]},{"i":"qz-sliding-window-q-s2","k":"quiz","s":"sliding-window","t":"Character Replacement never decreases maxf when shrinking. Why is the answer still right?","sub":"Sliding Window · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Character Replacement never decreases maxf when shrinking. Why is the answer still right?"],"w":"Sliding Window It’s a bug that happens to pass A stale maxf only blocks the window from growing; the best is only updated when a truly better maxf appears maxf can’t change Python recomputes it","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> A stale maxf only blocks the window from growing; the best is only updated when a truly better maxf appears<\/p><p>To beat the current best you need a bigger true max frequency. Until then, the window just slides at the same length.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sliding-window.html#q-s2"]]},{"i":"qz-sliding-window-q-s3","k":"quiz","s":"sliding-window","t":"Sliding Window Maximum: why store indexes in the deque, not values?","sub":"Sliding Window · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Sliding Window Maximum: why store indexes in the deque, not values?"],"w":"Sliding Window Indexes are smaller To know when the front has slid out of the window To sort them Deques can’t hold values","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> To know when the front has slid out of the window<\/p><p>Expiry depends on position. With duplicates, values alone can’t tell you which copy left.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sliding-window.html#q-s3"]]},{"i":"qz-sliding-window-q-s4","k":"quiz","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Longest subarray with sum ≤ k”, numbers may be negative. Window?","sub":"Sliding Window · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Longest subarray with sum ≤ k”, numbers may be negative. Window?"],"w":"Sliding Window Yes, same template No: with negatives, shrinking doesn’t reliably lower the sum, so validity isn’t monotone Only for k ≥ 0 Only if sorted","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> No: with negatives, shrinking doesn’t reliably lower the sum, so validity isn’t monotone<\/p><p>Use prefix sums with a monotonic structure (or a sorted list) instead.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sliding-window.html#q-s4"]]},{"i":"qz-sliding-window-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"sliding-window","t":"Minimum window substring: right moves over s (length n), left only moves forward. Time, ignoring the size of t?","sub":"Sliding Window · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Minimum window substring: right moves over s (length n), left only moves forward. Time, ignoring the size of t?"],"w":"Sliding Window O(n²) O(n) O(n log n) O(2ⁿ)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p>Both pointers move forward at most n times each.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sliding-window.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-sliding-window-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"sliding-window","t":"Sliding window maximum with a monotonic deque. Each index is appended once and popped at most once. Time?","sub":"Sliding Window · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Sliding window maximum with a monotonic deque. Each index is appended once and popped at most once. Time?"],"w":"Sliding Window O(n·k) O(n log k) O(n) O(k)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p>At most n pushes and n pops in total → O(n).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sliding-window.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-sliding-window-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Longest subarray with at most 2 distinct values.” (904)","sub":"Sliding Window · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Longest subarray with at most 2 distinct values.” (904)"],"w":"Sliding Window Fixed size Variable: shrink while distinct > 2 Deque Not a window","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Variable: shrink while distinct &gt; 2<\/p><p>At most K distinct is monotone. Count dict + shrink.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sliding-window.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sliding-window-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Number of subarrays with exactly 3 odd numbers.”","sub":"Sliding Window · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Number of subarrays with exactly 3 odd numbers.”"],"w":"Sliding Window Variable window directly atMost(3) − atMost(2) Fixed window of 3 Deque","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> atMost(3) − atMost(2)<\/p><p>“Exactly” isn’t monotone; subtract two “at most” counts.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sliding-window.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sliding-window-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sliding-window","t":"“Max number of vowels in any substring of length k.”","sub":"Sliding Window · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Max number of vowels in any substring of length k.”"],"w":"Sliding Window Fixed: add one, drop one Variable Prefix sums with dict Two pointers from both ends","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Fixed: add one, drop one<\/p><p>Size is given. Keep a running vowel count.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sliding-window.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-stack-0","k":"post","s":"stack","t":"The most recent unfinished thing","sub":"Stack · Last in, first out","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The most recent unfinished thing","explain The most recent unfinished thing","what is The most recent unfinished thing"],"w":"Stack Last in, first out","a":"<p>When you read <code>( [ {<\/code>, the next closer must match <code>{<\/code>, the most recent opener still waiting. Then <code>[<\/code>. A stack is exactly that waiting line: push when something opens, pop when it closes. If the top doesn't match, the string is broken.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","stack.html#s-the-most-recent-unfinished-thing"]]},{"i":"po-stack-2","k":"post","s":"stack","t":"A line of people waiting for a warmer day","sub":"Stack · The monotonic stack","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["A line of people waiting for a warmer day","explain A line of people waiting for a warmer day","what is A line of people waiting for a warmer day"],"w":"Stack The monotonic stack","a":"<p>Daily Temperatures: for each day, how long until a warmer one? Brute force looks ahead from every day: O(n²). Flip it around. Walk forward and keep a stack of days that haven't found their warmer day yet. When today is warmer than the day on top, today <i>is<\/i> that day's answer. Pop it and keep checking.<\/p><p>The days left in the stack are always in decreasing temperature order, which is why it's called monotonic.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","stack.html#s-a-line-of-people-waiting-for-a-warmer-day"]]},{"i":"po-stack-3","k":"post","s":"stack","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Stack · The monotonic stack","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Stack The monotonic stack","a":"<p>Filter by pattern. Name the reflex before revealing.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","stack.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-stack-4","k":"post","s":"stack","t":"How to price a stack solution","sub":"Stack · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a stack solution","explain How to price a stack solution","what is How to price a stack solution"],"w":"Stack Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Count pushes and pops, not loop nesting.<\/b> In a monotonic stack every element is pushed exactly once and popped at most once.<\/li><li><b>Total operations ≤ 2n → O(n)<\/b>, even though a <code>while<\/code> sits inside a <code>for<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Space<\/b> is the stack's maximum height: O(n) in the worst case (e.g. a strictly decreasing input never pops).<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","stack.html#s-how-to-price-a-stack-solution"]]},{"i":"po-stack-6","k":"post","s":"stack","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Stack · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Stack The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Bracket matching, RPN, decode<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Min stack operations<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> each<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Monotonic stack (next greater, spans)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>: each index pushed and popped once<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Largest rectangle in histogram<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Maximal rectangle in a matrix<\/td><td><code>O(R · C)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(C)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","stack.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"in-stack-0","k":"post","s":"stack","t":"A stack is “unfinished business”.","sub":"Stack · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["A stack is “unfinished business”."],"w":"Stack insight","a":"<p>Every stack problem has the same shape: something starts, something else interrupts it, and the interruption must finish first. Brackets, nested decodes, function calls, undo history.<\/p><p>That's also why recursion and stacks are interchangeable: the call stack is literally a stack of unfinished function calls. When recursion would go too deep, an explicit stack does the same job.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","stack.html#in-lifo"]]},{"i":"in-stack-1","k":"post","s":"stack","t":"The stack is a waiting room, and that’s why it’s O(n).","sub":"Stack · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The stack is a waiting room, and that’s why it’s O(n)."],"w":"Stack insight","a":"<p>In a monotonic stack, each element waits on the stack until the first element that beats it arrives. That element is its answer. Nothing is ever pushed twice, and nothing is popped twice.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">n pushes + at most n pops  →  O(n), no matter how the while loop looks<\/pre><p>Decreasing stack → answers “next greater”. Increasing stack → answers “next smaller”. Pick the direction by asking: what event releases someone from the waiting room?<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","stack.html#in-waiting"]]},{"i":"in-stack-2","k":"post","s":"stack","t":"One pop answers two questions.","sub":"Stack · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["One pop answers two questions."],"w":"Stack insight","a":"<p>When bar j is popped by bar i in the histogram, you learn two boundaries at once: i is the next smaller bar to its right (that's why it got popped), and the new top of the stack is the previous smaller bar on its left.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">width = i − (new top) − 1        area = height[j] × width<\/pre><p>Any problem asking “how far can this element extend before something smaller (or bigger) stops it” is this pattern: sum of subarray minimums, maximal rectangle, stock span.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","stack.html#in-boundary"]]},{"i":"in-stack-3","k":"post","s":"stack","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Stack · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Stack insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","stack.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-stack-q-k1","k":"quiz","s":"stack","t":"Input \"((\" . The loop finishes without returning False. What does is_valid return?","sub":"Stack · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Input \"((\" . The loop finishes without returning False. What does is_valid return?"],"w":"Stack True False, because the stack still holds two openers An error It depends on Python version","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> False, because the stack still holds two openers<\/p><p><code>return not stack<\/code>: leftover openers were never closed.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","stack.html#q-k1"]]},{"i":"qz-stack-q-k2","k":"quiz","s":"stack","t":"MinStack stores (value, min so far) . Why not one global min variable?","sub":"Stack · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["MinStack stores (value, min so far) . Why not one global min variable?"],"w":"Stack Pairs are faster After popping the current min, a single variable can’t recover the previous min Python has no global variables To support duplicates only","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> After popping the current min, a single variable can’t recover the previous min<\/p><p>Each frame remembers the min beneath it, so pops restore the old min in O(1).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","stack.html#q-k2"]]},{"i":"qz-stack-q-k3","k":"quiz","s":"stack","t":"For “next smaller element to the right”, which order do values on the stack keep?","sub":"Stack · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["For “next smaller element to the right”, which order do values on the stack keep?"],"w":"Stack Decreasing Increasing Sorted by index only Random","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Increasing<\/p><p>You pop when the current is smaller than the top, so whatever survives is increasing.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","stack.html#q-k3"]]},{"i":"qz-stack-q-k4","k":"quiz","s":"stack","t":"Largest Rectangle appends a sentinel 0 to heights. Why?","sub":"Stack · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Largest Rectangle appends a sentinel 0 to heights. Why?"],"w":"Stack To avoid an empty list It forces every remaining bar to be popped and measured at the end To handle negative heights For sorting","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> It forces every remaining bar to be popped and measured at the end<\/p><p>Without it, bars still on the stack at the end would never get their area computed.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","stack.html#q-k4"]]},{"i":"qz-stack-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"stack","t":"Valid parentheses on a string of length n. Time and space?","sub":"Stack · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Valid parentheses on a string of length n. Time and space?"],"w":"Stack O(n), O(1) O(n), O(n) O(n²), O(n) O(log n), O(n)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n), O(n)<\/p><p>One pass; the stack can hold up to n opening brackets (“((((…”).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","stack.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-stack-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"stack","t":"Largest rectangle in histogram with a monotonic stack. Time?","sub":"Stack · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Largest rectangle in histogram with a monotonic stack. Time?"],"w":"Stack O(n²) O(n log n) O(n) O(n³)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p>Each bar is pushed and popped once; each pop computes one area in O(1).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","stack.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-stack-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"stack","t":"“For each stock price, how many consecutive days up to today had price ≤ today?” (901)","sub":"Stack · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“For each stock price, how many consecutive days up to today had price ≤ today?” (901)"],"w":"Stack Plain stack Monotonic decreasing stack of (price, span) Queue Two pointers","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Monotonic decreasing stack of (price, span)<\/p><p>Pop days with price ≤ today and absorb their spans.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","stack.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-stack-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"stack","t":"“Remove duplicate letters so the result is the smallest in lexicographic order.” (316)","sub":"Stack · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Remove duplicate letters so the result is the smallest in lexicographic order.” (316)"],"w":"Stack Monotonic increasing stack with a “last occurrence” check Plain stack Hash set only Sliding window","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Monotonic increasing stack with a “last occurrence” check<\/p><p>Like Remove K Digits: pop a bigger top if it appears again later.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","stack.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-stack-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"stack","t":"“Simplify a Unix path like /a/./b/../../c/.”","sub":"Stack · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Simplify a Unix path like /a/./b/../../c/.”"],"w":"Stack Monotonic stack Plain stack of folder names Sliding window Two pointers","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Plain stack of folder names<\/p><p>“..” cancels the most recent folder: pop.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","stack.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-binary-search-0","k":"post","s":"binary-search","t":"Halve the haystack","sub":"Binary Search · Find the boundary","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Halve the haystack","explain Halve the haystack","what is Halve the haystack"],"w":"Binary Search Find the boundary","a":"<p>Sorted data lets one comparison throw away half of what's left. Check the middle: if the target is bigger, everything to the left of mid is useless, and vice versa. 1,000,000 elements need about 20 checks.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","binary-search.html#s-halve-the-haystack"]]},{"i":"po-binary-search-2","k":"post","s":"binary-search","t":"Guess, check, halve","sub":"Binary Search · Search the answer","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Guess, check, halve","explain Guess, check, halve","what is Guess, check, halve"],"w":"Binary Search Search the answer","a":"<p>Koko eats bananas at speed k. What's the slowest speed that finishes in h hours? Nothing is sorted here, but the <i>answers<\/i> are: if speed 5 works, then 6, 7, 8 also work. That's a FFFFTTTT pattern over speeds, so binary search finds the first T.<\/p><p>All you need is a <code>feasible(x)<\/code> function and a range [lo, hi] that surely contains the answer.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","binary-search.html#s-guess-check-halve"]]},{"i":"po-binary-search-3","k":"post","s":"binary-search","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Binary Search · Search the answer","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Binary Search Search the answer","a":"<p>Filter by type. Name the predicate before revealing.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","binary-search.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-binary-search-4","k":"post","s":"binary-search","t":"How to price a binary search","sub":"Binary Search · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a binary search","explain How to price a binary search","what is How to price a binary search"],"w":"Binary Search Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Count halvings.<\/b> n → n/2 → n/4 → … → 1 takes log₂ n steps. 1,000 → 10, 1,000,000 → 20, 1,000,000,000 → 30.<\/li><li><b>Multiply by the work per step.<\/b> Comparing a[mid] is O(1). Binary search on the answer calls a feasibility check each step, often O(n) → O(n log range).<\/li><li><b>Add the sort<\/b> if the data wasn't sorted: O(n log n), worth it only if you'll search many times.<\/li><li><b>Space<\/b>: O(1) iterative, O(log n) recursive.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","binary-search.html#s-how-to-price-a-binary-search"]]},{"i":"po-binary-search-6","k":"post","s":"binary-search","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Binary Search · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Binary Search The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Search a sorted array<\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Search a sorted R × C matrix as one list<\/td><td><code>O(log(R · C))<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Binary search on the answer<\/td><td><code>O(log(range) × cost of feasible)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Median of two sorted arrays<\/td><td><code>O(log(min(m, n)))<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Kth smallest in sorted matrix<\/td><td><code>O(n · log(max − min))<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","binary-search.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"in-binary-search-0","k":"post","s":"binary-search","t":"Binary search finds where a yes/no answer flips.","sub":"Binary Search · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Binary search finds where a yes/no answer flips."],"w":"Binary Search insight","a":"<p>Forget “find the target”. Every binary search is about a predicate that is False for a while and then True forever:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">F F F F F T T T T   →   find the first T<\/pre><p>“nums[i] ≥ target”, “version i is bad”, “speed k finishes in time”, “nums[mid] ≤ nums[hi]”. Once you can name that predicate and prove it only flips once, the template is always the same. Exact-match search is just a special case.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","binary-search.html#in-predicate"]]},{"i":"in-binary-search-1","k":"post","s":"binary-search","t":"Rotation breaks sortedness in exactly one place.","sub":"Binary Search · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Rotation breaks sortedness in exactly one place."],"w":"Binary Search insight","a":"<p>A rotated sorted array has one “cliff” where a big value is followed by a small one. Any mid splits the array into two halves, and the cliff can only be in one of them. The other half is perfectly sorted.<\/p><p>So each step: find the sorted half (compare nums[lo] with nums[mid]), check whether the target lies within its range, and throw away the half that can't contain it. Same O(log n).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","binary-search.html#in-halves"]]},{"i":"in-binary-search-2","k":"post","s":"binary-search","t":"When finding is hard but checking is easy, search the answers.","sub":"Binary Search · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["When finding is hard but checking is easy, search the answers."],"w":"Binary Search insight","a":"<p>Koko, shipping capacity, split array, minimize the max distance: you can't compute the answer directly, but given a candidate you can check it greedily in O(n). And candidates are monotone: if 7 works, 8 works.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">total = O(n) check × O(log(hi − lo)) candidates<\/pre><p>The setup is always: lo = smallest possibly valid answer, hi = one that surely works, find the first x where feasible(x) is True.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","binary-search.html#in-answerspace"]]},{"i":"in-binary-search-3","k":"post","s":"binary-search","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Binary Search · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Binary Search insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","binary-search.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-binary-search-q-b1","k":"quiz","s":"binary-search","t":"Lower-bound template: while lo < hi with hi = mid . What is true when the loop ends?","sub":"Binary Search · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Lower-bound template: while lo < hi with hi = mid . What is true when the loop ends?"],"w":"Binary Search lo > hi lo == hi, and it’s the first index where the predicate is True (or n if none) lo is the last False Nothing; you must check both","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> lo == hi, and it’s the first index where the predicate is True (or n if none)<\/p><p>The invariant “answer ∈ [lo, hi]” plus lo == hi pins it down.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","binary-search.html#q-b1"]]},{"i":"qz-binary-search-q-b2","k":"quiz","s":"binary-search","t":"Which loop can run forever?","sub":"Binary Search · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Which loop can run forever?"],"w":"Binary Search None of them This one: when hi = lo + 1 and ok(mid) is True, lo stays the same Only if a is empty Only with negatives","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1\nwhile lo &lt; hi:\n    mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n    if ok(mid):\n        lo = mid\n    else:\n        hi = mid - 1<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> This one: when hi = lo + 1 and ok(mid) is True, lo stays the same<\/p><p>mid rounds down to lo, and lo = mid changes nothing. Round up: (lo + hi + 1) // 2.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","binary-search.html#q-b2"]]},{"i":"qz-binary-search-q-b4","k":"quiz","s":"binary-search","t":"Find Min in Rotated Sorted Array compares nums[mid] with nums[hi] . Why not with nums[lo] ?","sub":"Binary Search · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Find Min in Rotated Sorted Array compares nums[mid] with nums[hi] . Why not with nums[lo] ?"],"w":"Binary Search No reason With nums[lo], an unrotated array looks the same as a rotated one and you go the wrong way It’s faster To handle duplicates","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> With nums[lo], an unrotated array looks the same as a rotated one and you go the wrong way<\/p><p>nums[lo] ≤ nums[mid] is true for both [1,2,3,4,5] (min left) and [3,4,5,1,2] (min right). Comparing with hi distinguishes them.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","binary-search.html#q-b4"]]},{"i":"qz-binary-search-q-b3","k":"quiz","s":"binary-search","t":"Koko: when hours(mid) ≤ h , why hi = mid and not hi = mid − 1 ?","sub":"Binary Search · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Koko: when hours(mid) ≤ h , why hi = mid and not hi = mid − 1 ?"],"w":"Binary Search Typo-tolerant mid itself might be the slowest speed that works It’s faster To avoid negatives","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> mid itself might be the slowest speed that works<\/p><p>A feasible mid is a candidate answer; discarding it could skip the answer.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","binary-search.html#q-b3"]]},{"i":"qz-binary-search-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"binary-search","t":"Search a 2-D matrix of R × C (rows sorted, each row starts after the previous ends) by treating it as one array. Time?","sub":"Binary Search · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Search a 2-D matrix of R × C (rows sorted, each row starts after the previous ends) by treating it as one array. Time?"],"w":"Binary Search O(R + C) O(log(R · C)) O(R log C) O(R · C)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(log(R · C))<\/p><p>It’s one sorted array of R·C items: log₂(R·C) halvings.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","binary-search.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-binary-search-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"binary-search","t":"Ship packages within D days: binary search capacity in [max(w), sum(w)], each check is O(n). Time?","sub":"Binary Search · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Ship packages within D days: binary search capacity in [max(w), sum(w)], each check is O(n). Time?"],"w":"Binary Search O(n) O(n log(sum w)) O(n²) O(log n)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n log(sum w))<\/p><p>log of the answer range × O(n) per feasibility check.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","binary-search.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-binary-search-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"binary-search","t":"“Minimum number of days so that you can make m bouquets.” (1482)","sub":"Binary Search · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Minimum number of days so that you can make m bouquets.” (1482)"],"w":"Binary Search Sort and two pointers Binary search days with feasible(day) = can make m bouquets Sliding window Heap","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Binary search days with feasible(day) = can make m bouquets<\/p><p>If you can make them by day d, you can by any later day. Monotone predicate on the answer.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","binary-search.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-binary-search-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"binary-search","t":"“Find the smallest divisor so the sum of ceil(n / d) ≤ threshold.” (1283)","sub":"Binary Search · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Find the smallest divisor so the sum of ceil(n / d) ≤ threshold.” (1283)"],"w":"Binary Search Binary search d Prefix sums DP Greedy sort","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Binary search d<\/p><p>Bigger divisor → smaller sum. First d where the sum fits.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","binary-search.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-binary-search-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"binary-search","t":"“Search a 2-D matrix where rows and columns are sorted but rows don’t continue each other.” (240)","sub":"Binary Search · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Search a 2-D matrix where rows and columns are sorted but rows don’t continue each other.” (240)"],"w":"Binary Search Flatten and binary search Staircase walk from the top-right corner Heap Union-find","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Staircase walk from the top-right corner<\/p><p>Not one sorted list. From top-right, each comparison removes a row or a column: O(R + C).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","binary-search.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-linked-list-0","k":"post","s":"linked-list","t":"Save, then cut","sub":"Linked List · Pointer surgery","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Save, then cut","explain Save, then cut","what is Save, then cut"],"w":"Linked List Pointer surgery","a":"<p>A linked list node only knows its <code>next<\/code>. The moment you overwrite <code>curr.next<\/code>, the rest of the list is unreachable unless you saved it first. Every list bug is a variation of that. So the rhythm is always: <b>save<\/b> what you're about to lose, <b>rewire<\/b>, then <b>step<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","linked-list.html#s-save-then-cut"]]},{"i":"po-linked-list-2","k":"post","s":"linked-list","t":"The tortoise and the hare","sub":"Linked List · Fast & slow","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The tortoise and the hare","explain The tortoise and the hare","what is The tortoise and the hare"],"w":"Linked List Fast & slow","a":"<p>Move <code>slow<\/code> one step and <code>fast<\/code> two. When fast hits the end, slow is in the middle. If there's a cycle, fast never hits the end; it laps slow and they meet inside the loop. No visited set required.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","linked-list.html#s-the-tortoise-and-the-hare"]]},{"i":"po-linked-list-3","k":"post","s":"linked-list","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Linked List · Fast & slow","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Linked List Fast & slow","a":"<p>Filter by technique. Draw the arrows, then reveal.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","linked-list.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-linked-list-4","k":"post","s":"linked-list","t":"How to price a linked-list solution","sub":"Linked List · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a linked-list solution","explain How to price a linked-list solution","what is How to price a linked-list solution"],"w":"Linked List Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Count hops.<\/b> One walk to the end is n hops → O(n). There's no random access, so “get the k-th node” is O(k).<\/li><li><b>Fast and slow pointers<\/b>: fast takes 2 hops per round, so the loop runs about n/2 times → still O(n), with O(1) space.<\/li><li><b>Recursion on a list<\/b> (reverse, merge) uses one stack frame per node → O(n) space. The iterative version is O(1).<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","linked-list.html#s-how-to-price-a-linked-list-solution"]]},{"i":"po-linked-list-6","k":"post","s":"linked-list","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Linked List · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Linked List The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technique<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Reverse (iterative)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Merge two sorted<\/td><td><code>O(m + n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Fast / slow (middle, cycle)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Merge k sorted with a heap<\/td><td><code>O(N log k)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Sort list (merge sort)<\/td><td><code>O(n log n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code> stack<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>LRU get / put<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(capacity)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","linked-list.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"in-linked-list-0","k":"post","s":"linked-list","t":"Draw it before you code it.","sub":"Linked List · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Draw it before you code it."],"w":"Linked List insight","a":"<p>List bugs are almost always an assignment in the wrong order. Before writing code, draw three boxes and the arrows, then write one line per arrow you change, asking for each: “after this line, can I still reach everything I need?”<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">save what you’ll lose   →   rewire   →   step<\/pre><p>Python's tuple assignment can do it in one line (<code>curr.next, prev, curr = prev, curr, curr.next<\/code>) because the whole right side is evaluated before any assignment happens.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","linked-list.html#in-save"]]},{"i":"in-linked-list-1","k":"post","s":"linked-list","t":"Why LRU needs both a dict and a doubly linked list.","sub":"Linked List · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Why LRU needs both a dict and a doubly linked list."],"w":"Linked List insight","a":"<p>You need two things in O(1): find an entry by key, and know which entry is the oldest. A dict does the first. A list ordered by recency does the second. But moving an entry to the front needs removing it from the middle, which in a singly linked list needs its predecessor.<\/p><p>A doubly linked node knows its prev, so <code>node.prev.next = node.next<\/code> unlinks it instantly. Sentinel head and tail nodes remove every empty-list special case. (Python's <code>OrderedDict<\/code> is exactly this structure.)<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","linked-list.html#in-lru"]]},{"i":"in-linked-list-2","k":"post","s":"linked-list","t":"Why phase 2 lands exactly on the entrance.","sub":"Linked List · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Why phase 2 lands exactly on the entrance."],"w":"Linked List insight","a":"<p>Let the distance from head to the entrance be a, and the meeting point be b steps into the cycle of length c. Slow walked a + b. Fast walked twice that, and the extra a + b is a whole number of laps: a + b = k·c.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">a = k·c − b   →   from the meeting point, a more steps reaches the entrance<\/pre><p>So one pointer from the head and one from the meeting point, both stepping by one, arrive at the entrance together. The same trick finds the duplicate in Find the Duplicate Number (287).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","linked-list.html#in-floyd"]]},{"i":"in-linked-list-3","k":"post","s":"linked-list","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Linked List · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Linked List insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","linked-list.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-linked-list-q-l1","k":"quiz","s":"linked-list","t":"In reverse, what happens if you write curr.next = prev before saving nxt = curr.next ?","sub":"Linked List · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In reverse, what happens if you write curr.next = prev before saving nxt = curr.next ?"],"w":"Linked List Nothing The rest of the list becomes unreachable, so the loop stops after one node Infinite loop It still works in Python","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> The rest of the list becomes unreachable, so the loop stops after one node<\/p><p>curr.next was the only reference to the remaining nodes.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","linked-list.html#q-l1"]]},{"i":"qz-linked-list-q-l2","k":"quiz","s":"linked-list","t":"Remove Nth From End uses a dummy and moves fast n + 1 steps. Why n + 1 and not n?","sub":"Linked List · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Remove Nth From End uses a dummy and moves fast n + 1 steps. Why n + 1 and not n?"],"w":"Linked List Off-by-one doesn’t matter So slow stops at the node before the target, which is the one you need to rewire Because lists are 1-indexed To handle cycles","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> So slow stops at the node <i>before<\/i> the target, which is the one you need to rewire<\/p><p>Deleting needs the predecessor. The dummy makes the predecessor exist even when the head is removed.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","linked-list.html#q-l2"]]},{"i":"qz-linked-list-q-l3","k":"quiz","s":"linked-list","t":"For 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 6, which node does while fast and fast.next return?","sub":"Linked List · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["For 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 6, which node does while fast and fast.next return?"],"w":"Linked List 3 4 Either, randomly 6","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 4<\/p><p>This version returns the second middle. Starting fast at head.next returns the first middle, which is what merge sort needs.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","linked-list.html#q-l3"]]},{"i":"qz-linked-list-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"linked-list","t":"Merge two sorted lists of lengths m and n iteratively. Time and extra space?","sub":"Linked List · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Merge two sorted lists of lengths m and n iteratively. Time and extra space?"],"w":"Linked List O(m·n), O(1) O(m + n), O(1) O(m + n), O(m + n) O(log(m + n)), O(1)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(m + n), O(1)<\/p><p>Each step moves one node; relinking existing nodes needs no new memory.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","linked-list.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-linked-list-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"linked-list","t":"Copy a list into a Python list, sort it, rebuild the linked list. Time and space?","sub":"Linked List · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Copy a list into a Python list, sort it, rebuild the linked list. Time and space?"],"w":"Linked List O(n), O(1) O(n log n), O(n) O(n²), O(1) O(n log n), O(1)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n log n), O(n)<\/p><p>The sort dominates time; the copy is O(n) space. Merge sort on the list itself can do O(n log n) with O(log n) stack.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","linked-list.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-linked-list-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"linked-list","t":"“Rotate the list to the right by k places.”","sub":"Linked List · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Rotate the list to the right by k places.”"],"w":"Linked List Reverse three times Find length, close the ring, cut at n − k % n Fast and slow only Heap","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Find length, close the ring, cut at n − k % n<\/p><p>Rotation is just choosing a new head on a ring.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","linked-list.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-linked-list-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"linked-list","t":"“Sort a linked list in O(n log n).”","sub":"Linked List · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Sort a linked list in O(n log n).”"],"w":"Linked List Copy to an array Merge sort: split at the middle with fast/slow, merge with a dummy Quick sort Heap of all nodes","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Merge sort: split at the middle with fast/slow, merge with a dummy<\/p><p>Merge sort needs no random access, just splitting and merging.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","linked-list.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-linked-list-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"linked-list","t":"“Partition the list so all nodes < x come before nodes ≥ x, keeping order.” (86)","sub":"Linked List · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Partition the list so all nodes < x come before nodes ≥ x, keeping order.” (86)"],"w":"Linked List Two dummy lists, then join them Reverse Fast and slow Sort","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Two dummy lists, then join them<\/p><p>Build “small” and “big” lists with their own dummies and tails, then connect small’s tail to big’s head.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","linked-list.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-0","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Three kinds of special cards","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trust the function","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Three kinds of special cards","explain Three kinds of special cards","what is Three kinds of special cards"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trust the function","a":"<p>Between the lessons and labs you'll keep running into these:<\/p><p>The labs whisper reflexes too: watch for the dark ⚡ line under the narration while you step.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-three-kinds-of-special-cards"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-1","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Recursion is delegation","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trust the function","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Recursion is delegation","explain Recursion is delegation","what is Recursion is delegation"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trust the function","a":"<p>You're in a dark cinema and want to know your row number. You can't see, so you tap the person in front: “Which row are you in?” They don't know either, so they tap the person in front of <em>them<\/em>. This repeats until someone in row 1 says “1”. Then answers travel back: 1, 2, 3… each person adds one and passes it on.<\/p><p>Nobody solved the whole problem. Each person did one tiny step and trusted the next person. That is recursion.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-recursion-is-delegation"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-2","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"The three questions","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trust the function","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The three questions","explain The three questions","what is The three questions"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trust the function","a":"<p>Every recursive function is the answer to three questions. Ask them in this order:<\/p><ul><li><b>What's the smallest input I can answer instantly?<\/b>That's the base case. Without it, the questions never stop.<\/li><li><b>If a smaller version were already solved, what would I ask it?<\/b>That's the recursive call. It has to move toward the base case.<\/li><li><b>How do I turn its answer into mine?<\/b>That's the combine step.<\/li><\/ul><p>Here are the answers for factorial:<\/p><p>Notice what's missing: tracing every call in your head. Don't. Assume the smaller call returns the right answer and only check one level. This is the <b>leap of faith<\/b>, and it's the whole trick.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-the-three-questions"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-3","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Two calls make a tree","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trust the function","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Two calls make a tree","explain Two calls make a tree","what is Two calls make a tree"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trust the function","a":"<p>When a function calls itself twice, the calls branch. <code>fib(n)<\/code> asks <code>fib(n - 1)<\/code> and <code>fib(n - 2)<\/code>, and each of those asks two more. Look at the tree below and you'll spot the same question asked again and again. That's wasted work.<\/p><p><b>Memoization<\/b> fixes it: write each answer down the first time, and next time just read it. Flip the switch and watch whole subtrees disappear.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-two-calls-make-a-tree"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-4","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Where recursion bites","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trust the function","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Where recursion bites","explain Where recursion bites","what is Where recursion bites"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trust the function","a":"<ul><li><b>No base case, or one you never reach.<\/b> Calls pile up until Python raises <code>RecursionError<\/code>. CPython's default limit is about 1000 frames.<\/li><li><b>Not shrinking.<\/b> <code>f(n)<\/code> must call something closer to the base case, like <code>f(n - 1)<\/code> or <code>f(node.left)<\/code>, never <code>f(n)<\/code> again.<\/li><li><b>Very deep inputs.<\/b> A skewed tree or long linked list with 10<sup>5<\/sup> nodes will overflow. Use an explicit stack, or raise the limit with <code>sys.setrecursionlimit<\/code> carefully.<\/li><li><b>Mutable default arguments.<\/b> <code>def f(x, seen=[])<\/code> shares one list across every call, forever. Default to <code>None<\/code> and create the list inside.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-where-recursion-bites"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-5","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Warm-ups interviewers still ask","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trust the function","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Warm-ups interviewers still ask","explain Warm-ups interviewers still ask","what is Warm-ups interviewers still ask"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trust the function","a":"<p>Pure recursion, no trees yet. Each one is the three questions and a leap of faith. If any of these feel hard, it's the leap of faith that needs practice, not the problem.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-warm-ups-interviewers-still-ask"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-6","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Every node is a tiny root","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trees are recursion you can see","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Every node is a tiny root","explain Every node is a tiny root","what is Every node is a tiny root"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trees are recursion you can see","a":"<p>A binary tree is either empty, or a node with a left tree and a right tree. The definition is recursive, so the solutions are too. Almost every tree problem fits this shape:<\/p><p>Your only jobs: decide what an empty tree returns, and how to combine left, right and the node itself. The recursion handles the rest.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-every-node-is-a-tiny-root"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-7","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Each node is passed three times","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trees are recursion you can see","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Each node is passed three times","explain Each node is passed three times","what is Each node is passed three times"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trees are recursion you can see","a":"<p>Walk around the tree hugging its outline, starting on the left of the root. You pass every node three times: on its <b>left side<\/b> before going down, <b>underneath<\/b> between its two subtrees, and on its <b>right side<\/b> after both.<\/p><p>Do the work on the first pass and you get preorder. Second pass: inorder. Third: postorder. It's the same walk every time; you only choose when to speak. Tap a button to number the passes.<\/p><p>Output: tap a traversal<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-each-node-is-passed-three-times"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-8","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Information flows two ways","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trees are recursion you can see","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Information flows two ways","explain Information flows two ways","what is Information flows two ways"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trees are recursion you can see","a":"<p>The hybrid shows up constantly in interviews: <b>return what the parent needs, but update a best-so-far variable on the side<\/b>. Diameter and max path sum both work this way. You'll see it in the problems below.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-information-flows-two-ways"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-9","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Reshaping a tree without losing it","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trees are recursion you can see","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Reshaping a tree without losing it","explain Reshaping a tree without losing it","what is Reshaping a tree without losing it"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trees are recursion you can see","a":"<p>Flattening, deleting and rebuilding all share one danger: overwrite a pointer before you've used it and that part of the tree is gone. The cleanest fix is to only rewire nodes whose subtrees are already finished. Watch Flatten (114) do exactly that.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-reshaping-a-tree-without-losing-it"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-10","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"The tree problems that matter","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trees are recursion you can see","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The tree problems that matter","explain The tree problems that matter","what is The tree problems that matter"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trees are recursion you can see","a":"<p>23 problems, from the classics to the ones that separate candidates: Flatten (114), Serialize (297), Path Sum III (437), Distance K (863), Cameras (968). Filter by pattern, answer the three questions yourself, then reveal the code.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-the-tree-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-11","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Watch the thinking happen","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Trees are recursion you can see","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Watch the thinking happen","explain Watch the thinking happen","what is Watch the thinking happen"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Trees are recursion you can see","a":"<p>Two problems you haven't seen yet. Don't jump to the code. Tap through the thoughts and try to say the next one before it appears.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-watch-the-thinking-happen"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-12","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"A maze with a breadcrumb trail","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Choose, explore, un-choose","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["A maze with a breadcrumb trail","explain A maze with a breadcrumb trail","what is A maze with a breadcrumb trail"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Choose, explore, un-choose","a":"<p>At each fork in a maze, you pick a path and keep walking. Dead end? Walk back to the last fork and try the next path. You never draw the whole maze; you just explore it one decision at a time.<\/p><p>In code, “walking back” means <b>undoing your last choice<\/b>, so the next option starts from exactly the state you had before. That undo is the only new idea compared to normal recursion.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-a-maze-with-a-breadcrumb-trail"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-13","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"The template","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Choose, explore, un-choose","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The template","explain The template","what is The template"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Choose, explore, un-choose","a":"<p>Every backtracking solution you'll write is this, with the blanks filled in:<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-the-template"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-14","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Four questions before you code","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Choose, explore, un-choose","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Four questions before you code","explain Four questions before you code","what is Four questions before you code"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Choose, explore, un-choose","a":"<ul><li><b>State: what am I carrying?<\/b>Usually a <code>path<\/code> list plus an index, a remaining sum, or a <code>used<\/code> array.<\/li><li><b>Choices: what can I do next from here?<\/b>This becomes your <code>for<\/code> loop.<\/li><li><b>Goal: when do I record an answer?<\/b>At every node (subsets), only at full length (permutations), or when a target hits zero.<\/li><li><b>Prune: when can I stop early?<\/b>Sum too big, string invalid, square attacked. Good pruning is what makes backtracking fast.<\/li><\/ul><p>The explorer below answers these four for each problem. Read them, then press Play.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-four-questions-before-you-code"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-15","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Start index or used[]?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Choose, explore, un-choose","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Start index or used[]?","explain Start index or used[]?","what is Start index or used[]?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Choose, explore, un-choose","a":"<p><b>Order doesn't matter<\/b> ({1, 2} is the same as {2, 1}): subsets, combinations, combination sum. Pass a <code>start<\/code> index and only look forward. That alone stops you from building the same set in a different order.<\/p><p><b>Order matters<\/b> ([1, 2] differs from [2, 1]): permutations. Any position can take any unused element, so loop from 0 and track <code>used<\/code>.<\/p><p><b>Can an element be reused?<\/b> Recurse with <code>i<\/code>. Each element at most once? Recurse with <code>i + 1<\/code>.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-start-index-or-used"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-16","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Killing duplicates","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Choose, explore, un-choose","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Killing duplicates","explain Killing duplicates","what is Killing duplicates"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Choose, explore, un-choose","a":"<p>Input has repeats, like <code>[1, 2, 2]<\/code>? The two 2s would grow identical subtrees and give you duplicate answers. Fix: <b>sort<\/b>, then skip a value if it equals the one right before it <b>at the same level<\/b>:<\/p><p>“Same level” is the key phrase. Siblings with the same value are duplicates, so skip the twin. But going <em>deeper<\/em>, picking the second 2 right after the first is fine; that's how you get <code>[2, 2]<\/code>. Run <b>Subsets II<\/b> in the explorer above to see the skipped branch in red.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-killing-duplicates"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-17","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Grids: mark, explore, unmark","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Choose, explore, un-choose","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Grids: mark, explore, unmark","explain Grids: mark, explore, unmark","what is Grids: mark, explore, unmark"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Choose, explore, un-choose","a":"<p>On a grid, your “path” is the set of cells you're standing on. Mark a cell <b>before<\/b> exploring its four neighbours so you don't walk in circles. Unmark it <b>on the way out<\/b> so a different path can use it later. Same choose / explore / un-choose rhythm, just on a board.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-grids-mark-explore-unmark"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-18","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"The backtracking problems that matter","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Choose, explore, un-choose","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The backtracking problems that matter","explain The backtracking problems that matter","what is The backtracking problems that matter"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Choose, explore, un-choose","a":"<p>The explorer already covered Subsets (78), Subsets II (90), Permutations (46), Combination Sum (39), Generate Parentheses (22), N-Queens (51) and Word Search (79). These are the rest, including the hard ones: Word Search II (212), Expression Add Operators (282), Unique Paths III (980). Answer the four questions before revealing.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-the-backtracking-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-19","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Watch the thinking happen","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Choose, explore, un-choose","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Watch the thinking happen","explain Watch the thinking happen","what is Watch the thinking happen"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Choose, explore, un-choose","a":"<p>Same drill: say each thought before you reveal it. Notice how often the move is a reflex you already locked in.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-watch-the-thinking-happen-2"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-20","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Three questions price any recursion","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Three questions price any recursion","explain Three questions price any recursion","what is Three questions price any recursion"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>How many calls does one call make, and on what size?<\/b> <code>fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)<\/code> → 2 calls, each about n − 1. <code>search(mid+1, hi)<\/code> → 1 call on half.<\/li><li><b>How much work happens inside one call, not counting the calls?<\/b> A comparison is O(1). A loop over the input, slicing <code>a[:mid]<\/code> or copying <code>path[:]<\/code> is O(n).<\/li><li><b>Draw the tree, add it up level by level.<\/b> Level total = (calls on that level) × (work per call). <b>Time<\/b> = sum of all levels. <b>Space<\/b> = how deep the tree goes (that's the most frames alive at once), plus any extra lists you keep.<\/li><\/ul><pre class=\"ap-pre\">time = Σ levels (calls × work per call)     space = max depth × frame + extra<\/pre>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-three-questions-price-any-recursion"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-21","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Watch the levels add up","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Watch the levels add up","explain Watch the levels add up","what is Watch the levels add up"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Counting the cost","a":"<p>Each tab below draws the call tree one level at a time. Boxes are calls (labelled with their input), the right column is that level's work. Watch which level is biggest: that's the whole story.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-watch-the-levels-add-up"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-22","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Space: the deepest point, not the total","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Space: the deepest point, not the total","explain Space: the deepest point, not the total","what is Space: the deepest point, not the total"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Counting the cost","a":"<p>fib(30) makes 2.7 million calls, but never more than 30 are alive at once. When a call returns, its frame is popped and the memory is reused. So <b>stack space = the height of the tree<\/b>, not its size. Then add anything you build on the side: a memo dict of n entries is O(n), the <code>path<\/code> list in backtracking is O(n), and the output list is usually counted separately.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-space-the-deepest-point-not-the-total"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-23","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Shortcuts worth memorising","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Shortcuts worth memorising","explain Shortcuts worth memorising","what is Shortcuts worth memorising"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Counting the cost","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table> <thead><tr><th>Shape<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><th>Example<\/th><\/tr><\/thead> <tbody> <tr><td>1 call on n − 1, O(1) work<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td>factorial, linked list recursion<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>1 call on n / 2, O(1) work<\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code><\/td><td>binary search, fast pow<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>2 calls on n / 2, O(1) work<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code><\/td><td>balanced tree traversal<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>2 calls on n / 2, O(n) work<\/td><td><code>O(n log n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td>merge sort<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>2 calls on n − 1<\/td><td><code>O(2ⁿ)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td>naive fib, Hanoi<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Memoised: S states × W work<\/td><td><code>O(S · W)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(S)<\/code><\/td><td>fib memo O(n), grid paths O(mn)<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Each node of a tree once<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(h)<\/code><\/td><td>height, diameter, path sum<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Subsets / permutations<\/td><td><code>O(n · 2ⁿ)<\/code> / <code>O(n · n!)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td>backtracking with a copy per answer<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody> <\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-shortcuts-worth-memorising"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-25","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table> <thead><tr><th>Problem<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Why<\/th><\/tr><\/thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Tree traversal / most tree DP<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>, space <code>O(h)<\/code><\/td><td>Each node once; stack as deep as the tree<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Subsets<\/td><td><code>O(n · 2ⁿ)<\/code><\/td><td>2ⁿ subsets, copying each costs up to n<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Permutations<\/td><td><code>O(n · n!)<\/code><\/td><td>n! leaves, copy of length n each<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Combinations (n choose k)<\/td><td><code>O(k · C(n, k))<\/code><\/td><td>One copy per combination<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Generate parentheses<\/td><td><code>O(4ⁿ / √n)<\/code><\/td><td>Catalan number of valid strings<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>N-Queens<\/td><td><code>O(n!)<\/code><\/td><td>Each row has fewer safe columns than the last<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Word search<\/td><td><code>O(R · C · 3ᴸ)<\/code><\/td><td>Every start, then 3 new directions per letter<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody> <\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-26","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex from the app, shuffled. Read the trigger, say the move out loud, then flip. Get a streak of 10 to finish the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"po-recursion-27","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Final round: name the pattern","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Final round: name the pattern","explain Final round: name the pattern","what is Final round: name the pattern"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking The decoder","a":"<p>Five problem statements. Pick the approach you'd start with.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#s-final-round-name-the-pattern"]]},{"i":"in-recursion-0","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"The leap of faith isn’t a leap.","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The leap of faith isn’t a leap."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking insight","a":"<p>It’s proof by induction. In math you prove a claim for the smallest case, then show “true for n − 1 implies true for n”. Your base case is the first part. Your recursive case is the second.<\/p><p>If both hold, the function is correct for <i>every<\/i> input, and you never traced a single call. That’s why experienced engineers don’t trace: they check those two pieces and stop.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">base case right  +  one level right (assuming smaller is right)  =  all levels right<\/pre>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#in-induction"]]},{"i":"in-recursion-1","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Every recursion secretly draws a tree.","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Every recursion secretly draws a tree."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking insight","a":"<p>One recursive call draws a line (factorial). Two calls draw a binary tree (fib). A loop of k calls draws a k-way tree (every backtracking problem in chapter 3).<\/p><p>Once you see the tree, three things fall out for free. Time is the number of boxes times the work in one box. Space is the height, because that’s the deepest the stack gets. And memoization is just noticing two boxes with the same label and merging them, which turns a huge tree into a small graph.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">time = boxes × work per box     space = height<\/pre>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#in-tree"]]},{"i":"in-recursion-2","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Your return value is a contract.","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Your return value is a contract."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking insight","a":"<p>Before writing a tree function, finish this sentence: “<code>f(node)<\/code> returns ___ for the subtree rooted at node.” For height: “the number of nodes on the longest downward path.”<\/p><p>Now ask: is that also the final answer? If yes, return it and you’re done. If not (diameter wants a two-sided path, but a parent needs a one-sided height), you’ve just discovered you need the hybrid: return the contract, track the real answer on the side.<\/p><p>Most tree bugs are a contract that quietly changes halfway through the function.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#in-contract"]]},{"i":"in-recursion-3","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Count it → DP. List it → backtrack.","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Count it → DP. List it → backtrack."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking insight","a":"<p>Both explore the same tree of choices. The question decides which tool.<\/p><p>If it wants a number (how many ways, minimum cost, is it possible?), different branches reach identical states, so you memoize and the tree collapses. If it wants every actual answer written out, there’s nothing to collapse: you must reach each leaf anyway, and backtracking does exactly that with no wasted work.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#in-countlist"]]},{"i":"in-recursion-4","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"The stack is the path.","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The stack is the path."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking insight","a":"<p>Look back at the explorer. The blue boxes from the root to the orange box are the call stack, and their labels are exactly what’s inside <code>path<\/code>. That’s the invariant:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">path == the choices along the current root-to-node chain<\/pre><p>Choose pushes a box. Explore descends. Un-choose pops it so the invariant holds again before the loop moves to the next sibling. Every backtracking bug is a broken invariant: a missing pop, a pop in the wrong place, or a reference saved instead of a copy.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#in-stackpath"]]},{"i":"in-recursion-5","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Pruning changes the tree, not the code.","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Pruning changes the tree, not the code."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking insight","a":"<p>The template never changes. Pruning just cuts a subtree before it grows. You have two levers: check validity <i>before<\/i> recursing rather than after building a full answer, and order your choices so failures happen early (sort, or fill the most constrained slot first).<\/p><p>For 8 queens, trying every column in every row would be 8⁸ ≈ 16.7 million boards. With row-by-row placement and O(1) attack checks, the search makes about 2,000 placements.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#in-prune"]]},{"i":"in-recursion-6","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Graph DFS is backtracking that never un-chooses.","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Graph DFS is backtracking that never un-chooses."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking insight","a":"<p>In a graph traversal (number of islands, clone graph), visited is permanent: each cell is processed once, total. In word search, visited is temporary: a cell is off-limits only while it’s on the current path, then released.<\/p><p>So ask one question: <b>can another path legitimately reuse this cell?<\/b> Yes → unmark on the way out (backtracking, exponential). No → keep it marked (plain DFS, linear).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#in-graphdfs"]]},{"i":"in-recursion-7","k":"post","s":"recursion","t":"Find the heaviest level and you have the answer.","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Find the heaviest level and you have the answer."],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking insight","a":"<p>Once the tree is drawn, the levels form a series, and only three things can happen:<\/p><p><b>Top-heavy<\/b>: work shrinks going down (n + n/2 + n/4 + … ≈ 2n). The root level wins → O(top). <b>Balanced<\/b>: every level costs the same (merge sort, n per level) → O(level × number of levels) = n log n. <b>Bottom-heavy<\/b>: work grows going down (1 + 2 + 4 + … + 2ⁿ) → the leaves win → O(number of leaves).<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">shrinking → top level · equal → level × depth · growing → leaf count<\/pre><p>That’s the Master Theorem without the algebra.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","recursion.html#in-cxdominate"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-q1","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"What does f(3) print?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["What does f(3) print?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking 3 2 1 1 2 3 3 2 1 0 Nothing","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">def f(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return\n    print(n)\n    f(n - 1)<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> 3 2 1<\/p><p>The print happens <i>before<\/i> the recursive call, so each frame speaks on the way down: 3, then 2, then 1. The base case prints nothing.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-q1"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-q2","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Now the print moved below the call. What does f(3) print?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Now the print moved below the call. What does f(3) print?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking 3 2 1 1 2 3 0 1 2 3 3 3 3","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">def f(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return\n    f(n - 1)\n    print(n)<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> 1 2 3<\/p><p>Each print waits until the call below it returns. The deepest frame (n = 1) finishes first, so output comes on the way back up: 1, 2, 3. This exact difference is preorder vs postorder.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-q2"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-q3","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"How many calls does naive fib(5) make in total?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["How many calls does naive fib(5) make in total?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking 5 9 15 32","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 15<\/p><p>calls(n) = calls(n−1) + calls(n−2) + 1, giving 1, 1, 3, 5, 9, 15. It grows roughly like 1.6ⁿ. With memoization it drops to 9 calls, only 6 of which do real work.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-q3"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-q4","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Which traversal of a binary search tree returns the values in sorted order?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Which traversal of a binary search tree returns the values in sorted order?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Preorder Inorder Postorder Level order","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Inorder<\/p><p>Inorder visits left subtree, node, right subtree. In a BST everything on the left is smaller and everything on the right is bigger, so the output comes out sorted. Great for “kth smallest” and validating a BST.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-q4"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-q5","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"A node needs both subtrees' answers before it can compute its own (height, deleting the tree). Which order is that?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["A node needs both subtrees' answers before it can compute its own (height, deleting the tree). Which order is that?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Preorder Inorder Postorder Level order","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Postorder<\/p><p>Postorder handles both children first and the node last. That's why the bottom-up template (ask left, ask right, combine) is postorder.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-q5"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-q6","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Spot the bug. What does subsets([1, 2]) return?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Spot the bug. What does subsets([1, 2]) return?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking [[], [1], [1, 2], [2]] [[], [], [], []] [[1, 2]] It raises an error","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">def subsets(nums):\n    res, path = [], []\n    def backtrack(start):\n        res.append(path)        # &lt;- look here\n        for i in range(start, len(nums)):\n            path.append(nums[i])\n            backtrack(i + 1)\n            path.pop()\n    backtrack(0)\n    return res<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> [[], [], [], []]<\/p><p><code>res<\/code> holds four references to the <i>same<\/i> list. After all the pops, that list is empty, so every entry shows <code>[]<\/code>. Always snapshot: <code>path[:]<\/code> or <code>list(path)<\/code>.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-q6"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-q7","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Combination Sum lets you reuse a number, e.g. [2, 2, 3] for target 7. Which recursive call makes reuse possible?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Combination Sum lets you reuse a number, e.g. [2, 2, 3] for target 7. Which recursive call makes reuse possible?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking backtrack(i + 1, remaining - c[i]) backtrack(i, remaining - c[i]) backtrack(0, remaining - c[i]) backtrack(start + 1, remaining - c[i])","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> backtrack(i, remaining - c[i])<\/p><p>Passing <code>i<\/code> lets the next level pick the same element again, while still never looking back at smaller indexes. Passing 0 would allow reuse but produce reordered duplicates like [2, 3, 2].<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-q7"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-q8","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"In Subsets II on [1, 2, 2] , someone writes if i > 0 and nums[i] == nums[i-1]: continue . What goes missing?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In Subsets II on [1, 2, 2] , someone writes if i > 0 and nums[i] == nums[i-1]: continue . What goes missing?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking [2, 2] and [1, 2, 2] Only [] Nothing, it still works [1]","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> [2, 2] and [1, 2, 2]<\/p><p><code>i &gt; 0<\/code> also blocks taking the second 2 <i>deeper<\/i> on a branch that already took the first 2. We only want to skip twins that are siblings at the same level, which is exactly what <code>i &gt; start<\/code> means.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-q8"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-cq1","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Time complexity?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Time complexity?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking O(n) O(n²) O(2ⁿ) O(log n)","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">def f(n):\n    if n &lt;= 1:\n        return 1\n    return f(n - 1) + f(n - 1)<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(2ⁿ)<\/p><p>2 calls on n − 1 with O(1) work: levels of 1, 2, 4, … 2ⁿ. Bottom-heavy → O(2ⁿ). (Writing <code>2 * f(n - 1)<\/code> would make it O(n).)<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-cq1"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-cq2","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Time complexity?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Time complexity?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking O(n log n) O(n) O(log n) O(n²)","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">def f(n):\n    if n &lt;= 1:\n        return\n    for i in range(n):\n        work()\n    f(n // 2)<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p>One call on n/2, O(n) loop: n + n/2 + n/4 + … ≈ 2n. Top-heavy → O(n).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-cq2"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-cq3","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Space complexity of naive fib(n) (not counting the answer)?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Space complexity of naive fib(n) (not counting the answer)?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking O(2ⁿ) O(n) O(1) O(log n)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p>Millions of calls, but at most n frames alive at once: the depth of the tree.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-cq3"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-cq4","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Time complexity?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Time complexity?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking O(n) O(n²) O(log n) O(n log n)","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">def is_pal(s):\n    if len(s) &lt;= 1:\n        return True\n    return s[0] == s[-1] and is_pal(s[1:-1])<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n²)<\/p><p>n/2 calls, but <code>s[1:-1]<\/code> copies the string each time: n + (n−2) + (n−4) + … ≈ n²/4 → O(n²). Pass indices to get O(n).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-cq4"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-cq5","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Time complexity?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Time complexity?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking O(n) O(n log n) O(n²) O(log n)","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">def f(a, lo, hi):\n    if lo &gt;= hi:\n        return\n    mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n    f(a, lo, mid)\n    f(a, mid + 1, hi)\n    for i in range(lo, hi + 1):\n        visit(a[i])<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n log n)<\/p><p>Two calls on halves plus O(n) work per call: every level costs n, log n levels → O(n log n). It’s merge sort’s shape.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-cq5"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-cq6","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"Grid paths with memo: paths(r, c) = paths(r-1, c) + paths(r, c-1) on an m × n grid. Time?","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Grid paths with memo: paths(r, c) = paths(r-1, c) + paths(r, c-1) on an m × n grid. Time?"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking O(2^(m+n)) O(m · n) O(m + n) O((m+n)!)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(m · n)<\/p><p>m·n distinct states, O(1) work each. Without memo it would be exponential.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-cq6"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"“Return every possible arrangement of the letters in \"abc\" .”","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Return every possible arrangement of the letters in \"abc\" .”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Start index, record every node used[], record at full length Grid DFS with marking Bottom-up tree recursion","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> used[], record at full length<\/p><p>“Arrangement” means order matters. Loop from 0 every time and track which letters are already placed.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"“Coins can be used unlimited times. List every combination that adds up to the amount.”","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Coins can be used unlimited times. List every combination that adds up to the amount.”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking used[], record at full length Start index, recurse with i + 1 Start index, recurse with i Level-order BFS","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Start index, recurse with i<\/p><p>A combination, so order doesn't matter: start index. Unlimited reuse: recurse with <code>i<\/code>. Sort and break when a coin exceeds what's left.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"“Split \"aab\" into pieces so every piece is a palindrome. Return all ways.”","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Split \"aab\" into pieces so every piece is a palindrome. Return all ways.”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Try every cut end from a start index used[] permutations Two-pointer only Postorder on a tree","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Try every cut end from a start index<\/p><p>Partition problems choose where the next piece ends. Loop <code>end<\/code> from start + 1, prune non-palindromes, recurse from <code>end<\/code>.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-d4","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"“Can the word be formed from sequentially adjacent cells, each used once?”","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Can the word be formed from sequentially adjacent cells, each used once?”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Start index subsets Grid DFS: mark, explore 4 ways, unmark Inorder traversal Sort and skip duplicates","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Grid DFS: mark, explore 4 ways, unmark<\/p><p>Adjacent cells, each used once: classic grid backtracking. Mark before exploring, unmark on the way out.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-d4"]]},{"i":"qz-recursion-q-d5","k":"quiz","s":"recursion","t":"“Is this binary tree height-balanced?”","sub":"Recursion & Backtracking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Is this binary tree height-balanced?”"],"w":"Recursion & Backtracking Top-down, pass depth as a parameter Bottom-up: return heights, combine Backtracking with used[] Level order with a queue","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Bottom-up: return heights, combine<\/p><p>Each node needs both subtree heights to decide, so return heights upward. Return −1 as a “not balanced” signal to stop early.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","recursion.html#q-d5"]]},{"i":"po-heap-0","k":"post","s":"heap","t":"A tree hiding in a list","sub":"Heap · How a heap works","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["A tree hiding in a list","explain A tree hiding in a list","what is A tree hiding in a list"],"w":"Heap How a heap works","a":"<p>Store the tree level by level in an array. Node <code>i<\/code> has children <code>2i + 1<\/code> and <code>2i + 2<\/code>, and parent <code>(i − 1) // 2<\/code>. No pointers needed.<\/p><p>The only rule (min-heap): every parent ≤ its children. So the root is the minimum. To push, add at the end and <b>sift up<\/b> while you're smaller than your parent. To pop, move the last leaf to the root and <b>sift down<\/b> toward the smaller child. Both walk one root-to-leaf path: O(log n).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","heap.html#s-a-tree-hiding-in-a-list"]]},{"i":"po-heap-1","k":"post","s":"heap","t":"The bouncer","sub":"Heap · Top K, merging, medians","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The bouncer","explain The bouncer","what is The bouncer"],"w":"Heap Top K, merging, medians","a":"<p>To keep the k largest of a stream, keep a <b>min<\/b>-heap of size k. Its root is the weakest of your top k, like a bouncer at the door. Every newcomer is compared with the bouncer: if it's bigger, the bouncer is thrown out. At the end, the root is exactly the kth largest.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","heap.html#s-the-bouncer"]]},{"i":"po-heap-3","k":"post","s":"heap","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Heap · Top K, merging, medians","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Heap Top K, merging, medians","a":"<p>Filter by pattern. Name the heap (min or max, size limit?) before revealing.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","heap.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-heap-4","k":"post","s":"heap","t":"How to price a heap solution","sub":"Heap · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a heap solution","explain How to price a heap solution","what is How to price a heap solution"],"w":"Heap Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>A heap is a complete binary tree<\/b>, so its height is ⌊log₂ n⌋. Push and pop sift along one root-to-leaf path → O(log n). Peeking at the top is O(1).<\/li><li><b>Multiply by how many pushes and pops you do.<\/b> n pushes + n pops → O(n log n).<\/li><li><b>Keep the heap small when you can.<\/b> Top-k with a heap capped at size k costs O(n log k), cheaper than sorting all n (O(n log n)).<\/li><li><b>heapify<\/b> on an existing list is O(n), not O(n log n): most nodes are near the bottom and barely move.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","heap.html#s-how-to-price-a-heap-solution"]]},{"i":"po-heap-6","k":"post","s":"heap","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Heap · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Heap The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation / pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>push / pop<\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>peek (heap[0])<\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>heapify a list<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Top k with a size-k heap<\/td><td><code>O(n log k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Merge k sorted, N items total<\/td><td><code>O(N log k)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Running median<\/td><td><code>O(log n)<\/code> add, <code>O(1)<\/code> query<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","heap.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"in-heap-0","k":"post","s":"heap","t":"A heap is lazy sorting.","sub":"Heap · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["A heap is lazy sorting."],"w":"Heap insight","a":"<p>Sorting fixes the order of everything: O(n log n). A heap only promises one thing: the root is the best. Everything else is “roughly ordered”.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">sorted list: everything in order      heap: only the top is guaranteed<\/pre><p>That's why it's cheaper to keep up to date. If a problem only ever asks “what's the best one right now?” while items keep arriving or changing, you don't need sorting, you need a heap.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","heap.html#in-partial"]]},{"i":"in-heap-1","k":"post","s":"heap","t":"Why the k largest live in a min-heap.","sub":"Heap · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Why the k largest live in a min-heap."],"w":"Heap insight","a":"<p>It feels backwards. But the question you ask on each new item is “does it beat the <i>weakest<\/i> of my current top k?”. A min-heap puts that weakest member at the root, ready to compare and evict in O(log k).<\/p><p>A max-heap of all n items also works (pop k times), but it costs O(n) memory and doesn't work on an endless stream. The size-k heap uses O(k) memory no matter how much data flows past.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","heap.html#in-bouncer"]]},{"i":"in-heap-2","k":"post","s":"heap","t":"Two heaps meet at the median.","sub":"Heap · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Two heaps meet at the median."],"w":"Heap insight","a":"<p>Split the numbers into a smaller half and a larger half. The median depends only on the largest of the smaller half and the smallest of the larger half. Those are exactly the tops of a max-heap and a min-heap.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">lo (max-heap) | hi (min-heap)      sizes differ by ≤ 1<\/pre><p>Every add is “push into lo, move lo's max into hi, rebalance”: three O(log n) operations keep both invariants.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","heap.html#in-twoheaps"]]},{"i":"in-heap-3","k":"post","s":"heap","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Heap · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Heap insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","heap.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-heap-q-p1","k":"quiz","s":"heap","t":"In an array-stored heap, where are the children of index 3?","sub":"Heap · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In an array-stored heap, where are the children of index 3?"],"w":"Heap 4 and 5 6 and 7 7 and 8 1 and 2","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 7 and 8<\/p><p>Children are 2i + 1 and 2i + 2: 7 and 8. The parent of i is (i − 1) // 2.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","heap.html#q-p1"]]},{"i":"qz-heap-q-p2","k":"quiz","s":"heap","t":"Turning an unsorted list of n items into a heap with heapify costs…","sub":"Heap · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Turning an unsorted list of n items into a heap with heapify costs…"],"w":"Heap O(n log n) O(n) O(log n) O(n²)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p>Most nodes are near the bottom and sift down only a little; the sum is linear.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","heap.html#q-p2"]]},{"i":"qz-heap-q-p4","k":"quiz","s":"heap","t":"In a min-heap, is heap[1] always the second smallest value?","sub":"Heap · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In a min-heap, is heap[1] always the second smallest value?"],"w":"Heap Yes No: the second smallest is heap[1] or heap[2] Only if the heap is sorted Only for even sizes","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> No: the second smallest is heap[1] or heap[2]<\/p><p>The two children of the root aren’t ordered relative to each other.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","heap.html#q-p4"]]},{"i":"qz-heap-q-p3","k":"quiz","s":"heap","t":"To find the 10 largest values in a stream of a billion numbers, use…","sub":"Heap · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["To find the 10 largest values in a stream of a billion numbers, use…"],"w":"Heap A max-heap of all numbers A min-heap capped at size 10 Sort everything A max-heap capped at size 10","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> A min-heap capped at size 10<\/p><p>The min-heap’s root is the weakest of your current top 10, the one to evict.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","heap.html#q-p3"]]},{"i":"qz-heap-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"heap","t":"Merge k sorted lists with N total nodes using a heap of the k current heads. Time?","sub":"Heap · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Merge k sorted lists with N total nodes using a heap of the k current heads. Time?"],"w":"Heap O(N k) O(N log k) O(N log N) O(k log N)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(N log k)<\/p><p>Each of the N nodes is pushed and popped once on a heap of size k.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","heap.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-heap-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"heap","t":"Median of a stream with two heaps. Cost per insertion?","sub":"Heap · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Median of a stream with two heaps. Cost per insertion?"],"w":"Heap O(1) O(log n) O(n) O(n log n)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(log n)<\/p><p>A push and possibly one rebalance move between heaps: a constant number of O(log n) operations.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","heap.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-heap-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"heap","t":"“Find the k pairs with the smallest sums from two sorted arrays.” (373)","sub":"Heap · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Find the k pairs with the smallest sums from two sorted arrays.” (373)"],"w":"Heap Min-heap seeded with (a[i] + b[0], i, 0), push (i, j + 1) on pop Sort all pairs Two pointers Max-heap of everything","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Min-heap seeded with (a[i] + b[0], i, 0), push (i, j + 1) on pop<\/p><p>It’s merge k sorted lists in disguise: each row i is a sorted list a[i] + b[j].<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","heap.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-heap-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"heap","t":"“Minimum cost to connect sticks: always join the two shortest.” (1167)","sub":"Heap · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Minimum cost to connect sticks: always join the two shortest.” (1167)"],"w":"Heap Max-heap Min-heap: pop two, push their sum Sort once Stack","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Min-heap: pop two, push their sum<\/p><p>Each step needs the two smallest current values, which change after every merge.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","heap.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-heap-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"heap","t":"“Return the k most frequent words, ties broken alphabetically.” (692)","sub":"Heap · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Return the k most frequent words, ties broken alphabetically.” (692)"],"w":"Heap Min-heap of size k with key (count, reversed word order) Plain sort by count Stack Two heaps","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Min-heap of size k with key (count, reversed word order)<\/p><p>Size-k heap; the tiebreak must make the alphabetically larger word the “weaker” one.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","heap.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-trie-0","k":"post","s":"trie","t":"Spell it out, one edge at a time","sub":"Trie · A tree of prefixes","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Spell it out, one edge at a time","explain Spell it out, one edge at a time","what is Spell it out, one edge at a time"],"w":"Trie A tree of prefixes","a":"<p>A trie node is just a dict of children (<code>char → node<\/code>) plus a flag saying “a word ends here”. To insert, walk the characters and create missing children. To search, walk them and fail as soon as a child is missing.<\/p><p>“cat”, “car” and “cart” all reuse the path c → a. A hash set can tell you if “car” is a word; a trie can also tell you every word that starts with “ca”.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","trie.html#s-spell-it-out-one-edge-at-a-time"]]},{"i":"po-trie-1","k":"post","s":"trie","t":"When a character could be anything","sub":"Trie · Searching beyond exact words","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["When a character could be anything","explain When a character could be anything","what is When a character could be anything"],"w":"Trie Searching beyond exact words","a":"<p>A <code>.<\/code> matches any letter, so at that position you can't follow one child: you must try them all. That's a DFS over the trie that branches at every wildcard and backtracks when a branch dies. Autocomplete is the same DFS without a pattern: walk to the prefix node, then collect every word below it.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","trie.html#s-when-a-character-could-be-anything"]]},{"i":"po-trie-3","k":"post","s":"trie","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Trie · Searching beyond exact words","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Trie Searching beyond exact words","a":"<p>Name what each node stores before revealing.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","trie.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-trie-4","k":"post","s":"trie","t":"How to price a trie solution","sub":"Trie · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a trie solution","explain How to price a trie solution","what is How to price a trie solution"],"w":"Trie Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Insert, search and startsWith cost O(L)<\/b>, where L is the length of the word: one node per character.<\/li><li><b>That doesn't change with the number of words.<\/b> Searching a list of N words is O(N·L); a trie is O(L).<\/li><li><b>Building<\/b> the trie is the total number of characters inserted.<\/li><li><b>Space<\/b> is the number of nodes: at most the total characters, less when prefixes are shared. Each node's children map adds overhead.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","trie.html#s-how-to-price-a-trie-solution"]]},{"i":"po-trie-6","k":"post","s":"trie","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Trie · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Trie The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>Insert / search / startsWith<\/td><td><code>O(L)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(total characters)<\/code> overall<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Wildcard search<\/td><td><code>O(26ᵈ · L)<\/code> worst, d = number of dots<\/td><td><code>O(L)<\/code> stack<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Autocomplete under a prefix<\/td><td><code>O(L + size of the subtree)<\/code><\/td><td><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Max XOR pair (binary trie)<\/td><td><code>O(n · 32)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n · 32)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Word Search II<\/td><td><code>O(R · C · 4 · 3ᴸ⁻¹)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(total characters)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","trie.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"in-trie-0","k":"post","s":"trie","t":"Cost depends on the word, not the dictionary.","sub":"Trie · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Cost depends on the word, not the dictionary."],"w":"Trie insight","a":"<p>In a trie, searching “cart” touches four nodes whether the trie holds 5 words or 5 million. The work is O(L), L being the length of what you’re looking up.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">insert / search / startsWith: O(L)      memory: O(total characters), shared prefixes stored once<\/pre><p>Compare with a sorted list: prefix search is O(L log n) with binary search. A set can’t answer prefix questions at all without scanning everything.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","trie.html#in-shared"]]},{"i":"in-trie-1","k":"post","s":"trie","t":"What a trie gives you that a hash set doesn’t.","sub":"Trie · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["What a trie gives you that a hash set doesn’t."],"w":"Trie insight","a":"<p>A hash set hashes the <i>whole<\/i> word, so it can only answer “is this exact word present?”. A trie stores the word one character at a time, so every intermediate state is meaningful: “is this a prefix of something?”, “which words continue from here?”, “stop as soon as no word can match”.<\/p><p>That last one is the big win in search problems: the moment a path doesn't exist, every word that would have needed it is ruled out together.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","trie.html#in-whytrie"]]},{"i":"in-trie-2","k":"post","s":"trie","t":"Bits are just a two-letter alphabet.","sub":"Trie · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Bits are just a two-letter alphabet."],"w":"Trie insight","a":"<p>Write each number as 32 bits, highest first, and insert it into a trie whose alphabet is {0, 1}. To maximize x XOR y, you want y to disagree with x on the highest possible bit, then the next, and so on.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">at each level: take the opposite bit if that child exists, else take the same bit<\/pre><p>Greedy works because bit k is worth more than all lower bits combined. The same idea answers “max XOR with a limit” queries when combined with sorting.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","trie.html#in-xor"]]},{"i":"in-trie-3","k":"post","s":"trie","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Trie · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Trie insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","trie.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-trie-q-t1","k":"quiz","s":"trie","t":"You inserted only “cat”. What do search(\"ca\") and startsWith(\"ca\") return?","sub":"Trie · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["You inserted only “cat”. What do search(\"ca\") and startsWith(\"ca\") return?"],"w":"Trie True, True False, True False, False True, False","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> False, True<\/p><p>The path c → a exists, but no word ends at “a”.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","trie.html#q-t1"]]},{"i":"qz-trie-q-t2","k":"quiz","s":"trie","t":"Searching a word of length L in a trie holding N words costs…","sub":"Trie · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Searching a word of length L in a trie holding N words costs…"],"w":"Trie O(N) O(log N) O(L) O(N · L)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(L)<\/p><p>You walk one node per character; N doesn’t matter.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","trie.html#q-t2"]]},{"i":"qz-trie-q-t3","k":"quiz","s":"trie","t":"Wildcard search for a pattern of only dots, like “...”, in the worst case visits…","sub":"Trie · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Wildcard search for a pattern of only dots, like “...”, in the worst case visits…"],"w":"Trie One path Every node down to depth 3 Only end nodes Nothing","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Every node down to depth 3<\/p><p>Every “.” branches into every child, so it’s a full DFS of the top levels.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","trie.html#q-t3"]]},{"i":"qz-trie-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"trie","t":"Insert N words of average length L into a trie. Total time?","sub":"Trie · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Insert N words of average length L into a trie. Total time?"],"w":"Trie O(N) O(N · L) O(L) O(N log N)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(N · L)<\/p><p>Each word costs O(L); N of them → O(N·L), i.e. the total number of characters.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","trie.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-trie-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"trie","t":"Word Search II prunes the board DFS with a trie of the dictionary. Why does the trie help?","sub":"Trie · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Word Search II prunes the board DFS with a trie of the dictionary. Why does the trie help?"],"w":"Trie It sorts the words A path stops as soon as its prefix isn’t in any word It removes duplicates It makes DFS iterative","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> A path stops as soon as its prefix isn’t in any word<\/p><p>Dead prefixes are cut immediately, instead of running one DFS per word.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","trie.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-trie-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"trie","t":"“Given a dictionary, check whether each of q query words exists.”","sub":"Trie · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Given a dictionary, check whether each of q query words exists.”"],"w":"Trie Trie required A set is enough Heap Union-find","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> A set is enough<\/p><p>Exact lookups only: a set is simpler.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","trie.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-trie-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"trie","t":"“Count how many dictionary words have each query as a prefix.”","sub":"Trie · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Count how many dictionary words have each query as a prefix.”"],"w":"Trie Set Trie with counts on nodes Sort and scan Stack","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Trie with counts on nodes<\/p><p>Prefix counts → counter stored on each trie node.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","trie.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-trie-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"trie","t":"“A stream of characters; after each one, report whether some dictionary word is a suffix of what you’ve seen.” (1032)","sub":"Trie · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“A stream of characters; after each one, report whether some dictionary word is a suffix of what you’ve seen.” (1032)"],"w":"Trie Trie of reversed words, walk backward from the newest char Set of words Heap Binary search","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Trie of reversed words, walk backward from the newest char<\/p><p>Suffix matching becomes prefix matching once words and the stream are reversed.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","trie.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-graphs-0","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"A grid is a graph in disguise","sub":"Graphs · Traverse anything","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["A grid is a graph in disguise","explain A grid is a graph in disguise","what is A grid is a graph in disguise"],"w":"Graphs Traverse anything","a":"<p>Every cell is a node. Its up, down, left and right neighbours are its edges. “Count the islands” is “count connected components”, and “minimum steps to reach the exit” is “shortest path”. You don't need to build an adjacency list; the grid already is one.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#s-a-grid-is-a-graph-in-disguise"]]},{"i":"po-graphs-1","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"Wide or deep?","sub":"Graphs · Traverse anything","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Wide or deep?","explain Wide or deep?","what is Wide or deep?"],"w":"Graphs Traverse anything","a":"<p>BFS explores in rings: everything 1 step away, then everything 2 steps away. That makes it the shortest-path tool for unweighted graphs. DFS dives down one path to the end before backing up: perfect for exploring, counting, cycle detection and anything recursive. Both visit every node and edge once: O(V + E).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#s-wide-or-deep"]]},{"i":"po-graphs-3","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"Take what has no prerequisites left","sub":"Graphs · Order & groups","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Take what has no prerequisites left","explain Take what has no prerequisites left","what is Take what has no prerequisites left"],"w":"Graphs Order & groups","a":"<p>Courses with prerequisites form a directed graph. A course is ready when all of its prerequisites are done, that is, when its <b>in-degree<\/b> drops to 0. Kahn's algorithm keeps a queue of ready courses, takes one, and lowers the in-degree of everything it unlocks. If some courses never become ready, they're stuck in a cycle.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#s-take-what-has-no-prerequisites-left"]]},{"i":"po-graphs-4","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"Union-find: who's in my group?","sub":"Graphs · Order & groups","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Union-find: who's in my group?","explain Union-find: who's in my group?","what is Union-find: who's in my group?"],"w":"Graphs Order & groups","a":"<p>Each node points to a parent; following parents leads to the group's representative (the root). <code>find<\/code> walks to the root, <code>union<\/code> hangs one root under the other. If two nodes already share a root, the new edge connects nothing new: it closes a cycle.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#s-union-find-who-s-in-my-group"]]},{"i":"po-graphs-5","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"Settle the closest node first","sub":"Graphs · Shortest paths & spanning trees","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Settle the closest node first","explain Settle the closest node first","what is Settle the closest node first"],"w":"Graphs Shortest paths & spanning trees","a":"<p>With weights, the node that's fewest edges away isn't necessarily the closest. Dijkstra keeps a min-heap of (distance, node). The node popped first has the smallest known distance, and with non-negative weights nothing later can beat it, so it's settled. Relax its edges and repeat.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#s-settle-the-closest-node-first"]]},{"i":"po-graphs-6","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Graphs · Shortest paths & spanning trees","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Graphs Shortest paths & spanning trees","a":"<p>Filter by technique. Name the graph (nodes, edges, weights) before revealing.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-graphs-7","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"How to price a graph traversal","sub":"Graphs · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a graph traversal","explain How to price a graph traversal","what is How to price a graph traversal"],"w":"Graphs Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Each vertex is visited once<\/b> (the visited set guarantees it) → O(V).<\/li><li><b>Each edge is looked at once<\/b> from each end (twice if undirected) when you scan neighbour lists → O(E).<\/li><li><b>Add them, don't multiply<\/b>: BFS/DFS/topological sort are O(V + E). For a grid, V = R·C and E ≈ 4·R·C → O(R·C).<\/li><li><b>Priority queues add a log<\/b>: Dijkstra is O((V + E) log V). Space: visited + queue/stack → O(V).<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#s-how-to-price-a-graph-traversal"]]},{"i":"po-graphs-9","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Graphs · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Graphs The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Use when<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>BFS / DFS<\/td><td><code>O(V + E)<\/code><\/td><td>Reachability, components, unweighted shortest path<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Kahn’s topological sort<\/td><td><code>O(V + E)<\/code><\/td><td>Ordering with dependencies, directed cycle check<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Union-find (path compression + rank)<\/td><td><code>~O(α(n))<\/code> per op<\/td><td>Dynamic connectivity, redundant edges<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Dijkstra with a heap<\/td><td><code>O(E log V)<\/code><\/td><td>Non-negative weights<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Bellman-Ford (k rounds)<\/td><td><code>O(k · E)<\/code><\/td><td>Negative weights or a stop limit<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Prim / Kruskal MST<\/td><td><code>O(E log V)<\/code><\/td><td>Cheapest way to connect everything<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"in-graphs-0","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"Every grid problem is a graph problem.","sub":"Graphs · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Every grid problem is a graph problem."],"w":"Graphs insight","a":"<p>Nodes: cells. Edges: moves (4 directions, sometimes 8, sometimes knight jumps). Visited: a set of (r, c), or overwrite the cell in place. Once you say that sentence, “number of islands”, “shortest path in a maze” and “rotting oranges” stop being three problems and become DFS, BFS and multi-source BFS.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(R × C) time: each cell visited once, 4 edges each<\/pre>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#in-grid"]]},{"i":"in-graphs-1","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"BFS layers are distances.","sub":"Graphs · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["BFS layers are distances."],"w":"Graphs insight","a":"<p>BFS pops everything at distance d before anything at distance d + 1, because the queue is first in, first out. So the first time a node is seen is through a shortest path.<\/p><p>That's also why you mark nodes when enqueuing: the first discovery already has the best distance, and every later discovery would be a duplicate. With weights, distance by edges is no longer distance by cost, and BFS gives way to Dijkstra.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#in-bfsdist"]]},{"i":"in-graphs-2","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"In-degree = unfinished prerequisites.","sub":"Graphs · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["In-degree = unfinished prerequisites."],"w":"Graphs insight","a":"<p>Kahn's algorithm is just bookkeeping: each course knows how many prerequisites are still pending. Finishing a course lowers that count for everything it unlocks. A course whose count hits 0 joins the queue.<\/p><p>The cycle check comes free: a course in a cycle waits on another course in the same cycle, so its count never drops to 0 and it never comes out. Fewer than n courses in the output ⇒ cycle.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#in-topo"]]},{"i":"in-graphs-3","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"Why union-find is almost O(1).","sub":"Graphs · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Why union-find is almost O(1)."],"w":"Graphs insight","a":"<p>Naively, find walks a chain of parents that can grow long. Two tricks fix that. <b>Path compression<\/b>: while walking to the root, point nodes closer to it, so the next find is shorter. <b>Union by rank/size<\/b>: hang the smaller tree under the bigger one, so trees stay shallow.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">both together → amortized O(α(n)), effectively constant<\/pre><p>In interviews, path compression alone is usually enough; mention rank for full marks.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#in-uf"]]},{"i":"in-graphs-4","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"Why Dijkstra breaks with negative edges.","sub":"Graphs · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Why Dijkstra breaks with negative edges."],"w":"Graphs insight","a":"<p>Dijkstra settles a node the moment it's popped, assuming no later path can be shorter. That's true only if adding edges never decreases a path's cost.<\/p><p>With a negative edge, a longer detour could end up cheaper after you've already settled the node. Then use Bellman-Ford (relax every edge V − 1 times), which also detects negative cycles. Dijkstra also works for any monotone cost, like “max height so far”.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#in-dijkstra"]]},{"i":"in-graphs-5","k":"post","s":"graphs","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Graphs · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Graphs insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","graphs.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-g1","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"Number of Islands: what if you never mark land as visited during the flood?","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Number of Islands: what if you never mark land as visited during the flood?"],"w":"Graphs It still works, just slower The DFS loops forever between neighbouring land cells It counts water It returns 0","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> The DFS loops forever between neighbouring land cells<\/p><p>Two land neighbours keep calling each other. Sink the cell (or add it to a set) before recursing.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-g1"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-g2","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"Shortest path in an unweighted graph: which traversal?","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Shortest path in an unweighted graph: which traversal?"],"w":"Graphs DFS BFS Either; same result Dijkstra is required","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> BFS<\/p><p>BFS explores by distance. DFS may find a long path first.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-g2"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-g3","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"Kahn’s algorithm returns only 4 of 6 courses. What does that mean?","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Kahn’s algorithm returns only 4 of 6 courses. What does that mean?"],"w":"Graphs Two courses have no prerequisites There’s a cycle among the missing courses A bug Two courses are disconnected","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> There’s a cycle among the missing courses<\/p><p>Disconnected courses still come out (in-degree 0). Only a cycle keeps nodes stuck.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-g3"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-g4","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"In union-find, union(a, b) finds find(a) == find(b) . What does that tell you?","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In union-find, union(a, b) finds find(a) == find(b) . What does that tell you?"],"w":"Graphs a and b are the same node a and b were already connected, so edge (a, b) closes a cycle The parents array is broken You must restart","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> a and b were already connected, so edge (a, b) closes a cycle<\/p><p>This is exactly how Redundant Connection and Graph Valid Tree detect cycles.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-g4"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-g5","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"Can Dijkstra handle a graph with negative edge weights?","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Can Dijkstra handle a graph with negative edge weights?"],"w":"Graphs Yes, always No: a settled node might later be reachable more cheaply Only if the graph is a tree Only with a max-heap","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> No: a settled node might later be reachable more cheaply<\/p><p>Use Bellman-Ford for negative weights.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-g5"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"Number of islands with DFS on a 1000 × 1000 grid. Roughly how many cell visits?","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Number of islands with DFS on a 1000 × 1000 grid. Roughly how many cell visits?"],"w":"Graphs ~1,000 ~1,000,000 ~10⁹ ~2¹⁰⁰⁰","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> ~1,000,000<\/p><p>Each of the 10⁶ cells is visited a constant number of times: O(R·C).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"Course Schedule (Kahn’s topological sort) with V courses and E prerequisites. Time?","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Course Schedule (Kahn’s topological sort) with V courses and E prerequisites. Time?"],"w":"Graphs O(V²) O(V + E) O(E log V) O(V · E)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(V + E)<\/p><p>Each course enters the queue once, each prerequisite edge decrements one in-degree once.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"“Minimum knight moves from (0, 0) to (x, y) on an infinite board.”","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Minimum knight moves from (0, 0) to (x, y) on an infinite board.”"],"w":"Graphs DFS BFS over positions Dijkstra Union-find","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> BFS over positions<\/p><p>Every move costs 1: BFS on an implicit graph of positions.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"“Accounts with a shared email belong to the same person; merge them.” (721)","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Accounts with a shared email belong to the same person; merge them.” (721)"],"w":"Graphs Topological sort Union-find over emails (or DFS) Dijkstra Two pointers","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Union-find over emails (or DFS)<\/p><p>Grouping by shared items is connected components; union-find handles it directly.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-graphs-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"graphs","t":"“Path with minimum effort: minimize the largest height difference along the path.” (1631)","sub":"Graphs · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Path with minimum effort: minimize the largest height difference along the path.” (1631)"],"w":"Graphs BFS Dijkstra with max instead of sum (or binary search + BFS) Topological sort Kruskal only","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Dijkstra with max instead of sum (or binary search + BFS)<\/p><p>Minimax path → Dijkstra on the max edge so far.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","graphs.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-dp-0","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"Four versions of the same idea","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Recursion that remembers","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Four versions of the same idea","explain Four versions of the same idea","what is Four versions of the same idea"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Recursion that remembers","a":"<p>Climbing Stairs: one or two steps at a time, how many ways to reach step n? The last move was either 1 step (from n − 1) or 2 steps (from n − 2), so <code>ways(n) = ways(n − 1) + ways(n − 2)<\/code>. Write that as recursion and it works, but it recomputes the same values exponentially often.<\/p><p>Add a cache and it's O(n). Flip it into a loop that fills a table from small to large and you have bottom-up DP. Notice each value only needs the last two, and the table shrinks to two variables. Same recurrence, four costumes.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#s-four-versions-of-the-same-idea"]]},{"i":"po-dp-1","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"The recipe","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Recursion that remembers","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The recipe","explain The recipe","what is The recipe"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Recursion that remembers","a":"<ul><li><b>State: what does dp[i] mean?<\/b>Write it as a full sentence: “dp[i] = the best I can do using the first i houses”.<\/li><li><b>Recurrence: how does dp[i] use smaller states?<\/b>Ask “what was the last decision?” Each option points to a smaller state.<\/li><li><b>Base cases: the smallest states.<\/b>Usually dp[0], sometimes the first row and column.<\/li><li><b>Order: fill so inputs are ready.<\/b>If dp[i] reads dp[i − 1], go left to right. Then optimize space if only a few previous values are read.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#s-the-recipe"]]},{"i":"po-dp-2","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"What was the last decision?","sub":"Dynamic Programming · 1-D patterns","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["What was the last decision?","explain What was the last decision?","what is What was the last decision?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming 1-D patterns","a":"<p>House Robber: at house i you either skip it (keep dp[i − 1]) or rob it (dp[i − 2] plus its money). Coin Change: the last coin used was one of the coins, so dp[a] looks at dp[a − c] for every coin. LIS: the run ending at i extended some earlier run ending at j. Every 1-D recurrence comes from listing the possible last moves.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#s-what-was-the-last-decision"]]},{"i":"po-dp-4","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"Prefixes against prefixes","sub":"Dynamic Programming · 2-D tables","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Prefixes against prefixes","explain Prefixes against prefixes","what is Prefixes against prefixes"],"w":"Dynamic Programming 2-D tables","a":"<p>For two strings, let <code>dp[i][j]<\/code> describe the first i characters of one against the first j of the other. Then only the last characters matter: if they match, extend the diagonal; if not, try dropping one character from either side. Size the table (m + 1) × (n + 1) so the empty prefix is row 0 and column 0.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#s-prefixes-against-prefixes"]]},{"i":"po-dp-5","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Dynamic Programming · 2-D tables","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Dynamic Programming 2-D tables","a":"<p>Filter by shape. Write the state sentence before revealing.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-dp-6","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"How to price a DP solution","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a DP solution","explain How to price a DP solution","what is How to price a DP solution"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Count the states<\/b>: the size of the table (or of the memo dict). 1-D over n → n states; 2-D over m × n → m·n.<\/li><li><b>Count the work per state<\/b>: how many earlier states one cell looks at. Climbing stairs: 2 → O(1). LIS: all earlier j → O(n).<\/li><li><b>Time = states × work per state.<\/b> Space = the table, often shrinkable to one or two rows when each row only reads the previous one.<\/li><li><b>Compare with plain recursion<\/b>: same states, recomputed exponentially many times.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#s-how-to-price-a-dp-solution"]]},{"i":"po-dp-8","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Dynamic Programming · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Dynamic Programming The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Shape<\/th><th>Time<\/th><th>Space<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>1-D, constant work per state<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(1)<\/code> with rolling variables<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Coin change / unbounded knapsack<\/td><td><code>O(amount · coins)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(amount)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>LIS<\/td><td><code>O(n²)<\/code>, or <code>O(n log n)<\/code> with tails + binary search<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Two strings / grid<\/td><td><code>O(m · n)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code> with one rolling row<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>0/1 knapsack, subset sum<\/td><td><code>O(n · capacity)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(capacity)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Interval DP (burst balloons)<\/td><td><code>O(n³)<\/code><\/td><td><code>O(n²)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"in-dp-0","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"DP = recursion + a notebook.","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["DP = recursion + a notebook."],"w":"Dynamic Programming insight","a":"<p>Two properties make DP possible. <b>Optimal substructure<\/b>: the best answer is built from best answers to smaller subproblems. <b>Overlapping subproblems<\/b>: those smaller subproblems repeat across branches.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">time = number of distinct states × work per state<\/pre><p>Plain recursion for fib(40) makes ~330 million calls; there are only 41 distinct states. A cache turns the exponential call tree into a short list. That's the whole trick.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#in-recursion"]]},{"i":"in-dp-1","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"Choosing the state is the real problem.","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Choosing the state is the real problem."],"w":"Dynamic Programming insight","a":"<p>Once the state is right, the recurrence usually writes itself. To find it, ask what the next decision depends on. House robber: only whether the previous house was robbed, so dp[i] over prefixes suffices. Stock with cooldown: whether you hold a share and whether you just sold, so three states per day.<\/p><p>If your recurrence needs information the state doesn't carry, add a dimension. If two different histories lead to the same future, they should share a state.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#in-state"]]},{"i":"in-dp-2","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"Loop order decides what gets counted.","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Loop order decides what gets counted."],"w":"Dynamic Programming insight","a":"<p>Coin Change II with coins [1, 2] and amount 3. Coins outer: you first count all ways using only 1s, then add 2s on top, so “1 + 2” and “2 + 1” are the same combination: 2 ways.<\/p><p>Amount outer: for each amount you try every coin last, so “1 + 2” and “2 + 1” are different sequences: 3 ways (that's Combination Sum IV). Same recurrence, swapped loops, different question.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#in-combos"]]},{"i":"in-dp-3","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"Read the table like a map.","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Read the table like a map."],"w":"Dynamic Programming insight","a":"<p>In two-string DP, every cell sits at the end of two prefixes. Moving down means consuming a character of the first string, moving right consumes one of the second, and moving diagonally consumes both.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">above = drop from a      left = drop from b      diagonal = use both<\/pre><p>Edit distance, LCS, interleaving string and distinct subsequences are all the same map with different rules for each move. Backtracking from the bottom-right corner along the chosen moves reconstructs the actual answer.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#in-table"]]},{"i":"in-dp-4","k":"post","s":"dp","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Dynamic Programming insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","dp.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-p1","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"Why is plain recursive climb(n) exponential?","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Why is plain recursive climb(n) exponential?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Recursion is slow in Python It recomputes the same smaller n many times in different branches The base case is wrong It uses too much memory","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> It recomputes the same smaller n many times in different branches<\/p><p>climb(n − 2) is computed by climb(n) and again inside climb(n − 1), and so on all the way down.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-p1"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-p2","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"In House Robber, what exactly does dp[i] mean?","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In House Robber, what exactly does dp[i] mean?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Money in house i Best total from the first i houses Whether house i is robbed Best total if house i is robbed","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Best total from the first i houses<\/p><p>A precise state sentence makes the recurrence obvious: skip → dp[i − 1], take → dp[i − 2] + nums[i − 1].<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-p2"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-p3","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"Coin Change II counts combinations with coins as the outer loop. If you swap the loops, you count…","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Coin Change II counts combinations with coins as the outer loop. If you swap the loops, you count…"],"w":"Dynamic Programming The same thing Ordered sequences (permutations) instead of combinations Only one combination Nothing; it errors","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Ordered sequences (permutations) instead of combinations<\/p><p>Amount outer tries every coin as the last one, so 1 + 2 and 2 + 1 count separately.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-p3"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-p5","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"In LCS, the last characters match. Which cell do you extend?","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In LCS, the last characters match. Which cell do you extend?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Above Left Diagonal (both prefixes shorter by one) Max of all three","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Diagonal (both prefixes shorter by one)<\/p><p>The match uses one character from each string, so both prefixes shrink.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-p5"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-p4","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"0/1 knapsack in one row, but the capacity loop goes forward . What breaks?","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["0/1 knapsack in one row, but the capacity loop goes forward . What breaks?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming Nothing Items can be reused, making it unbounded knapsack It gets slower The answer is always 0","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Items can be reused, making it unbounded knapsack<\/p><p>dp[c − w] was already updated by this item, so the item can be counted again.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-p4"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"Coin change (min coins) for amount A with C coin types, bottom-up. Time and space?","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Coin change (min coins) for amount A with C coin types, bottom-up. Time and space?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming O(A + C), O(A) O(A · C), O(A) O(C^A), O(A) O(A²), O(1)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(A · C), O(A)<\/p><p>A states × C choices each; the table is size A + 1.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"Edit distance between strings of length m and n. Best possible space with the usual DP?","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Edit distance between strings of length m and n. Best possible space with the usual DP?"],"w":"Dynamic Programming O(m·n) O(min(m, n)) O(1) O(m + n)²","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(min(m, n))<\/p><p>Each row needs only the previous row; make the row the shorter string.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"“Minimum number of perfect squares that sum to n.” (279)","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Minimum number of perfect squares that sum to n.” (279)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming dp[i] = fewest squares summing to i, unbounded knapsack Greedy largest square first BFS only Binary search","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> dp[i] = fewest squares summing to i, unbounded knapsack<\/p><p>Exactly coin change with coins 1, 4, 9, …<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"“Is s3 an interleaving of s1 and s2?” (97)","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Is s3 an interleaving of s1 and s2?” (97)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming dp[i][j] = can s1[:i] and s2[:j] form s3[:i + j] Two pointers greedy Stack Heap","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> dp[i][j] = can s1[:i] and s2[:j] form s3[:i + j]<\/p><p>Two-string DP: the last character of s3[:i + j] came from s1 or from s2.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-dp-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"dp","t":"“Max profit with at most 2 transactions.” (123)","sub":"Dynamic Programming · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Max profit with at most 2 transactions.” (123)"],"w":"Dynamic Programming State machine: buy1, sell1, buy2, sell2 Greedy Sort prices Prefix sums only","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> State machine: buy1, sell1, buy2, sell2<\/p><p>Each day updates four states from yesterday’s values.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","dp.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-greedy-intervals-0","k":"post","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Greedy needs a reason","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Take the best move, never look back","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Greedy needs a reason","explain Greedy needs a reason","what is Greedy needs a reason"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Take the best move, never look back","a":"<p>A greedy algorithm makes one irreversible choice per step: take the farthest reach, drop a negative prefix, pick the meeting that ends first. That's why it's O(n) or O(n log n) instead of exploring every option like DP or backtracking.<\/p><p>The catch: greedy is often wrong. Before trusting it, have a one-line reason, usually “any optimal answer can be swapped to include my choice without getting worse”. No reason? Reach for DP.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","greedy-intervals.html#s-greedy-needs-a-reason"]]},{"i":"po-greedy-intervals-2","k":"post","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Sort by start to merge, by end to schedule","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Intervals","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Sort by start to merge, by end to schedule","explain Sort by start to merge, by end to schedule","what is Sort by start to merge, by end to schedule"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Intervals","a":"<p><b>Merging<\/b> overlapping intervals: sort by start, then each interval either extends the last merged one or starts a new one. <b>Keeping as many as possible<\/b> (or removing the fewest, or shooting the fewest arrows): sort by end and keep whatever finishes first, because it leaves the most room for everything after.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","greedy-intervals.html#s-sort-by-start-to-merge-by-end-to-schedule"]]},{"i":"po-greedy-intervals-3","k":"post","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"The problems that matter","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Intervals","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The problems that matter","explain The problems that matter","what is The problems that matter"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Intervals","a":"<p>Filter by kind. For greedy ones, say the reason it's safe before revealing.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","greedy-intervals.html#s-the-problems-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-greedy-intervals-4","k":"post","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"How to price a greedy / interval solution","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a greedy / interval solution","explain How to price a greedy / interval solution","what is How to price a greedy / interval solution"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Find the sort.<\/b> Almost every interval problem starts by sorting by start or end: O(n log n).<\/li><li><b>Price the scan.<\/b> One pass deciding merge/skip/count is O(n).<\/li><li><b>Add the heap if there is one<\/b> (meeting rooms, scheduling): one push and pop per interval → O(n log n).<\/li><li><b>Total = the biggest piece<\/b>, usually the sort. Space: the output list, or the heap: O(n).<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","greedy-intervals.html#s-how-to-price-a-greedy-interval-solution"]]},{"i":"po-greedy-intervals-6","k":"post","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Complexities to say out loud","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Complexities to say out loud","explain Complexities to say out loud","what is Complexities to say out loud"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Time<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>One-pass greedy (reach, Kadane, gas station)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code>, <code>O(1)<\/code> space<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Sort + sweep (merge, non-overlap, arrows)<\/td><td><code>O(n log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Meeting rooms (sweep or heap)<\/td><td><code>O(n log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Insert interval (already sorted)<\/td><td><code>O(n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>Offline queries with a heap<\/td><td><code>O((n + q) log n)<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","greedy-intervals.html#s-complexities-to-say-out-loud"]]},{"i":"in-greedy-intervals-0","k":"post","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"The exchange argument: how to trust a greedy choice.","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The exchange argument: how to trust a greedy choice."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals insight","a":"<p>Take any optimal solution that doesn't use your greedy choice. Swap one of its choices for yours. If the result is still valid and no worse, then some optimal solution uses your choice, and you can safely commit to it. Repeat for every step.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">optimal without my choice → swap → still optimal → greedy is safe<\/pre><p>For “keep the most non-overlapping intervals”: swap the first interval of any optimal set for the one that ends earliest. It ends no later, so it conflicts with nothing the old one didn't.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","greedy-intervals.html#in-exchange"]]},{"i":"in-greedy-intervals-1","k":"post","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Why “sort by end” wins scheduling problems.","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Why “sort by end” wins scheduling problems."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals insight","a":"<p>Sorting by start feels natural, but a long interval that starts first can block many short ones. Sorting by end means each kept interval finishes as early as possible, so it leaves the maximum room for everything after.<\/p><p>Merging is different: there you care about the shape of the union, not about how many survive, so sort by start. Ask yourself which one you need: “combine them” (start) or “pick as many as possible” (end).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","greedy-intervals.html#in-byend"]]},{"i":"in-greedy-intervals-2","k":"post","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Turn intervals into events.","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Turn intervals into events."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals insight","a":"<p>Each interval is two events: +1 at its start, −1 at its end. Sort all events and sweep, keeping a running total. The running total at any point is how many intervals are active right there.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">max running total = rooms needed = max overlap<\/pre><p>The same trick answers “busiest time”, “car pooling capacity” and “my calendar” questions. With a difference array it works on integer timelines without sorting.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","greedy-intervals.html#in-sweep"]]},{"i":"in-greedy-intervals-3","k":"post","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Greedy & Intervals insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","greedy-intervals.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-greedy-intervals-q-q1","k":"quiz","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Jump Game with nums = [3, 2, 1, 0, 4] : what happens?","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Jump Game with nums = [3, 2, 1, 0, 4] : what happens?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Returns True At i = 4, reach is only 3, so it returns False Infinite loop Returns 4","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> At i = 4, reach is only 3, so it returns False<\/p><p>Every path lands on index 3, whose 0 traps you.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","greedy-intervals.html#q-q1"]]},{"i":"qz-greedy-intervals-q-q2","k":"quiz","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Kadane: what does cur = max(x, cur + x) do when cur is negative?","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Kadane: what does cur = max(x, cur + x) do when cur is negative?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Keeps adding Starts a new run at x, since a negative prefix only lowers the sum Resets best Skips x","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Starts a new run at x, since a negative prefix only lowers the sum<\/p><p>x alone beats x plus something negative.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","greedy-intervals.html#q-q2"]]},{"i":"qz-greedy-intervals-q-q3","k":"quiz","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"To keep the most non-overlapping intervals, sort by…","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["To keep the most non-overlapping intervals, sort by…"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Start End Length It doesn’t matter","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> End<\/p><p>Finishing earliest leaves the most room. Sorting by start can pick one long interval that blocks many.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","greedy-intervals.html#q-q3"]]},{"i":"qz-greedy-intervals-q-q4","k":"quiz","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Are [1, 2] and [2, 3] overlapping?","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Are [1, 2] and [2, 3] overlapping?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Always yes Always no It depends on the problem: check whether touching endpoints count Only if sorted","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> It depends on the problem: check whether touching endpoints count<\/p><p>Merge Intervals (56) merges them; Non-overlapping Intervals (435) treats them as compatible. Read the statement.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","greedy-intervals.html#q-q4"]]},{"i":"qz-greedy-intervals-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Insert one interval into an already sorted, non-overlapping list. Best time?","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Insert one interval into an already sorted, non-overlapping list. Best time?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals O(n log n) O(n) O(log n) O(n²)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n)<\/p><p>No sort needed: one pass to copy, merge and copy the rest. (Binary search finds the spot in O(log n), but the output still costs O(n).)<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","greedy-intervals.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-greedy-intervals-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"Jump Game greedy: track the farthest reachable index in one pass. Time and space?","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Jump Game greedy: track the farthest reachable index in one pass. Time and space?"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals O(n²), O(n) O(n), O(1) O(n log n), O(1) O(2ⁿ), O(n)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p>One loop, one variable. The DP version would be O(n²).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","greedy-intervals.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-greedy-intervals-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Fewest coins to make an amount with coins [1, 3, 4].”","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Fewest coins to make an amount with coins [1, 3, 4].”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Greedy: largest coin first DP: greedy fails (6 = 3 + 3, not 4 + 1 + 1) Sort by end Kadane","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> DP: greedy fails (6 = 3 + 3, not 4 + 1 + 1)<\/p><p>No exchange argument holds for arbitrary coin systems.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","greedy-intervals.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-greedy-intervals-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Minimum number of platforms so no train waits.”","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Minimum number of platforms so no train waits.”"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Sweep arrivals and departures DP Backtracking Binary search only","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Sweep arrivals and departures<\/p><p>It’s Meeting Rooms II with trains.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","greedy-intervals.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-greedy-intervals-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"greedy-intervals","t":"“Assign cookies to children so the most children are content.” (455)","sub":"Greedy & Intervals · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Assign cookies to children so the most children are content.” (455)"],"w":"Greedy & Intervals Sort both; give each child the smallest cookie that satisfies them DP over subsets Heap only Union-find","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Sort both; give each child the smallest cookie that satisfies them<\/p><p>Using the smallest sufficient cookie never hurts a later child: exchange argument.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","greedy-intervals.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-bits-0","k":"post","s":"bits","t":"Think in columns of bits","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Five tricks that do everything","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Think in columns of bits","explain Think in columns of bits","what is Think in columns of bits"],"w":"Bit Manipulation Five tricks that do everything","a":"<p>Bitwise operators work on every bit position independently. <code>&amp;<\/code> keeps bits set in both, <code>|<\/code> keeps bits set in either, <code>^<\/code> keeps bits set in exactly one. That last one is the star: <code>a ^ a = 0<\/code> and <code>a ^ 0 = a<\/code>, so XOR-ing a list makes every pair vanish.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","bits.html#s-think-in-columns-of-bits"]]},{"i":"po-bits-2","k":"post","s":"bits","t":"Every subset is a number","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Masks: a number is a subset","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Every subset is a number","explain Every subset is a number","what is Every subset is a number"],"w":"Bit Manipulation Masks: a number is a subset","a":"<p>With items [a, b, c], the mask <code>101<\/code> means “take a and c”. Looping <code>mask<\/code> from 0 to 7 visits all 8 subsets with no recursion. Bit i is in the subset when <code>mask &gt;&gt; i &amp; 1<\/code> is 1. The same idea powers bitmask DP, where a whole set of visited nodes fits into one integer.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","bits.html#s-every-subset-is-a-number"]]},{"i":"po-bits-4","k":"post","s":"bits","t":"How to price a bit-manipulation solution","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Counting the cost","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["How to price a bit-manipulation solution","explain How to price a bit-manipulation solution","what is How to price a bit-manipulation solution"],"w":"Bit Manipulation Counting the cost","a":"<ul><li><b>Single operations<\/b> (&amp;, |, ^, ~, shifts) on a machine word are O(1).<\/li><li><b>Looping over the bits of n<\/b> runs once per bit: ⌊log₂ n⌋ + 1 times → O(log n), which is O(32) = O(1) for 32-bit ints.<\/li><li><b>Kernighan's trick<\/b> <code>n &amp;= n - 1<\/code> runs once per <i>set<\/i> bit: even fewer.<\/li><li><b>Over a range 0…n<\/b> multiply by n, or reuse answers: <code>bits[i] = bits[i &gt;&gt; 1] + (i &amp; 1)<\/code> → O(n).<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","bits.html#s-how-to-price-a-bit-manipulation-solution"]]},{"i":"po-bits-6","k":"post","s":"bits","t":"Cheat sheet","sub":"Bit Manipulation · The decoder","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Cheat sheet","explain Cheat sheet","what is Cheat sheet"],"w":"Bit Manipulation The decoder","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Expression<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td><code>x &amp; 1<\/code><\/td><td>Is x odd? (lowest bit)<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x &gt;&gt; i &amp; 1<\/code><\/td><td>Bit i of x<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x | (1 &lt;&lt; i)<\/code> / <code>x &amp; ~(1 &lt;&lt; i)<\/code><\/td><td>Set / clear bit i<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x ^ (1 &lt;&lt; i)<\/code><\/td><td>Toggle bit i<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x &amp; (x − 1)<\/code><\/td><td>Clear the lowest set bit<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x &amp; −x<\/code><\/td><td>Keep only the lowest set bit<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>x &gt; 0 and x &amp; (x − 1) == 0<\/code><\/td><td>Power of two<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td><code>a ^ b ^ b<\/code><\/td><td>a (pairs cancel)<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div>","L":[["Read it in the app","bits.html#s-cheat-sheet"]]},{"i":"in-bits-0","k":"post","s":"bits","t":"XOR is “add without carry”, and it undoes itself.","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["XOR is “add without carry”, and it undoes itself."],"w":"Bit Manipulation insight","a":"<p>Three properties do all the work: <code>a ^ 0 = a<\/code>, <code>a ^ a = 0<\/code>, and XOR is commutative and associative. So any sequence can be rearranged so equal values sit next to each other and cancel.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">4 ^ 1 ^ 2 ^ 1 ^ 2 = 4 ^ (1 ^ 1) ^ (2 ^ 2) = 4<\/pre><p>It also means XOR is its own inverse: if c = a ^ b then a = c ^ b. That's why it shows up in “find the missing / single number”, swaps without a temp variable, and simple checksums.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","bits.html#in-xor"]]},{"i":"in-bits-1","k":"post","s":"bits","t":"What n − 1 really does.","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["What n − 1 really does."],"w":"Bit Manipulation insight","a":"<p>Subtracting 1 from a binary number turns the lowest 1 into 0 and every 0 below it into 1, like 101100 − 1 = 101011. Everything above that bit is untouched.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">101100 &amp; 101011 = 101000   (the lowest 1 is gone)<\/pre><p>So n &amp; (n − 1) deletes exactly one set bit. Loop it and you count set bits in O(number of ones), test powers of two in O(1), and in Fenwick trees n − (n &amp; −n) walks to the parent.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","bits.html#in-kernighan"]]},{"i":"in-bits-2","k":"post","s":"bits","t":"An integer is a tiny set.","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["An integer is a tiny set."],"w":"Bit Manipulation insight","a":"<p>With n ≤ 20 items, a set of chosen items fits in one integer: bit i means item i is in. Union is <code>|<\/code>, intersection is <code>&amp;<\/code>, “add item i” is <code>| 1 &lt;&lt; i<\/code>, and “is i in?” is <code>&gt;&gt; i &amp; 1<\/code>.<\/p><p>That turns “which items have I used?” into a number you can memoize on: bitmask DP like travelling salesman, “partition to k subsets” or “shortest path visiting all nodes” uses states of (mask, current node).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","bits.html#in-mask"]]},{"i":"in-bits-3","k":"post","s":"bits","t":"The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n.","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Look deeper","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["The doubling test: run it at n, then at 2n."],"w":"Bit Manipulation insight","a":"<p>You don’t need algebra to recognise a complexity. Double the input and watch what happens to the work:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">O(1) same · O(log n) +1 step · O(n) ×2 · O(n log n) a bit over ×2 · O(n²) ×4 · O(n³) ×8 · O(2ⁿ) squares<\/pre><p>That’s exactly what the <b>×<\/b> numbers in the lab show. In an interview, say it the same way: “if the input doubles, this does twice the work, so it’s linear.”<\/p><p>For <b>space<\/b>, ask the same question about memory: what grows when n doubles? A set of seen items doubles (O(n)); two pointers don’t (O(1)).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","bits.html#in-cxdouble"]]},{"i":"qz-bits-q-b1","k":"quiz","s":"bits","t":"What is 5 ^ 3 ^ 5 ?","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["What is 5 ^ 3 ^ 5 ?"],"w":"Bit Manipulation 13 3 5 0","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 3<\/p><p>The two 5s cancel; order doesn’t matter.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","bits.html#q-b1"]]},{"i":"qz-bits-q-b2","k":"quiz","s":"bits","t":"What is 12 & 11 ? (12 = 1100, 11 = 1011)","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["What is 12 & 11 ? (12 = 1100, 11 = 1011)"],"w":"Bit Manipulation 8 4 0 12","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 8<\/p><p>1100 &amp; 1011 = 1000 = 8: the lowest set bit of 12 was erased.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","bits.html#q-b2"]]},{"i":"qz-bits-q-b3","k":"quiz","s":"bits","t":"What is 12 & −12 ?","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["What is 12 & −12 ?"],"w":"Bit Manipulation 12 4 8 1","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 4<\/p><p>It keeps only the lowest set bit of 1100, which is 0100 = 4.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","bits.html#q-b3"]]},{"i":"qz-bits-q-b4","k":"quiz","s":"bits","t":"In Python, ~5 is…","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In Python, ~5 is…"],"w":"Bit Manipulation 2 −6 4294967290 Error","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> −6<\/p><p>~x = −x − 1 for Python’s unbounded ints. Use a 32-bit mask when a problem assumes fixed width.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","bits.html#q-b4"]]},{"i":"qz-bits-q-cxq1","k":"quiz","s":"bits","t":"Single Number: XOR all n elements together. Time and space?","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Single Number: XOR all n elements together. Time and space?"],"w":"Bit Manipulation O(n), O(n) O(n), O(1) O(n log n), O(1) O(1), O(1)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n), O(1)<\/p><p>One pass, one accumulator.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","bits.html#q-cxq1"]]},{"i":"qz-bits-q-cxq2","k":"quiz","s":"bits","t":"Generate all subsets of n items with a bitmask from 0 to 2ⁿ − 1, building each subset by checking n bits. Time?","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Generate all subsets of n items with a bitmask from 0 to 2ⁿ − 1, building each subset by checking n bits. Time?"],"w":"Bit Manipulation O(2ⁿ) O(n · 2ⁿ) O(n²) O(n!)","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> O(n · 2ⁿ)<\/p><p>2ⁿ masks × n bit checks each.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","bits.html#q-cxq2"]]},{"i":"qz-bits-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"bits","t":"“Find the number that appears once when every other appears exactly three times.”","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Find the number that appears once when every other appears exactly three times.”"],"w":"Bit Manipulation XOR all Count bits mod 3 Sort x & −x","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Count bits mod 3<\/p><p>XOR only cancels pairs; triples need counting mod 3.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","bits.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-bits-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"bits","t":"“Hamming distance between x and y.”","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Hamming distance between x and y.”"],"w":"Bit Manipulation Popcount of x ^ y x & y x | y Sum of digits","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Popcount of x ^ y<\/p><p>x ^ y has 1s exactly where they differ; count them.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","bits.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-bits-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"bits","t":"“Shortest path that visits every node in a small graph (n ≤ 12).” (847)","sub":"Bit Manipulation · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Shortest path that visits every node in a small graph (n ≤ 12).” (847)"],"w":"Bit Manipulation Plain BFS by node BFS over states (node, visited mask) Union-find Kadane","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> BFS over states (node, visited mask)<\/p><p>The visited set is part of the state; a mask makes it a small integer.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","bits.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-start-0","k":"post","s":"sd-start","t":"The one idea","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · Design is about change","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The one idea","explain The one idea","what is The one idea"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking Design is about change","a":"<p>Code that never changes doesn't need design. Design exists because <b>requirements keep changing<\/b>: a new payment method, a new notification channel, ten times more users. Good design means a new requirement touches <b>one small place<\/b> instead of everything.<\/p><p>So every design decision, at every level, comes down to three questions:<\/p><p>Step through the lab: the same requirement changes hit a naive class and a designed one. Watch which boxes turn red.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-start.html#s-the-one-idea"]]},{"i":"po-sd-start-1","k":"post","s":"sd-start","t":"A box is a class","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · Reading UML in 5 minutes","cat":"System design concepts","q":["A box is a class","explain A box is a class","what is A box is a class"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking Reading UML in 5 minutes","a":"<p>Three compartments: the <b>name<\/b> on top, <b>fields<\/b> in the middle, <b>methods<\/b> at the bottom. <code>-<\/code> means private, <code>+<\/code> public, <code>#<\/code> protected. <code>«interface»<\/code> above the name marks an interface; italics mean abstract.<\/p><p>The lines between boxes are where the meaning is. There are only six, and each one is a Java construct. Step through the lab: each step adds one arrow and highlights the Java that creates it.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-start.html#s-a-box-is-a-class"]]},{"i":"po-sd-start-2","k":"post","s":"sd-start","t":"The six arrows, as a cheat sheet","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · Reading UML in 5 minutes","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The six arrows, as a cheat sheet","explain The six arrows, as a cheat sheet","what is The six arrows, as a cheat sheet"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking Reading UML in 5 minutes","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Arrow<\/th><th>Means<\/th><th>Java<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody> <tr><td>solid line, hollow triangle ▷<\/td><td>IS-A (inheritance)<\/td><td><code>class Dog extends Animal<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>dashed line, hollow triangle ▷<\/td><td>implements a contract<\/td><td><code>class Card implements Payment<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>filled diamond ◆ at the owner<\/td><td>owns a part (composition): part dies with owner<\/td><td><code>private final Engine engine = new Engine();<\/code><\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>hollow diamond ◇ at the owner<\/td><td>has a reference (aggregation): part lives on<\/td><td><code>List&lt;Player&gt; players<\/code> passed in<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>solid arrow →<\/td><td>knows about (association)<\/td><td>a field pointing to another object<\/td><\/tr> <tr><td>dashed arrow ⇢<\/td><td>uses briefly (dependency)<\/td><td>a parameter or local variable<\/td><\/tr> <\/tbody><\/table><\/div><p>Numbers at the end of a line are <b>multiplicity<\/b>: <code>1<\/code>, <code>0..1<\/code>, <code>*<\/code> (many). <code>Level ◆── * Spot<\/code> reads “a level owns many spots”.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-start.html#s-the-six-arrows-as-a-cheat-sheet"]]},{"i":"po-sd-start-3","k":"post","s":"sd-start","t":"Four layers, one idea","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · The map","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Four layers, one idea","explain Four layers, one idea","what is Four layers, one idea"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking The map","a":"<ul><li><b>Java &amp; OOP<\/b> gives you the tools: interfaces, polymorphism, composition.<\/li><li><b>SOLID<\/b> says when to use them: five smells and their cures.<\/li><li><b>Patterns<\/b> are SOLID applied to problems that keep recurring. Each one hides one kind of change.<\/li><li><b>LLD problems<\/b> combine 3–4 patterns into one design. <b>HLD<\/b> applies the same moves to whole servers: a queue is an Observer between machines, an API gateway is a Chain of Responsibility, a cache is a Proxy.<\/li><\/ul><p>Follow the chains in the lab. Each step lights up one idea travelling from a principle, through a pattern, into an LLD problem, and out to an HLD system.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-start.html#s-four-layers-one-idea"]]},{"i":"po-sd-start-4","k":"post","s":"sd-start","t":"LLD round vs HLD round","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · The two interviews","cat":"System design concepts","q":["LLD round vs HLD round","explain LLD round vs HLD round","what is LLD round vs HLD round"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking The two interviews","a":"<p>Both rounds reward the same habit: say what will change and what will break, <i>before<\/i> you draw.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-start.html#s-lld-round-vs-hld-round"]]},{"i":"po-sd-start-6","k":"post","s":"sd-start","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · The two interviews","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking The two interviews","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-start.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-start-0","k":"post","s":"sd-start","t":"Every pattern is the same move with a different “what changes”.","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Every pattern is the same move with a different “what changes”."],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking insight","a":"<p>Strategy: the <b>algorithm<\/b> changes. State: the <b>mode<\/b> changes. Observer: the <b>list of reactions<\/b> changes. Decorator: the <b>combination of extras<\/b> changes. Factory: <b>which class to create<\/b> changes. Adapter: the <b>third-party API<\/b> changes.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">what changes → interface → implementations → inject<\/pre><p>So when a pattern feels confusing, ask: “what is the thing that changes here?” The diagram will make sense immediately.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-start.html#in-samemove"]]},{"i":"in-sd-start-1","k":"post","s":"sd-start","t":"HLD is LLD with network cables.","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["HLD is LLD with network cables."],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking insight","a":"<p>A method call becomes an HTTP/gRPC call. An interface becomes an API contract. A list of listeners becomes a Kafka topic. A synchronized block becomes a database row lock. A HashMap becomes Redis.<\/p><p>The problems also scale up: what was a race between two threads is now a race between two servers; what was a slow method is now a slow service that needs a timeout and a circuit breaker. Same thinking, bigger boxes.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-start.html#in-scaleup"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-start-q-s1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-start","t":"A food app adds new discount types every month (flat, %, BOGO, first-order). What should you isolate behind an interface?","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["A food app adds new discount types every month (flat, %, BOGO, first-order). What should you isolate behind an interface?"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking The Order class The discount rule The database The restaurant list","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> The discount rule<\/p><p>The discount rule is the part that keeps changing. <code>interface Discount { Money apply(Cart c); }<\/code> and one class per rule.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-start.html#q-s1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-start-q-u1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-start","t":"A dashed line with a hollow triangle from UpiPayment to Payment means…","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["A dashed line with a hollow triangle from UpiPayment to Payment means…"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking UpiPayment extends Payment UpiPayment implements the Payment interface UpiPayment uses Payment as a parameter Payment owns UpiPayment","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> UpiPayment implements the Payment interface<\/p><p>Dashed + triangle = implements. Solid + triangle would be extends.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-start.html#q-u1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-start-q-u2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-start","t":"ParkingLot ◆── * Level . What does it say?","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["ParkingLot ◆── * Level . What does it say?"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking A level owns many lots A parking lot owns many levels, which don’t exist without it Levels are passed in and shared ParkingLot extends Level","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> A parking lot owns many levels, which don’t exist without it<\/p><p>Filled diamond at the owner = composition; <code>*<\/code> on the Level end = many levels.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-start.html#q-u2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-start-q-u3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-start","t":"class Checkout { void pay(Order o, PaymentGateway g) { g.charge(o); } } . Which line from Checkout to PaymentGateway?","sub":"Start Here: Design Thinking · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["class Checkout { void pay(Order o, PaymentGateway g) { g.charge(o); } } . Which line from Checkout to PaymentGateway?"],"w":"Start Here: Design Thinking Composition ◆ Inheritance ▷ Dependency ⇢ (dashed arrow) Aggregation ◇","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Dependency ⇢ (dashed arrow)<\/p><p>The gateway is only a parameter used during one call: a dependency.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-start.html#q-u3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-java-0","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"The four pillars, in one breath each","sub":"Java & OOP · Objects & references","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The four pillars, in one breath each","explain The four pillars, in one breath each","what is The four pillars, in one breath each"],"w":"Java & OOP Objects & references","a":"<ul><li><b>Encapsulation<\/b>Keep fields private and expose behaviour. The object guards its own rules (a balance can't go negative).<\/li><li><b>Abstraction<\/b>Show what something does, hide how. Callers use <code>PaymentGateway.pay()<\/code>, not the HTTP calls behind it.<\/li><li><b>Inheritance<\/b>A subclass reuses and specialises a parent. Powerful, but it couples the child to every detail of the parent.<\/li><li><b>Polymorphism<\/b>One call, many behaviours. <code>shape.area()<\/code> runs the right code for a circle or a square.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#s-the-four-pillars-in-one-breath-each"]]},{"i":"po-sd-java-1","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"Variables hold arrows, not objects","sub":"Java & OOP · Objects & references","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Variables hold arrows, not objects","explain Variables hold arrows, not objects","what is Variables hold arrows, not objects"],"w":"Java & OOP Objects & references","a":"<p>In Java an object lives on the heap. A variable like <code>Dog a<\/code> holds a <b>reference<\/b>: an arrow pointing at it. <code>Dog b = a;<\/code> copies the arrow, so now two arrows point at <i>one<\/i> dog. Change the dog through <code>b<\/code> and <code>a<\/code> sees it too.<\/p><p>Java is always <b>pass-by-value<\/b>, but the value it passes for an object is the arrow. A method can change the object the arrow points to, yet reassigning its own parameter changes nothing for the caller.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#s-variables-hold-arrows-not-objects"]]},{"i":"po-sd-java-2","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"A contract versus a half-built machine","sub":"Java & OOP · Contracts & polymorphism","cat":"System design concepts","q":["A contract versus a half-built machine","explain A contract versus a half-built machine","what is A contract versus a half-built machine"],"w":"Java & OOP Contracts & polymorphism","a":"<p>Default to an interface. Reach for an abstract class only when the subclasses genuinely share state or a fixed algorithm.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#s-a-contract-versus-a-half-built-machine"]]},{"i":"po-sd-java-3","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"Which method actually runs?","sub":"Java & OOP · Contracts & polymorphism","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Which method actually runs?","explain Which method actually runs?","what is Which method actually runs?"],"w":"Java & OOP Contracts & polymorphism","a":"<p>Two different decisions happen at two different times. The <b>compiler<\/b> checks the <i>declared<\/i> type: can an <code>Animal<\/code> do <code>speak()<\/code>? At <b>runtime<\/b>, the JVM looks at the <i>actual<\/i> object and runs its version: <code>Dog.speak()<\/code>. That runtime choice is <b>overriding<\/b> (dynamic dispatch). Choosing between methods with the same name but different parameters is <b>overloading<\/b>, and the compiler settles it from the declared types.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#s-which-method-actually-runs"]]},{"i":"po-sd-java-4","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"IS-A or HAS-A?","sub":"Java & OOP · Design sense","cat":"System design concepts","q":["IS-A or HAS-A?","explain IS-A or HAS-A?","what is IS-A or HAS-A?"],"w":"Java & OOP Design sense","a":"<p>Inheritance says “a <code>Car<\/code> <b>is a<\/b> <code>Vehicle<\/code>”. Composition says “a <code>Car<\/code> <b>has an<\/b> <code>Engine<\/code>”. Inheritance is fixed at compile time and leaks the parent's details into every child. Composition lets you swap parts at runtime.<\/p><p>Model coffee with inheritance and every combination of milk, sugar and syrup needs its own class: <code>MilkSugarCoffee<\/code>, <code>MilkSyrupCoffee<\/code>… 3 add-ons already give 8 classes. With composition you combine a few small objects instead. That is the whole idea behind Decorator and Strategy.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#s-is-a-or-has-a"]]},{"i":"po-sd-java-5","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"The topics that matter","sub":"Java & OOP · Design sense","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The topics that matter","explain The topics that matter","what is The topics that matter"],"w":"Java & OOP Design sense","a":"<p>Each card is one interview topic. Say the answer out loud first, then reveal the Java.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#s-the-topics-that-matter"]]},{"i":"po-sd-java-8","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Java & OOP · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Java & OOP The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-java-0","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"Most pattern code is just arrows between objects.","sub":"Java & OOP · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Most pattern code is just arrows between objects."],"w":"Java & OOP insight","a":"<p>Decorator wraps an object by holding an arrow to it. Strategy holds an arrow to an algorithm. Observer keeps a list of arrows to listeners. Once you picture variables as arrows into the heap, pattern diagrams read like a map.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">assignment copies arrows · methods follow arrows · new makes objects<\/pre><p>It also explains classic bugs: two lists sharing one inner object, or a “defensive copy” that only copied the outer arrow.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#in-arrows"]]},{"i":"in-sd-java-1","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"Polymorphism is the engine every pattern runs on.","sub":"Java & OOP · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Polymorphism is the engine every pattern runs on."],"w":"Java & OOP insight","a":"<p>Nearly every pattern replaces an <code>if (type == …)<\/code> chain with a method call on an interface. The caller asks <code>strategy.apply()<\/code>, <code>state.next()<\/code> or <code>handler.handle()<\/code>, and dynamic dispatch picks the right code.<\/p><p>So when you see a growing <code>switch<\/code> on a type field, the refactor is almost always: make an interface, move each branch into its own class, call through the interface.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#in-patterns"]]},{"i":"in-sd-java-2","k":"post","s":"sd-java","t":"Coupling and cohesion are the “why” behind SOLID.","sub":"Java & OOP · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Coupling and cohesion are the “why” behind SOLID."],"w":"Java & OOP insight","a":"<p><b>Coupling<\/b> is how much one class knows about another. <b>Cohesion<\/b> is how related the things inside one class are. Good designs have low coupling and high cohesion.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">SRP raises cohesion · DIP and ISP lower coupling · OCP falls out of both<\/pre><p>In an interview, naming these two words while justifying a design choice instantly sounds senior.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-java.html#in-coupling"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-java-q-j1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-java","t":"What does this print?","sub":"Java & OOP · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["What does this print?"],"w":"Java & OOP 2 3 Compile error It depends on the JVM","a":"<pre class=\"ap-pre\">List&lt;Integer&gt; a = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(1, 2));\nList&lt;Integer&gt; b = a;\nb.add(3);\nSystem.out.println(a.size());<\/pre><p><b>Answer:<\/b> 3<\/p><p><code>b = a<\/code> copies the reference. Both variables point at one list, so adding through b is visible through a.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-java.html#q-j1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-java-q-j2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-java","t":"Two Point(1, 2) objects override equals but not hashCode . You put one in a HashSet and call contains with the other. Result?","sub":"Java & OOP · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Two Point(1, 2) objects override equals but not hashCode . You put one in a HashSet and call contains with the other. Result?"],"w":"Java & OOP Always true Usually false Compile error Throws","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Usually false<\/p><p>Different default hash codes send the lookup to a different bucket, so equals is never even called.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-java.html#q-j2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-java-q-j3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-java","t":"Animal a = new Dog(); greet(a); with overloads greet(Animal) and greet(Dog) . Which runs?","sub":"Java & OOP · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Animal a = new Dog(); greet(a); with overloads greet(Animal) and greet(Dog) . Which runs?"],"w":"Java & OOP greet(Dog) greet(Animal) Ambiguous, compile error Depends on runtime","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> greet(Animal)<\/p><p>Overloads are resolved at compile time from the declared type, Animal.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-java.html#q-j3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-java-q-j4","k":"quiz","s":"sd-java","t":"Why can’t you write new T() inside a generic class?","sub":"Java & OOP · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Why can’t you write new T() inside a generic class?"],"w":"Java & OOP T might be abstract Type erasure removes T at runtime Generics only work with interfaces You can, it just warns","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Type erasure removes T at runtime<\/p><p>After compilation T is erased to Object (or its bound), so the JVM doesn’t know which class to construct. Pass a <code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;<\/code> or a <code>Class&lt;T&gt;<\/code> instead.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-java.html#q-j4"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-java-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-java","t":"“A method changes a field on the object you passed, but setting the parameter to a new object has no effect outside.” Why?","sub":"Java & OOP · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“A method changes a field on the object you passed, but setting the parameter to a new object has no effect outside.” Why?"],"w":"Java & OOP Java is pass-by-reference Java passes the reference by value Garbage collection The object is immutable","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Java passes the reference by value<\/p><p>The method gets a copy of the arrow: it can follow it, but repointing the copy doesn’t touch the caller’s variable.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-java.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-java-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-java","t":"“Add sound, colour and size options in any combination without a class per combination.”","sub":"Java & OOP · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Add sound, colour and size options in any combination without a class per combination.”"],"w":"Java & OOP Deep inheritance Composition (HAS-A), e.g. Decorator Overloading Static methods","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Composition (HAS-A), e.g. Decorator<\/p><p>Combinations multiply with inheritance; composition stacks small objects instead.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-java.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-java-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-java","t":"“Several unrelated classes (Invoice, User, Report) must all be exportable.”","sub":"Java & OOP · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Several unrelated classes (Invoice, User, Report) must all be exportable.”"],"w":"Java & OOP Abstract class Exportable Interface Exportable Enum A static utility","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Interface Exportable<\/p><p>Unrelated classes sharing a capability, with no shared state: an interface.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-java.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-solid-0","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"SOLID is a list of smells and their cures","sub":"SOLID · S, O & L","cat":"System design concepts","q":["SOLID is a list of smells and their cures","explain SOLID is a list of smells and their cures","what is SOLID is a list of smells and their cures"],"w":"SOLID S, O & L","a":"<p>Remember the one idea from the Start page: <i>find what changes and isolate it<\/i>. SOLID is five specific ways that idea gets broken, each with a recognisable smell:<\/p><ul><li><b>S<\/b>ingle Responsibility. Smell: one class changes for several unrelated reasons. Cure: split it.<\/li><li><b>O<\/b>pen/Closed. Smell: every new type edits an <code>if-else<\/code>. Cure: interface + new class.<\/li><li><b>L<\/b>iskov Substitution. Smell: a subclass breaks what the parent promised. Cure: a different hierarchy.<\/li><li><b>I<\/b>nterface Segregation. Smell: classes implement methods they don't need. Cure: smaller interfaces.<\/li><li><b>D<\/b>ependency Inversion. Smell: business logic says <code>new MySqlRepo()<\/code>. Cure: depend on an interface, inject it.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#s-solid-is-a-list-of-smells-and-their-cures"]]},{"i":"po-sd-solid-1","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"S: one reason to change","sub":"SOLID · S, O & L","cat":"System design concepts","q":["S: one reason to change","explain S: one reason to change","what is S: one reason to change"],"w":"SOLID S, O & L","a":"<p>Ask: <b>who could ask me to change this class?<\/b> If the answer is “the finance team, the design team <i>and<\/i> the DBA”, it has three responsibilities. “Responsibility” means <i>reason to change<\/i>, not “does one thing”.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#s-s-one-reason-to-change"]]},{"i":"po-sd-solid-2","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"O: add, don't edit","sub":"SOLID · S, O & L","cat":"System design concepts","q":["O: add, don't edit","explain O: add, don't edit","what is O: add, don't edit"],"w":"SOLID S, O & L","a":"<p>Working, tested code should stay closed. New behaviour arrives as a <b>new class<\/b> plugged into an existing interface. The tell is a <code>switch<\/code> on a type that grows every sprint. This is the move from the Start page, exactly.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#s-o-add-don-t-edit"]]},{"i":"po-sd-solid-3","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"L: a subclass must keep the parent's promises","sub":"SOLID · S, O & L","cat":"System design concepts","q":["L: a subclass must keep the parent's promises","explain L: a subclass must keep the parent's promises","what is L: a subclass must keep the parent's promises"],"w":"SOLID S, O & L","a":"<p>If code works with a <code>Rectangle<\/code>, it must work with every subclass of it. <code>Square extends Rectangle<\/code> looks right (“a square <i>is a<\/i> rectangle” in maths) but breaks callers that set width and height separately. IS-A in code is about <b>behaviour<\/b>, not real-world categories.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#s-l-a-subclass-must-keep-the-parent-s-promises"]]},{"i":"po-sd-solid-4","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"I: no one should depend on methods they don't use","sub":"SOLID · I & D","cat":"System design concepts","q":["I: no one should depend on methods they don't use","explain I: no one should depend on methods they don't use","what is I: no one should depend on methods they don't use"],"w":"SOLID I & D","a":"<p>A fat <code>Worker<\/code> interface forces a <code>Robot<\/code> to implement <code>eat()<\/code> with an empty body or an exception. Notice that the cure for ISP is the same as for the LSP smell above: <b>split the contract<\/b> so each class only signs what it can honour.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#s-i-no-one-should-depend-on-methods-they-don-t-use"]]},{"i":"po-sd-solid-5","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"D: flip the arrow","sub":"SOLID · I & D","cat":"System design concepts","q":["D: flip the arrow","explain D: flip the arrow","what is D: flip the arrow"],"w":"SOLID I & D","a":"<p>High-level policy (<code>OrderService<\/code>) shouldn't point at low-level detail (<code>MySqlOrderRepo<\/code>). Both should point at an abstraction (<code>OrderRepo<\/code>) that the <b>policy side owns<\/b>. Watch the arrow on the database class turn around in the diagram.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#s-d-flip-the-arrow"]]},{"i":"po-sd-solid-6","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"The five, as interview cards","sub":"SOLID · I & D","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The five, as interview cards","explain The five, as interview cards","what is The five, as interview cards"],"w":"SOLID I & D","a":"<p>Say the smell and the cure out loud first, check the diagram, then reveal the before/after Java.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#s-the-five-as-interview-cards"]]},{"i":"po-sd-solid-9","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"SOLID · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"SOLID The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-solid-0","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"Each principle is a cure for one smell.","sub":"SOLID · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Each principle is a cure for one smell."],"w":"SOLID insight","a":"<p>Reviewers don’t think “does this obey OCP?”. They notice a smell: a growing switch, a class nobody wants to touch, an empty override, a <code>new<\/code> in business logic. The principle is the name of the cure.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">growing switch → O · god class → S · empty override → L/I · new Concrete() → D<\/pre>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#in-smells"]]},{"i":"in-sd-solid-1","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"“Inversion” means the interface belongs to the caller.","sub":"SOLID · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["“Inversion” means the interface belongs to the caller."],"w":"SOLID insight","a":"<p>Without DIP, the policy layer imports the database layer. With DIP, the policy layer <b>owns<\/b> the interface and the database layer imports it. The source-code arrow now points from detail to policy, the opposite of the call direction.<\/p><p>That’s why a well-layered app can swap MySQL for DynamoDB without recompiling the business module.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#in-flip"]]},{"i":"in-sd-solid-2","k":"post","s":"sd-solid","t":"Patterns are SOLID applied to recurring problems.","sub":"SOLID · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Patterns are SOLID applied to recurring problems."],"w":"SOLID insight","a":"<p><b>Strategy<\/b> and <b>State<\/b> are OCP for algorithms and modes. <b>Decorator<\/b> is OCP for adding features. <b>Factory<\/b> keeps <code>new<\/code> out of policy code (DIP). <b>Adapter<\/b> lets a third-party class satisfy your interface (DIP + ISP). <b>Facade<\/b> gives clients a small interface (ISP).<\/p><p>When you justify a pattern in an interview, name the principle it serves.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-solid.html#in-topatterns"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-solid-q-s1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-solid","t":"A ReportService has a method with a 9-branch switch(format) for PDF, CSV, XLSX… and product wants JSON next week. Which principle is under pressure?","sub":"SOLID · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["A ReportService has a method with a 9-branch switch(format) for PDF, CSV, XLSX… and product wants JSON next week. Which principle is under pressure?"],"w":"SOLID SRP OCP LSP ISP","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> OCP<\/p><p>Adding a format means editing a working method. Put each format behind a <code>ReportFormatter<\/code> interface.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-solid.html#q-s1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-solid-q-s2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-solid","t":"Square extends Rectangle . A test sets width 5, height 4 and expects area 20. With a Square it gets 16. Which principle broke?","sub":"SOLID · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Square extends Rectangle . A test sets width 5, height 4 and expects area 20. With a Square it gets 16. Which principle broke?"],"w":"SOLID SRP OCP LSP DIP","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> LSP<\/p><p>A Square can’t be substituted where a Rectangle is expected because it changes the meaning of setWidth/setHeight.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-solid.html#q-s2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-solid-q-s3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-solid","t":"Your OrderService unit tests need a running MySQL instance. What’s the best fix?","sub":"SOLID · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Your OrderService unit tests need a running MySQL instance. What’s the best fix?"],"w":"SOLID Use an in-memory MySQL Make OrderRepo an interface and inject a fake Make the repo static Mock the JDBC driver","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Make OrderRepo an interface and inject a fake<\/p><p>Depend on an abstraction and inject it (DIP); tests pass an in-memory implementation.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-solid.html#q-s3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-solid-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-solid","t":"“Printer interface has print, scan and fax. A basic printer throws on scan and fax.”","sub":"SOLID · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Printer interface has print, scan and fax. A basic printer throws on scan and fax.”"],"w":"SOLID SRP OCP LSP ISP","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> ISP<\/p><p>The interface is too fat. Split into Printable, Scannable, Faxable. (The throwing overrides also smell of LSP.)<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-solid.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-solid-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-solid","t":"“The User class validates input, hashes passwords, sends welcome emails and writes to the DB.”","sub":"SOLID · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“The User class validates input, hashes passwords, sends welcome emails and writes to the DB.”"],"w":"SOLID SRP ISP DIP LSP","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> SRP<\/p><p>Four reasons to change in one class. Split into focused collaborators.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-solid.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-solid-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-solid","t":"“NotificationService does new SmtpClient() and we want to add SMS.”","sub":"SOLID · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“NotificationService does new SmtpClient() and we want to add SMS.”"],"w":"SOLID LSP DIP (and OCP) ISP SRP only","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> DIP (and OCP)<\/p><p>Depend on a <code>Channel<\/code> interface and inject implementations; adding SMS becomes a new class.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-solid.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-creational-0","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"What changes, for creational patterns?","sub":"Creational Patterns · One & many","cat":"System design concepts","q":["What changes, for creational patterns?","explain What changes, for creational patterns?","what is What changes, for creational patterns?"],"w":"Creational Patterns One & many","a":"<p>Every <code>new ConcreteClass()<\/code> welds the caller to that class. Creational patterns ask: <b>what about creation will change?<\/b><\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#s-what-changes-for-creational-patterns"]]},{"i":"po-sd-creational-1","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"Singleton: exactly one, reachable everywhere","sub":"Creational Patterns · One & many","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Singleton: exactly one, reachable everywhere","explain Singleton: exactly one, reachable everywhere","what is Singleton: exactly one, reachable everywhere"],"w":"Creational Patterns One & many","a":"<p>Analogy: a country has one central bank. Anyone can reach it, nobody can open a second one. In code: a <b>private constructor<\/b> (nobody else can <code>new<\/code> it) plus a <b>static accessor<\/b> that hands out the one instance.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#s-singleton-exactly-one-reachable-everywhere"]]},{"i":"po-sd-creational-2","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"The real interview question: two threads at once","sub":"Creational Patterns · One & many","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The real interview question: two threads at once","explain The real interview question: two threads at once","what is The real interview question: two threads at once"],"w":"Creational Patterns One & many","a":"<p>The lazy version <code>if (instance == null) instance = new …<\/code> breaks when two threads run it together. This is the same <b>check-then-act<\/b> bug that double-books seats in BookMyShow. Watch it happen, then the fix.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#s-the-real-interview-question-two-threads-at-once"]]},{"i":"po-sd-creational-3","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"Factory: one place decides which class","sub":"Creational Patterns · One & many","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Factory: one place decides which class","explain Factory: one place decides which class","what is Factory: one place decides which class"],"w":"Creational Patterns One & many","a":"<p>Analogy: you order “a coffee” at the counter; the barista decides which machine makes it. The checkout shouldn't know there are <code>CardPayment<\/code>, <code>UpiPayment<\/code> and <code>WalletPayment<\/code> classes. It asks a factory for “a Payment for UPI” and gets back the interface.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#s-factory-one-place-decides-which-class"]]},{"i":"po-sd-creational-4","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"Factory Method and Abstract Factory","sub":"Creational Patterns · One & many","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Factory Method and Abstract Factory","explain Factory Method and Abstract Factory","what is Factory Method and Abstract Factory"],"w":"Creational Patterns One & many","a":"<p><b>Factory Method<\/b>: a base class runs a fixed algorithm (<code>deliver()<\/code>) but leaves one step, “create the transport”, to subclasses. It's Template Method where the varying step is creation.<\/p><p><b>Abstract Factory<\/b>: when several objects must <i>match<\/i> (Mac button + Mac checkbox), one factory per family creates all of them, so you can never mix families.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#s-factory-method-and-abstract-factory"]]},{"i":"po-sd-creational-5","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"Builder: no more telescoping constructors","sub":"Creational Patterns · Build & copy","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Builder: no more telescoping constructors","explain Builder: no more telescoping constructors","what is Builder: no more telescoping constructors"],"w":"Creational Patterns Build & copy","a":"<p>Analogy: ordering at Subway. You don't shout eleven words at once; you pick bread, then cheese, then veggies, then say “done”. A Builder gives every parameter a <b>name<\/b>, keeps <b>defaults<\/b>, validates once in <code>build()<\/code>, and returns an <b>immutable<\/b> object.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#s-builder-no-more-telescoping-constructors"]]},{"i":"po-sd-creational-6","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"Prototype: copy a configured object","sub":"Creational Patterns · Build & copy","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Prototype: copy a configured object","explain Prototype: copy a configured object","what is Prototype: copy a configured object"],"w":"Creational Patterns Build & copy","a":"<p>Analogy: a document template. You don't retype the letterhead; you make a copy and edit it. The trap is the copy itself: a <b>shallow<\/b> copy shares inner lists with the original.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#s-prototype-copy-a-configured-object"]]},{"i":"po-sd-creational-7","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"The patterns, as interview cards","sub":"Creational Patterns · Build & copy","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The patterns, as interview cards","explain The patterns, as interview cards","what is The patterns, as interview cards"],"w":"Creational Patterns Build & copy","a":"<p>Check the diagram, say when you'd use it, then reveal the Java.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#s-the-patterns-as-interview-cards"]]},{"i":"po-sd-creational-10","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Creational Patterns · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Creational Patterns The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-creational-0","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"A singleton is a global variable with good manners.","sub":"Creational Patterns · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["A singleton is a global variable with good manners."],"w":"Creational Patterns insight","a":"<p>It solves “only one”, but <code>Config.getInstance()<\/code> sprinkled through the code hides the dependency and makes every test share the same state. Senior answer: create one instance at startup and <b>inject<\/b> it. Spring beans are singletons by default and nobody calls <code>getInstance()<\/code>.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">one instance ≠ global access · keep the first, avoid the second<\/pre>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#in-global"]]},{"i":"in-sd-creational-1","k":"post","s":"sd-creational","t":"Builder is three wins in one.","sub":"Creational Patterns · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Builder is three wins in one."],"w":"Creational Patterns insight","a":"<p><b>Named parameters<\/b>: <code>.cheese(true)<\/code> instead of a mystery boolean. <b>One validation point<\/b>: <code>build()<\/code> can check rules that involve several fields. <b>Immutability<\/b>: the final object has only final fields, so it is safe to share across threads.<\/p><p>Say all three when asked “why not just setters?”. Setters give you the first, but leave a half-configured, mutable object.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-creational.html#in-builder"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-creational-q-c1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-creational","t":"Two threads call the naive lazy getInstance() at the same moment. What can happen?","sub":"Creational Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Two threads call the naive lazy getInstance() at the same moment. What can happen?"],"w":"Creational Patterns Deadlock Two instances get created NullPointerException always Nothing, statics are thread-safe","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Two instances get created<\/p><p>Both see null before either assigns. Check-then-act is not atomic without a lock.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-creational.html#q-c1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-creational-q-c2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-creational","t":"You need a button, checkbox and menu that all match the OS theme. Which pattern?","sub":"Creational Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["You need a button, checkbox and menu that all match the OS theme. Which pattern?"],"w":"Creational Patterns Simple Factory Factory Method Abstract Factory Prototype","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Abstract Factory<\/p><p>A family of related products that must be consistent → one factory per family.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-creational.html#q-c2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-creational-q-c3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-creational","t":"Main reason to validate inside build() rather than in each setter?","sub":"Creational Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Main reason to validate inside build() rather than in each setter?"],"w":"Creational Patterns Speed Rules can involve several fields, checked once on a complete object Setters can’t throw Builders must be static","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Rules can involve several fields, checked once on a complete object<\/p><p>For example “thin crust can’t have more than 3 toppings” needs both fields at once.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-creational.html#q-c3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-creational-q-c4","k":"quiz","s":"sd-creational","t":"Document copy = original.shallowCopy(); copy.pages.add(\"x\"); What happens to original.pages ?","sub":"Creational Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Document copy = original.shallowCopy(); copy.pages.add(\"x\"); What happens to original.pages ?"],"w":"Creational Patterns Unchanged Also gets \"x\" Throws Becomes null","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Also gets \"x\"<\/p><p>A shallow copy shares the same list object. Copy the list: <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(other.pages)<\/code>.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-creational.html#q-c4"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-creational-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-creational","t":"“An HTTP request object with URL, method, 12 optional headers, timeout and body.”","sub":"Creational Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“An HTTP request object with URL, method, 12 optional headers, timeout and body.”"],"w":"Creational Patterns Singleton Builder Prototype Abstract Factory","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Builder<\/p><p>Many optional parts and an immutable result: Builder (exactly what <code>HttpRequest.newBuilder()<\/code> does).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-creational.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-creational-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-creational","t":"“Create a Car, Bike or Truck object depending on the vehicle type at the parking gate.”","sub":"Creational Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Create a Car, Bike or Truck object depending on the vehicle type at the parking gate.”"],"w":"Creational Patterns Factory Builder Prototype Singleton","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Factory<\/p><p>The class depends on input → factory returning a Vehicle.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-creational.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-creational-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-creational","t":"“Spawn 500 enemies that share a heavy configuration but then move independently.”","sub":"Creational Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Spawn 500 enemies that share a heavy configuration but then move independently.”"],"w":"Creational Patterns Singleton Prototype Abstract Factory Builder","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Prototype<\/p><p>Configure once, copy many. (Shared immutable parts could also use Flyweight.)<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-creational.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-structural-0","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"What changes, for structural patterns?","sub":"Structural Patterns · Wrappers","cat":"System design concepts","q":["What changes, for structural patterns?","explain What changes, for structural patterns?","what is What changes, for structural patterns?"],"w":"Structural Patterns Wrappers","a":"<p>Structural patterns are about <b>how objects are combined<\/b>. The question is still “what changes?”:<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#s-what-changes-for-structural-patterns"]]},{"i":"po-sd-structural-1","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"Decorator: features in any combination","sub":"Structural Patterns · Wrappers","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Decorator: features in any combination","explain Decorator: features in any combination","what is Decorator: features in any combination"],"w":"Structural Patterns Wrappers","a":"<p>Analogy: a coffee order. Espresso, then milk around it, then whip around that. Each add-on is a small class that <b>is a<\/b> Beverage and <b>has a<\/b> Beverage inside: it delegates inward, then adds its bit. The diagram has a famous shape: an arrow going <i>up<\/i> to the interface <b>and<\/b> a diamond pointing back at it.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#s-decorator-features-in-any-combination"]]},{"i":"po-sd-structural-2","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"Watch the calls run","sub":"Structural Patterns · Wrappers","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Watch the calls run","explain Watch the calls run","what is Watch the calls run"],"w":"Structural Patterns Wrappers","a":"<p>The class diagram shows the <i>shape<\/i>; this lab shows the <i>objects at runtime<\/i>: one call travelling through the wrappers and the answers coming back, like recursion.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#s-watch-the-calls-run"]]},{"i":"po-sd-structural-3","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"Proxy: same interface, controls access","sub":"Structural Patterns · Wrappers","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Proxy: same interface, controls access","explain Proxy: same interface, controls access","what is Proxy: same interface, controls access"],"w":"Structural Patterns Wrappers","a":"<p>Analogy: a receptionist. You think you're talking to the doctor's office; the receptionist answers what she can (cache), checks your appointment (permission), and only then lets you through. The proxy implements the same interface and holds the real object.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#s-proxy-same-interface-controls-access"]]},{"i":"po-sd-structural-4","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"Adapter and Facade: translate, simplify","sub":"Structural Patterns · Wrappers","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Adapter and Facade: translate, simplify","explain Adapter and Facade: translate, simplify","what is Adapter and Facade: translate, simplify"],"w":"Structural Patterns Wrappers","a":"<p><b>Adapter<\/b> = a travel plug: your laptop expects an Indian socket, the hotel has a UK one. <b>Facade<\/b> = a hotel concierge: one person who calls the taxi, restaurant and theatre for you.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#s-adapter-and-facade-translate-simplify"]]},{"i":"po-sd-structural-5","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"Composite: treat one and many the same","sub":"Structural Patterns · Trees & splits","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Composite: treat one and many the same","explain Composite: treat one and many the same","what is Composite: treat one and many the same"],"w":"Structural Patterns Trees & splits","a":"<p>Analogy: a folder on your laptop. Asking a folder its size makes it ask each child, and children can be files or more folders. In the diagram, the group <b>is a<\/b> Node and <b>has many<\/b> Nodes. That self-reference is what lets it nest to any depth.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#s-composite-treat-one-and-many-the-same"]]},{"i":"po-sd-structural-6","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"Bridge and Flyweight","sub":"Structural Patterns · Trees & splits","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Bridge and Flyweight","explain Bridge and Flyweight","what is Bridge and Flyweight"],"w":"Structural Patterns Trees & splits","a":"<p><b>Bridge<\/b>: shapes × renderers would need a class for every pair. Instead, a shape <b>has a<\/b> renderer: two small hierarchies joined by one line (the “bridge”). <b>Flyweight<\/b>: a million characters share 20 style objects instead of carrying their own.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#s-bridge-and-flyweight"]]},{"i":"po-sd-structural-7","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"The patterns, as interview cards","sub":"Structural Patterns · Trees & splits","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The patterns, as interview cards","explain The patterns, as interview cards","what is The patterns, as interview cards"],"w":"Structural Patterns Trees & splits","a":"<p>Check the diagram, say the one-line tell, then reveal the Java.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#s-the-patterns-as-interview-cards"]]},{"i":"po-sd-structural-10","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Structural Patterns · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Structural Patterns The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-structural-0","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"Same shape, different intent: name the intent.","sub":"Structural Patterns · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Same shape, different intent: name the intent."],"w":"Structural Patterns insight","a":"<p>Decorator, Proxy and (object) Adapter all hold a reference to another object and forward calls. The code can be nearly identical. What differs is the <b>reason<\/b>:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">Decorator adds · Proxy guards · Adapter translates · Facade simplifies<\/pre><p>If asked “isn’t that just a Decorator?”, answer with intent: “It doesn’t add behaviour, it controls access, so it’s a Proxy.”<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#in-intent"]]},{"i":"in-sd-structural-1","k":"post","s":"sd-structural","t":"Composite is recursion on objects.","sub":"Structural Patterns · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Composite is recursion on objects."],"w":"Structural Patterns insight","a":"<p>A folder’s <code>size()<\/code> is the same shape as a tree DFS: the leaf is the base case, the group combines its children’s answers. If you’re comfortable with recursion from the DSA track, Composite is just that, spread across classes.<\/p><p>Decorator is recursion too: a linked list of wrappers where the innermost object is the base case.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-structural.html#in-recursion"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-structural-q-t1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-structural","t":"new Milk(new Milk(new Espresso())) with Espresso = 100 and Milk = +10. What does cost() return?","sub":"Structural Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["new Milk(new Milk(new Espresso())) with Espresso = 100 and Milk = +10. What does cost() return?"],"w":"Structural Patterns 110 120 100 Compile error","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 120<\/p><p>Each Milk delegates inward and adds 10: 100 → 110 → 120.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-structural.html#q-t1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-structural-q-t2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-structural","t":"Your app expects sendSms(to, text) ; the new vendor SDK has dispatch(MessageRequest) . Which pattern?","sub":"Structural Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Your app expects sendSms(to, text) ; the new vendor SDK has dispatch(MessageRequest) . Which pattern?"],"w":"Structural Patterns Facade Adapter Bridge Decorator","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Adapter<\/p><p>Translate one interface into another you already depend on.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-structural.html#q-t2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-structural-q-t3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-structural","t":"4 notification types × 3 channels with inheritance means 12 classes. With Bridge?","sub":"Structural Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["4 notification types × 3 channels with inheritance means 12 classes. With Bridge?"],"w":"Structural Patterns 12 7 4 3","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 7<\/p><p>4 notification classes + 3 channel classes; the notification holds a channel.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-structural.html#q-t3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-structural-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-structural","t":"“Add logging, retries and metrics to an HTTP client, any combination, configurable at startup.”","sub":"Structural Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Add logging, retries and metrics to an HTTP client, any combination, configurable at startup.”"],"w":"Structural Patterns Adapter Decorator Facade Flyweight","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Decorator<\/p><p>Stackable features on the same interface.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-structural.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-structural-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-structural","t":"“Load a high-resolution image only when it’s first displayed.”","sub":"Structural Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Load a high-resolution image only when it’s first displayed.”"],"w":"Structural Patterns Virtual Proxy Decorator Composite Builder","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Virtual Proxy<\/p><p>Same interface, delays creating the expensive real object.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-structural.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-structural-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-structural","t":"“A menu contains items and submenus; render the whole thing with one call.”","sub":"Structural Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“A menu contains items and submenus; render the whole thing with one call.”"],"w":"Structural Patterns Composite Bridge Facade Chain of Responsibility","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Composite<\/p><p>Part-whole tree with a shared operation.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-structural.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-behavioral-1","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Strategy: the algorithm is a swappable object","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Swap & notify","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Strategy: the algorithm is a swappable object","explain Strategy: the algorithm is a swappable object","what is Strategy: the algorithm is a swappable object"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Swap & notify","a":"<p>Analogy: Google Maps asks “car, bike or walk?” The route-finding <i>job<\/i> stays the same; the <i>algorithm<\/i> is swapped. If you did the OCP lab on the SOLID page, you've already built this diagram.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#s-strategy-the-algorithm-is-a-swappable-object"]]},{"i":"po-sd-behavioral-2","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Observer: one change, many listeners","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Swap & notify","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Observer: one change, many listeners","explain Observer: one change, many listeners","what is Observer: one change, many listeners"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Swap & notify","a":"<p>Analogy: a YouTube channel. You subscribe once; every new video notifies you. The channel doesn't know who you are or what you'll do. Same skeleton as Strategy, but the context holds a <b>list<\/b> and calls <b>all<\/b> of them.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#s-observer-one-change-many-listeners"]]},{"i":"po-sd-behavioral-3","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Command: an action becomes an object","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Swap & notify","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Command: an action becomes an object","explain Command: an action becomes an object","what is Command: an action becomes an object"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Swap & notify","a":"<p>Analogy: a waiter's order slip. The slip can be queued in the kitchen, cancelled, or replayed, because the request is written down as a thing. Memento is its partner: a saved snapshot you can go back to.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#s-command-an-action-becomes-an-object"]]},{"i":"po-sd-behavioral-4","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Watch them run","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Swap & notify","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Watch them run","explain Watch them run","what is Watch them run"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Swap & notify","a":"<p>The diagrams show the classes. This lab shows the objects in action: a fan-out, an undo stack, a state machine and a chain of checks.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#s-watch-them-run"]]},{"i":"po-sd-behavioral-5","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"State: each mode is a class","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Flow & state","cat":"System design concepts","q":["State: each mode is a class","explain State: each mode is a class","what is State: each mode is a class"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Flow & state","a":"<p>Analogy: a traffic light. Pressing the pedestrian button means different things on red and on green. Instead of an <code>if (state == …)<\/code> in every method, each state is a class that knows what's allowed and which state comes next.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#s-state-each-mode-is-a-class"]]},{"i":"po-sd-behavioral-6","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Chain of Responsibility: a line of handlers","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Flow & state","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Chain of Responsibility: a line of handlers","explain Chain of Responsibility: a line of handlers","what is Chain of Responsibility: a line of handlers"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Flow & state","a":"<p>Analogy: airport security. ID check, then baggage scan, then body scan; any one can stop you. Each handler knows only the next one.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#s-chain-of-responsibility-a-line-of-handlers"]]},{"i":"po-sd-behavioral-7","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Template Method, Iterator, Mediator","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Flow & state","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Template Method, Iterator, Mediator","explain Template Method, Iterator, Mediator","what is Template Method, Iterator, Mediator"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Flow & state","a":"<p><b>Template Method<\/b>: a recipe with one blank (“parse the file”). <b>Iterator<\/b>: a remote control's “next channel” without knowing how channels are stored. <b>Mediator<\/b>: an air-traffic tower; planes talk to the tower, never to each other.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#s-template-method-iterator-mediator"]]},{"i":"po-sd-behavioral-8","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"The patterns, as interview cards","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Flow & state","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The patterns, as interview cards","explain The patterns, as interview cards","what is The patterns, as interview cards"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Flow & state","a":"<p>Check the diagram, say the one-line tell, then reveal the Java.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#s-the-patterns-as-interview-cards"]]},{"i":"po-sd-behavioral-11","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-behavioral-0","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Command turns a verb into a noun.","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Command turns a verb into a noun."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns insight","a":"<p><code>editor.insert(\"hi\")<\/code> is a verb: it happens and is gone. <code>new InsertText(editor, 0, \"hi\")<\/code> is a noun: a thing you can hold. Things can be pushed on a stack (undo), put in a queue (job systems), serialised (event sourcing) or replayed (macros).<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">action as object → store · queue · log · undo<\/pre>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#in-noun"]]},{"i":"in-sd-behavioral-1","k":"post","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"Strategy is chosen; State evolves.","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Strategy is chosen; State evolves."],"w":"Behavioral Patterns insight","a":"<p>The class diagrams are identical: a context holding an interface. The difference is who changes the object. With <b>Strategy<\/b> the client picks it once (“use surge pricing”). With <b>State<\/b> the states themselves return the next state as events arrive (Idle → HasCoin → Dispensing).<\/p><p>If the question mentions a lifecycle or transitions, it’s State. If it mentions interchangeable algorithms or policies, it’s Strategy.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-behavioral.html#in-statevs"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-behavioral-q-b1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"The order service calls 3 listeners synchronously. The SMS listener takes 4 seconds. What happens to place() ?","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["The order service calls 3 listeners synchronously. The SMS listener takes 4 seconds. What happens to place() ?"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Unaffected It takes at least 4 extra seconds SMS is skipped It throws","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> It takes at least 4 extra seconds<\/p><p>Plain Observer runs listeners in the caller’s thread. Make slow listeners async or publish to a queue.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-behavioral.html#q-b1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-behavioral-q-b2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"An order goes Created → Paid → Shipped → Delivered, and cancel is only allowed before Shipped. Best fit?","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["An order goes Created → Paid → Shipped → Delivered, and cancel is only allowed before Shipped. Best fit?"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Strategy State Observer Template Method","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> State<\/p><p>A lifecycle with transition rules: each state knows what’s allowed and what comes next.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-behavioral.html#q-b2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-behavioral-q-b3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"CSV, Excel and JSON importers all validate → parse → save → audit; only parse differs. Pattern?","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["CSV, Excel and JSON importers all validate → parse → save → audit; only parse differs. Pattern?"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Strategy Template Method Chain of Responsibility Command","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Template Method<\/p><p>Fixed skeleton with one varying step. (Strategy would also work by injecting a Parser; Template uses inheritance.)<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-behavioral.html#q-b3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-behavioral-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"“Users can undo and redo the last 50 actions in a drawing app.”","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Users can undo and redo the last 50 actions in a drawing app.”"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Memento only Command Observer Iterator","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Command<\/p><p>Actions as objects on undo/redo stacks. (Memento can complement it for complex state.)<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-behavioral.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-behavioral-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"“Leave requests up to 2 days go to the lead, up to 10 to the manager, more to HR.”","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Leave requests up to 2 days go to the lead, up to 10 to the manager, more to HR.”"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Chain of Responsibility State Mediator Strategy","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Chain of Responsibility<\/p><p>Escalating handlers, each either approves or passes it on.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-behavioral.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-behavioral-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-behavioral","t":"“A stock price changes and all open dashboards must update.”","sub":"Behavioral Patterns · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“A stock price changes and all open dashboards must update.”"],"w":"Behavioral Patterns Observer Command Facade Memento","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Observer<\/p><p>One subject, many subscribers reacting to changes.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-behavioral.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-lld-0","k":"post","s":"sd-lld","t":"Requirements → nouns → diagram → patterns → code","sub":"LLD Problems · The method","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Requirements → nouns → diagram → patterns → code","explain Requirements → nouns → diagram → patterns → code","what is Requirements → nouns → diagram → patterns → code"],"w":"LLD Problems The method","a":"<ul><li><b>Clarify<\/b> (3–5 min). Actors, the 3–5 core use cases, what's out of scope. Write them down.<\/li><li><b>Nouns become classes.<\/b> Underline nouns in the use cases. Fixed categories (vehicle type, spot size) become <code>enum<\/code>s.<\/li><li><b>Draw the relationships.<\/b> Who owns whom (◆ with multiplicity), who just knows whom (→). This is the class diagram from the Start page.<\/li><li><b>Verbs become methods<\/b>, on the class that owns the data: <code>lot.park(vehicle)<\/code>, <code>spot.tryOccupy(v)<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Ask what will change<\/b> and put it behind an interface: pricing → Strategy, modes → State, reactions → Observer, checks → Chain.<\/li><li><b>Concurrency and edge cases.<\/b> Two users grabbing the same spot or seat? Full lot? Payment failure?<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-lld.html#s-requirements-nouns-diagram-patterns-code"]]},{"i":"po-sd-lld-1","k":"post","s":"sd-lld","t":"Think it through before drawing","sub":"LLD Problems · Parking Lot","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Think it through before drawing","explain Think it through before drawing","what is Think it through before drawing"],"w":"LLD Problems Parking Lot","a":"<p>Now step through the diagram. Watch the requirement text: blue words are nouns (classes), amber words are verbs (methods).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-lld.html#s-think-it-through-before-drawing"]]},{"i":"po-sd-lld-2","k":"post","s":"sd-lld","t":"Watch it run","sub":"LLD Problems · Parking Lot","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Watch it run","explain Watch it run","what is Watch it run"],"w":"LLD Problems Parking Lot","a":"<p>The diagram is the design; this is the design in motion: cars arriving, spots being claimed, a fee computed on exit.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-lld.html#s-watch-it-run"]]},{"i":"po-sd-lld-6","k":"post","s":"sd-lld","t":"Check-then-act is not atomic","sub":"LLD Problems · Booking & races","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Check-then-act is not atomic","explain Check-then-act is not atomic","what is Check-then-act is not atomic"],"w":"LLD Problems Booking & races","a":"<p><code>if (seat.isFree()) seat.book(user);<\/code> reads, decides, then writes. Two threads can both read “free” before either writes, and both book. It's the same bug as the lazy Singleton race on the Creational page. Fixes, from one JVM to many servers: a lock; an atomic compare-and-set; a <b>conditional UPDATE<\/b> in the database.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-lld.html#s-check-then-act-is-not-atomic"]]},{"i":"po-sd-lld-7","k":"post","s":"sd-lld","t":"The problems, as cards","sub":"LLD Problems · Booking & races","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The problems, as cards","explain The problems, as cards","what is The problems, as cards"],"w":"LLD Problems Booking & races","a":"<p>Check the diagram, name the patterns, then reveal the Java.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-lld.html#s-the-problems-as-cards"]]},{"i":"po-sd-lld-10","k":"post","s":"sd-lld","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"LLD Problems · Pick a pattern","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"LLD Problems Pick a pattern","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-lld.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-lld-0","k":"post","s":"sd-lld","t":"Patterns go where the requirements will change.","sub":"LLD Problems · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Patterns go where the requirements will change."],"w":"LLD Problems insight","a":"<p>After you list classes, ask: “What will product ask to change next quarter?” Pricing rules, spot allocation, notification channels, payment methods. Each answer is an interface with a Strategy behind it. Things that won’t change (a ticket has an entry time) stay plain fields.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">stable → fields · likely to change → interface · lifecycle → State<\/pre>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-lld.html#in-variation"]]},{"i":"in-sd-lld-1","k":"post","s":"sd-lld","t":"Pick the referee by where the state lives.","sub":"LLD Problems · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Pick the referee by where the state lives."],"w":"LLD Problems insight","a":"<p>If the shared state is in one JVM’s memory, Java gives you the referee: <code>synchronized<\/code>, <code>ReentrantLock<\/code>, atomics, concurrent maps. If several servers share it, the referee must be shared too: the database (row locks, conditional updates, unique constraints), or Redis (<code>SET key value NX PX 30000<\/code> for a lock with expiry).<\/p><p>In LLD rounds, write the JVM version and then <b>say<\/b> how it changes with many servers. That one sentence is often what separates SDE-1 from SDE-2 answers.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-lld.html#in-onejvm"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-lld-q-l1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-lld","t":"Requirement: “Charges are hourly on weekdays and flat on weekends, and the business may add event pricing.” How do you model charging?","sub":"LLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Requirement: “Charges are hourly on weekdays and flat on weekends, and the business may add event pricing.” How do you model charging?"],"w":"LLD Problems if-else in ParkingLot.exit() A FeePolicy interface with one class per rule A field per rule on Ticket Subclass ParkingLot per rule","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> A FeePolicy interface with one class per rule<\/p><p>A changing business rule is a Strategy; ParkingLot just calls fees.fee(ticket, now).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-lld.html#q-l1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-lld-q-l2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-lld","t":"Two cars reach the last free spot at the same moment via different gates (same JVM). What prevents both parking there?","sub":"LLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Two cars reach the last free spot at the same moment via different gates (same JVM). What prevents both parking there?"],"w":"LLD Problems Nothing; rare enough A synchronized tryOccupy (check + set in one step) Making Spot immutable A larger thread pool","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> A synchronized tryOccupy (check + set in one step)<\/p><p>Claim the spot atomically; the loser moves on to the next spot.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-lld.html#q-l2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-lld-q-l3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-lld","t":"Elevator at floor 6 going up with requests at 8 and 2. Someone presses 4. Next stop under SCAN?","sub":"LLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Elevator at floor 6 going up with requests at 8 and 2. Someone presses 4. Next stop under SCAN?"],"w":"LLD Problems 4 8 2 Back to 6","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 8<\/p><p>Keep serving requests ahead in the current direction (8) before reversing for 4 and 2.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-lld.html#q-l3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-lld-q-l4","k":"quiz","s":"sd-lld","t":"Ten app servers book seats. Which actually prevents double booking?","sub":"LLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Ten app servers book seats. Which actually prevents double booking?"],"w":"LLD Problems synchronized on the book() method ConcurrentHashMap.putIfAbsent UPDATE … WHERE status = 'FREE' and check rows affected volatile seat status","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> UPDATE … WHERE status = 'FREE' and check rows affected<\/p><p>Only shared storage can arbitrate between servers; JVM-level tools only protect one process.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-lld.html#q-l4"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-lld-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-lld","t":"“A notification should be sent by email, SMS and push when a booking is confirmed; more channels later.”","sub":"LLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“A notification should be sent by email, SMS and push when a booking is confirmed; more channels later.”"],"w":"LLD Problems Observer (or Strategy per channel) Singleton Builder Prototype","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Observer (or Strategy per channel)<\/p><p>Listeners react to the booking-confirmed event; each channel is a pluggable implementation.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-lld.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-lld-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-lld","t":"“A traffic signal cycles Red → Green → Yellow and pedestrians can request a crossing only on Red.”","sub":"LLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“A traffic signal cycles Red → Green → Yellow and pedestrians can request a crossing only on Red.”"],"w":"LLD Problems Strategy State Composite Facade","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> State<\/p><p>Allowed actions depend on the current light; transitions are owned by the states.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-lld.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-lld-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-lld","t":"“Support multiple elevator dispatch algorithms and switch at night.”","sub":"LLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Support multiple elevator dispatch algorithms and switch at night.”"],"w":"LLD Problems State Strategy Observer Command","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Strategy<\/p><p>Interchangeable algorithms chosen by configuration or time: Strategy.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-lld.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-basics-0","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"The framework (say the steps out loud)","sub":"System Design Basics · The interview","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The framework (say the steps out loud)","explain The framework (say the steps out loud)","what is The framework (say the steps out loud)"],"w":"System Design Basics The interview","a":"<ul><li><b>Requirements<\/b> (5 min). Functional: the 3–4 features that matter. Non-functional: scale, latency, availability vs consistency, read/write ratio.<\/li><li><b>Estimates<\/b> (3 min). Users → requests per second → storage per year → bandwidth. Rough is fine; the point is to find the bottleneck.<\/li><li><b>API<\/b> (3 min). The 3–5 endpoints with inputs and outputs.<\/li><li><b>Data model<\/b> (5 min). Entities, keys, and which database fits the access pattern.<\/li><li><b>High-level design<\/b> (10 min). Client → load balancer → services → cache → database → queues. Walk one request through it.<\/li><li><b>Deep dive<\/b> (15 min). The one or two hard parts: hot keys, fan-out, consistency, failure. This is where SDE-2 is judged.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#s-the-framework-say-the-steps-out-loud"]]},{"i":"po-sd-basics-1","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"Back-of-the-envelope, the only numbers you need","sub":"System Design Basics · The interview","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Back-of-the-envelope, the only numbers you need","explain Back-of-the-envelope, the only numbers you need","what is Back-of-the-envelope, the only numbers you need"],"w":"System Design Basics The interview","a":"<p><b>1 day ≈ 86,400 s ≈ 10<sup>5<\/sup> s.<\/b> So 1 million requests a day ≈ 12 per second. 100M DAU × 10 requests = 10<sup>9<\/sup>/day ≈ 12k QPS average; peak is 2–3× that.<\/p><p><b>Storage<\/b>: records per day × size × 365 × years. <b>Latency ladder<\/b>: memory ~100 ns, SSD read ~100 µs, same-datacenter round trip ~0.5 ms, disk seek ~10 ms, cross-continent ~150 ms. One Postgres box handles a few thousand simple writes per second; Redis ~100k ops/s.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#s-back-of-the-envelope-the-only-numbers-you-need"]]},{"i":"po-sd-basics-2","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"Vertical vs horizontal","sub":"System Design Basics · Scale","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Vertical vs horizontal","explain Vertical vs horizontal","what is Vertical vs horizontal"],"w":"System Design Basics Scale","a":"<p>The key move is making app servers <b>stateless<\/b>: sessions go to Redis or a signed token, files go to object storage. Then any server can handle any request, and you add servers freely. Step through the first lab tab.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#s-vertical-vs-horizontal"]]},{"i":"po-sd-basics-3","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"L4 vs L7, and the API gateway","sub":"System Design Basics · Scale","cat":"System design concepts","q":["L4 vs L7, and the API gateway","explain L4 vs L7, and the API gateway","what is L4 vs L7, and the API gateway"],"w":"System Design Basics Scale","a":"<p>An <b>L4<\/b> load balancer routes by IP and port: fast, protocol-agnostic. An <b>L7<\/b> load balancer reads HTTP: it can route <code>/api/orders<\/code> to one pool and <code>/images<\/code> to another, terminate TLS, and set cookies.<\/p><p>An <b>API gateway<\/b> is an L7 front door for microservices that also does the cross-cutting chores: authentication, rate limiting, request routing, aggregation, logging. Think of it as a Facade plus a Chain of Responsibility at the network edge.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#s-l4-vs-l7-and-the-api-gateway"]]},{"i":"po-sd-basics-4","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"API design checklist","sub":"System Design Basics · Talking to clients","cat":"System design concepts","q":["API design checklist","explain API design checklist","what is API design checklist"],"w":"System Design Basics Talking to clients","a":"<ul><li><b>Resources as nouns<\/b>: <code>POST /orders<\/code>, <code>GET /orders/{id}<\/code>. Verbs are HTTP methods.<\/li><li><b>Status codes that mean something<\/b>: 201 created, 400 bad input, 401/403 auth, 404, 409 conflict, 429 too many requests, 5xx server.<\/li><li><b>Cursor pagination<\/b> for feeds: <code>?after=&lt;last_id&gt;&amp;limit=20<\/code>. Offset pagination gets slow and skips items when new rows arrive.<\/li><li><b>Idempotency keys<\/b> on writes that cost money: the client sends <code>Idempotency-Key<\/code>; retries return the stored result.<\/li><li><b>Versioning<\/b>: <code>/v1/<\/code> in the path or a header; never break existing clients.<\/li><\/ul><p>REST for public CRUD APIs, gRPC for fast internal service-to-service calls, GraphQL when many clients need different shapes of the same data.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#s-api-design-checklist"]]},{"i":"po-sd-basics-5","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"Real-time: four ways to get news to the client","sub":"System Design Basics · Talking to clients","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Real-time: four ways to get news to the client","explain Real-time: four ways to get news to the client","what is Real-time: four ways to get news to the client"],"w":"System Design Basics Talking to clients","a":"<p><b>Short polling<\/b>: ask every few seconds. Simple, wasteful. <b>Long polling<\/b>: ask, and the server holds the request until there's news. <b>Server-Sent Events<\/b>: one long HTTP response the server keeps writing to; one-way, auto-reconnect. <b>WebSockets<\/b>: a full-duplex connection; both sides send any time. Chat and multiplayer need WebSockets; live scores and notifications are fine with SSE.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#s-real-time-four-ways-to-get-news-to-the-client"]]},{"i":"po-sd-basics-6","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"The building blocks, as cards","sub":"System Design Basics · Talking to clients","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The building blocks, as cards","explain The building blocks, as cards","what is The building blocks, as cards"],"w":"System Design Basics Talking to clients","a":"<p>Say when you'd use it first, then reveal the code.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#s-the-building-blocks-as-cards"]]},{"i":"po-sd-basics-9","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"System Design Basics · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"System Design Basics The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-basics-0","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"Estimates exist to find the bottleneck, not to be precise.","sub":"System Design Basics · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Estimates exist to find the bottleneck, not to be precise."],"w":"System Design Basics insight","a":"<p>Nobody checks your arithmetic to the digit. They check whether the numbers changed your design. “4k reads/s of tiny records with a 100:1 ratio” → cache, and one database is fine for writes. “50k writes/s” → one primary can’t take it, so shard or use a write-optimised store.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">numbers → bottleneck → the component you add<\/pre>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#in-bottleneck"]]},{"i":"in-sd-basics-1","k":"post","s":"sd-basics","t":"Pick real-time tech by direction and frequency.","sub":"System Design Basics · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Pick real-time tech by direction and frequency."],"w":"System Design Basics insight","a":"<p>Ask two questions. <b>Who talks?<\/b> Only the server → SSE (or polling). Both → WebSocket. <b>How often?<\/b> Every few minutes → polling is cheaper than keeping a million sockets open. Many times per second → a persistent connection.<\/p><p>Mobile apps add push notifications (APNs/FCM) for when the app is closed: no socket survives that.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-basics.html#in-connections"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-basics-q-b1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-basics","t":"50M daily active users, each making 20 requests a day. Roughly what average QPS?","sub":"System Design Basics · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["50M daily active users, each making 20 requests a day. Roughly what average QPS?"],"w":"System Design Basics ~1k ~10k ~100k ~1M","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> ~10k<\/p><p>10<sup>9<\/sup> requests / 10<sup>5<\/sup> s ≈ 10k QPS average (plan for ~30k peak).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-basics.html#q-b1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-basics-q-b2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-basics","t":"You add a second app server behind a load balancer, and users randomly get logged out. Most likely cause?","sub":"System Design Basics · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["You add a second app server behind a load balancer, and users randomly get logged out. Most likely cause?"],"w":"System Design Basics DNS caching Sessions stored in each server’s memory The LB is L4 Too few threads","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Sessions stored in each server’s memory<\/p><p>Stateful servers: the other server doesn’t know the session. Move sessions to Redis or use signed tokens.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-basics.html#q-b2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-basics-q-b3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-basics","t":"A feed uses ?page=500&size=20 (OFFSET 10000). Two problems?","sub":"System Design Basics · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["A feed uses ?page=500&size=20 (OFFSET 10000). Two problems?"],"w":"System Design Basics None Slow deep pages; items skip or repeat when new rows arrive Only slow Only duplicates","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Slow deep pages; items skip or repeat when new rows arrive<\/p><p>OFFSET still reads and discards 10,000 rows, and inserts shift every page. Use a cursor on an indexed key.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-basics.html#q-b3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-basics-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-basics","t":"“Live cricket score updates on a web page, millions of viewers, no input from viewers.”","sub":"System Design Basics · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Live cricket score updates on a web page, millions of viewers, no input from viewers.”"],"w":"System Design Basics WebSockets Server-Sent Events (or CDN-cached polling) Long polling only gRPC","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Server-Sent Events (or CDN-cached polling)<\/p><p>One-way server→client stream; SSE is simpler than WebSockets. Polling a CDN-cached endpoint every few seconds also works at that scale.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-basics.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-basics-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-basics","t":"“Client retries POST /payments after a timeout; the first one actually succeeded.”","sub":"System Design Basics · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Client retries POST /payments after a timeout; the first one actually succeeded.”"],"w":"System Design Basics Return 409 Idempotency key Disable retries Use PUT","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Idempotency key<\/p><p>The server recognises the key and returns the original result instead of charging again.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-basics.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-basics-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-basics","t":"“Internal service calls are slow JSON over HTTP/1.1, hundreds of thousands per second.”","sub":"System Design Basics · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Internal service calls are slow JSON over HTTP/1.1, hundreds of thousands per second.”"],"w":"System Design Basics GraphQL gRPC SOAP WebSockets","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> gRPC<\/p><p>Binary protobuf over HTTP/2 with multiplexing: built for internal high-throughput RPC.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-basics.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-databases-0","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"The database zoo, by access pattern","sub":"Databases · Choosing","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The database zoo, by access pattern","explain The database zoo, by access pattern","what is The database zoo, by access pattern"],"w":"Databases Choosing","a":"<ul><li><b>Relational<\/b> (Postgres, MySQL): tables, joins, transactions. Default for money, orders, users, anything with relationships and invariants.<\/li><li><b>Key-value<\/b> (Redis, DynamoDB): get/put by key, extremely fast. Sessions, caches, URL codes, carts.<\/li><li><b>Document<\/b> (MongoDB): JSON documents with flexible fields, fetched whole. Product catalogs, profiles, CMS content.<\/li><li><b>Wide-column<\/b> (Cassandra, HBase): huge write throughput, rows partitioned by key and sorted by a clustering column. Chat messages, time series, activity feeds.<\/li><li><b>Search<\/b> (Elasticsearch): inverted index for full-text and filters. Product search, logs.<\/li><li><b>Graph<\/b> (Neo4j): relationships are the data. Friend-of-friend, recommendations, fraud rings.<\/li><li><b>Object storage<\/b> (S3) + <b>time-series<\/b> (InfluxDB) + <b>column analytics<\/b> (BigQuery, ClickHouse) for blobs, metrics and reporting.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#s-the-database-zoo-by-access-pattern"]]},{"i":"po-sd-databases-1","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"SQL vs NoSQL, the honest version","sub":"Databases · Choosing","cat":"System design concepts","q":["SQL vs NoSQL, the honest version","explain SQL vs NoSQL, the honest version","what is SQL vs NoSQL, the honest version"],"w":"Databases Choosing","a":"<p>Say it as a trade-off: \"Orders need transactions and ad-hoc reporting, so Postgres. Chat messages are append-heavy, read by conversation and time, and huge, so Cassandra.\"<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#s-sql-vs-nosql-the-honest-version"]]},{"i":"po-sd-databases-2","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"An index is a sorted copy you can binary-search","sub":"Databases · Indexes & ACID","cat":"System design concepts","q":["An index is a sorted copy you can binary-search","explain An index is a sorted copy you can binary-search","what is An index is a sorted copy you can binary-search"],"w":"Databases Indexes & ACID","a":"<p>Without an index, <code>WHERE email = ?<\/code> reads every row: O(n). A <b>B-tree index<\/b> keeps the column sorted in a shallow tree of pages, so the lookup touches 3–4 pages even for a billion rows: O(log n). The cost: every insert and update must also update the index, and it takes disk.<\/p><p>A <b>composite index<\/b> on <code>(user_id, created_at)<\/code> is sorted by user first, then time. It serves <code>WHERE user_id = ?<\/code> and <code>WHERE user_id = ? ORDER BY created_at<\/code>, but not <code>WHERE created_at &gt; ?<\/code> alone: the <b>leftmost-prefix rule<\/b>.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#s-an-index-is-a-sorted-copy-you-can-binary-search"]]},{"i":"po-sd-databases-3","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"ACID, and the isolation levels behind the I","sub":"Databases · Indexes & ACID","cat":"System design concepts","q":["ACID, and the isolation levels behind the I","explain ACID, and the isolation levels behind the I","what is ACID, and the isolation levels behind the I"],"w":"Databases Indexes & ACID","a":"<p><b>Atomic<\/b>: all or nothing. <b>Consistent<\/b>: constraints hold before and after. <b>Isolated<\/b>: concurrent transactions don't see each other's half-done work. <b>Durable<\/b>: committed means it survives a crash (write-ahead log).<\/p><p>Isolation has levels, each preventing one more anomaly: <b>Read Uncommitted<\/b> (allows dirty reads) → <b>Read Committed<\/b> (Postgres default; allows non-repeatable reads) → <b>Repeatable Read<\/b> (MySQL InnoDB default; mostly stops phantoms there) → <b>Serializable<\/b> (as if one at a time; slowest). The <b>lost update<\/b> in the lab's last tab happens at Read Committed if you read-then-write in application code.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#s-acid-and-the-isolation-levels-behind-the-i"]]},{"i":"po-sd-databases-4","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"Replication: copies of the same data","sub":"Databases · Scaling data","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Replication: copies of the same data","explain Replication: copies of the same data","what is Replication: copies of the same data"],"w":"Databases Scaling data","a":"<p>A <b>primary<\/b> takes writes and streams changes to <b>replicas<\/b> that serve reads. Async replication is fast but replicas lag, so a user may not see their own just-written comment: route that user's reads to the primary for a few seconds (<b>read-your-writes<\/b>). Sync replication waits for a replica to confirm: no data loss on failover, slower writes. Leaderless systems (Cassandra, Dynamo) write to N nodes and use quorums: W + R &gt; N means reads see the latest write.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#s-replication-copies-of-the-same-data"]]},{"i":"po-sd-databases-5","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"Sharding: split the data across machines","sub":"Databases · Scaling data","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Sharding: split the data across machines","explain Sharding: split the data across machines","what is Sharding: split the data across machines"],"w":"Databases Scaling data","a":"<p>When one primary can't take the writes or hold the data, partition rows by a <b>shard key<\/b>. A good key has high cardinality, spreads load evenly, and keeps the data a query needs on one shard (<code>user_id<\/code> for a user's orders). Bad keys create <b>hot shards<\/b> (country = India) or force scatter-gather queries across every shard.<\/p><p>How do you map keys to shards? <code>hash(key) % N<\/code> is simple, but changing N moves almost every key. <b>Consistent hashing<\/b> puts servers and keys on a ring; a key belongs to the next server clockwise, so adding a server only moves the keys in one arc. Watch both in the lab.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#s-sharding-split-the-data-across-machines"]]},{"i":"po-sd-databases-6","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"Database questions interviewers actually ask","sub":"Databases · Scaling data","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Database questions interviewers actually ask","explain Database questions interviewers actually ask","what is Database questions interviewers actually ask"],"w":"Databases Scaling data","a":"<p>Answer out loud first, then reveal the SQL or Java.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#s-database-questions-interviewers-actually-ask"]]},{"i":"po-sd-databases-9","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Databases · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Databases The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-databases-0","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"Each isolation level stops one more anomaly.","sub":"Databases · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Each isolation level stops one more anomaly."],"w":"Databases insight","a":"<p><b>Dirty read<\/b>: you see uncommitted data that later rolls back. <b>Non-repeatable read<\/b>: you read a row twice in one transaction and get different values. <b>Phantom<\/b>: you re-run a range query and new rows appear. <b>Lost update<\/b>: two read-modify-writes overwrite each other.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">Read Uncommitted &lt; Read Committed &lt; Repeatable Read &lt; Serializable<\/pre><p>Higher levels are safer and slower (more locking or more aborted transactions). Most apps run at Read Committed and fix specific races with atomic updates or <code>FOR UPDATE<\/code>.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#in-isolation"]]},{"i":"in-sd-databases-1","k":"post","s":"sd-databases","t":"Sharding has three questions, not one.","sub":"Databases · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Sharding has three questions, not one."],"w":"Databases insight","a":"<p><b>Which key?<\/b> (user_id, not country). <b>Which mapping?<\/b> (consistent hashing or a range/lookup table). <b>What breaks?<\/b> Cross-shard joins and transactions, global unique IDs (use Snowflake-style IDs), and rebalancing hot shards.<\/p><p>Say all three in an interview, plus “I’d shard only after replicas, caching and vertical scaling stop being enough.”<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-databases.html#in-vnodes"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-databases-q-q1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-databases","t":"An e-commerce checkout must reserve stock, create an order and record a payment, all or nothing. First choice of store?","sub":"Databases · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["An e-commerce checkout must reserve stock, create an order and record a payment, all or nothing. First choice of store?"],"w":"Databases Cassandra Relational DB with a transaction Redis Elasticsearch","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Relational DB with a transaction<\/p><p>Multi-row atomicity and constraints are what relational databases are built for.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-databases.html#q-q1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-databases-q-q2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-databases","t":"Index on (user_id, created_at) . Which query can NOT use it efficiently?","sub":"Databases · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Index on (user_id, created_at) . Which query can NOT use it efficiently?"],"w":"Databases WHERE user_id = 5 WHERE user_id = 5 AND created_at > X WHERE created_at > X WHERE user_id = 5 ORDER BY created_at","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> WHERE created_at &gt; X<\/p><p>No user_id condition, so the leftmost column is missing; times are sorted only within each user.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-databases.html#q-q2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-databases-q-q3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-databases","t":"Two transactions run SELECT stock (both see 1) then UPDATE SET stock = 0 and both create an order. What went wrong?","sub":"Databases · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Two transactions run SELECT stock (both see 1) then UPDATE SET stock = 0 and both create an order. What went wrong?"],"w":"Databases Dirty read Lost update / check-then-act race Phantom read Deadlock","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Lost update / check-then-act race<\/p><p>Both checked a stale value. Use <code>UPDATE … SET stock = stock - 1 WHERE stock &gt; 0<\/code> and check rows affected.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-databases.html#q-q3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-databases-q-q4","k":"quiz","s":"sd-databases","t":"You cache with hash(key) % 10 across 10 Redis nodes and add an 11th. Roughly what fraction of keys now map to a different node?","sub":"Databases · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["You cache with hash(key) % 10 across 10 Redis nodes and add an 11th. Roughly what fraction of keys now map to a different node?"],"w":"Databases ~1/11 ~1/2 ~90% 0","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> ~90%<\/p><p>With mod N almost every key changes node; consistent hashing would move about 1/11.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-databases.html#q-q4"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-databases-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-databases","t":"“Store billions of chat messages, always read as the latest 50 in a conversation.”","sub":"Databases · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Store billions of chat messages, always read as the latest 50 in a conversation.”"],"w":"Databases MySQL single table Cassandra partitioned by conversation_id, clustered by time Neo4j Elasticsearch","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Cassandra partitioned by conversation_id, clustered by time<\/p><p>Append-heavy, one access pattern, partition + clustering key fits exactly.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-databases.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-databases-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-databases","t":"“Users sometimes don’t see the comment they just posted.”","sub":"Databases · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Users sometimes don’t see the comment they just posted.”"],"w":"Databases Bigger primary Read-your-writes: read from primary after a write Serializable isolation Drop the replicas","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Read-your-writes: read from primary after a write<\/p><p>Replica lag; route that user’s reads to the primary briefly (or wait for the replica to catch up).<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-databases.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-databases-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-databases","t":"“The orders page runs 101 queries for 100 orders.”","sub":"Databases · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“The orders page runs 101 queries for 100 orders.”"],"w":"Databases Add an index N+1 problem: JOIN FETCH or batch IN query Add a replica Increase pool size","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> N+1 problem: JOIN FETCH or batch IN query<\/p><p>One query for orders plus one per order for items. Fetch them together.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-databases.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-0","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Caching strategies","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Speed","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Caching strategies","explain Caching strategies","what is Caching strategies"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Speed","a":"<ul><li><b>Cache-aside<\/b> (lazy): read cache → on miss read DB → put in cache with TTL. On write, update DB then <b>delete<\/b> the key. The default.<\/li><li><b>Read-through<\/b>: the cache library loads from the DB itself on a miss. Same idea, less app code.<\/li><li><b>Write-through<\/b>: write cache and DB together. Always fresh, slower writes.<\/li><li><b>Write-back<\/b> (write-behind): write cache now, DB later in batches. Fast, can lose data if the cache dies.<\/li><\/ul><p>Eviction when full: <b>LRU<\/b> (least recently used) is the default; LFU for skewed popularity; TTL for freshness.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-caching-strategies"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-1","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"CDN and object storage","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Speed","cat":"System design concepts","q":["CDN and object storage","explain CDN and object storage","what is CDN and object storage"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Speed","a":"<p>Large files (images, video, uploads) go to <b>object storage<\/b> (S3/GCS), never the database; the DB stores only the key. Clients upload directly to S3 with a <b>pre-signed URL<\/b>, so your servers never proxy gigabytes. A <b>CDN<\/b> caches those files (and static JS/CSS) at edge locations near users: lower latency, and most traffic never reaches you.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-cdn-and-object-storage"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-2","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Queues and Kafka","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Async & consistency","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Queues and Kafka","explain Queues and Kafka","what is Queues and Kafka"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Async & consistency","a":"<p>A <b>message queue<\/b> (SQS, RabbitMQ) hands each job to one worker and deletes it when acknowledged: great for background work. <b>Kafka<\/b> is a durable, replayable <b>log<\/b>: a topic is split into <b>partitions<\/b>, each an ordered append-only file. Messages with the same key go to the same partition, so they stay in order. A <b>consumer group<\/b> splits partitions among its members; each group gets every message, so email and analytics can read the same topic independently.<\/p><p>Delivery is usually <b>at-least-once<\/b>: after a crash a message may come again. So consumers must be <b>idempotent<\/b> (dedupe by message ID).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-queues-and-kafka"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-3","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Sagas, outbox, CQRS","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Async & consistency","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Sagas, outbox, CQRS","explain Sagas, outbox, CQRS","what is Sagas, outbox, CQRS"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Async & consistency","a":"<p>Each microservice owns its database, so \"place order\" can't be one ACID transaction across Order, Payment and Inventory. A <b>saga<\/b> runs local transactions step by step; if one fails, earlier steps are undone by <b>compensating<\/b> actions (refund, release stock). <b>Orchestrated<\/b> sagas have a coordinator; <b>choreographed<\/b> ones react to each other's events.<\/p><p>The <b>outbox pattern<\/b> fixes the dual-write bug (DB commit succeeds, Kafka publish fails): write the event into an <code>outbox<\/code> table in the <i>same<\/i> transaction, and a relay publishes it. <b>CQRS<\/b> separates the write model from read models that are built from events and shaped for queries.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-sagas-outbox-cqrs"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-4","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"CAP and consistency models","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Async & consistency","cat":"System design concepts","q":["CAP and consistency models","explain CAP and consistency models","what is CAP and consistency models"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Async & consistency","a":"<p>During a network <b>partition<\/b>, a distributed store must choose: refuse some requests to stay <b>consistent<\/b> (CP: banking, inventory counts, leader election) or answer with possibly stale data to stay <b>available<\/b> (AP: likes, feeds, shopping carts). Without a partition, the trade-off is latency vs consistency (PACELC).<\/p><p>Models from strong to weak: <b>linearizable<\/b> (reads see the latest write), <b>read-your-writes<\/b>, <b>monotonic reads<\/b>, <b>eventual<\/b> (replicas converge if writes stop).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-cap-and-consistency-models"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-5","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Resilience: timeouts, retries, breakers, bulkheads","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Staying up","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Resilience: timeouts, retries, breakers, bulkheads","explain Resilience: timeouts, retries, breakers, bulkheads","what is Resilience: timeouts, retries, breakers, bulkheads"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Staying up","a":"<p>Every network call gets a <b>timeout<\/b>. Retry only idempotent calls, with <b>exponential backoff and jitter<\/b> so clients don't retry in lockstep. A <b>circuit breaker<\/b> stops calling a failing dependency for a while (OPEN), then lets a few trial calls through (HALF-OPEN) before closing again. <b>Bulkheads<\/b> give each dependency its own thread pool so one slow service can't starve the rest. Always have a <b>fallback<\/b>: cached data, a default, or a friendly error.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-resilience-timeouts-retries-breakers-bulkheads"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-6","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"The algorithm toolbox","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Staying up","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The algorithm toolbox","explain The algorithm toolbox","what is The algorithm toolbox"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Staying up","a":"<ul><li><b>Bloom filter<\/b>: \"definitely not present\" or \"probably present\" in a few bits per item. Skip DB lookups for usernames, URLs already crawled.<\/li><li><b>Geohash / quadtree<\/b>: encode lat/long into a string whose prefix means \"same area\". Nearby search becomes a prefix query.<\/li><li><b>HyperLogLog<\/b>: count unique visitors in ~12 KB with ~1% error.<\/li><li><b>Count-min sketch<\/b>: approximate top-K / heavy hitters (trending hashtags).<\/li><li><b>Token bucket / sliding window<\/b>: rate limiting (see the HLD topic).<\/li><li><b>Snowflake IDs<\/b>: 64-bit, time-sortable unique IDs without a central counter.<\/li><\/ul>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-the-algorithm-toolbox"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-7","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Availability, observability, security","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Staying up","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Availability, observability, security","explain Availability, observability, security","what is Availability, observability, security"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Staying up","a":"<p><b>Nines<\/b>: 99.9% allows ~8.8 hours of downtime a year; 99.99% allows ~53 minutes. Serial dependencies multiply (two 99.9% services in series ≈ 99.8%); redundancy in parallel raises it. <b>Observability<\/b>: metrics (RED: rate, errors, duration), structured logs with a trace ID, distributed tracing, and alerts on SLOs.<\/p><p><b>Auth<\/b>: authentication (who you are) vs authorisation (what you may do). <b>JWT<\/b>: a signed token any service can verify without a DB call; keep it short-lived with a refresh token. <b>OAuth 2.0<\/b>: delegated access (\"log in with Google\"). Hash passwords with bcrypt/argon2, use TLS everywhere, and rate-limit login.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-availability-observability-security"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-8","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"The building blocks, as cards","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Staying up","cat":"System design concepts","q":["The building blocks, as cards","explain The building blocks, as cards","what is The building blocks, as cards"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Staying up","a":"<p>Say when you'd use it first, then reveal the code.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-the-building-blocks-as-cards"]]},{"i":"po-sd-distributed-11","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-distributed-0","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Every distributed bug is a dual write in disguise.","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Every distributed bug is a dual write in disguise."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience insight","a":"<p>“Update the DB and the cache”, “save the order and publish the event”, “charge the card and mark it paid”. Two systems, no shared transaction, so a crash between the writes leaves them disagreeing.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">make one write the source of truth · derive the other from it · make the derivation retry-safe<\/pre><p>Cache: write DB, delete cache. Events: outbox. Payments: idempotency key + reconciliation.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#in-dualwrite"]]},{"i":"in-sd-distributed-1","k":"post","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Availability multiplies in series and adds in parallel.","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Availability multiplies in series and adds in parallel."],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience insight","a":"<p>A request that needs the gateway, the service and the database, each 99.9%, is up only 0.999³ ≈ 99.7% of the time. Two redundant replicas of a 99% component, either of which works, give 1 − 0.01² = 99.99%.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">serial: A × B · parallel: 1 − (1 − A)(1 − B)<\/pre><p>So the way to add nines is redundancy and removing hard dependencies from the critical path (async, caches, fallbacks).<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-distributed.html#in-nines"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-distributed-q-c1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-distributed","t":"After updating a product, you write the new value into the cache (not delete). Two updates race. What can go wrong?","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["After updating a product, you write the new value into the cache (not delete). Two updates race. What can go wrong?"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Nothing The cache can end up holding the older value indefinitely (until TTL) The DB gets the old value Cache crashes","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> The cache can end up holding the older value indefinitely (until TTL)<\/p><p>Writer A updates DB, writer B updates DB and cache, then A’s slower cache write lands last. Deleting the key avoids it.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-distributed.html#q-c1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-distributed-q-c2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Topic with 4 partitions, consumer group with 6 consumers. How many consumers receive messages?","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Topic with 4 partitions, consumer group with 6 consumers. How many consumers receive messages?"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience 6 4 1 2","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 4<\/p><p>Each partition goes to one consumer in a group; 2 sit idle. Partitions cap parallelism.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-distributed.html#q-c2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-distributed-q-c3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Which feature should choose CP over AP?","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Which feature should choose CP over AP?"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Like counts Social feed Bank account balance Online presence dots","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Bank account balance<\/p><p>Showing or acting on a stale balance can lose money; better to refuse during a partition.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-distributed.html#q-c3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-distributed-q-c4","k":"quiz","s":"sd-distributed","t":"Three services in series, each 99.9% available. Approximate end-to-end availability?","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Three services in series, each 99.9% available. Approximate end-to-end availability?"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience 99.9% 99.7% 99.99% 97%","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> 99.7%<\/p><p>0.999³ ≈ 0.997.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-distributed.html#q-c4"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-distributed-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-distributed","t":"“After a deploy, every server retries a failing dependency every 100 ms and it never recovers.”","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“After a deploy, every server retries a failing dependency every 100 ms and it never recovers.”"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience More retries Exponential backoff with jitter + circuit breaker Bigger thread pool Disable timeouts","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Exponential backoff with jitter + circuit breaker<\/p><p>Synchronised retries hammer the dependency; backoff spreads them, a breaker stops them.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-distributed.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-distributed-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-distributed","t":"“Check whether a username is taken across 1B users without hitting the DB for most checks.”","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Check whether a username is taken across 1B users without hitting the DB for most checks.”"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience HyperLogLog Bloom filter Geohash Count-min sketch","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Bloom filter<\/p><p>A Bloom “no” is certain, so most new usernames skip the DB; only “maybe” goes to the DB.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-distributed.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-distributed-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-distributed","t":"“Order saved in Postgres but the OrderPlaced event sometimes never reaches Kafka.”","sub":"Caching, Queues & Resilience · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Order saved in Postgres but the OrderPlaced event sometimes never reaches Kafka.”"],"w":"Caching, Queues & Resilience Saga Outbox pattern CQRS Bigger Kafka cluster","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Outbox pattern<\/p><p>Dual-write gap; write the event to an outbox table in the same transaction.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-distributed.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"po-sd-hld-0","k":"post","s":"sd-hld","t":"Every design is the same six moves","sub":"HLD Problems · The framework","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Every design is the same six moves","explain Every design is the same six moves","what is Every design is the same six moves"],"w":"HLD Problems The framework","a":"<ul><li><b>Requirements<\/b>: 3–4 core features + scale, latency, consistency.<\/li><li><b>Numbers<\/b>: reads/s and writes/s separately, storage, bandwidth. They decide the design.<\/li><li><b>API<\/b>: 3–5 endpoints.<\/li><li><b>Data<\/b>: entities, keys, and the store that fits the access pattern.<\/li><li><b>Architecture<\/b>: walk one write and one read through LB → services → cache → DB → queue. Add a box only to fix a problem you can name.<\/li><li><b>Deep dive<\/b>: the one thing that makes <i>this<\/i> system hard.<\/li><\/ul><p>In every lab below, the tag on each step tells you which move you're on: 📋 requirements, 🔢 numbers, 🔌 API, 🗄️ data, 🔧 architecture, 🔍 deep dive, 📈 scale.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-hld.html#s-every-design-is-the-same-six-moves"]]},{"i":"po-sd-hld-13","k":"post","s":"sd-hld","t":"All eleven, as cards","sub":"HLD Problems · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["All eleven, as cards","explain All eleven, as cards","what is All eleven, as cards"],"w":"HLD Problems The decoder","a":"<p>Say the numbers and the hard part out loud, then open the thinking and code.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-hld.html#s-all-eleven-as-cards"]]},{"i":"po-sd-hld-15","k":"post","s":"sd-hld","t":"Reflex drill","sub":"HLD Problems · The decoder","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Reflex drill","explain Reflex drill","what is Reflex drill"],"w":"HLD Problems The decoder","a":"<p>Every reflex in this topic, shuffled. Ten in a row finishes the chapter.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-hld.html#s-reflex-drill"]]},{"i":"in-sd-hld-0","k":"post","s":"sd-hld","t":"Every HLD question has one hard part. Find it early.","sub":"HLD Problems · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Every HLD question has one hard part. Find it early."],"w":"HLD Problems insight","a":"<p>The boxes (LB, services, cache, DB, queue) look the same for every system. Interviewers grade the deep dive: what makes <i>this<\/i> system hard.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">URL shortener → unique codes · Feed → fan-out · Chat → routing to sockets · Uber → moving locations · Booking → double booking · Dropbox → chunk sync · Rate limiter → atomic counters<\/pre><p>Name the hard part out loud after the estimates and steer the remaining time there.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-hld.html#in-hardpart"]]},{"i":"in-sd-hld-1","k":"post","s":"sd-hld","t":"Eleven problems, about five recurring moves.","sub":"HLD Problems · Look deeper","cat":"System design concepts","q":["Eleven problems, about five recurring moves."],"w":"HLD Problems insight","a":"<p>Read-heavy → cache. Slow or spiky work → queue. Big files → object storage + CDN. Location → geo index. Contended resource → atomic conditional update or lock. Real-time → WebSocket + presence. Money → idempotency + outbox.<\/p><p>Once you can name the move for a requirement, every new problem is a recombination.<\/p>","L":[["Read it in the app","sd-hld.html#in-patterns"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-hld-q-h1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-hld","t":"Why not generate the short code as the first 7 characters of MD5(longUrl)?","sub":"HLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Why not generate the short code as the first 7 characters of MD5(longUrl)?"],"w":"HLD Problems MD5 is too slow Collisions: different URLs can share a prefix, so you need checks and retries It’s too long It can’t be Base62","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Collisions: different URLs can share a prefix, so you need checks and retries<\/p><p>Truncated hashes collide; counter-based IDs encoded in Base62 are unique by construction.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-hld.html#q-h1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-hld-q-h2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-hld","t":"A user follows 300 normal people and 2 celebrities with 50M followers each. How does their home feed get the celebrity posts in a hybrid design?","sub":"HLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["A user follows 300 normal people and 2 celebrities with 50M followers each. How does their home feed get the celebrity posts in a hybrid design?"],"w":"HLD Problems Pushed into their timeline at post time Pulled and merged when they open the feed Not shown Emailed","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Pulled and merged when they open the feed<\/p><p>Celebrity posts aren’t fanned out on write; they’re fetched and merged at read time.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-hld.html#q-h2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-hld-q-h3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-hld","t":"In a chat system, the recipient is connected to a different WebSocket server than the sender. How does the message reach them?","sub":"HLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["In a chat system, the recipient is connected to a different WebSocket server than the sender. How does the message reach them?"],"w":"HLD Problems Broadcast to all servers Look up the recipient’s server in a presence registry and route via pub/sub The client polls Store it and wait","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Look up the recipient’s server in a presence registry and route via pub/sub<\/p><p>Presence (user → server) plus pub/sub between connection servers; push notification if offline.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-hld.html#q-h3"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-hld-q-h4","k":"quiz","s":"sd-hld","t":"Where do drivers’ live locations (updated every 4 s) belong in Uber’s design?","sub":"HLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["Where do drivers’ live locations (updated every 4 s) belong in Uber’s design?"],"w":"HLD Problems Main Postgres table updated every 4 s An in-memory geo index (Redis GEO / quadtree) S3 Kafka only","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> An in-memory geo index (Redis GEO / quadtree)<\/p><p>High-frequency, short-lived data needing proximity queries → in-memory geo index.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-hld.html#q-h4"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-hld-q-d1","k":"quiz","s":"sd-hld","t":"“Design Dropbox.”","sub":"HLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Design Dropbox.”"],"w":"HLD Problems Fan-out Chunking, dedupe by hash and conflict-aware sync Geo index Rate limiting","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Chunking, dedupe by hash and conflict-aware sync<\/p><p>The interesting part is moving only changed chunks and reconciling versions across devices.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-hld.html#q-d1"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-hld-q-d2","k":"quiz","s":"sd-hld","t":"“Design an API rate limiter for 10 gateway servers.”","sub":"HLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Design an API rate limiter for 10 gateway servers.”"],"w":"HLD Problems Local counters per server Token bucket state in Redis, updated atomically A SQL table per request Client-side throttling only","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Token bucket state in Redis, updated atomically<\/p><p>Shared, atomic state across servers; Lua makes read-refill-decrement one operation.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-hld.html#q-d2"]]},{"i":"qz-sd-hld-q-d3","k":"quiz","s":"sd-hld","t":"“Design Airbnb booking.”","sub":"HLD Problems · Quiz","cat":"Quiz questions","q":["“Design Airbnb booking.”"],"w":"HLD Problems Video transcoding Preventing double booking across date ranges Typeahead Fan-out","a":"<p><b>Answer:<\/b> Preventing double booking across date ranges<\/p><p>Atomic hold of every night in the range, then confirm after payment.<\/p>","L":[["Try the quiz in the app","sd-hld.html#q-d3"]]},{"i":"c-ci-approach","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How to approach a coding interview question","cat":"Coding interview","q":["How to approach a coding interview question","how do I approach a coding interview problem","what to do first in a coding interview","coding interview framework","steps to solve a problem in an interview","UMPIRE method","how to start solving a leetcode question in interview"],"w":"framework steps clarify brute force optimise test","a":"<p>Use the same six steps every time, out loud:<\/p><ol><li><b>Clarify<\/b> (2 min): restate the problem, ask about input size, duplicates, negatives, empty input, sorted or not, what to return.<\/li><li><b>Examples<\/b>: walk one normal example and one edge case by hand.<\/li><li><b>Brute force<\/b>: say it and its Big-O in one sentence. It proves you understand the problem.<\/li><li><b>Optimise<\/b>: ask \"what is the inner loop searching for?\" and match it to a pattern (hash map, two pointers, window, heap, BFS, DP…). State the new Big-O before coding.<\/li><li><b>Code<\/b>: clean names, small helpers, talk while typing.<\/li><li><b>Test<\/b>: trace your example line by line, then the edge cases. Fix bugs calmly; say what you found.<\/li><\/ol><p>The interviewer is grading communication and problem solving as much as the final code.<\/p>","r":["c-ci-stuck","c-ci-edge","c-ci-constraints"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-ci-stuck","k":"cur","s":"","t":"What to do when you are stuck in an interview","cat":"Coding interview","q":["What to do when you are stuck in an interview","I am stuck in an interview what should I do","how to get unstuck","stuck on a problem","blank mind in coding interview","how to ask for hints"],"w":"stuck hint silent","a":"<p>Being stuck is normal; going silent is the real mistake.<\/p><ul><li><b>Say what you know<\/b>: \"The brute force is O(n²) because for each element I scan the rest.\"<\/li><li><b>Name the bottleneck<\/b>: what is the inner loop looking for? Can a set, map, sort, heap or prefix sum answer that faster?<\/li><li><b>Try a smaller or sorted version<\/b> of the input and solve it by hand. Write down what your hand did; that is often the algorithm.<\/li><li><b>Run the pattern checklist<\/b>: sorted → two pointers / binary search; contiguous → sliding window or prefix sums; \"all combinations\" → backtracking; \"min/max ways\" with overlapping choices → DP; graph/grid → BFS/DFS; \"k largest\" → heap.<\/li><li><b>Ask a focused question<\/b>: \"Is it OK if I use O(n) extra memory?\" Interviewers give hints to people who show their thinking.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-ci-approach","c-ci-pattern"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-ci-edge","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Edge cases to check in coding interviews","cat":"Coding interview","q":["Edge cases to check in coding interviews","what edge cases should I test","edge case checklist","common edge cases","test cases to try before submitting"],"w":"edge cases testing","a":"<p>Run through this list before you say \"done\":<\/p><ul><li>Empty input, a single element, two elements<\/li><li>All elements equal; duplicates<\/li><li>Negative numbers, zero, very large values (overflow in Java/C++: use <code>long<\/code>)<\/li><li>Already sorted / reverse sorted<\/li><li>Target not present; answer at the very first or very last index<\/li><li>Strings: empty string, one character, spaces, upper vs lower case, Unicode if relevant<\/li><li>Linked lists: <code>null<\/code> head, one node, cycle<\/li><li>Trees: <code>null<\/code> root, skewed tree (recursion depth)<\/li><li>Graphs: disconnected components, self-loops, cycles<\/li><li>Off-by-one: <code>&lt;<\/code> vs <code>&lt;=<\/code> in loops and binary search bounds<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-ci-approach"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-ci-constraints","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Reading constraints to pick the complexity","cat":"Coding interview","q":["Reading constraints to pick the complexity","what complexity do I need for n = 10^5","how to use constraints to choose algorithm","n up to 10^5 which complexity","input size to time complexity","constraints to big o"],"w":"constraints big o n 10^5 time limit","a":"<p>About 10⁸ simple operations run in roughly a second, so the input size tells you the target:<\/p><div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>n (max)<\/th><th>Target complexity<\/th><th>Typical approach<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>≤ 10<\/td><td>O(n!) or O(2ⁿ·n)<\/td><td>permutations, brute-force backtracking<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>≤ 20<\/td><td>O(2ⁿ)<\/td><td>subsets, bitmask DP<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>≤ 500<\/td><td>O(n³)<\/td><td>interval DP, Floyd–Warshall<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>≤ 5,000<\/td><td>O(n²)<\/td><td>2D DP, all pairs<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>≤ 10⁵ – 10⁶<\/td><td>O(n log n) or O(n)<\/td><td>sorting, heap, two pointers, hashing<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>≥ 10⁸ or \"huge\"<\/td><td>O(log n) or O(1)<\/td><td>binary search, math<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div><p>Mention this out loud: \"n is up to 10⁵, so O(n²) is too slow; I need O(n log n).\" It shows you think before coding.<\/p>","r":["c-bo-basics"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-ci-pattern","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How to recognise which pattern a problem needs","cat":"Coding interview","q":["How to recognise which pattern a problem needs","which pattern should I use","which pattern to use for a coding problem","which algorithm pattern fits this problem","how to identify the pattern","pattern recognition leetcode","how do I know which algorithm to use","cheat sheet of dsa patterns","problem keywords to pattern"],"w":"pattern decoder keywords clue","a":"<p>Match the clue in the problem to the tool:<\/p><div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Clue in the problem<\/th><th>Reach for<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>\"seen before\", \"pair sums to target\", \"count / group\"<\/td><td>Hash map or set<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Sorted array, pair/triplet, palindrome, in-place<\/td><td>Two pointers<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Contiguous subarray/substring, \"longest/shortest with …\"<\/td><td>Sliding window<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Subarray sum equals k, range sums<\/td><td>Prefix sums + map<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\"Next greater/smaller\", matching brackets<\/td><td>(Monotonic) stack<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Sorted / monotonic yes-no, \"minimum possible maximum\"<\/td><td>Binary search (on the answer)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>k largest / smallest, merge k lists, running median<\/td><td>Heap<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>All combinations / permutations / subsets<\/td><td>Backtracking<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Prefix search, autocomplete, word dictionary<\/td><td>Trie<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Grid, islands, shortest steps, dependencies<\/td><td>BFS / DFS / topological sort<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\"Number of ways\", \"min cost\", overlapping choices<\/td><td>Dynamic programming<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Intervals, scheduling, \"max number of non-overlapping\"<\/td><td>Sort + greedy<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\"Appears once\", flags, subsets as numbers<\/td><td>Bit manipulation<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div><p>The <b>Practice → Mixed drill<\/b> in this app trains exactly this: reflexes shuffled across topics.<\/p>","r":["c-ci-stuck"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-ci-practice","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How to practise DSA effectively","cat":"Coding interview","q":["How to practise DSA effectively","how many leetcode problems should I solve","how to practice dsa","how to prepare for coding interviews in 4 weeks","best way to study leetcode","how to remember solutions","spaced repetition for dsa","how long to prepare for sde 2 interview"],"w":"practice plan how many problems weeks","a":"<p>Quality beats count. Around 150–250 well-understood problems covering every pattern is enough for most SDE-2 loops.<\/p><ul><li><b>Learn by pattern, not by number.<\/b> Do a topic, then mix topics so you practise <i>recognising<\/i> the pattern cold.<\/li><li><b>Time-box<\/b>: 20–25 minutes trying, then read the approach, then re-solve it from scratch the next day without looking.<\/li><li><b>Spaced repetition<\/b>: revisit a problem after 1, 3 and 7 days. The app's Study plan schedules these reviews for you.<\/li><li><b>Say it out loud<\/b> (that's what Pip is for): approach, complexity, edge cases, before typing.<\/li><li><b>Mock interviews<\/b>: at least a few timed, with someone watching or a timer and camera.<\/li><li><b>Track mistakes<\/b>: write one line per bug (\"forgot empty input\", \"off-by-one in binary search\"). Those lines become your checklist.<\/li><\/ul><p>Short on time? Use the <b>Crunch list<\/b>: the minimum set of questions that covers every pattern.<\/p>","r":["c-app-plan","c-app-crunch"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-ci-communicate","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How to communicate while coding","cat":"Coding interview","q":["How to communicate while coding","how to think out loud in interview","what to say while coding","communication in coding interview","should I talk while coding"],"w":"talk think aloud explain","a":"<p>Narrate decisions, not keystrokes.<\/p><ul><li>Before coding: \"I'll use a map from value to index so each lookup is O(1). Overall O(n) time, O(n) space.\"<\/li><li>While coding: name what each block does (\"this loop expands the window; this one shrinks it while it's invalid\").<\/li><li>When you choose between options, say the trade-off (\"sorting is O(n log n) but needs no extra memory; the hash map is O(n) with O(n) memory\").<\/li><li>After coding: trace one example, then edge cases, then restate the complexity.<\/li><li>If the interviewer hints, acknowledge it and build on it; don't defend the old idea.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-ci-approach"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-ci-sde2","k":"cur","s":"","t":"What is expected at SDE-2 level","cat":"Coding interview","q":["What is expected at SDE-2 level","what does sde 2 interview expect","sde2 vs sde1 difference","what level of system design for sde 2","sde-2 interview rounds","what do google microsoft look for in sde2"],"w":"sde2 level expectations rounds google microsoft walmart","a":"<p>The exact loop differs by company and changes over time, so confirm with your recruiter. Typically for SDE-2 / L4-level roles:<\/p><ul><li><b>Coding (2–3 rounds)<\/b>: medium problems, sometimes a hard follow-up. You're expected to reach an optimal solution with clean, bug-free code and clear complexity analysis, with few hints.<\/li><li><b>System design (often 1 round)<\/b>: design a real system end to end. More depth than SDE-1: trade-offs, data model, scaling, failure handling. Some companies use a low-level design (classes, patterns) round instead or as well.<\/li><li><b>Behavioral<\/b>: ownership of a feature end to end, handling ambiguity, influencing without authority, mentoring juniors, learning from failures.<\/li><\/ul><p>Your Salesforce experience counts: pick 4–5 projects and prepare STAR stories with measurable impact.<\/p>","r":["c-bh-star","c-sd-framework"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-ci-language","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Which language to use in coding interviews","cat":"Coding interview","q":["Which language to use in coding interviews","python or java for coding interviews","which language is best for leetcode","should I use java in interviews","c++ vs python interview"],"w":"language python java cpp","a":"<p>Use the language you can write fastest with the fewest bugs. Most companies allow any mainstream language.<\/p><ul><li><b>Python<\/b>: shortest code; built-ins like <code>collections.Counter<\/code>, <code>deque<\/code>, <code>heapq<\/code>, <code>bisect<\/code>, <code>functools.lru_cache<\/code>. Watch recursion depth (default limit ~1000).<\/li><li><b>Java<\/b>: verbose but explicit types help catch mistakes; know <code>HashMap<\/code>, <code>ArrayDeque<\/code>, <code>PriorityQueue<\/code>, <code>TreeMap<\/code>, and watch <code>int<\/code> overflow.<\/li><li><b>C++<\/b>: fastest at runtime; know <code>unordered_map<\/code>, <code>vector<\/code>, <code>priority_queue<\/code> (max-heap by default), <code>set<\/code>/<code>map<\/code> (ordered).<\/li><\/ul><p>Every solution in this app switches between Python, Java and C++ with the tabs above the code.<\/p>","r":["c-app-lang"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bo-basics","k":"cur","s":"","t":"What is Big-O notation","cat":"Complexity","q":["What is Big-O notation","what is big o","explain time complexity","what is space complexity","how to calculate time complexity","big o for beginners"],"w":"big o time space complexity growth","a":"<p>Big-O describes how the running time (or memory) <b>grows<\/b> as the input size n grows, ignoring constants.<\/p><ul><li>Count the work in the worst case. One loop over n → O(n). A loop inside a loop over n → O(n²).<\/li><li>Drop constants and smaller terms: O(2n + 10) → O(n); O(n² + n) → O(n²).<\/li><li>Halving the problem each step → O(log n). Sorting → O(n log n).<\/li><li><b>Space<\/b> counts extra memory you allocate <i>plus<\/i> the recursion stack.<\/li><\/ul><p>Common order, fastest to slowest: O(1) &lt; O(log n) &lt; O(n) &lt; O(n log n) &lt; O(n²) &lt; O(2ⁿ) &lt; O(n!).<\/p><p>Every topic in this app ends with a \"Counting the cost\" chapter with labs to practise this.<\/p>","r":["c-bo-recursion","c-bo-amortized","c-ci-constraints"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bo-recursion","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Time complexity of recursion","cat":"Complexity","q":["Time complexity of recursion","how to find complexity of recursive function","recursion time complexity","master theorem","complexity of fibonacci recursion","recursion tree method"],"w":"recursion master theorem recursion tree memoization","a":"<p>Draw the recursion tree: <b>total work = (number of calls) × (work per call)<\/b>.<\/p><ul><li>Each call makes <b>b<\/b> calls, depth <b>d<\/b> → about bᵈ calls. Naive Fibonacci: 2 calls, depth n → O(2ⁿ).<\/li><li>With memoization, each distinct state is computed once → O(number of states × work per state). Fibonacci → O(n).<\/li><li>Divide and conquer (merge sort): 2 halves, O(n) merge per level, log n levels → O(n log n).<\/li><li>Master theorem for T(n) = a·T(n/b) + O(nᵈ): compare a with bᵈ. Equal → O(nᵈ log n); a smaller → O(nᵈ); a bigger → O(n^(log_b a)).<\/li><li><b>Space<\/b> = maximum recursion depth (stack frames) + memo table.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-bo-basics"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bo-amortized","k":"cur","s":"","t":"What is amortized complexity","cat":"Complexity","q":["What is amortized complexity","what is amortized time","why is append O(1) amortized","amortized analysis dynamic array","monotonic stack why O(n)"],"w":"amortized dynamic array doubling","a":"<p>Amortized cost is the <b>average cost per operation over a long sequence<\/b>, even if a single operation is occasionally expensive.<\/p><ul><li>A dynamic array (Python list, Java <code>ArrayList<\/code>) doubles its capacity when full. That copy is O(n), but it happens so rarely that n appends cost O(n) total → O(1) amortized each.<\/li><li>A monotonic stack pops inside a loop, but each element is pushed once and popped at most once → O(n) total.<\/li><li>A sliding window's left pointer only moves forward → O(n) total even though it's a loop inside a loop.<\/li><\/ul><p>Interview phrasing: \"Each element enters and leaves once, so it's O(n) amortized.\"<\/p>","r":["c-bo-basics"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bo-ds","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Complexity of common data structure operations","cat":"Complexity","q":["Complexity of common data structure operations","time complexity of hashmap operations","complexity of heap push pop","big o of data structures cheat sheet","treemap complexity","complexity of python list operations","arraylist vs linkedlist complexity","what is the complexity of priority queue"],"w":"cheat sheet operations hashmap heap treemap array list deque","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Structure<\/th><th>Access / search<\/th><th>Insert<\/th><th>Delete<\/th><th>Notes<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Array / ArrayList / list<\/td><td>O(1) by index, O(n) search<\/td><td>O(1) amortized at end, O(n) middle<\/td><td>O(n) middle<\/td><td><code>pop()<\/code> at end O(1); <code>pop(0)<\/code> O(n)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Linked list<\/td><td>O(n)<\/td><td>O(1) at a known node<\/td><td>O(1) at a known node<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hash map / set<\/td><td>O(1) average<\/td><td>O(1) average<\/td><td>O(1) average<\/td><td>O(n) worst with bad hashing<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Balanced BST (TreeMap, std::map)<\/td><td>O(log n)<\/td><td>O(log n)<\/td><td>O(log n)<\/td><td>sorted order, floor/ceiling<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Heap (PriorityQueue, heapq)<\/td><td>O(1) peek min<\/td><td>O(log n)<\/td><td>O(log n) pop<\/td><td>building from n items: O(n)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Deque (ArrayDeque, deque)<\/td><td>O(1) both ends<\/td><td>O(1)<\/td><td>O(1)<\/td><td>use for BFS queues<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Trie<\/td><td>O(L) for a word of length L<\/td><td>O(L)<\/td><td>O(L)<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div><p>Sorting: O(n log n). Binary search: O(log n).<\/p>","r":["c-bo-sorting"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bo-sorting","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Sorting algorithms and their complexity","cat":"Complexity","q":["Sorting algorithms and their complexity","which sorting algorithm is fastest","merge sort vs quick sort","complexity of sorting algorithms","is quicksort stable","what sort does python use","what sort does java use","counting sort when"],"w":"sorting merge quick heap timsort stable","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm<\/th><th>Best<\/th><th>Average<\/th><th>Worst<\/th><th>Space<\/th><th>Stable<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Merge sort<\/td><td>n log n<\/td><td>n log n<\/td><td>n log n<\/td><td>O(n)<\/td><td>yes<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Quick sort<\/td><td>n log n<\/td><td>n log n<\/td><td>n² (bad pivots)<\/td><td>O(log n)<\/td><td>no<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Heap sort<\/td><td>n log n<\/td><td>n log n<\/td><td>n log n<\/td><td>O(1)<\/td><td>no<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Insertion sort<\/td><td>n<\/td><td>n²<\/td><td>n²<\/td><td>O(1)<\/td><td>yes<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Counting / bucket sort<\/td><td>n + k<\/td><td>n + k<\/td><td>n + k<\/td><td>O(n + k)<\/td><td>yes<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div><ul><li>Python's <code>sorted<\/code> and Java's <code>Collections.sort<\/code> (objects) use <b>Timsort<\/b>: stable, O(n log n), very fast on partly sorted data. Java sorts primitive arrays with dual-pivot quicksort.<\/li><li>Comparison sorts can't beat O(n log n); counting/radix sort can when values are small integers.<\/li><li>In interviews, call the built-in sort and say \"O(n log n)\". Write merge or quick sort only if asked.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-algo-mergesort","c-algo-quickselect"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bo-hash-worst","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Why is hash map lookup O(1) and when is it O(n)","cat":"Complexity","q":["Why is hash map lookup O(1) and when is it O(n)","why hashmap is O(1)","hash map worst case","hash collisions explained","how does hashing work"],"w":"hash collision load factor rehash","a":"<p>A hash map turns the key into an array index with a hash function, so a lookup jumps straight to one bucket: <b>O(1) on average<\/b>.<\/p><ul><li><b>Collisions<\/b>: two keys can land in the same bucket. Buckets hold a small list (Java 8+ turns long chains into red-black trees, so worst case per bucket becomes O(log n)).<\/li><li><b>Worst case O(n)<\/b>: a terrible hash function puts everything in one bucket.<\/li><li><b>Resizing<\/b>: when the load factor (Java default 0.75) is exceeded, the table doubles and rehashes: O(n) once, O(1) amortized.<\/li><li><b>Hidden cost<\/b>: hashing a key of length k is O(k), so hashing strings isn't free.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-hashmap","c-bo-ds"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-cmp-bfs-dfs","k":"cur","s":"","t":"BFS vs DFS","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["BFS vs DFS","difference between bfs and dfs","when to use bfs vs dfs","bfs or dfs for shortest path","breadth first vs depth first"],"w":"bfs dfs queue stack shortest path traversal graph","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th><\/th><th>BFS<\/th><th>DFS<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Uses<\/td><td>queue<\/td><td>stack / recursion<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Explores<\/td><td>level by level<\/td><td>one path to the end, then backtracks<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Shortest path (unweighted)<\/td><td><b>yes<\/b>, first arrival is shortest<\/td><td>no<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Memory<\/td><td>O(width)<\/td><td>O(depth)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Good for<\/td><td>min steps, nearest X, level order<\/td><td>all paths, cycle detection, topological sort, connected components, backtracking<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div><p>Both visit every node and edge once: <b>O(V + E)<\/b>. Mark nodes visited when you <b>enqueue<\/b> them in BFS, or you'll add duplicates.<\/p><p>Watch \"The BFS wave\" film in the Graphs topic to see it.<\/p>","r":["c-algo-dijkstra","c-algo-topo"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-cmp-dp-greedy","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Dynamic programming vs greedy","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Dynamic programming vs greedy","dp vs greedy","when does greedy work","how to know if a problem is dp or greedy","why greedy fails coin change"],"w":"dp greedy exchange argument counterexample","a":"<ul><li><b>Greedy<\/b> makes the locally best choice and never looks back. It's correct only when you can prove a local choice is always safe (an exchange argument), e.g. interval scheduling by earliest end time, jump game, gas station.<\/li><li><b>DP<\/b> tries every choice but reuses answers to overlapping subproblems. Use it when a greedy choice can be wrong.<\/li><\/ul><p>Quick test: try to break greedy with a tiny counterexample. Coins {1, 3, 4}, amount 6: greedy takes 4+1+1 (3 coins) but 3+3 is 2. Counterexample found → use DP.<\/p><p>If greedy works, it's usually simpler and faster (often O(n log n) for the sort).<\/p>","r":["c-cmp-memo-tab"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-cmp-memo-tab","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Memoization vs tabulation (top-down vs bottom-up DP)","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Memoization vs tabulation (top-down vs bottom-up DP)","top down vs bottom up dp","memoization vs tabulation","should I use memo or table","how to convert recursion to dp"],"w":"memoization tabulation top down bottom up dp","a":"<ul><li><b>Top-down (memoization)<\/b>: write the recursion, cache results (<code>@lru_cache<\/code> in Python, a <code>HashMap<\/code> or array in Java). Easiest to get right from the recurrence; only computes reachable states. Costs recursion stack depth.<\/li><li><b>Bottom-up (tabulation)<\/b>: fill a table from the base cases up in a loop. No recursion limit, often faster, and makes <b>space optimisation<\/b> obvious (keep only the last row or last two values).<\/li><\/ul><p>Interview path: define the state in words (\"dp[i] = best answer for the first i items\"), write the recurrence, code it top-down, then offer to convert it bottom-up and shrink the space.<\/p>","r":["c-cmp-dp-greedy"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-cmp-array-ll","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Array vs linked list","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Array vs linked list","array vs linked list","when to use linked list","arraylist vs linkedlist java","why arrays are faster than linked lists"],"w":"array linked list cache locality","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th><\/th><th>Array / ArrayList<\/th><th>Linked list<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Access by index<\/td><td>O(1)<\/td><td>O(n)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Insert/delete at a known spot<\/td><td>O(n) (shifting)<\/td><td>O(1)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Memory<\/td><td>contiguous, cache-friendly<\/td><td>extra pointer per node, scattered<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div><p>In practice arrays win almost always because CPUs love contiguous memory. Linked lists shine when you hold node references and splice often: <b>LRU cache<\/b> (hash map + doubly linked list), queues, and many interview problems that test pointer handling.<\/p>","r":["c-algo-lru"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-cmp-heap-bst","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Heap vs balanced BST (TreeMap)","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Heap vs balanced BST (TreeMap)","heap vs bst","priority queue vs treemap","when to use treeset","heap or sorted set"],"w":"heap bst treemap treeset priority queue ordered","a":"<ul><li><b>Heap<\/b>: O(1) peek of min (or max), O(log n) push/pop. Can't search or remove arbitrary items fast. Perfect for top-k, scheduling, Dijkstra, merge k lists.<\/li><li><b>Balanced BST<\/b> (Java <code>TreeMap<\/code>/<code>TreeSet<\/code>, C++ <code>std::set<\/code>/<code>map<\/code>): everything sorted, O(log n) insert/delete/search, plus <code>floor<\/code>, <code>ceiling<\/code>, range queries, and both min and max. Use for sliding-window max with deletions, calendar booking, ordered events.<\/li><\/ul><p>Python has no built-in balanced BST; use <code>heapq<\/code> with lazy deletion or <code>bisect<\/code> on a sorted list (O(n) inserts) or the <code>sortedcontainers<\/code> library if allowed.<\/p>","r":["c-bo-ds"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-cmp-window-pointers","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Sliding window vs two pointers","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Sliding window vs two pointers","difference between sliding window and two pointers","is sliding window two pointers","when to use sliding window"],"w":"sliding window two pointers contiguous sorted","a":"<p>Sliding window <b>is<\/b> a two-pointer technique, specialised for <b>contiguous<\/b> ranges:<\/p><ul><li><b>Sliding window<\/b>: both pointers move the same direction; the range between them is the answer candidate (longest substring, minimum window, max sum of size k). Needs the \"valid/invalid\" property to change monotonically as the window grows or shrinks.<\/li><li><b>Two pointers (converging)<\/b>: start at both ends of a <b>sorted<\/b> array and move inward (pair sum, container with most water, palindromes).<\/li><li><b>Fast/slow pointers<\/b>: same direction at different speeds (cycle detection, middle of a list, remove duplicates in place).<\/li><\/ul><p>Negative numbers often break the sliding window for sums; use prefix sums + a hash map instead.<\/p>","r":["c-algo-prefix"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-cmp-recursion-iter","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Recursion vs iteration","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Recursion vs iteration","recursion vs iteration","should I use recursion or a loop","stack overflow recursion depth","convert recursion to iteration"],"w":"recursion iteration stack overflow depth","a":"<ul><li>Recursion is natural for trees, backtracking, divide-and-conquer and top-down DP. Each call costs a stack frame: depth 10⁵ will overflow Python's default limit (~1000) and can overflow Java's stack.<\/li><li>Iteration (with an explicit stack or queue) avoids depth limits and is often a bit faster.<\/li><li>Any recursion can be made iterative with your own stack. Tail recursion isn't optimised in Python or Java.<\/li><\/ul><p>In interviews: recursion is fine when depth is O(log n) or the problem is tree-shaped; mention the depth risk for skewed trees or long linked lists.<\/p>","r":["c-bo-recursion"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-dijkstra","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Dijkstra's algorithm","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Dijkstra's algorithm","what is dijkstra","shortest path weighted graph","how does dijkstra work","dijkstra complexity","dijkstra vs bellman ford","network delay time approach"],"w":"dijkstra shortest path weighted heap bellman ford","a":"<p>Shortest paths from one source in a graph with <b>non-negative<\/b> weights.<\/p><ol><li><code>dist[src] = 0<\/code>, everything else ∞. Push <code>(0, src)<\/code> on a min-heap.<\/li><li>Pop the closest node. If its distance is stale (larger than <code>dist<\/code>), skip it.<\/li><li>Relax each edge: if <code>d + w &lt; dist[v]<\/code>, update and push <code>(dist[v], v)<\/code>.<\/li><\/ol><pre class=\"ap-pre\">dist = {src: 0}; pq = [(0, src)]\nwhile pq:\n    d, u = heapq.heappop(pq)\n    if d &gt; dist.get(u, inf): continue\n    for v, w in graph[u]:\n        if d + w &lt; dist.get(v, inf):\n            dist[v] = d + w\n            heapq.heappush(pq, (d + w, v))<\/pre><p>Complexity <b>O((V + E) log V)<\/b>. Negative edges → Bellman–Ford O(V·E). All unit weights → plain BFS. Weights only 0/1 → 0-1 BFS with a deque.<\/p>","r":["c-cmp-bfs-dfs"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-unionfind","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)","what is union find","disjoint set union explained","when to use union find","number of connected components union find","path compression union by rank"],"w":"union find dsu disjoint set components kruskal","a":"<p>Tracks which items are in the same group, with near-O(1) merges and lookups.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">parent = list(range(n)); rank = [0] * n\ndef find(x):\n    while parent[x] != x:\n        parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]   # path compression\n        x = parent[x]\n    return x\ndef union(a, b):\n    ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n    if ra == rb: return False          # already connected → cycle\n    if rank[ra] &lt; rank[rb]: ra, rb = rb, ra\n    parent[rb] = ra\n    if rank[ra] == rank[rb]: rank[ra] += 1\n    return True<\/pre><p>Use it for: connected components that grow over time, detecting a cycle in an undirected graph, Kruskal's MST, \"accounts merge\", redundant connection. Each operation is O(α(n)), effectively constant.<\/p>","r":["c-algo-mst"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-topo","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Topological sort","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Topological sort","what is topological sort","course schedule approach","kahn algorithm","dependency ordering","detect cycle in directed graph"],"w":"topological sort kahn indegree dag cycle course schedule","a":"<p>Orders a <b>directed acyclic graph<\/b> so every edge goes from earlier to later (tasks before the tasks that depend on them).<\/p><p><b>Kahn's algorithm (BFS)<\/b>: count in-degrees; queue all nodes with in-degree 0; pop one, append it to the order, decrement its neighbours' in-degrees and enqueue any that hit 0. If the order has fewer than V nodes, there's a <b>cycle<\/b>.<\/p><p><b>DFS version<\/b>: post-order DFS, then reverse; a node seen \"in progress\" again means a cycle.<\/p><p>O(V + E). Classic problems: Course Schedule I/II, Alien Dictionary, build order.<\/p>","r":["c-cmp-bfs-dfs"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-mst","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Minimum spanning tree (Kruskal and Prim)","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Minimum spanning tree (Kruskal and Prim)","what is a minimum spanning tree","kruskal vs prim","min cost to connect all points"],"w":"mst kruskal prim spanning tree","a":"<p>A minimum spanning tree connects every node with the smallest total edge weight and no cycles.<\/p><ul><li><b>Kruskal<\/b>: sort edges by weight; add each edge if it joins two different components (Union-Find). O(E log E).<\/li><li><b>Prim<\/b>: grow from one node, always adding the cheapest edge leaving the tree (min-heap). O(E log V); good for dense graphs.<\/li><\/ul><p>Classic: \"Min Cost to Connect All Points\" (LeetCode 1584).<\/p>","r":["c-algo-unionfind"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-mergesort","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Merge sort","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Merge sort","how does merge sort work","merge sort code","count inversions merge sort"],"w":"merge sort divide conquer stable","a":"<p>Divide and conquer: split in half, sort each half recursively, merge the two sorted halves with two pointers.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">def merge_sort(a):\n    if len(a) &lt;= 1: return a\n    m = len(a) // 2\n    L, R = merge_sort(a[:m]), merge_sort(a[m:])\n    out, i, j = [], 0, 0\n    while i &lt; len(L) and j &lt; len(R):\n        if L[i] &lt;= R[j]: out.append(L[i]); i += 1\n        else: out.append(R[j]); j += 1\n    return out + L[i:] + R[j:]<\/pre><p>O(n log n) always, O(n) extra space, stable. The merge step is also how you count inversions and sort linked lists (LeetCode 148).<\/p>","r":["c-bo-sorting"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-quickselect","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Quickselect for the k-th largest element","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Quickselect for the k-th largest element","quickselect","kth largest element without sorting","kth largest heap vs quickselect"],"w":"quickselect kth largest partition","a":"<p>Find the k-th largest without fully sorting:<\/p><ul><li><b>Heap<\/b>: keep a min-heap of size k; its root is the answer. O(n log k), simple and safe. Good for streams.<\/li><li><b>Quickselect<\/b>: partition around a pivot like quicksort, then recurse into only the side that holds index n−k. <b>O(n) average<\/b>, O(n²) worst; a random pivot makes the worst case unlikely.<\/li><li><b>Sort<\/b>: O(n log n), the easy fallback.<\/li><\/ul><p>Say all three and their trade-offs; then code the heap unless they ask for O(n).<\/p>","r":["c-cmp-heap-bst"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-kadane","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Kadane's algorithm (maximum subarray)","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Kadane's algorithm (maximum subarray)","kadane algorithm","maximum subarray sum","max sum contiguous subarray"],"w":"kadane maximum subarray dp","a":"<p>Keep the best sum of a subarray <b>ending here<\/b>: either extend the previous one or start fresh.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">best = cur = nums[0]\nfor x in nums[1:]:\n    cur = max(x, cur + x)\n    best = max(best, cur)<\/pre><p>O(n) time, O(1) space. It's a 1-D DP where <code>cur<\/code> is <code>dp[i]<\/code>. Variants: maximum product subarray (track both max and min), circular subarray (total − minimum subarray).<\/p>","r":["c-cmp-memo-tab"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-prefix","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Prefix sums","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Prefix sums","what are prefix sums","subarray sum equals k","range sum query","prefix sum with hashmap"],"w":"prefix sum subarray sum k range query","a":"<p><code>pre[i]<\/code> = sum of the first i elements, with <code>pre[0] = 0<\/code>. Then <code>sum(l..r) = pre[r+1] − pre[l]<\/code> in O(1).<\/p><p>For \"count subarrays with sum k\" (negatives allowed), walk once with a running sum and a map of how often each prefix sum has appeared, seeded <code>{0: 1}<\/code>:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">count, run, seen = 0, 0, {0: 1}\nfor x in nums:\n    run += x\n    count += seen.get(run - k, 0)\n    seen[run] = seen.get(run, 0) + 1<\/pre><p>O(n). 2-D prefix sums answer rectangle sums in O(1) after O(rows × cols) setup.<\/p>","r":["c-cmp-window-pointers"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-lru","k":"cur","s":"","t":"LRU cache design","cat":"Algorithms","q":["LRU cache design","how to implement lru cache","lru cache data structure","lru cache o(1)","linkedhashmap lru"],"w":"lru cache linked hash map doubly linked list","a":"<p>O(1) <code>get<\/code> and <code>put<\/code> with a <b>hash map + doubly linked list<\/b>:<\/p><ul><li>The map goes from key → list node.<\/li><li>The list keeps order of use: most recent at the head, least recent at the tail.<\/li><li><code>get<\/code>: look up the node, move it to the head.<\/li><li><code>put<\/code>: update or insert at the head; if over capacity, remove the tail node and its map entry.<\/li><\/ul><p>Shortcuts: Python <code>OrderedDict<\/code> (<code>move_to_end<\/code>, <code>popitem(last=False)<\/code>); Java <code>LinkedHashMap<\/code> with <code>accessOrder = true<\/code> and an overridden <code>removeEldestEntry<\/code>. Interviewers usually want the hand-built version.<\/p>","r":["c-cmp-array-ll"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-floyd","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Floyd's cycle detection (fast and slow pointers)","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Floyd's cycle detection (fast and slow pointers)","how to detect cycle in linked list","tortoise and hare","find start of cycle","find duplicate number floyd"],"w":"floyd cycle fast slow pointers tortoise hare","a":"<p>Move <code>slow<\/code> one step and <code>fast<\/code> two steps. If there's a cycle they meet inside it; if <code>fast<\/code> hits <code>null<\/code>, there's no cycle.<\/p><p>To find where the cycle <b>starts<\/b>: after they meet, move one pointer back to the head and advance both one step at a time; they meet at the cycle's entry.<\/p><p>O(n) time, O(1) space. Same idea finds the middle of a list and solves \"Find the Duplicate Number\" (LeetCode 287) by treating values as next-pointers.<\/p>","r":["c-cmp-window-pointers"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-algo-bsearch-template","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Binary search template without off-by-one bugs","cat":"Algorithms","q":["Binary search template without off-by-one bugs","binary search template","lower bound binary search","how to avoid off by one in binary search","first true binary search","binary search on answer"],"w":"binary search template lower bound answer off by one","a":"<p>Search for the <b>first index where a condition becomes true<\/b> (works for lower bound, first bad version, and binary search on the answer):<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">lo, hi = 0, n          # answer is in [lo, hi]\nwhile lo &lt; hi:\n    mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n    if condition(mid):\n        hi = mid       # mid might be the answer\n    else:\n        lo = mid + 1   # mid is definitely not\nreturn lo<\/pre><ul><li>Invariant: everything before <code>lo<\/code> is false, everything from <code>hi<\/code> on is true.<\/li><li>For \"minimum capacity / speed / days\" problems, binary search over the answer range and let <code>condition<\/code> be \"is this value feasible?\" (Koko Eating Bananas, Ship Packages).<\/li><li>Java/C++: use <code>lo + (hi - lo) / 2<\/code> to avoid overflow.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-bo-basics"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-framework","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How to approach a system design interview","cat":"System design","q":["How to approach a system design interview","how to approach system design interview","system design interview framework","steps in hld interview","how to start a system design question","what to do in a 45 minute system design round"],"w":"framework hld steps requirements estimates api","a":"<p>A repeatable 45-minute shape:<\/p><ol><li><b>Requirements (5 min)<\/b>: 3–4 core features; non-functional needs (scale, latency, availability vs consistency, read/write ratio).<\/li><li><b>Estimates (3 min)<\/b>: users → requests per second → storage per year → bandwidth. Rough numbers are fine; you're finding the bottleneck.<\/li><li><b>API (3 min)<\/b>: the main endpoints with inputs and outputs.<\/li><li><b>Data model (5 min)<\/b>: entities, keys, SQL or NoSQL and why.<\/li><li><b>High-level design (10 min)<\/b>: clients → load balancer → services → cache → databases → queues. Walk one request end to end.<\/li><li><b>Deep dives (15 min)<\/b>: the hard parts: hot keys, sharding, consistency, failure handling, the core algorithm.<\/li><li><b>Wrap up<\/b>: bottlenecks, monitoring, what you'd do with more time.<\/li><\/ol><p>Drive the conversation, state trade-offs, and check in with the interviewer at each step.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-estimation","c-ci-sde2"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-estimation","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Back-of-the-envelope estimation numbers","cat":"System design","q":["Back-of-the-envelope estimation numbers","how to do capacity estimation","back of the envelope calculation","latency numbers every programmer should know","how to calculate qps","how much storage per year estimate"],"w":"estimation qps storage latency numbers powers of two","a":"<p>Handy numbers:<\/p><ul><li>1 day ≈ 86,400 s ≈ <b>10⁵ s<\/b>. 1M requests/day ≈ 12 per second; 100M/day ≈ 1,200/s. Peak ≈ 2–3× average.<\/li><li>Powers: 2¹⁰ ≈ 1 thousand (KB), 2²⁰ ≈ 1 million (MB), 2³⁰ ≈ 1 billion (GB), 2⁴⁰ ≈ 1 trillion (TB).<\/li><li>Rough latencies: memory read ~100 ns; SSD random read ~0.1 ms; same-datacenter round trip ~0.5 ms; disk seek ~10 ms; cross-continent round trip ~100–150 ms.<\/li><li>A single well-tuned server handles thousands of simple requests per second; one Redis node ~100k ops/s; one SQL primary low thousands of writes/s.<\/li><\/ul><p>Method: DAU × actions per user ÷ 10⁵ = average QPS. Size per record × records per day × 365 × years = storage. Then say which number drives the design (e.g. \"reads are 100× writes, so cache aggressively\").<\/p>","r":["c-sd-framework"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-cap","k":"cur","s":"","t":"CAP theorem","cat":"System design","q":["CAP theorem","what is cap theorem","cap theorem explained","cp vs ap systems","consistency vs availability","what is pacelc"],"w":"cap consistency availability partition pacelc","a":"<p>In a distributed system, when a <b>network partition<\/b> happens you must choose between:<\/p><ul><li><b>Consistency (CP)<\/b>: every read sees the latest write; some requests fail during the partition. Examples: banking ledgers, inventory counts, ZooKeeper/etcd.<\/li><li><b>Availability (AP)<\/b>: every request gets an answer, possibly stale; nodes reconcile later. Examples: social feeds, shopping carts, DNS, Cassandra/DynamoDB defaults.<\/li><\/ul><p>Partitions are unavoidable, so \"CA\" isn't a real choice for distributed systems. <b>PACELC<\/b> adds: <i>Else<\/i> (no partition), you still trade <b>Latency vs Consistency<\/b>.<\/p><p>In interviews, decide per feature: \"Payments must be consistent; the like counter can be eventually consistent.\"<\/p>","r":["c-sd-consistency"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-consistency","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Consistency models","cat":"System design","q":["Consistency models","strong vs eventual consistency","what is eventual consistency","read your own writes","consistency models explained"],"w":"consistency strong eventual causal read your writes","a":"<p>From strongest to weakest:<\/p><ul><li><b>Strong (linearizable)<\/b>: once a write is acknowledged, every read sees it. Costs latency (consensus or a single leader).<\/li><li><b>Read-your-writes<\/b>: a user always sees their own updates (route their reads to the leader for a few seconds, or use a version check).<\/li><li><b>Monotonic reads<\/b>: a user never sees time go backwards (stick them to one replica).<\/li><li><b>Causal<\/b>: effects are never seen before their causes (a reply never appears before the comment).<\/li><li><b>Eventual<\/b>: replicas converge if writes stop; reads may be stale for a while.<\/li><\/ul><p>Most products mix them: strong for money and inventory, eventual for counters, feeds and search.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-cap","c-sd-replication"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-sql-nosql","k":"cur","s":"","t":"SQL vs NoSQL: how to choose","cat":"System design","q":["SQL vs NoSQL: how to choose","sql vs nosql","when to use nosql","when to use relational database","which database should I choose","mongodb vs postgres","cassandra vs dynamodb vs mongodb"],"w":"sql nosql database choice relational document key value cassandra","a":"<p>Start with <b>SQL<\/b> (Postgres/MySQL) unless you have a reason not to: transactions, joins, constraints, flexible queries.<\/p><p>Pick <b>NoSQL<\/b> for a specific need:<\/p><ul><li><b>Key-value<\/b> (Redis, DynamoDB): simple lookups by key at huge scale; sessions, carts, caches.<\/li><li><b>Wide-column<\/b> (Cassandra, HBase): very high write throughput, time-series, messages; you design tables around queries.<\/li><li><b>Document<\/b> (MongoDB): nested, evolving JSON per entity; catalogs, profiles.<\/li><li><b>Graph<\/b> (Neo4j): many-hop relationship queries.<\/li><li><b>Search<\/b> (Elasticsearch): full-text and faceted search; usually a secondary index fed from the main DB.<\/li><\/ul><p>Say the access pattern first (\"we always read a user's messages by conversation and time\"), then choose the store that fits it.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-indexing","c-sd-sharding"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-acid-base","k":"cur","s":"","t":"ACID vs BASE","cat":"System design","q":["ACID vs BASE","acid vs base","what is acid","what are transaction isolation levels","dirty read phantom read"],"w":"acid base isolation levels transactions","a":"<p><b>ACID<\/b> (relational transactions): Atomic (all or nothing), Consistent (constraints hold), Isolated (concurrent transactions don't see each other's half-done work), Durable (committed survives crashes via the write-ahead log).<\/p><p>Isolation levels, weakest to strongest: Read Uncommitted (dirty reads) → Read Committed (Postgres default) → Repeatable Read (MySQL InnoDB default) → Serializable.<\/p><p><b>BASE<\/b> (many NoSQL systems): Basically Available, Soft state, Eventually consistent: favours availability and scale over immediate consistency.<\/p><p>The Databases topic in this app has a lab that reproduces the lost-update anomaly.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-sql-nosql"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-indexing","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Database indexing (B-tree vs LSM tree)","cat":"System design","q":["Database indexing (B-tree vs LSM tree)","how do database indexes work","b tree vs lsm tree","what is a composite index","when not to add an index","covering index"],"w":"index b tree lsm composite covering","a":"<p>An index is a sorted structure that lets the database find rows without scanning the table.<\/p><ul><li><b>B+ tree<\/b> (Postgres, MySQL): balanced, O(log n) lookups and range scans; updates happen in place. Great for read-heavy workloads.<\/li><li><b>LSM tree<\/b> (Cassandra, RocksDB): writes go to memory then flush to sorted files that are merged in the background. Very fast writes; reads may check several files (Bloom filters help).<\/li><li><b>Composite index<\/b> <code>(a, b)<\/code> works for filters on <code>a<\/code> or <code>a AND b<\/code>, not <code>b<\/code> alone (leftmost prefix rule).<\/li><li><b>Covering index<\/b> includes every column the query needs, so the table isn't touched.<\/li><li>Costs: every index slows writes and uses space. Index columns you filter, join or sort on.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-sql-nosql"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-replication","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Database replication","cat":"System design","q":["Database replication","what is database replication","leader follower replication","master slave replication","replication lag","multi leader replication","sync vs async replication"],"w":"replication leader follower lag failover quorum","a":"<p>Copies of the same data on several machines, for read scaling and availability.<\/p><ul><li><b>Leader–follower<\/b>: all writes go to the leader, which streams changes to followers; reads can go to followers. Simple and common.<\/li><li><b>Sync vs async<\/b>: synchronous followers never lose acknowledged writes but add latency; async followers can lag (stale reads) and can lose the last writes if the leader dies.<\/li><li><b>Failover<\/b>: promote a follower when the leader fails; watch out for split brain (two leaders).<\/li><li><b>Multi-leader / leaderless<\/b> (Dynamo-style, quorum <code>R + W &gt; N<\/code>): writes accepted in many places; needs conflict resolution (last-write-wins, vector clocks, CRDTs).<\/li><\/ul><p>Replication copies data; <b>sharding<\/b> splits it. Big systems do both.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-sharding","c-sd-consistency"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-sharding","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Database sharding (partitioning)","cat":"System design","q":["Database sharding (partitioning)","what is sharding","how to shard a database","sharding strategies","hash vs range partitioning","choosing a shard key","hot partition problem"],"w":"sharding partition shard key hash range hot key","a":"<p>Split one big dataset across machines so each holds a part and takes part of the writes.<\/p><ul><li><b>Hash sharding<\/b>: <code>shard = hash(key) % N<\/code> (or consistent hashing). Even spread; range queries hit every shard.<\/li><li><b>Range sharding<\/b>: by key ranges (A–F, G–M…) or time. Good for range scans; risks hot spots on the newest range.<\/li><li><b>Directory / lookup service<\/b>: a table maps keys to shards; flexible, but one more component.<\/li><\/ul><p>Picking the <b>shard key<\/b> is the key decision: high cardinality, even load, and most queries should touch one shard (e.g. <code>user_id<\/code> for a user's data). Problems to mention: hot keys (celebrities), cross-shard joins and transactions, and resharding (consistent hashing or many virtual shards help).<\/p>","r":["c-sd-consistent-hashing","c-sd-replication"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-consistent-hashing","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Consistent hashing","cat":"System design","q":["Consistent hashing","what is consistent hashing","why consistent hashing","virtual nodes consistent hashing","hash ring"],"w":"consistent hashing ring virtual nodes","a":"<p>With <code>hash(key) % N<\/code>, changing N moves almost every key. Consistent hashing puts servers and keys on a ring; each key belongs to the next server clockwise.<\/p><ul><li>Adding or removing a server only moves the keys in one slice (about 1/N of them).<\/li><li><b>Virtual nodes<\/b>: each server appears at many points on the ring, which evens out the load and spreads a failed server's keys across many others.<\/li><\/ul><p>Used in Cassandra, DynamoDB, CDNs, distributed caches and load balancers. Watch the \"Consistent hashing\" film in the Databases topic.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-sharding"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-caching","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Caching strategies","cat":"System design","q":["Caching strategies","caching strategies","cache aside vs write through","write back cache","what to cache","cache invalidation","cache eviction policies lru lfu","cache stampede thundering herd"],"w":"cache aside write through write back ttl eviction lru stampede redis","a":"<p>Patterns:<\/p><ul><li><b>Cache-aside (lazy)<\/b>: read cache → on miss read DB and fill cache. On write: update DB, then <b>delete<\/b> the key. Most common.<\/li><li><b>Write-through<\/b>: write to cache and DB together; cache always warm, writes slower.<\/li><li><b>Write-back<\/b>: write to cache, flush to DB later; fastest writes, risk of loss.<\/li><li><b>Read-through<\/b>: the cache library loads from the DB itself.<\/li><\/ul><p>Also: set a <b>TTL<\/b>; eviction policy LRU (most common) or LFU; protect against a <b>stampede<\/b> (many misses at once) with request coalescing or a lock per key, plus TTL jitter. Cache what is read often and changes rarely; the <b>hit rate<\/b> is the number that matters.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-cdn"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-cdn","k":"cur","s":"","t":"What is a CDN","cat":"System design","q":["What is a CDN","what is a cdn","how does a cdn work","push vs pull cdn","when to use cdn"],"w":"cdn edge static content cache","a":"<p>A content delivery network caches static (and some dynamic) content on edge servers near users.<\/p><ul><li>Cuts latency (served from a nearby city) and offloads your origin.<\/li><li><b>Pull CDN<\/b>: the edge fetches from your origin on the first miss and caches it with a TTL. Simple; most common.<\/li><li><b>Push CDN<\/b>: you upload content ahead of time; good for big, rarely changing files.<\/li><li>Invalidate by versioned file names (<code>app.3f9c.js<\/code>) rather than purging.<\/li><\/ul><p>Use it for images, video segments, JS/CSS and downloads. Mention it in any read-heavy, global design.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-caching"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-load-balancer","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Load balancers","cat":"System design","q":["Load balancers","what is a load balancer","load balancing algorithms","l4 vs l7 load balancer","round robin vs least connections","sticky sessions"],"w":"load balancer round robin least connections l4 l7 health check","a":"<p>Spreads requests over many servers and stops sending to unhealthy ones (health checks).<\/p><ul><li><b>Algorithms<\/b>: round robin, weighted round robin, least connections, least response time, IP/consistent hash (keeps a user on one server).<\/li><li><b>L4<\/b> (TCP/UDP): fast, routes on IP and port. <b>L7<\/b> (HTTP): routes on path, headers or cookies; can do TLS termination, rate limiting, A/B routing.<\/li><li><b>Sticky sessions<\/b> pin a user to a server; prefer stateless servers with sessions in Redis instead.<\/li><li>Avoid a single point of failure: run LBs in pairs (active–passive) or use DNS plus managed cloud LBs.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-api-gateway"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-api-gateway","k":"cur","s":"","t":"API gateway","cat":"System design","q":["API gateway","what is an api gateway","api gateway vs load balancer","backend for frontend"],"w":"api gateway auth rate limit routing bff","a":"<p>A single entry point in front of many services. It handles cross-cutting work: <b>authentication<\/b>, <b>rate limiting<\/b>, request routing to the right microservice, TLS termination, request/response transformation, caching, logging and metrics.<\/p><p>vs a load balancer: an LB spreads traffic across copies of one service; a gateway routes to <i>different<\/i> services and applies policies. They're often used together. <b>BFF (backend for frontend)<\/b>: a gateway tailored to one client type (mobile vs web).<\/p>","r":["c-sd-load-balancer","c-sd-rate-limit"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-rate-limit","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Rate limiting algorithms","cat":"System design","q":["Rate limiting algorithms","how to design a rate limiter","token bucket vs leaky bucket","sliding window rate limiter","fixed window counter","distributed rate limiting"],"w":"rate limiter token bucket leaky bucket sliding window redis 429","a":"<ul><li><b>Token bucket<\/b>: tokens refill at rate r up to capacity B; each request spends one. Allows bursts up to B. Most common (AWS, Stripe).<\/li><li><b>Leaky bucket<\/b>: requests queue and leave at a constant rate; smooths traffic.<\/li><li><b>Fixed window counter<\/b>: count per minute; simple but allows 2× bursts at window edges.<\/li><li><b>Sliding window log<\/b>: store timestamps; exact but memory-heavy.<\/li><li><b>Sliding window counter<\/b>: weighted mix of current and previous window; good accuracy, little memory.<\/li><\/ul><p>Distributed: keep counters in <b>Redis<\/b> and update atomically (Lua script or <code>INCR<\/code> + <code>EXPIRE<\/code>). Return <b>429 Too Many Requests<\/b> with a <code>Retry-After<\/code> header. Limit per user, API key or IP. Watch the token bucket film in the HLD topic.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-api-gateway"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-queues","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Message queues and Kafka vs RabbitMQ","cat":"System design","q":["Message queues and Kafka vs RabbitMQ","what is a message queue","kafka vs rabbitmq","when to use a message queue","kafka partitions consumer groups","pub sub vs queue","sqs vs kafka"],"w":"message queue kafka rabbitmq sqs partitions consumer group pub sub","a":"<p>Queues decouple producers from consumers: absorb spikes, retry failures, and let slow work happen asynchronously.<\/p><ul><li><b>RabbitMQ / SQS<\/b> (message broker): a message goes to one consumer and is deleted when acknowledged. Great for task queues and routing.<\/li><li><b>Kafka<\/b> (distributed log): messages are appended to partitioned topics and <b>kept<\/b> for a retention period. Consumer groups read at their own offsets and can replay. Ordering is guaranteed <b>within a partition<\/b> (choose the key, e.g. <code>order_id<\/code>). Built for very high throughput, event streaming and analytics.<\/li><li><b>Pub/sub<\/b>: every subscriber gets a copy (fan-out).<\/li><\/ul><p>Add a dead-letter queue for poison messages, and make consumers <b>idempotent<\/b>.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-delivery","c-sd-idempotency"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-delivery","k":"cur","s":"","t":"At-most-once, at-least-once, exactly-once delivery","cat":"System design","q":["At-most-once, at-least-once, exactly-once delivery","exactly once delivery","at least once vs at most once","message delivery guarantees"],"w":"delivery semantics exactly once at least once idempotent","a":"<ul><li><b>At-most-once<\/b>: send and forget; messages can be lost, never duplicated.<\/li><li><b>At-least-once<\/b>: retry until acknowledged; nothing lost, but <b>duplicates<\/b> happen. The usual default.<\/li><li><b>Exactly-once<\/b>: really \"effectively once\": at-least-once delivery plus <b>idempotent processing<\/b> (dedupe by message ID or idempotency key), or transactional systems like Kafka transactions within Kafka.<\/li><\/ul><p>Interview line: \"We use at-least-once delivery and make the consumer idempotent with a processed-IDs table.\"<\/p>","r":["c-sd-idempotency","c-sd-queues"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-idempotency","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Idempotency and idempotency keys","cat":"System design","q":["Idempotency and idempotency keys","what is idempotency","idempotency key payments","how to avoid double charging","idempotent api"],"w":"idempotency key retry payments double charge","a":"<p>An operation is idempotent if doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once.<\/p><ul><li>GET, PUT and DELETE are meant to be idempotent; POST usually isn't.<\/li><li><b>Idempotency key<\/b>: the client sends a unique key (UUID) with a write such as a payment. The server stores <code>key → result<\/code>; a retry with the same key returns the stored result instead of charging again.<\/li><li>Store keys with a unique constraint and a TTL (e.g. 24 h).<\/li><\/ul><p>Essential with retries, at-least-once queues and flaky mobile networks.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-delivery","c-sd-payments"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-saga","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Distributed transactions: 2PC, saga and outbox","cat":"System design","q":["Distributed transactions: 2PC, saga and outbox","what is saga pattern","two phase commit","outbox pattern","distributed transaction microservices","choreography vs orchestration"],"w":"saga 2pc two phase commit outbox compensating choreography orchestration","a":"<p>When one business action spans several services' databases:<\/p><ul><li><b>Two-phase commit (2PC)<\/b>: a coordinator asks everyone to prepare, then commit. Strong, but blocking and fragile; rare in microservices.<\/li><li><b>Saga<\/b>: a chain of local transactions, each with a <b>compensating action<\/b> to undo it (book hotel → book flight fails → cancel hotel). <i>Orchestration<\/i>: a central coordinator drives the steps. <i>Choreography<\/i>: services react to each other's events.<\/li><li><b>Transactional outbox<\/b>: write the business change and an \"event to publish\" row in the <b>same<\/b> DB transaction; a relay publishes outbox rows to the queue. Avoids \"DB updated but event lost\".<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-idempotency"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-resilience","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Circuit breakers, retries and timeouts","cat":"System design","q":["Circuit breakers, retries and timeouts","what is a circuit breaker","retry with exponential backoff","how to handle downstream failures","bulkhead pattern","graceful degradation"],"w":"circuit breaker retry backoff jitter timeout bulkhead fallback resilience","a":"<p>Protect your service when a dependency is slow or down:<\/p><ul><li><b>Timeouts<\/b> on every network call; never wait forever.<\/li><li><b>Retries with exponential backoff and jitter<\/b> (e.g. 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms ± random) for transient errors, only for idempotent operations.<\/li><li><b>Circuit breaker<\/b>: after too many failures, <i>open<\/i> and fail fast for a while; then <i>half-open<\/i> to test; <i>close<\/i> when healthy. Stops cascading failures.<\/li><li><b>Bulkhead<\/b>: separate thread pools/connections per dependency so one slow service can't exhaust everything.<\/li><li><b>Fallbacks<\/b>: cached or default data, a degraded feature instead of an error page.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-queues"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-micro-mono","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Microservices vs monolith","cat":"System design","q":["Microservices vs monolith","microservices vs monolith","when to use microservices","disadvantages of microservices"],"w":"microservices monolith trade offs","a":"<ul><li><b>Monolith<\/b>: one deployable. Simple to build, test, debug and deploy; transactions are easy. Gets slow to change as teams and code grow.<\/li><li><b>Microservices<\/b>: independently deployable services owned by separate teams, each with its own data. Scale and deploy parts independently, but you pay in network calls, distributed transactions, observability and operational overhead.<\/li><\/ul><p>Good answer: start with a well-modularised monolith; split out services along clear business boundaries when team size, scaling needs or release speed demand it.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-saga"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-api-styles","k":"cur","s":"","t":"REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL","cat":"System design","q":["REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL","rest vs grpc","rest vs graphql","when to use grpc","api design best practices","how to design rest api"],"w":"rest grpc graphql api design","a":"<ul><li><b>REST<\/b> (JSON over HTTP): resources and verbs (<code>GET /users/42<\/code>). Simple, cacheable, universal. Default for public APIs.<\/li><li><b>gRPC<\/b> (Protobuf over HTTP/2): typed contracts, fast binary encoding, streaming. Great for internal service-to-service calls.<\/li><li><b>GraphQL<\/b>: the client asks for exactly the fields it needs in one round trip. Good for varied front ends; harder to cache and to protect from expensive queries.<\/li><\/ul><p>REST tips: nouns in paths, plural resources, proper status codes, <b>cursor pagination<\/b>, versioning (<code>/v1<\/code>), idempotency keys for unsafe retries.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-pagination","c-sd-realtime"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-realtime","k":"cur","s":"","t":"WebSockets vs SSE vs long polling","cat":"System design","q":["WebSockets vs SSE vs long polling","websocket vs polling","websockets vs server sent events","how does chat app send messages in real time","long polling"],"w":"websocket sse long polling real time chat","a":"<ul><li><b>Short polling<\/b>: the client asks every few seconds. Simple, wasteful.<\/li><li><b>Long polling<\/b>: the server holds the request until there's data or a timeout. Works everywhere.<\/li><li><b>Server-Sent Events (SSE)<\/b>: one-way server → client stream over HTTP; auto-reconnect. Good for feeds, notifications, live scores.<\/li><li><b>WebSockets<\/b>: full-duplex persistent connection. Best for chat, multiplayer, collaborative editing.<\/li><\/ul><p>At scale: connection servers keep sockets; a pub/sub layer (Redis, Kafka) routes messages to the server holding the recipient's connection.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-chat"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-pagination","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Offset vs cursor pagination","cat":"System design","q":["Offset vs cursor pagination","cursor vs offset pagination","how to paginate a feed","keyset pagination"],"w":"pagination cursor offset keyset","a":"<ul><li><b>Offset<\/b> (<code>LIMIT 20 OFFSET 4000<\/code>): simple and supports jumping to page N, but slow for deep pages (the DB still walks 4,000 rows) and items shift when new data arrives.<\/li><li><b>Cursor / keyset<\/b> (<code>WHERE created_at &lt; :last_seen ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20<\/code>): fast at any depth using an index, stable under inserts. Return an opaque <code>next_cursor<\/code>.<\/li><\/ul><p>Feeds and infinite scroll → cursor. Admin tables with page numbers → offset is fine.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-api-styles"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-ids","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Generating unique IDs at scale","cat":"System design","q":["Generating unique IDs at scale","how to generate unique ids in distributed system","snowflake id","uuid vs auto increment","ticket server"],"w":"unique id snowflake uuid ulid base62","a":"<ul><li><b>Auto-increment<\/b>: simple on one DB; a bottleneck and leaks counts when sharded.<\/li><li><b>UUID v4<\/b>: no coordination, 128-bit, random so it fragments B-tree indexes. <b>UUID v7 / ULID<\/b> are time-ordered and index-friendly.<\/li><li><b>Snowflake<\/b> (64-bit): timestamp (41 bits) + machine ID (10) + per-ms sequence (12). Roughly time-sorted, ~4k IDs per ms per machine, no coordination.<\/li><li><b>Ticket server / range leasing<\/b>: each server leases a block of IDs from a central counter (used for URL shorteners with Base62).<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-url-shortener"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-bloom","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Bloom filters","cat":"System design","q":["Bloom filters","what is a bloom filter","bloom filter use cases","probabilistic data structures"],"w":"bloom filter hyperloglog count min sketch probabilistic","a":"<p>A compact bit array with k hash functions that answers \"<b>definitely not present<\/b>\" or \"<b>probably present<\/b>\". No false negatives, tunable false positives, can't delete (counting Bloom filters can).<\/p><p>Uses: skip disk lookups for missing keys (LSM trees, Cassandra), check \"username taken?\" cheaply, web crawlers' \"already visited?\", blocking known-bad URLs. Related: <b>HyperLogLog<\/b> counts distinct items in a few KB; <b>Count-Min Sketch<\/b> estimates frequencies (top-k trending).<\/p>","r":["c-sd-crawler"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-geo","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Geospatial indexing (geohash, quadtree)","cat":"System design","q":["Geospatial indexing (geohash, quadtree)","how to find nearby drivers","geohash explained","quadtree vs geohash","proximity service design"],"w":"geohash quadtree nearby location uber h3 redis geo","a":"<p>To answer \"what's near me?\" fast:<\/p><ul><li><b>Geohash<\/b>: encodes lat/long into a string; nearby places share prefixes. Store in a normal index; search your cell plus its 8 neighbours.<\/li><li><b>Quadtree<\/b>: recursively splits the map into 4 cells until each has few points; adapts to density (cities vs oceans).<\/li><li><b>Uber's H3<\/b>: hexagonal cells; equal neighbour distances.<\/li><li>Moving objects (drivers) update often: keep current locations in memory (Redis <code>GEOADD<\/code>/<code>GEOSEARCH<\/code>), not in the main DB.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-uber"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-auth","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Authentication vs authorization, OAuth and JWT","cat":"System design","q":["Authentication vs authorization, OAuth and JWT","authentication vs authorization","what is oauth","how does jwt work","session vs jwt","oauth2 flow","sso"],"w":"authentication authorization oauth jwt session sso oidc","a":"<ul><li><b>Authentication<\/b>: who are you? (login, MFA). <b>Authorization<\/b>: what may you do? (roles, permissions, policies).<\/li><li><b>Sessions<\/b>: server stores the session; the browser holds an opaque cookie. Easy to revoke.<\/li><li><b>JWT<\/b>: a signed token carrying claims (user ID, roles, expiry). Services verify it without a DB call; hard to revoke early, so keep it short-lived and pair it with a refresh token.<\/li><li><b>OAuth 2.0<\/b>: lets an app act on a user's behalf at another service without the password (authorization code flow with PKCE for web/mobile). <b>OpenID Connect<\/b> adds login (ID tokens) on top. SSO uses OIDC or SAML.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-api-gateway"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-observability","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Monitoring and observability","cat":"System design","q":["Monitoring and observability","how to monitor a system","metrics logs traces","what is observability","what to alert on","sli slo sla"],"w":"monitoring observability metrics logs traces sli slo sla","a":"<p>Three pillars:<\/p><ul><li><b>Metrics<\/b>: counters and histograms (QPS, error rate, p50/p99 latency, saturation). Prometheus + Grafana.<\/li><li><b>Logs<\/b>: structured events with request IDs. ELK / OpenSearch.<\/li><li><b>Traces<\/b>: one request's path across services (OpenTelemetry, Jaeger) to find the slow hop.<\/li><\/ul><p>Define <b>SLIs<\/b> (what you measure), <b>SLOs<\/b> (targets, e.g. 99.9% of requests &lt; 300 ms) and <b>SLAs<\/b> (contracts). Alert on user-facing symptoms (error rate, latency) rather than every CPU spike. Mention dashboards and alerts in every design's wrap-up.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-framework"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-availability","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Availability nines and redundancy","cat":"System design","q":["Availability nines and redundancy","what does 99.99 availability mean","how many nines","how to make a system highly available","single point of failure"],"w":"availability nines redundancy failover spof","a":"<p>Downtime per year: 99% ≈ 3.65 days · 99.9% ≈ 8.8 hours · 99.99% ≈ 53 minutes · 99.999% ≈ 5 minutes.<\/p><ul><li>Components in <b>series<\/b> multiply: two 99.9% services in a chain ≈ 99.8%.<\/li><li>Redundant copies in <b>parallel<\/b> improve it: two independent 99% replicas ≈ 99.99%.<\/li><li>Remove single points of failure: multiple instances behind LBs, DB replicas with automatic failover, multi-AZ (and for critical systems, multi-region), backups you've actually restored.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-resilience"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-search","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How search works (inverted index)","cat":"System design","q":["How search works (inverted index)","how does elasticsearch work","inverted index","full text search design","how to design search"],"w":"search inverted index elasticsearch ranking bm25","a":"<p>An <b>inverted index<\/b> maps each word to the list of documents containing it (like a book's index). A query intersects those lists and ranks the results (TF-IDF / BM25, plus signals like popularity and recency).<\/p><p>Pipeline: the primary DB stays the source of truth → change events (CDC or a queue) → indexer tokenises, lowercases and stems text → search cluster (Elasticsearch/OpenSearch) sharded by document. For prefix suggestions, use a trie or edge n-grams (see the autocomplete design in the HLD topic).<\/p>","r":["c-sd-typeahead"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-url-shortener","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a URL shortener","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a URL shortener","design url shortener","design tinyurl","design bitly"],"w":"url shortener tinyurl base62 redirect","a":"<p><b>Core<\/b>: <code>POST /shorten {url}<\/code> → <code>abc123<\/code>; <code>GET /abc123<\/code> → 302 redirect.<\/p><ul><li><b>IDs<\/b>: counter ranges leased per server + Base62 (7 chars ≈ 3.5 trillion codes). Avoid hash-and-truncate collisions.<\/li><li><b>Storage<\/b>: key-value store <code>code → long URL, owner, expiry<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Reads ≫ writes<\/b>: Redis cache in front, CDN/edge for hot links.<\/li><li><b>302 vs 301<\/b>: 302 keeps every click visible for analytics; 301 lets browsers cache.<\/li><li><b>Analytics<\/b>: write click events to a queue → batch aggregation.<\/li><\/ul><p>The HLD topic has a full step-by-step build with numbers.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-ids"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-chat","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a chat app (WhatsApp)","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a chat app (WhatsApp)","design whatsapp","design chat application","design messenger","design slack"],"w":"chat whatsapp websocket messages presence","a":"<ul><li><b>Connections<\/b>: clients keep a WebSocket to a gateway server; a presence/session service maps <code>user → gateway<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Send<\/b>: sender → chat service → store message (Cassandra, partitioned by <code>conversation_id<\/code>, sorted by time) → look up recipient's gateway via pub/sub → push. Offline → push notification + fetch on reconnect.<\/li><li><b>Ordering<\/b>: per-conversation sequence numbers. <b>Delivery receipts<\/b>: sent/delivered/read acks.<\/li><li><b>Groups<\/b>: fan-out on write for small groups; for huge channels, fan-out on read.<\/li><li><b>Media<\/b>: upload to blob storage via pre-signed URL; send only the link. End-to-end encryption: keys on devices; the server just relays ciphertext.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-realtime"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-newsfeed","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a news feed (Twitter/Instagram)","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a news feed (Twitter/Instagram)","design twitter","design news feed","design instagram feed","fan out on write vs fan out on read"],"w":"news feed twitter instagram fan out celebrity","a":"<ul><li><b>Post<\/b>: store posts in a DB + media in blob storage/CDN.<\/li><li><b>Feed generation<\/b>: <b>fan-out on write<\/b> (push the post ID into each follower's precomputed feed in Redis) makes reads instant; <b>fan-out on read<\/b> (merge followees' recent posts at read time) avoids writing to millions of feeds.<\/li><li><b>Hybrid<\/b>: push for normal users, pull for celebrities with huge follower counts, merge at read.<\/li><li><b>Ranking<\/b>: start chronological, then a ranking service scores candidates.<\/li><li>Cursor pagination, cache hot feeds, and accept eventual consistency.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-pagination","c-sd-caching"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-uber","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a ride-hailing app (Uber)","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a ride-hailing app (Uber)","design uber","design ola","design lyft","ride sharing system design"],"w":"uber ride hailing location matching geohash","a":"<ul><li><b>Driver locations<\/b>: drivers send GPS every ~4 s to a location service; keep current positions in memory with a geo index (geohash/H3 cells in Redis).<\/li><li><b>Matching<\/b>: rider requests → find nearby available drivers in surrounding cells → offer to the best one with a timeout → lock the driver (avoid double booking with a conditional update).<\/li><li><b>Trip service<\/b>: state machine (requested → accepted → arrived → in progress → completed), persisted in a strongly consistent DB.<\/li><li><b>ETA and pricing<\/b>: map/routing service; surge from supply/demand per cell.<\/li><li><b>Real-time updates<\/b>: WebSockets for both apps. Payments asynchronously after the trip.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-geo"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-youtube","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a video platform (YouTube/Netflix)","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a video platform (YouTube/Netflix)","design youtube","design netflix","video streaming system design"],"w":"youtube netflix video transcoding cdn streaming","a":"<ul><li><b>Upload<\/b>: client uploads to blob storage via a pre-signed URL (resumable, chunked).<\/li><li><b>Processing pipeline<\/b> (queue + workers): transcode into several resolutions and codecs, split into segments for adaptive streaming (HLS/DASH), make thumbnails.<\/li><li><b>Streaming<\/b>: segments served from a <b>CDN<\/b>; the player switches bitrate based on bandwidth.<\/li><li><b>Metadata<\/b> (title, owner, counts) in a DB + cache; search via an inverted index.<\/li><li><b>Views/likes<\/b>: counters updated asynchronously (queue → aggregator), eventually consistent.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-cdn","c-sd-queues"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-notification","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a notification system","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a notification system","design notification service","push notification system design","email sms push notifications"],"w":"notification push email sms queue","a":"<ul><li><b>API<\/b>: services call <code>send(user, template, data, channels, priority)<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Pipeline<\/b>: validate → check user preferences and rate limits → render template → enqueue per channel (push, SMS, email, in-app) → channel workers call providers (APNs/FCM, Twilio, SES).<\/li><li><b>Reliability<\/b>: at-least-once with idempotency keys to avoid duplicates; retries with backoff; dead-letter queue.<\/li><li><b>Priorities<\/b>: separate queues for OTPs vs marketing. Scheduled sends via a delay queue.<\/li><li>Track delivery/open events for analytics.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-queues","c-sd-idempotency"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-typeahead","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design search autocomplete (typeahead)","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design search autocomplete (typeahead)","design autocomplete","design typeahead","search suggestions system design","google search autocomplete"],"w":"autocomplete typeahead trie top k suggestions","a":"<ul><li><b>Data<\/b>: a <b>trie<\/b> of popular queries where each node stores its top-k completions (precomputed), so a lookup is O(prefix length).<\/li><li><b>Build offline<\/b>: aggregate search logs (daily/hourly) → rebuild the trie → ship snapshots to suggestion servers; shard by prefix.<\/li><li><b>Serve<\/b>: in-memory, very low latency; cache hot prefixes at the edge/CDN; the client debounces keystrokes (~100–200 ms).<\/li><li><b>Freshness<\/b>: a small real-time layer for trending queries; filter offensive terms.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-search"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-dropbox","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design file storage and sync (Dropbox/Google Drive)","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design file storage and sync (Dropbox/Google Drive)","design dropbox","design google drive","file sync system design"],"w":"dropbox google drive chunks sync metadata","a":"<ul><li>Split files into <b>chunks<\/b> (~4 MB) identified by content hash; upload only changed chunks (dedupe across users).<\/li><li><b>Blob storage<\/b> (S3) for chunks; a <b>metadata DB<\/b> for files, versions, chunk lists, sharing permissions.<\/li><li><b>Sync<\/b>: the client watches local changes, uploads chunks, commits a new version; other devices get notified (long polling/WebSocket) and download the diff.<\/li><li><b>Conflicts<\/b>: keep both versions (\"conflicted copy\") or last-writer-wins per file.<\/li><li>Pre-signed URLs so clients talk to blob storage directly.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-cdn"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-crawler","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a web crawler","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a web crawler","design web crawler","how does a crawler work","distributed crawler"],"w":"web crawler frontier politeness robots bloom","a":"<ul><li><b>URL frontier<\/b>: queues of URLs to fetch, prioritised, and <b>polite<\/b> (one host at a time, respect <code>robots.txt<\/code> and crawl delays).<\/li><li><b>Fetchers<\/b> (many workers) download pages; DNS cache; timeouts.<\/li><li><b>Parser<\/b> extracts links and content; <b>dedupe<\/b> URLs with a Bloom filter / seen-set and content with checksums (SimHash for near-duplicates).<\/li><li>Store raw pages in blob storage; send content to the indexer.<\/li><li>Partition the frontier by host hash so one worker owns each site.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-bloom"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-payments","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a payment system","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a payment system","design payment system","design stripe","how to avoid double payment","payment gateway design"],"w":"payment stripe ledger idempotency reconciliation","a":"<ul><li><b>Flow<\/b>: create a payment intent (status <code>pending<\/code>) → call the payment provider → webhook/poll for the result → update status → ledger entries.<\/li><li><b>Idempotency keys<\/b> on every request so retries never double-charge.<\/li><li><b>Ledger<\/b>: append-only, double-entry (every debit has a matching credit); never update balances in place without a record.<\/li><li><b>Consistency<\/b>: strong for money (relational DB, transactions); outbox pattern to publish \"payment succeeded\" events reliably.<\/li><li><b>Reconciliation<\/b> job compares your records with provider reports daily. Encrypt card data or keep it with the provider (tokenisation, PCI scope).<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-idempotency","c-sd-saga"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-kv-store","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a distributed key-value store","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a distributed key-value store","design key value store","design dynamodb","design distributed cache","design redis"],"w":"key value store dynamo quorum consistent hashing lsm gossip","a":"<ul><li><b>Partitioning<\/b>: consistent hashing with virtual nodes.<\/li><li><b>Replication<\/b>: each key on N nodes; quorum reads/writes (<code>R + W &gt; N<\/code> for strong-ish reads; e.g. N=3, W=2, R=2).<\/li><li><b>Storage engine<\/b>: LSM tree (memtable + SSTables + compaction) with Bloom filters.<\/li><li><b>Failures<\/b>: gossip for membership, hinted handoff for temporarily down nodes, Merkle trees for anti-entropy repair.<\/li><li><b>Conflicts<\/b>: last-write-wins timestamps or vector clocks. A cache variant (Redis-like) keeps data in memory with eviction and optional persistence.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-consistent-hashing","c-sd-replication"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-leaderboard","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a real-time leaderboard","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a real-time leaderboard","design leaderboard","top k players ranking system","gaming leaderboard design"],"w":"leaderboard redis sorted set rank top k","a":"<ul><li><b>Redis sorted set<\/b>: <code>ZINCRBY board score user<\/code> on each game result; <code>ZREVRANGE board 0 9<\/code> for the top 10; <code>ZREVRANK<\/code> for a user's rank. All O(log n).<\/li><li>Persist scores in a DB as the source of truth; rebuild Redis from it on failure.<\/li><li>Huge scale: shard boards by region/time window, or approximate global top-k by merging shard top-k lists.<\/li><li>Time-boxed boards (daily/weekly): separate keys with TTL.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-sd-caching"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-ticketing","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Design a ticket booking system (BookMyShow)","cat":"System design classics","q":["Design a ticket booking system (BookMyShow)","design bookmyshow","design ticket booking","seat reservation system design","how to prevent double booking seats"],"w":"bookmyshow ticket booking seat hold double booking lock","a":"<ul><li><b>Seat hold<\/b>: when a user picks seats, hold them for ~10 minutes: a row with status <code>held<\/code>, <code>held_until<\/code>, <code>user<\/code>, updated with a conditional write (<code>UPDATE … WHERE status='available'<\/code>) or a Redis lock with TTL.<\/li><li><b>Payment<\/b> within the hold window → mark <code>booked<\/code> in a transaction; expired holds return to <code>available<\/code> (background job or lazy check).<\/li><li>Strong consistency for seats (relational DB); cache show listings and seat maps aggressively.<\/li><li>Flash sales: a virtual waiting room / queue to admit users gradually.<\/li><\/ul><p>The LLD topic in this app models BookMyShow's classes step by step.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-payments"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-lld-approach","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How to approach a low-level design (LLD) interview","cat":"OOP & LLD","q":["How to approach a low-level design (LLD) interview","how to approach lld interview","low level design interview steps","machine coding round tips","object oriented design interview"],"w":"lld low level design machine coding classes patterns","a":"<ol><li><b>Clarify scope<\/b>: list the 4–6 use cases you'll support (e.g. parking lot: park, unpark, pay, show availability).<\/li><li><b>Find the nouns<\/b> → classes (ParkingLot, Floor, Spot, Vehicle, Ticket, Payment) and the <b>verbs<\/b> → methods.<\/li><li><b>Relationships<\/b>: has-a (composition) vs is-a (inheritance); draw a quick class diagram.<\/li><li><b>Patterns where they fit<\/b>: Strategy for pricing/assignment rules, Factory for creating vehicles/spots, Observer for display boards, State for elevators or orders, Singleton (carefully) for the lot.<\/li><li><b>Code the core flow<\/b> with interfaces, enums and clear names; keep classes small (SOLID).<\/li><li><b>Concurrency<\/b>: two cars grabbing one spot → lock or atomic compare-and-set.<\/li><li><b>Extensibility<\/b>: show how a new vehicle type or pricing rule plugs in without editing old code.<\/li><\/ol><p>The LLD topic here builds Parking Lot, ATM, Elevator and BookMyShow step by step.<\/p>","r":["c-oop-pillars","c-lld-patterns"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-oop-pillars","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Four pillars of OOP","cat":"OOP & LLD","q":["Four pillars of OOP","what are the pillars of oop","encapsulation abstraction inheritance polymorphism","explain polymorphism","what is encapsulation"],"w":"oop pillars encapsulation abstraction inheritance polymorphism","a":"<ul><li><b>Encapsulation<\/b>: keep state private and expose behaviour through methods, so invariants can't be broken from outside.<\/li><li><b>Abstraction<\/b>: show <i>what<\/i> an object does, hide <i>how<\/i> (interfaces, abstract classes).<\/li><li><b>Inheritance<\/b>: a subclass reuses and extends a parent's behaviour (is-a). Use sparingly.<\/li><li><b>Polymorphism<\/b>: one interface, many implementations. <i>Runtime<\/i> (overriding: <code>Shape s = new Circle(); s.area()<\/code>) and <i>compile-time<\/i> (overloading).<\/li><\/ul><p>Interview tip: give a tiny example for each from a real system (payments: <code>PaymentMethod<\/code> interface with <code>Card<\/code>, <code>Upi<\/code>, <code>Wallet<\/code>).<\/p>","r":["c-oop-composition","c-oop-interface-abstract"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-oop-composition","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Composition vs inheritance","cat":"OOP & LLD","q":["Composition vs inheritance","composition vs inheritance","favor composition over inheritance","has a vs is a"],"w":"composition inheritance has a is a","a":"<ul><li><b>Inheritance (is-a)<\/b> couples the child to the parent's internals; deep hierarchies get brittle, and a change in the base class ripples down.<\/li><li><b>Composition (has-a)<\/b>: build behaviour by holding other objects (often behind interfaces) and delegating to them. Swap parts at runtime, test them in isolation.<\/li><\/ul><p>Rule of thumb: inherit only for a true is-a relationship where the child can always stand in for the parent (Liskov). Otherwise compose: <code>Car<\/code> <i>has an<\/i> <code>Engine<\/code>; a <code>Duck<\/code> <i>has a<\/i> <code>FlyBehavior<\/code> (Strategy pattern).<\/p>","r":["c-oop-pillars"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-oop-interface-abstract","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Interface vs abstract class (Java)","cat":"OOP & LLD","q":["Interface vs abstract class (Java)","interface vs abstract class","when to use abstract class in java","default methods in interface"],"w":"interface abstract class java default methods","a":"<ul><li><b>Interface<\/b>: a contract of capabilities. A class can implement many. Since Java 8 it can have <code>default<\/code> and <code>static<\/code> methods; no instance state.<\/li><li><b>Abstract class<\/b>: a partial implementation with shared state and constructors. A class can extend only one.<\/li><\/ul><p>Use an interface for \"can do\" roles (<code>Comparable<\/code>, <code>PaymentMethod<\/code>); use an abstract class when several related classes share real code and fields (<code>AbstractVehicle<\/code> with plate number and common logic).<\/p>","r":["c-oop-pillars"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-lld-patterns","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Which design pattern to use when","cat":"OOP & LLD","q":["Which design pattern to use when","design patterns cheat sheet","which design pattern to use","most important design patterns for interviews","list of design patterns"],"w":"design patterns strategy observer factory singleton decorator adapter","a":"<div class=\"ap-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation<\/th><th>Pattern<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Swap an algorithm or rule at runtime (pricing, routing)<\/td><td>Strategy<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Notify many listeners when something changes<\/td><td>Observer<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Object behaves differently by state (elevator, order)<\/td><td>State<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Create objects without naming concrete classes<\/td><td>Factory / Abstract Factory<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Build an object with many optional parts<\/td><td>Builder<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Exactly one shared instance (config, registry)<\/td><td>Singleton (prefer DI)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Add behaviour without changing a class (logging, caching)<\/td><td>Decorator<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Make an incompatible interface fit<\/td><td>Adapter<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Control access (lazy load, cache, auth)<\/td><td>Proxy<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Tree of parts treated uniformly (files/folders)<\/td><td>Composite<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Pass a request along handlers (filters, approvals)<\/td><td>Chain of Responsibility<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Turn an action into an object (undo, queues)<\/td><td>Command<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fixed algorithm skeleton, varying steps<\/td><td>Template Method<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div><p>Each one is built as a class diagram, step by step, in the Creational, Structural and Behavioral topics.<\/p>","r":["c-lld-approach"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-lld-concurrency","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Handling concurrency in LLD problems","cat":"OOP & LLD","q":["Handling concurrency in LLD problems","how to handle concurrency in lld","thread safety in parking lot","race condition in booking system","optimistic vs pessimistic locking"],"w":"concurrency race condition lock optimistic pessimistic","a":"<p>Find the shared resource (spot, seat, account balance) and protect the check-then-act:<\/p><ul><li><b>In memory<\/b>: <code>synchronized<\/code>/<code>ReentrantLock<\/code> around the critical section, or atomic compare-and-set (<code>AtomicInteger<\/code>, <code>ConcurrentHashMap.putIfAbsent<\/code>). Keep lock scope small; lock in a fixed order to avoid deadlock.<\/li><li><b>In the database<\/b>: <i>pessimistic<\/i> <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE<\/code>, or <i>optimistic<\/i> locking with a <code>version<\/code> column (<code>UPDATE … WHERE id=? AND version=?<\/code>; retry if 0 rows changed). Optimistic wins when conflicts are rare.<\/li><li><b>Across services<\/b>: a unique constraint, a conditional write, or a distributed lock with TTL (Redis) and fencing tokens.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-concurrency","c-sd-ticketing"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-java-hashmap","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How HashMap works internally in Java","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["How HashMap works internally in Java","how does hashmap work internally","hashmap internal implementation java","hashmap treeify","hashmap load factor","hashmap vs hashtable vs concurrenthashmap"],"w":"hashmap internals bucket treeify load factor rehash equals hashcode","a":"<ul><li>An array of buckets (default capacity 16). <code>index = hash(key) &amp; (n − 1)<\/code>, where <code>hash<\/code> mixes the high bits of <code>key.hashCode()<\/code>.<\/li><li>Collisions chain in a linked list; since Java 8 a bucket with more than 8 entries (and table ≥ 64) becomes a <b>red-black tree<\/b> → O(log n) worst per bucket.<\/li><li>When size exceeds capacity × <b>load factor 0.75<\/b>, the table <b>doubles<\/b> and entries are redistributed.<\/li><li><code>get<\/code> uses <code>hashCode()<\/code> to find the bucket, then <code>equals()<\/code> to find the key, so keys must implement both consistently and should be immutable.<\/li><li>Not thread-safe: use <code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code> (fine-grained locking/CAS). <code>Hashtable<\/code> is legacy (one big lock). <code>LinkedHashMap<\/code> keeps insertion/access order; <code>TreeMap<\/code> keeps keys sorted.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-equals","c-bo-hash-worst"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-java-equals","k":"cur","s":"","t":"equals() and hashCode() contract","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["equals() and hashCode() contract","equals and hashcode contract","why override hashcode when overriding equals","== vs equals in java"],"w":"equals hashcode contract == java","a":"<ul><li>If <code>a.equals(b)<\/code> then <code>a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()<\/code> <b>must<\/b> hold. (The reverse isn't required.)<\/li><li>Override both or neither; otherwise equal objects land in different HashMap buckets and lookups fail.<\/li><li>Use the same fields in both; prefer immutable fields. <code>Objects.equals<\/code> / <code>Objects.hash<\/code> or Java <b>records<\/b> generate them correctly.<\/li><li><code>==<\/code> compares references (or primitive values); <code>equals<\/code> compares logical equality. Strings: always use <code>equals<\/code>.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-hashmap"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-java-string","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Why is String immutable in Java","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["Why is String immutable in Java","why string is immutable","string pool java","string vs stringbuilder vs stringbuffer","string concatenation in loop"],"w":"string immutable pool stringbuilder","a":"<p>Immutability makes strings safe to share: they can be cached in the <b>string pool<\/b>, used as HashMap keys (hash cached), shared across threads without locks, and trusted in security-sensitive places (class names, file paths, URLs).<\/p><ul><li><code>\"abc\"<\/code> literals are pooled; <code>new String(\"abc\")<\/code> creates a new object.<\/li><li>Concatenating in a loop creates a new String each time: O(n²) total. Use <b>StringBuilder<\/b> (not synchronized, fast) or StringBuffer (synchronized, legacy).<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-equals"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-java-memory-gc","k":"cur","s":"","t":"JVM memory and garbage collection","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["JVM memory and garbage collection","jvm memory model heap stack","how does garbage collection work in java","g1 garbage collector","memory leak in java","stack vs heap java"],"w":"jvm heap stack gc garbage collection g1 memory leak","a":"<ul><li><b>Stack<\/b>: one per thread; holds method frames, local primitives and references. Too-deep recursion → <code>StackOverflowError<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Heap<\/b>: all objects; shared. Split into young generation (new objects; frequent fast \"minor\" GCs) and old generation (long-lived objects).<\/li><li><b>Metaspace<\/b>: class metadata.<\/li><li>GC finds objects no longer reachable from roots and frees them. <b>G1<\/b> (default since Java 9) collects regions incrementally with pause-time goals; <b>ZGC/Shenandoah<\/b> aim for very short pauses on huge heaps.<\/li><li>Java leaks are usually forgotten references: static collections that only grow, caches without eviction, unremoved listeners, ThreadLocals in thread pools.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-concurrency"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-java-concurrency","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Java concurrency basics: synchronized, volatile, atomic","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["Java concurrency basics: synchronized, volatile, atomic","synchronized vs volatile","what is volatile in java","atomicinteger","reentrantlock vs synchronized","what is a deadlock","java thread safety"],"w":"synchronized volatile atomic reentrantlock deadlock thread safe","a":"<ul><li><b>synchronized<\/b>: mutual exclusion + visibility for a block or method (intrinsic lock).<\/li><li><b>volatile<\/b>: guarantees visibility of the latest write across threads and prevents reordering, but <b>not<\/b> atomicity (<code>count++<\/code> is still unsafe).<\/li><li><b>Atomic classes<\/b> (<code>AtomicInteger<\/code>, <code>LongAdder<\/code>): lock-free compare-and-set for counters.<\/li><li><b>ReentrantLock<\/b>: like synchronized plus <code>tryLock<\/code>, timeouts, fairness and multiple conditions.<\/li><li><b>Concurrent collections<\/b>: <code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code>, <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList<\/code>, <code>BlockingQueue<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Deadlock<\/b> needs mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait; avoid it with a global lock order or <code>tryLock<\/code> with timeout.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-executors","c-lld-concurrency"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-java-executors","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Thread pools, ExecutorService and CompletableFuture","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["Thread pools, ExecutorService and CompletableFuture","executorservice in java","thread pool types","completablefuture example","virtual threads java 21","callable vs runnable"],"w":"executorservice thread pool completablefuture virtual threads callable","a":"<ul><li>Don't create raw threads per task; submit to an <b>ExecutorService<\/b> (<code>Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n)<\/code> or a configured <code>ThreadPoolExecutor<\/code> with a bounded queue and a rejection policy).<\/li><li><code>Runnable<\/code> returns nothing; <code>Callable&lt;T&gt;<\/code> returns a <code>Future&lt;T&gt;<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>CompletableFuture<\/b> composes async steps: <code>supplyAsync(...).thenApply(...).thenCombine(...).exceptionally(...)<\/code>; pass your own executor for I/O.<\/li><li><b>Virtual threads<\/b> (Java 21): very cheap threads for blocking I/O (<code>Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()<\/code>), so simple blocking code scales to many concurrent requests.<\/li><li>Always shut pools down and size them: CPU-bound ≈ number of cores; I/O-bound larger.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-concurrency"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-java-collections","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Choosing a Java collection","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["Choosing a Java collection","which java collection to use","arraylist vs linkedlist java","hashset vs treeset","arraydeque vs stack","priorityqueue java comparator"],"w":"java collections arraylist arraydeque treemap priorityqueue comparator","a":"<ul><li><b>List<\/b>: <code>ArrayList<\/code> almost always (<code>LinkedList<\/code> rarely wins).<\/li><li><b>Queue/stack<\/b>: <code>ArrayDeque<\/code> (faster than <code>Stack<\/code> and <code>LinkedList<\/code>); <code>push/pop/peek<\/code> for a stack, <code>offer/poll/peek<\/code> for a queue.<\/li><li><b>Set/Map<\/b>: <code>HashSet<\/code>/<code>HashMap<\/code> for O(1); <code>LinkedHashMap<\/code> for insertion or access order (LRU); <code>TreeSet<\/code>/<code>TreeMap<\/code> for sorted keys and <code>floor<\/code>/<code>ceiling<\/code>.<\/li><li><b>Heap<\/b>: <code>PriorityQueue<\/code> is a min-heap; max-heap with <code>new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Comparator.reverseOrder())<\/code> or <code>(a, b) -&gt; b - a<\/code> (beware overflow; prefer <code>Integer.compare(b, a)<\/code>).<\/li><li><b>Comparable<\/b> = natural order inside the class; <b>Comparator<\/b> = external, composable order (<code>Comparator.comparing(Person::age).thenComparing(Person::name)<\/code>).<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-bo-ds"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-java-streams","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Java 8+ features: streams, lambdas, Optional, records","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["Java 8+ features: streams, lambdas, Optional, records","java 8 features","java streams example","optional in java","what are records in java","map vs flatmap"],"w":"java 8 streams lambda optional records flatmap","a":"<ul><li><b>Lambdas &amp; functional interfaces<\/b> (<code>Function<\/code>, <code>Predicate<\/code>, <code>Supplier<\/code>, <code>Consumer<\/code>).<\/li><li><b>Streams<\/b>: <code>list.stream().filter(x -&gt; x &gt; 0).map(x -&gt; x * 2).collect(Collectors.toList())<\/code>; <code>groupingBy<\/code>, <code>counting<\/code>, <code>toMap<\/code>. Lazy; one pass; avoid side effects. <code>flatMap<\/code> flattens nested streams.<\/li><li><b>Optional<\/b>: return type for \"maybe no value\"; <code>orElse<\/code>, <code>orElseGet<\/code>, <code>map<\/code>. Don't use it for fields or parameters.<\/li><li><b>Records<\/b> (Java 16): immutable data carriers with generated constructor, getters, <code>equals<\/code>, <code>hashCode<\/code>, <code>toString<\/code>.<\/li><li>Also: <code>var<\/code> (local type inference), text blocks, switch expressions, sealed classes, pattern matching for <code>instanceof<\/code>.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-collections"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-java-exceptions","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Checked vs unchecked exceptions","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["Checked vs unchecked exceptions","checked vs unchecked exceptions","final finally finalize difference","try with resources"],"w":"checked unchecked exceptions final finally finalize","a":"<ul><li><b>Checked<\/b> (<code>IOException<\/code>, <code>SQLException<\/code>): must be declared or caught; for recoverable conditions outside your control.<\/li><li><b>Unchecked<\/b> (<code>RuntimeException<\/code>: <code>NullPointerException<\/code>, <code>IllegalArgumentException<\/code>): programming errors; not forced.<\/li><li><b>Errors<\/b> (<code>OutOfMemoryError<\/code>, <code>StackOverflowError<\/code>): don't catch.<\/li><li><code>try-with-resources<\/code> closes <code>AutoCloseable<\/code> resources automatically.<\/li><li><code>final<\/code> (constant / no override / no subclass) vs <code>finally<\/code> (always runs after try/catch) vs <code>finalize<\/code> (deprecated GC hook; never rely on it).<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-java-streams"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-spring-di","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Spring Boot: dependency injection and bean scopes","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["Spring Boot: dependency injection and bean scopes","what is dependency injection","spring bean scopes","constructor vs field injection","how does spring boot auto configuration work","component vs bean annotation"],"w":"spring boot dependency injection bean scope autowired autoconfiguration","a":"<ul><li><b>Dependency injection<\/b>: objects receive their collaborators instead of creating them, so you can swap implementations and test with mocks. Prefer <b>constructor injection<\/b> (immutable, explicit, testable) over <code>@Autowired<\/code> fields.<\/li><li><code>@Component<\/code>/<code>@Service<\/code>/<code>@Repository<\/code> mark classes for component scanning; <code>@Bean<\/code> methods in a <code>@Configuration<\/code> class create beans you configure by hand (e.g. third-party classes).<\/li><li><b>Scopes<\/b>: <code>singleton<\/code> (default, one per context: must be thread-safe and stateless), <code>prototype<\/code> (new each injection), <code>request<\/code>/<code>session<\/code> (web).<\/li><li><b>Auto-configuration<\/b>: Spring Boot looks at the classpath and properties and creates sensible beans (<code>@ConditionalOnClass<\/code>, <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean<\/code>); your own beans override them.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-spring-transactional"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-spring-transactional","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Spring @Transactional pitfalls","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["Spring @Transactional pitfalls","how does transactional work in spring","transactional not working","self invocation transactional","transaction propagation"],"w":"spring transactional proxy self invocation propagation rollback","a":"<p><code>@Transactional<\/code> works through a <b>proxy<\/b> around the bean:<\/p><ul><li><b>Self-invocation<\/b> (calling a <code>@Transactional<\/code> method from another method of the same class) bypasses the proxy, so no transaction starts.<\/li><li>Only <code>public<\/code> methods are proxied by default.<\/li><li>By default it rolls back on <b>unchecked<\/b> exceptions only; add <code>rollbackFor = Exception.class<\/code> for checked ones.<\/li><li><b>Propagation<\/b>: <code>REQUIRED<\/code> (join or create, default), <code>REQUIRES_NEW<\/code> (suspend and start a new one, e.g. audit logs), <code>NESTED<\/code>.<\/li><li>Keep transactions short; don't make remote HTTP calls inside them. Use <code>readOnly = true<\/code> for queries.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-spring-di","c-sd-acid-base"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-spring-n-plus-1","k":"cur","s":"","t":"N+1 query problem (JPA/Hibernate)","cat":"Java & Spring","q":["N+1 query problem (JPA/Hibernate)","what is n+1 query problem","lazy vs eager loading jpa","hibernate fetch join"],"w":"n+1 jpa hibernate lazy eager fetch join","a":"<p>Loading 100 orders and then lazily touching each order's customer fires 1 + 100 queries.<\/p><p>Fixes: <code>JOIN FETCH<\/code> in JPQL, <code>@EntityGraph<\/code>, batch fetching (<code>@BatchSize<\/code> / <code>hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size<\/code>), or a DTO projection query. Keep associations <code>LAZY<\/code> by default and fetch what each use case needs. Spot it by enabling SQL logging or counting queries in tests.<\/p>","r":["c-spring-transactional"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bh-star","k":"cur","s":"","t":"STAR method for behavioral questions","cat":"Behavioral","q":["STAR method for behavioral questions","how to answer behavioral questions","star method","what is star format","behavioral interview tips"],"w":"behavioral star situation task action result stories","a":"<p><b>S<\/b>ituation (1–2 lines of context) → <b>T<\/b>ask (your responsibility) → <b>A<\/b>ction (what <i>you<\/i> did, in detail; most of the answer) → <b>R<\/b>esult (measurable impact + what you learned).<\/p><ul><li>Prepare 6–8 stories from real projects that can each cover several questions: ownership, conflict, failure, ambiguity, tight deadline, mentoring, a technical deep dive.<\/li><li>Use \"I\", not \"we\", for your actions; quantify results (latency −40%, 3 teams unblocked, incidents halved).<\/li><li>Keep each answer to about 2 minutes, then let them dig in.<\/li><li>End with the lesson and what you do differently now.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-bh-intro","c-bh-conflict","c-bh-failure"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bh-intro","k":"cur","s":"","t":"\"Tell me about yourself\"","cat":"Behavioral","q":["\"Tell me about yourself\"","tell me about yourself","how to introduce myself in interview","self introduction for software engineer"],"w":"tell me about yourself introduction pitch","a":"<p>A 60–90 second story: <b>present → past → future<\/b>.<\/p><ol><li><i>Present<\/i>: \"I'm a software engineer at Salesforce working on Marketing Cloud / Data Cloud, mostly Java and Spring Boot services…\" plus the scale or impact of what you own.<\/li><li><i>Past<\/i>: one or two highlights that show growth (a system you designed, a hard problem you solved, something you led).<\/li><li><i>Future<\/i>: why this role: \"I want to work on … at larger scale / own systems end to end, which is why this team excites me.\"<\/li><\/ol><p>Tailor the highlights to the job description, and end on a hook they'll want to ask about.<\/p>","r":["c-bh-star","c-bh-why"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bh-why","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Why do you want to leave / why this company?","cat":"Behavioral","q":["Why do you want to leave / why this company?","why do you want to leave your current job","why this company","why should we hire you","reason for job change"],"w":"why leave why company why hire motivation","a":"<p>Stay positive and forward-looking; never criticise your current employer.<\/p><ul><li><b>Why leave<\/b>: \"I've learned a lot owning X; I'm looking for bigger scale / a different problem space / more ownership of system design, and this role offers that.\"<\/li><li><b>Why this company<\/b>: name something specific: a product you use, a technical challenge they've written about, the team's domain. Connect it to your experience.<\/li><li><b>Why hire you<\/b>: two or three strengths with proof (\"I've shipped …, I can own a service end to end, I mentor juniors\").<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-bh-intro"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bh-conflict","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Tell me about a conflict or disagreement","cat":"Behavioral","q":["Tell me about a conflict or disagreement","tell me about a conflict with a coworker","disagreement with manager","how do you handle disagreements","time you pushed back"],"w":"conflict disagreement push back manager teammate","a":"<p>Show you disagree with <b>data and empathy<\/b>, then commit.<\/p><ul><li><i>Situation<\/i>: a real technical disagreement (design choice, priority, deadline), not a personality clash.<\/li><li><i>Action<\/i>: you listened to understand their constraints, framed the options with data (benchmarks, cost, risk), proposed an experiment or a compromise, and escalated only if needed, together.<\/li><li><i>Result<\/i>: the decision, its outcome, and the relationship afterwards. If you lost the argument: \"I disagreed and committed fully, and here's what happened.\"<\/li><li><i>Lesson<\/i>: what you now do earlier (align on goals first, write a short design doc).<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-bh-star"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bh-failure","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Tell me about a failure or mistake","cat":"Behavioral","q":["Tell me about a failure or mistake","tell me about a time you failed","biggest mistake at work","production incident you caused","what is your weakness"],"w":"failure mistake incident weakness learning","a":"<p>Pick a real, moderate failure you owned (a bug that reached production, a missed estimate), not a disguised humblebrag.<\/p><ul><li>Explain briefly what went wrong and <b>your<\/b> part in it.<\/li><li>Focus on the response: how you mitigated quickly, communicated, and found the root cause.<\/li><li>Show the lasting fix: tests, alerts, a checklist, a design change, so it can't recur.<\/li><li><b>Weakness<\/b>: name a real one you're actively improving, with the steps you're taking (\"I used to over-engineer early; now I write a one-page design and get feedback before building\").<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-bh-star"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bh-ownership","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Ownership, ambiguity and leading without authority","cat":"Behavioral","q":["Ownership, ambiguity and leading without authority","tell me about a time you took ownership","handling ambiguous requirements","leading without authority","tight deadline example","mentoring junior engineers","most challenging project"],"w":"ownership ambiguity influence deadline mentoring challenging project","a":"<p>Strong SDE-2 stories show you <b>drove<\/b> something beyond your ticket:<\/p><ul><li><i>Ownership<\/i>: you noticed a problem nobody owned (flaky deploys, a slow query, an on-call pain), proposed a fix, got buy-in, delivered it and measured the impact.<\/li><li><i>Ambiguity<\/i>: requirements were unclear; you wrote down assumptions, asked the right people, shipped a first slice and iterated.<\/li><li><i>Influence<\/i>: you aligned another team using data and a clear proposal, not authority.<\/li><li><i>Deadline<\/i>: you cut scope deliberately, communicated trade-offs early and protected quality where it mattered.<\/li><li><i>Mentoring<\/i>: you onboarded or unblocked a junior (code reviews, pairing, docs) and they grew.<\/li><\/ul><p>Have one story ready for each; many can be the same project told from different angles.<\/p>","r":["c-bh-star"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-bh-ask","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Questions to ask the interviewer","cat":"Behavioral","q":["Questions to ask the interviewer","what questions should I ask the interviewer","questions to ask at the end of interview"],"w":"questions to ask interviewer end of interview","a":"<p>Ask things you genuinely want to know:<\/p><ul><li>\"What does a great first six months look like in this role?\"<\/li><li>\"What's the biggest technical challenge the team is facing this year?\"<\/li><li>\"How are design decisions made and reviewed?\"<\/li><li>\"How do on-call and incidents work here?\"<\/li><li>\"How does the team measure success, and how is performance evaluated?\"<\/li><li>To the hiring manager: \"What would make someone thrive on your team versus struggle?\"<\/li><\/ul><p>Avoid asking about salary or perks in technical rounds; save those for the recruiter.<\/p>","r":["c-bh-star"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-start","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Where should I start in this app","cat":"Using this app","q":["Where should I start in this app","how do I use this app","where should I start","what is rubber duck dojo","how is this app organised","what order should I study topics"],"w":"start app how to use order roadmap","a":"<p>Rubber Duck Dojo has two roadmaps on the home page:<\/p><ul><li><b>DSA<\/b> (13 topics, in order: Arrays &amp; Hashing → … → Bit Manipulation). Each topic has chapters with short posts, step-through <b>labs<\/b>, <b>reflexes<\/b> (\"when you see X, do Y\"), quizzes, problems with code in Python/Java/C++, and a \"Counting the cost\" chapter.<\/li><li><b>System design<\/b> (11 topics): design thinking and UML, Java/OOP, SOLID, design patterns, LLD problems, building blocks, databases, distributed systems and the HLD classics.<\/li><\/ul><p>Start with topic 1 of DSA and alternate with System Design. Use the <b>Study plan<\/b> if you have a deadline. Ask me (Pip) anything along the way.<\/p>","r":["c-app-plan","c-app-features"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-features","k":"cur","s":"","t":"What features does the app have","cat":"Using this app","q":["What features does the app have","what can this app do","list of features","what are labs","what are reflexes","what is xp"],"w":"features labs reflexes xp cinema saved notes crunch plan","a":"<ul><li><b>Labs<\/b>: interactive step-through visualisations of each algorithm.<\/li><li><b>Reflexes<\/b>: \"see this → do that\" cards you lock in; the <b>Mixed drill<\/b> in Practice shuffles them across topics.<\/li><li><b>Problems<\/b>: each with plain-words explanation, a walkthrough, code in Python/Java/C++, a LeetCode link and a <b>Try it here<\/b> Python editor that runs tests in your browser.<\/li><li><b>Dojo Cinema<\/b>: short narrated animated films per topic.<\/li><li><b>Revision notes<\/b>, <b>Crunch list<\/b> and <b>Study plan<\/b> pages.<\/li><li><b>Saved<\/b>: bookmark anything (🔖) and open the Saved drawer from the top bar.<\/li><li><b>Pip<\/b>: your rubber duck: daily XP goal, streak, level-ups, and this Ask Pip chat.<\/li><\/ul>","r":["c-app-start"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-plan","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How does the study plan work","cat":"Using this app","q":["How does the study plan work","how does the study plan work","make me a study plan","study schedule","plan for interview in 30 days"],"w":"study plan schedule days weeks","a":"<p>Open <b>🗓 Study plan<\/b> (Revise section on the home page). Pick how many weeks you have and minutes per day; it builds a day-by-day schedule that interleaves DSA and System Design, adds spaced review days, and finishes with crunch days of the most important questions. Tick items off and it tracks whether you're ahead or behind.<\/p>","r":["c-app-crunch","c-ci-practice"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-crunch","k":"cur","s":"","t":"What is the crunch list","cat":"Using this app","q":["What is the crunch list","what is the crunch list","minimum questions to do before interview","last minute preparation","interview tomorrow what to revise"],"w":"crunch list last minute minimum questions tiers","a":"<p>The <b>🎯 Crunch list<\/b> is the minimum set of questions that still covers every pattern, in three tiers: <b>1 day<\/b>, <b>3 days<\/b> and <b>1 week<\/b>, for both DSA and System Design. Each item has Hint → Plan → Code reveals so you can try it cold. Interview tomorrow? Do tier 1 and skim the <b>Revision notes<\/b> for the topics you're weakest in.<\/p>","r":["c-app-plan"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-lang","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How to switch code between Python, Java and C++","cat":"Using this app","q":["How to switch code between Python, Java and C++","how to see java code","change language to c++","code in java instead of python","language switch"],"w":"language switch java cpp python tabs","a":"<p>Use the <b>Python / Java / C++<\/b> tabs above any code block. Your choice is remembered across the whole app (problems, templates, quizzes, notes and crunch list). A few quiz snippets are Python-specific and show a note instead.<\/p>","r":["c-ci-language"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-ide","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How does the code editor (Try it here) work","cat":"Using this app","q":["How does the code editor (Try it here) work","how to run code in the app","try it here editor","can I run java code","ide in the app","compile errors in editor"],"w":"editor ide run code python tests leetcode","a":"<p>On each problem, <b>▶ Try it here<\/b> opens an editor with a starter function. <b>Python<\/b> runs right in your browser against the problem's examples and shows compile errors, runtime errors with the line, and a pass/fail table per test. For <b>Java and C++<\/b>, use <b>Copy &amp; open on LeetCode<\/b> to run it there. Your drafts are saved in this browser.<\/p>","r":["c-app-features"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-progress","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Where is my progress saved","cat":"Using this app","q":["Where is my progress saved","where is my progress saved","will I lose my progress","progress on another device","reset my progress","sync progress"],"w":"progress saved localstorage reset device sync","a":"<p>Progress (ticks, XP, streak, saved items, drafts) is stored <b>in this browser<\/b> (localStorage). It survives updates to the app, but it doesn't move to another browser or device, and the claude.ai link and the GitHub link keep separate progress.<\/p><p>To reset, clear this site's data in your browser settings. Saved items may also sync through your claude.ai account when you open the claude.ai version signed in.<\/p>","r":["c-app-features"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-pip","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Who is Pip","cat":"Using this app","q":["Who is Pip","who is pip","what is the duck","how does the streak work","how to level up","daily goal"],"w":"pip duck streak xp level daily goal","a":"<p>I'm Pip, your rubber duck 🐥. Programmers explain code to a rubber duck to find their own bugs; explain your approach to me out loud before you code.<\/p><p>I also keep your <b>daily XP goal<\/b> (40/80/150), your <b>streak<\/b> (days you hit the goal) and your <b>level<\/b>: I grow from an egg into a grown-up duck as you finish the course. Tap me in the corner for today's progress and a quick reflex quiz.<\/p>","r":["c-app-features"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-cinema","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How to watch the Dojo Cinema films","cat":"Using this app","q":["How to watch the Dojo Cinema films","where are the videos","how to watch films","dojo cinema","turn off voice in films"],"w":"cinema films videos voice captions","a":"<p>Each topic page has a <b>Watch the film<\/b> banner near the top, and the home page has a <b>Cinema<\/b> section with all 19 films. Controls: space to play/pause, ← → to step, <b>m<\/b> to toggle the voice-over, <b>c<\/b> for captions, <b>f<\/b> for full screen. At the end you get a recap, a link to the topic's lab and YouTube searches for more explanations.<\/p>","r":["c-app-features"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-app-ask","k":"cur","s":"","t":"What can I ask Pip","cat":"Using this app","q":["What can I ask Pip","what can I ask you","what questions can you answer","help","how do you work","are you an ai"],"w":"help what can you answer agent","a":"<p>I have prepared answers for everything in this app plus the usual interview topics:<\/p><ul><li><b>Any problem<\/b> by name or LeetCode number (\"LC 76\", \"how to solve trapping rain water\"): hint, approach, complexity and code.<\/li><li><b>Patterns and concepts<\/b> (\"when to use a monotonic stack\", \"BFS vs DFS\", \"what is amortized complexity\").<\/li><li><b>System design<\/b> fundamentals and classic designs (\"design WhatsApp\", \"consistent hashing\", \"Kafka vs RabbitMQ\").<\/li><li><b>Java &amp; Spring<\/b>, <b>OOP/LLD<\/b>, <b>behavioral<\/b> answers, and <b>how to use the app<\/b>.<\/li><\/ul><p>Open the <b>Question bank<\/b> tab to browse everything. On the claude.ai version, if I don't have a prepared answer, I can ask Claude live.<\/p>","r":["c-app-start"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-sd-learn","k":"cur","s":"","t":"How to prepare for system design interviews","cat":"System design","q":["How to prepare for system design interviews","how to learn system design","best way to prepare for system design","system design preparation plan","resources for system design","how to get better at system design"],"w":"learn system design prepare plan resources","a":"<ol><li><b>Learn the building blocks first<\/b>: load balancers, caches, databases (SQL vs NoSQL, indexes, replication, sharding), queues, CDNs, consistent hashing, rate limiting. In this app: System design basics → Databases → Distributed systems.<\/li><li><b>Learn one framework<\/b> and use it every time: requirements → estimates → API → data model → high-level design → deep dives → wrap-up.<\/li><li><b>Study the classics<\/b> end to end: URL shortener, rate limiter, chat, news feed, notification system, ride hailing, video streaming, file sync, typeahead (the HLD topic builds 11 of them box by box).<\/li><li><b>Practise out loud<\/b> with a timer (45 minutes), drawing boxes as you talk. Then compare with a reference design and note what you missed.<\/li><li><b>Read real architectures<\/b>: company engineering blogs and talks show how trade-offs play out in production.<\/li><li><b>For LLD rounds<\/b>: SOLID, the main design patterns, and 4–5 classic problems (parking lot, elevator, ATM, BookMyShow).<\/li><\/ol><p>A useful habit: for every design, name the one hardest part (hot keys, ordering, consistency, fan-out) and go deep on it.<\/p>","r":["c-sd-framework","c-sd-estimation"],"L":[]},{"i":"c-prob-78","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Subsets (backtracking)","cat":"DSA problems","q":["Subsets (backtracking)","subsets","leetcode 78","all subsets of an array","power set","generate all subsets"],"w":"subsets backtracking power set take skip","a":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> backtracking; each number is either taken or skipped.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">def subsets(nums):\n    res, path = [], []\n    def bt(i):\n        if i == len(nums):\n            res.append(path[:]); return\n        path.append(nums[i]); bt(i + 1)   # take\n        path.pop();           bt(i + 1)   # skip\n    bt(0)\n    return res<\/pre><ul><li>2ⁿ subsets, each copied in O(n) → <b>O(n · 2ⁿ)<\/b> time, O(n) recursion depth.<\/li><li>Duplicates in the input (Subsets II, LC 90): sort first, and in the \"for loop\" version skip <code>nums[i] == nums[i-1]<\/code> when <code>i &gt; start<\/code>.<\/li><li>Bitmask version: every number from 0 to 2ⁿ − 1 is one subset (see Bit Manipulation).<\/li><li>Watch \"The decision tree\" film in the Recursion topic.<\/li><\/ul>","r":[],"L":[["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/subsets/"],["Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html"],["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/subsets/"]],"n":78,"sub":"LeetCode 78 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking"},{"i":"c-prob-90","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Subsets II (with duplicates)","cat":"DSA problems","q":["Subsets II (with duplicates)","subsets ii","subsets with duplicates","leetcode 90"],"w":"subsets duplicates sort skip","a":"<p>Sort, then use the \"for loop\" backtracking and skip a duplicate at the same depth:<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">def subsets_with_dup(nums):\n    nums.sort(); res, path = [], []\n    def bt(start):\n        res.append(path[:])\n        for i in range(start, len(nums)):\n            if i &gt; start and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]:\n                continue          # same value already tried at this depth\n            path.append(nums[i]); bt(i + 1); path.pop()\n    bt(0)\n    return res<\/pre><p>O(n · 2ⁿ) time. The <code>i &gt; start<\/code> (not <code>i &gt; 0<\/code>) is the classic bug: <code>i &gt; 0<\/code> also drops valid subsets like [2, 2].<\/p>","r":[],"L":[["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/subsets-ii/"],["Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html"]],"n":90,"sub":"LeetCode 90 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking"},{"i":"c-prob-46","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Permutations","cat":"DSA problems","q":["Permutations","permutations","leetcode 46","all permutations of an array","generate permutations"],"w":"permutations backtracking used","a":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> backtracking with a <code>used<\/code> set; at each level pick any unused number.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">def permute(nums):\n    res, path, used = [], [], [False] * len(nums)\n    def bt():\n        if len(path) == len(nums):\n            res.append(path[:]); return\n        for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n            if used[i]: continue\n            used[i] = True; path.append(x)\n            bt()\n            path.pop(); used[i] = False\n    bt()\n    return res<\/pre><p>n! permutations × O(n) copy → <b>O(n · n!)<\/b>. With duplicates (LC 47): sort and skip <code>nums[i] == nums[i-1] and not used[i-1]<\/code>.<\/p>","r":[],"L":[["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/permutations/"],["Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html"]],"n":46,"sub":"LeetCode 46 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking"},{"i":"c-prob-39","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Combination Sum","cat":"DSA problems","q":["Combination Sum","combination sum","leetcode 39","combinations that sum to target reuse"],"w":"combination sum backtracking reuse prune","a":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> backtracking where a number may be reused, so recurse with the <i>same<\/i> index after taking it.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">def combination_sum(cands, target):\n    cands.sort(); res, path = [], []\n    def bt(start, remain):\n        if remain == 0:\n            res.append(path[:]); return\n        for i in range(start, len(cands)):\n            if cands[i] &gt; remain: break      # sorted → prune\n            path.append(cands[i])\n            bt(i, remain - cands[i])          # i, not i+1: reuse allowed\n            path.pop()\n    bt(0, target)\n    return res<\/pre><p>Exponential in the worst case (about O(n^(T/m)) with T = target, m = smallest candidate). Combination Sum II (each number once, duplicates in input): use <code>i + 1<\/code> and skip equal neighbours at the same depth.<\/p>","r":[],"L":[["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/combination-sum/"],["Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html"]],"n":39,"sub":"LeetCode 39 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking"},{"i":"c-prob-51","k":"cur","s":"","t":"N-Queens","cat":"DSA problems","q":["N-Queens","n queens","n-queens","leetcode 51","place n queens on a chessboard"],"w":"n queens backtracking diagonals","a":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> backtracking row by row, with O(1) conflict checks using three sets: columns, <code>r − c<\/code> diagonals and <code>r + c<\/code> anti-diagonals.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">def solve_n_queens(n):\n    res, cols, d1, d2 = [], set(), set(), set()\n    board = [[&#x27;.&#x27;] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    def bt(r):\n        if r == n:\n            res.append([&#x27;&#x27;.join(row) for row in board]); return\n        for c in range(n):\n            if c in cols or r - c in d1 or r + c in d2: continue\n            cols.add(c); d1.add(r - c); d2.add(r + c); board[r][c] = &#x27;Q&#x27;\n            bt(r + 1)\n            cols.remove(c); d1.remove(r - c); d2.remove(r + c); board[r][c] = &#x27;.&#x27;\n    bt(0)\n    return res<\/pre><p>About O(n!) time, O(n) extra space. N-Queens II (LC 52) just counts solutions. The Recursion topic has an N-Queens lab.<\/p>","r":[],"L":[["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/n-queens/"],["Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html"]],"n":51,"sub":"LeetCode 51 · Hard · Recursion & Backtracking"},{"i":"c-prob-79","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Word Search","cat":"DSA problems","q":["Word Search","word search","leetcode 79","find word in grid","word exists in board"],"w":"word search grid dfs backtracking","a":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> DFS + backtracking from every cell; mark the cell used while it's on the path, then restore it.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">def exist(board, word):\n    R, C = len(board), len(board[0])\n    def dfs(r, c, k):\n        if k == len(word): return True\n        if not (0 &lt;= r &lt; R and 0 &lt;= c &lt; C) or board[r][c] != word[k]: return False\n        ch, board[r][c] = board[r][c], &#x27;#&#x27;          # mark\n        found = any(dfs(r + dr, c + dc, k + 1) for dr, dc in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)))\n        board[r][c] = ch                             # restore\n        return found\n    return any(dfs(r, c, 0) for r in range(R) for c in range(C))<\/pre><p>O(R·C·4^L) worst case for a word of length L; O(L) recursion depth. Many words at once → <b>Word Search II<\/b> with a trie (LC 212).<\/p>","r":[],"L":[["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/word-search/"],["Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html"]],"n":79,"sub":"LeetCode 79 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking"},{"i":"c-prob-22","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Generate Parentheses","cat":"DSA problems","q":["Generate Parentheses","generate parentheses","leetcode 22","all valid parentheses combinations"],"w":"generate parentheses backtracking catalan","a":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> backtracking with counts: add <code>(<\/code> while opens &lt; n; add <code>)<\/code> while closes &lt; opens.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">def generate_parenthesis(n):\n    res = []\n    def bt(s, open_, close):\n        if len(s) == 2 * n:\n            res.append(s); return\n        if open_ &lt; n:     bt(s + &#x27;(&#x27;, open_ + 1, close)\n        if close &lt; open_: bt(s + &#x27;)&#x27;, open_, close + 1)\n    bt(&#x27;&#x27;, 0, 0)\n    return res<\/pre><p>The number of results is the n-th Catalan number, about 4ⁿ / (n·√n).<\/p>","r":[],"L":[["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/generate-parentheses/"],["Recursion & Backtracking","recursion.html"]],"n":22,"sub":"LeetCode 22 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking"},{"i":"c-prob-102","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Binary Tree Level Order Traversal","cat":"DSA problems","q":["Binary Tree Level Order Traversal","level order traversal","leetcode 102","bfs on a tree","print tree level by level"],"w":"level order bfs tree queue","a":"<p><b>Pattern:<\/b> BFS with a queue, processing one level at a time (<code>for _ in range(len(q))<\/code>).<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">from collections import deque\ndef level_order(root):\n    if not root: return []\n    res, q = [], deque([root])\n    while q:\n        level = []\n        for _ in range(len(q)):\n            node = q.popleft(); level.append(node.val)\n            if node.left:  q.append(node.left)\n            if node.right: q.append(node.right)\n        res.append(level)\n    return res<\/pre><p>O(n) time, O(width) space. Variants: zigzag (LC 103), right side view (LC 199), averages per level.<\/p>","r":[],"L":[["LeetCode","https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-level-order-traversal/"],["Graphs","graphs.html"]],"n":102,"sub":"LeetCode 102 · Medium · Recursion & Backtracking"},{"i":"c-monostack","k":"cur","s":"","t":"Monotonic stack: when and how","cat":"DSA concepts","q":["Monotonic stack: when and how","when to use a monotonic stack","monotonic stack","next greater element pattern","monotonic increasing stack"],"w":"monotonic stack next greater smaller histogram","a":"<p>Use it when, for <b>every<\/b> element, you need the first bigger (or smaller) element to its left or right: next greater element, daily temperatures, stock span, largest rectangle in a histogram, trapping rain water.<\/p><p>Keep a stack of indices whose answer isn't known yet, kept in decreasing (or increasing) order. When a new element breaks the order, pop: the new element <i>is<\/i> the answer for everything it pops.<\/p><pre class=\"ap-pre\">def next_greater(nums):\n    ans, st = [-1] * len(nums), []      # st holds indices, values decreasing\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        while st and nums[st[-1]] &lt; x:\n            ans[st.pop()] = x\n        st.append(i)\n    return ans<\/pre><p>Each index is pushed and popped once → <b>O(n)<\/b>. Watch \"Last in, first out\" in Dojo Cinema.<\/p>","r":[],"L":[["Stack topic","stack.html"]]}]};